ganization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/daa9e601-815f-506c-b41d-bfb0872e16ea', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/daa9e601-815f-506c-b41d-bfb0872e16ea'}, {'abstract': 'Prominent investment funds managed hundreds of millions of dollars in Qaddafi regime assets, charging tens of millions of dollars in fees and producing low returns, according to a document.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/business/global/01libya.html', 'snippet': 'Prominent investment funds managed hundreds of millions of dollars in Qaddafi regime assets, charging tens of millions of dollars in fees and producing low returns, according to a document.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prominent American and European investment funds managed hundreds of millions of dollars in Qaddafi regime assets poorly, charging tens of millions of dollars in fees and producing low returns, according to a document obtained by the advocacy group Global Witness. The banks appeared to have taken advantage of a Libyan investment fund that was poorly managed and "a mess," according to a western official who spoke on condition of anonymity.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/business/LIBYA/LIBYA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Western Funds Are Said to Have Managed Libyan Money Poorly', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Western Funds Are Said to Have Managed Libyan Money Poorly', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sovereign Wealth Funds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HSBC Holdings PLC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T00:44:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Rohde', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohde', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c634cd61-214f-591b-82bc-8dfb53171abc', 'word_count': 890, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c634cd61-214f-591b-82bc-8dfb53171abc'}, {'abstract': 'The Ministry of Finance will repeal three rules on Friday that favor Chinese companies in competitions for government technology purchases, according to a statement posted this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/world/asia/01briefs-China.html', 'snippet': 'The Ministry of Finance will repeal three rules on Friday that favor Chinese companies in competitions for government technology purchases, according to a statement posted this week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Ministry of Finance will repeal three rules on Friday that favor Chinese companies in competitions for government technology purchases, according to a statement posted this week on the ministry’s Web site. China has long supported a policy of “indigenous innovation,” which grants advantages to Chinese companies over foreign rivals, especially in technology industries, and tries to enforce technology transfer from foreign companies in business deals. Foreign companies and governments have complained that such rules violate free-trade agreements. In January, when President Hu Jintao of China visited the White House, Chinese officials promised to drop some of those rules. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Rules Favoring Local Companies for State Contracts Will Be Eased', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | ASIA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Rules Favoring Local Companies For State Contracts Will Be Eased', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T00:46:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce33df91-f4d5-5ad8-a37a-9b0ae0f742a8', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce33df91-f4d5-5ad8-a37a-9b0ae0f742a8'}, {'abstract': 'Fairy tales helped me understand the suffering of my patients.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/opinion/01gribben.html', 'snippet': 'Fairy tales helped me understand the suffering of my patients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Birmingham, Ala.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Practicing Medicine Can Be Grimm Work', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Practicing Medicine Can Be Grimm Work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grimm, Brothers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fairy Tales', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T00:47:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gribben', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gribben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/57209477-4b17-5f4f-8a1b-ad75968a90c3', 'word_count': 823, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57209477-4b17-5f4f-8a1b-ad75968a90c3'}, {'abstract': 'Olajide Oluwaseun Noibi, 24, flew to Los Angeles from New York using an old boarding pass that belonged to another traveler, according to a criminal complaint.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/nyregion/olajide-oluwaseun-noibi-sneaked-onto-plane-with-old-pass.html', 'snippet': 'Olajide Oluwaseun Noibi, 24, flew to Los Angeles from New York using an old boarding pass that belonged to another traveler, according to a criminal complaint.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the F.B.I. caught up to Olajide Oluwaseun Noibi at Los Angeles International Airport on Wednesday, he was trying to talk a Delta Air Lines agent into letting him get on a flight to Atlanta. Mr. Noibi, 24, faced some resistance because the boarding pass in his hand was issued in another person’s name and was for a flight that had departed a day earlier, according to court documents.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wrong Name, Wrong Date, Yet Let on a Jet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wrong Name, Wrong Date, Yet Let on a Jet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Security and Warning Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Security Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '