, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T01:47:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eec024c0-a4dc-5d7f-8fa4-d6c51a7e49de', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eec024c0-a4dc-5d7f-8fa4-d6c51a7e49de'}, {'abstract': 'The challenge to a new Boeing plant in South Carolina by a union in Seattle has turned into a political firefight between the foes and partisans of organized labor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/business/01boeing.html', 'snippet': 'The challenge to a new Boeing plant in South Carolina by a union in Seattle has turned into a political firefight between the foes and partisans of organized labor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NORTH CHARLESTON, S.C. — Boeing’s gigantic new $750 million airplane factory here is the pride of South Carolina, the biggest single investment ever made in a state that is far more associated with old-line textile mills than state-of-the-art manufacturing. In just a few weeks, 1,000 workers will begin assembling the first of what they hope will be hundreds of 787 Dreamliners. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/business/BOEING/BOEING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/01/business/BOEING/BOEING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/business/BOEING/BOEING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/business/BOEING/BOEING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/01/business/BOEING/BOEING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Boeing Labor Dispute Is Making New Factory a Political Football', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Boeing Labor Dispute Is Making New Factory a Political Football', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (State)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Labor Relations Board', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boeing Company', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T01:49:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3f7b04b-a4f5-5c31-b2ea-3ff23d640be6', 'word_count': 1155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3f7b04b-a4f5-5c31-b2ea-3ff23d640be6'}, {'abstract': 'The Borders Group picked the Najafi Companies, a private equity firm that owns the Book-of-the-Month Club, as the lead bidder in an auction for the bankrupt bookseller.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/borders-picks-najafi-companies-as-lead-bidder/', 'snippet': 'The Borders Group picked the Najafi Companies, a private equity firm that owns the Book-of-the-Month Club, as the lead bidder in an auction for the bankrupt bookseller.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Borders Group said on Thursday evening that it had picked the Najafi Companies, a private equity firm that owns the Book-of-the-Month Club, as the lead bidder in a forthcoming court-supervised auction for the bankrupt bookseller.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Borders Picks Najafi Companies as Lead Bidder', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Borders Group Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Najafi Cos', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T01:50:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/276f8312-4881-555d-a2de-4c56eeb8064b', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/276f8312-4881-555d-a2de-4c56eeb8064b'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court must address doubts about its legitimacy as guardian of the rule of law.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/opinion/01fri1.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court must address doubts about its legitimacy as guardian of the rule of law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ethical judgments of the Supreme Court justices became an important issue in the just completed term. The court cannot maintain its legitimacy as guardian of the rule of law when justices behave like politicians. Yet, in several instances, justices acted in ways that weakened the court’s reputation for being independent and impartial. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ethics, Politics and the Law', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ethics, Politics and the Law: The Supreme Court must address doubts about its legitimacy as the rule of law’s guardian', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T01:52:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee52e17f-e69a-5264-afb8-d7104ed2a44c', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee52e17f-e69a-5264-afb8-d7104ed2a44c'}, {'abstract': 'This new history of Britain’s role in the American Civil War examines the battle the Union and the Confederacy waged for English support.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-a-world-on-fire-by-amanda-foreman.html', 'snippet': 'This new history of Britain’s role in the American Civil War examines the battle the Union and the Confederacy waged for English support.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Was it a civil war twice over? Not only did the “war between the states” divide the American people, it sundered the larger English-speaking community stretching across the Atlantic. The conflict was followed with consuming interest by the British, it affected them directly, many of them fought in it — and it split them into two camps, just as it did the Americans.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Wheatcroft-1308677430079/Wheatcroft-1308677430079-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 799, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Wheatcroft-1308677430079/Wheatcroft-1308677430079-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Wheatcroft-1308677430079/Wheatcroft-1308677430079-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the British Nearly Supported the Confederacy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Cousins’ War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foreman, Amanda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T01:54:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoffrey Wheatcroft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wheatcroft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bd5b108-b1f6-523f-9035-0f4202a90dac', 'word_count': 1711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bd5b108-b1f6-523f-9035-0f4202a90dac'}, {'abstract': '“Love Etc.” tries to explore the true nature of love in the city through the lives of eight New Yorkers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/movies/love-etc-review.html', 'snippet': '“Love Etc.” tries to explore the true nature of love in the city through the lives of eight New Yorkers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Did you watch “When Harry Met Sally” and wish it had dispensed with Billy Crystal and Meg Ryan? Well, of course you did. What I mean is, did you wish that the interstitial “interviews” with longtime couples could be expanded into their own feature while retaining the film’s calculated sentimentality? If so, Jill Andresevic’s “Love Etc.” is the movie you’ve been waiting for.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/love-span/ROUNDUP-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 307, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/love-span/ROUNDUP-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 307}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/love-span/ROUNDUP-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 525, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/love-span/ROUNDUP-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/love-span/ROUNDUP-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploring Love’s True Nature', 'kicker': 'Movie Review ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love Etc.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Love (Emotion)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T01:58:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0bdffd6-7a23-5f38-a15c-4681de48cd8b', 'word_count': 337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0bdffd6-7a23-5f38-a15c-4681de48cd8b'}, {'abstract': 'Chris McGlothlin crams as much as possible into today’s grid.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/shiloh/', 'snippet': 'Chris McGlothlin crams as much as possible into today’s grid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRIDAY’S PUZZLE — How long, on average, does it take you to suss out that there is something fishy going on in a crossword? Let’s say we’re talking about rebuses, which is actually quite convenient because today’s grid happens to have a rebus element in it. It’s not quite like other rebuses, though, and it’s kind of clever. If you are still wondering how to enter multiple letters in a grid, please read Martin Herbach’s wonderful instructional write-up here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday: Priest at Shiloh', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T02:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ff6657e-65be-55e2-8603-3d4b31113fd8', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ff6657e-65be-55e2-8603-3d4b31113fd8'}, {'abstract': '“Measure for Measure” at the Delacorte Theater in Central Park, is a dark, absorbing show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/theater/reviews/measure-for-measure-at-delacorte-theater-review.html', 'snippet': '“Measure for Measure” at the Delacorte Theater in Central Park, is a dark, absorbing show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Righteousness probably doesn’t rank too high on any list of the qualities that people find seductive. Nobody tunes in to a televangelist in search of an aphrodisiac, I’d be willing to bet.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/MEASURE-span/MEASURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/MEASURE-span/MEASURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/MEASURE-span/MEASURE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/MEASURE-span/MEASURE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/MEASURE-span/MEASURE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hold Onto Your Morals: Life Is Tough', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Measure for Measure'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hold Onto Your Morals: Life Is Tough', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pisoni, Lorenzo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations'