ping a longer-term strategy to close the budget deficit, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/whats-a-crisis-and-what-isnt/', 'snippet': 'Focusing on employment policies and job creation is more urgent than developing a longer-term strategy to close the budget deficit, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Laura D’Andrea Tyson is a professor at the Haas School of Business at the University of California, Berkeley, and served as chairwoman of the Council of Economic Advisers under President Clinton.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s a Crisis and What Isn’t', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congressional Budget Office', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T10:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': "By Laura D'Andrea Tyson", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': "D'andrea", 'lastname': 'Tyson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86d53766-76e7-5313-991a-3248bb98ee7d', 'word_count': 1360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86d53766-76e7-5313-991a-3248bb98ee7d'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/the-early-word-early-deadline/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Early Deadline', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T10:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96f6833d-ac53-5693-aa23-6d788276f713', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96f6833d-ac53-5693-aa23-6d788276f713'}, {'abstract': 'Antonio Gates is No. 1 on this list. Why do you think San Diego’s passing game survived a rash of injuries last season?', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/top-10-tight-ends-in-n-f-l/', 'snippet': 'Antonio Gates is No. 1 on this list. Why do you think San Diego’s passing game survived a rash of injuries last season?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Friendly Disclaimer: The following list is influenced largely -– but not strictly -– by film study of all 32 teams from the 2010 season. Stats weren’t acknowledged, but players with poor numbers generally don’t make top 10 lists anyway. Vague enough for you? Criteria for top 10 lists tend to be. That’s why most of you will have no trouble finding some disagreement with what you’re about to read. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Top 10 Tight Ends in N.F.L.', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T10:01:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cbd9a0b-83ea-5217-adc5-222ded86c88a', 'word_count': 419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cbd9a0b-83ea-5217-adc5-222ded86c88a'}, {'abstract': 'How a failing levee system could turn Sacramento into a modern-day Atlantis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/magazine/sacramento-levees-pose-risk-to-california-and-the-country.html', 'snippet': 'How a failing levee system could turn Sacramento into a modern-day Atlantis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People tend to underestimate the power of floods: six inches of fast-moving water can knock you down; two feet of water can float most cars away. Floods kill an average of 127 Americans a year — more than tornadoes or hurricanes — and cause more than $2 billion of property damage annually, according to the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Association.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/magazine/mag-03sacramento-t_CA0/mag-03sacramento-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 596, 'width': 949, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/magazine/mag-03sacramento-t_CA0/mag-03sacramento-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/magazine/mag-03sacramento-t_CA0/mag-03sacramento-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'California’s Next Nightmare', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California’s Next Nightmare', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Levees and Dams', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sacramento (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T10:06:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': "By ALEX PRUD'HOMME", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "PRUD'HOMME", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7a99c1d-dcc4-5080-a5cc-2a4f5c376873', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7a99c1d-dcc4-5080-a5cc-2a4f5c376873'}, {'abstract': 'Paid back, but wanting payback.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/magazine/the-ethicist-closing-the-books.html', 'snippet': 'Paid back, but wanting payback.', 'lead_paragraph': ' I am the chief financial officer for a financial-services company, and I found our bookkeeper using the corporate charge card for her personal use. The misappropriation was approximately $47,000 over a six-month period. She forged a partner’s signature to acquire a card in her name. We fired her, and she paid back the funds in exchange for our not pressing charges. But I cannot get closure if she is not punished for this egregious betrayal. I recommended that we call her husband, as I think family humiliation would be punishment enough. Would this be ethical? NAME WITHHELD,NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'ima