ideo', '_id': 'nyt://video/79acef43-1699-5e9e-a56d-5bffb89809b6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/79acef43-1699-5e9e-a56d-5bffb89809b6'}, {'abstract': 'Apple’s iPhoto program can automatically pull in pictures from a connected digital camera, but you can also easily save images attached to Mail messages too.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/tip-of-the-week-saving-e-mailed-photos-for-macs/', 'snippet': 'Apple’s iPhoto program can automatically pull in pictures from a connected digital camera, but you can also easily save images attached to Mail messages too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apple’s iPhoto program, which comes free on most new Macs, can automatically pull in pictures from a connected digital camera, but you can also easily save images attached to Mail messages too. When you open a message in the Mac OS X Mail program and see photos that you’d like to add to the iPhoto library, click and hold the cursor down on the small blue Save button in the message header. At the bottom of the drop-down menu that appears, select the “Save to iPhoto” option to save all pictures into the iPhoto program. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip of the Week: Saving E-Mailed Photos for Macs', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T12:17:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_materi