 market.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/providence-to-buy-blackboard-for-1-64-billion/', 'snippet': 'Providence will pay $45 a share for the college coursework software maker, in the private equity firm’s latest foray into the education market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Providence Equity Partners agreed on Friday to buy Blackboard Inc., a maker of college coursework software, for $1.64 billion in cash, in the private equity firm’s latest foray into the education market.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Providence to Buy Blackboard for $1.64 Billion', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Blackboard Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Providence Equity Partners Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T12:42:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be44bef5-63b8-5598-a8ed-2740442a4639', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be44bef5-63b8-5598-a8ed-2740442a4639'}, {'abstract': 'Hotel chains offer bikes to guests, local laws fighting fat come under siege, why you should be nice to the medical receptionist and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/friday-reading-why-to-be-nice-to-medical-receptionists/', 'snippet': 'Hotel chains offer bikes to guests, local laws fighting fat come under siege, why you should be nice to the medical receptionist and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: Be Nice to Medical Receptionists', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T12:44:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ecaa080-685a-5af0-a20c-0a308c3ee91b', 'word_count': 44, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ecaa080-685a-5af0-a20c-0a308c3ee91b'}, {'abstract': 'The Moment recently caught up with the Aesop founder, Dennis Paphitis, to talk about architecture, why art inspires him and what he thinks about all-natural skin care claims.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/vain-glorious-a-chat-with-aesop/', 'snippet': 'The Moment recently caught up with the Aesop founder, Dennis Paphitis, to talk about architecture, why art inspires him and what he thinks about all-natural skin care claims.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Australian skin care brand Aesop — Greek for “storyteller”— is a cult favorite among the type of discerning consumers who prefer their jeans dark, their espresso straight up and their bathrooms uncluttered. And like the Aesop products, the Aesop boutiques make innovative use of unusual ingredients, so there’s no sense of shopping at a chain when you pop in to pick up your Parsley Seed Anti-Oxidant Facial Serum (which, incidentally, is brilliant for maintaining a glowing complexion through the depths of sticky East Coast summers). Case in point: the first of Aesop’s four planned New York shops, which opens in the Graybar Passage in Grand Central Terminal next week, will be built primarily from recycled 19th-century newspapers. Locations on Elizabeth Street and University Place will follow by the end of the summer, and a flagship on Bleecker Street will open in December. The Moment recently caught up with the Aesop founder, Dennis Paphitis, to talk about architecture, why art inspires him and what he thinks about all-natural skin care claims.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vain Glorious | Aesop Takes New York', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T13:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy MacDonell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4586e94e-e24d-5d74-b139-222f8d6e04d5', 'word_count': 1143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4586e94e-e24d-5d74-b139-222f8d6e04d5'}, {'abstract': 'The Republicans have a response to President Obama’s call for them to stay in Washington until the debt debate ends.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/republicans-downtime-throwdown/', 'snippet': 'The Republicans have a response to President Obama’s call for them to stay in Washington until the debt debate ends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In politics, sometimes attacking your opponent can give them an opening to attack back. That’s what President Obama did by urging Republican lawmakers to stay in Washington until the debt debate is over.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Republicans’ Downtime Throwdown', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T13:39:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b3ecd9e-6659-5c69-a48d-a81746ad0281', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b3ecd9e-6659-5c69-a48d-a81746ad0281'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the morning newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/morning-take-out-277/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the morning newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOP STORIES ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T13:40:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/631b1e2f-05e7-5935-98ab-e87693a413be', 'word_count': 1870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/631b1e2f-05e7-5935-98ab-e87693a413be'}, {'abstract': 'Live analysis of the 2011 Wimbledon men’s semifinal between Rafael Nadal and Andy Murray.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/live-soon-nadal-vs-murray-2/', 'snippet': 'Live analysis of the 2011 Wimbledon men’s semifinal between Rafael Nadal and Andy Murray.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — Britain can exhale now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nadal Tops Murray for Berth in Final', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T13:44:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2353c1db-d414-5501-a242-28a817810ee2', 'word_count': 2470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2353c1db-d414-5501-a242-28a817810ee2'}, {'abstract': 'In the long run, still dead, but not forgotten.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/keynes-film-festival/', 'snippet': 'In the long run, still dead, but not forgotten.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A guy with a beard talks about a guy with a mustache. Videos of the lecture and Q&A.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Keynes Film Festival', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T13:55:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e58b18c-8e50-52c7-b865-59a4d0c4e4be', 'word_count': 18, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e58b18c-8e50-52c7-b865-59a4d0c4e4be'}, {'abstract': 'Interest rate spike, not.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/soar-losers/', 'snippet': 'Interest rate spike, not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like everyone who has argued that continuing low interest rates vindicate the Keynesian view of how macroeconomics works, I’ve faced a lot of claims that the low rates were artificial, that as soon as the Fed stopped buying bonds rates would soar. Some big-name private-sector people, notably Bill Gross, have made similar claims.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Soar Losers', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T14:04:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13ba8f15-19f6-580c-960e-e759a213d957', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13ba8f15-19f6-580c-960e-e759a213d957'}, {'abstract': 'Those who think a little bit of Wall Street panic may be just the medicine to break the federal debt ceiling impasse may not have reckoned on the turbulent unpredictability of recent investor behavior in the credit markets.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/the-danger-in-depending-on-markets-to-motivate-debt-deal/', 'snippet': 'Those who think a little bit of Wall Street panic may be just the medicine to break the federal debt ceiling impasse may not have reckoned on the turbulent unpredictability of recent investor behavior in the credit markets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many astute political observers worry that an elegant resolution to the negotiations over the federal debt limit may not be in the making — and that there is a tangible possibility that the United States will default on its debts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Danger in Counting on Markets to Drive a Debt-Limit Deal', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T14:06:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ff6aded-0cde-5519-af49-7e584a9b27b7', 'word_count': 1307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ff6aded-0cde-5519-af49-7e584a9b27b7'}, {'abstract': 'Priscilla Winn Barlow, who led the school from 1997 to 2003, came out of retirement to replace Stephanie J. Hull, who departed abruptly on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/formerly-brearley-leader-returns-as-interim-head/', 'snippet': 'Priscilla Winn Barlow, who led the school from 1997 to 2003, came out of retirement to replace Stephanie J. Hull, who departed abruptly on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brearley School‘s trustees moved quickly on Friday to contain any damage from the abrupt resignation of its head of school, naming a former Brearley leader as interim head of school.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Former Brearley Leader Returns as Interim Head', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name':