 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Emily B. Hager|Ben Solomon|Ted Trautman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/635be165-6e76-533b-8e6d-265d8868c956', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/635be165-6e76-533b-8e6d-265d8868c956'}, {'abstract': 'For her latest venture, “Biophilia,” Björk has brought together recording-studio collaborators, instrument makers, smartphone app designers, scientists and a musicologist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/arts/music/bjorks-biophilia-at-the-manchester-international-festival.html', 'snippet': 'For her latest venture, “Biophilia,” Björk has brought together recording-studio collaborators, instrument makers, smartphone app designers, scientists and a musicologist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANCHESTER, England — In one song that Bjork performed here on Thursday night, the bass lines were zaps of artificial lightning from a Tesla coil in a cage. In another, a sizable Victorian-looking contraption of stainless steel, the Sharpsichord — part giant music box, part harp, part gramophone — used a rotating barrel to play deep, bonging counterpoint. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/BJORK/BJORK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/BJORK/BJORK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 349}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/BJORK/BJORK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/BJORK/BJORK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/BJORK/BJORK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Science of Song, the Song of Science', 'kicker': "Critic's Notebook", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Science of Song, the Song of Science', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bjork', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T14:41:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd79c129-af09-56d4-9742-ce1bcbde73ad', 'word_count': 1471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd79c129-af09-56d4-9742-ce1bcbde73ad'}, {'abstract': 'Reading the textbook makes you a hippie.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/a-small-note-on-the-stimulus-debate/', 'snippet': 'Reading the textbook makes you a hippie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One small followup on the issue of being prematurely right about the stimulus being too small: you might think that hippies like me were basing their views on some wild and crazy, unorthodox version of economics, while the wise, judicious people who thought that $787 billion was just right were using standard analysis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Small Note on the Stimulus Debate', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T14:41:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb85c655-3de3-5e2c-9143-a0aa36ccaccf', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb85c655-3de3-5e2c-9143-a0aa36ccaccf'}, {'abstract': 'Debating sex selection and abortion with the author of “Unnatural Selection”', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/a-response-to-mara-hvistendahl-i/', 'snippet': 'Debating sex selection and abortion with the author of “Unnatural Selection”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The author of “Unnatural Selection” has kindly written a reply to my column on her book. Not surprisingly, she takes exception to my suggestion that sex-selective abortion poses a problem for pro-choice liberals, arguing that there’s no contradiction at all between supporting abortion rights and trying to restrict/ban abortions based on sex:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Response to Mara Hvistendahl (I)', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T14:44:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c70207b-f9fd-5022-b4bf-07936cb2fe8d', 'word_count': 815, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c70207b-f9fd-5022-b4bf-07936cb2fe8d'}, {'abstract': 'All but services deemed essential cease amid an impa