ator, has announced a joint venture with a Hong Kong investment company for a new superhero franchise aimed at Chinese and foreign audiences, The Associated Press reports. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stan Lee Starts Chinese Superhero Franchise', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Stan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T15:18:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d782589-a299-5105-b8a7-7aefad9da117', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d782589-a299-5105-b8a7-7aefad9da117'}, {'abstract': 'We found ourselves in a dilemma for this week’s issue: four different cover treatments and many different opinions about which one was the most aesthetically pleasing. First we did an internal poll in the art department.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/how-do-you-prefer-your-heart/', 'snippet': 'We found ourselves in a dilemma for this week’s issue: four different cover treatments and many different opinions about which one was the most aesthetically pleasing. First we did an internal poll in the art department.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We found ourselves in a dilemma for this week’s issue: four different cover treatments and many different opinions about which one was the most aesthetically pleasing. First we did an internal poll in the art department. The rules were simple, vote on your favorite cover by putting a small wooden chip (broken coffee stirrer) into the matching cup. Only problem was, we had a tie and we needed more votes. So we opened up the judging to the entire staff and 37 staff members from every department voted.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Do You Prefer Your Heart?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T15:19:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arem Duplessis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Duplessis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c90bab16-47af-57a4-a492-0b0f9277ba86', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c90bab16-47af-57a4-a492-0b0f9277ba86'}, {'abstract': 'The gathering appeared to be the biggest demonstration since the Syrian uprising began nearly four months ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/world/middleeast/02syria.html', 'snippet': 'The gathering appeared to be the biggest demonstration since the Syrian uprising began nearly four months ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — Tens of thousands of protesters poured Friday into the streets of Hama, a Syrian city abandoned by the military and security forces, gathering in the country’s biggest demonstration in nearly four months of unrest and staking a festive claim to a region that bore the brunt of a ferocious government crackdown a generation ago.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/world/2syria2_cnd/2syria2_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/02/world/2syria2_cnd/2syria2_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/world/2syria2_cnd/2syria2_cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/world/2syria2_cnd/2syria2_cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/world/2syria2_cnd/2syria2_cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Police Absent, Protests Surge in Syrian City', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Police Absent, Protests Surge in Syrian City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T15:24:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Shadid', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shadid', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc83f117-2888-55ce-9fd1-a1872b7aea68', 'word_count': 1108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc83f117-2888-55ce-9fd1-a1872b7aea68'}, {'abstract': 'Owners and players are expected to continue talking Friday in Minneapolis; the sides are sparring over issues like how to divide revenue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/sports/football/nfl-labor-deal-is-likely-to-be-weeks-away.html', 'snippet': 'Owners and players are expected to continue talking Friday in Minneapolis; the sides are sparring over issues like how to divide revenue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'N.F.L. owners and players continued to negotiate late Thursday evening, but progress slowed as time dwindled for striking a labor deal that would avoid the disruption of the preseason.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.F.L. Deal Is Said to Be Weeks Away as Talks Restart', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'N.F.L. Deal Is Said to Be Weeks Away as Talks Restart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodell, Roger', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, DeMaurice', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T15:27:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Battista', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Battista', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eab7c06d-0eb3-599f-9541-d10770de87ce', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eab7c06d-0eb3-599f-9541-d10770de87ce'}, {'abstract': 'The Marc by Marc Jacobs collection, in stores this month, is dedicated to those infected with jungle fever.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/what-would-tarzan-think/', 'snippet': 'The Marc by Marc Jacobs collection, in stores this month, is dedicated to those infected with jungle fever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For those infected with jungle fever (see Prada’s chic monkey and banana frocks for spring), the good news is you can follow the trend into fall. The Marc by Marc Jacobs collection, in stores this month, features panthers and antelopes on everything from drop pants to jacquard blazers. The prints are the handiwork of Michael Roberts, who, when he’s not working for Vanity Fair as style editor at large, is illustrating childen’s pop-up books about the jungle. This clutch, for example, with its sleek appliqué ($328), is entirely fierce. Go to marcjacobs.com. For the scarf, $178, go to nordstrom.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Would Tarzan Think?', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T15:33:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kavitha Surana', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kavitha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Surana', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fe7fd39-ab6b-5f79-b122-4781930dd7f5', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fe7fd39-ab6b-5f79-b122-4781930dd7f5'}, {'abstract': 'Canine DNA is now being used to identify the culprits who fail to clean up after their pets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/us/02dogs.html', 'snippet': 'Canine DNA is now being used to identify the culprits who fail to clean up after their pets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sherlock Holmes had the case of the dog that didn’t bark, but it has taken two dozen apartment complexes and a testing company in Tennessee to bring the art of canine detection into the “CSI” age.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/us/DOGS/DOGS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 713, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/us/DOGS/DOGS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/us/DOGS/DOGS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tracing Unscooped Dog Waste Back to the Culprit', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tracing Unscooped Dog Waste to the Culprit, With Science', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T15:34:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Zezima', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zezima', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3985b83-11ab-581c-9131-11ca249992b5', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3985b83-11ab-581c-9131-11ca249992b5'}, {'abstract': 'You have to admire automakers that take risks, but the 2011 Nissan Murano Crosscabriolet is a poorly conceived one that hits virtually no high notes as either a crossover or a convertible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/automobiles/autoreviews/03WHEEL.html', 'snippet': 'You have to admire automakers that take risks, but the 2011 Nissan Murano Crosscabriolet is a poorly conceived one that hits virtually no high notes as either a crossover or a convertible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN a world of play-it-safe auto design driven by focus groups, it’s good to see companies take some chances.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/automobiles/JP-WHEEL/JP-WHEEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/03/automobiles/JP-WHEEL/JP-WHEEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/automobiles/JP-WHEEL/JP-WHEEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 608, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/automobiles/JP-WHEEL/JP-WHEEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/automobiles/JP-WHEEL/JP-WHEEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ride of Frankenstein: Daring, or Just Deranged?', 'kicker': 'Behind the Wheel | 2011 Nissan Murano Crosscabriolet', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Ride of Frankenstein: Daring, or Just Deranged?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nissan Motor Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T15:37:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'subsection_name': 'New Cars', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Ulrich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ulrich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c13e37c3-55a9-5149-bf26-d8ff9c0e53ab', 'word_count': 1371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c13e37c3-55a9-5149-bf26-d8ff9c0e53ab'}, {'abstract': 'Mayor Glenn Singer explains how Golden Beach, Fla., can undertake a $35 million capital improvement project while most towns face crippling budget woes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/us/02questions.html', 'snippet': 'Mayor Glenn Singer explains how Golden Beach, Fla., can undertake a $35 million capital improvement project while most towns face crippling budget woes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While American cities large and small are grappling with budget deficits of historic proportions — laying off teachers and police officers and closing libraries — the town of Golden Beach, Fla., is doing fine, thank you. The town, nestled along the Atlantic Ocean in Miami-Dade County, has a minuscule unemployment rate, plenty of money and little crime. Among its current or former homeowners are Ricky Martin, Bill Gates and Paul Newman. The town also has a history of exclusion — at one point in the 1980s it closed most of its roads to the outside world as part of an effort to deter crime. These days, the town has entry gates staffed by security officers. Mayor Glenn Singer, elected this year to his fourth term, discusses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Defying the Trend, a Small Town Loosens Its Belt', 'kicker': 'Asked and Answered | Glenn Singer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Defying the Trend, a Small Town Loosens Its Belt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T15:42:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/d99d5a09-e6df-5396-b08c-9f50830b0278', 'word_count': 809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d99d5a09-e6df-5396-b08c-9f50830b0278'}, {'abstract': 'Malaysia Airlines send infants further back in the plane.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/banning-babies-in-first-class/', 'snippet': 'Malaysia Airlines send infants further back in the plane.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As you depart for whichever backyard, or beach, or local park, or airport marks the start of your Independence Day weekend, an update on Malaysia Airlines’ announcement that infants will not be allowed up front when the airline introduces its new Airbus A380 aircraft next year. The airline already has such a ban on its 747-400 fleet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Banning Babies in First Class', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T15:50:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 