 hot dogs and views of fireworks.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/weekend-fare-fourth-of-july-edition/', 'snippet': 'Celebrating Independence weekend with wine, cookouts, candy and ice cream sandwiches, crayfish and, of course, hot dogs and views of fireworks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Free tastes of wine from Italy’s Veneto region will be served on Friday from 2 to 4 p.m. at Enoteca Di Palo, 200 Grand Street (Mott Street), in Little Italy. From 6 to 8 p.m., sparkling wines from the region will be available at I Tre Merli, 463 West Broadway (Prince Street), in SoHo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Fare: Fourth of July Edition', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kobayashi, Takeru', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Dylan's Candy Bar", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:07:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bao Ong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cf00d18-8f5c-5f3c-8e45-41c31b6fce9a', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cf00d18-8f5c-5f3c-8e45-41c31b6fce9a'}, {'abstract': 'The 90th anniversary of the founding of the Chinese Communist Party was observed with a stiff and scripted, though much-hyped, event, with an emphasis on social stability.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/world/asia/02china.html', 'snippet': 'The 90th anniversary of the founding of the Chinese Communist Party was observed with a stiff and scripted, though much-hyped, event, with an emphasis on social stability.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — The party for the Party turned out to be a doctrinaire affair, as President Hu Jintao expounded Friday on the benefits of 90 years of communism with Chinese characteristics while workplaces around the country held red-song singalongs.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/world/02china2/02china2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/02/world/02china2/02china2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/world/02china2/02china2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/world/02china2/02china2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/world/02china2/02china2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'By-the-Book Celebration for China’s Communists on Party’s 90th Birthday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'By-the-Book Celebration For China’s Communists On Party’s 90th Birthday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Communism (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mao Zedong', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:08:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cd37fbd-84ea-5a01-8fa3-c8eb3fbf2383', 'word_count': 999, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cd37fbd-84ea-5a01-8fa3-c8eb3fbf2383'}, {'abstract': 'While Disney focuses on lucrative franchises, Pixar maintains it is devoted to the story. In “Cars 2,” Disney won.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/business/02stewart.html', 'snippet': 'While Disney focuses on lucrative franchises, Pixar maintains it is devoted to the story. In “Cars 2,” Disney won.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Walt Disney Company bought Pixar Animation Studios for $7.4 billion in 2006, there was understandable concern that the media conglomerate that drove the famed Disney animation unit into the ground with duds like “Home on the Range” and “Chicken Little” would extinguish the creative flame that had nurtured the quirky and much-loved “Toy Story” and “Finding Nemo.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Collision of Creativity and Cash', 'kicker': 'Common Sense', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Collision Of Creativity And Cash', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pixar Animation Studios', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMATED FILMS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walt Disney Company', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:09:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By James B. Stewart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76053b29-949b-5ba3-b5eb-adb8fda4d18e', 'word_count': 1392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76053b29-949b-5ba3-b5eb-adb8fda4d18e'}, {'abstract': 'Aug. 2 is still the last day the government will be able to pay all its bills, according to a new Treasury estimate.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/debt-ceiling-date-does-not-budge/', 'snippet': 'Aug. 2 is still the last day the government will be able to pay all its bills, according to a new Treasury estimate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Aug. 2 debt ceiling deadline is holding firm. The Treasury Department said Friday morning that its latest estimates still show that’s the date the government will exhaust its ability to pay all of its bills, unless Congress agrees to raise the total amount that the United States can borrow.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debt Ceiling Date Does Not Budge', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:10:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/653536fc-1665-5aff-98f7-0871de996f26', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/653536fc-1665-5aff-98f7-0871de996f26'}, {'abstract': 'The letter outlines interviews with the hotel housekeeper who had accused Mr. Strauss-Kahn, and raises questions about her version of events.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/01/nyregion/20110701-Strauss-Kahn-letter.html', 'snippet': 'The letter outlines interviews with the hotel housekeeper who had accused Mr. Strauss-Kahn, and raises questions about her version of events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The letter outlines interviews with the hotel housekeeper who had accused Mr. Strauss-Kahn, and raises questions about her version of events.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letter From District Attorney to Defense in Strauss-Kahn Case', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vance, Cyrus R Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:13:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/eb20be8d-bf62-5359-bc2b-8be115a0946e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/eb20be8d-bf62-5359-bc2b-8be115a0946e'}, {'abstract': 'Phil McCaughey', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/the-man-who-keeps-the-boats-afloat-in-flushing-bay/', 'snippet': 'Phil McCaughey', 'lead_paragraph': 'Phil McCaughey wiped the dusty sweat off his sunburned neck and pulled a Post-It out of his pocket.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Man Who Keeps the Boats Afloat in Flushing Bay', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Flushing (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:13:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71fd5a32-5122-56ac-8348-461b358c5cd5', 'word_count': 917, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71fd5a32-5122-56ac-8348-461b358c5cd5'}, {'abstract': 'The protest came just days after renewed clashes between protesters and the police left hundreds injured.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/world/middleeast/02egypt.html', 'snippet': 'The protest came just days after renewed clashes between protesters and the police left hundreds injured.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — Thousands of protesters returned to Tahrir Square here on Friday to voice frustration with what they called the slow pace of change five months after the revolution. The demonstration occurred just days after renewed clashes between protesters and the police left hundreds injured and underscored the lingering distrust between Egyptians and a police force long seen as a pillar of the former government. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/world/02Egypt/02Egypt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/world/02Egypt/02Egypt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/world/02Egypt/02Egypt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Thousands in Cairo Return to Tahrir Square to Protest the Slow Pace of Change', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Thousands in Cairo Return to Tahrir Square to Protest the Slow Pace of Change', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Said, Khaled', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adly, Habib el-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tahrir Square (Cairo)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:16:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liam Stack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f93f2484-89f2-5f36-b20c-00b045afaf4c', 'word_count': 825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f93f2484-89f2-5f36-b20c-00b045afaf4c'}, {'abstract': '“Pirates of the Caribbean: On Stranger Tides” is about to become the eighth movie to break the $1 billion mark at the global box office.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/pirates-booty-approaches-1-billion/', 'snippet': '“Pirates of the Caribbean: On Stranger Tides” is about to become the eighth movie to break the $1 billion mark at the global box office.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just a few months ago, the people behind “Pirates of the Caribbean: On Stranger Tides” were shivering in their timbers. Would their $250 million movie win back fans, the ones who were fed up with bloated running times and bewildering plots? Or would the audience respond with a great big yo ho hum?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Pirates’ Booty Approaches $1 Billion', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bruckheimer, Jerry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Depp, Johnny', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marshall, Rob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ross, Rich', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walt Disney Company', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:17:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea53a55c-78ca-5816-a77a-371a13ba50d5', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea53a55c-78ca-5816-a77a-371a13ba50d5'}, {'abstract': 'The N.B.A. has taken a page out of the N.F.L.’s ill-advised playbook and locked out its players.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/sports/basketball/leading-off-a-lesson-not-learned.html', 'snippet': 'The N.B.A. has taken a page out of the N.F.L.’s ill-advised playbook and locked out its players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you can muster some outrage, by all means go ahead and vent it on the N.B.A. Apparently learning nothing from the N.F.L.’s ongoing debacle, the N.B.A. motored full steam ahead with its lockout plans on Thursday, another league bent on image annihilation in the name of saving themselves from their own business failings. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Lesson Not Learned', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Lesson Not Learned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:19:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/558ca039-8cc1-5aaf-8436-155c18dfc760', 'word_count': 560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/558ca039-8cc1-5aaf-8436-155c18dfc760'}, {'abstract': 'In the singer-songwriter Josh Ritter’s first novel, a West Virginia farm boy heeds voices he began hearing in the trenches of World War I.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-brights-passage-by-josh-ritter.html', 'snippet': 'In the singer-songwriter Josh Ritter’s first novel, a West Virginia farm boy heeds voices he began hearing in the trenches of World War I.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Henry Bright is pretty much your ordinary 20th-century West Virginia farmboy, with one notable exception: when he winds up in the World War I trenches in France, he begins hearing voices.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/KING/KING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 922, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/KING/KING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/KING/KING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Josh Ritter’s Novel of Appalachia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Appalachian Angel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'APPALACHIAN REGION', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ritter, Josh', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:19:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen King', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'King', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/059bfae1-20ba-5973-9d21-a706ddaba7e1', 'word_count': 1139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/059bfae1-20ba-5973-9d21-a706ddaba7e1'}, {'abstract': 'Writing about her great-grandfather, the socialist Norman Thomas, Louisa Thomas considers how conscience fares when society deems it subversive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-conscience-by-louisa-thomas.html', 'snippet': 'Writing about her great-grandfather, the socialist Norman Thomas, Louisa Thomas considers how conscience fares when society deems it subversive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In every presidential election from 1928 to 1948, Americans had the option of voting for Norman Thomas, the perennial Socialist Party candidate. Very few chose to do so. Thomas’s best result — 2.2 percent of the ballot nationwide — was in 1932, and he never came close to that share again. Yet when he died in 1968 at the age of 84, he was remembered as a courageous idealist who, in the words of a tribute in The New York Times, “spoke to the feelings that most Americans have about themselves.” In other words, he was an advocate for fairness, decency, justice and equality, a man to be admired though hardly one to be voted into office.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/bkr-Riding-t_CA0/bkr-Riding-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 964, 'width': 694, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/bkr-Riding-t_CA0/bkr-Riding-t_CA0-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/bkr-Riding-t_CA0/bkr-Riding-t_CA0-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Pacifist in the Family', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Courage of Their Convictions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conscientious Objectors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Socialism (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Norman', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:20:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Riding', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Riding', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c095f05b-7495-538f-8904-13e250593f31', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c095f05b-7495-538f-8904-13e250593f31'}, {'abstract': 'Pamela Haag examines the phenomenon of marriages that are not unhappy enough to break up, but not exactly happy, either.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-marriage-confidential-by-pamela-haag.html', 'snippet': 'Pamela Haag examines the phenomenon of marriages that are not unhappy enough to break up, but not exactly happy, either.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How does one write a whole book on the boredom of marriage without becoming boring oneself? Pamela Haag’s “Marriage Confidential: The Post-Romantic Age of Workhorse Wives, Royal Children, Undersexed Spouses and Rebel Couples Who Are Rewriting the Rules” can, in spite of its exceptionally boppy subtitle, only partly answer that question.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Roiphe/Roiphe-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 926, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Roiphe/Roiphe-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Roiphe/Roiphe-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Not Quite Unhappy Marriage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ambivalently Ever After', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Love (Emotion)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Divorce, Separations and Annulments', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:20:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Roiphe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roiphe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f39c6947-ac97-53cc-a5c7-e82bc9557ef9', 'word_count': 1217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f39c6947-ac97-53cc-a5c7-e82bc9557ef9'}, {'abstract': 'In this graphic memoir, Chester Brown gives up on romance and pursues sex with prostitutes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-paying-for-it-by-chester-brown.html', 'snippet': 'In this graphic memoir, Chester Brown gives up on romance and pursues sex with prostitutes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who has not seen the remorseful preachers, politicians, actors and other men in the news, begging forgiveness when caught with their pants down and publicly outed as “johns,” patrons of the world’s oldest profession? To be a known john is to be trailed by shame and stigma, at least in the United States and Canada. There are millions of johns, but for one to come out voluntarily — with honesty, integrity and pride — is indeed rare. In my nearly 40 years in the world of sex workers, I’ve known only one: Fred Cherry. In the 1980s in New York, Cherry sued Mayor Ed Koch for the legal right to pay for sex with prostitutes (a case Cherry ultimately lost). To ascertain if any other such brave johns had slipped my mind, I surveyed a few of my older prostitute friends. “No, just Fred Cherry,” they confirmed. (Today, I might count Charlie Sheen.) So for the Canadian comic artist Chester Brown to announce in his graphic memoir, “Paying for It,” that he is a john is truly groundbreaking.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Sprinkle/Sprinkle-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 700, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Sprinkle/Sprinkle-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Sprinkle/Sprinkle-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A John’s Story', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A John’s Story', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostitution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Sprinkle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sprinkle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/623d7bd5-50ad-508e-ac02-bed98c87e708', 'word_count': 996, 'uri': 'nyt://article/623d7bd5-50ad-508e-ac02-bed98c87e708'}, {'abstract': 'A new translation of Aristotle’s “Ethics” addresses the perennial question of well-being.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-aristotles-nicomachean-ethics.html', 'snippet': 'A new translation of Aristotle’s “Ethics” addresses the perennial question of well-being.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some time in the 1920s, the Conservative statesman F. E. Smith — Lord Birkenhead — gave a copy of the “Nicomachean Ethics” to his close friend Winston Churchill. He did so saying there were those who thought this was the greatest book of all time. Churchill returned it some weeks later, saying it was all very interesting, but he had already thought most of it out for himself. But it is the very genius of Aristotle — as it is of every great teacher — to make you think he is uncovering your own thought in his. In Churchill’s case, it is also probable that the classical tradition informed more of his upbringing, at home and at school, than he realized. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Jaffer/Jaffer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 957, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Jaffer/Jaffer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Jaffer/Jaffer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Aristotle and the Higher Good', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Faith and Reason', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:21:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harry V. Jaffa', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harry', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Jaffa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/292610f5-a890-5b6c-914e-903995bc185e', 'word_count': 1268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/292610f5-a890-5b6c-914e-903995bc185e'}, {'abstract': 'Bharati Mukherjee’s eighth novel is a kind of parable of the new India.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-miss-new-india-by-bharati-mukherjee.html', 'snippet': 'Bharati Mukherjee’s eighth novel is a kind of parable of the new India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nations are narratives. Every country is shaped by its particular set of ideas and myths. Inevitably these are simplifications, often clichés, but they hold a country together, imposing a certain coherence on diverse populations.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Kapur/Kapur-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 967, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Kapur/Kapur-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Kapur/Kapur-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Parable of the New India', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One in 1.2 Billion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mukherjee, Bharati', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:21:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Akash Kapur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Akash', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kapur', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a76eca4-6790-5a55-a051-2d2f7907b117', 'word_count': 808, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a76eca4-6790-5a55-a051-2d2f7907b117'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, a suburban dad accidentally contributes to a fatal accident, and tries to hide his actions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-long-drive-home-by-will-allison.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, a suburban dad accidentally contributes to a fatal accident, and tries to hide his actions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Tom Wolfe’s “Bonfire of the Vanities,” the mistress of a Wall Street bond salesman mows down a black youth in the South Bronx, and he encourages her to keep driving. In Will Allison’s second novel, “Long Drive Home,” another \xadmiddle-aged money man tries to cover up his role in a racially tinged fatal accident. In Allison’s case, the beleaguered white guy is Glen Bauer, a suburban New Jersey accountant who, offended by the sight of a black teenager in a convertible barreling too fast down his sleepy residential block, swerves briefly into his path to give the kid a scare. Instead, he crashes to his death. What’s more, just as in “Bonfire,” the accident creates a downward spiral in the protagonist’s life that seems to have a momentum of its own.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Rosenfeld/Rosenfeld-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 782, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Rosenfeld/Rosenfeld-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Rosenfeld/Rosenfeld-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Novel of Race and Road Rage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unintended Consequences', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Allison, Will', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:21:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lucinda Rosenfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lucinda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/79c8d658-fae5-5544-8985-e03825cd18d5', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79c8d658-fae5-5544-8985-e03825cd18d5'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist draws on interviews to trace the evolution of race relations in the post-civil-rights era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-the-end-of-anger-by-ellis-cose.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist draws on interviews to trace the evolution of race relations in the post-civil-rights era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dramatic stories of hardship and injustice have always been at the center of the struggle for racial equality. Homer Plessy refused to ride in a segregated black train car in late-19th-\xadcentury Louisiana; the World War II veteran George Dorsey and three other African-\xadAmericans were killed by a racist mob outside Atlanta in 1946; Emmett Till was lynched by white bigots in Mississippi in 1955; and in that same year, Rosa Parks sparked the Montgomery bus boycott when she would not surrender her seat to a white passenger. These events and many others inspired activists to press for change and convinced average Americans that change was necessary. The worst abuses of the Jim Crow era have been eliminated, but the moral outrage inspired by a personal encounter with a bigot remains the most powerful vehicle for conveying the injuries and indignities of racial inequality. Accordingly, civil rights lawyers seek out plaintiffs whose grievances reflect larger in\xadjustices, and activists rally around people who symbolize pervasive social problems.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Ford/Ford-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Ford/Ford-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Ford/Ford-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking Stock of Race in Obama’s America', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When We Talk About Race', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cose, Ellis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights and Liberties', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:23:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By RICHARD THOMPSON FORD', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'Thompson', 'lastname': 'FORD', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e12ba6e-fca7-5624-9906-eb408b7c3ae4', 'word_count': 1111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e12ba6e-fca7-5624-9906-eb408b7c3ae4'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of Erich von Manstein, a general who made Hitler’s military dreams a reality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-manstein-by-mungo-melvin.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of Erich von Manstein, a general who made Hitler’s military dreams a reality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In war, the finest generals may win battles, but they are ultimately condemned to defeat if their political masters are fools. One such inferior superior was Hitler, a former corporal who imposed his clumsy strategic visions on the German Army — which obeyed, willingly, to the last man’s last round. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/ROSE/ROSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/ROSE/ROSE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/ROSE/ROSE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Commanding Hitler’s Soldiers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Panzer Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hitler, Adolf', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:23:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By ALEXANDER ROSE', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexander', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ROSE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3523957b-9cb0-5b4c-838a-92923826eb5f', 'word_count': 1120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3523957b-9cb0-5b4c-838a-92923826eb5f'}, {'abstract': 'In a novel and poems, Laura Kasischke considers college ghostlore, mortality and grief through generations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/book-review-the-raising-and-space-in-chains-by-laura-kasischke.html', 'snippet': 'In a novel and poems, Laura Kasischke considers college ghostlore, mortality and grief through generations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If and when you go away to college you can become somebody else; you can kill off your old self and be reborn. So generations of young people have believed. But once they have done it — ditching provincial tastes and hometown boyfriends for a sorority, a fraternity or an undergraduate bohemia — they may trade one conformity for another; they may feel haunted by their former lives. No wonder teenage life, on and off campus, seems to fit stories of vampires, ghosts, the undead. Laura Kasischke is hardly the first to use such figures (as fans of “Buffy the Vampire Slayer” know) but with her new novel, “The Raising,” she pursues them almost perfectly. Almost a supernatural thriller, almost a campus satire and almost but not quite a coming-of-age tale, “The Raising” is also the best of Kasischke’s eight novels, the one with the broadest canvas, the most observation, its large cast arranged with a scary economy of detail.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Subb-BURT/Subb-BURT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Subb-BURT/Subb-BURT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Subb-BURT/Subb-BURT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Terror of Teenage Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Terror of Teenage Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ghosts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kasischke, Laura', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:24:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Burt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/31f93376-6eb7-5a73-a4b1-f95c37767f8a', 'word_count': 1376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31f93376-6eb7-5a73-a4b1-f95c37767f8a'}, {'abstract': 'Novels by Banana Yoshimoto, Marcelo Figueras, Helon Habila and Johanna Skibsrud.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/fiction-chronicle-novels-by-banana-yoshimoto-marcelo-figueras-helon-habila-and-johanna-skibsrud.html', 'snippet': 'Novels by Banana Yoshimoto, Marcelo Figueras, Helon Habila and Johanna Skibsrud.', 'lead_paragraph': ' THE LAKE  By Banana Yoshimoto.Translated by Michael Emmerich.  Melville House, $23.95.  ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Sawhney/Sawhney-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 857, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skibsrud, Johanna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Habila, Helon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:24:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hirsh Sawhney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hirsh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sawhney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ade8d11b-a1ec-517c-953f-4a920bf0410a', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ade8d11b-a1ec-517c-953f-4a920bf0410a'}, {'abstract': 'On one side, we had John McPhee against Nabokov. On the other, Martin Amis against David Foster Wallace.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/essay-a-tournament-of-tennis-writing.html', 'snippet': 'On one side, we had John McPhee against Nabokov. On the other, Martin Amis against David Foster Wallace.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Wow, what a tournament we witnessed this week, Johnny Mac! Before we came back on the air, we were both saying that we’ve been just flat-out blown away by the talent at this year’s Intertemporal Tennis Writers Classic. Some of the greatest writers in history came and competed and showed us what it means to write extraordinarily well about tennis. This tournament attracts the world’s sharpest minds because tennis is a global sport that’s steeped in tradition, and more, it’s a gentlemanly clash of wills where you’re out there alone, and the way you play reveals so much about who you are. Somewhere between chess and boxing lies tennis. So you have to be an elite writer to get through this tournament. That’s why no one was surprised to see Touré get knocked out in the first round. Sure, he’s done some nice stories, like his profile of Jennifer Capriati for Vogue, in which he hits with her. Do we have a clip from that? ‘The whole incident takes on the feel of intense aerobics mixed with a back-alley mugging, and you’re just deflecting and defending and, eventually, after eight or nine minutes, your arm starts to throb and your lungs start to shriek and each new shot racing at you is like a punch to the chest and you feel her inner bully.’ Touré’s a solid player, but in this tournament your forehand is your vocabulary, your backhand is your eye for detail, your ability to turn words into poetry and rhythm is your volley, your use of metaphors and symbolism is your overhead, and your deep understanding of the sport is your all-important first serve. Touré writes passionately and knows tennis, but some whispered that he might have gotten into the tournament only because of affirmative action. Maybe.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/bkr-Toure-t_CA0/bkr-Toure-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 177, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/bkr-Toure-t_CA0/bkr-Toure-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 177}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/bkr-Toure-t_CA0/bkr-Toure-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 302, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/bkr-Toure-t_CA0/bkr-Toure-t_CA0-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/bkr-Toure-t_CA0/bkr-Toure-t_CA0-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tennis by the Book', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tennis by the Book', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nabokov, Vladimir', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, David Foster', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McPhee, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AMIS, MARTIN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:24:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By TOURÉ', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Touré', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dff1ce9f-fb3c-5ad8-9d74-94d575674bdb', 'word_count': 1350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dff1ce9f-fb3c-5ad8-9d74-94d575674bdb'}, {'abstract': 'Mystery novels by Ruth Rendell, Hakan Nesser, Helen Grant and Conor Fitzgerald.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/crime-novels-by-ruth-rendell-hakan-nesser-helen-grant-and-conor-fitzgerald.html', 'snippet': 'Mystery novels by Ruth Rendell, Hakan Nesser, Helen Grant and Conor Fitzgerald.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing brings neighbors together faster than a murder on\xa0\xa0the block. Or so says Ruth Rendell, who subjects the residents of an\xa0\xa0apartment house in Kenilworth (“as dreary as only a London outer suburb\xa0\xa0can be”) to intense psychological scrutiny in her morbidly witty\xa0\xa0suspense novel TIGERLILY’S ORCHIDS (Scribner, $26) before selecting one of them as her murder victim.\xa0', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Crime/Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Death Among Neighbors', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Death Among Neighbors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nesser, Hakan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rendell, Ruth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Helen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:25:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/77f3c6bc-e9a7-511b-8380-3eacf73fe00b', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77f3c6bc-e9a7-511b-8380-3eacf73fe00b'}, {'abstract': '“From the day I gave away my virginity at 17 I started documenting my sexual experiences,” Annie Sprinkle told us via e-mail. “I still am, 40 years later.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/up-front-annie-sprinkle.html', 'snippet': '“From the day I gave away my virginity at 17 I started documenting my sexual experiences,” Annie Sprinkle told us via e-mail. “I still am, 40 years later.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Annie Sprinkle, who in this issue reviews Chester Brown’s “Paying for It: A Comic-Strip Memoir About Being a John,” is no stranger to the documentary urge. “From the day I gave away my virginity at 17 I started documenting my sexual experiences,” Sprinkle told us via e-mail. “I still am, 40 years later.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 568, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 