rticle', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/53ad05ea-2011-5a47-9b2f-17d8972a19bd', 'word_count': 259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53ad05ea-2011-5a47-9b2f-17d8972a19bd'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SWEET THUNDER: The Life and Times of Sugar Ray Robinson,  by Wil Haygood (Lawrence Hill, $18.95.) This ambitious portrait places Ray Robinson as a central figure in the rise of urban African-Americans in the 20th century, tracing Robinson’s life from his birth in Detroit in 1921 as Walker Smith Jr.; to Harlem and the peak of his success as a prizefighter in the 1940s and ’50s; to his humbling final years in Los Angeles before his death in 1989.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:25:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd84cc5a-2cf4-52db-a34d-470d73d15c1a', 'word_count': 518, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd84cc5a-2cf4-52db-a34d-470d73d15c1a'}, {'abstract': 'Janet Evanovich has had seven books on the hardcover fiction lists over the past three years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/10/books/review/bestsellers-weekly-graphic.html', 'snippet': 'Janet Evanovich has had seven books on the hardcover fiction lists over the past three years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Janet Evanovich has had seven books on the hardcover fiction lists over the past three years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Best Sellers Weekly Graphic: 7 Going on 17', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:26:45+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/199fc4c8-3cbb-5e17-b4f1-fd260642e474', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/199fc4c8-3cbb-5e17-b4f1-fd260642e474'}, {'abstract': 'Janet Evanovich’s “Smokin’ Seventeen” jumps past Tom Clancy’s “Against All Enemies” to give Evanovich her 12th straight No. 1 hardcover best seller in her Stephanie Plum series.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Janet Evanovich’s “Smokin’ Seventeen” jumps past Tom Clancy’s “Against All Enemies” to give Evanovich her 12th straight No. 1 hardcover best seller in her Stephanie Plum series.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERSEY GIRL: Janet Evanovich’s “Smokin’ Seventeen” jumps past Tom Clancy’s “Against All Enemies” to give Evanovich her 12th straight No. 1 hardcover best seller in her Stephanie Plum series. Evanovich may have left her hometown, South River, N.J., for the more scenic climes of Naples, Fla., but her endearingly semi-competent bounty hunter heroine is still running around the Garden State chasing bad guys, juggling boyfriends and serving as a one-woman stimulus program for the used-car industry. Evanovich’s Web site includes a memorial page for the ill-fated vehicles that have crossed Stephanie’s path, from the Chevy Nova in “One for the Money” (“Left for dead, which is O.K. since it had already been vandalized and had no muffler, tailpipe or radio”) to the Carpet Car in “Hot Six” (“caught on fire from flaming dog poop in a bag”), the Honda CRV in “Hard Eight” (“firebombed by a rabbit”) and a maroon Econoline van from “Fearless Fourteen,” which “wasn’t really Stephanie’s, but caught her bad car juju anyway, struck by a russet potato and exploded, as predicted by Gary the Stalker.” Normally, Evanovich also holds a summerlong naming contest for next year’s book (“Holy Cannoli Seventeen,” anyone?), but she recently announced her next title early, during an interview on the “Today” show. Yup, you guessed it: “Explosive Eighteen.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/10insidelist/10insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/10insidelist/10insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T16:27:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organizatio