o a Reuters Breakingviews editor, Rob Cox, and a columnist, Antony Currie, about cutbacks in staff and compensation on Wall Street. How does the current round of layoffs and austerity in the financial industry compare with the “Armageddon” at the height of the financial crisis?', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/weathering-wall-streets-job-cuts/', 'snippet': 'DealBook’s Susanne Craig speaks to a Reuters Breakingviews editor, Rob Cox, and a columnist, Antony Currie, about cutbacks in staff and compensation on Wall Street. How does the current round of layoffs and austerity in the financial industry comp...', 'lead_paragraph': 'DealBook’s Susanne Craig speaks to a Reuters Breakingviews editor, Rob Cox, and a columnist, Antony Currie, about cutbacks in staff and compensation on Wall Street. How does the current round of layoffs and austerity in the financial industry compare with the “Armageddon” at the height of the financial crisis?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weathering Wall Street’s Job Cuts', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wall Street (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T17:27:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/454b4ba0-c6d0-56f0-a51e-0280e8d033bd', 'word_count': 49, 'uri': 'nyt://article/454b4ba0-c6d0-56f0-a51e-0280e8d033bd'}, {'abstract': 'A slide show of photographs from the past week in New York City and the region. Subjects include the closing of an elementary school, the same-sex marriage law in New York and a disappearing artist at ground zero.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/07/01/nyregion/20110701WIP.html', 'snippet': 'A slide show of photographs from the past week in New York City and the region. Subjects include the closing of an elementary school, the same-sex marriage law in New York and a disappearing artist at ground zero.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A slide show of photographs from the past week in New York City and the region. Subjects include the closing of an elementary school, the same-sex marriage law in New York and a disappearing artist at ground zero.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/nyregion/20110701WIP-slide-9PEU/20110701WIP-slide-9PEU-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/nyregion/20110701WIP-slide-9PEU/20110701WIP-slide-9PEU-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/01/nyregion/20110701WIP-slide-9PEU/20110701WIP-slide-9PEU-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Week in Pictures for July 1', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T17:27:15+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/d35ce800-59e7-5980-84ec-01a3a3c9d869', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/d35ce800-59e7-5980-84ec-01a3a3c9d869'}, {'abstract': 'At the moment I’m in a village in Burgundy, staying at La Source des Fées, a vineyard owned by Thierry Nouvel and Philippe Greffet. Maybe because of the distance from the men’s shows, it’s easier to see what they lacked.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/where-did-the-fun-go/', 'snippet': 'At the moment I’m in a village in Burgundy, staying at La Source des Fées, a vineyard owned by Thierry Nouvel and Philippe Greffet. Maybe because of the distance from the men’s shows, it’s easier to see what they lacked.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few years ago, just as the recession hit, the Chambre Syndicale, the French fashion authority, decided to separate the June men’s shows and the haute couture by a week. The two groups of shows had followed each other, as they still do in January. A week of free time in Europe is no longer regarded by publications with the joie de vivre of the past, so I usually split the time between work and pleasure. At the moment I’m in a village in Burgundy, staying at La Source des Fées, a vineyard owned by Thierry Nouvel and Philippe Greffet. The place is as lovely as it is laid back, with delicious dinners, and each night brings new guests — Dutch, Swedes, French. It’s the beginning of the European summer. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Did the Fun Go?', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T17:27:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a8f2906-59ac-5b53-9b2d-7d999147fb38', 'word_count': 476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a8f2906-59ac-5b53-9b2d-7d999147fb38'}, {'abstract': 'Harlem Stage will give a new dance work by choreographers Nora Chipaumire and Jawole Willa Jo Zollar its world premiere in October, and the D.J. Rich Medina will transform Harlem Stage Gatehouse into a space to celebrate the life of Fela Kuti with music, dance and conversation on Nov. 19.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/harlem-stage-announces-fall-lineup/', 'snippet': 'Harlem Stage will give a new dance work by choreographers Nora Chipaumire and Jawole Willa Jo Zollar its world premiere in October, and the D.J. Rich Medina will transform Harlem Stage Gatehouse into a space to celebrate the life of Fela Kuti with...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harlem Stage, the multidisciplinary performing arts center supporting artists of color that has made a huge name for itself while managing to weather the recent economic storms, announced on Friday its fall 2011 season of new dance, film, theater and music. Among the highlights are “Visible,” a new work by choreographers Nora Chipaumire and Jawole Willa Jo Zollar, which will have its world premiere Oct. 10-15 at the Harlem Stage Gatehouse. Two new editions of the WeDaPeoplesCabaret will jump-start the Uptown Nights series in September. Carl Hancock Rux is the curator on Sept. 17, with a program featuring Nona Hendryx, the political monologist Reno and the author and producer Nelson George. The Grammy-nominated trumpeter Christian Scott will offer two nights with his quintet on Oct. 28 and Oct. 29. The D.J. Rich Medina will transform the Harlem Stage Gatehouse into a space to celebrate the life of Fela Anikulapo Kuti on Nov. 19 with music, dance and conversation. On Nov. 10, as part of its Harlem Stride – Community Concert series in partnership with the Weill Music Institute of Carnegie Hall, the Grammy nominee Miguel Zenon leads his quintet in a program featuring music by great songwriters from Puerto Rico. Tickets are free. Ticket prices for most Harlem Stage performances are $20 or less; some are free and generally don’t go higher than $45. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Harlem Stage Announces Fall Lineup', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harlem Stage', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'HARLEM (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T17:30:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98a23aa5-213d-59e8-b070-5eddad2794ad', 'word_count': 227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98a23aa5-213d-59e8-b070-5eddad2794ad'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column focuses on summer camps that teach money skills.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/a-camp-option-but-is-it-useful/', 'snippet': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column focuses on summer camps that teach money skills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wealthy families have long realized that their children need to learn about money, Paul Sullivan writes in his Wealth Matters column this week. They will often send their children to retreats like the one run by Citi Private Bank, which focuses on investing, running businesses and giving money to charity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Camp Option, but Is It Useful?', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camps and Camping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T17:32:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bucks Editors', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bucks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Editors', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8aaa0d7-0eec-5153-8333-9a4d5f1a0f0b', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8aaa0d7-0eec-5153-8333-9a4d5f1a0f0b'}, {'abstract': 'Designers have long favored sleek, flush door handles on concept cars. Now they are becoming common on production models.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/07/03/automobiles/03handle-slideshow.html', 'snippet': 'Designers have long favored sleek, flush door handles on concept cars. Now they are becoming common on production models.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Designers have long favored sleek, flush door handles on concept cars. Now they are becoming common on production models.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/automobiles/03handle-slideshow-slide-4IUX/03handle-slideshow-slide-4IUX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/automobiles/03handle-slideshow-slide-4IUX/03handle-slideshow-slide-4IUX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/automobiles/03handle-slideshow-slide-4IUX/03handle-slideshow-slide-4IUX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hidden Entry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T17:35:44+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/9a38abfd-68dc-5cb5-ac97-bcf61c0908da', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/9a38abfd-68dc-5cb5-ac97-bcf61c0908da'}, {'abstract': 'How to look smartly festive for our most patriotic holiday.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/new-americana-dressing-for-the-fourth/', 'snippet': 'How to look smartly festive for our most patriotic holiday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dressing for a holiday often revolves around a slew of clichés that make us look (and feel) cheesy– orange and black, anyone? — as opposed to smartly festive. It certainly doesn’t have to. This fourth of July, opt for the unexpected in some coolly patriotic looks like Lanvin’s lamé shorts rather than classic denim, and pair them with bright, playful Superga sneakers. In the evening, go for relaxed Americana by mixing a Sonia by Sonia Rykiel T-shirt with a ladylike Donna Karan silk skirt. For a patriotic décolletage, wear this red lace bra from Deborah Marquit with a sexy sheer Equipment top. The black and white stripes (as opposed to boring blue and red) are as chic as a Mount Vernon fireworks display. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Americana | Dressing for the Fourth', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T17:45:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily Miesmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miesmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5ef3af6-9d74-5b55-ab34-aa03ecb5db77', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5ef3af6-9d74-5b55-ab34-aa03ecb5db77'}, {'abstract': 'Both the nature of phone calls and who initiates them may affect relationships between parents and teenagers.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/what-cellphone-calls-say-about-parent-teenager-relations/', 'snippet': 'Both the nature of phone calls and who initiates them may affect relationships between parents and teenagers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Poor communication is a common complaint when it comes to parents and teenagers. What happens when you throw a cellphone into the mix?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Cellphone Calls Say About Parent-Teenager Relations', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T17:49:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roni Caryn Rabin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roni', 'middlename': 'Caryn', 'lastname': 'Rabin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d94bd10d-c8c2-56a9-b3e3-158b74d8a5ae', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d94bd10d-c8c2-56a9-b3e3-158b74d8a5ae'}, {'abstract': 'The fire has burned tens of thousands of acres, but officials said they were confident it would not affect the Los Alamos National Laboratory.', 'web_ur