s $4.3 billion in long-term federal financing for treatment and financial compensation for those suffering lingering effects of the terrorist attacks.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/911-health-care-law-takes-effect/', 'snippet': 'The measure provides $4.3 billion in long-term federal financing for treatment and financial compensation for those suffering lingering effects of the terrorist attacks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For almost 10 years, people who say they were sickened by the terrorist attacks of Sept. 11 and their aftermath have relied on a patchwork of short-term federal, city and private money for their health care.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '9/11 Health Care Law Takes Effect', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'James Zadroga 9-11 Health and Compensation Act', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zadroga, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T19:02:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anemona Hartocollis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anemona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartocollis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41983358-f098-578c-9365-da8c21299f1d', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41983358-f098-578c-9365-da8c21299f1d'}, {'abstract': 'A chef who has won recognition in Portland, Ore., has been hired at Compose, which will reopen in September after renovation.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/compose-to-close-then-reopen-in-fall-with-matthew-lightner-as-chef/', 'snippet': 'A chef who has won recognition in Portland, Ore., has been hired at Compose, which will reopen in September after renovation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The departure of Nick Curtin  left Compose in TriBeCa somewhat rudderless last month, but not for long. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Compose to Close, Then Reopen in Fall With Matthew Lightner as Chef', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T19:06:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12f1bcf0-6c70-59f5-995d-6d8004cf770a', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12f1bcf0-6c70-59f5-995d-6d8004cf770a'}, {'abstract': 'A four-bedroom Park Avenue co-op was listed for $24.5 million, and a five-bedroom Time Warner Center condo was listed for $24.95 million.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/big-deal-sold-for-20500000/', 'snippet': 'A four-bedroom Park Avenue co-op was listed for $24.5 million, and a five-bedroom Time Warner Center condo was listed for $24.95 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two apartments closing for $20.5 million, a condominium in the Time Warner Center and a co-op at 640 Park Avenue, tied for the highest sale of the week, according to Streeteasy.com and city property records.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Deal | Sold for $20,500,000', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T19:09:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kershaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5000735f-0368-558e-a8da-5a816a259c35', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5000735f-0368-558e-a8da-5a816a259c35'}, {'abstract': 'For CBS, a lineup of repeats was enough to place first among the TV networks on Thursday night.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/seen-it-before-cbs-wins-the-night-with-repeats/', 'snippet': 'For CBS, a lineup of repeats was enough to place first among the TV networks on Thursday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For CBS, an all-repeat lineup eked out a first-place finish for the network in the ratings on Thursday as “CSI” and “The Mentalist” delivered the night’s largest audiences in prime time. According to Nielsen’s estimates, 7.8 million viewers tuned in for a rerun of “The Mentalist” at 10 p.m.. while “CSI” attracted 6.8 million at 9. “Wipeout” on ABC led the 8 p.m. hour with 6.5 million viewers, outperforming CBS’s combination of “The Big Bang Theory” (6.6 million) and “Rules of Engagement” (4.9 million). ABC earned a second-place finish, drawing 6.1 million at 9 p.m. for “Expedition Impossible” and 5.4 million at 10 for a new installment of “Rookie Blue.” Fox was third, averaging 5 million for “So You Think You Can Dance” at 8 p.m. followed by a rerun of “Glee” (2.9 million) at 9. NBC trailed in fourth with sitcom repeats and a new episode of “Love Bites” (1.8 million) at 10. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seen It Before: CBS Wins the Night With Repeats', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T19:09:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/641b8646-e1c1-526a-85fe-105c986bfcf9', 'word_count': 155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/641b8646-e1c1-526a-85fe-105c986bfcf9'}, {'abstract': 'Letter in response to Christopher Hitchens’s review of David Mamet’s book “The Secret Knowledge.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/mamet-talk.html', 'snippet': 'Letter in response to Christopher Hitchens’s review of David Mamet’s book “The Secret Knowledge.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mamet Talk', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mamet Talk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T19:11:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f45870d0-ee67-5e56-ab7f-adb21c1dd27a', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f45870d0-ee67-5e56-ab7f-adb21c1dd27a'}, {'abstract': 'Letters in response to Emily Bazelon’s review of Lynn Peril’s “Swimming in the Steno Pool.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/ripples-in-the-steno-pool.html', 'snippet': 'Letters in response to Emily Bazelon’s review of Lynn