of.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/how-should-we-cover-africa/', 'snippet': 'In writing about Africa’s conflicts and poverty, do we paint a picture of the continent that is misleading, and may even make it harder for Africa to succeed?', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column is a misnomer: it’s not really a column and it will appear online Friday, rather than Sunday. It’s the cover story for our new Sunday Review section, which used to be the NY Times’s Week in Review section, and it’s going up early on the Web.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Should We Cover Africa?', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T19:35:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43af462e-3cc7-543a-9e04-b69cf44a670e', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43af462e-3cc7-543a-9e04-b69cf44a670e'}, {'abstract': 'A slide show of photographs of cultural events from this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/07/03/arts/design/20110703-WIC-SS.html', 'snippet': 'A slide show of photographs of cultural events from this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A slide show of photographs of cultural events from this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/arts/design/20110703-WIC-SS-slide-3M18/20110703-WIC-SS-slide-3M18-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/arts/design/20110703-WIC-SS-slide-3M18/20110703-WIC-SS-slide-3M18-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/arts/design/20110703-WIC-SS-slide-3M18/20110703-WIC-SS-slide-3M18-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Week in Culture Pictures, July 1', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T19:37:24+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/a62a9db5-d532-548f-8b71-551dfd46bde8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/a62a9db5-d532-548f-8b71-551dfd46bde8'}, {'abstract': 'This is surely an uncomfortable piece for some married couples to read together. As I write in the story, in every marriage there are things best left unsaid.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/extra-savage/', 'snippet': 'This is surely an uncomfortable piece for some married couples to read together. As I write in the story, in every marriage there are things best left unsaid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I discovered Dan Savage, the subject of my cover story this weekend, in 2004, when I became editor of the alternative weekly paper in New Haven. I did not know much about alt weeklies, and to get ideas I looked around for some of the best. Somebody said I had to check out The Stranger, the bigger of Seattle’s two alt weeklies. So I e-mailed the editor, who was this Dan Savage guy. I figured that couldn’t be his real name; it was probably some alt weekly stunt. But “Dan Savage” sent me a bunch of issues, and the paper was brilliant: smart, good-looking, edgy. Inside, the editor wrote a really good sex column called Savage Love, and I\xa0asked my publisher if we could drop our syndicated sex column and replace it with Savage Love. “Can’t,” he said. “Too dirty. People would object.” That was about all I knew of Dan Savage for the next couple years, although occasionally I would see links to Savage’s work on Andrew Sullivan’s blog.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Extra Savage', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Savage, Dan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T19:37:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Oppenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oppenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/352f451d-5ebd-551c-9076-68772d914cdb', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/352f451d-5ebd-551c-9076-68772d914cdb'}, {'abstract': 'A new documentary, “Project Nim,” retells the sad, absurd story of Nim Chimpsky, neither human nor ape.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/movies/the-heartbreaking-story-of-project-nim.html', 'snippet': 'A new documentary, “Project Nim,” retells the sad, absurd story of Nim Chimpsky, neither human nor ape.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TALKING animals are a dime a dozen in animated blockbusters. But what about the real thing? And what, for that matter, would it mean for an animal to talk? What indeed separates an animal from a human? These are a few of the questions raised in a new documentary about the life of a chimpanzee named Nim Chimpsky, the subject of a radical language experiment in the 1970s. “Project Nim,” which opens Friday in New York and Chicago and will be shown next year on HBO, is named after the study, which began in 1973 with an infant Nim living with an Upper West Side family and ended after much trial and tribulation in a Texas animal sanctuary, where he died in 2000.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/arts/NIM-1/NIM-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/03/arts/NIM-1/NIM-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/arts/NIM-1/NIM-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 994, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/arts/NIM-1/NIM-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/arts/NIM-1/NIM-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Experiment That Evolved Into a Tragedy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Experiment That Evolved Into a Tragedy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marsh, James (1963- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monkeys and Apes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sign Language', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T19:37:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicolas Rapold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicolas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rapold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29860f8f-0ab1-5ebb-a225-b602806c1545', 'word_count': 1017, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29860f8f-0ab1-5ebb-a225-b602806c1545'}, {'abstract': 'Melkor, a tree in Redwood National and State Parks in Northern California, has a 70-foot, 4-inch circumference and is 349 feet tall.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/redwood-monster-is-a-co-champ/', 'snippet': 'Melkor, a tree in Redwood National and State Parks in Northern California, has a 70-foot, 4-inch circumference and is 349 feet tall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In theory, the tallest people and buildings and trees stand out. Take a really, really tall tree like a giant redwood. Hard to miss, right?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Redwood Monster Is a Co-Champ', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T19:38:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leslie Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leslie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': Non