to seafood salad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/dining/shrimp-and-cheese-taboo-no-more-a-good-appetite.html', 'snippet': 'Combining seafood and cheese may be a longstanding taboo, but pecorino can add necessary verve to seafood salad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COMBINING seafood and cheese is one of those longstanding taboos that defy explanation. Why not grate a little Parmesan on a plate of pasta with white clam sauce? Because no one in Italy does it, and that is that.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/06/dining/06appe_span/06appe_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/06/dining/06appe_span/06appe_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/06/dining/06appe_span/APPE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/06/dining/06appe_span/06appe_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/06/dining/06appe_span/06appe_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Reason for Cheese on Shrimp (No, Really)', 'kicker': 'A Good Appetite', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Reason For Cheese On Shrimp (No, Really)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seafood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T21:04:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a947d4f5-9a65-5d22-b35a-273e13a84075', 'word_count': 259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a947d4f5-9a65-5d22-b35a-273e13a84075'}, {'abstract': 'In a major study of smokers in which CT scans were found to reduce deaths from lung cancer by 20 percent, nearly 40 percent of patients receiving scans had a false-positive scare.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/health/research/05prognosis.html', 'snippet': 'In a major study of smokers in which CT scans were found to reduce deaths from lung cancer by 20 percent, nearly 40 percent of patients receiving scans had a false-positive scare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Federal health officials announced last fall that smokers screened for cancer by low-dose spiral CT scans were 20 percent less likely to die of lung cancer than those who had chest X-rays. But the study’s final results, published online Wednesday in The New England Journal of Medicine, also describe the downsides of screening, including a high rate of false-positive scares. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prevention: Many False Alarms in Scans That Cut Cancer Deaths', 'kicker': 'Vital Signs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prognosis: Many False Alarms in Lung Cancer Scans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lung Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T21:08:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'o