ents had cruised past the 400 mark. But more striking than the volume of responses was their variety and complexity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Sunday: A Referendum on Infidelity', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T22:21:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abc9a69d-5703-5c26-b386-eded1d36fb14', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abc9a69d-5703-5c26-b386-eded1d36fb14'}, {'abstract': 'No longer under the watch of the Hawk Cam, the fledgling hawk can sometimes be spotted around Washington Square.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/out-of-the-spotlight-pip-isnt-above-a-cameo/', 'snippet': 'No longer under the watch of the Hawk Cam, the fledgling hawk can sometimes be spotted around Washington Square.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pip the red-tailed hawk has kept a relatively low profile since flying the nest last week — certainly when compared with being watched round the clock on the Hawk Cam.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Out of the Spotlight, Pip Isn’t Above a Cameo', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birdwatching', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hawks (Birds)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington Square Park (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T22:23:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily S. Rueb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Rueb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d4c82eb-0706-5aa1-93c7-ebb466e20ef2', 'word_count': 350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d4c82eb-0706-5aa1-93c7-ebb466e20ef2'}, {'abstract': 'David Borden and his Mother Mallard’s Portable Masterpiece Company performed synthesized movements for the Issue Project Room’s Darmstadt Institute: Classic of the Avant-Garde series.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/arts/music/david-borden-at-issue-project-room-review.html', 'snippet': 'David Borden and his Mother Mallard’s Portable Masterpiece Company performed synthesized movements for the Issue Project Room’s Darmstadt Institute: Classic of the Avant-Garde series.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Borden formed his colorfully named ensemble, Mother Mallard’s Portable Masterpiece Company, in 1969, when he was experimenting with early Moog synthesizers and Minimalist compositional notions. Mr. Borden has composed prolifically for the group since then, and on Wednesday evening he brought his players to the Issue Project Room for an installment of the space’s Darmstadt Institute: Classics of the Avant-Garde series. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/ALT-BORDEN/ALT-BORDEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Continuing a Continuing Story by Renewing His Early New Music', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Continuing a Continuing Story by Renewing His Early New Music', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Borden, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T22:24:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c63deca3-b4b8-5e1c-b6a7-8efaf4c357e3', 'word_count': 458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c63deca3-b4b8-5e1c-b6a7-8efaf4c357e3'}, {'abstract': 'Radar L.A., a Los Angeles festival featuring international theater troupes, was organized by the producer of the Under the Radar festival in New York and partners in Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/theater/radar-la-a-theater-festival-in-collaboration.html', 'snippet': 'Radar L.A., a Los Angeles festival featuring international theater troupes, was organized by the producer of the Under the Radar festival in New York and partners in Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — How do you take an acclaimed theater festival in one city and turn it into a national franchise, an endeavor that could easily set off turf warfare among artists? Mark Russell thinks he has an answer.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/franchise/franchise-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Theater Brand Goes West', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York Theater Brand Goes West', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Under the Radar Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T22:24:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2034414-27d3-5c9d-b93e-03a11a029b44', 'word_count': 1031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2034414-27d3-5c9d-b93e-03a11a029b44'}, {'abstract': 'Purify my mind.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/i-forgive-canada/', 'snippet': 'Purify my mind.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I Forgive Canada', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T22:25:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f75d2285-9661-5272-9302-3507b7c5a8b9', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f75d2285-9661-5272-9302-3507b7c5a8b9'}, {'abstract': 'The paperback game — a variation on games with poetry or Bartlett’s Familiar Quotations — lets players create their own opening sentences to genre novels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/books/paperback-game-fun-with-literary-opening-lines.html', 'snippet': 'The paperback game — a variation on games with poetry or Bartlett’s Familiar Quotations — lets players create their own opening sentences to genre novels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The best after-dinner games need no special equipment to play: no dice, cards, tiles or machines that bleat at wrong answers. Classics like charades and the dictionary game — sometimes called Fictionary, and tweaked for the unimaginative under the brand name Balderdash — call at most for paper and pencils. They’re D.I.Y. and lo-fi.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/PAPERBACK/PAPERBACK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/PAPERBACK/PAPERBACK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/PAPERBACK/PAPERBACK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/PAPERBACK/PAPERBACK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/PAPERBACK/PAPERBACK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Scrabble When You Can Play Novelist?', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s Scrabble When You Can Play Novelist?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T22:25:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f05ee4de-97be-5efa-bff2-1075e433a4b2', 'word_count': 1167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f05ee4de-97be-5efa-bff2-1075e433a4b2'}, {'abstract': 'R. Kelly performed a (mostly) brisk survey of his catalog at the Prudential Center in Newark on Thursday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/arts/music/r-kelly-at-the-prudential-center-review.html', 'snippet': 'R. Kelly performed a (mostly) brisk survey of his catalog at the Prudential Center in Newark on Thursday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWARK — R. Kelly isn’t a singer given to grand ideological gesture, but last year the release of his 10th album, “Love Letter,” appeared to be making a statement. For almost two decades he’s been the id of modern R&B, even as, for several years in the 2000s, he operated under the shadow of child pornography charges, of which he was acquitted in 2008.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/RKELLY/RKELLY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/RKELLY/RKELLY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 346}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/RKELLY/RKELLY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/RKELLY/RKELLY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/RKELLY/RKELLY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ever the Charmer, Brazen and Sly, but at a Faster Clip', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ever the Charmer, Brazen and Sly, But at a Faster Clip', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T22:26:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5589bdcc-d35f-5309-9361-3b33ca7e5296', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5589bdcc-d35f-5309-9361-3b33ca7e5296'}, {'abstract': 'It took a frustrated young Washington lobbyist to fix a clothing problem that has bothered professional women for years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/fashion/for-women-a-redesigned-shirt-is-more-modest.html', 'snippet': 'It took a frustrated young Washington lobbyist to fix a clothing problem that has bothered professional women for years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE first lady’s oft-bared arms aside, dress codes for women in Washington’s bread-and-butter jobs (law, Congress, lobbying) have remained steadfastly more prim than in other cities. Remember the Hillary Rodham Clinton cleavage scandal of 2007, when Mrs. Clinton, a senator at the time, was criticized for showing a flash of décolletage on the Senate floor? The offending hint of bust was barely visible; she had merely worn a blouse that dipped lower than her clavicle. It didn’t happen again.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/fashion/NOTICED/NOTICED-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/03/fashion/NOTICED/NOTICED-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/fashion/NOTICED/NOTICED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/fashion/NOTICED/NOTICED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/fashion/NOTICED/NOTICED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Shirt Closes a Gap in Modesty', 'kicker': 'Noticed', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A New Shirt Closes a Gap In Modesty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T22:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Wildman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wildman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71c06118-0379-5922-ab32-2ae5faca4d28', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71c06118-0379-5922-ab32-2ae5faca4d28'}, {'abstract': 'On HLN, the coverage of the Casey Anthony child-murder case seems to never stop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/arts/television/hln-stays-focused-on-casey-anthony-case.html', 'snippet': 'On HLN, the coverage of the Casey Anthony child-murder case seems to never stop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some people will never serve on a jury. But anyone watching HLN’s coverage of the trial of Casey Anthony, the young woman charged with killing her 2-year-old daughter, Caylee, has a visceral sense of what it’s like to be on duty in that courtroom in Orlando, Fla. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/ANTHONY/ANTHONY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 342, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/ANTHONY/ANTHONY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 342}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/ANTHONY/ANTHONY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/ANTHONY/ANTHONY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/arts/ANTHONY/ANTHONY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Pause in Pursuit of Justice (and Ratings)', 'kicker': 'The TV Watch', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Pause in Pursuit of Justice (and Ratings)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anthony, Casey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HLN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-01T22:31:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_d