page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Names of the Dead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Names of the Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:10:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/d228fe87-d1ab-55cb-805d-1fd03db34c03', 'word_count': 46, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d228fe87-d1ab-55cb-805d-1fd03db34c03'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama should mandate, in an executive order, that all federal contractors obey the wage and hour laws already on the books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/opinion/02nittoli.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama should mandate, in an executive order, that all federal contractors obey the wage and hour laws already on the books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DESPITE persistent unemployment and stagnant wages, few believe that our cash-strapped government is likely to simply create better-paying jobs. But there is a way for this country to get more from the millions of jobs we already finance with federal dollars, while reducing the cost of entitlement programs.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pay Workers Fairly and Save Money', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pay Workers Fairly and Save Money', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Procurement', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:14:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janice M. Nittoli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7613406b-7b1c-5065-99f3-1ff8c8ab9fb8', 'word_count': 562, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7613406b-7b1c-5065-99f3-1ff8c8ab9fb8'}, {'abstract': 'Redistricting, campaign finance reform and curbing state pensions are still on the agenda in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/opinion/02sat2.html', 'snippet': 'Redistricting, campaign finance reform and curbing state pensions are still on the agenda in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo showed that it was possible to get New York’s chronically inert Legislature to approve several of his top priorities this year. He made history, getting the new law allowing same-sex marriage passed. He started the clean-up of Albany’s tainted government with an ethics bill that requires more transparency. And he prevailed in getting a more rational tuition plan to help the state’s public universities. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Gov. Cuomo Left Undone', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Gov. Cuomo Left Undone: Redistricting, campaign finance reform and curbing state pensions are still on the agenda', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Redistricting and Reapportionment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:15:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/48d51d4a-595c-5854-927e-943a0410764d', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48d51d4a-595c-5854-927e-943a0410764d'}, {'abstract': 'The military is now leaner and more oriented toward challenges of the 21st century, but change has only started.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/opinion/02sat1.html', 'snippet': 'The military is now leaner and more oriented toward challenges of the 21st century, but change has only started.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Gates was already a 40-year veteran of the national security establishment when President George W. Bush summoned him to assume command of a demoralized and embattled Pentagon in late 2006. He inherited incoherent spending priorities, a broken procurement process and two disastrously mismanaged wars. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pentagon After Mr. Gates', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Pentagon After Mr. Gates: Leaner, more oriented toward challenges of the 21st century, but change has only started', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense Contracts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon V', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Robert M', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:17:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/411728bc-38d2-5906-8c0e-6f5f1e9e5271', 'word_count': 524, 'uri': 'nyt://article/411728bc-38d2-5906-8c0e-6f5f1e9e5271'}, {'abstract': 'In Washington, Metrorail commuters will have to wait longer for repairs to the aging escalators.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/us/02escalators.html', 'snippet': 'In Washington, Metrorail commuters will have to wait longer for repairs to the aging escalators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Late for work, Cassandra Jackson hurried through the Washington subway the other week only to confront a broken escalator that was supposed to carry her to the street. Exactly 351 steps later she made it to the top.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/us/ESCALATOR-3/ESCALATOR-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/us/ESCALATOR-3/ESCALATOR-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/us/ESCALATOR-3/ESCALATOR-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Washington, a Trek to Daylight', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Capital Subways, A Long Life of Climb', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Metropolitan Area Transit Authority', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elevators and Escalators', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:22:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sean Collins Walsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sean', 'middlename': 'Collins', 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd446fcc-1ee0-5178-8636-35fc3abc6cbd', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd446fcc-1ee0-5178-8636-35fc3abc6cbd'}, {'abstract': 'A match featuring two of the most powerful players — Maria Sharapova and Petra Kvitova — is sure to result in both players dictating play during the Wimbledon final on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/sports/tennis/in-match-to-determine-wimbledons-queen-power-is-king.html', 'snippet': 'A match featuring two of the most powerful players — Maria Sharapova and Petra Kvitova — is sure to result in both players dictating play during the Wimbledon final on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — In a match featuring two of the most powerful players in women’s tennis, Maria Sharapova faces Petra Kvitova in the Wimbledon singles final on Saturday. For Sharapova, the match is her first Grand Slam final since winning the Australian Open in 2008. Kvitova reached the semifinals here a year ago, and has steadily improved since, but is making her first appearance in a major final.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/sports/y-women---2/y-women---2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 944, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/sports/y-women---2/y-women---2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/sports/y-women---2/y-women---2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'To Determine Queen, Power Will Be King', 'kicker': 'Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Determine Queen, Power Will Be King', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharapova, Maria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kvitova, Petra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:25:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9ce50b2-8dc2-5416-a50f-61025cecd55e', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9ce50b2-8dc2-5416-a50f-61025cecd55e'}, {'abstract': 'Poland took over the rotating presidency of the European Union, and disapproved of the wave of national interests competing with regional pursuits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/world/europe/02poland.html', 'snippet': 'Poland took over the rotating presidency of the European Union, and disapproved of the wave of national interests competing with regional pursuits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once counted among the least enthusiastic members of the European Union, Poland took over its rotating presidency on Friday with an outspoken attack on the leaders of the bloc’s large countries for pursuing national interests instead of regional concerns. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Nationalism Surges in Europe, Poland Begins Bloc Presidency', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Nationalism Surges in Europe, Poland Begins Bloc Presidency', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tusk, Donald', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Poland', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:29:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Castle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fe066ff-f20d-54d8-8ea9-a02ceb4247c9', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fe066ff-f20d-54d8-8ea9-a02ceb4247c9'}, {'abstract': 'Why the Union Navy placed a high priority on blockading Texas.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/the-war-comes-to-galveston/', 'snippet': 'Why the Union Navy placed a high priority on blockading Texas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Commander James Alden was by all accounts a resourceful and clever naval officer, talents he put to use in June 1861 as he and his ship helped establish the sprawling Union blockade against Confederate ports. Aboard his now-oddly named steamer, the South Carolina, he dropped munitions to federal troops at Fort Pickens, near Pensacola, Fla., then sailed west to join the Gulf Blockading Squadron off Louisiana. On July 2 the ship suddenly appeared off Galveston, one of the busiest ports in the South and a boom town riding a wave of cotton exports to Europe. Riding the low Gulf breakers, the South Carolina, brought to Texas President Lincoln’s Anaconda strategy: strangling the South by sea.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The War Comes to Galveston', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Jefferson F', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Houston, Sam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Naval Academy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Galveston (Tex)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:30:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50e3d906-bf20-5593-b311-ce97b2c2564e', 'word_count': 1298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50e3d906-bf20-5593-b311-ce97b2c2564e'}, {'abstract': 'The Hudson Valley Center for Contemporary Art is Showing 12 newcomers’ works in its “First Look III” show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/nyregion/hudson-valley-center-for-contemporary-art-shows-12-newcomers-work.html', 'snippet': 'The Hudson Valley Center for Contemporary Art is Showing 12 newcomers’ works in its “First Look III” show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAST November, the Hudson Valley Center for Contemporary Art assembled a team of 18 gallery owners, curators, collectors and artists for the purpose of making studio visits — not to already-established artists, but to art students in master of fine arts programs across the United States. The results of their travels to nearly 2,000 studios at more than 30 colleges and universities — and of a subsequent selection process that winnowed candidates they would spotlight to 12 — can be seen in “First Look III,” now on view at the museum.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/nyregion/03ARTWE2/03ARTWE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/03/nyregion/03ARTWE2/03ARTWE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/nyregion/03ARTWE2/03ARTWE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Voices Emerging From College Studios', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Voices Emerging From College Studios', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hudson Valley Center for Contemporary Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:32:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Hodara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24b2b17e-29e1-59c7-954f-cf261526078d', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24b2b17e-29e1-59c7-954f-cf261526078d'}, {'abstract': 'The Hudson Valley Shakespeare Festival’s sole non-Elizabethan offering this summer is a Jules Verne adaptation shot through with silliness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/nyregion/around-the-world-in-80-days-at-boscobel-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Hudson Valley Shakespeare Festival’s sole non-Elizabethan offering this summer is a Jules Verne adaptation shot through with silliness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The story of a stuffy late-19th-century Englishman betting members of his equally stuffy gentleman’s club that he can circumnavigate the globe in 80 days has a certain amount of wackiness built in. Or it did, at least, by the time the Jules Verne novel that tells the tale was adapted into Mike Todd’s all-star 1956 Hollywood movie.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/nyregion/03THEATWE_SPAN/03THEATWE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/0