n on Friday preventing new regulations from taking effect that would have forced the closing of two of the state’s three abortion clinics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/us/02brfs-JUDGEBLOCKSA_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'A federal judge issued a preliminary injunction on Friday preventing new regulations from taking effect that would have forced the closing of two of the state’s three abortion clinics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal judge issued a preliminary injunction on Friday preventing new regulations from taking effect that would have forced the closing of two of the state’s three abortion clinics. The clinics have been unable to meet requirements of the regulations, which were issued this month and were supposed to take effect on Friday. A third clinic, operated by Planned Parenthood, was granted a license this week. In an hourlong hearing in District Court in Kansas City, lawyers for the state argued the regulations represented an appropriate way to protect the safety of patients, while lawyers for the clinics called them unnecessarily onerous. The judge, Carlos Murgia, noted that one provider had turned down two women seeking abortions because of fetal abnormalities as evidence that there would be irreparable harm if the regulations were allowed to go into effect pending the outcome of the case. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kansas: Judge Blocks Abortion Rules', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | PLAINS', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kansas: Judge Blocks Abortion Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:53:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. G. Sulzberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Sulzberger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2638a9e5-79a6-5c13-9339-d07b9c0c72cc', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2638a9e5-79a6-5c13-9339-d07b9c0c72cc'}, {'abstract': 'A guide to cultural and recreational goings-on around New Jersey.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/nyregion/events-in-new-jersey.html', 'snippet': 'A guide to cultural and recreational goings-on around New Jersey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A guide to cultural and recreational goings-on around the state this week. Items may be submitted by mail to New Jersey Calendar, Metropolitan, The New York Times, 620 Eighth Avenue, New York, N.Y. 10018-1405; or by e-mail to njtowns@nytimes.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Events in New Jersey', 'kicker': 'Calendar', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Events in New Jersey', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:53:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e0d18d8-ab3d-5124-b645-5e67019a8593', 'word_count': 3262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e0d18d8-ab3d-5124-b645-5e67019a8593'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Rick Scott on Friday lifted his freeze on the start of construction for a commuter rail system in central Florida.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/us/02brfs-RAILPROJECTW_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Rick Scott on Friday lifted his freeze on the start of construction for a commuter rail system in central Florida.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Rick Scott on Friday lifted his freeze on the start of construction for a commuter rail system in central Florida. The 61-mile, $1.2 billion project has been strongly backed by the business community and Representative John L. Mica, a Republican who is chairman of the House Transportation Committee, but opposed by Tea Party activists and other critics. Mr. Scott had expressed concern because local governments would be asked to cover any revenue shortfalls after seven years of operation. But he said he felt that he had no choice but to approve the deal, as supporters had threatened to sue the state because the Legislature had authorized the spending. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Florida: Rail Project Will Move Forward', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | SOUTH', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Florida: Rail Project Will Move Forward', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mica, John L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:54:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Lipton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lipton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/df7148d5-677f-5085-a826-0620055c1235', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df7148d5-677f-5085-a826-0620055c1235'}, {'abstract': 'The House Ethics Committee confirmed Friday that it is investigating two lawmakers, Representatives Gregory W. Meeks, Democrat of New York, and Jean Schmidt, Republican of Ohio.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/us/02brfs-HOUSEETHICSP_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The House Ethics Committee confirmed Friday that it is investigating two lawmakers, Representatives Gregory W. Meeks, Democrat of New York, and Jean Schmidt, Republican of Ohio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The House Ethics Committee confirmed Friday that it is investigating two lawmakers, Representatives Gregory W. Meeks, Democrat of New York, and Jean Schmidt, Republican of Ohio. The committee said it needed more time for its inquiries, but said that fact does not “itself indicate that any violation has occurred.” Mr. Meeks is under investigation because of a $40,000 loan he received in 2007 and initially did not disclose from a Queens real estate broker who had been repeatedly investigated for fraud and predatory lending. Ms. Schmidt is being investigated on accusations she accepted free legal services from a Turkish-American group. Joe Jansen, an aide to Ms. Schmidt, said she intends to pay back the Turkish-American group, but has not done so at the request of the ethics investigators. The committee said it would decide how to proceed by Aug. 16. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'House Ethics Panel Confirms Two Investigations', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WASHINGTON', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'House Ethics Panel Confirms Two Investigations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meeks, Gregory W', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmidt, Jean', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:56:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Lipton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lipton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/15498de3-2d9b-5cd7-94c4-77de879ec2c7', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15498de3-2d9b-5cd7-94c4-77de879ec2c7'}, {'abstract': 'The city’s efforts to stop using endangered tropical hardwoods as it replaces the Coney Island Boardwalk’s planks raise aesthetic, pragmatic and linguistic issues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/nyregion/fighting-over-rain-forest-ipe-in-coney-island-boardwalk.html', 'snippet': 'The city’s efforts to stop using endangered tropical hardwoods as it replaces the Coney Island Boardwalk’s planks raise aesthetic, pragmatic and linguistic issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Coney Island Boardwalk, opened in 1923, has inspired songs (The Drifters’ “Under the Boardwalk”), plays (Herb Gardner’s “The Goodbye People”) and fiction (Delmore Schwartz’s “In Dreams Begin Responsibilities”). Now, it is inspiring a question of Talmudic complexity: Can a boardwalk be called a boardwalk if it is not made of boards? ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/nyregion/BOARDWALK/BOARDWALK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/02/nyregion/BOARDWALK/BOARDWALK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/nyregion/BOARDWALK/BOARDWALK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/02/nyregion/BOARDWALK/BOARDWALK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/02/nyregion/BOARDWALK/BOARDWALK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fight Over Keeping Boards in the Boardwalk', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Fight Over Keeping Boards in the Boardwalk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CONEY ISLAND (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wood and Wood Products', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concrete', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:56:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Berger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d930e85-b989-5d9f-816f-63cf631c9d57', 'word_count': 1108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d930e85-b989-5d9f-816f-63cf631c9d57'}, {'abstract': 'When Karen Miller learned she had celiac disease, she decided to bake gluten-free goods that would taste “as good as any other.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/nyregion/gluten-free-baking-at-three-dogs.html', 'snippet': 'When Karen Miller learned she had celiac disease, she decided to bake gluten-free goods that would taste “as good as any other.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When she was an undergraduate, Karen Miller took a cake-decorating class with a friend. She liked it and was good at it, but there was one problem: she couldn’t eat anything she made.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sweet, Moist and Gluten-Free', 'kicker': 'Quick Bite | Briarcliff Manor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sweet, Moist and Gluten-Free', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gluten', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:57:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily DeNitto', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeNitto', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f361c2bf-aec8-5c2c-8114-34f0ac829233', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f361c2bf-aec8-5c2c-8114-34f0ac829233'}, {'abstract': 'Rosa Merenda, the chef and co-owner of La Scarbitta, adds warmth and personal enthusiasm to this new restaurant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/nyregion/rosas-la-scarbitta-ristorante-in-mamaroneck-review.html', 'snippet': 'Rosa Merenda, the chef and co-owner of La Scarbitta, adds warmth and personal enthusiasm to this new restaurant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS you enter Rosa’s La Scarbitta Ristorante, a new Italian restaurant in Mamaroneck, you will see a message painted on the wall: “Come in as a stranger, leave as a member of the family.” It would be easy to dismiss it as so much marketing nonsense, but by the time you’ve met Rosa Merenda, the chef and co-owner (and you will), chatted with her husband and partner, Angelo, and eaten their food, you will indeed feel that you’ve just had a sweet familial experience.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/nyregion/03SUBDINEWE1/03SUBDINEWE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 812, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/nyregion/03SUBDINEWE1/03SUBDINEWE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/nyregion/03SUBDINEWE1/03SUBDINEWE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prepare to Be Part of the Chef’s Family', 'kicker': 'Westchester Dining | Mamaroneck', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prepare to Be Part Of the Chef’s Family', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T01:57:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily DeNitto', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeNitto', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/85c23d2d-1185-5278-8478-ceceff2a042b', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85c23d2d-1185-5278-8478-ceceff2a042b'}, {'abstract': 'Colorful dry rosés match barbecued ribs and grilled foods like salmon, tuna steaks, lobster, chicken, hot dogs and shish kebab.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/nyregion/season-of-the-grill-and-roses.html', 'snippet': 'Colorful dry rosés match barbecued ribs and grilled foods like salmon, tuna steaks, lobster, chicken, hot dogs and shish kebab.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like summer flowers, colorful dry rosés — pink, coral, salmon, near-red, almost orange — abound at East End wineries. ', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 