ht seasons with the Phoenix Suns. He led the Knicks to their first winning record (42-40) in 10 years and their first playoff berth in seven. Stoudemire, 29, was limited by a pulled muscle in his back during the first-round series against Boston, and the Knicks were swept. He was in Manhattan for a promotion before the N.B.A. imposed a lockout last week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '30 Seconds With Amar’e Stoudemire', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "Stoudemire, Amar'e", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T15:47:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vincent M. Mallozzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vincent', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44012d82-376f-5416-8603-ff6ad1e78252', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44012d82-376f-5416-8603-ff6ad1e78252'}, {'abstract': 'The 1999 N.B.A. labor deal imposed salary caps and a luxury tax, but team owners say those controls no longer deter profligate spending, and so they are seeking new limits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/sports/basketball/nba-owners-threaten-lockout-to-revamp-labor-deal.html', 'snippet': 'The 1999 N.B.A. labor deal imposed salary caps and a luxury tax, but team owners say those controls no longer deter profligate spending, and so they are seeking new limits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Labor disputes in the N.B.A. have a way of producing strange and unintended consequences: $30 million contracts for backup centers, trades of retired players and David Stern’s infamous beard, to name a few.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For N.B.A. Owners, 1999 Labor Deal Was Win With Regrets', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For N.B.A., 1999 Agreement Was a Win With Regrets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stern, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T15:49:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Beck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b1368e5-7bc6-51c8-aa74-bc78adb0b490', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b1368e5-7bc6-51c8-aa74-bc78adb0b490'}, {'abstract': 'An outdoorsy Acrostic from Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/outdoorsy/', 'snippet': 'An outdoorsy Acrostic from Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SECOND SUNDAY PUZZLE — I like to enjoy a good meal outdoors, and I wish my kids felt the same way. They definitely fall into the “indoors eating” category, even at restaurants with outdoor cafes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Variety: An Outdoorsy Acrostic', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-02T16:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52ced3f7-ae50-568d-a967-1de8562fb499', 'word_count': 187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52ced3f7-ae50-568d-a967-1de8562fb499'}, {'abstract': 'King Abdullah II is trying to placate a public that is growing increasingly angry over allegations of corruption in the government.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/world/middleeast/03jordan.html', 'snippet': 'King Abdullah II is trying to placate a public that is growing increasingly angry over allegations of corruption in the government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — With antigovernment demonstrations growing across Jordan in recent weeks, King Abdullah II approved a cabinet shuffle on Saturday that brought in a number of new officials, notably the interior minister, but the public’s anger over accusations of corruption seemed unlikely to subside.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/world/sub-03jordan/sub-03jordan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/03/world/sub-03jordan/sub-03jordan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/world/sub-03jordan/sub-03jordan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'ca