re granted in perfunctory hearings. Later, Mr. Poordavoud returned to court on separate criminal charges after a violent episode involving a gun.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Court Hearings in Poordavoud and Anthony Cases', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Second Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T01:23:30+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/7c25345a-4287-53e1-b687-5b35ff3e1da3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/7c25345a-4287-53e1-b687-5b35ff3e1da3'}, {'abstract': 'Across the country, states are increasingly allowing people who lost their firearm rights because of mental health issues to appeal to have them restored.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/us/03guns.html', 'snippet': 'Across the country, states are increasingly allowing people who lost their firearm rights because of mental health issues to appeal to have them restored.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PULASKI, Va. — In May 2009, Sam French hit bottom, once again. A relative found him face down in his carport “talking gibberish,” according to court records. He later told medical personnel that he had been conversing with a bear in his backyard and hearing voices. His family figured he had gone off his medication for bipolar disorder, and a judge ordered him involuntarily committed — the fourth time in five years he had been hospitalized by court order.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/us/GUN-10/GUN-10-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/us/GUN-10/GUN-10-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/us/GUN-10/GUN-10-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Some With Histories of Mental Illness Petition to Get Their Gun Rights Back', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mixing Guns and Mental Illness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Rifle Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cho, Seung-Hui', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T01:47:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Luo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Luo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d710748f-25f2-583c-872c-242a81ca55ac', 'word_count': 4794, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d710748f-25f2-583c-872c-242a81ca55ac'}, {'abstract': 'Sweden and the United States both won Saturday and advanced to the quarterfinals of the Women’s World Cup.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/saturday-at-the-womens-world-cup/', 'snippet': 'Sweden and the United States both won Saturday and advanced to the quarterfinals of the Women’s World Cup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sweden once again struggled to score but secured its second straight 1-0 victory on Saturday, beating North Korea in Augsburg, Germany. Midfielder Lisa Dahlkvist scored in the 64th minute. The United States followed with a 3-0 win against Colombia in Sinsheim, sending both the Americans and the Swedes to the quarterfinals. It could have been worse for the Colombians: the Americans missed at least a half-dozen other good scoring chances, and had a 27-12 advantage in shots.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saturday at the Women’s World Cup', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T01:49:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/626e18f1-e8a8-5089-a086-da11674bde64', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/626e18f1-e8a8-5089-a086-da11674bde64'}, {'abstract': 'America’s debt ceiling and Congress’ failed budget talks continue to dominate the Sunday talk shows.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/02/sunday-breakfast-menu-july-3/', 'snippet': 'America’s debt ceiling and Congress’ failed budget talks continue to dominate the Sunday talk shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'America’s debt ceiling and the failed budget talks in Congress will again dominate the Sunday talk shows this week. Guests will debate the nation’s spending issues and entitlement programs before analyzing the war in Afghanistan and the transforming effects of immigration.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday Breakfast Menu, July 3', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T02:06:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f354072a-93e6-54aa-ace3-3d785141e8b4', 'word_count': 284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f354072a-93e6-54aa-ace3-3d785141e8b4'}, {'abstract': 'Lottery numbers for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/nyregion/lottery-numbers-for-new-york-new-jersey-and-connecticut.html', 'snippet': 'Lottery numbers for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'July 2, 2011', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lottery Numbers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lottery Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lotteries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T02:27:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/50c9de27-7df2-5e99-b1c8-8d2c7f67c48f', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50c9de27-7df2-5e99-b1c8-8d2c7f67c48f'}, {'abstract': 'None of the Yankees had faced the Mets rookie Dillon Gee, but a third time through the order was all they needed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/sports/baseball/yankees-get-used-to-mets-gee-then-rough-him-up.html', 'snippet': 'None of the Yankees had faced the Mets rookie Dillon Gee, but a third time through the order was all they needed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the Yankees’ hitters, it seems, unfamiliarity breeds contempt, or at least confusion. Facing pitchers they have never seen before has long been a formula for frustration for the Yankees, although they recently have shown an ability to adapt more quickly.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yanks Get Used to Gee and Then Get to Him', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yanks Get Used to Gee And Then Get to Him', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gee, Dillon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T02:43:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76b49865-df93-5ef7-a5c0-c2b92d4deb0a', 'word_count': 715, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76b49865-df93-5ef7-a5c0-c2b92d4deb0a'}, {'abstract': 'Two of the nation’s biggest lenders are quietly modifying loans for tens of thousands of borrowers who have not asked for help but whom the banks deem to be at special risk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/business/03loans.html', 'snippet': 'Two of the nation’s biggest lenders are quietly modifying loans for tens of thousands of borrowers who have not asked for help but whom the banks deem to be at special risk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As millions of Americans struggle in foreclosure with little hope of relief, big banks are going to borrowers who are not even in default and cutting their debt or easing the mortgage terms, sometimes with no questions asked. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/us/JP-LOANS/JP-LOANS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/us/JP-LOANS/JP-LOANS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/us/JP-LOANS/JP-LOANS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Banks Easing Terms on Loans Deemed as Risks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Big Banks Easing Terms on Loans Deemed as Risks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T02:52:34+0000', 'document_type': 'articl