ive and the health of the auto industry over the next decade and beyond.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/us/4mileage-1/4mileage-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 798, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/us/4mileage-1/4mileage-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/04/us/4mileage-1/4mileage-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Carmakers and White House Haggling Over Mileage Rules', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama Seeking A Steep Increase In Auto Mileage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fuel Efficiency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:09:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Energy & Environment ', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7ed593c-0fab-56a4-bb59-441ca408fdc8', 'word_count': 1142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7ed593c-0fab-56a4-bb59-441ca408fdc8'}, {'abstract': 'Paramount’s overseas division has decided the Captain America name has too much brand value — even in tricky spots like France — to keep it off cinema marquees.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/soft-pedal-captain-america-overseas-hollywood-says-no/', 'snippet': 'Paramount’s overseas division has decided the Captain America name has too much brand value — even in tricky spots like France — to keep it off cinema marquees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Last week, Marvel Studios started to roll out ads for its newest movie star. He’s big and brawny! He’s star-spangled! He’s Captain America, marching into multiplexes everywhere!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Soft-Pedal Captain America Overseas? Hollywood Says No', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marvel Studios', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paramount Pictures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:11:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a31797d-19d0-5529-8b90-4632174a2597', 'word_count': 567, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a31797d-19d0-5529-8b90-4632174a2597'}, {'abstract': 'In “Mama, I Want to Sing: The Next Generation” a gospel musical is given a fresh look by a new generation of performers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/theater/reviews/mama-i-want-to-sing-at-the-dempsey-theater.html', 'snippet': 'In “Mama, I Want to Sing: The Next Generation” a gospel musical is given a fresh look by a new generation of performers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For much of the 1980s and beyond “Mama, I Want to Sing” was a fixture among Harlem audiences, logging more than 2,500 performances. This mom-and-pop gospel musical also yielded a handful of sequels, hybrids and other spinoffs.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting the Story of a Dutiful Daughter With a Hit Song', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Mama, I Want to Sing: The Next Generation'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Revisiting the Story of a Dutiful Daughter With a Hit Song', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gospel Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:11:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Grode', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grode', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/00926ca1-d0b0-520c-a18a-9b20c8e0026f', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00926ca1-d0b0-520c-a18a-9b20c8e0026f'}, {'abstract': 'Senators John Cornyn and John McCain said they were open to tax reform — but not tax increases — as part of a deal over the debt ceiling.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/us/politics/04budget.html', 'snippet': 'Senators John Cornyn and John McCain said they were open to tax reform — but not tax increases — as part of a deal over the debt ceiling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Two senior Republicans said Sunday that they might be open to raising new government revenue as part of a deal to resolve the dispute over the federal debt ceiling, but they warned that there was little time to enact a comprehensive deal. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/us/politics/mccain-cornyn/mccain-cornyn-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/us/politics/mccain-cornyn/mccain-cornyn-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/us/politics/mccain-cornyn/mccain-cornyn-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '2 Republicans Open Door to Increases in Revenue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2 Republicans Open Door To Increases In Revenue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cornyn, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCain, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:13:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ede20a8-2811-59b4-86f4-66385d1d63f5', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ede20a8-2811-59b4-86f4-66385d1d63f5'}, {'abstract': 'The Irish novelist Benjamin Black (a pseudonym for John Banville) and the British mystery writer Ruth Rendell have new books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/books/mysteries-by-benjamin-black-and-ruth-rendell-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Irish novelist Benjamin Black (a pseudonym for John Banville) and the British mystery writer Ruth Rendell have new books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Both Benjamin Black and Ruth Rendell have written inviting new novels filled with nicely malevolent characters. It hardly seems sporting to relegate these books to the murder-story genre just because some of those characters wind up dead of unnatural causes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/arts/Book-1-1309706460826/Book-1-1309706460826-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Refreshing Splashes of Summertime Homicide', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Refreshing Splashes Of Summertime Homicide', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rendell, Ruth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Banville, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:22:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/22aa45d6-181c-5d65-83b5-eae5bb6c97d0', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22aa45d6-181c-5d65-83b5-eae5bb6c97d0'}, {'abstract': 'In the days before July Fourth, “Transformers: Dark of the Moon” benefited from 3-D abroad; “Larry Crowne” bombed at home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/movies/holiday-box-office-booms-overseas-sputters-domestically.html', 'snippet': 'In the days before July Fourth, “Transformers: Dark of the Moon” benefited from 3-D abroad; “Larry Crowne” bombed at home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Hollywood’s Fourth of July fireworks were overseas. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/arts/box/box-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/04/arts/box/box-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/arts/box/box-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Holiday Box Office Sputters Domestically but Explodes Overseas', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Holiday Box Office Sputters Domestically but Explodes Overseas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:24:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd4269d0-9014-5604-ae21-0738d944310c', 'word_count': 821, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd4269d0-9014-5604-ae21-0738d944310c'}, {'abstract': '“Portraits in Dramatic Time” is a new video installation that used ultra-high-speed, high-definition cameras to record several well-known theater and film performers in a scene.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/arts/design/extreme-slow-motion-portraits-at-lincoln-center.html', 'snippet': '“Portraits in Dramatic Time” is a new video installation that used ultra-high-speed, high-definition cameras to record several well-known theater and film performers in a scene.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Alan Rickman, Patti LuPone and William H. Macy tend to move pretty quickly between jobs in Hollywood and on Broadway. But the photographer and visual artist David Michalek has managed to slow them down for the next month, using extreme-slow-motion video portraiture to project their images onto the David H. Koch Theater in Lincoln Center Plaza. Beginning Tuesday Mr. Michalek will present “Portraits in Dramatic Time,” a new video installation that used ultra-high-speed, high-definition cameras to record several well-known theater and film performers in a scene. Then he slows the frames to display each emotion in larger-than-life detail as it is projected onto a screen that’s 85 feet wide and 45 feet high. Like “Slow Dancing” — his first installation of this nature, which featured dancers performing ballet, folk dance and even voguing, krumping and break-dancing in slow motion— “Portraits” represents Broadway, avant garde theater and Chinese opera, as well as several other theatrical genres, enhanced with costumes, production design, makeup and lighting. The installation will be on view at Lincoln Center through July 31.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Extreme-Slow-Motion Portraits at Lincoln Center', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Extreme-Slow-Motion Portraits at Lincoln Center', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:28:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/79ee90ed-9ec1-596c-8064-73f45398855d', 'word_count': 178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79ee90ed-9ec1-596c-8064-73f45398855d'}, {'abstract': 'The 25th anniversary of the London premiere of “Phantom of the Opera” will be celebrated with a special production at the Royal Albert Hall in London in October, Playbill.com reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/theater/anniversary-planned-for-phantom.html', 'snippet': 'The 25th anniversary of the London premiere of “Phantom of the Opera” will be celebrated with a special production at the Royal Albert Hall in London in October, Playbill.com reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 25th anniversary of the London premiere of “Phantom of the Opera,” the Andrew Lloyd Webber musical about a masked man who haunts a young soprano in a Paris Opera house, will be celebrated with a special production at the Royal Albert Hall in London in October, Playbill.com reported. As the Phantom and Christine, Ramin Karimloo and Sierra Boggess, above, with Mr. Lloyd Webber, left, will lead a cast of 200 people in limited performances on Oct. 1 and 2. Mr. Karimloo and Ms. Boggess, who is currently starring in the Broadway revival of Terrence McNally’s “Master Class,” originated the same roles in the “Phantom” sequel, “Love Never Dies,” in the West End of London in March 2010. The original “Phantom,” which opened there on Oct. 9, 1986, with staging by Hal Prince and Gillian Lynne, is still running. The anniversary show will be directed by Laurence Connor, who has served as associate director of the London production. Ms. Lynne will return to provide musical staging and choreography. Matt Kinley will design the set, with Maria Björnson’s original design as inspiration. “Though this special staging will naturally be drawn from the brilliant original, it will be both a spectacular and unique production,” Cameron Mackintosh, who is producing, said in a statement.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Anniversary Planned for ‘Phantom’', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Anniversary Planned For ‘Phantom’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lloyd Webber, Andrew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:29:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b17528c1-bf46-550f-816a-19596f310911', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b17528c1-bf46-550f-816a-19596f310911'}, {'abstract': 'Ireland comes to Midtown Manhattan in two eccentric new shows opening 59E59 Theater’s fall season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/theater/clowns-and-comedy-open-59e59-season.html', 'snippet': 'Ireland comes to Midtown Manhattan in two eccentric new shows opening 59E59 Theater’s fall season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ireland comes to Midtown Manhattan in two eccentric new shows opening 59E59 Theater’s fall season. In September Cirque de Légume, a clown duo from Dublin, will use vegetables and physical comedy to create what the company calls “pure mayhem.” The Inis Nua Theater Company of Philadelphia will mix commedia dell’arte, Japanese Kabuki theater and mime to present “Dublin by Lamplight,” a story of poetry and politics at the National Theater of Ireland in 1904. In October a large church in a small town will become the center of controversy in “The Bus” by James Lantz, followed by “Any Given Monday,” an irreverent comedy about a man trying to save his marriage through terrible means, by Bruce Graham, a former writer for the television show “Roseanne.” Last — but certainly not the least draw for 59E59 audiences — the popular “Brits Off Broadway” returns to the theater for its eighth New York program. Before the season begins, Throughline Artists, a company that promotes communication between established and emerging playwrights, presents “Summer Shorts 5.” This festival of short plays brings new works by Alexander Dinelaris (“Still Life”), Christopher Durang (“Miss Witherspoon”) and Neil LaBute (“reasons to be pretty”), among several other playwrights, beginning Aug. 4 and running through Sept. 3.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Clowns and Comedy Open 59E59 Season', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Clowns and Comedy Open 59E59 Season', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clowns', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7762ff50-42fe-531d-b85e-cec56388cfa2', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7762ff50-42fe-531d-b85e-cec56388cfa2'}, {'abstract': 'The rock band Radiohead has made a move to join the world of social networking in China, The Associated Press reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/arts/music/radiohead-in-china.html', 'snippet': 'The rock band Radiohead has made a move to join the world of social networking in China, The Associated Press reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The rock band Radiohead has made a move to join the world of social networking in China, The Associated Press reported. The group has been critical of the Chinese government’s censorship in the past, performing at Free Tibet concerts in December and urging fans through its Web site to campaign for the release of Liu Xiaobo, who won last year’s Nobel Peace Prize. But the band has started a page on the “weibo” site of Sina.com, which has been described as the Chinese equivalent of Twitter. The group may not be able to keep up its activism through the site, since Internet content is screened by the government, but so far it has posted only one comment: “testing the weibo.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Radiohead in China', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Radiohead in China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Radiohead', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:31:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d303b257-4adc-5bee-a414-6d6e306b83f5', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d303b257-4adc-5bee-a414-6d6e306b83f5'}, {'abstract': 'The Barnes Foundation said last week that it had passed its $200 million fund-raising goal to continue construction on its new home in Philadelphia, The Philadelphia Inquirer reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/arts/design/barnes-foundation-meets-200-million-goal.html', 'snippet': 'The Barnes Foundation said last week that it had passed its $200 million fund-raising goal to continue construction on its new home in Philadelphia, The Philadelphia Inquirer reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite continued litigation to prevent the move of its paintings and artifacts, the Barnes Foundation said last week that it had passed its $200 million fund-raising goal to continue construction on its new home in Philadelphia, The Philadelphia Inquirer reported. Of that money $40 million has come in the form of private donations in the past year; $150 million will go toward construction of the Philadelphia building and associated costs; and $50 million will go to the foundation’s endowment. Proponents of the move have claimed that the current location in Merion, Pa., has hurt attendance and membership. The collection was formerly prohibited from being moved according to a trust indenture created by the founder, Albert C. Barnes, but the indenture was overturned in 2006 by the Montgomery County Orphans’ Court. The Merion gallery closed on Sunday. Opponents of the move have a court hearing set for Aug. 1 to prevent it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Barnes Foundation Meets $200 Million Goal', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Barnes Foundation Meets $200 Million Goal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barnes Foundation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philadelphia (Pa)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:32:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/41214009-693e-5d67-b8c8-672e5d836858', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41214009-693e-5d67-b8c8-672e5d836858'}, {'abstract': 'Mourners honored the 40th anniversary of the death of Jim Morrison; television audiences will get a preview of “The X Factor” before the 2011 Major League Baseball All-Star Game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/arts/footnotes.html', 'snippet': 'Mourners honored the 40th anniversary of the death of Jim Morrison; television audiences will get a preview of “The X Factor” before the 2011 Major League Baseball All-Star Game.', 'lead_paragraph': '   Ray Manzarek and Robby Krieger, former members of the Doors, joined numerous other mourners, above, at the grave of Jim Morrison in Paris on Sunday, The Associated Press reported. They were there to honor the 40th anniversary of the death of Morrison, the Doors’ lead singer, who died in July 1971. ... Television audiences will get a preview of Simon Cowell’s “X Factor” just before the 2011 Major League Baseball All-Star Game on July 12 on Fox, TV Guide reported. The network has not released the details of the preview.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnotes', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnotes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:34:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4310dc50-b601-5846-ae62-7a373a0ce5b1', 'word_count': 92, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4310dc50-b601-5846-ae62-7a373a0ce5b1'}, {'abstract': 'Last month AARP introduced a free Internet radio service, with 18 channels programmed by the Concord Music Group, which releases music by baby boomer stars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/business/media/04aarp.html', 'snippet': 'Last month AARP introduced a free Internet radio service, with 18 channels programmed by the Concord Music Group, which releases music by baby boomer stars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With constantly changing technology, and blogs that cover the latest indie bands minute-by-minute, online music is usually considered a young person’s game. But now the AARP, one of the biggest symbols of life in the gray years, is betting that a custom digital player on its Web site will rekindle its members’ love for discovering new music.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'AARP Begins an Internet Radio Service, Mixing Familiar Hits With New Artists', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'AARP Begins an Internet Radio Service, Mixing Familiar Hits With New Artists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AARP', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Concord Music Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T21:40:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c5ce0b8-0b5f-5c08-8ddb-3caa9cc31f62', 'word_count': 690, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c5ce0b8-0b5f-5c08-8ddb-3caa9cc31f62'}, {'abstract': 'The sense of guilt and betrayal surviving soldiers feel originates not just in what they’ve done, but in who they are.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/war-and-the-moral-logic-of-survivor-guilt/', 'snippet': 'The sense of guilt and betrayal surviving soldiers feel originates not just in what they’ve done, but in who they are.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If there is one thing we have learned from returning war veterans — especially those of the last decade — it’s that the emotional reality of the soldier at home is often at odds with that of the civilian public they left behind. \xa0And while friends and families of returning service members may be experiencing gratefulness or relief this holiday, many of those they’ve welcomed home are likely struggling with other emotions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Moral Logic of Survivor Guilt', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T22:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Sherman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sherman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a6274dd-5740-52b3-8d29-c5cb2c367ceb', 'word_count': 1925, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a6274dd-5740-52b3-8d29-c5cb2c367ceb'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Johnson makes his debut. Get the picture?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/greek/', 'snippet': 'Paul Johnson makes his debut. Get the picture?', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — If you are a movie lover like I am, this will be a fun puzzle for you, even though it’s not the Independence Day theme you might have been expecting. It’s nothing brain-breaking either, but then again it’s Monday, and we’re just easing our way back into the groove.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Next-to-Last Greek Letter', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T22:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4dd2f23-7884-516a-91ae-c459c443b6fe', 'word_count': 596, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4dd2f23-7884-516a-91ae-c459c443b6fe'}, {'abstract': 'The two-time champion was beaten in four sets by someone who took his playbook and executed it better.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/sports/tennis/wimbledon-nadals-best-isnt-enough-against-djokovic.html', 'snippet': 'The two-time champion was beaten in four sets by someone who took his playbook and executed it better.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — By the final points of his fifth Wimbledon final, Rafael Nadal had the same hollow-eyed expression he usually sees in his opponents. His last stroke Sunday, a backhand that sailed long, ended his 20-match winning streak at the All England Club and his mastery in the majors over Novak Djokovic, who cemented his No. 1 ranking with a 6-4, 6-1, 1-6, 6-3 victory. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/sports/YTENNIS/YTENNIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/sports/YTENNIS/YTENNIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/04/sports/YTENNIS/YTENNIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nadal Finds Out Just How His Opponents Have Felt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nadal Finds Out Just How His Opponents Have Felt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Djokovic, Novak', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T22:03:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/067633fd-a7e7-5d2b-92c6-9dd046c7e672', 'word_count': 752, 'uri': 'nyt://article/067633fd-a7e7-5d2b-92c6-9dd046c7e672'}, {'abstract': 'For a coming book, two professors asked their peers to submit essays that explore the parallels between world history and Star Wars history.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/studying-how-star-wars-mined-history/', 'snippet': 'For a coming book, two professors asked their peers to submit essays that explore the parallels between world history and Star Wars history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The events in “Star Wars,” the opening credits reveal, happened “a long time ago in a galaxy far, far away.” But did similar events happen before?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Studying How ‘Star Wars’ Mined History', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Star Wars (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lucas, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T22:15:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1c7df2b-2375-5e95-9079-a9071b0309d9', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1c7df2b-2375-5e95-9079-a9071b0309d9'}, {'abstract': 'The decades-long relationship between NBC and Wimbledon ended Sunday when a