 the Oshogbo School, he had a consciously naïve style in which he depicted village scenes, animals and deities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/arts/design/prince-twins-seven-seven-nigerian-artist-dies-at-67.html', 'snippet': 'A leading representative of the Oshogbo School, he had a consciously naïve style in which he depicted village scenes, animals and deities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prince Twins Seven-Seven, a prominent Nigerian artist and leading representative of the Oshogbo School, whose brightly colored, intricately patterned paintings evoked the world of Yoruba folklore and religion, died on June 16 in Ibadan, Nigeria. He was 67. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/sports/04twins-1/04twins-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 707, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/sports/04twins-1/04twins-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/05/sports/04twins-1/04twins-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prince Twins Seven-Seven, Nigerian Artist, Dies at 67', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prince Twins Seven-Seven, 67, An Artist of Nigerian Folklore', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nigeria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T22:43:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2b4ed25-f5d2-5809-a0d8-f8e9aa43fa30', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2b4ed25-f5d2-5809-a0d8-f8e9aa43fa30'}, {'abstract': 'While donations to a legal defense fund have slowed, the fund matters more for what it has come to represent: tangible evidence of reader loyalty to a newspaper.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/readers-rally-around-washington-city-paper/', 'snippet': 'While donations to a legal defense fund have slowed, the fund matters more for what it has come to represent: tangible evidence of reader loyalty to a newspaper.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Daniel Snyder, the owner of the Washington Redskins, filed a defamation lawsuit against the Washington City Paper in February, readers rushed to show their support — and the newspaper gave them an outlet. The City Paper set up a fund for reader donations meant to offset the litigation fees, collecting 600 donations totaling $28,000 in the first three weeks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Readers Rally Around Washington City Paper', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libel and Slander', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snyder, Daniel M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington City Paper', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Redskins', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T22:53:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72a9c89f-2d80-5e2e-adf6-7b759e1997ff', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72a9c89f-2d80-5e2e-adf6-7b759e1997ff'}, {'abstract': 'who should play Fanny? Readers offered wide-ranging suggestions, from theater insiders like Lea Michele, Idina Menzel and Sutton Foster to outside-the-box choices like Lady Gaga, Sarah Silverman and Fran Drescher.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/curtain-down-heads-up-readers-cast-funny-girl/', 'snippet': 'who should play Fanny? Readers offered wide-ranging suggestions, from theater insiders like Lea Michele, Idina Menzel and Sutton Foster to outside-the-box choices like Lady Gaga, Sarah Silverman and Fran Drescher.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Marilyn Michaels, a singer and comedian, wrote a Theater Talkback post last week with suggestions for the people who will be casting the demanding role of Fanny Brice in the coming revival of “Funny Girl.” Ms. Michaels, who played Fanny in the national touring production after Barbra Streisand originated the role on Broadway in 1964, wrote that the character demands not only a great voice but also a solid understanding of a certain kind of humor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Curtain Down, Heads Up: Readers Cast ‘Funny Girl’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brice, Fanny', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lady Gaga', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Michele, Lea', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T23:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d7b3746-67d1-574d-a15d-9b23cd2aadbd', 'word_count': 701, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d7b3746-67d1-574d-a15d-9b23cd2aadbd'}, {'abstract': 'The fees that cable and satellite operators pay local stations have produced big revenues, and some disputes. Now, networks want a share of these fees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/business/media/04retrans.html', 'snippet': 'The fees that cable and satellite operators pay local stations have produced big revenues, and some disputes. Now, networks want a share of these fees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two weeks ago, KSFX, a Fox television station in the Ozarks, told its viewers that the Fox part was going away in the fall. The station said it would still show all of its local newscasts, but the station’s fans on Facebook had other concerns. What about “American Idol”? they asked. What about “Bones”?', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/business/04jpretrans/04jpretrans-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/business/04jpretrans/04jpretrans-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/04/business/04jpretrans/04jpretrans-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Networks Want Slices of a New Pie', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Networks Want Slices Of a New Pie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T23:19:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0846f93-b6e5-52bb-aca1-71ce61a60d64', 'word_count': 969, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0846f93-b6e5-52bb-aca1-71ce61a60d64'}, {'abstract': 'Federal agencies have little incentive to go after fines because they don’t actually keep the money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/business/04views.html', 'snippet': 'Federal agencies have little incentive to go after fines because they don’t actually keep the money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Uncle Sam could use better debt collectors. Fraudsters, polluters and other corporate and white-collar miscreants owe government agencies more than $65 billion in fines. Only pennies on the dollar are ever paid. With private companies making millions buying claims from creditors, maybe they and the feds should do business.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debt Collections Elude Uncle Sam', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Debt Collections Elude Uncle Sam', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Municipal Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T23:37:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Reynolds Holding and Agnes T. Crane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Reynolds', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holding', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Agnes', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Crane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffea8a22-edde-50e5-a622-cb587fa3d142', 'word_count': 700, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffea8a22-edde-50e5-a622-cb587fa3d142'}, {'abstract': 'In February, Mayor Bloomberg placed Jamaica High on a list of schools it planned to close. But it is puzzling how a school can be labeled failing and yet produce successful students.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/nyregion/a-failing-school-not-to-these-students-at-jamaica-high.html', 'snippet': 'In February, Mayor Bloomberg placed Jamaica High on a list of schools it planned to close. But it is puzzling how a school can be labeled failing and yet produce successful students.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everyone knows Jamaica High is a bad school. The past two years, it has received D’s on its report card from the city and been labeled persistently dangerous by the state. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/nyregion/oneducation3/oneducation3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/04/nyregion/oneducation3/oneducation3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/nyregion/oneducation3/oneducation3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/nyregion/oneducation3/oneducation3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/04/nyregion/oneducation3/oneducation3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Failing School? Not to These Students', 'kicker': 'On Education', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Failing School? Not to These Students', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduation Rates', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T23:39:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Winerip', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Winerip', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb08fc57-d935-564a-9ec9-beac9c9a374d', 'word_count': 1120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb08fc57-d935-564a-9ec9-beac9c9a374d'}, {'abstract': 'One expert said the case involving the former head of the International Monetary Fund exposed the “punish first, figure out what happened later” state of American justice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/nyregion/soon-after-strauss-kahn-arrest-now-shaky-case-seemed-solid.html', 'snippet': 'One expert said the case involving the former head of the International Monetary Fund exposed the “punish first, figure out what happened later” state of American justice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A hotel housekeeper whose account of being sexually attacked was so compelling that it brought tears to the eyes of seasoned investigators. Preliminary forensic and electronic evidence, as well as interviews with witnesses, that poked no immediate holes in her story. And an accused man, Dominique Strauss-Kahn, with a ticket for a flight to France, approaching boarding time at Kennedy International Airport.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/nyregion/police/police-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Now-Shaky Case Against Strauss-Kahn Once Seemed So Solid', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Now-Shaky Case Against Strauss-Kahn Once Seemed So Solid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vance, Cyrus R Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-03T23:45:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1455a773-d4fc-5ce4-8224-7794b2152b9b', 'word_count': 1245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1455a773-d4fc-5ce4-8224-7794b2152b9b'}, {'abstract': 'Technology that allows doors to be unlocked remotely is moving to the smartphone, causing some analysts to say the key will soon become obsolete.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/technology/04key.html', 'snippet': 'Technology that allows doors to be unlocked remotely is moving to the smartphone, causing some analysts to say the key will soon become obsolete.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — Front pockets and purses are slowly being emptied of one of civilization’s most basic and enduring tools: the key. It’s being swallowed by the cellphone. \xa0', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/business/04jpkey/04jpkey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/04/business/04jpkey/04jpkey-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 346}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/business/04jpkey/04jpkey-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/04/business/04jpkey/04jpkey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/04/business/04jpkey/04jpkey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tools of Entry, No Need for a Key Chain', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Need For a Key Chain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sma