er, N.H., home of Michelle and Paul Chretien on Sunday night. “Our goal is to win you over,” he said. “I know it’s one voter at a time, and each vote, I know it’s 11 handshakes.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Caucus Click: Huntsman in New Hampshire', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-04T01:46:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca22bb02-c309-582b-afb7-50402befcc13', 'word_count': 51, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca22bb02-c309-582b-afb7-50402befcc13'}, {'abstract': 'How gay marriages will start to redefine the institution, for everyone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/04/opinion/04douthat.html', 'snippet': 'How gay marriages will start to redefine the institution, for everyone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 44 states, the future of gay marriage still depends on legislatures, governors and voters — and eventually, perhaps, Supreme Court Justice Anthony Kennedy. But in New York, as in five states before it, gay marriage’s future is in the hands of gay couples themselves.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-jumbo.png', 'height': 