mbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/world/05cairo-inline1/05cairo-inline1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/05/world/05cairo-inline1/05cairo-inline1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Violence Erupts as Police Accused of Egypt Killings Get Bail', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Violence Erupts as Police Accused of Egypt Killings Get Bail', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cairo (Egypt)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T00:21:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liam Stack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57869922-69e9-5f7e-9cfc-278a57bb0232', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57869922-69e9-5f7e-9cfc-278a57bb0232'}, {'abstract': 'The “Making of the President” documentaries, which are being released on DVD, capture the broad scope of the books by Theodore H. White.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/arts/television/making-of-the-president-documentaries-are-on-dvd.html', 'snippet': 'The “Making of the President” documentaries, which are being released on DVD, capture the broad scope of the books by Theodore H. White.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few students of popular culture, amateur or professional, consider the 1960s a period when creativity flourished on American television. Westerns of the kind that did not threaten to obscure the legacy of John Ford prevailed, speaking to the pastoral fantasies of millions of Americans who had vacated decaying cities for the tempered pleasures of suburban life. The same demographic realities could be held accountable for the absurdist escapism of something like “Green Acres.” Between 1950 and 1960, the United States population swelled by about 28 million; two-thirds of that growth occurred in suburbia. Television had come to occupy a place in 44 million homes. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/arts/White/White-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 892, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/arts/White/White-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/05/arts/White/White-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How We Picked Our Presidents, 1960s Style', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How We Picked Our Presidents, 1960s Style', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Theodore H', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T00:23:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62c3205e-8776-5394-9707-1c9497382b96', 'word_count': 1019, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62c3205e-8776-5394-9707-1c9497382b96'}, {'abstract': '“Swan Lake” is more popular than ever. Still, the versions by New York City Ballet and American Ballet Theater leave room for improvement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/arts/dance/swan-lake-one-classic-ballet-many-interpretations.html', 'snippet': '“Swan Lake” is more popular than ever. Still, the versions by New York City Ballet and American Ballet Theater leave room for improvement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We dance, of course, for joy. But ballet, with its built-in dichotomy of gender roles (women alone rise on point, men alone may do the partnering), has a romantic tension that can seem poignant, even tragic. The full-length “Swan Lake” alternates between scenes of celebration (Acts I and III) and romantic tragedy (Acts II and IV), and its classicism can express the tragic dimension in ways that are the very stuff that ballet is made of and yet are singular to this ballet. It’s not just romantic and noble, it’s fateful and laden with doom. And the storm Tchaikovsky composed in Act IV becomes as cosmic as the one in Shakespeare’s “King Lear.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/arts/sub-Swan/sub-Swan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 544, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/arts/sub-Swan/sub-Swan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/05/arts/sub-Swan/sub-Swan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One Classic Ballet, Many Interpretations', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Classic Ballet, Many Interpretations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKenzie, Kevin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mearns, Sara', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sadler's Wells", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martins, Peter', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T00:25:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cecfbb2-4429-55a6-8565-a8ac6470e5dc', 'word_count': 1127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cecfbb2-4429-55a6-8565-a8ac6470e5dc'}, {'abstract': 'Residents and business owners complain that unlicensed, unregulated car dealers clog traffic and hog parking.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/us/05autos.html', 'snippet': 'Residents and business owners complain that unlicensed, unregulated car dealers clog traffic and hog parking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WALNUT PARK, Calif. — They show up bright and early on Saturday mornings, parking cars and trucks along the side of Pacific Boulevard and polishing them inside and out. Once they have chalked prices onto the windshields (an old Honda Civic for $1,900) they are open for business: a pop-up used car lot on the side of the road. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/us/AUTOS-3/AUTOS-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/05/us/AUTOS-3/AUTOS-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/us/AUTOS-3/AUTOS-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/us/AUTOS-3/AUTOS-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/05/us/AUTOS-3/AUTOS-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Complaints Lead to Ban on Pop-Up Car Markets', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Complaints Lead to Ban On Pop-Up Car Markets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T00:27:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Lovett', 'person': [{'fi