humbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who’s on the Family Tree? Now It’s Complicated', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who’s on the Family Tree? Now It’s Complicated', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surrogate Motherhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genealogy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T00:36:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura M. Holson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a8c961c-2452-596b-ab61-f3a323122594', 'word_count': 1114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a8c961c-2452-596b-ab61-f3a323122594'}, {'abstract': 'The widening use of new techniques to extract oil from shale is expected to help produce petroleum in other countries, as well.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/business/global/05shale.html', 'snippet': 'The widening use of new techniques to extract oil from shale is expected to help produce petroleum in other countries, as well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just east of Argentina’s Andean foothills, an oil field called the Vaca Muerta — “dead cow” in English — has finally come to life. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/business/SHALE/SHALE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 723, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/business/SHALE/SHALE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/05/business/SHALE/SHALE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Argentina Hopes for a Big Payoff in Its Shale Oil Field Discovery', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Argentina Hopes for a Big Payoff in Its Shale Oil Field Discovery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drilling and Boring', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shale', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T00:37:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clifford Krauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clifford', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28d29817-8334-5d59-a99f-0ef777991a65', 'word_count': 1161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28d29817-8334-5d59-a99f-0ef777991a65'}, {'abstract': 'The case of a possibly fake art treasure has led to a $2 million lawsuit. Peter Paul Biro, a forensic art expert from Montreal, filed a complaint last Wednesday against David Grann.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/arts/design/forensic-analyst-sues-over-new-yorker-article.html', 'snippet': 'The case of a possibly fake art treasure has led to a $2 million lawsuit. Peter Paul Biro, a forensic art expert from Montreal, filed a complaint last Wednesday against David Grann.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The case of a possibly fake art treasure has led to a $2 million lawsuit. Peter Paul Biro, a forensic art expert from Montreal, filed a complaint last Wednesday against David Grann, a writer for The New Yorker, and against Advance Publications, which owns Condé Nast and The New Yorker, over a July 2010 New Yorker article that Mr. Grann wrote about the difficulties of authenticating art. In the article, Mr. Grann examined Mr. Biro’s methods — unearthing fingerprints on paintings and matching them to artists — and found some inconsistencies in his story. In the suit, filed in Manhattan, Mr. Biro contends that the reporting is “a false and defamatory screed” against him, “written and published with malice and an indifference to the standards of responsible journalism,” and that it has caused damage to his reputation, his business and his health. Mr. Biro also sued Dan Rattiner of Dan’s Papers, a Hamptons weekly newspaper, for his follow-up article. David Remnick, the editor of The New Yorker, told AdWeek: “David Grann’s reporting on this story and everything else he does is painstaking in both its attention to the facts and tone. We stand with David Grann and behind the story and believe the suit has no merit.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Forensic Analyst Sues Over New Yorker Article', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Forensic Analyst Sues Over New Yorker Article', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T00:38:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/13ffa02e-1b7f-54d9-9fa9-57b3b8c33572', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13ffa02e-1b7f-54d9-9fa9-57b3b8c33572'}, {'abstract': 'An African bank has announced an endowment for the Tate Modern museum in London.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/arts/design/african-art-endowment.html', 'snippet': 'An African bank has announced an endowment for the Tate Modern museum in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An African bank has announced an endowment for the Tate Modern museum in London to promote African art. The bank, Guaranty Trust Bank, based in Lagos, Nigeria, will support the creation of an African art curator, an acquisition fund for African art, and an exchange project between Africa and London for collectors, institutions and young artists. The sum of the endowment was not announced. “This is the beginning of being able to give African art the focus it deserves with audiences around the world,” Chris Dercon, the director of the Tate, said in a statement.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'African Art Endowment', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'African Art Endowment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T00:39:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e802d324-5a71-5f1f-ab59-df834d861c1d', 'word_count': 97, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e802d324-5a71-5f1f-ab59-df834d861c1d'}, {'abstract': 'Chord Overstreet, who plays the guitar-strumming Sam Evans, will not be returning to “Glee.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/arts/television/a-glee-regular-hangs-up-his-guitar.html', 'snippet': 'Chord Overstreet, who plays the guitar-strumming Sam Evans, will not be returning to “Glee.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even singing football heroes can’t linger in high school forever. Chord Overstreet, who plays the guitar-strumming Sam Evans, will not be returning to “Glee” as a series regular, according to TV Line. The show’s creator, Ryan Murphy, has made some casting changes for the coming fall season; he has also promoted Darren Criss, who plays Blaine, an openly gay student, and Harry Shum Jr., as the dancer Mike Chang, to series regulars from guest stars. On Friday Mr. Overstreet, finishing up a “Glee” stage tour, seemed to make reference to the news on his Twitter feed: “well its been a good yr,” he wrote, “too bad its over, time for summer and starting fresh.” He also posted about preparing to record an album. But Gleeks need not despair; Mr. Overstreet may still appear on “Glee” as a guest star, according to people close to the production, and he is expected to be seen in “Glee Live! 3D!,” a movie about the tour, to open in August. Three seasons in, Mr. Murphy doesn’t intend his characters to be in perpetual high school, he recently told Ryan Seacrest in a radio interview. “The show to me is about the way you express yourself as you start to move out into the world as a fresh young person,” Mr. Murphy later told TV Line. “Do we really want to have an eight-year senior? The only way somebody could stay on the show is if they flunk, and that is always a possibility. I think Brittany is pulling straight Fs.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A ‘Glee’ Regular Hangs Up His Guitar', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A ‘Glee’ Regular Hangs Up His Guitar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': []