_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86fb73f5-e0b7-5754-9784-d43a853f31c1', 'word_count': 4194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86fb73f5-e0b7-5754-9784-d43a853f31c1'}, {'abstract': 'The city manager of Wilmington, N.C., proposed a 2012 budget that left a gap of about $7 million between anticipated revenue and spending. To balance the budget, the city must make some difficult choices. Today, you’re in charge. Review the options below and close the gap, keeping in mind that spending less now might mean spending more later, and that your decisions will affect your political popularity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/05/us/wilmington-budget.html', 'snippet': 'The city manager of Wilmington, N.C., proposed a 2012 budget that left a gap of about $7 million between anticipated revenue and spending. To balance the budget, the city must make some difficult choices. Today, you’re in charge. Review the option...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The city manager of Wilmington, N.C., proposed a 2012 budget that left a gap of about $7 million between anticipated revenue and spending. To balance the budget, the city must make some difficult choices. Today, you’re in charge. Review the options below and close the gap, keeping in mind that spending less now might mean spending more later, and that your decisions will affect your political popularity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiscal Puzzle: Balancing Wilmington’s Budget', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wilmington (NC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Local Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T02:47:11+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/fd5b474f-a1cb-5763-9748-82a069fc7f31', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/fd5b474f-a1cb-5763-9748-82a069fc7f31'}, {'abstract': 'Greek commandos boarded and turned back a Canadian boat bound for the Gaza Strip on Monday, in the latest setback for the all-but-stymied Gaza flotilla.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/world/europe/05briefs-Greece.html', 'snippet': 'Greek commandos boarded and turned back a Canadian boat bound for the Gaza Strip on Monday, in the latest setback for the all-but-stymied Gaza flotilla.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Greek commandos boarded and turned back a Canadian boat bound for the Gaza Strip on Monday, in the latest setback for the all-but-stymied Gaza flotilla. The Hellenic Coast Guard stopped the boat about 20 minutes after it set sail from the island of Crete, organizers said, and impounded it at a military dock. Aboard were 43 activists from Canada, Australia, Belgium, Denmark, Germany and Turkey, said Sandra Ruch, a coordinator for the boat. The flotilla had planned to try to breach Israel’s naval blockade of Gaza. Greek authorities turned back an American boat on Friday that had set sail from a port near Athens. The government had prohibited all vessels from departing Greek ports for the “maritime area of Gaza” in order to ensure the safety of passengers. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greece: Flotilla Boat Is Impounded', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Greece: Flotilla Boat Is Impounded', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boats and Boating', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T02:53:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Sayare', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sayare', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fae8bfe-0b78-5ef6-89d6-be762f6157cf', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fae8bfe-0b78-5ef6-89d6-be762f6157cf'}, {'abstract': 'The Vatican on Monday condemned the most recent ordination of a bishop in China without papal consent, saying that Pope Benedict XVI was “deeply saddened” by the illegitimate election.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/world/europe/05briefs-Vatican.html', 'snippet': 'The Vatican on Monday condemned the most recent ordination of a bishop in China without papal consent, saying that Pope Benedict XVI was “deeply saddened” by the illegitimate election.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Vatican on Monday condemned the most recent ordination of a bishop in China without papal consent, saying that Pope Benedict XVI was “deeply saddened” by the illegitimate election that damages the unity of the church. The Vatican said that Paul Lei Shiyin, ordained last week by China’s state-controlled Catholic Church as the bishop of Leshan, was not recognized as a bishop in the Roman Catholic Church and risked automatic excommunication. China’s church ordained another bishop in November, a point of deep contention between Rome and Beijing. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vatican: Ordination of Bishop by China is Condemned', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vatican: Ordination of Bishop By China is Condemned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benedict XVI', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T03:02:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gaia Pianigiani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gaia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pianigiani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a812e31a-1cc2-56b5-9780-9ec521ba4810', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a812e31a-1cc2-56b5-9780-9ec521ba4810'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Defense has identified 4,460 American service members who have died since the start of the Iraq war and 1,636 who have died as a part of the Afghan war and related operations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/us/05list.html', 'snippet': 'The Department of Defense has identified 4,460 American service members who have died since the start of the Iraq war and 1,636 who have died as a part of the Afghan war and related operations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Department of Defense has identified 4,460 American service members who have died since the start of the Iraq war and 1,636 who have died as a part of the Afghan war and related operations. It confirmed the deaths of the following Americans recently: ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Names of the Dead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Names of the Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T03:06:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bf4425c-a2db-58fe-861e-f58a45f904f7', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bf4425c-a2db-58fe-861e-f58a45f904f7'}, {'abstract': 'The brother of an unarmed teenager whose killing by paramilitary troops in Karachi last month was caught on video testified on Monday in the trial of the soldiers accused of the killing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/world/asia/05briefs-Pakistan.html', 'snippet': 'The brother of an unarmed teenager whose killing by paramilitary troops in Karachi last month was caught on video testified on Monday in the trial of the soldiers accused of the killing.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The brother of an unarmed teenager whose killing by paramilitary troops in Karachi last month was caught on video testified on Monday in the trial of the soldiers accused of the killing. The video, broadcast on national television, showed paramilitary troops stopping Sarfaraz Shah, 19; accusing him of involvement in a robbery; and fatally shooting him as he begged for mercy. “Sarfaraz’s only crime was that he was strolling in a public park because there was no electricity in our house,” his brother, Salik Shah, said after the hearing. The Sindh Rangers paramilitary forces “have a license to kill in this city,” he said. “The brutality has to stop.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan: Victim’s Brother Testifies in Case of Killing Caught on Video', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan: Victim&#8217;s Brother TestifiesIn Case of Killing Caught on Video', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recordings and Downloads (Video)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T03:08:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sahar Habib Ghazi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sahar', 'middlename': 'Habib', 'lastname': 'Ghazi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/40775388-c62c-5f66-ac66-1a463a9edb0b', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40775388-c62c-5f66-ac66-1a463a9edb0b'}, {'abstract': 'New York City’s public housing towers are the shame of the city, ungainly and forgotten stepchildren.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/nyregion/nyc-housing-authority-neglects-mold.html', 'snippet': 'New York City’s public housing towers are the shame of the city, ungainly and forgotten stepchildren.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sarita Latchman, a vibrant 42-year-old mother and former parks worker, has a sound like a baby’s rattle at the back of her throat. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Home Is Where the Mold Is', 'kicker': 'Gotham', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Home Is Where The Mold Is', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public and Subsidized Housing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mold', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing Authority (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T03:12:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Powell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Powell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/016d9264-f9fc-5a48-adee-c6a97e9eccad', 'word_count': 797, 'uri': 'nyt://article/016d9264-f9fc-5a48-adee-c6a97e9eccad'}, {'abstract': 'The controversy over a gun-trafficking investigation has renewed questions about whether the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives should exist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/us/politics/05guns.html', 'snippet': 'The controversy over a gun-trafficking investigation has renewed questions about whether the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives should exist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The last time the federal Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives was caught up in scandal, its director faced accusations of lavish spending on his new office. Before he resigned, Congress, flexing its oversight muscles, decided the Senate would confirm all future A.T.F. chiefs. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/us/GUNS-1/GUNS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/05/us/GUNS-1/GUNS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/us/GUNS-1/G