paragraph': 'Sad news arrived over the holiday weekend. Ray Deter, a longtime owner of d.b.a., a groundbreaking New York beer bar, died Sunday after being critically injured in a bicycle accident on June 27. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering Ray Deter of d.b.a.', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T14:37:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f35b052d-2036-5f51-bbe7-8cbf43b00ed4', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f35b052d-2036-5f51-bbe7-8cbf43b00ed4'}, {'abstract': 'Independent estimates of the N.B.A. financial condition reflect a league that has grown at a somewhat tepid rate compared to other sports, and which has an uneven distribution of revenues between teams — but which is fundamentally a healthy and profitable business.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/calling-foul-on-n-b-a-s-claims-of-financial-distress/', 'snippet': 'Independent estimates of the N.B.A. financial condition reflect a league that has grown at a somewhat tepid rate compared to other sports, and which has an uneven distribution of revenues between teams — but which is fundamentally a healthy and pr...', 'lead_paragraph': 'At midnight on Thursday, the N.B.A. locked out its players in what could be the start of a long labor dispute. Some observers, like ESPN’s Michael Wilbon, believe the entire 2011-12 season could be threatened.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Calling Foul on N.B.A.’s Claims of Financial Distress', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T14:45:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/310c2e15-078f-5772-80de-7cc81f3df5ec', 'word_count': 2096, 'uri': 'nyt://article/310c2e15-078f-5772-80de-7cc81f3df5ec'}, {'abstract': 'AfterDeadline Commas are small things, easily overlooked in writing, editing and proofing. But nothing makes an otherwise well-wrought sentence look amateurish as quickly as a comma that appears where it has no business, or the lack of one where it’s needed.', 'web_url': 'https://topics.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/commas-sure-throw-a-few-in/', 'snippet': 'AfterDeadline Commas are small things, easily overlooked in writing, editing and proofing. But nothing makes an otherwise well-wrought sentence look amateurish as quickly as a comma that appears where it has no business, or the lack of one where i...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Notes from the newsroom on grammar, usage and style. (Some frequently asked questions are here.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Commas? Sure, Throw a Few In', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T14:51:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'r