nnual pilgrimage to Les Recontres d’Arles in France this week, we are proud to say that The New York Times Magazine Photographs exhibition is one of the year’s big draws. Our photo director, Kathy Ryan, co-curated the show with Lesley A. Martin, the publisher of Aperture Books.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Our Opening in Arles', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APERTURE FOUNDATION', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARLES (FRANCE)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T21:13:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stacey Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76492ed6-0361-5460-943f-6a51f9a1bd0f', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76492ed6-0361-5460-943f-6a51f9a1bd0f'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama invited Democratic and Republican Congressional leaders to meet with him in the White House on Thursday, saying that “greater progress is in sight” on a deal to raise the nation’s debt limit and reduce the deficit.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/obama-seeks-to-reopen-budget-talks/', 'snippet': 'President Obama invited Democratic and Republican Congressional leaders to meet with him in the White House on Thursday, saying that “greater progress is in sight” on a deal to raise the nation’s debt limit and reduce the deficit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama on Tuesday invited Democratic and Republican Congressional leaders to meet with him in the White House on Thursday, saying that “greater progress is in sight” on a deal to raise the nation’s debt limit and reduce the deficit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Seeks to Reopen Budget Talks', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T21:13:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e547970-acf7-5dc0-902e-b19defc37454', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e547970-acf7-5dc0-902e-b19defc37454'}, {'abstract': 'To compete with the BMW M School and Mercedes-Benz AMG Driving Academy, owners of Jaguar’s R-badged cars receive entry to a closed-course track session.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/for-jaguar-drivers-a-letter-brings-its-privileges/', 'snippet': 'To compete with the BMW M School and Mercedes-Benz AMG Driving Academy, owners of Jaguar’s R-badged cars receive entry to a closed-course track session.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I think back to driving school, I cannot stave off the memory of fighting sleep at a cramped desk while being lectured about illegal U-turns and the perils of driving at speed. But at the Jaguar R Performance Academy, which I attended late last month at Monticello Motor Club northwest of New York City, I was wide awake to the instructors’ message about respecting speed as well as, more thrillingly, how to handle it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Jaguar Drivers, a Letter Brings Its Privileges', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jaguar Cars Ltd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T21:20:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Schembari', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schembari', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36a1e3c9-9bb9-5a41-9e34-95c084cd68ac', 'word_count': 605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36a1e3c9-9bb9-5a41-9e34-95c084cd68ac'}, {'abstract': '“Rapt,” a French thriller opening Wednesday at Film Forum, is about the shockwaves that result from the kidnapping of an industrialist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/movies/rapt-lucas-belvauxs-french-thriller-review.html', 'snippet': '“Rapt,” a French thriller opening Wednesday at Film Forum, is about the shockwaves that result from the kidnapping of an industrialist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Worlds collide in “Rapt,” a solid yet fleet French thriller about a society kidnapping and its shockwaves. You see one world in the gangbuster opening that, with a racing camera, follows a sleek, unidentified man as he hurtles through a day in a matter of moments, rushing out of his home, whooshing into an office, bolting from a meeting and all but flinging himself into the arms of one woman before embracing another. This frenetic activity soon jolts to a halt, though, when his chauffeured car is jacked and he’s snatched by armed kidnappers who turn him into a victim even as the outside world transforms him into the accused.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/RAPT/RAPT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 691, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/RAPT/RAPT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/RAPT/RAPT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Humbling of a Powerful Frenchman', 'kicker': "Movie Review | ‘Rapt'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Humbling of a Powerful Frenchman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Attal, Yvan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Belvaux, Lucas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T21:25:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6819b30-8168-53e2-8af6-f43c431384dc', 'word_count': 838, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6819b30-8168-53e2-8af6-f43c431384dc'}, {'abstract': 'Designed by Zaha Hadid, the new Guangzhou Opera House is gorgeous to look at. It is also a magnificent example of how a single building can redeem a moribund urban environment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/arts/design/guangzhou-opera-house-designed-by-zaha-hadid-review.html', 'snippet': 'Designed by Zaha Hadid, the new Guangzhou Opera House is gorgeous to look at. It is also a magnificent example of how a single building can redeem a moribund urban environment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GUANGZHOU, China — It says something about the state of architecture today that the most alluring opera house built anywhere in the world in decades is in a generic new business district at the outer edge of this city, has no resident company and a second-rate program.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/Hadid/Hadid-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/Hadid/Hadid-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 346}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/Hadid/Hadid-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 607, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/Hadid/Hadid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/Hadid/Hadid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese Gem That Elevates Its Setting', 'kicker': 'Architecture Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chinese Gem That Elevates Its Setting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guangzhou (China)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hadid, Zaha', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T21:34:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicolai Ouroussoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicolai', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ouroussoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0658d712-c1cd-5903-b7a9-c4a36f9ba33a', 'word_count': 1130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0658d712-c1cd-5903-b7a9-c4a36f9ba33a'}, {'abstract': 'The city’s bar scene is thriving, thanks to bartenders like Newton, inspired by old standards and a desire to keep New Orleans in its rightful place as a major American cocktail destination.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/drink-up-new-cocktails-in-nawlins/', 'snippet': 'The city’s bar scene is thriving, thanks to bartenders like Newton, inspired by old standards and a desire to keep New Orleans in its rightful place as a major American cocktail destination.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I came here with AmeriCorps after the storm and never really left.” That’s the story of T. Cole Newton, a post-Katrina New\xa0Orleans\xa0transplant and the owner of 12 Mile Limit, a new bar in the Mid-City neighborhood that doubles as Newton’s own private cocktail laboratory. “This place has been a bar for 90 years,” he says, “and after the storm it was under two feet of water.” The people who used to own the place, he added, “threw a lot of wild parties here.” Cocktails are the specialty now, immensely creative drinks that celebrate the bartending history of\xa0New\xa0Orleans\xa0while embracing a\xa0new\xa0influx of ideas and characters. “Everyone becomes a\xa0New\xa0Orleanian, if you give them long enough,” Newton says. “Miss your flight, you’ll be here five years”.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Drink Up | New Cocktails in N’awlins', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T21:35:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jordan Michelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jordan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Michelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b89ec9f5-4fae-55f7-81d7-65e64c7f441e', 'word_count': 1058, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b89ec9f5-4fae-55f7-81d7-65e64c7f441e'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the Guangzhou Opera House, designed by Zaha Hadid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/07/06/arts/design/hadid.html', 'snippet': 'A look at the Guangzhou Opera House, designed by Zaha Hadid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A look at the Guangzhou Opera House, designed by Zaha Hadid.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/design/hadid-slide-2AIT/hadid-slide-2AIT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 440, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/design/hadid-slide-2AIT/hadid-slide-2AIT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/06/arts/design/hadid-slide-2AIT/hadid-slide-2AIT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Building’s Fluid Forms', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hadid, Zaha', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T21:37:32+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/0de5279e-0124-5c76-9ebc-e762e820b021', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/0de5279e-0124-5c76-9ebc-e762e820b021'}, {'abstract': "American intelligence reports say Pakistan’s spy agency ordered a journalist's death; Casey Anthony’s trial was to Twitter as O.J. Simpson’s was to cable news; and a British tabloid’s phone hacking.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/multimedia/100000000895261/timescast--july-5-2011.html', 'snippet': "American intelligence reports say Pakistan’s spy agency ordered a journalist's death; Casey Anthony’s trial was to Twitter as O.J. Simpson’s was to cable news; and a British tabloid’s phone hacking.", 'lead_paragraph': "American intelligence reports say Pakistan’s spy agency ordered a journalist's death; Casey Anthony’s trial was to Twitter as O.J. Simpson’s was to cable news; and a British tabloid’s phone hacking.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/multimedia/video-tc-110705/video-tc-110705-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/05/multimedia/video-tc-110705/video-tc-110705-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/multimedia/video-tc-110705/video-tc-110705-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/05/multimedia/video-tc-110705/video-tc-110705-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'TimesCast | July 5, 2011', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T21:39:58+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'ContinuousNews', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Zena Barakat|Ben Solomon|Emily B. Hager|Ted Trautman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zena', 'middlename': 'Barakat', 'lastname': 'Ben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/b6c2817a-3b2f-5df5-9b98-3379057c1681', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/b6c2817a-3b2f-5df5-9b98-3379057c1681'}, {'abstract': 'NBC topped the ratings on Monday, a night of mostly repeats and holiday programming.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/arts/television/fireworks-lift-nbc.html', 'snippet': 'NBC topped the ratings on Monday, a night of mostly repeats and holiday programming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NBC topped the ratings on Monday, a night of mostly repeats and holiday programming, as the annual “Macy’s Fourth of July Fireworks Spectacular” attracted its most viewers in 11 years. According to Nielsen’s estimates 8.6 million people tuned in to the NBC broadcast at 9 p.m., more than for any other program on the night and up from 6.9 million viewers a year ago. CBS ranked second for the night, leading the 10 p.m. hour with its own Fourth of July special, “Boston Pops Fireworks Spectacular,” which drew 6.6 million viewers, outperforming a repeat of NBC’s special in the hour (5.3 million) and a repeat of ABC’s “Extreme Makeover: Weight Loss Edition” (3 million). ABC, in third place, averaged 3.1 million viewers from 8 to 10 for a rerun of “The Bachelorette” while Fox was fourth with repeats of “MasterChef” (2.3 million) and “House” (1.5 million).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fireworks Lift NBC', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fireworks Lift NBC', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T21:41:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fe303ca-b1ad-5a54-838f-0c57754f0dbf', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fe303ca-b1ad-5a54-838f-0c57754f0dbf'}, {'abstract': 'Verizon will switch to a tiered-pricing plan for mobile data on July 7, abandoning unlimited-data plans. But one market-research firm says it won’t make a huge difference to many consumers.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/05/verizon-to-drop-its-unlimited-mobile-data-plan-july-7/', 'snippet': 'Verizon will switch to a tiered-pricing plan for mobile data on July 7, abandoning unlimited-data plans. But one market-research firm says it won’t make a huge difference to many consumers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On July 7, Verizon will become the third major mobile phone carrier to ditch its unlimited-data plan and go to tiered pricing, following AT&T and T-Mobile.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Verizon to Drop Its Unlimited Mobile-Data Plan', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AT&T Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'T-Mobile', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Verizon Communications Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-05T21:41:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9db6d73-8dda-5f7e-bde0-5c8cdb7a1c36', 'word_count': 429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9db6d73-8dda-5f7e-bde0-5c8cdb7a1c36'}, {'abstract': 'The band Phish gave its fans a one-band festival in the Finger Lakes region of New York, with plenty of attractions around the edges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/arts/music/phish-gives-its-fans-a-festival-in-watkins-glen-ny.html', 'snippet': 'The band Phish gave its fans a one-band festival in the Finger Lakes region of New York, with plenty of attractions around the edges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WATKINS GLEN, N.Y. — When your friend volunteers to work at a music festival in exchange for a ticket and ends up with the title of “Good Vibe Regulator” at a custom-built all-night disco bar modeled after a giant pinball machine, you know you’ve ended up someplace pretty cool. Last weekend that meant Super Ball IX, the ninth festival by Phish, the eclectically funky rock quartet.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'ra