.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Quidsi, the company that started Diapers.com, is doing all it can to make sure people can run their errands without getting off the couch. Next up: Wag.com, which sells pet supplies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Would You Like Some Dog Food With Your Diapers?', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Quidsi Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T12:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f345167-5d81-5a0e-ae8f-f5d3686cee81', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f345167-5d81-5a0e-ae8f-f5d3686cee81'}, {'abstract': 'A steep increase in demand for rhino horn in Asia linked to a belief in its medicinal or status value has resulted in the killings of 200 rhinoceroses in South Africa alone over the last six months.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/rhino-poaching-threatens-conservation-gains/', 'snippet': 'A steep increase in demand for rhino horn in Asia linked to a belief in its medicinal or status value has resulted in the killings of 200 rhinoceroses in South Africa alone over the last six months.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twenty years of careful conservation efforts have nurtured rhinoceros populations in Africa back to relative health, after they were close to extinction in the early 1990s. Now those efforts risk being undone because of a sharp increase in poaching.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rhino Poaching Threatens Conservation Gains', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rhinoceroses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T12:09:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bettina Wassener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bettina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wassener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bc9ea27-1f1c-57af-8146-104826de799f', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bc9ea27-1f1c-57af-8146-104826de799f'}, {'abstract': 'Vince Gilligan doesn’t have a twisted, violent mind. But you’d never know that from his twisted, brilliant show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/magazine/the-dark-art-of-breaking-bad.html', 'snippet': 'Vince Gilligan doesn’t have a twisted, violent mind. But you’d never know that from his twisted, brilliant show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the first three seasons of the AMC series “Breaking Bad,” Aaron Paul — or rather, his meth-dealing character, Jesse Pinkman — has been slapped, mauled and beaten purple by, respectively, a hit man, a sociopath and a federal drug-enforcement agent. If he were a piñata, the candy would have poured out of this guy long ago. And apparently there is little mercy for Paul in the new season on the way. For there Paul was, one day in late May, standing on Tijeras Avenue in downtown Albuquerque, being tasered by a brawny man in sunglasses.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10bad1/10bad1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10bad1/10bad1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10bad1/10bad1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dark Art of ‘Breaking Bad’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art Of Darkness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilligan, Vince', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMC (TV Network)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T12:20:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Segal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Segal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a24ffac6-c953-542d-9e9e-958c5b60d200', 'word_count': 3631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a24ffac6-c953-542d-9e9e-958c5b60d200'}, {'abstract': 'Many layers of emotion about parenting.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/why-caylees-death-is-personal/', 'snippet': 'Many layers of emotion about parenting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was in a doctor’s examination room yesterday, waiting for a specialist I had never met. She swept in at 2:30 and didn’t say hello, or ask me what I was there for. Instead she blurted, “Did you hear the verdict?” and we discussed the case for 10 minutes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Caylee’s Death Is Personal', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T12:22:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97d8629d-1fb7-572e-b3b2-be424ba673a2', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97d8629d-1fb7-572e-b3b2-be424ba673a2'}, {'abstract': 'What you need to know to tell an ade from a fizz.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/a-fount-of-soda-knowledge/', 'snippet': 'What you need to know to tell an ade from a fizz.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was researching the history of the American soda fountain for my story this week, all roads led to Gregory Cohen, probably the country’s leading expert on the institution. Mr. Cohen, 43, grew up in Midwood in Brooklyn, frequenting Chodosh’s corner store and soda fountain (long closed, it was a fount of egg creams and memories that now has its own Facebook page. If you grew up drinking ginger yips, he knows that you are probably from Georgia, and on top of that he will mix you one at his soda fountain, Lofty Pursuits, in Tallahassee, Fla. (It’s a combination of ginger syrup, soda water, and vanilla ice cream.) His masterwork is a complete taxonomy of soda fountain drinks, compiled from a lifetime of interviews and research in antique manuals like “Ice Cream at the Soda Fountain,” a two-volume set published by the United States Department of Commerce soon after Prohibition went into effect. To learn the precise difference between a milkshake and a malted, an ice cream soda and a French soda, an ade and a fizz, follow the “flow chart.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fount of Soda Knowledge', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T12:30:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Moskin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moskin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b13db56c-0688-53bc-9fa6-40bb62d25b84', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b13db56c-0688-53bc-9fa6-40bb62d25b84'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney’s presidential campaign reported on Wednesday that it raised a total of $18.25 million in the second quarter of this year, more than four times that of his rivals for the Republican presidential nomination.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/romney-reports-fund-raising-numbers/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney’s presidential campaign reported on Wednesday that it raised a total of $18.25 million in the second quarter of this year, more than four times that of his rivals for the Republican presidential nomination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney’s presidential campaign reported on Wednesday that it had raised $18.25 million in the second quarter of this year, more than four times what his rivals for the Republican presidential nomination have raised. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Reports Fund-Raising Numbers', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T12:30:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Rutenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rutenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb62e54f-23e0-551f-8507-13a2337a0bfc', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb62e54f-23e0-551f-8507-13a2337a0bfc'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Possible fabrications in her bid for asylum may complicate matters for the Guinean woman who says the former I.M.F. chief sexually assaulted her.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/a-potential-problem-for-strauss-kahns-accuser/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Possible fabrications in her bid for asylum may complicate matters for the Guinean woman who says the former I.M.F. chief sexually assaulted her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Could it be that when all is said and done, the Guinean woman who has accused Dominique Strauss-Kahn of sexually assaulting her in a Midtown hotel will find herself deported?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A ‘Potential Problem’ for Strauss-Kahn’s Accuser', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asylum, Right of', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Immigration and Customs Enforcement (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T12:48:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65c59fa0-d112-51a0-ae7e-471d103b01c7', 'word_count': 730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65c59fa0-d112-51a0-ae7e-471d103b01c7'}, {'abstract': 'There are plenty of so-called weight-loss wizards and body sculptors out there, but few can boast the results (or high-profile clientele) that Angelo Sorrenti does.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/vain-glorious-the-diet-guru/', 'snippet': 'There are plenty of so-called weight-loss wizards and body sculptors out there, but few can boast the results (or high-profile clientele) that Angelo Sorrenti does.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Angelo Sorrenti, a martial arts champion who is known as the Miracle Trainer, shows off some of his moves in this video, created exclusively for T by the fashion photographer Jacob Sutton.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vain Glorious | The Miracle Trainer', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T13:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sandra Ballentine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ballentine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f603a50d-9fa5-56dc-a0f6-ebfb9d768105', 'word_count': 869, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f603a50d-9fa5-56dc-a0f6-ebfb9d768105'}, {'abstract': 'Witnesses said a man in a dark jacket and dark glasses took “Tête de Femme (Head of a Woman),” a 1965 pencil drawing, from the Weinstein Gallery on Tuesday morning.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/picasso-work-is-stolen-from-san-francisco-gallery/', 'snippet': 'Witnesses said a man in a dark jacket and dark glasses took “Tête de Femme (Head of a Woman),” a 1965 pencil drawing, from the Weinstein Gallery on Tuesday morning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No bigger than a standard sheet of paper, Picasso’s “Tête de Femme (Head of a Woman)” is hardly one of his largest works, but that apparently made it all the easier for a thief to steal it from a gallery in San Francisco on Tuesday. The San Francisco Chronicle reported that the work, a 1965 pencil drawing measuring 10 5/8 inches by 8 1/4 inches, was taken from the Weinstein Gallery in the city’s Union Square neighborhood; the robbery was reported to the police at 11:41 a.m. and the work was being offered by the gallery for more than $200,000. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Picasso Work Is Stolen From San Francisco Gallery', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Francisco Chronicle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T13:07:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e1a1750-1566-5211-a96a-d06c923ade56', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e1a1750-1566-5211-a96a-d06c923ade56'}, {'abstract': "After losing their first match against Palestine, and drawing their second, the Afghan soccer team went out in the preliminary qualifying rounds for FIFA's 2014 World Cup in Brazil.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/multimedia/100000000895402/shot-that-ended-afghanistans-world-cup.html', 'snippet': "After losing their first match against Palestine, and drawing their second, the Afghan soccer team went out in the preliminary qualifying rounds for FIFA's 2014 World Cup in Brazil.", 'lead_paragraph': "After losing their first match against Palestine, and drawing their second, the Afghan soccer team went out in the preliminary qualifying rounds for FIFA's 2014 World Cup in Brazil.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/multimedia/video-afghan-soccer/video-afghan-soccer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/05/multimedia/video-afghan-soccer/video-afghan-soccer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/05/multimedia/video-afghan-soccer/video-afghan-soccer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/05/multimedia/video-afghan-soccer/video-afghan-soccer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': "Shot That Ended Afghanistan's World Cup", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T13:12:20+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Stephen Farrell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farrell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/d4a6daf1-71d4-5d04-b5d6-b3d8e4c96ee4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/d4a6daf1-71d4-5d04-b5d6-b3d8e4c96ee4'}, {'abstract': 'It’s the end of QE2 as we know it, and I feel fine.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/world-not-ended/', 'snippet': 'It’s the end of QE2 as we know it, and I feel fine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'QE2 — “quantitative easing 2″, the Fed’s program of buying long-term bonds — ended last week. For a program that arguably did very little, QE2 attracted an amazing amount of vitriol. On one side, it was accused of fomenting “currency wars”, driving up inflation and/or exchange rates in developing countries. On the other, it was claimed that the program was masking the true effects of the US budget deficit, and that interest rates would soar after it ended.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'World, Not Ended', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T13:20:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8036d54-d754-5dc7-826b-4419405288f6', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8036d54-d754-5dc7-826b-4419405288f6'}, {'abstract': 'Cyclist-friendly cities that allow bikes on trains, Verizon to drop unlimited mobile-data plan, what not to say to a daughter-in-law and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/wednesday-reading-cyclist-friendly-cities-allow-bikes-on-trains/', 'snippet': 'Cyclist-friendly cities that allow bikes on trains, Verizon to drop unlimited mobile-data plan, what not to say to a daughter-in-law and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wednesday Reading: Cyclist-Friendly Cities Allow Bikes on Trains', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T13:36:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d23b7224-14ab-5c42-bfbb-63a339ce97b6', 'word_count': 132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d23b7224-14ab-5c42-bfbb-63a339ce97b6'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/morning-take-out-279/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Deal Makers Soak Up Sun Valley | Technology and media executives converged on the Allen & Company conference in Sun Valley to talk deals and mingle with the likes of Warren E. Buffett, Bill Gates, Oprah Winfrey, Rupert Murdoch and Mark Zuckerberg.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T13:40:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54f86a9f-fe4f-5acb-8aca-946961a43fe5', 'word_count': 2096, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54f86a9f-fe4f-5acb-8aca-946961a43fe5'}, {'abstract': 'For now the market seems to be focusing on the short term, looking past deeper troubles in the economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/business/07markets.html', 'snippet': 'For now the market seems to be focusing on the short term, looking past deeper troubles in the economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stock markets posted their sixth advance in seven days, as gains in transportation and consumer-staple companies overshadowed slower growth in the services industry and China’s interest-rate hike.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Equities Rise Modestly Amid Conflicting Signals', 'kicker': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Equities Rise Modestly Amid Conflicting Signals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T13:41:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bloomberg News', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bloomberg', 