eminder that the White House is eager to exploit whatever technologies will help get out his message, unfiltered.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Social Media Take on Role of Presidential Questioner', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2008', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dorsey, Jack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook.com', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T14:09:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f1139df-71b3-5a45-afcd-df616f1082ed', 'word_count': 785, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f1139df-71b3-5a45-afcd-df616f1082ed'}, {'abstract': 'A Swiss gallery owner who represents Ai Weiwei said that the artist is creating art again though he cannot meet with journalists to discuss it because of the conditions of his release.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/world/asia/07artist.html', 'snippet': 'A Swiss gallery owner who represents Ai Weiwei said that the artist is creating art again though he cannot meet with journalists to discuss it because of the conditions of his release.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — A Swiss gallery owner who represents Ai Weiwei, the dissident artist and international celebrity recently let out of detention, said Wednesday that Mr. Ai was working on his art again, even though he could not do interviews or meet with journalists because of the conditions of his release.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Freed Chinese Artist Reported to Be Back at Work, Though Barred From Talking to Press', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Freed Chinese Artist Reported to Be Back at Work, Though Barred From Talking to Press', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ai Weiwei', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T14:21:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34c83a0c-7aa8-55e7-8831-1344d5d98f85', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34c83a0c-7aa8-55e7-8831-1344d5d98f85'}, {'abstract': 'Production of most paper cash fell markedly last year, as credit cards remained entrenched and paper bills were lasting much longer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/business/07currency.html', 'snippet': 'Production of most paper cash fell markedly last year, as credit cards remained entrenched and paper bills were lasting much longer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The number of dollar bills rolling off the great government presses here and in Fort Worth fell to a modern low in the last fiscal year. Production of $5 bills also dropped to the lowest level in 30 years. And for the first time in that period, the Treasury Department did not print any $10 bills.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/business/CURRENCY/CURRENCY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 631, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/business/CURRENCY/CURRENCY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/07/business/CURRENCY/CURRENCY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Plastic Reigns, the Treasury Slows Its Printing Presses', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Plastic Reigns, Printing of Money Slows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Currency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Cards', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T14:24:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d5fbff5-5114-5824-8c75-597b005ce894', 'word_count': 1115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d5fbff5-5114-5824-8c75-597b005ce894'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/what-were-reading-219/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T14:29:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad39c745-0cf6-5602-91ad-c88fa547e338', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad39c745-0cf6-5602-91ad-c88fa547e338'}, {'abstract': 'To illustrate how tastes in blockbusters have, er, evolved, here are the five top-grossing films of 1979 and the five top-grossing films of 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/magazine/when-grown-ups-went-to-see-films-about-grown-ups.html', 'snippet': 'To illustrate how tastes in blockbusters have, er, evolved, here are the five top-grossing films of 1979 and the five top-grossing films of 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of the many notable things about “Kramer vs. Kramer” — nine Oscar nominations, five wins — perhaps the most surprising is this: It was the highest-grossing film of 1979. To repeat: “Kramer vs. Kramer,” a film about a divorce, was the highest-grossing film released that year. (To be fair, the fifth-highest-grossing film released that year was about a man going insane in Vietnam.) To further illustrate how tastes in blockbusters have, er, evolved, here are the five top-grossing films of 1979 and the five top-grossing films of 2010.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '45', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Grown-Ups Went to See Films About Grown-Ups', 'kicker': 'Bonus Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Grown-Ups Went to See Films About Grown-Ups', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T14:30:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfaa3e9a-62a8-5c81-b3e1-6ec531492a34', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfaa3e9a-62a8-5c81-b3e1-6ec531492a34'}, {'abstract': 'In real life, a broken marriage is still a painful and damaging ordeal. So how did popular culture go from “Kramer vs. Kramer” to a sitcom called “Happily Divorced”?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/magazine/the-divorce-delusion.html', 'snippet': 'In real life, a broken marriage is still a painful and damaging ordeal. So how did popular culture go from “Kramer vs. Kramer” to a sitcom called “Happily Divorced”?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer of 1979, six months before the Oscar-winning divorce drama “Kramer vs. Kramer” hit the theaters, my mother foreshadowed Joanna Kramer’s abrupt departure by moving out of our house. She’d been told by a priest that this was the best strategy for saving her marriage, so she left not so much to find herself (like Meryl Streep’s character) as to find a job, something my father (like Dustin Hoffman’s Ted Kramer) had strictly forbidden her to do.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10riff_span/10riff_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 103, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10riff_span/10riff_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 103}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10riff_span/mag-10Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 209, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10riff_span/10riff_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10riff_span/10riff_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Divorce Delusion', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘A Joyful Kabuki Mask to Obscure The Anguish of Marital Bliss Gone Sour’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Divorce, Separations and Annulments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TV Land', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T14:31:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Havrilesky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Havrilesky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8092ef2c-c351-517b-bd57-b0f7360acd03', 'word_count': 1852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8092ef2c-c351-517b-bd57-b0f7360acd03'}, {'abstract': 'The public editor weighs in on a business columnist’s speaking engagement before P.R. professionals.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/times-curbs-pogues-pr-appearances/', 'snippet': 'The public editor weighs in on a business columnist’s s