ts this summer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Flying (in Harness) Over Pines or Rhinos', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Crested Butte (Colo)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T16:17:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94a0d1e1-d9b6-509c-8ebd-f65a9e5a1dbe', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94a0d1e1-d9b6-509c-8ebd-f65a9e5a1dbe'}, {'abstract': 'A prominent dean of admissions reassures applicants that some of the best personal essays she’s read involve work in fast-food restaurants.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/work/', 'snippet': 'A prominent dean of admissions reassures applicants that some of the best personal essays she’s read involve work in fast-food restaurants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While some high-end college counselors may recommend that their clients embark on expensive and exotic community-service endeavors in third-world countries in pursuit of an elite college acceptance, other applicants find themselves working in their hometown to save money for school. These students may, understandably, worry that their part-time job will be a blemish on their application.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When a Part-Time Job Is Your Extracurricular Activity', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T16:29:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily Altavena', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Altavena', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f59efdae-a7f3-59d6-a96e-ae02495c7ac4', 'word_count': 655, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f59efdae-a7f3-59d6-a96e-ae02495c7ac4'}, {'abstract': 'Competitors hurtle across an unstable surface at more than 60 m.p.h. while jockeying for position against hundreds of rivals — and without helmets, despite violent wipeouts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/10/magazine/look-windsurfing.html', 'snippet': 'Competitors hurtle across an unstable surface at more than 60 m.p.h. while jockeying for position against hundreds of rivals — and without helmets, despite violent wipeouts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Competitors hurtle across an unstable surface at more than 60 m.p.h. while jockeying for position against hundreds of rivals — and without helmets, despite violent wipeouts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Blown Away', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Windsurfing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T16:31:46+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/d10d423b-0a3d-58ab-81fe-45aaff86f374', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/d10d423b-0a3d-58ab-81fe-45aaff86f374'}, {'abstract': 'Even when a medical mistake is caught and a potential crisis averted, the health care worker who made the error is left with the knowledge of almost harming a patient he or she was trying to protect, writes nurse Theresa Brown.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/when-nurses-make-mistakes/', 'snippet': 'Even when a medical mistake is caught and a potential crisis averted, the health care worker who made the error is left with the knowledge of almost harming a patient he or she was trying to protect, writes nurse Theresa Brown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year, a Seattle nurse named Kim Hiatt committed suicide. Ms. Hiatt’s death came nearly seven months after she had given an unintended overdose to an infant heart patient, a medical error that was said to have contributed to the child’s death days later. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Nurses Make Mistakes', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Big or Small, Nursesu2019 Mistakes Reverberate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suicides and Suicide Attempts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T16:40:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Theresa Brown, R.N', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Theresa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'R.', 'middlename': 'N', 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_materia