ps://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/for-gay-couples-eager-to-wed-at-first-possible-moment/', 'snippet': 'The city of Binghamton is planning to open its municipal offices to accept marriage applications on July 24, the day the same-sex marriage law takes effect.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gay couples seeking to marry in New York State at the first possible moment will have at least one option to do so — but, right now at least, it would require a trip to the state’s Southern Tier.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Gay Couples Eager to Wed at First Possible Moment …', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T17:05:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca9e4377-a5e4-5ea1-9800-f5f6169f56dc', 'word_count': 426, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca9e4377-a5e4-5ea1-9800-f5f6169f56dc'}, {'abstract': 'The judge presiding over Roger Clemens’s perjury trial questioned why the House of Representatives has not turned over an audio tape of Clemens’s 2008 deposition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/sports/baseball/clemens-trial-begins-with-dispute-over-audio-tape.html', 'snippet': 'The judge presiding over Roger Clemens’s perjury trial questioned why the House of Representatives has not turned over an audio tape of Clemens’s 2008 deposition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Nearly 140 people, including some of the most high-profile players in recent baseball history, were listed as possible witnesses in Roger Clemens’s perjury trial that began Wednesday in federal court.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/sports/baseball/07clemensspan/07clemens-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/07/sports/baseball/07clemensspan/07clemens-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/sports/baseball/07clemensspan/07clemens-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 605, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/sports/baseball/07clemensspan/07clemens-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/07/sports/baseball/07clemensspan/07clemens-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Clemens Perjury Trial Begins With Dispute Over an Audio Tape', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In The Matter Of... Clemens Perjury Trial Begins With Dispute Over an Audio Tape', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walton, Reggie B', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Steroids', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clemens, Roger', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Perjury', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T17:06:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Macur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macur', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f754fa5-0a7e-583d-992e-8fbdf1ee75bb', 'word_count': 809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f754fa5-0a7e-583d-992e-8fbdf1ee75bb'}, {'abstract': 'The Valentino haute couture collection was primarily based on tulle and lace. The hours of handiwork given were clearly worth the effort.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/just-one-look-valentino/', 'snippet': 'The Valentino haute couture collection was primarily based on tulle and lace. The hours of handiwork given were clearly worth the effort.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Talk about transparency: the Valentino haute couture collection was not only primarily based on tulle and lace, but Maria Grazia Chiuri and Pierpaolo Piccioli, the designers at the helm of the house, also informed the audience of the number of hours it took the atelier to render their light as air creations. Those precious embroideries and appliques take hundreds of hours of handwork. The results? My god. As exquisite a set of dresses as a princess could wish for, especially a youngish princess with a hankering for 30s/70s long lengths, raised waists, and all grown-up grooviness. Here, a dress of gold lace inset into tulle with a dusting of hand painted feathers in violet, rose and amber (to paint the feathers? 215 hours).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Valentino', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T17:15:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sally Singer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sally', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Singer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3d94498-6b31-50e8-bff3-d8bb915c57fe', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3d94498-6b31-50e8-bff3-d8bb915c57fe'}, {'abstract': 'With no spare spacecraft available to rescue the astronauts if something goes wrong, NASA is sending four people, rather than the usual six or seven.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/science/space/07shuttle.html', 'snippet': 'With no spare spacecraft available to rescue the astronauts if something goes wrong, NASA is sending four people, rather than the usual six or seven.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KENNEDY SPACE CENTER, Fla. — Normally, when NASA launches a space shuttle, there are either six or seven astronauts aboard. So why, on the 135th and final launching of the 30-year-old space shuttle program, will there be only four?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/science/07shuttle/07shuttle-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/science/07shuttle/07shuttle-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/07/science/07shuttle/07shuttle-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Only 4 Fliers for Last Shuttle Launch', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Skeleton Crew Gears Up For Last Shuttle Flight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Space Station', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Space', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Atlantis (Space Shuttle)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T17:17:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Space & Cosmos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenneth Chang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenneth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78db0ac1-52e3-53a0-8bc6-3ca97da6abbe', 'word_count': 895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78db0ac1-52e3-53a0-8bc6-3ca97da6abbe'}, {'abstract': 'Journalists from across the country are helping generate questions for President Obama’s “Twitter Town Hall.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/journalists-cull-questions-for-obamas-twitter-town-hall/', 'snippet': 'Journalists from across the country are helping generate questions for President Obama’s “Twitter Town Hall.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To get the conversation started for today’s first Twitter Town Hall at the White House, Twitter executives turned to a handful of journalists from across the country to help generate questions about jobs and the economy for President Obama.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Journalists Cull Questions for Obama’s ‘Twitter Town Hall’', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T17:19:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df2a2e6e-72ba-511e-bd48-bcb9b67abe16', 'word_count': 602, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df2a2e6e-72ba-511e-bd48-bcb9b67abe16'}, {'abstract': 'The sex columnist’s strange case against sexual fidelity.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/dan-savage-versus-monogamy/', 'snippet': 'The sex columnist’s strange case against sexual fidelity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My column on Monday discussed Mark Oppenheimer’s profile of Dan Savage in the Sunday Times Magazine, but it only skimmed the surface of the issues raised by Savage’s case for controlled infidelity in marriage (gay and straight). I thought these points from Eve Tushnet were worth highlighting:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dan Savage Versus Monogamy', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T17:21:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c67d51e-7bbb-50b8-9d57-17981f56c56e', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c67d51e-7bbb-50b8-9d57-17981f56c56e'}, {'abstract': 'Making the world safe for another Depression.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/the-armageddon-caucus/', 'snippet': 'Making the world safe for another Depression.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In general I’m not big on worrying about how we’re setting ourselves up for the next crisis; after all, we’re nowhere near done with the current crisis. Yet it is worth noting that current trends in our policy and political discourse, in addition to blocking efforts to promote recovery, is also setting the stage for a much worse crisis further down the line.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Armageddon Caucus', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T17:22:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7e94dc0-0704-5bf9-9896-7559545edf6a', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7e94dc0-0704-5bf9-9896-7559545edf6a'}, {'abstract': 'The cable news channel also said Erin Burnett, a new hire from CNBC, would take over the 7 p.m. slot on weekdays.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/cnn-cancels-in-the-arena-with-eliot-spitzer/', 'snippet': 'The cable news channel also said Erin Burnett, a new hire from CNBC, would take over the 7 p.m. slot on weekdays.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:01 p.m. | Updated CNN on Wednesday canceled Eliot Spitzer’s 8 p.m. political talk show, “In the Arena,” after only nine months amid a broader shake-up that shifts Anderson Cooper’s 10 p.m. nightly newscast into Mr. Spitzer’s time slot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CNN Cancels ‘In the Arena’ With Eliot Spitzer', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spitzer, Eliot L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CNN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T17:22:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02cc2bba-93c7-5589-b3cd-f35e45e7e9a5', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02cc2bba-93c7-5589-b3cd-f35e45e7e9a5'}, {'abstract': 'TD Bank and TCF Financial are among those that stand to gain the most from the relaxed rules, according to Moody’s.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/debit-card-rules-save-banks-3-5-billion-moodys-says/', 'snippet': 'TD Bank and TCF Financial are among those that stand to gain the most from the relaxed rules, according to Moody’s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While new caps on debit card fees will cost Wall Street billions of dollars in precious revenue, the dent could have been much worse. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debit Card Rules Save Banks $3.5 Billion, Moody’s Says', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debit Cards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Moody's Corporation", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T17:23:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a12c55d-be9a-5123-84b5-e6ca9f2726f6', 'word_count': 327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a12c55d-be9a-5123-84b5-e6ca9f2726f6'}, {'abstract': '“America’s Got Talent,” which has a been a summer success for NBC for five years, is a smash hit this summer, drawing its highest ratings.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/nbcs-talent-draws-even-more-viewers/', 'snippet': '“America’s Got Talent,” which has a been a summer success for NBC for five years, is a smash hit this summer, drawing its highest ratings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NBC thought it had the breakout hit of the summer with “The Voice,” but it looks like it has been holding an even higher card in its summer hand.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'NBC’s ‘Talent’ Draws Even More Viewers', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T17:31:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifie