ondon to “meet with some friends,” but also to raise money from American expatriates.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/romney-holds-london-fund-raiser/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney traveled to London to “meet with some friends,” but also to raise money from American expatriates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Mitt Romney told reporters outside a lavish town house in the heart of the upscale Mayfair neighborhood on Wednesday evening that he was “meeting a few friends” while in London. The invitation from his campaign to attend an evening reception, on a street where a house was listed recently for $26 million, was more candid: “Suggested contribution to Romney for President: $2,500 per person.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Holds London Fund-Raiser', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T20:54:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d50a2128-5a73-5dda-9cb2-9513f25d41d1', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d50a2128-5a73-5dda-9cb2-9513f25d41d1'}, {'abstract': 'The nonprofit group Sing for Hope placed 88 pianos in public places outdoors in New York City for two weeks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/arts/music/sing-for-hopes-pop-up-pianos.html', 'snippet': 'The nonprofit group Sing for Hope placed 88 pianos in public places outdoors in New York City for two weeks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Talk about “sounds of the city.” On Friday night I had one of those only-in-New-York experiences, a mix of musical gratification and urban racket. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/PIANO1/PIANO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/PIANO1/PIANO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 324}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/PIANO1/PIANO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 747, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/PIANO1/PIANO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/PIANO1/PIANO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sirens, Trucks’ Gears and Piano Melodies in the Great Outdoors', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sirens, Trucks’ Gears and Piano Melodies in the Great Outdoors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sing for Hope', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T20:57:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f67cc30c-d7ee-51d3-9d5e-639bf1bb2519', 'word_count': 553, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f67cc30c-d7ee-51d3-9d5e-639bf1bb2519'}, {'abstract': 'Once again, it’s time for our monthly clash of the titans, a battle of semantic juggernauts.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/sentence-of-the-month-bonanza-throwdown-showdown/', 'snippet': 'Once again, it’s time for our monthly clash of the titans, a battle of semantic juggernauts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once again, it’s time for our monthly clash of the titans, a battle of semantic juggernauts — a sweat-drenched, take-no-prisoners, blood-guzzling thumb-wrestling match between the best of all the immortal clauses I happened to read and tweet in June. It’s the historic fourth edition of Shamblanderson’s Sentence of the Month Bonanza Throwdown Showdown. The stakes, as always, are suffocatingly high. This month’s winner will face off at the end of the year with Joe DiMaggio, David Foster Wallace, Bill McKibben and others in Shamblanderson’s Too-Big-To-Even-Be-Believed Best Sentence of the Year Bare-Knuckle Apocalyptic Imbroglio Faceoff. (I’m looking into making this a live event at Yankee Stadium or Madison Square Garden. I’ll pass on details as soon as I have them.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sentence of the Month Bonanza Throwdown Showdown', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, Sam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diaz, Junot', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T20:59:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c782f189-f4ab-5f98-83f9-d437a15b23b0', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c782f189-f4ab-5f98-83f9-d437a15b23b0'}, {'abstract': 'Bebe Zeva, the 18-year-old Internet fashion celebrity, recently attended the premiere of a documentary about her.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/fashion/the-blogger-bebe-zeva-is-featured-in-a-video.html', 'snippet': 'Bebe Zeva, the 18-year-old Internet fashion celebrity, recently attended the premiere of a documentary about her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON a recent Friday night, Bebe Zeva, a teenage fashion blogger, journalist and model based in Las Vegas, was dining at the restaurant Lodge in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, with a group of 20-something contributors to the online magazine Thought Catalog. ', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/fashion/Z-UP-CLOSE-1/Z-UP-CLOSE-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 687, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/fashion/Z-UP-CLOSE-1/Z-UP-CLOSE-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/07/fashion/Z-UP-CLOSE-1/Z-UP-CLOSE-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Becoming One of the ‘Relevant’', 'kicker': 'Up Close', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Becoming One Of the ‘Relevant’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T21:01:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christian Lorentzen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lorentzen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa5cfb7e-9654-577d-bb4c-3f6ea0a4e2c0', 'word_count': 1300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa5cfb7e-9654-577d-bb4c-3f6ea0a4e2c0'}, {'abstract': 'Investigators are said to believe one man is responsible for 15 robberies or attempted robberies since December, mainly in Brooklyn. The last one was on July 1.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/theres-a-busy-new-bank-robber-the-police-suspect/', 'snippet': 'Investigators are said to believe one man is responsible for 15 robberies or attempted robberies since December, mainly in Brooklyn. The last one was on July 1.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some three months after the authorities captured the so-called Holiday Bandit and charged him with robbing nine banks, the police said another prolific bank robber was on the loose in the city.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'There’s a Busy New Bank Robber, the Police Suspect', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T21:01:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd803692-77ce-5bd8-b9fe-0615a6ab641a', 'word_count': 321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd803692-77ce-5bd8-b9fe-0615a6ab641a'}, {'abstract': 'Rebels seized control of a mountain village, moving toward a supply route to the capital that they hope to sever.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/world/africa/07rebels.html', 'snippet': 'Rebels seized control of a mountain village, moving toward a supply route to the capital that they hope to sever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'QAWALISH, Libya — Rebels opposed to Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi seized control of this village in the mountains on Wednesday, extending their hold in western Libya and inching toward a supply route to the capital that they hope to sever.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/world/07rebels_span/07rebels-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/07/world/07rebels_span/07rebels-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/world/07rebels_span/07rebels-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/world/07rebels_span/07rebels-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/07/world/07rebels_span/07rebels-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Libyan Rebels Gain Inches Toward Link to Tripoli', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Libyan Rebels Gain Inches Toward Link To Tripoli', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T21:02:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. J. Chivers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Chivers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d8baef3-e2c8-556f-b81f-10d0e46e1e05', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d8baef3-e2c8-556f-b81f-10d0e46e1e05'}, {'abstract': 'The National Theater in London is presenting Ibsen’s “Emperor and Galilean,” from 1873, that foreshadowed his more famous works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/theater/ibsens-emperor-and-galilean-at-national-theater-in-london.html', 'snippet': 'The National Theater in London is presenting Ibsen’s “Emperor and Galilean,” from 1873, that foreshadowed his more famous works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Of the roughly two dozen major plays by Henrik Ibsen, it was not masterpieces like “Hedda Gabler” and “A Doll’s House” that he described as his most important work. Rather it was the little-known “Emperor and Galilean” of 1873, a deeply personal reckoning with powerlessness that foreshadowed his famous domestic plays, but whose daunting structure — nine hours long, dozens of characters — led Ibsen to classify it as closet drama, meant to be read in private.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/IBSEN1/IBSEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 351, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/IBSEN1/IBSEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 351}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/IBSEN1/IBSEN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 647, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/IBSEN1/IBSEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/IBSEN1/IBSEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Wait for Premiere of Long Play by Ibsen', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Long Wait For Premiere Of Long Play By Ibsen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Theater Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ibsen, Henrik', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T21:03:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7261b24-93ee-5fd5-b209-0d0103489f55', 'word_count': 1109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7261b24-93ee-5fd5-b209-0d0103489f55'}, {'abstract': 'Some of Jules Feiffer’s dancer cartoons have been filmed and will be shown at the River to River Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/arts/dance/jules-feiffers-dancer-at-river-to-river-festival.html', 'snippet': 'Some of Jules Feiffer’s dancer cartoons have been filmed and will be shown at the River to River Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ebullient, emotional dancer first came to the public’s attention in 1957. Year after year she was moved to express herself: trenchant political criticism, urban anxiety, seasonal highs and lows. You name it, she danced it. Accompanied by sparse words, her lithe, flexible body sprang and collapsed in modern dance poses, creating a choreographed phrase across the newspaper page. And then in 2000, still looking trim in her unitard, she retired. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/FEIFFER/FEIFFER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/FEIFFER/FEIFFER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 349}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/FEIFFER/FEIFFER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 622, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/FEIFFER/FEIFFER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/07/arts/FEIFFER/FEIFFER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Feiffer’s Dancer Returns to Leap Ever So Seriously', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Feiffer’s Dancer Returns To Leap Ever So Seriously', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'River to River Festival (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feiffer, Jules', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T21:05:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia La Rocco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Rocco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f3f8152-9baf-566f-9082-7c2a129d4097', 'word_count': 1066, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f3f8152-9baf-566f-9082-7c2a129d4097'}, {'abstract': 'UBS said on Wednesday that it had hired Michael Ruppert, a defense industry banker.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/ubs-hires-defense-banker/', 'snippet': 'UBS said on Wednesday that it had hired Michael Ruppert, a defense industry banker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UBS said on Wednesday that it had hired Michael Ruppert, a defense industry banker. Mr. Ruppert, who had worked at Lockheed Martin and Lehman Brothers, joins UBS from Lazard, where he worked in its Washington office. He will be based in New York and report to Karl Knapp. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'UBS Hires Defense Banker', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lazard LLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T21:11:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bdd171b-28e3-55ea-ab23-4d4390726506', 'word_count': 49, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bdd171b-28e3-55ea-ab23-4d4390726506'}, {'abstract': 'As a recent debate on the budget shows, inductive reasoning can take us only so far.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/arguing-from-the-facts/', 'snippet': 'As a recent debate on the budget shows, inductive reasoning can take us only so far.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Stone is featuring occasional posts by Gary Gutting, a professor of philosophy at the University of Notre Dame, that apply critical thinking to information and events that have appeared in the news.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Arguing From the Facts', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T21:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Gutting', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gutting', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cf53dc6-67c1-5b0c-9ea7-623b2f8edd76', 'word_count': 1016, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cf53dc6-67c1-5b0c-9ea7-623b2f8edd76'}, {'abstract': 'While designers are delivering an abundance of glamour, the front-row look couldn’t be simpler.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/06/summer-style-buttoned-down/', 'snippet': 'While designers are delivering an abundance of glamour, the front-row look couldn’t be simpler.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With Paris in the throes of couture, fashion fanatics expect extravagance on and off the runways. And while designers are delivering an abundance of glamour, the front-row look couldn’t be simpler- without a trace of the Balenciaga, Chanel and Celine ensembles we’ve come to expect from the fashion flock. Instead, we’re seeing a lot of slouchy Oxford shirts and plain, tailored trousers. Does this trio of taste makers — French Vogue’s editor in chief, Emmanuelle Alt, the model Caroline De Maigret and the heiress Bianca Brandolini d’Adda — portend a new austerity? And will it linger into fall? Or is it just simply too hot to dress up?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Summer Style | Buttoned Down', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-06T21:19:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/264e3449-b880-5d71-bc19-8673d1ab74ae', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/264e3449-b880-5d71-bc19-8673d1ab74ae'}, {'abstract': 'Scottish actor James McAvoy discusses his career and upcoming role in “X-Men: First Class” with Times reporter Dave Itzkoff.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2011/07/06/arts/TTMcEvoy.html', 'snippet': 'Scottish actor James McAvoy discusses his career and upcoming role in “X-Men: First Class” with Times reporter Dave Itzkoff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scottish actor James McAvoy discusses his career and upcoming role in “X-Men: First Class” with Times reporter Dave Itzkoff.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'James McAvoy: First Class', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': Non