e about the energy usage of TV set-top boxes and DVRs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor: ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Energy-Efficient Electronics: An Industry View', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Energy-Efficient Electronics: An Industry View', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy Efficiency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservation of Resources', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Electronics', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T01:14:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e7c911d-caae-5c03-9cc0-76094d84a19f', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e7c911d-caae-5c03-9cc0-76094d84a19f'}, {'abstract': 'A book deal for Julian Assange, the founder of WikiLeaks, has reportedly fallen through over concerns the information could be used against him by United States prosecutors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/world/europe/07briefs-WIKILEAKSMEM_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'A book deal for Julian Assange, the founder of WikiLeaks, has reportedly fallen through over concerns the information could be used against him by United States prosecutors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A book deal that Julian Assange, the founder of WikiLeaks, signed only six months ago has fallen through, according to a report in The Guardian on Wednesday, but Canongate, his publisher in Britain, said it was still expecting to publish the book. Canongate did not provide a date for its release, however. According to The Guardian report, Mr. Assange, left, had expressed concern that the memoir could be used against him by prosecutors in the United States. American officials have been weighing whether to try to charge Mr. Assange since WikiLeaks released a trove of State Department cables, but it is unclear where that effort stands. The book deal was announced in December; Mr. Assange said he expected to earn $1.7 million for the memoir, which was being ghostwritten by Andrew O’Hagan, a novelist. The memoir was originally scheduled for publication in April 2011 in Britain, although Mr. Assange’s American publisher, Knopf, did not release a publication date. “With projects like these, you never know about delivery, and you never know about timetables,” said Paul Bogaards, a spokesman for Knopf, part of Random House. “My understanding is that the book has been postponed.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'WikiLeaks Memoir ‘Postponed’', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'WikiLeaks Memoir ‘Postponed’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assange, Julian P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WikiLeaks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T01:15:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3aa1af7d-6fc7-5555-a1b5-b22d9fb02ae8', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3aa1af7d-6fc7-5555-a1b5-b22d9fb02ae8'}, {'abstract': 'The Shabab, the Islamist rebel group fighting the government of Somalia, announced that it would again allow aid organizations to operate in the areas the group controls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/world/africa/07briefs-ABOUTFACEONA_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The Shabab, the Islamist rebel group fighting the government of Somalia, announced that it would again allow aid organizations to operate in the areas the group controls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Shabab, the Islamist rebel group fighting the government of Somalia, announced that it would again allow aid organizations to operate in the areas the group controls. A rebel spokesman, Mohamoud Raghe, said in a news conference in Mogadishu that the aid groups were going to be allowed to work in order to help bring relief to areas hard-hit by a severe drought. The Shabab said its drought committee would work with the organizations. The rebels in the past accused many aid groups of acting as spies and of being anti-Muslim. Some analysts said the Shabab decided to allow the aid groups to work again because it wanted to stop the flow of hundreds of people to government-controlled areas in search of food. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Somalia: About-Face on Aid', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | AFRICA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Somalia: About-Face on Aid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Somalia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Al-Shabab', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T01:17:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohamed Ibrahim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohamed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ibrahim', 'qualifier': Non