Fred Wilpon and Saul Katz in connection to the Madoff Ponzi scheme.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2002, Jed S. Rakoff, a federal district court judge in Manhattan, declared the death penalty unconstitutional. No one — neither defense lawyers nor prosecutors — had asked him for such a ruling. But Rakoff, who felt strongly about the growing number of exonerations of death row inmates through DNA evidence, went ahead and did it.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/sports/RAKOFF/RAKOFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 427, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/07/sports/RAKOFF/RAKOFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/07/sports/RAKOFF/RAKOFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Federal Judge Holds Financial Fate of Wilpon and Katz', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In The Matter Of... Federal Judge Holds Financial Fate Of Wilpon and Katz', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Picard, Irving H', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madoff, Bernard L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ponzi Schemes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rakoff, Jed S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Katz, Saul B', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilpon, Fred', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decision