me': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nissan Motor Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Union of Concerned Scientists', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T10:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cheryl Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cheryl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7f5aff9-bd57-571d-988a-f2a8bacec858', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7f5aff9-bd57-571d-988a-f2a8bacec858'}, {'abstract': 'How you can limit how loud an iPod Shuffle can play.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/qa-lowering-the-volume-on-the-ipod-shuffle/', 'snippet': 'How you can limit how loud an iPod Shuffle can play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Lowering the Volume on the iPod Shuffle', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Digital Audio Players', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPod', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'iTunes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T10:11:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22825549-fdf5-50ef-8aef-4d38f43e20b6', 'word_count': 197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22825549-fdf5-50ef-8aef-4d38f43e20b6'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Lange, the comedian and sometime sidekick to Howard Stern, joined Nick DiPaolo on Fox Sports Radio to riff on current events, and answered a question about returning to Mr. Stern’s program.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/for-one-night-artie-lange-returns-to-radio/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Lange, the comedian and sometime sidekick to Howard Stern, joined Nick DiPaolo on Fox Sports Radio to riff on current events, and answered a question about returning to Mr. Stern’s program.', 'lead_paragraph': "It’s been more than a year and a half since Artie Lange, the ragged and randy comedian and sometime sidekick to Howard Stern, was last heard live on the radio, a streak that came to an end on Wednesday night. On a Fox Sports Radio program hosted by his friend and fellow comedian Nick DiPaolo (who in turn was filling in for Tony Bruno), Mr. Lange joined the show to take questions from listeners and riff on current events. According to a live blog of the program posted by Punchline magazine, discussion of the sad circumstances that kept Mr. Lange off the airwaves was not abundant, though Mr. Lange said he’s been getting sober at a rehabilitation facility in West Palm Beach, Fla., and Mr. DiPaolo introduced him to the show by joking, “You might have seen him on ‘Intervention.'”", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For One Night, Artie Lange Returns to Radio', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lange, Artie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stern, Howard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T10:26:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1035d792-6667-509e-a834-0ef75c5cceb2', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1035d792-6667-509e-a834-0ef75c5cceb2'}, {'abstract': 'South Korean prosecutors indicted more than 50 people for fixing soccer matches in the country’s K-League.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/sports/soccer/08iht-soccer08.html', 'snippet': 'South Korean prosecutors indicted more than 50 people for fixing soccer matches in the country’s K-League.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — South Korean prosecutors indicted 46 players and 11 others on Thursday on charges of fixing soccer matches in the professional K-League.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '46 Players Are Indicted in South Korea Scandal', 'kicker': 'Soccer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '46 Players Are Indicted In South Korea Scandal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheating', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gambling', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T10:54:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/305cbf70-f10b-5665-b083-fc609e87fc15', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/305cbf70-f10b-5665-b083-fc609e87fc15'}, {'abstract': 'What the education-reform movement needs to do next.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/magazine/reforming-the-school-reformers.html', 'snippet': 'What the education-reform movement needs to do next.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the early days of the education-reform movement, a decade or so ago, you’d often hear from reformers a powerful rallying cry: “No excuses.” For too long, they said, poverty had been used as an excuse by complacent educators and bureaucrats who refused to believe that poor students could achieve at high levels. Reform-minded school leaders took the opposite approach, insisting that students in the South Bronx should be held to the same standards as kids in Scarsdale. Amazingly enough, those high expectations often paid off, producing test results at some low-income urban schools that would impress parents in any affluent suburb.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10lede_span/mag-10lede-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 254, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10lede_span/mag-10lede-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 254}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10lede_span/mag-10lede-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10lede_span/10lede_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10lede_span/10lede_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No, Seriously: No Excuses', 'kicker': 'First', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No, Seriously: No Excuses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ravitch, Diane', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T11:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Tough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aae15582-85e1-52db-b9f8-9832e69ff33c', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aae15582-85e1-52db-b9f8-9832e69ff33c'}, {'abstract': 'The reform, known as combined reporting, failed in part because reliable estimates of how much money it can bring to state coffers are difficult to come by.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/rhode-island-nixes-tax-reform-said-to-favor-small-business/', 'snippet': 'The reform, known as combined reporting, failed in part because reliable estimates of how much money it can bring to state coffers are difficult to come by.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Are national corporations using state laws to shave millions of dollars off their tax bills — and gain an advantage over smaller rivals? That’s the claim some lawmakers and activists are making in statehouses around the country, and as states grapple with deep cuts in spending and seek new sources of revenue, it is putting big business on the defensive. This year, the fight moved to Rhode Island.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rhode Island Rejects Tax Change Said to Favor Small Business', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhode Island', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T11:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robb Mandelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mandelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fc560f2-ac3f-5c34-847a-1ea515282022', 'word_count': 1466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fc560f2-ac3f-5c34-847a-1ea515282022'}, {'abstract': 'The bank is continuing to nudge the cost of money back to pre-crisis levels, despite heightened fears about Greek debt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/business/global/08rates.html', 'snippet': 'The bank is continuing to nudge the cost of money back to pre-crisis levels, despite heightened fears about Greek debt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANKFURT — The European Central Bank maintained a pose of studied aloofness Thursday toward the Greek debt crisis, pushing its benchmark interest rate nearer to precrisis levels and laying the onus on government leaders to cope with rising tensions that threaten the integrity of the euro zone.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/business/Rates/Rates-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Eu