icker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Honolulu (Hawaii)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T19:06:42+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/050557d7-5d93-51c5-8eab-a6fffe2edba1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/050557d7-5d93-51c5-8eab-a6fffe2edba1'}, {'abstract': 'The city brims with summer’s delights: overflowing farmers’ markets and lei stands, and Hawaiian music spilling onto beaches from oceanfront lounges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/travel/36-hours-in-honolulu.html', 'snippet': 'The city brims with summer’s delights: overflowing farmers’ markets and lei stands, and Hawaiian music spilling onto beaches from oceanfront lounges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONOLULU has never been hotter, and not just because it’s July. The city’s "it" factor has been on the rise since President Obama announced, two years ago in Singapore, that his self-described “home state of Hawaii” would host the 2011 Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum. This November, the 21-nation Pacific Rim summit may bring as many as 10,000 people to Waikiki, the renowned Honolulu destination on Oahu’s south shore. While the city primps for the media glare, it also brims with summer’s delights. Mangoes and sweet summer fruit add splashy colors to the market stands. Pikake, puakenikeni, gardenia and tuberose grace Chinatown lei stands, and Hawaiian music spills onto beaches from sunset-drenched oceanfront lounges.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/travel/10HOURS_SPAN/10HOURS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/10/travel/10HOURS_SPAN/10HOURS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 225}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/travel/10HOURS_SPAN/10HOURS_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/travel/10HOURS_SPAN/10HOURS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/travel/10HOURS_SPAN/10HOURS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Honolulu', 'kicker': '36 Hours', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Honolulu', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Honolulu (Hawaii)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T19:06:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jocelyn Fujii', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jocelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fujii', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e226359-6e89-5ba3-9131-038148ae24b3', 'word_count': 1388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e226359-6e89-5ba3-9131-038148ae24b3'}, {'abstract': 'A letter apparently written to Abdul Qadeer Khan by a senior North Korean official 13 years ago has become the strongest evidence yet suggesting that Pakistan’s top military officials were involved in the secret sale of equipment to the North.', 'web_url': 'https:/