'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurel Graeber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graeber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cee0751-4f48-5778-b482-0eb54900880e', 'word_count': 3015, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cee0751-4f48-5778-b482-0eb54900880e'}, {'abstract': 'The Martin House Complex in Buffalo is looking for the original windows for the main house there, which was designed by Frank Lloyd Wright.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/arts/design/martin-house-seeks-original-frank-lloyd-wright-windows.html', 'snippet': 'The Martin House Complex in Buffalo is looking for the original windows for the main house there, which was designed by Frank Lloyd Wright.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Darwin D. Martin, a soap-factory executive in Buffalo, was a serial client of Frank Lloyd Wright. Between 1903 and 1928, the charismatic architect designed a half-dozen buildings for Martin’s family, including a carriage house and a mausoleum. Wright referred to his shy, bookish patron as “my best friend,” borrowed $70,000 from him over the years and never repaid the loans.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/ANTIQUES-1-1310046900248/ANTIQUES-1-1310046900248-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 744, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/ANTIQUES-1-1310046900248/ANTIQUES-1-1310046900248-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/ANTIQUES-1-1310046900248/ANTIQUES-1-1310046900248-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hunting for Windows in a House by Frank Lloyd Wright', 'kicker': 'Antiques', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hunting Windows For Wright House', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wright, Frank Lloyd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T21:49:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eve M. Kahn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/33efb16f-176b-5ecb-819c-924894ffcb6b', 'word_count': 1062, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33efb16f-176b-5ecb-819c-924894ffcb6b'}, {'abstract': 'Karen Schnelwar loved her Upper West Side neighborhood. So when it came time to buy a place, she decided to limit her search to a radius of a few blocks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/realestate/hiding-in-plain-sight.html', 'snippet': 'Karen Schnelwar loved her Upper West Side neighborhood. So when it came time to buy a place, she decided to limit her search to a radius of a few blocks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AFTER 14 years as a renter in the same building, Karen Schnelwar believed that buying a one-bedroom was a wise course of action. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/realestate/Hunt4/Hunt4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/realestate/Hunt4/Hunt4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/realestate/Hunt4/Hunt4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hiding in Plain Sight', 'kicker': 'The Hunt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hiding in Plain Sight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper West Side (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T21:52:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e77c66bc-0444-53bc-9c7b-259ece6f3898', 'word_count': 1138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e77c66bc-0444-53bc-9c7b-259ece6f3898'}, {'abstract': 'Competition to acquire Bruce Nauman’s latest video and sound work was fierce from the moment it went on view at the Sperone Westwater Gallery on the Bowery last fall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/arts/design/2-continents-1-work-and-31-hand-positions.html', 'snippet': 'Competition to acquire Bruce Nauman’s latest video and sound work was fierce from the moment it went on view at the Sperone Westwater Gallery on the Bowery last fall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Competition to acquire “For Beginners (all the combinations of the thumb and fingers),” Bruce Nauman’s latest video and sound work, was fierce from the moment it went on view at the Sperone Westwater Gallery on the Bowery last fall. Michael Govan, director of the Los Angeles County Museum of Art, wanted the work because he said it “sums up the history of art.” Only trouble: François Pinault, a big collector of Mr. Nauman’s work, wanted it too. Mr. Pinault, the luxury-goods magnate and owner of Christie’s, is constantly on the prowl for great new art to put in his two spaces in Venice, the Punta della Dogana and the Palazzo Grassi.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/SUB-VOGEL/SUB-VOGEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 695, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/SUB-VOGEL/SUB-VOGEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/SUB-VOGEL/SUB-VOGEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '2 Continents, 1 Work and 31 Hand Positions', 'kicker': 'Inside Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2 Continents, 1 Work and 31 Hand Positions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinault, Francois', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nauman, Bruce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T21:53:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c651c0c5-abed-55e8-b44f-e34107203c06', 'word_count': 1006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c651c0c5-abed-55e8-b44f-e34107203c06'}, {'abstract': 'Of seven pet store owners or employees interviewed, some with multiple shops, all but one said there were guidelines for dealing with intoxicated customers.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/puppy-purchasing-when-drunk-a-common-city-scourge/', 'snippet': 'Of seven pet store owners or employees interviewed, some with multiple shops, all but one said there were guidelines for dealing with intoxicated customers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City pet shop ownership has its challenges: Pushy customers. Yapping pooches. And, it seems, drunken puppy purchasing. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Puppy Purchasing When Drunk, a Common City Scourge', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T21:54:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72cab991-859b-5d00-aa41-3f4a0a6b20cd', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72cab991-859b-5d00-aa41-3f4a0a6b20cd'}, {'abstract': 'Third Rail’s iPhone case can accept more than one battery, providing extra-long talk time.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/backup-power-for-the-iphone/', 'snippet': 'Third Rail’s iPhone case can accept more than one battery, providing extra-long talk time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Third Rail System may be the lightest iPhone case with an auxiliary battery, or it may be the heaviest. I’ll explain.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Backup Power for the iPhone', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Batteries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'THIRD RAIL', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T21:55:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebd8841b-e185-5282-8cc8-17c2710e1da3', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebd8841b-e185-5282-8cc8-17c2710e1da3'}, {'abstract': 'Screaming men, ghosts of few words and stranglers set the tone for “North by New York,” an exhibition at Scandinavia House.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/arts/design/north-by-new-york-at-scandinavia-house-review.html', 'snippet': 'Screaming men, ghosts of few words and stranglers set the tone for “North by New York,” an exhibition at Scandinavia House.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scandinavian people are cool like Bergman; southern Europeans are hot like Fellini. This sounds like a stereotype, but you will find little to disconfirm the first part, at least, in “North by New York: New Nordic Art” at Scandinavia House, the American-Scandinavian Foundation’s cultural center. Here, with some mild exceptions, emotional (and imaginative) restraint is the rule. This mixed bag of 15 contemporary artists is not a big enough sample to allow scientific conclusions, though all the Nordic nations are represented: Denmark, Sweden, Norway, Iceland and Finland. Who knows what else the guest curators, Robert Storr and Francesca Pietropaolo, might have picked that might have altered the general mood of guarded sobriety?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/NORTH-span/NORTH-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/NORTH-span/NORTH-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/NORTH-span/NORTH-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/NORTH-span/NORTH-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/NORTH-span/NORTH-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chilly Scenes (and Sounds) of Scandinavia', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chilly Scenes (and Sounds) of Scandinavia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scandinavia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scandinavia House', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T21:56:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e74acd89-736f-5b26-a2ec-b68ac595be02', 'word_count': 858, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e74acd89-736f-5b26-a2ec-b68ac595be02'}, {'abstract': '“Mother India,” a small exhibition at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, gathers sculptures, paintings and drawings representing Devi, the Great Mother goddess of India.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/arts/design/mother-india-at-metropolitan-museum-review.html', 'snippet': '“Mother India,” a small exhibition at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, gathers sculptures, paintings and drawings representing Devi, the Great Mother goddess of India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What would Western civilization have been like had it worshiped not a single, remote, male god, but two or more vividly present divine beings of both genders? Would we now have a kinder, gentler and more balanced society? Maybe not, but if the visual culture of India is any indicator, our art of the past thousand years might have been sexier.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/GODDESS-1/GODDESS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/GODDESS-1/GODDESS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/GODDESS-1/GODDESS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Life-Affirming Female Force, Ready to Rumble', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline