e': 'subject', 'value': 'Nonprofit Organizations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T22:35:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Strom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2a0ce81-e147-55d4-95c7-a653ca02909a', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2a0ce81-e147-55d4-95c7-a653ca02909a'}, {'abstract': 'For 14 years, the courtyard at MoMA PS1 in Queens has been home to Warm Up, a summer event that mixes music, art and modern design.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/is-it-summer-time-to-party-at-the-museum/', 'snippet': 'For 14 years, the courtyard at MoMA PS1 in Queens has been home to Warm Up, a summer event that mixes music, art and modern design.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three young girls zipped across the crowded dance floor, dresses fluttering, as a new D.J. took the stage. Their parents watched from beneath a small grove of plum and oak trees, drinking beers and discussing the exhibition of Ryan Trecartin videos. Nearby, two intricately coiffed hipsters in tight black cut-offs dipped their feet in a pool and waited to play table tennis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is It Summer? Time to Party at the Museum', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PS 1 Contemporary Art Center', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T22:36:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugh Ryan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a507f0c2-db04-5191-aaa7-346367687a00', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a507f0c2-db04-5191-aaa7-346367687a00'}, {'abstract': 'Move over, concrete jungle! New York is suddenly an art city of ceramics.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/artifacts-feats-of-clay/', 'snippet': 'Move over, concrete jungle! New York is suddenly an art city of ceramics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Move over, concrete jungle! New York is suddenly a city of ceramics. By some odd coincidence, restless aggregations of fired clay are popping up in galleries all over town. Don’t expect the stuff of any potter’s wheel in any of them. These are unique, handworked forms that constitute some of the most radical experiments in art today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artifacts | Feats of Clay', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T22:39:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Yablonsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yablonsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/943c0849-ee89-5c18-b20c-ae0f647851cc', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/943c0849-ee89-5c18-b20c-ae0f647851cc'}, {'abstract': 'In Catherine Breillat’s “Sleeping Beauty” the princess doesn’t fall into a 100-year slumber, she hits the road for some adventure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/movies/sleeping-beauty-from-catherine-breillat-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Catherine Breillat’s “Sleeping Beauty” the princess doesn’t fall into a 100-year slumber, she hits the road for some adventure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “The Sleeping Beauty,” Catherine Breillat’s wily and witty retelling of the fairy tale, the enchanted princess doesn’t just fall into a 100-year slumber, she also hits the road, walking and riding into adventure. Simone de Beauvoir wrote in “The Second Sex,” “Woman is Sleeping Beauty, Donkey Skin, Cinderella, Snow White, the one who receives and endures.” After taking up Bluebeard in her previous movie, Ms. Breillat has turned to a more familiar tale, transforming a story of the universal woman into that of a specific girl, Anastasia (played first by Carla Besnainou and then by Julia Artamonov) and the world she makes from her imaginative longing.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/SLEEPING-span/SLEEPING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/SLEEPING-span/SLEEPING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/SLEEPING-span/SLEEPING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/SLEEPING-span/SLEEPING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/SLEEPING-span/SLEEPING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Sleep for a Beauty Who Seeks Adventure', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The Sleeping Beauty'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Sleep for a Beauty Who Seeks Adventure', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fairy Tales', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breillat, Catherine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T22:47:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5656df5-398e-5f0b-ba03-57dc40321152', 'word_count': 817, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5656df5-398e-5f0b-ba03-57dc40321152'}, {'abstract': 'Investment banks have always prized discretion among employees. But at Goldman Sachs, a public persona can be a liability.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/07/at-goldman-mounting-pressure-on-staff-to-keep-a-low-profile/', 'snippet': 'Investment banks have always prized discretion among employees. But at Goldman Sachs, a public persona can be a liability.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Allen Mask, a former Goldman employee who now works at Google, is pursuing his passion for hip-hop music.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Goldman, Pressure on Staff to Keep a Low Profile', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-07T22:49:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person'