ss is employing government agencies to harass independent farmers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a lot going on in “Farmageddon,” Kristin Canty’s anxiety-laden documentary about government oversight of our farming and food production. Part consumer-rights advocacy, part abuse-of-power exposé, the film dances between the two as if uncertain where to settle. But though neither thread is as fully developed as we might want, Ms. Canty’s main concern — that agribusiness is employing government agencies to harass small, independent farmers — comes through loud and clear.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/08farm-span/08farm-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/08farm-span/08farm-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/08farm-span/08farm-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/08farm-span/08farm-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/08/arts/08farm-span/08farm-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Government Oversight in Farming', 'kicker': 'Movie Review  ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Farmageddon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T03:48:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/21de86dc-6020-5355-96ad-910e23d101ad', 'word_count': 331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21de86dc-6020-5355-96ad-910e23d101ad'}, {'abstract': 'Two soldiers were killed Thursday by a roadside bomb that exploded close to a checkpoint near Victory Base Complex in Baghdad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/world/middleeast/08briefs-BOMBKILLS2AM_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Two soldiers were killed Thursday by a roadside bomb that exploded close to a checkpoint near Victory Base Complex in Baghdad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In another ominous sign of the heightened risks here for American troops as they prepare to withdraw from the country, two soldiers were killed Thursday by a roadside bomb that exploded close to a checkpoint at the United States’ military’s largest base in Iraq, Victory Base Complex in Baghdad. American officials said the bomb was a device known as an E.F.P., which pierces armor. In June the American military suffered its most combat deaths in three years. Most of the attacks have been attributed to rockets fired at bases by Shiite militias. But Thursday’s attack was jarring for American officials, as it struck near a checkpoint that is constantly guarded and under surveillance. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iraq: Bomb Kills 2 American Soldiers', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | MIDDLE EAST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iraq: Bomb Kills 2 American Soldiers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T03:55:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Arango', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arango', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ef5da54-d5ca-525c-a433-ddbad7cccf29', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ef5da54-d5ca-525c-a433-ddbad7cccf29'}, {'abstract': 'One recommendation expected from the Government Accounting Standards Board is a new way to calculate today’s value of pensions to be paid in the future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/business/proposal-hopes-to-improve-disclosures-on-public-pensions.html', 'snippet': 'One recommendation expected from the Government Accounting Standards Board is a new way to calculate today’s value of pensions to be paid in the future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The board that writes accounting rules for states and cities plans to issue a proposed solution Friday to one of the hottest disputes in public finance, whether governments are truthfully disclosing the size and condition of their pension funds, and how to fix any deficiencies.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Proposal for Drawing a Clearer Picture of Public Pension Finances', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Proposal for Drawing a Clearer Picture of Public Pension Finances', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'ke