ington that as long as there is a renewable fuels standard that mandates the use of increasing amounts of biofuels, ethanol can prosper.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/compromise-relieves-ethanol-industry/', 'snippet': 'Even with ethanol subsidies cut, a consensus has arisen in Washington that as long as there is a renewable fuels standard that mandates the use of increasing amounts of biofuels, ethanol can prosper.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Any other year, the ethanol industry would have declared a defeat, not a victory.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ethanol Industry Welcomes Compromise', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biofuels', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crop Controls and Subsidies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs (Tariff)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethanol', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feinstein, Dianne', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klobuchar, Amy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thune, John R', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T12:16:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clifford Krauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clifford', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5002ae1a-8b82-5c1c-bf08-f17e55c4da3c', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5002ae1a-8b82-5c1c-bf08-f17e55c4da3c'}, {'abstract': 'Tyson versus Sperling, sense and nonsense.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/nec-and-nec/', 'snippet': 'Tyson versus Sperling, sense and nonsense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Laura Tyson — who headed the National Economic Council under Clinton — has an excellent, clear, sober discussion of the economic problem at the FT. She calls for long-run fiscal restraint, but more, not less, spending right now, with the economy deeply depressed. Laura is anything but a radical; what she’s saying is basically macroeconomics 101.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'NEC and NEC', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T12:25:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b8e9a10-3042-56a8-83a5-396f8734d9ce', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b8e9a10-3042-56a8-83a5-396f8734d9ce'}, {'abstract': 'The Queen of Soul’s career has included 18 Grammys, more than 75 million records sold, being the first woman inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame and a Presidential Medal of Freedom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/magazine/long-live-aretha-the-queen-of-soul.html', 'snippet': 'The Queen of Soul’s career has included 18 Grammys, more than 75 million records sold, being the first woman inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame and a Presidential Medal of Freedom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aretha Franklin sat alone with a Coke. It was the night of her 69th birthday, and all around, guests were filing into the Park Room at the Helmsley Park Lane Hotel on Central Park South, bobbing to live music from the vibraphonist Roy Ayers or the mambo prince Tito Puente Jr. Franklin has given herself big birthday parties before, but this one had a certain urgency. A few months earlier, in December, she announced she had undergone an unnamed surgical “procedure,” and word spread that she had pancreatic cancer (which she denied); other reports speculated that she’d had gastric-\xadbypass surgery to get control of a weight problem that appeared to have pushed her over 250 pounds (which she denied). The organizers of the Grammy Awards quickly put together a tribute for her, and the sudden and shocking weight loss displayed in a taped thank-you played during the ceremony in February only kept advance-obituary writers scrambling for whatever superlatives were left to describe a career that has included 18 Grammys, upward of 75 million records sold, being the first woman inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame and a Presidential Medal of Freedom.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10aretha2/10aretha2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10aretha2/10aretha2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10aretha2/10aretha2-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10aretha2/10aretha2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1494, 'width': 1120, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10aretha2/10aretha2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10aretha2/10aretha2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/magazine/10aretha2/10aretha2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A-R-E-T-H-A', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A-R-E-T-H-A', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gospel Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franklin, Aretha', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T12:30:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Hoerburger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoerburger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d500441d-d1ab-5368-a6bc-16c21fcf2262', 'word_count': 2831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d500441d-d1ab-5368-a6bc-16c21fcf2262'}, {'abstract': 'Entertainment value, anyway.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/benefits-of-the-euro-crisis/', 'snippet': 'Entertainment value, anyway.', 'lead_paragra