'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f329134-10d3-51cf-be22-02ddeee83590', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f329134-10d3-51cf-be22-02ddeee83590'}, {'abstract': 'Amid the widening phone-hacking controversy at the media empire, British officials said it could take awhile to assess the proposed acquisition of BSkyB.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/news-corp-deal-to-buy-bskyb-in-limbo/', 'snippet': 'Amid the widening phone-hacking controversy at the media empire, British officials said it could take awhile to assess the proposed acquisition of BSkyB.', 'lead_paragraph': 'News Corporation‘s deal to buy the pay-television company British Sky Broadcasting is in limbo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Corp. Deal for BSkyB in Limbo', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Sky Broadcasting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T12:47:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/afb6191a-82ed-5162-a5e5-ccfcb7342450', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afb6191a-82ed-5162-a5e5-ccfcb7342450'}, {'abstract': 'A writer shares her thoughts on this season of couture.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/letter-from-the-couture/', 'snippet': 'A writer shares her thoughts on this season of couture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was young and living just outside Paris, I became obsessed with the odd, exquisite and endangered, which included the haute couture ateliers that I’d only read about in books. I named a stuffed frog Gripoix, probably because it sounded somehow a bit like crapaud, the French word for toad, but more elegant. There was the chance that the toy would turn into a prince, and in that case, I would need to go to Chanel to have a dress made for our fairy-tale wedding. This season was my first at couture as an adult — one married to a man, not an amphibian — with a mature perspective on whimsy. Everything is so fast now, but the atelier creations are the sartorial version of slow food. The work is green and ethical: there is no waste, as each piece is custom-made with a client in mind (no discount racks or fast fashion to fill landfills). Every artisan is properly treated. Can we say the same of certain mass-manufactured goods?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letter From the Couture', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T13:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie La Cava', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Cava', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7059f0d-6984-5df5-9f0b-d37c26b739f9', 'word_count': 980, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7059f0d-6984-5df5-9f0b-d37c26b739f9'}, {'abstract': 'The employment report: even worse than you think.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/falling-wages/', 'snippet': 'The employment report: even worse than you think.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ugh. That was a seriously ugly jobs report (pdf). Almost no job creation, with slow private-sector growth offset by falling public-sector employment; a falling employment-population ratio; and (I don’t know how many people have picked this up), an actual decline in wages, albeit a small one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Falling Wages', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T13:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/275b129e-6867-539a-b6af-e16e6d8f5a76', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/275b129e-6867-539a-b6af-e16e6d8f5a76'}, {'abstract': 'In his Central Park West studio, the artist recalled his encounters with heroic athletes like Ali and Namath, who were also his subjects.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/leroy-neiman-still-bright-and-bold-and-name-dropping-at-90/', 'snippet': 'In his Central Park West studio, the artist recalled his encounters with heroic athletes like Ali and Namath, who were also his subjects.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Do you mind?” LeRoy Neiman asks as he leans forward in his painting studio and reaches for a great-smelling cigar.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'LeRoy Neiman, Still Bright and Bold and Name-Dropping at 90', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Neiman, LeRoy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T13:10:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/873ac774-1c20-55eb-847d-1c5a421cd454', 'word_count': 690, 'uri': 'nyt://article/873ac774-1c20-55eb-847d-1c5a421cd454'}, {'abstract': 'The original quarter-munching, index-finger-jamming arcade game features such characters as the alien that flaps its wings up and down and the boxlike tank that slides from side to side at the bottom of the screen.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/hollywood-takes-aim-at-a-space-invaders-movie/', 'snippet': 'The original quarter-munching, index-finger-jamming arcade game features such characters as the alien that flaps its wings up and down and the boxlike tank that slides from side to side at the bottom of the screen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until the studios figure out a way to make a movie version of Pong, the primordial video game that features two blocks knocking a ball of pixels around the screen, it seems Hollywood has found the most, shall we say, minimalist source material for a game-to-film adaptation: Space Invaders, the quarter-munching, index-finger-jamming classic that first touched down in arcades in 1978, is being prepared for the big screen, The Hollywood Reporter said. Lorenzo di Bonaventura, a producer of the “Transformers” and “G. I. Joe” movie franchises, and Gigi Pritzker of Odd Lot Entertainment have optioned the film rights to Space Invaders, and boy do they have their work cut out for them: whereas even a Pac-Man movie would give you a built-in roster of dramatis personae (Jack Black as the pellet-gobbling hero, anyone?), Space Invaders features such characters as the alien that flaps its wings up and down and the boxlike tank that slides from side to side at the bottom of the screen. The producers did not immediately announce a creative team for the movie, nor how much money they will spend to recreate the level where the Space Invaders turn invisible. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hollywood Takes Aim at a Space Invaders Movie', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Jack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Di Bonaventura, Lorenzo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T13:11:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cf76dbe-3cba-5a3d-9715-68f11f2c478b', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cf76dbe-3cba-5a3d-9715-68f11f2c478b'}, {'abstract': 'The acquisition of Gulf Stream Asset Management will bolster the private-equity giant’s loan management business.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/apollo-buys-corporate-credit-firm/', 'snippet': 'The acquisition of Gulf Stream Asset Management will bolster the private-equity giant’s loan management business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apollo Global Management said on Friday that it had agreed to acquire Gulf Stream Asset Management, a corporate credit investor based in Charlotte, N.C.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apollo Buys Corporate Credit Firm', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apollo Global Management', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T13:13:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61b2cc17-6ff3-51f5-b2c8-73f87018dcff', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61b2cc17-6ff3-51f5-b2c8-73f87018dcff'}, {'abstract': 'Prime Minister David Cameron of Britain called for two separate public inquiries into British newspapers in the wake of a scandal at a British tabloid once edited by his own former spokesman.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/statement-by-cameron-on-press-inquiries/', 'snippet': 'Prime Minister David Cameron of Britain called for two separate public inquiries into British newspapers in the wake of a scandal at a British tabloid once edited by his own former spokesman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Following the arrest of a former tabloid editor who was until January his spokesman, Prime Minister David Cameron of Britain called on Friday for two separate investigations into the specific misconduct at The News of the World and the need for better regulation of newspapers, as my colleagues John Burns and Alan Cowell report.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Statement by Cameron on Press Inquiries', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Rebekah', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cameron, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coulson, Andy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T13:15:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c0a0788-00b6-5ec1-882f-4cdfc7d3aa08', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c0a0788-00b6-5ec1-882f-4cdfc7d3aa08'}, {'abstract': 'Despite predictions to the contrary, the share of adults with jobs is now at its lowest level since the recession began.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/overly-optimistic-once-again/', 'snippet': 'Despite predictions to the contrary, the share of adults with jobs is now at its lowest level since the recession began.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This morning’s jobs report is terrible. For the second straight month, the economy created only a tiny number of jobs — 18,000 in June, 25,000 in May. Both numbers are far below what’s needed to keep up with normal population growth (roughly 125,000 jobs).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Overly Optimistic, Once Again', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T13:19:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24918ce1-3e39-594f-bc0a-e07ac976cb55', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24918ce1-3e39-594f-bc0a-e07ac976cb55'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/morning-take-out-281/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOP STORIES ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T13:39:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b91e118a-d2d4-5b63-9af6-6ca6db24ca86', 'word_count': 1818, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b91e118a-d2d4-5b63-9af6-6ca6db24ca86'}, {'abstract': 'In Hama, Syria’s fourth-largest city, tens of thousands of Syrians protested as the French and American ambassadors remained there in a gesture of support.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/world/middleeast/09syria.html', 'snippet': 'In Hama, Syria’s fourth-largest city, tens of thousands of Syrians protested as the French and American ambassadors remained there in a gesture of support.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — Tens of thousands of Syrians on Friday poured into a square that has emerged as a focus of defiance in Hama, Syria’s fourth-largest city, as the French and American ambassadors stayed there for a second day in what their countries called a gesture of support for demonstrators and Syrian officials lambasted as interference.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/09/world/middleeast/09syria/09syria-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/09/world/middleeast/09syria/09syria-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/09/world/middleeast/09syria/09syria-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Envoys Stay in Syrian City Where Protests Continue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headlin