e_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bdd1cff-352f-5234-b0cb-2c2b5092e0e7', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bdd1cff-352f-5234-b0cb-2c2b5092e0e7'}, {'abstract': 'People who have followed the Dominique Strauss-Kahn case have been left to speculate and, if they dare, to draw their own conclusions; City Room readers are asked to share their thoughts.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/what-do-you-think-happened-in-room-2806/', 'snippet': 'People who have followed the Dominique Strauss-Kahn case have been left to speculate and, if they dare, to draw their own conclusions; City Room readers are asked to share their thoughts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whatever actually transpired between Dominique Strauss-Kahn and a 32-year-old housekeeper in Room 2806 of the Sofitel may ultimately be known only to the two of them, barring the unlikely possibility that they both tell all and their accounts match.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weighing Different Versions of Hotel Encounter', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:17:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f7463de-62ff-5617-aa62-2d898ecc63b2', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f7463de-62ff-5617-aa62-2d898ecc63b2'}, {'abstract': 'Eva Gabrielsson recalls her 32-year partnership with the best-selling author of the Millennium trilogy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/book-review-there-are-things-i-want-you-to-know-about-stieg-larsson-and-me-by-eva-gabrielsson.html', 'snippet': 'Eva Gabrielsson recalls her 32-year partnership with the best-selling author of the Millennium trilogy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eva Gabrielsson and Stieg Larsson spent 32 years together in Sweden and were soul mates, collaborators and fellow travelers. But one thing they were not was husband and wife, a fact that became critical when Larsson died unexpectedly in 2004 at the age of 50. That Larsson wrote an improbably successful trilogy of novels that began with “The Girl With the Dragon Tattoo” and went on to sell more than 50 million books worldwide complicated every aspect of his passing.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/CARR/CARR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 632, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/CARR/CARR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/CARR/CARR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering Stieg Larsson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scenes From an Unmarriage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sweden', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larsson, Stieg', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gabrielsson, Eva', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:27:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e4a6fb6-1dad-5e30-8eee-426c23c0aa50', 'word_count': 1194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e4a6fb6-1dad-5e30-8eee-426c23c0aa50'}, {'abstract': 'A pair of biographies about the trial lawyer Clarence Darrow draw on newly unearthed documents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/book-review-biographies-of-clarence-darrow-by-andrew-e-kersten-and-john-a-farrell.html', 'snippet': 'A pair of biographies about the trial lawyer Clarence Darrow draw on newly unearthed documents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early on the evening of Oct. 28, 1893, 25-year-old Patrick Prendergast came calling at the home of Mayor Carter Harrison of Chicago. The maid asked him to wait in the foyer while she fetched His Honor. But he ran past her and headed down the hall. The commotion drew Harrison out of his library. There was a confrontation. Words were exchanged. Then Prendergast pulled out a revolver and shot the mayor dead.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/bkr-Boyle-t_CA0/bkr-Boyle-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 346, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/bkr-Boyle-t_CA0/bkr-Boyle-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/bkr-Boyle-t_CA0/bkr-Boyle-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Clarence Darrow, Equal Opportunity Defender', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Equal Opportunity Defender', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Darrow, Clarence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:27:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Boyle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc7421e9-5092-5694-8566-5f74bb0ebcf4', 'word_count': 1205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc7421e9-5092-5694-8566-5f74bb0ebcf4'}, {'abstract': 'Cuts by state and local governments have cost the economy about 1 million jobs over the past two years.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/the-cost-of-austerity/', 'snippet': 'Cuts by state and local governments have cost the economy about 1 million jobs over the past two years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The main problem with the job market is the lack of hiring by private employers. Above all, they aren’t sure what the future will bring.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Cost of Austerity', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:28:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41ad9791-0125-53af-bded-044ae9abb889', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41ad9791-0125-53af-bded-044ae9abb889'}, {'abstract': 'Two biographies examine the spiritedness and formidable success of the Abstract Expressionist painters Lee Krasner and Joan Mitchell.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/book-review-biographies-of-lee-krasner-and-joan-mitchell.html', 'snippet': 'Two biographies examine the spiritedness and formidable success of the Abstract Expressionist painters Lee Krasner and Joan Mitchell.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Biographers are forever readjusting our sense of the fantastically mutable relationship between an individual and a society. All men and women are shaped by the world in which they live, and it remains unclear to what extent even the figures who loom largest, a Churchill or a Proust, have precipitated or only crystallized the values or visions with which they are now inevitably associated. “Another time has other lives to live,” W. H. Auden once observed. True enough. But to what extent can a person transform a particular time, even turn it into another time? The question is anything but academic. And when a biographer’s subject is a 20th-century woman who wants to make her mark in a field hitherto mostly open to men, the question becomes almost alarmingly urgent. Is the story really about a society that has changed enough that a woman can take the next step? Are there cases where the personal triumph actually brings about a social transformation? Or do many of these stories end in a standoff, the woman simultaneously victor and victim, embodying within a society new possibilities that remain in many respects impossibilities? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Perl/Perl-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 886, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Perl/Perl-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Perl/Perl-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lee Krasner and Joan Mitchell: Abstract Expressionist Lives', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Freedom of Expressionism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levin, Gail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krasner, Lee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pollock, Jackson', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mitchell, Joan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Albers, Patricia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:28:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jed Perl', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perl', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae8f2763-7da9-51fe-b98c-648230ca1529', 'word_count': 2794, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae8f2763-7da9-51fe-b98c-648230ca1529'}, {'abstract': 'A provocative and lively collection of lectures and essays about the intersection of race and sports, from a professor of American culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/book-review-a-level-playing-field-by-gerald-l-early.html', 'snippet': 'A provocative and lively collection of lectures and essays about the intersection of race and sports, from a professor of American culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The intersection of race and sports is one of the most dangerous in American culture. Even the crash-proof Rush Limbaugh cracked up on that curve while actually saying something that was essentially true. Al Campanis, the Los Angeles Dodgers executive who declared that African-Americans “may not have some of the necessities” to be baseball managers or general managers, and Jimmy (the Greek) Snyder, the CBS gambler in residence who said that blacks were better athletes than whites because they had been so bred since slavery, crashed and were carted off forever.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Lipsyte/Lipsyte-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Lipsyte/Lipsyte-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Lipsyte/Lipsyte-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tracing the Color Line on the Playing Field', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sports as Politics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:28:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Lipsyte', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lipsyte', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8895943-4f66-560a-99ad-8f1efa398ae3', 'word_count': 934, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8895943-4f66-560a-99ad-8f1efa398ae3'}, {'abstract': 'Breaking the so-called golden handcuffs of finance can be tough, but several Goldman Sachs have successfully left the fold to pursue their passions.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/riffing-on-life-after-goldman-sachs/', 'snippet': 'Breaking the so-called golden handcuffs of finance can be tough, but several Goldman Sachs have successfully left the fold to pursue their passions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Breaking the so-called golden handcuffs of finance can be tough. But a recent DealBook article highlighted several Goldman Sachs dropouts who have successfully left the fold to pursue their passions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Riffing on Life After Goldman Sachs', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:30:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e70fc2f7-9818-572b-81d5-059d153356f5', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e70fc2f7-9818-572b-81d5-059d153356f5'}, {'abstract': 'Through one family’s ordeal, the Salvadoran novelist Horacio Castellanos Moya depicts a country in the grip of a despot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/book-review-tyrant-memory-by-horacio-castellanos-moya.html', 'snippet': 'Through one family’s ordeal, the Salvadoran novelist Horacio Castellanos Moya depicts a country in the grip of a despot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In one of Horacio Castellanos Moya’s novels, “Revulsion: Thomas Bernhard in San Salvador” (1997), a Salvadoran returns home after 18 years abroad and finds that everything enrages him: what people eat; what they read (or don’t — the university has closed its literature department); what’s become of the leftist opposition (“rats that traded the military uniform of the guerrilla for a suit and a tie”); the statue of the president (“a criminal psychopath killer of archbishops turned into a heroic forefather”). Castellanos Moya would later say he sought to reveal how “being under the pressure of violence is a kind of national sickness.” His diagnosis was sharp enough to earn him death threats.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Glaser/Glaser-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1020, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Glaser/Glaser-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Glaser/Glaser-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Salvadoran Exile Novelist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lords of Misrule', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Coups D'Etat and Attempted Coups D'Etat", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'El Salvador', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:32:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Glaser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glaser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d83ebe48-ccb5-5b10-8731-d48f3a9abaa7', 'word_count': 1299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d83ebe48-ccb5-5b10-8731-d48f3a9abaa7'}, {'abstract': 'In Dana Spiotta’s novel, a woman struggles with the loss of her brother, an unrecognized rock star.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/book-review-stone-arabia-by-dana-spiotta.html', 'snippet': 'In Dana Spiotta’s novel, a woman struggles with the loss of her brother, an unrecognized rock star.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Google search of “Stone Arabia” reveals that it’s a small town in upstate New York populated primarily by the Amish. Why it was chosen as the title of Dana Spiotta’s third novel (her previous novel, “Eat the Document,” was a finalist for the National Book Award in 2006) isn’t clear until the book’s final 20 pages. In the meantime, the title rides along quietly on the cover, evocative, mysterious, incongruously poetic. So too is the narrative identity of the novel, which shifts fluidly from first to third person, from one sibling’s voice and point of view to another’s. The brother and sister at the heart of the book are Nik and Denise Kranis, who came of age in Los Angeles in the 1970s and are now in their late 40s; the novel is set in 2004, the year Denise loses her brother.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/CHRISTENSEN/CHRISTENSEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 904, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/CHRISTENSEN/CHRISTENSEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/CHRISTENSEN/CHRISTENSEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Imaginary Rock Star', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Her Brother’s Keeper', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spiotta, Dana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:33:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Christensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Christensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/36f26424-7b99-56a4-af26-02b00f19b985', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36f26424-7b99-56a4-af26-02b00f19b985'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Robotham’s thriller borrows from real events, like the financial crisis and the disappearance of billions of dollars in Iraq.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/book-review-the-wreckage-by-michael-robotham.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Robotham’s thriller borrows from real events, like the financial crisis and the disappearance of billions of dollars in Iraq.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On his Web site, the Australian novelist Michael Robotham is prominently identified as an “international crime writer,” but if you dig a little deeper, you’ll discover that he worked as a journalist on Fleet Street before turning to novel-writing in 2004. Since then, he’s written seven novels including his latest, “The Wreckage.” That’s seven books in seven years: writer’s block is one problem this guy doesn’t have.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/NOCERA/NOCERA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/NOCERA/NOCERA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/NOCERA/NOCERA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Thriller of Iraq and the Financial Crisis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Blood Money', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:33:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a558048-b88a-5b44-a7e8-38fa3e62efa9', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a558048-b88a-5b44-a7e8-38fa3e62efa9'}, {'abstract': 'A look at René Blum, who made a place in history running the Ballets Russes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/book-review-rene-blum-and-the-ballets-russes-by-judith-chazin-bennahum.html', 'snippet': 'A look at René Blum, who made a place in history running the Ballets Russes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who was René Blum? Today, he is perhaps best known as the brother of Léon Blum, who in 1936 became the first Socialist and Jewish prime minister of France. But René, who was close to his brother, also had his own wide-ranging and impressive life, in literature, art, theater and ballet. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/HOMANS/HOMANS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 901, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/HOMANS/HOMANS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/HOMANS/HOMANS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'René Blum: Life of a Dance Master', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dance Master', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ballets Russes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:33:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Homans', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Homans', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fd89ee7-a65c-54f2-a58f-fb0ac7de5dac', 'word_count': 1240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fd89ee7-a65c-54f2-a58f-fb0ac7de5dac'}, {'abstract': 'A magisterial study of the role of the French language in its 18th-century heyday, when it was it was still the international idiom of choice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/book-review-when-the-world-spoke-french-by-marc-fumaroli.html', 'snippet': 'A magisterial study of the role of the French language in its 18th-century heyday, when it was it was still the international idiom of choice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few months ago, WikiLeaks’ publication of confidential cables from American embassies around the world inspired a mock news item headlined “Sarkozy Admits French Language a Hoax.” According to this report, France’s diplomatic missives were revealed to have been written in English, leading the French president to confess that “the French really speak English, except in the presence of the British.” He went on to explain that the French language “was in fact complete gibberish,” invented by William the Conqueror’s troops during their invasion of England in order to “seem a bit more exotic” to the locals.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Weber/Weber-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 911, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Weber/Weber-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/Weber/Weber-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When French Was the Language of Enlightenment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Language of Enlightenment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Richard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Language', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:33:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Weber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/50a97882-26f5-502a-a493-eb29fa51cfe0', 'word_count': 1218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50a97882-26f5-502a-a493-eb29fa51cfe0'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama appeared largely alone in seeing the bad employment news as a call to boldness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/us/politics/09fiscal.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama appeared largely alone in seeing the bad employment news as a call to boldness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama’s drive for a supersize budget deal was further complicated on Friday by the release of unexpectedly weak employment figures, which Republicans seized on to bolster their arguments against possible tax increases and Democrats said were reason to limit painful spending cuts.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/09/us/FISCAL/FISCAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 801, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/09/us/FISCAL/FISCAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/09/us/FISCAL/FISCAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jobs Report Reinforces Parties in Deficit Talks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jobs Report Reinforces Parties in Deficit Talks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:33:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17a30fe0-c1c2-563a-9f1c-a7fe2de4efb8', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17a30fe0-c1c2-563a-9f1c-a7fe2de4efb8'}, {'abstract': 'Joe Levin, a practicing Orthodox Jew, does everything from background checks for marital arrangements to investigations into international banks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/nyregion/hasidic-private-eyes-beat-is-the-mean-streets-of-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'Joe Levin, a practicing Orthodox Jew, does everything from background checks for marital arrangements to investigations into international banks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JOE LEVIN, a private investigator in Brooklyn, was waiting to meet a new client in the parking lot of a kosher supermarket in Borough Park one recent morning. Glancing in the side-view mirror of his chauffeured sport utility vehicle, Mr. Levin said he liked this particular spot because he knew the manager, the delivery man and the security guard, who lets him borrow footage from the lot’s surveillance equipment. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/nyregion/10PRIVATE-EYE/10PRIVATE-EYE-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/10/nyregion/10PRIVATE-EYE/10PRIVATE-EYE-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/nyregion/10PRIVATE-EYE/10PRIVATE-EYE-jumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/nyregion/10PRIVATE-EYE/10PRIVATE-EYE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/nyregion/10PRIVATE-EYE/10PRIVATE-EYE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hasidic Sleuth’s Beat: Mean Streets of Brooklyn', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Private Eye Wears a Skullcap', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hasidism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detectives (Private)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:34:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jed Lipinski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lipinski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b111631-1ddf-51be-831b-dd7cead8429a', 'word_count': 1605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b111631-1ddf-51be-831b-dd7cead8429a'}, {'abstract': 'Orlando Figes explains how the Crimean War, a major turning point in European and Middle Eastern history, resonates today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/book-review-the-crimean-war-a-history-by-orlando-figes.html', 'snippet': 'Orlando Figes explains how the Crimean War, a major turning point in European and Middle Eastern history, resonates today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Crimean War was the first major war to be covered by professional foreign correspondents, who reported on the disastrous blundering of commanders and the horrors of medical treatment at the battlefront. Today, we remember fragmentary stories: the charge of the Light Brigade, symbolizing the blundering; Florence Nightingale, for the medical treatment. But the real war has faded away, eclipsed by the two vastly worse world wars that were to come.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/BASS/BASS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/BASS/BASS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/BASS/BASS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why the Crimean War Matters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For God and Nation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Balkan States', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Figes, Orlando', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ottoman Empire', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:35:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary J. Bass', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Bass', 'qualifier': None, 'tit