 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Real Werner Erhard', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Real Werner Erhard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:35:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a7a34e4-670a-5696-8a2d-9d913813b2e0', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a7a34e4-670a-5696-8a2d-9d913813b2e0'}, {'abstract': 'Letter in response to Evelyn McDonnell’s review of “Out of the Vinyl Deeps: Ellen Willis on Rock Music.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/listen-to-the-originals.html', 'snippet': 'Letter in response to Evelyn McDonnell’s review of “Out of the Vinyl Deeps: Ellen Willis on Rock Music.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Listen to the Originals', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Listen To the Originals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:35:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/6faab461-658b-5696-993c-7a7d67c35371', 'word_count': 61, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6faab461-658b-5696-993c-7a7d67c35371'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A WORLD ON FIRE: Britain’s Crucial Role in the American Civil War,  by Amanda Foreman (Random House, $35.) While Union and Confederate guns blazed, a battle was also being waged for English hearts and minds, at both the elite and popular levels.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books Of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:36:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/bbe51b44-ebf2-5124-8b09-14faee7a2ab6', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bbe51b44-ebf2-5124-8b09-14faee7a2ab6'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FAITHFUL PLACE,  by Tana French (Penguin, $16.) Like her two previous mysteries, “In the Woods” (2007) and “The Likeness” (2008), French’s latest is set in Dublin and steeped in local history. When a 22-year-old murder comes to light, Frank Mackey, a tough-minded detective, is pulled back to his poor but proud neighborhood and the family entanglements he’d wisely left behind years ago.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:36:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/7222279c-89ad-5b77-963a-df3b442921d4', 'word_count': 512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7222279c-89ad-5b77-963a-df3b442921d4'}, {'abstract': 'Should serial best-seller writers be banned from the list after one appearance, to make way for new blood? The authors of the latest grisly crime thriller out of Sweden might say yes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Should serial best-seller writers be banned from the list after one appearance, to make way for new blood? The authors of the latest grisly crime thriller out of Sweden might say yes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRESH FACES: Frequent readers of this column may have heard occasional grumbling about the lack of new writers on the fiction list, which is increasingly dominated by genre writers with well-oiled marketing machines and multiple previous hits under their belts. In a recent essay in Book\xadforum, the critic Michael Dirda went further, arguing that authors should be disqualified from the list after one appearance, to give newcomers a fairer shot at commercial glory. By promoting the same writers week after week, Dirda wrote, the list currently functions as “a restraint of trade, a visible hand that crushes the life out of the \xadliterary marketplace.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/17insidelist/17insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/17insidelist/17insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:36:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9aaad2dd-31c1-5e9f-b101-4d66bf63184a', 'word_count': 585, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9aaad2dd-31c1-5e9f-b101-4d66bf63184a'}, {'abstract': 'All writers are mystery writers, mucking around in a world studded with clues, in pursuit of bad guys.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/books/review/the-writer-as-detective.html', 'snippet': 'All writers are mystery writers, mucking around in a world studded with clues, in pursuit of bad guys.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since 9-year-olds didn’t wear suit jackets, I had to carry my revolver in a jury-rigged shoulder holster under my polo shirt. The look was that of a kid who had just snitched a mango from a fruit stand, and was unsuccessfully trying to conceal it. The cap gun was cold against my chest, yet I maintained a grim, professional demeanor, lest my suspects spot any weakness and get the upper hand. I trailed them among the secret stores and wholesale houses of New York’s East 20s, a neither-here-nor-there area north of Gramercy Park, where I lived. The place looked innocuous enough, but was clearly teeming with crime. The businessmen I shadowed also looked harmless, to anyone but me. I trailed them at short distances, making it easy for them to notice me, because if the killer did not know he was being followed, no one else would either. I saw myself as acting simultaneously in real time and in a film noir, so I was both tracking my quarry and watching myself do it. For his part, the killer, sensing danger, would turn around from time to time, confused and annoyed at being pursued by a kid with a mango in his shirt.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/bkr-Rosenblatt-t_CA0/bkr-Rosenblatt-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 614, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/bkr-Rosenblatt-t_CA0/bkr-Rosenblatt-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/books/review/bkr-Rosenblatt-t_CA0/bkr-Rosenblatt-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Writer as Detective', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Spooky Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detectives (Private)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:36:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roger Rosenblatt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roger', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenblatt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/129d2c61-a597-5441-abf2-cbb5e9c79ff2', 'word_count': 1419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/129d2c61-a597-5441-abf2-cbb5e9c79ff2'}, {'abstract': 'Investment advisers agree that political and financial upheavals will continue for a time, and a long view may be the best way to navigate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/your-money/investors-urged-to-keep-eyes-on-the-horizon-in-a-choppy-economy.html', 'snippet': 'Investment advisers agree that political and financial upheavals will continue for a time, and a long view may be the best way to navigate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE investment professionals I have consulted in each of the last two quarters are united today in one thing: taking the long view may be the best way to navigate a potentially bumpy second half of the year.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/09/your-money/09wealth/09wealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/09/your-money/09wealth/09wealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/09/your-money/09wealth/09wealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Advisers Keep Eyes on the Horizon in a Choppy Economy', 'kicker': 'Wealth Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Advisers Keep Eyes on the Horizon in a Choppy Economy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stern, Marc D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Net Worth Individuals', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:37:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c46204b8-4706-59c8-abce-390aae45aa02', 'word_count': 1320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c46204b8-4706-59c8-abce-390aae45aa02'}, {'abstract': 'Arriving in London Thursday afternoon, after seeing the couture collections in Paris, was a surreal experience.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/hermione-granger-fashion-plate/', 'snippet': 'Arriving in London Thursday afternoon, after seeing the couture collections in Paris, was a surreal experience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Arriving in London Thursday afternoon, after seeing the couture collections in Paris, was a surreal experience — and not just because of the breaking news that The News of the World was being shut down by Rupert Murdoch because of the hacking scandal. Outside my hotel, thousands of teenagers were swarming their way toward Leicester Square, hundreds of policemen herding them on their way, and at least three or four helicopters hovering overhead. What was going on?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hermione Granger: Fashion Plate', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watson, Emma', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows: Part 2 (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:37:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Emmrich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Emmrich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63ecfbd4-a52e-5de4-8b3e-2308546b4a29', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63ecfbd4-a52e-5de4-8b3e-2308546b4a29'}, {'abstract': 'Though thunderstorms threatened, Atlantis was launched for NASA’s 135th and final shuttle flight, bound for the International Space Station with four astronauts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/science/space/09shuttle.html', 'snippet': 'Though thunderstorms threatened, Atlantis was launched for NASA’s 135th and final shuttle flight, bound for the International Space Station with four astronauts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KENNEDY SPACE CENTER, Fla. — For the last time, the engines of a space shuttle roared, the ground rumbled, and the shuttle Atlantis rose off the launching pad and disappeared into the clouds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/09/us/09shuttle_span/09shuttle_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/09/us/09shuttle_span/09shuttle_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/09/us/09shuttle_span/09shuttle_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/09/us/09shuttle_span/09shuttle_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/09/us/09shuttle_span/09shuttle_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Atlantis Lifts Off for Last Space Shuttle Mission', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Atlantis Lifts Off for Last Space Shuttle Mission', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Atlantis (Space Shuttle)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'SPACE SHUTTLE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:43:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Space & Cosmos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenneth Chang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenneth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abd0e26e-37c1-5cf4-9bf6-f54644047026', 'word_count': 764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abd0e26e-37c1-5cf4-9bf6-f54644047026'}, {'abstract': 'N.H.T.S.A. is toughening its stance on the creation of a standard alert sound when vehicles are running purely on battery power.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/safety-agency-takes-step-to-make-hybrids-and-e-v-s-noisier/', 'snippet': 'N.H.T.S.A. is toughening its stance on the creation of a standard alert sound when vehicles are running purely on battery power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration announced Thursday that it proposed regulations to protect pedestrians and people with visual impairments from accidents involving hybrids and purely electric vehicles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Safety Agency Takes Step to Make Hybrids and E.V.’s Noisier', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nissan Motor Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:44:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cheryl Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cheryl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8dbdfd7e-861b-5ea6-8521-274c42fbabec', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8dbdfd7e-861b-5ea6-8521-274c42fbabec'}, {'abstract': 'A federal appeals court vacated a preliminary injunction and ruled that the lockout in the N.F.L. could continue, a victory for owners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/sports/football/appeals-court-rules-nfl-lockout-is-legal.html', 'snippet': 'A federal appeals court vacated a preliminary injunction and ruled that the lockout in the N.F.L. could continue, a victory for owners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Progress toward a new N.F.L. labor deal slowed Friday when a federal appeals court gave the league a narrow victory, ruling that its lockout of players could continue. But the court left open the question of whether the N.F.L. could be sued for antitrust violations. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Court Rules Lockout by N.F.L. Can Go On', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Court Rules Lockout By N.F.L. Can Go On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:48:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Battista', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Battista', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f74deb87-0c76-5b6e-a926-5ad0a774686d', 'word_count': 713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f74deb87-0c76-5b6e-a926-5ad0a774686d'}, {'abstract': 'Taking photos, and more, while pregnant.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/pregnancy-as-art/', 'snippet': 'Taking photos, and more, while pregnant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thursday, David Beckham posted a photo to his Facebook page of his wife, Victoria, pregnant (and stunning) in a bikini. It was one of the few public shots of the singer while pregnant. As she told Women’s Wear Daily last month: “I’m not really one of these people that likes to go out and pose and flaunt being pregnant. Not like there’s anything wrong in that. I’m so proud to be pregnant and I feel so blessed and so happy. I really do. But I’m just not that kind of person. So, no, I won’t be taking my clothes off. I don’t think anyone needs to see that, other than my husband. Absolutely not.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pregnancy as Art', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Obstetrics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:48:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11a48ef8-5c48-5aee-a036-d839fcd7d7ce', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11a48ef8-5c48-5aee-a036-d839fcd7d7ce'}, {'abstract': 'It never occurred to me that my military lifestyle was a foreign concept to civilians until a conversation I shared with a nonmilitary friend during my husband’s second combat deployment.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/a-spouse-confronts-the-military-civilian-divide/', 'snippet': 'It never occurred to me that my military lifestyle was a foreign concept to civilians until a conversation I shared with a nonmilitary friend during my husband’s second combat deployment.', 'lead_paragraph': ' As long as I’ve been a military spouse, I’ve always lived in a military town, surrounded by military families. Typical discussions with friends revolve around the trials and tribulations of deployments, packing and moving, and our husbands’ unpredictable work schedules. I’m blanketed with empathy, and I never have to articulate my feelings because those in the military community understand.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Spouse Confronts the Military-Civilian Divide', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Blue Star Families', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T15:54:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Sweeney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sweeney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7ac55eb-5e5c-5542-90e8-cb8f2a77ab93', 'word_count': 1128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7ac55eb-5e5c-5542-90e8-cb8f2a77ab93'}, {'abstract': "In this homemade video, Naima J. Cook and Ben W. Shephard recall how a friend's insistence led to their fateful encounter.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/fashion/weddings/100000000899460/naima--ben.html', 'snippet': "In this homemade video, Naima J. Cook and Ben W. Shephard recall how a friend's insistence led to their fateful encounter.", 'lead_paragraph': "In this homemade video, Naima J. Cook and Ben W. Shephard recall how a friend's insistence led to their fateful encounter.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/fashion/vid