ginal': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/290dffca-6bc8-536e-98e1-61826572d524', 'word_count': 7, 'uri': 'nyt://article/290dffca-6bc8-536e-98e1-61826572d524'}, {'abstract': 'Suggestions are pouring in about whether (and how) to dispose of a trove discovered in a Hindu temple.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/world/asia/09temple.html', 'snippet': 'Suggestions are pouring in about whether (and how) to dispose of a trove discovered in a Hindu temple.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THIRUVANANTHAPURAM, India — What should India, rising but still plagued by poverty, do with a newly discovered treasure of gold coins, statues and jewels in the vault of a Hindu temple, valued at some $22 billion?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/09/world/asia/09temple/09temple-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A $22 Billion Question for India: What to Do With a Treasure?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A $22 Billion Question for India: What to Do With a Treasure?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jewels and Jewelry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kerala (India)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T21:14:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vikas Bajaj', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vikas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bajaj', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ccd0c78-5591-584a-acba-e485c575a533', 'word_count': 1063, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ccd0c78-5591-584a-acba-e485c575a533'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists on expedition in the Philippines discover new echinoderms deep in the sea, along with plastic discarded by humans.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/chasing-down-sea-lilies/', 'snippet': 'Scientists on expedition in the Philippines discover new echinoderms deep in the sea, along with plastic discarded by humans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rich Mooi, curator of invertebrate zoology and geology at the California Academy of Sciences, writes about his expedition to the Philippines, where he surveyed echinoderms off the coast of Luzon Island.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chasing Down Sea Lilies', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'California Academy of Sciences', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philippines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T21:14:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rich Mooi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rich', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mooi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d51a53cd-927c-59a1-b0b9-395cdb1060ac', 'word_count': 1200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d51a53cd-927c-59a1-b0b9-395cdb1060ac'}, {'abstract': 'Using the small artichokes from foggy coastal California, you can make garlicky Roman-style artichokes quite effortlessly at home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/dining/cooking-baby-artichokes-city-kitchen.html', 'snippet': 'Using the small artichokes from foggy coastal California, you can make garlicky Roman-style artichokes quite effortlessly at home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN Italy, artichokes are almost as common as onions, so normal and necessary are they considered. Every little vegetable shop has a crate of artichokes sitting casually by the front door, and entire stalls are devoted to them at the market. A pile of violet-tinged freshly picked beauties with their silvery leaves is quite an enticing sight. And the stalls come complete with little old ladies who will trim and prepare them for you if you want to pay a little extra. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/dining/13kitch1_span/13kitch1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/13/dining/13kitch1_span/13kitch1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/dining/13kitch1_span/KITCH-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/dining/13kitch1_span/13kitch1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/13/dining/13kitch1_span/13kitch1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bringing Up Baby Artichoke', 'kicker': 'City Kitchen', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bringing Up Baby Artichoke', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Artichokes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T21:22:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Tanis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd6b4576-ff16-5928-8bfe-5198dc04fa74', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd6b4576-ff16-5928-8bfe-5198dc04fa74'}, {'abstract': 'Robert and Michelle King speculate about the pressures faced by wives of errant politicians as they decide whether to forgive their husbands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/fashion/creators-of-cbss-good-wife-on-forgiveness.html', 'snippet': 'Robert and Michelle King speculate about the pressures faced by wives of errant politicians as they decide whether to forgive their husbands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two years ago, “The Good Wife,” a CBS drama, opened with a familiar tableau: a politician apologizing for sexual peccadilloes. By his side, also caught in the glare of cameras, was his pale, stone-faced wife. What was going through her mind?\xa0Since then,\xa0 politicians continue to\xa0face the cameras asking forgiveness. But wives have become conspicuous by their absence; the public no longer expects them to stand by their philanderers. What are these wives thinking? The show’s creators, Robert and Michelle King, who write together and have been married since 1987, speculate about the\xa0pressures\xa0 peculiar to the wives of errant politicians as they weigh whether to forgive their husbands.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/fashion/FORGIVENESS1/FORGIVENESS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/10/fashion/FORGIVENESS1/FORGIVENESS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/fashion/FORGIVENESS1/FORGIVENESS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/fashion/FORGIVENESS1/FORGIVENESS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/fashion/FORGIVENESS1/FORGIVENESS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Would ‘The Good Wife’ Do?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Would ‘The Good Wife’ Do?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADULTERY', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T21:24:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert King and Michelle King', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'King', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'King', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c059e881-0005-50c5-9a37-7e4e92387c25', 'word_count': 1048, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c059e881-0005-50c5-9a37-7e4e92387c25'}, {'abstract': 'The simple design of ticket stubs from 1983, found while emptying out some old boxes, is kind of charming, considering the fancy ones that are given out today.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/artifacts-of-a-simpler-and-cheaper-time/', 'snippet': 'The simple design of ticket stubs from 1983, found while emptying out some old boxes, is kind of charming, considering the fancy ones that are given out today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tom Jolly, our former sports editor, is the Associate Managing Editor of The New York Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artifacts of a Simpler, and Cheaper, Time', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Series', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baltimore Orioles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago White Sox', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia Phillies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baltimore (Md)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T21:26:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23ec31b2-ac94-5125-99b8-21ae6c8ba54f', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23ec31b2-ac94-5125-99b8-21ae6c8ba54f'}, {'abstract': 'Baby artichokes are easy to prepare.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/08/artichokes-sweet-young-things/', 'snippet': 'Baby artichokes are easy to prepare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Baby artichokes from foggy California are delicious and no trouble to prepare. Just peel a few outer petals to reveal the tender heart. To offset their natural sweetness, take a tip from Italy and pan-fry them for a tasty snack with drinks, an antipasto or light lunch, or an excellent accompaniment to simple grilled fish.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artichokes, Sweet Young Things', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Artichokes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-08T21:27:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Tanis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/031ccc47-7e81-5e04-995c-8eac7f4682a4', 'word_count': 111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/031ccc47-7e81-5e04-995c-8eac7f4682a4'}, {'abstract': "The Times's economics columnist David Leonhardt questions if President Obama is to blame for the slow job growth.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/business/economy/100000000937462/timescast--job-growth-falters.html', 'snippet': "The Times's economics columnist David Leonhardt questions if President Obama is to blame for the slow job growth.", 'lead_paragraph': "The Times's economics columnist David Leonhardt questions if President Obama is to blame for the slow job growth.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'cre