rts Briefing | Soccer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. to Face Mexico Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold Cup (Soccer)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-09T02:15:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By NYT', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nyt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2be16f3-f64a-52b4-a922-93141fd0d116', 'word_count': 75, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2be16f3-f64a-52b4-a922-93141fd0d116'}, {'abstract': 'An owner of the Blue Duck Bakery Café moved his bread-baking operation to Southold and found that the wild yeast floating around made it the perfect area in which to make bread.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/nyregion/for-blue-duck-bakery-on-long-island-yeast-in-the-air-helps.html', 'snippet': 'An owner of the Blue Duck Bakery Café moved his bread-baking operation to Southold and found that the wild yeast floating around made it the perfect area in which to make bread.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Keith Kouris, 54, opened the Blue Duck Bakery Café in Southampton with his wife, Nancy, in 1999, all the breads were made in the little kitchen in back. As the business grew — Blue Duck breads are now in markets and restaurants from Montauk to Manhattan — Mr. Kouris moved his bread-baking operation in 2008 to Southold.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yeast Is in the Air? Then Bake!', 'kicker': 'Quick Bite | Southampton and Southold', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yeast Is in the Air? Then Bake!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-09T02:22:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan M. Novick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b5ec167-7984-573c-9b99-6df043ba4539', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b5ec167-7984-573c-9b99-6df043ba4539'}, {'abstract': 'Mitch and Toni’s American Bistro is a new venture from two veterans of the much-missed Bistro M.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/nyregion/mitch-and-tonis-american-bistro-in-albertson-ny-review.html', 'snippet': 'Mitch and Toni’s American Bistro is a new venture from two veterans of the much-missed Bistro M.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN Bistro M closed in April, foodies across Long Island were unhappy. The tiny restaurant, nestled beside the railroad tracks in Glen Head, was one of the best around. But their dismay was short-lived, because in May, Mitchell SuDock, an owner and the executive chef of Bistro M, and Toni Contino, the dining room manager there, opened Mitch and Toni’s American Bistro.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/nyregion/10DINELI1/10DINELI1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 690, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/0