d_count': 1058, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bb38a7d-74e3-5057-a4d6-13826c15265e'}, {'abstract': 'President Mikheil Saakashvili’s administration denied Friday that the arrest this week of a group of photographers on suspicions of spying was made in retaliation for their journalistic work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/world/europe/09briefs-Georgiabrief.html', 'snippet': 'President Mikheil Saakashvili’s administration denied Friday that the arrest this week of a group of photographers on suspicions of spying was made in retaliation for their journalistic work.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Responding to concerns raised by local rights groups and photojournalists, President Mikheil Saakashvili’s administration denied Friday that the arrest this week of a group of photographers on suspicions of spying was made in retaliation for their journalistic work. Manana Manjgaladze, Mr. Saakashvili’s spokeswoman, said the case “is about a serious leakage of information from our institutions, not about journalism or media activities.” A civil rights group, the Georgian Young Lawyers’ Association, asked the Interior Ministry to publicly justify the secrecy concerning the case. One of the four remaining detainees, Giorgi Abdaladze, a freelancer, has announced a hunger strike, according to his lawyer. The police released a fifth, a photographer for The Associated Press, early Thursday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Georgia: Photographers Still Jailed', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Georgia: Photographers Still Jailed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia (Georgian Republic)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saakashvili, Mikheil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-09T04:18:15+0000'