: {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38d0a397-09c4-565b-864b-9c86067660f6', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38d0a397-09c4-565b-864b-9c86067660f6'}, {'abstract': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon give me a run for my money.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/tougher-cryptic/', 'snippet': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon give me a run for my money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Administrivial Chance-To-Win-Valuable-Prizes Update: If you are planning to be in the New York City area on or about the date of August 6 between the hours of 11AM and 5 PM, sign yourself up for Lollapuzzoola 4, the crossword tournament that is just about the most fun you can have with a sharp #2 pencil. Your hosts, Brian Cimmet and Patrick Blindauer, tell me that the constructors of the puzzles you will be wrestling to the ground are Mike Nothnagel, Elizabeth C. Gorski, Andrea Carla Michaels, Tony Orbach, Doug Peterson, and Byron Walden. There will also be non-tournament games by Neville Fogarty, Allison Cimmet and Todd McClary. Click here to sign yourself up for an amazingly fun day!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Variety: A Tougher-Than-Usual Cryptic', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-09T16:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ba03b71-c412-5dd5-ab55-0d33a35e4c9d', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ba03b71-c412-5dd5-ab55-0d33a35e4c9d'}, {'abstract': 'The disparity between the performance of China’s economy and its stocks illustrates the risk in mutual funds that invest in just one developing nation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/business/mutfund/chinas-growth-doesnt-ensure-mutual-fund-gains.html', 'snippet': 'The disparity between the performance of China’s economy and its stocks illustrates the risk in mutual funds that invest in just one developing nation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ECONOMIC superlatives abound about China. It’s the world’s biggest auto market. It’s the largest producer of steel and, of course, the largest exporter. Yet there has been nothing extraordinary lately about the performance of China-focused mutual funds.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/business/10mf-CHINA/10mf-CHINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/10/business/10mf-CHINA/10mf-CHINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/business/10mf-CHINA/10mf-CHINA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China’s Rapid Growth Doesn’t Ensure Stock Gains', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China’s Rapid Growth Doesn’t Ensure Stock Gains', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mutual Funds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World an