nternational House of Prayer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prayers and Prayer Books', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-09T22:43:00+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/3b0ec725-7c9a-535f-a69e-6ab2c1f11e5a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/3b0ec725-7c9a-535f-a69e-6ab2c1f11e5a'}, {'abstract': 'A Christian ministry has drawn thousands with its emphasis on perpetual prayer, but some say it has a cultlike atmosphere.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/us/10prayer.html', 'snippet': 'A Christian ministry has drawn thousands with its emphasis on perpetual prayer, but some say it has a cultlike atmosphere.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KANSAS CITY, Mo. — The worship music, throbbing soft-rock appeals performed by live bands, has continued here without pause, day and night, since May 1999. Voices calling to Jesus or pleading with God to help tornado victims or make Congress ban abortion resound in an auditorium that is the physical and spiritual heart of the International House of Prayer, a Christian ministry rapidly blossoming into a movement. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/us/PRAYER-1/PRAYER-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/10/us/PRAYER-1/PRAYER-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/us/PRAYER-1/PRAYER-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 646, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/us/PRAYER-1/PRAYER-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/us/PRAYER-1/PRAYER-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Worship Never Pauses', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where Worship Never Pauses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ministers (Protestant)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prayers and Prayer Books', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evangelical Movement', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas City (Mo)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-09T22:44:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1fd4a4d-fe44-5831-a048-1022ee3bda98', 'word_count': 1286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1fd4a4d-fe44-5831-a048-1022ee3bda98'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Montgomery, a right-hander drafted in the 11th round last month, struck out five Rome Braves in the ninth inning of his debut for Class A Charleston.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/15-strikes-1-inning/', 'snippet': 'Mark Montgomery, a right-hander drafted in the 11th round last month, struck out five Rome Braves in the ninth inning of his debut for Class A Charleston.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On June 24 against Colorado, A. J. Burnett became the first Yankee to record four strikeouts in an inning. A week later, a Yankees prospect outdid him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '15 Strikes, 1 Inning', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burnett, A J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-09T22:47:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/658c6c0e-510f-596c-a983-6f001686327a', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/658c6c0e-510f-596c-a983-6f001686327a'}, {'abstract': 'Tampa Bay’s David Price allowed Derek Jeter’s 3,000th career hit Saturday. But three years ago, he offered up another memorable moment against the Yankees captain.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/for-price-and-jeter-not-their-first-historic-meeting/', 'snippet': 'Tampa Bay’s David Price allowed Derek Jeter’s 3,000th career hit Saturday. But three years ago, he offered up another memorable moment against the Yankees captain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As of Saturday, the first and last home runs that the Tampa Bay Rays’ David Price has allowed in the majors have come off the bat of Derek Jeter. Price, of course, will give up more, but he has forever linked his name to Jeter’s as the pitcher who gave up the Yankee captain’s 3,000th career hit. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Price and Jeter, Not Their First Historic Meeting', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeter, Derek', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Price, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-09T22:52:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samantha Farlow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samantha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farlow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8616bbf-9694-5ec2-acc5-a205193b3da0', 'word_count': 347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8616bbf-9694-5ec2-acc5-a205193b3da0'}, {'abstract': 'Michelle Wie needed a 35-foot putt on the final hole Saturday to make the cut on the number at the United States Women’s Open.', 'web_url': 'https://onpar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/09/with-final-putt-wie-makes-the-cut/', 'snippet': 'Michelle Wie needed a 35-foot putt on the final hole Saturday to make the cut on the number at the United States Women’s Open.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COLORADO SPRINGS, Colo. — After she punctuated her second round with a 35-foot putt, Michelle Wie pumped her right arm as if she was hand-cranking ice cream. Wie stood over the putt on the final green knowing she had to drain it to have any chance of making the 36-hole cut at the United States Women’s Open.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With Final Putt, Wie Makes the Cut', 'kicker': 'On Par', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "United States Women's Open (Golf)", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'