d a double exhibition to Brassaï, the famed Hungarian artist born Gyula Halasz. One show focuses on street pieces by the artist, shown without context, the other on his relationships with his contemporaries. The shows, jointly titled “In the Studio and On the Street,” run through Aug. 28.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dual Berlin Shows Highlight the Photographer Brassaï', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brassai', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T10:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Moroz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moroz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efbd12b0-f4f4-5018-aa28-7a334f3dd195', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efbd12b0-f4f4-5018-aa28-7a334f3dd195'}, {'abstract': 'A New York Waterway ferry trip to Weehawken, N.J., called “a slaughterhouse” in the late 18th and early 19th centuries for duels, including the one that left Alexander Hamilton dying and Aaron Burr’s reputation in tatters.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/retracing-hamiltons-ride-to-fatal-duel-on-july-11-1804/', 'snippet': 'A New York Waterway ferry trip to Weehawken, N.J., called “a slaughterhouse” in the late 18th and early 19th centuries for duels, including the one that left Alexander Hamilton dying and Aaron Burr’s reputation in tatters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question was, have you ever taken a murderer across the Hudson?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'hea