: None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09dff304-ca24-5cad-b818-ad36637eae95', 'word_count': 84, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09dff304-ca24-5cad-b818-ad36637eae95'}, {'abstract': 'The Berenstain Bears of the durable children’s-book series come to life onstage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/theater/reviews/berenstain-bears-live-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Berenstain Bears of the durable children’s-book series come to life onstage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bear Country, which Stan and Jan Berenstain created for their popular Berenstain Bears children’s book series, resembles human sitcom land a few decades ago. Each story about the Bears — Mama, Papa and their cubs, generically named Brother and Sister — features a nagging problem that homespun wisdom neatly and sweetly solves. With titles like “The Berenstain Bears and the Messy Room” and “The Berenstain Bears and Too Much TV,” the books promise gentle laughs, lessons and escapades, but nothing too naughty — the Cosbys with fur.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/BERENSTEIN/BERENSTEIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bears With a Treehouse and Tidy Tales for Children', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Berenstain Bears Live!'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bears With a Treehouse And Tidy Tales for Children', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T18:50:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurel Graeber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graeber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/da41a1ef-58a4-5b3b-bc2f-f0ab3eaf6bb7', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da41a1ef-58a4-5b3b-bc2f-f0ab3eaf6bb7'}, {'abstract': 'Tax cuts and temporary aid.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/where-the-money-went/', 'snippet': 'Tax cuts and temporary aid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Somehow I missed the BEA’s very useful page tracking the Recovery Act and how it is translated into taxes and spending. (Thanks to the commenter who mentioned it). It’s especially useful for thinking about what the Obama stimulus really involved — and what it didn’t.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where The Money Went', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T18:54:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0286756c-98e4-5e51-bb5d-ac924c2eee21', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0286756c-98e4-5e51-bb5d-ac924c2eee21'}, {'abstract': 'A last-gasp goal on a header by Abby Wambach allowed the Americans to take their chances in a shootout, and they advanced to the semifinals against France.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/sports/soccer/2011-womens-world-cup-us-ousts-brazil-on-penalty-kicks.html', 'snippet': 'A last-gasp goal on a header by Abby Wambach allowed the Americans to take their chances in a shootout, and they advanced to the semifinals against France.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has become a defining characteristic of American soccer, the certitude that even when skill and tactics are insufficient, and referees’ decisions are contrary and damaging, somehow resolve can prevail. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/sports/11soccer-1/11soccer-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/sports/11soccer-1/11soccer-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/sports/11soccer-1/11soccer-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Grit and 10 Players, U.S. Women Oust Brazil', 'kicker': 'United States 2 (5), Brazil 2 (3), Penalty Kicks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Grit and 10 Players, U.S. Women Oust Brazil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wambach, Abby', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Solo, Hope', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "United States Women's National Soccer Team", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's World Cup (Soccer)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marta', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T18:54:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeré Longman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeré', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Longman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/106d6b18-99c6-5b09-9bd6-e75090811718', 'word_count': 1093, 'uri': 'nyt://article/106d6b18-99c6-5b09-9bd6-e75090811718'}, {'abstract': 'Newsmax has become a potent force by offering what its chief executive calls “news that Americans in the heartland would like to see.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/business/media/newsmax-a-compass-for-conservative-politics.html', 'snippet': 'Newsmax has become a potent force by offering what its chief executive calls “news that Americans in the heartland would like to see.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEST PALM BEACH, Fla. — Without the soupy South Florida humidity and the fastidiously clipped lawn, the Newsmax headquarters here would be a lot like one of those diners in New Hampshire and Iowa where autographed pictures of politicians cover the walls.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/business/media/11newsmax/11newsmax-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/business/media/11newsmax/11newsmax-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/business/media/11newsmax/11newsmax-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Compass for Conservative Politics', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Magazine Serves as a Compass To the Conservative Ins and Outs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magazines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T18:57:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e120fc03-2af0-5382-8e3f-418b4edf26d1', 'word_count': 1195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e120fc03-2af0-5382-8e3f-418b4edf26d1'}, {'abstract': 'An amazing comeback by the U.S. women’s national team Sunday against Brazil sends the Americans to the semifinals of the Women’s World Cup against France.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/womens-world-cup-u-s-wins-in-a-shootout/', 'snippet': 'An amazing comeback by the U.S. women’s national team Sunday against Brazil sends the Americans to the semifinals of the Women’s World Cup against France.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Down to perhaps its last gasp, the United States women’s national team got the tying goal from Abby Wambach on a late header to force a penalty-kick shootout Sunday and the Americans incongruously and incredibly eliminated Brazil in the quarterfinals of the Women’s World Cup in Dresden, Germany. “These girls just bring out the best in each other,” United States Coach Pia Sundhage told ESPN after the match.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Women’s World Cup: U.S. Wins in a Shootout', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's World Cup (Soccer)", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marta', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Solo, Hope', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sundhage, Pia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wambach, Abby', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "United States Women's National Soccer Team", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dresden (Germany)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T19:04:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32367619-616d-5d40-b9d9-3fdc26225880', 'word_count': 98, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32367619-616d-5d40-b9d9-3fdc26225880'}, {'abstract': '“Shock Value” tells the story of how the seminal shockers of the late 1960s and early ’70s came to be and how a handful of films and filmmakers brought the scary horror movies back to life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/books/shock-value-by-jason-zinoman-review.html', 'snippet': '“Shock Value” tells the story of how the seminal shockers of the late 1960s and early ’70s came to be and how a handful of films and filmmakers brought the scary horror movies back to life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a cultural commodity that got any respect, the horror movie was dead by the early 1970s. The collapse of the studio system, the aging of its stars, the decline of Grade-A monster movies like 1931’s “Frankenstein” into rubber-suit rip-offs aimed at the teenage trade — all were stakes driven into a once vibrant genre. By 1975 a scary movie was called by any other name. Time magazine’s cover story on “Jaws” ran 3,300 words, and not one of them was “horror.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 703, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sons of Rosemary’s Baby: Birth of the New Horror', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sons of Rosemary’s Baby: Birth of the New Horror', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zinoman, Jason', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T19:56:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ty Burr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ty', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c41164c-5845-5465-b1e0-3c9217979400', 'word_count': 1052, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c41164c-5845-5465-b1e0-3c9217979400'}, {'abstract': 'George T. M. Shackelford, chairman of art of Europe and curator of modern art at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, is moving to Texas to become senior deputy director of the Kimbell Art Museum in Fort Worth.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/a-job-change-for-boston-curator/', 'snippet': 'George T. M. Shackelford, chairman of art of Europe and curator of modern art at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, is moving to Texas to become senior deputy director of the Kimbell Art Museum in Fort Worth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'George T. M. Shackelford, chairman of art of Europe and curator of modern art at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, is moving to Texas to become senior deputy director of the Kimbell Art Museum in Fort Worth. A leading scholar of late 19th- and early 20th-century French art, Mr. Shackelford is co-organizing “Degas and the Nude,” with Xavier Rey, curator of paintings at the Musée d’Orsay in Paris. The show is scheduled to open on Oct. 9 in Boston before going to Paris. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Job Change for Boston Curator', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kimbell Art Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Fine Arts (Boston)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T20:23:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/988f771a-2034-5783-89ab-f06bb9e83cb1', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/988f771a-2034-5783-89ab-f06bb9e83cb1'}, {'abstract': 'Robinson Cano scored the only run thanks to two miscues by Tampa Bay, but Alex Rodriguez will miss 4-6 weeks after knee surgery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/sports/baseball/sabathia-dominates-and-yankees-beat-rays-on-two-bad-throws.html', 'snippet': 'Robinson Cano scored the only run thanks to two miscues by Tampa Bay, but Alex Rodriguez will miss 4-6 weeks after knee surgery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It had been a gorgeous weekend for the Yankees, with Friday night’s dreary rainout quickly brightened by the euphoria of Saturday’s Derek Jeter extravaganza. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/sports/baseball/11yanks-pic2/11yanks-pic2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/sports/baseball/11yanks-pic2/11yanks-pic2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/sports/baseball/11yanks-pic2/11yanks-pic2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Another Yankees Victory, News of a Big Loss', 'kicker': 'Yankees 1, Rays 0', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Another Yankees Victory, News of a Big Loss', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shields, James', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Upton, B J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodriguez, Alex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sabathia, C C', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tampa Bay Rays', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T20:25:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/796c4706-3dea-5d9a-b04b-50d8b10a052d', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/796c4706-3dea-5d9a-b04b-50d8b10a052d'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Roselli, a crooner from Hoboken, N.J., who was often overshadowed by Frank Sinatra, sang standards to adoring crowds for decades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/arts/music/jimmy-roselli-italian-american-singer-dies-at-85.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Roselli, a crooner from Hoboken, N.J., who was often overshadowed by Frank Sinatra, sang standards to adoring crowds for decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He was a skinny Italian-American kid from Hoboken, N.J., who could croon like an angel. Before long, his singing made women swoon and grown men cry. For decades, he sang standards to adoring crowds worldwide, including, notably, “My Way.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/arts/obit-roselli/obit-roselli-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1023, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/10/arts/obit-roselli/obit-roselli-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/10/arts/obit-roselli/obit-roselli-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jimmy Roselli, Italian-American Singer, Dies at 85', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jimmy Roselli, Hoboken’s Other Crooner, Dies at 85', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Italian-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roselli, Jimmy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T20:31:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margalit Fox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margalit', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/718ead85-5890-5744-8e0d-b9e27a72c8de', 'word_count': 917, 'uri': 'nyt://article/718ead85-5890-5744-8e0d-b9e27a72c8de'}, {'abstract': 'The medical establishment is seeing addiction more as a physical disease, and 10 medical schools have introduced residency programs in addiction medicine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/health/11addictions.html', 'snippet': 'The medical establishment is seeing addiction more as a physical disease, and 10 medical schools have introduced residency programs in addiction medicine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is an age-old debate over alcoholism: is the problem in the sufferer’s head — something that can be overcome through willpower, spirituality or talk therapy, perhaps — or is it a physical disease, one that needs continuing medical treatment in much the same way as, say, diabetes or epilepsy? ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/us/11addiction/11addiction-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/us/11addiction/11addiction-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/us/11addiction/11addiction-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rethinking Addiction’s Roots, and Its Treatment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Medicine Adds Slots for Study Of Addictions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alcohol Abuse', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Therapy and Rehabilitation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medical Schools', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T20:33:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Quenqua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quenqua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a4d030e-23b9-51d5-91a8-42c34ddf844b', 'word_count': 1173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a4d030e-23b9-51d5-91a8-42c34ddf844b'}, {'abstract': 'Alex Rodriguez will have arthroscopic surgery on his right knee Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/rodriguez-to-have-surgery-will-miss-four-to-six-weeks/', 'snippet': 'Alex Rodriguez will have arthroscopic surgery on his right knee Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shortly after the Yankees beat the Rays, 1-0, Sunday, the team announced that the All-Star third baseman Alex Rodriguez will undergo arthroscopic surgery on his right knee Monday at the University of Miami Hospital in Florida. Expected recovery time is four to six weeks, the Yankees said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rodriguez to Have Surgery, Will Miss Four to Six Weeks', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodriguez, Alex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T20:40:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8416eb13-c7e6-5f7b-b292-1f99ea9a4e15', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8416eb13-c7e6-5f7b-b292-1f99ea9a4e15'}, {'abstract': 'About 80 people were rescued after the disaster involving the Bulgaria riverboat, out for a cruise on the Volga River.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/world/europe/11volga.html', 'snippet': 'About 80 people were rescued after the disaster involving the Bulgaria riverboat, out for a cruise on the Volga River.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — A riverboat filled with families cruising on the Volga River sank on Sunday, and about 100 people were missing hours later, feeding fears that the episode could be the country’s worst such accident in recent history. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/europe/VOLGA/VOLGA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/11/world/europe/VOLGA/VOLGA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/europe/VOLGA/VOLGA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/europe/VOLGA/VOLGA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/world/europe/VOLGA/VOLGA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '100 Missing After Riverboat Full of Russian Families Sinks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '100 Missing After Riverboat Full of Russian Families Sinks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Volga River', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boating Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T20:55:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdb9685f-71d0-5e6e-91a4-45dd3ceb80d6', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdb9685f-71d0-5e6e-91a4-45dd3ceb80d6'}, {'abstract': 'From an online match, an example of the Dentist’s Coup.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/crosswords/bridge/dentists-coup-from-an-online-match-bridge.html', 'snippet': 'From an online match, an example of the Dentist’s Coup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pair events are in some ways artificial because overtricks can count for so much. Chicago or teams, where making or breaking the contract is paramount, is a purer form of the game. Occasionally, though, that quest for the overtrick produces some good technical play, as in the diagramed deal from a 12-board fast pairs on Bridge Base Online (bridgebase.com) on June 30.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Landing an Overtrick via Some Dental Work', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Landing an Overtrick via Some Dental Work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T20:57:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63d42f03-9b8b-5930-b98a-c068ce20e0f6', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63d42f03-9b8b-5930-b98a-c068ce20e0f6'}, {'abstract': 'Ken Belson answers reader questions about his three-part series on baseball in Japan in the wake of this year’s tsunami and earthquake.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/answering-questions-on-baseballs-role-in-japans-recovery/', 'snippet': 'Ken Belson answers reader questions about his three-part series on baseball in Japan in the wake of this year’s tsunami and earthquake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ken Belson is writing a three-part series on high school baseball in Japan and the steadying influence it has had for some in the wake of the tsunami and earthquake that hit the country in March and the nuclear crisis that has followed. The first article in the series appeared in Sunday’s Times. Ken will answer reader questions this week about the series and his reporting in Japan. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Answering Questions on Baseball’s Role in Japan’s Recovery', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Belson, Ken', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:10:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37d430bc-2e1f-5789-b9dc-ceb0bb8b850b', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37d430bc-2e1f-5789-b9dc-ceb0bb8b850b'}, {'abstract': 'In the long run, policies are all dead?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/the-long-and-the-short-of-it/', 'snippet': 'In the long run, policies are all dead?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A number of people have been telling me about David Brooks and Ruth Marcus agreeing that there’s not much government can do about short-run economic performance, that we need to focus on long-run solutions. It’s a common sentiment inside the Beltway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Long and the Short of It', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:16:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f52da999-2cde-54cb-860c-516258455ab3', 'word_count': 155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f52da999-2cde-54cb-860c-516258455ab3'}, {'abstract': 'Major League Soccer on Sunday announced its First XI, players selected by fans to face Manchester United in the all-star game at Red Bull Arena on July 27.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/fans-pick-m-l-s-all-star-first-xi/', 'snippet': 'Major League Soccer on Sunday announced its First XI, players selected by fans to face Manchester United in the all-star game at Red Bull Arena on July 27.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A poll of M.L.S. fans, at least the ones who voted online, via text message and on Twitter, named the league’s First XI for the All-Star Game, on Sunday. The Galaxy put four players on the list, the Red Bulls three.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fans Pick M.L.S. All-Star First XI', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALL STAR GAMES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Backe, Hans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garber, Don', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lindpere, Joel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Soccer', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:19:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f9772f2-6987-5057-8301-8b1c62f57a09', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f9772f2-6987-5057-8301-8b1c62f57a09'}, {'abstract': 'The Internet forum, that great old standby of Web 1.0., has become an endangered species.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/remembrance-of-message-boards-past/', 'snippet': 'The Internet forum, that great old standby of Web 1.0., has become an endangered species.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It took my husband and me eight months to conceive our first child. Much of that time was passed in bored agitation, like a long wait at a Verizon Wireless store. To pass the time, swap information, and quiet my mind, I turned to an online message board for women who are Trying to Conceive — TTC, one of the first message-board acronyms I learned. Then, rapidly, I learned the rest of the lingo known to the voluble and surprisingly large community of women who turn to the Internet to ask intensely personal questions: TWW (two-week wait), BFN (“big fat negative”) and OPK (ovulator prediction kit).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Old Internet Neighborhoods', 'kicker': 'Virginia Heffernan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:30:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Virginia Heffernan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Virginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heffernan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c1bc9b3-e8ba-5d8c-8860-5c7f6ed7b9f7', 'word_count': 1184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c1bc9b3-e8ba-5d8c-8860-5c7f6ed7b9f7'}, {'abstract': 'The reunited Seattle band Soundgarden, led by Chris Cornell, played a set of old material.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/arts/music/reunited-soundgarden-at-the-prudential-center-review.html', 'snippet': 'The reunited Seattle band Soundgarden, led by Chris Cornell, played a set of old material.', 'lead_paragraph': 'First came the drone: a buzzing swarm of electric guitar tones. A clear, arching guitar line rose out of it. Then bass and drums clobbered their way in with a riff, and Chris Cornell lofted his voice into the moaning, chantlike melody of “Searching With My Good Eye Closed,” intoning, “I’m on my way/Lookin’ for the paradigm.” After 14 years between tours, Soundgarden was back in full force, its paradigm intact, when it performed on Friday night at the Prudential Center in Newark. “Sorry for the long wait,” Mr. Cornell told the crowd. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/GARDEN-1310329530334/GARDEN-1310329530334-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 873, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/GARDEN-1310329530334/GARDEN-1310329530334-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/GARDEN-1310329530334/GARDEN-1310329530334-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Delivering Squalls of Grunge Anew', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Delivering Squalls Of Grunge Anew', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Soundgarden', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:35:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/301cca30-a41b-551c-b30a-a075b705c48b', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/301cca30-a41b-551c-b30a-a075b705c48b'}, {'abstract': 'The Tanglewood festival began with aplomb on Friday evening, with Charles Dutoit celebrating the 30th anniversary of his debut with the Boston Symphony Orchestra.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/arts/music/tanglewoods-season-opens-with-all-italian-program-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Tanglewood festival began with aplomb on Friday evening, with Charles Dutoit celebrating the 30th anniversary of his debut with the Boston Symphony Orchestra.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LENOX, Mass. — James Levine has long been a mainstay of Tanglewood, though he will not appear at the Boston Symphony Orchestra’s summer home this season; shortly after resigning as the ensemble’s music director in March Mr. Levine cancelled all engagements here. But the show went on with aplomb at Tanglewood’s rainy opening night on Friday, with Charles Dutoit celebrating the 30th anniversary of his debut with the orchestra and conducting an all-Italian program of operatic and symphonic works.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/SUBTANGLE/SUBTANGLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 246, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/SUBTANGLE/SUBTANGLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 246}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/SUBTANGLE/SUBTANGLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 419, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/SUBTANGLE/SUBTANGLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/SUBTANGLE/SUBTANGLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tanglewood’s Season Opens With Good Friends Like Verdi and Bellini', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tanglewood’s Season Opens With Good Friends Like Verdi and Bellini', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tanglewood Music Festival (Lenox, Mass)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dutoit, Charles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:36:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb8d1948-d618-5eee-bab9-e3fb040ce1b7', 'word_count': 703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb8d1948-d618-5eee-bab9-e3fb040ce1b7'}, {'abstract': '“Guillaume Tell,” Rossini’s 39th and final opera, was performed in a concert version at Caramoor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/arts/music/rossinis-opera-guillaume-tell-at-caramoor-review.html', 'snippet': '“Guillaume Tell,” Rossini’s 39th and final opera, was performed in a concert version at Caramoor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KATONAH, N.Y. — Great minds have long grappled with one of opera’s enduring mysteries: Why did the composer Gioachino Rossini, at the height of his fame and his creative powers in 1829, stop composing operas and effectively retire after the premiere of “Guillaume Tell” at the Paris Opera that year?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/CANTO/CANTO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/CANTO/CANTO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/CANTO/CANTO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Return to Rossini’s Days of Yesteryear', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Return to Rossini’s Days of Yesteryear', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rossini, Gioacchino', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Caramoor International Music Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:37:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aaee56d2-ff4d-5c45-a08a-0cafed672d88', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aaee56d2-ff4d-5c45-a08a-0cafed672d88'}, {'abstract': 'Tap City festival’s “Salute to the Copasetics: The Masters of Swing” was a tribute to tap-dancing elders and their magic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/arts/dance/salute-to-copasetics-tap-dance-at-symphony-space-review.html', 'snippet': 'Tap City festival’s “Salute to the Copasetics: The Masters of Swing” was a tribute to tap-dancing elders and their magic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Historical film footage of tap-dancers is often less than ideal: the images grainy or full of wavy lines, the sound muddy or distorted, the camera sliding all over the place. And still it’s usually enchanting. Even on recordings and whether doing the simplest steps or telling the silliest jokes, the older generations of hoofers have an undeniable presence and artistry. You can’t stop watching.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/COPACETICS/COPACETICS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 825, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Syncopated Tap Rhythms Tattooed Onto the Floor', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Syncopated Tap Rhythms Tattooed Onto the Floor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:39:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia La Rocco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Rocco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cb45b9f-9f30-5c9c-9e53-46788a84f78f', 'word_count': 513, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cb45b9f-9f30-5c9c-9e53-46788a84f78f'}, {'abstract': 'American Ballet Theater coped impressively with dancers missing due to injury during its spring season, but the company was held back nonetheless by a repertory that does not do justice to some classics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/arts/dance/american-ballet-theaters-season-of-injuries-and-triumphs.html', 'snippet': 'American Ballet Theater coped impressively with dancers missing due to injury during its spring season, but the company was held back nonetheless by a repertory that does not do justice to some classics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'History often happens during American Ballet Theater’s eight-week spring season, principally because of its stars. This season, which ended Saturday, was distinguished by three guest artists in particular: Alina Cojocaru (from the Royal Ballet of London) in three roles, with her enchanting radiance and heart-catching fragility, and Natalia Osipova and Ivan Vasiliev (the Bolshoi Ballet in Moscow), each in two, with their ardent vitality and astounding elevation. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/ABT/ABT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 706, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/ABT/ABT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/ABT/ABT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Injuries and Triumphs Color a Ballet Season', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Injuries and Triumphs Color a Ballet Season', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:41:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13dee96a-2c90-585e-bac7-53e318586359', 'word_count': 1054, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13dee96a-2c90-585e-bac7-53e318586359'}, {'abstract': 'After a sizzling first quarter, the weakening economy and an array of worrisome global events have Wall Street planning for big revenue drops and new rounds of layoffs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/business/economy/banks-expected-to-post-weak-2nd-quarter-results.html', 'snippet': 'After a sizzling first quarter, the weakening economy and an array of worrisome global events have Wall Street planning for big revenue drops and new rounds of layoffs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Only a few short months ago, JPMorgan Chase traders were on such a roll that they did not have a single losing day in the first quarter.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/business/11jpbanks/11jpbanks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 701, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/business/11jpbanks/11jpbanks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/business/11jpbanks/11jpbanks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wall St. Banks Expected to Post Weak 2nd-Quarter Results', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Weak Results Are Projected For Wall Street', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:55:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Dash', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dash', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a268f92-a726-511b-a5b5-8143dba65181', 'word_count': 1156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a268f92-a726-511b-a5b5-8143dba65181'}, {'abstract': 'The English musician Sbtrkt appeared, albeit behind his trademark mask, at MoMA PS1 on Saturday afternoon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/arts/music/sbtrkt-and-sampha-at-moma-ps1-review.html', 'snippet': 'The English musician Sbtrkt appeared, albeit behind his trademark mask, at MoMA PS1 on Saturday afternoon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thursday night Sbtrkt was at Camp Bisco, the annual festival — organized by the Disco Biscuits and held in upstate New York — that rounds up the progressive end of the jam band and improvisational electronic music spectrum. On Friday night he was at Wrongbar, a sleek spot in Toronto, where the hip-hop superstar Drake stopped in for a quick collaborative performance.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/SBTRKT/SBTRKT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 358, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/SBTRKT/SBTRKT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 358}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/SBTRKT/SBTRKT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/SBTRKT/SBTRKT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/SBTRKT/SBTRKT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Behind the Mask, Giving Hints of How It’s Done', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Behind the Mask, Giving Hints of How It’s Done', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T21:59:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c6bce5b-bbff-562c-a653-b214f27a94d8', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c6bce5b-bbff-562c-a653-b214f27a94d8'}, {'abstract': 'Ellen Leuschner makes her debut, appearing here for the first time.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/pun/', 'snippet': 'Ellen Leuschner makes her debut, appearing here for the first time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — Welcome to the Department of Redundancy Department, where we try to describe things adjectively in a way that means the same thing as the noun we’re describing. Synonomously speaking, of course.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Response to a Bad Pun', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T22:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/392f2da6-60ca-57dc-8a20-a1fbaeeff958', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/392f2da6-60ca-57dc-8a20-a1fbaeeff958'}, {'abstract': 'In Jamaica Bay, Constance Hockaday has created one of New York’s loopiest getaways.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/arts/design/boggsville-boatel-constance-hockadays-jamaica-bay-project.html', 'snippet': 'In Jamaica Bay, Constance Hockaday has created one of New York’s loopiest getaways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As boat christenings go, this one was rollicking. In lieu of a Champagne bottle smashed against a hull, there was late-night bourbon and diving off the roof of the Queen Zenobia into dark waters lighted with natural phosphorescence. Nine strangers in bathing suits floated on an overwhelmed inflatable raft; a couple held hands on a pair of deck chairs; a moose head in a houseboat was decorated with a headlamp and a bra. Though the official ceremony would come later, on Friday night the Boggsville Boatel and Boat-In Theater, New York City’s newest and perhaps loopiest tourist outpost, was open for business.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/BOATEL/BOATEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/BOATEL/BOATEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/BOATEL/BOATEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Check In, Swim Out: A Floating Hotel as Art', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Check In, Swim Out: A Floating Hotel as Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Houseboats', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boats and Boating', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jamaica Bay (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T22:03:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f38782e-67db-5aa2-8351-c55bfe4db250', 'word_count': 1519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f38782e-67db-5aa2-8351-c55bfe4db250'}, {'abstract': '“Alphas,” a series beginning Monday night on Syfy, is a science-fiction action series about crime fighters with superpowers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/arts/television/alphas-pilot-on-syfy-stars-david-strathairn-review.html', 'snippet': '“Alphas,” a series beginning Monday night on Syfy, is a science-fiction action series about crime fighters with superpowers.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Alphas,” a series beginning Monday night on Syfy, is an uneasy hybrid: a science-fiction action series that also wants to be a heart-tugging law-and-order procedural, the type where the crime-fighting team represents a bickering but affectionate family.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/ALPHAS/ALPHAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 285, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/ALPHAS/ALPHAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 285}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/ALPHAS/ALPHAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 487, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/ALPHAS/ALPHAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/ALPHAS/ALPHAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mind Over Crime for a Superpowered Team', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mind Over Crime for a Superpowered Team', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science Fiction', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Syfy Channel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T22:09:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4864b74a-9f31-57e2-be3f-07caad2ecdb6', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4864b74a-9f31-57e2-be3f-07caad2ecdb6'}, {'abstract': 'American manufacturers in Mexico are expanding along the border, a stark paradox during violent times there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/world/americas/11matamoros.html', 'snippet': 'American manufacturers in Mexico are expanding along the border, a stark paradox during violent times there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MATAMOROS, Mexico — When the latest bloody headlines from the drug war in Mexico reach headquarters in New York, Ken Chandler, the manager of an American electronics manufacturing plant here, jumps on the phone.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/11matamoros1_span/11matamoros1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/11/world/11matamoros1_span/11matamoros1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/11matamoros1_span/11matamoros1_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/11matamoros1_span/11matamoros1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/world/11matamoros1_span/11matamoros1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Despite Violence, U.S. Firms Expand in Mexico', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Despite Violence, Mexico Plants Hum at Border', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T22:16:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randal C. Archibold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randal', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Archibold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa0f3353-9196-590c-bc7f-42b395483202', 'word_count': 1248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa0f3353-9196-590c-bc7f-42b395483202'}, {'abstract': 'Automakers’ growth forecasts by far exceed the market, suggesting disappointment down the road.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/business/carmakers-goals-stretch-credulity.html', 'snippet': 'Automakers’ growth forecasts by far exceed the market, suggesting disappointment down the road.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world’s carmakers are indulging in pipe dreams again. Carlos Ghosn, Nissan’s chief executive, predicted in June that the company would sell 82 percent more vehicles globally in 2016 than last year. Alan R. Mulally, the chief at Ford Motor, earlier projected a 50 percent bump, while Ferdinand Piëch, chairman of Volkswagen, is shooting for just a tad less growth than that. If the rest of the industry is as optimistic, in about five years manufacturers will be producing almost 20 million more cars than market forecasters expect will be sold.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/business/views/views-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 763, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/business/views/views-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/business/views/views-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Carmakers’ Goals Stretch Credulity', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Carmakers’ Goals Stretch Credulity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Compensation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bonuses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-10T22:16:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By ANTONY CURRIE and ROBERT CYRAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Antony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CURRIE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CYRAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article