still under construction, into a barracks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Thomas U. Walter returned to Washington at the beginning of July 1861, he found an appalling mess. For 10 years he had served as the architect in charge of building the new Senate and House wings of the United States Capitol and the cast iron dome that would crown the rotunda. When war broke out, Congress was not in session and the building was empty. The Union Army took it over.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The U.S. Capitol at War', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T00:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By guy gugliotta', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0779db2c-6c0e-554e-8c49-9a2a60e084b6', 'word_count': 1792, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0779db2c-6c0e-554e-8c49-9a2a60e084b6'}, {'abstract': 'A mother said a school concluded in 12 days that her son did not meet standards, raising a question about whether charter schools cherry-pick students.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/nyregion/charter-school-sends-message-thrive-or-transfer.html', 'snippet': 'A mother said a school concluded in 12 days that her son did not meet standards, raising a question about whether charter schools cherry-pick students.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2008, when Katherine Sprowal’s son, Matthew, was selected in a lottery to attend the Harlem Success Academy 3 charter school, she was thrilled. “I felt like we were getting the best private school, and we didn’t have to pay for it,” she recalled. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/nyregion/oneducation/oneducation-articleLarge