g to music at home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The hottest new club isn’t in Los Angeles or New York. It’s on a Web site called Turntable.fm, where big-headed cartoon characters populate the D.J. booth.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/business/11jpturntable/11jpturntable-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/11/business/11jpturntable/11jpturntable-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/business/11jpturntable/11jpturntable-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/business/11jpturntable/11jpturntable-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/business/11jpturntable/11jpturntable-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Music Site Lets Users Play D.J. to Virtual, and Discerning, Crowds', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Music Site Lets Users Play D.J. To Virtual, Yet Discerning, Crowds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disc Jockeys', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T00:25:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcd88bea-39ec-5bd7-83a8-7294ec257080', 'word_count': 1196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcd88bea-39ec-5bd7-83a8-7294ec257080'}, {'abstract': 'His accusers allege that the Rev. Clement A. Hageman was “dumped” in impoverished, nonwhite communities in Arizona and New Mexico by church officials to avoid scandal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/us/11priest.html', 'snippet': 'His accusers allege that the Rev. Clement A. Hageman was “dumped” in impoverished, nonwhite communities in Arizona and New Mexico by church officials to avoid scandal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WINSLOW, Ariz. — Alfred Moya was stopping at a restaurant in rural Gallup, N.M., on his way home to Phoenix in the summer of 2007 when he happened to glance at a newspaper article about children who had been sexually abused by a priest. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/us/PRIEST-1/PRIEST-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Accusations of Abuse by Priest Dating to Early 1940s', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Accusations of Abuse by Priest Dating to Early 1940s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Priests', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Mexico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T00:26:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Frosch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frosch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb4c1717-b18a-5b1c-9b4d-e62e9370f910', 'word_count': 1089, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb4c1717-b18a-5b1c-9b4d-e62e9370f910'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent editorial about New York attempting to follow a model of required work and education for young welfare recipients.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/opinion/lweb11welfare.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a recent editorial about New York attempting to follow a model of required work and education for young welfare recipients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jobs for Welfare Recipients: New York’s Approach', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jobs for Welfare Recipients: New York’s Approach', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Welfare (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T00:39:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a797325-0eb5-5f2f-a41a-3acf5e15741d', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a797325-0eb5-5f2f-a41a-3acf5e15741d'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Petit worked with Jean Cocteau, Danny Kaye and his own wife, the ballerina Zizi Jeanmaire, among many others. Sex and suffering were the themes of his most renowned pieces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/arts/dance/roland-petit-choreographer-is-dead-at-87.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Petit worked with Jean Cocteau, Danny Kaye and his own wife, the ballerina Zizi Jeanmaire, among many others. Sex and suffering were the themes of his most renowned pieces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roland Petit, France’s leading choreographer\xa0after World War II, who swept away time-honored ballet taboos and whose mix of entertainment and art also made his work popular in Hollywood, died Sunday in Geneva. He was 87 and had lived in Switzerland for more than a decade.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/petit-obit/petit-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/petit-obit/petit-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/petit-obit/petit-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 611, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/petit-obit/petit-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/arts/petit-obit/petit-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Roland Petit, Choreographer, Dies at 87; Conquered Ballet Taboos and Hollywood', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Roland Petit, Choreographer, Dies at 87; Conquered Ballet Taboos and Hollywood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T00:55:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Kisselgoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kisselgoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ee3aedd-20b1-554f-8271-1ae496319cf3', 'word_count': 961, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ee3aedd-20b1-554f-8271-1ae496319cf3'}, {'abstract': 'A detective’s account offers another window into an unusually close relationship between The News of the World and the British police.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/world/europe/11britain.html', 'snippet': 'A detective’s account offers another window into an unusually close relationship between The News of the World and the British police.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — On Jan. 9, 2003, Rebekah Wade, until recently the editor of The News of the World, was summoned to an unusual meeting at Scotland Yard, the headquarters of the Metropolitan Police Service here. Detective Superintendent David Cook, the lead investigator in a gruesome cold-case killing of a man found with an ax in his head, confronted Ms. Wade with a worrying accusation: He and his family were being followed and photographed, he said, by people hired by her newspaper. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/BRITAIN/BRITAIN-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/11/world/BRITAIN/BRITAIN-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/BRITAIN/BRITAIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/BRITAIN/BRITAIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/world/BRITAIN/BRITAIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cozy Ties Mark Newspaper’s Dealings With Scotland Yard', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Detective Says The News of the World Spied on Him With Impunity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scotland Yard (Metropolitan Police Service)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Rebekah', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coulson, Andy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T00:57:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jo Becker and Sarah Lyall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Becker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af9f71e6-0464-5502-9f94-0d0c9334bf16', 'word_count': 1255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af9f71e6-0464-5502-9f94-0d0c9334bf16'}, {'abstract': 'TheGrio.com and NewsOne.com will collaborate on news and advertising.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/10/black-news-in-new-focus/', 'snippet': 'TheGrio.com and NewsOne.com will collaborate on news and advertising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NBC News and Interactive One, a network of Web sites aimed at African-Americans, plan to announce a partnership on Monday that will combine the editorial and sales resources of both properties. The partnership will focus on aligning NBC’s African-American news site, TheGrio.com, and Interactive One’s news Web site, NewsOne.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Black News in New Focus', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NBC News', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T00:57:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanzina Vega', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanzina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vega', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bf7476a-7486-5d9a-99e1-c36e704f47eb', 'word_count': 286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bf7476a-7486-5d9a-99e1-c36e704f47eb'}, {'abstract': 'Opposition figures said they would not take part in talks, which officials said are aimed at a transition to multiparty democracy, until the government ended its crackdown on protesters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/world/middleeast/11syria.html', 'snippet': 'Opposition figures said they would not take part in talks, which officials said are aimed at a transition to multiparty democracy, until the government ended its crackdown on protesters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — Syrian officials on Sunday formally opened what they described as a national dialogue aimed at a transition to multiparty democracy, but the country’s opposition leaders boycotted the event, calling it a sham to mask the government’s brutal crackdown on the pro-democracy protests that have shaken Syria’s ruling Assad family.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/middleeast/SUB-SYRIA-2/SUB-SYRIA-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 586, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/11/world/middleeast/SUB-SYRIA-2/SUB-SYRIA-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/11/world/middleeast/SUB-SYRIA-2/SUB-SYRIA-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Syrian Opposition Leaders Boycott a Government Dialogue Opening', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Syrian Opposition Leaders Boycott a Government Dialogue Opening', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1,