ion': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c057f333-d9f2-5c42-bfde-639013a3f983', 'word_count': 1494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c057f333-d9f2-5c42-bfde-639013a3f983'}, {'abstract': 'Ziyah Gafic explores a Cold War labyrinth 900 feet below a Bosnian town.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/inside-titos-nuclear-bunker/', 'snippet': 'Ziyah Gafic explores a Cold War labyrinth 900 feet below a Bosnian town.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the convulsions that upended Yugoslavia in the 1990s, a secret came to light near the Bosnian town of Konjic. There — it turned out — the dictator Josip Broz Tito had constructed a labyrinthine bunker complex 900 feet below ground to protect him and his inner circle in the event of a nuclear attack. Today, it seems as remote as Yugoslavia itself.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside Tito’s Nuclear Bunker', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tito, Josip Broz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bosnia and Herzegovina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T09:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/813b706f-9eb8-51da-b8a5-a7d49a5da1cd', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/813b706f-9eb8-51da-b8a5-a7d49a5da1cd'}, {'abstract': 'The small bronze cobblestones, commemorating Holocaust victims, can be easy to miss, but they are scattered, in plain sight, all over the streets of Prague and numerous other cities across Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/plaques-in-prague-commemorate-holocaust-victims/', 'snippet': 'The small bronze cobblestones, commemorating Holocaust victims, can be easy to miss, but they are scattered, in plain sight, all over the streets of Prague and numerous other cities across Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The small bronze cobblestones can be easy to miss, but they are scattered, in plain sight, all over the streets of Prague and numerous other cities across Europe. Collectively these simple, handmade stone plaques, called Stolpersteine (“stumbling blocks”), conceived and designed by the German artist Gunter Demnig, comprise one of the largest Holocaust memorials in the world. They serve as poignant public markers, announcing to passersby that the buildings the stones are set in front of\xa0 were once the homes of men, women and children who were carted away and murdered by the Nazi regime during World War II. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Plaques in Prague Commemorate Holocaust Victims', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Prague (Czech Republic)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': "By Julie O'Shea", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Shea", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbc105e7-9493-5a51-9c54-cccef8e0b9e4', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbc105e7-9493-5a51-9c54-cccef8e0b9e4'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/