r play area.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/world/europe/12russia.html', 'snippet': 'Shortly before a riverboat on the Volga lurched to one side and then slowly capsized, an estimated 40 children had been moved into an interior play area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KAZAN, Russia — The rain was not relenting on Sunday afternoon as the Bulgaria left the riverbanks for the open expanse of the reservoir. So the baby sitters gathered the children together and led them to the activity room to play. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/world/RUSSIA-1/RUSSIA-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/12/world/RUSSIA-1/RUSSIA-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/world/RUSSIA-1/RUSSIA-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/world/RUSSIA-1/RUSSIA-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/12/world/RUSSIA-1/RUSSIA-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scores of Children Among 128 Dead in Russian Sinking', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Children Trapped in Boat’s Playroom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boating Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drownings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T12:29:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer and Ellen Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72d3e197-cc8e-5ca8-9fd8-72f3eb4fccc1', 'word_count': 975, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72d3e197-cc8e-5ca8-9fd8-72f3eb4fccc1'}, {'abstract': 'There’s something about money.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/monetary-rage/', 'snippet': 'There’s something about money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We had dinner last night with Margaret Ray and Dave Anderson, the authors of the AP adaptation of our textbook (which is terrific, by the way). Over our $350 $22 bottle of wine, we talked about various issues involved in trying to explain economics — and everyone agreed that monetary economics is where people are most likely to get not only confused, but furious. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monetary Rage', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T12:33:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b11f525-db48-58eb-95b5-fab3b9040eff', 'word_count': 333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b11f525-db48-58eb-95b5-fab3b9040eff'}, {'abstract': 'Financial markets reflected rising fears that Italy could become the next victim of Europe’s debt crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/business/daily-stock-market-activity.html', 'snippet': 'Financial markets reflected rising fears that Italy could become the next victim of Europe’s debt crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Financial and energy companies took a beating on Wall Street on Monday as stocks in the United States followed global markets lower on concerns about the euro zone debt crisis.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Global Markets Tumble on Continued Worries Over Europe’s Debt Crisis', 'kicker': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Global Markets Tumble on Continued Worries Over Europe’s Debt Crisis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T12:35:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bbefe85-d501-5194-aa46-f3071af3ef86', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bbefe85-d501-5194-aa46-f3071af3ef86'}, {'abstract': 'The joint bid by Peabody and ArcelorMittal for MacArthur might be a long shot, as two other major investors can effectively block a takeover.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/peabody-and-arcelor-seek-control-of-macarthur-coal/', 'snippet': 'The joint bid by Peabody and ArcelorMittal for MacArthur might be a long shot, as two other major investors can effectively block a takeover.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Peabody and Arcelor Seek Control of Macarthur Coal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T12:37:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c536d391-4d83-5431-b0c8-c6ee6899aa31', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c536d391-4d83-5431-b0c8-c6ee6899aa31'}, {'abstract': 'We have our own money.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/why-italy-why-not-america/', 'snippet': 'We have our own money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A good question from some commenters. But it’s a question I’ve written about before:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Italy? Why Not America?', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T12:50:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36e0ec7d-bf6d-5096-91cc-4765a458e521', 'word_count': 93, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36e0ec7d-bf6d-5096-91cc-4765a458e521'}, {'abstract': 'The band says its tour will continue without him.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/bass-player-for-coheed-and-cambria-charged-with-robbing-pharmacy/', 'snippet': 'The band says its tour will continue without him.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Just hours before his band was to open for Soundgarden on Sunday, the bass player for Coheed and Cambria, the alternative rock band from Nyack, N.Y., was arrested on charges of robbing a pharmacy in Massachusetts to get prescription painkillers, the Associated Press reported.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bass Player for Coheed and Cambria Charged With Robbing Pharmacy', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Coheed and Cambria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Soundgarden', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nyack (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T12:55:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eecd1458-9192-5849-a029-31132ccf9c1c', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eecd1458-9192-5849-a029-31132ccf9c1c'}, {'abstract': 'The owners of Leather Spa, New York’s go-to shoe repair, share their tricks and tips for taking care of all types of footwear.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/sole-mates-shoe-saving-tips-from-the-pros/', 'snippet': 'The owners of Leather Spa, New York’s go-to shoe repair, share their tricks and tips for taking care of all types of footwear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During summer months, the feet are most on display. And while many of us go out of our way to pamper our toes with pedicures, our shoes can take the real beating. Just because you aren’t tromping through snow and slush doesn’t mean your footwear doesn’t need T.L.C. Just ask David Mesquita. Along with his father, Carlos, David owns Leather Spa, New York’s go-to shoe repair. “Pre-treating shoes is actually a bigger part of our business than repairing destroyed ones,” says David, who sees some 500 pairs a day. Here, the Mesquita men share their tricks and tips for taking care of all types of footwear before the damage is done.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sole Mates | Shoe Saving Tips From the Pros', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T13:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emilia Ferrara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emilia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ferrara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7477332b-ba7f-574d-9588-f787f179830c', 'word_count': 409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7477332b-ba7f-574d-9588-f787f179830c'}, {'abstract': 'We’re taking a month off in order to tend to some back-end issues, but Historic Headlines will publish daily again starting Monday, Aug. 8.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/historic-headlines-will-be-back-august-8/', 'snippet': 'We’re taking a month off in order to tend to some back-end issues, but Historic Headlines will publish daily again starting Monday, Aug. 8.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thank you to those of you who wrote in, enthusiastically, to tell us you like our new offering, Historic Headlines. Summer is when we experiment on the blog, and hearing from you helps us decide what to keep, and how to refine it, for the school year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Historic Headlines Will Be Back Aug. 8', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T13:28:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f0c9163-b0a6-53cb-b676-bbbc9cc38e34', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f0c9163-b0a6-53cb-b676-bbbc9cc38e34'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: The French in New York celebrated Bastille Day a bit early, and Dominique Strauss-Kahn was nowhere to be seen.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/ah-how-the-french-celebrate/', 'snippet': 'The Day: The French in New York celebrated Bastille Day a bit early, and Dominique Strauss-Kahn was nowhere to be seen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As best as one could tell, the most famous Frenchman in New York — you may also safely call him the most notorious — was not to be found at Bastille Day celebrations held on Sunday along East 60th Street. We didn’t make it to a similar event held on Smith Street in Brooklyn, where the French population is said to be growing. But we assume he wasn’t there, either.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ah, How the French Celebrate', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T13:53:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d323dc9-adfc-5d33-9759-e890a6953aa9', 'word_count': 1118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d323dc9-adfc-5d33-9759-e890a6953aa9'}, {'abstract': 'In 1982, when the unemployment rate was similar to the rate in recent years, the share of people who experienced any spell of unemployment was much higher.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/the-concentrated-pain-of-job-loss/', 'snippet': 'In 1982, when the unemployment rate was similar to the rate in recent years, the share of people who experienced any spell of unemployment was much higher.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I wanted to add one other explanation — to those Catherine Rampell cited, in her much-discussed Sunday article — for why unemployment has not become a larger political issue. Given how high unemployment is, there are surprisingly few people who have experienced unemployment in the last couple of years. This is the flip side of the historically high average length of the unemployment: joblessness is concentrated among a subset of the population, rather than affecting a larger group of people for shorter periods of time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Concentrated Pain of Job Loss', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T14:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df2a6040-dc93-5540-a36e-3ab9dff9a0c8', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df2a6040-dc93-5540-a36e-3ab9dff9a0c8'}, {'abstract': 'The church’s investment arm, which owns shares in the News Corporation, sent a letter to Rupert Murdoch asking that senior executives be held accountable for failures at The News of the World newspaper.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/church-of-england-calls-for-accountability-in-news-corp-scandal/', 'snippet': 'The church’s investment arm, which owns shares in the News Corporation, sent a letter to Rupert Murdoch asking that senior executives be held accountable for failures at The News of the World newspaper.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Church of England has entered the debate about the phone-hacking scandal at The News of the World — as an investor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Church of England Seeks Accountability From News Corp. in Hacking Scandal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T14:07:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1392161-5c2e-5b4a-8fda-a4b5c563c79c', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1392161-5c2e-5b4a-8fda-a4b5c563c79c'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/what-were-reading-222/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T14:11:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06a2c976-1c9b-54f3-934c-c7de4cee262b', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06a2c976-1c9b-54f3-934c-c7de4cee262b'}, {'abstract': 'Shubbak, the first festival of contemporary Arabic art in London, offers near-full immersion in the region’s art scene.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/orient-express/', 'snippet': 'Shubbak, the first festival of contemporary Arabic art in London, offers near-full immersion in the region’s art scene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travel to several Middle Eastern countries may be discouraged, but for three weeks in July, Shubbak, the first festival of contemporary Arabic art in London, offers near-full immersion in the region’s art scene, with some 70 exhibitions, plays, readings, film screenings and dance performances across the city. “The daily news reports of political and social developments in the region have understandably influenced how people think about Arab countries,” explains London’s mayor, Boris Johnson, who is sponsoring the festival. “Shubbak offers something new, showing that the quality of contemporary art coming out of the region is brilliant and challenging.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Orient Express', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T14:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shirine Saad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shirine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f5975d2-64ca-5588-945e-86f194383cac', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f5975d2-64ca-5588-945e-86f194383cac'}, {'abstract': 'Sometimes coaches know stuff. Sometimes they get it wrong.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/one-play-erases-doubts-about-a-coach/', 'snippet': 'Sometimes coaches know stuff. Sometimes they get it wrong.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes coaches know stuff. Sometimes they get it wrong.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Play Erases Doubts About a Coach', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup (Soccer)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T14:30:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Vecsey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vecsey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12c3b890-aa3a-55f4-9e0f-8bb658553ae3', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12c3b890-aa3a-55f4-9e0f-8bb658553ae3'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/morning-take-out-282/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nestle to Buy Control of China’s Biggest Confectioner | Nestlé said on Monday that it had agreed to pay $1.7 billion for a 60 percent stake in a big Chinese confectioner, its second significant foray into the rapidly growing Chinese market in less than three months.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T14:32:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a752a67-7b5b-5b2c-ae85-236b3a953d1e', 'word_count': 1903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a752a67-7b5b-5b2c-ae85-236b3a953d1e'}, {'abstract': 'Homes with supposedly hypoallergenic breeds had as much pet allergen as homes with ordinary dogs, according to a new report.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/the-myth-of-the-allergy-free-dog/', 'snippet': 'Homes with supposedly hypoallergenic breeds had as much pet allergen as homes with ordinary dogs, according to a new report.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dog lovers with a pet allergy have a big problem when it comes to having a dog in the home. But the usual solution — getting a breed known to be hypoallergenic — may not be a safe bet. In fact, there may be no such thing as a low-allergy or allergy-free dog, according to a new report. The study found that the quantities of dog allergens in homes with supposedly hypoallergenic breeds are no different from those in homes with dogs widely considered non-hypoallergenic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Myth of the Allergy-Free Dog', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALLERGIES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T14:37:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e549808f-bfbd-5941-bf55-8c453bfe918e', 'word_count': 629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e549808f-bfbd-5941-bf55-8c453bfe918e'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama holds his second news conference in less than two weeks, just hours before he  meets with Congressional leaders on raising the nation’s debt ceiling.\xa0', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/obama-to-hold-news-conference-on-budget-talks/', 'snippet': 'President Obama holds his second news conference in less than two weeks, just hours before he  meets with Congressional leaders on raising the nation’s debt ceiling.\xa0', 'lead_paragraph': '12:57 p.m. | Updated  President Obama challenged Republicans on Monday to live up to their demands to cut the nation’s deficit and address its long-term debt by enacting spending cuts, revenue increases and changes to entitlement programs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Changing Tone, Obama Pressures Both Parties on Budget Deal', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T14:47:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d58fa02-c993-5007-995e-39346a780720', 'word_count': 1178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d58fa02-c993-5007-995e-39346a780720'}, {'abstract': 'At Silverstone, site of Ferrari’s first grand prix victory in 1951, Fernando Alonso delivered the Italian team’s first win of 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/monday-motorsports-alonso-win-caps-milestone-week-for-ferrari/', 'snippet': 'At Silverstone, site of Ferrari’s first grand prix victory in 1951, Fernando Alonso delivered the Italian team’s first win of 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Heritage had its day at the Silverstone Circuit in Northhamptonshire, England, as Ferrari, the oldest team in Formula One, commemorated the 60th anniversary of its first grand prix victory with, fittingly, a victory.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday Motorsports: Alonso Win Caps Milestone Week for Ferrari', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nascar Sprint Cup Series', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALONSO, FERNANDO', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Busch, Kyle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franchitti, Dario', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harvick, Kevin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Power, Will', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Webber, Mark', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ferrari SpA', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Formula One', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn of Stock Car Auto Racing', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Red Bull', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Illinois', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kentucky', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Toronto (Ontario)', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T14:48:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc820fca-7702-5a97-b55e-075f1da0ad27', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc820fca-7702-5a97-b55e-075f1da0ad27'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the celebrities who were targeted by The News of the World, including the actors Hugh Grant and Steve Coogan and the singer George Michael, have led the cheers over the tabloid’s demise.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/celebrity-targets-of-tabloid-cheer-its-demise/', 'snippet': 'Some of the celebrities who were targeted by The News of the World, including the actors Hugh Grant and Steve Coogan and the singer George Michael, have led the cheers over the tabloid’s demise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As my colleagues John F. Burns and Alan Cowell report, there was widespread revulsion in Britain last week when it was revealed that journalists at The News of the World, a British tabloid newspaper, had hacked into the voice mail of a 13-year-old girl following her abduction in 2002.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Celebrity Targets of Tabloid Cheer Its Demise', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Celebrities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Rebekah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coogan, Steve', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dowler, Milly', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Hugh', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Michael, George', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T15:08:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87d3866f-44c2-532f-8df1-48b5f9440194', 'word_count': 983, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87d3866f-44c2-532f-8df1-48b5f9440194'}, {'abstract': 'Three different but equally theatrical productions in London provide a kind of catharsis for theatergoers who are fed up with the hypocrites who rule their worlds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/theater/richard-iii-at-old-vic-and-other-london-shows-offer-catharsis.html', 'snippet': 'Three different but equally theatrical productions in London provide a kind of catharsis for theatergoers who are fed up with the hypocrites who rule their worlds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — If a cloud of sawdust seems to hang over the Old Vic Theater these days, that’s because Kevin Spacey is chewing his way through the scenery there like an atomic termite. In a ripping old-fashioned star turn as Shakespeare’s Richard III, Mr. Spacey gives fierce and flashy physical life to every twist of a power-mad man’s corkscrew mind. Richard may be slowed down by a hunched back and hobbled gait, but this performance spins a classic, much-interpreted character until we’re all dizzy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/12brantley-web/12brantley-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/12brantley-web/12brantley-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/12brantley-web/12brantley-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/12brantley-web/12brantley-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/12brantley-web/12brantley-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Old Stories, Spun Anew', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Old Stories, Spun Anew', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of 