 weak to punch through soil so dry it might as well be pavement.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/us/DROUGHT/DROUGHT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/12/us/DROUGHT/DROUGHT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/us/DROUGHT/DROUGHT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/us/DROUGHT/DROUGHT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/12/us/DROUGHT/DROUGHT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drought Spreads Pain From Florida to Arizona', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drought Spreads Pain From Florida to Arizona', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drought', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southwestern States (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T18:21:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kim Severson and Kirk Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Kirk', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5623a61c-691f-5478-b3ed-6cdaa8ffa17b', 'word_count': 1460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5623a61c-691f-5478-b3ed-6cdaa8ffa17b'}, {'abstract': 'Why conservatives shouldn’t assume that they’ll have this much leverage in the next deficit negotiations.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/the-case-for-going-big/', 'snippet': 'Why conservatives shouldn’t assume that they’ll have this much leverage in the next deficit negotiations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My column today has a generally sympathetic take on the G.O.P.’s brinksmanship in the ongoing debt ceiling negotiations, but it ends with a big caveat: Some of the same factors that make this a good time for Republicans to take the hardest possible line (a Democratic president who wants a conservative-seeming deficit package, the likelihood that future tax increases are in the offing with or without Republican concessions this time around, etc.) also make it a good moment for Republicans to go for a big deal rather than a small deal, even if the big deal is somewhat less than ideological pure. For an example of what I mean, consider this line from James Pethokoukis, hailing John Boehner for pulling a Reagan at Reykjavik and walking away from the lure of a big deficit deal:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case For Going Big', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T18:22:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By ROSS DOUTHAT', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DOUTHAT', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role'