ew York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/McGregor/McGregor-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/McGregor/McGregor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/McGregor/McGregor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Echoing the Tones of Francis Bacon', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Echoing The Tones Of Francis Bacon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paris Opera Ballet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGregor, Wayne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T22:23:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f9105cf-e54f-5160-9519-21768e880263', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f9105cf-e54f-5160-9519-21768e880263'}, {'abstract': 'A combination of increased enforcement of penalties to prevent use of a mobile phone while driving, and a high-profile education campaign, helped reduce incidents of distracted driving in Syracuse and Hartford.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/two-cities-find-an-effective-way-to-combat-distracted-driving/', 'snippet': 'A combination of increased enforcement of penalties to prevent use of a mobile phone while driving, and a high-profile education campaign, helped reduce incidents of distracted driving in Syracuse and Hartford.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the last year, police in Syracuse and Hartford have been tough on multitasking drivers. Combined, officers in the two cities have handed out nearly 20,000 tickets for illegal use of a phone while driving — either for texting or use of a handheld phone.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Cities Find an Effective Way to Combat Distracted Driving', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T22:27:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Richtel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richtel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73291b34-4736-52fe-8cc3-68d2c11e78ee', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73291b34-4736-52fe-8cc3-68d2c11e78ee'}, {'abstract': 'The violinist Miguel Atwood-Ferguson performed with his ensemble at Annunciation Park in Harlem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/arts/music/miguel-atwood-ferguson-at-annunciation-park-review.html', 'snippet': 'The violinist Miguel Atwood-Ferguson performed with his ensemble at Annunciation Park in Harlem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the magnetic forces holding together a lot of New York’s better jazz musicians right now is the hip-hop composer and producer J Dilla, who died in 2006. It has to do with the fascinatingly human lurch and pop of his sampled rhythm tracks, but something bigger, too: the improvisational curiosity he had about rhythm and sound. He’s at the heart of the contention that hip-hop and jazz have internal points of contact, not just philosophical ones. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/ATWOOD1/ATWOOD1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/ATWOOD1/ATWOOD1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/12/arts/ATWOOD1/ATWOOD1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Improvisation in Harlem, Corner of Hip-Hop and Jazz', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Improvisation in Harlem,Corner of Hip-Hop and Jazz', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harlem Stage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-11T22:29:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/65650da1-b527-5a91-9e47-df3ba1953b40', 'word_count': 675, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65650da1-b527-5a91-9e47-df3ba1953b40'}, {'abstract': 'Three recent thrillers — all set in the seedy underbelly of the finance world and written by former financiers — are bringing Wall Street minutiae to Main Street masses.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/11/in-wall-st-nov