porters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/what-were-reading-223/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T14:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55d4ca51-9efe-5cbe-88d0-e04c1c82ec0d', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55d4ca51-9efe-5cbe-88d0-e04c1c82ec0d'}, {'abstract': 'A new app delivers a twist on the check-in: Letting people broadcast where they are going, rather than where they are currently.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/forecast-app-tell-friends-where-youre-going/', 'snippet': 'A new app delivers a twist on the check-in: Letting people broadcast where they are going, rather than where they are currently.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Check-in applications like Foursquare tell your friends where you are at any given moment. Forecast, a new mobile application, tells you where your friends are going to be.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Forecast App Tells Friends Where You’re Going', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T14:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1659c573-a0d1-5037-8de9-267fabddb188', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1659c573-a0d1-5037-8de9-267fabddb188'}, {'abstract': 'The administration has been mocked for what proved to be overly optimistic projections for the economy. It seems to have learned its lesson, and then some.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/lowering-economic-expectations/', 'snippet': 'The administration has been mocked for what proved to be overly optimistic projections for the economy. It seems to have learned its lesson, and then some.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As my colleague David Leonhardt has written, government officials (both in the White House and at the Federal Reserve) have been overly optimistic in their economic forecasts.\xa0In particular, conservatives like to make fun of the administration’s rosy projections from 2009 for how the economy would behave under the Recovery Act; the administration’s economic advisers predicted unemployment today would be below 7 percent, rather than the 9.2 percent we’re actually stuck with. Buzzwords like “shovel-ready” and “Recovery Summer” have also become major mockery talking points.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lowering Economic Expectations', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Geithner, Timothy F', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T14:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8b47773-8532-51c9-ba58-d39c257d6817', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8b47773-8532-51c9-ba58-d39c257d6817'}, {'abstract': 'After almost 10 years of constant fighting, there are only nine Medal of Honor recipients in the American ranks, and only two of them are alive to actually wear it.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/longer-wars-fewer-medals-of-honor/', 'snippet': 'After almost 10 years of constant fighting, there are only nine Medal of Honor recipients in the American ranks, and only two of them are alive to actually wear it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, President Obama will award the Medal of Honor, the United States’ highest military decoration, to Sgt. First Class Leroy A. Petry, who lost his right hand in 2008 while tossing away an insurgent grenade that could have killed two of his fellow Army Rangers in Afghanistan. It will be only the second time that the medal has been given to a living soldier in the nearly 10 years since the Sept. 11 attacks, and only the ninth time the medal has been awarded to an Iraq or Afghanistan veteran.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Longer Wars, Fewer Medals of Honor', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medal of Honor (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lynch, Jessica', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tillman, Pat', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T14:01:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebekah Sanderlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebekah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanderlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95a4e344-789f-5d1e-b681-a033c4e66e5a', 'word_count': 1335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95a4e344-789f-5d1e-b681-a033c4e66e5a'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/morning-take-out-283/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An Addition to the List of Tax Loopholes | President Obama has talked about eliminating tax breaks for partners at hedge funds and private equity firms — along with those of corporate jet owners, oil companies and others taking advantage of quirks in the tax code.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T14:04:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bca90aaa-94b4-50f6-bb0e-0f0db3da41d8', 'word_count': 1843, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bca90aaa-94b4-50f6-bb0e-0f0db3da41d8'}, {'abstract': 'Gaz Métro of Canada, owner of the Green Mountain Power Corporation, said on Tuesday that it would buy the Central Vermont Public Service Corporation for $472 million in a deal that would join the two largest electric utilities in Vermont.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/gaz-metro-bests-fortis-to-buy-vermont-utility/', 'snippet': 'Gaz Métro of Canada, owner of the Green Mountain Power Corporation, said on Tuesday that it would buy the Central Vermont Public Service Corporation for $472 million in a deal that would join the two largest electric utilities in Vermont.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gaz Métro of Canada, the owner of the Green Mountain Power Corporation, said on Tuesday it would buy the Central Vermont Public Service Corporation for $472 million in a deal that would join the two largest electric utilities in Vermont.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gaz Metro Bests Fortis to Buy Vermont Utility', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Vermont Public Service Corporation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fortis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fortis Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vermont', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T14:10:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbe71979-0ed3-58b2-9bcb-2d6772b169bc', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbe71979-0ed3-58b2-9bcb-2d6772b169bc'}, {'abstract': 'Humberto Leon and Carol Lim, newly named creative directors of Kenzo, show no signs of stopping at Opening Ceremony with their latest collaboration.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/tucker-blair-for-opening-ceremony/', 'snippet': 'Humberto Leon and Carol Lim, newly named creative directors of Kenzo, show no signs of stopping at Opening Ceremony with their latest collaboration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Tuesday, Opening Ceremony’s Humberto Leon and Carol Lim were named creative directors of Kenzo, the vibrant, print-happy French house founded by the Japanese designer Kenzo Takada and owned by LVMH. And still, the two are showing no signs of slowing their constant flow of designer collaborations. Aiming to have you in stitches this summer, Opening Ceremony releases its first collection of needlepoint belts with Tucker Blair, the Washington-based purveyor of the classic preppy pant cinchers. Out with the lobsters, anchors and other yacht club clichés and in with leopard spots, hopping bunnies and fluorescents. A belt of international flags winks at the hip store’s Olympics-emulating moniker and its yearly “host” country shopping experience, celebrating the styles of different nations. Available exclusively at Opening Ceremony stores and online starting Friday, these embroidered beauties make for perfect waist watching.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tucker Blair for Opening Ceremony', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T14:12:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Pask', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pask', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/008f61ba-9e1a-50f4-9bcb-e78e8666d1d4', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/008f61ba-9e1a-50f4-9bcb-e78e8666d1d4'}, {'abstract': 'New York hospitals could lose more than $1 billion a year for training doctors and providing services like trauma centers and burn units.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/nyregion/deficit-plan-could-cost-ny-medical-centers-1-billion.html', 'snippet': 'New York hospitals could lose more than $1 billion a year for training doctors and providing services like trauma centers and burn units.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York State’s prestigious teaching hospitals could lose more than $1 billion a year as part of plans under negotiation in Washington to reduce the federal deficit that the hospitals say will lead to drastic service reductions.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/nyregion/hospitals1-1310509980087/hospitals1-1310509980087-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/13/nyregion/hospitals1-1310509980087/hospitals1-1310509980087-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/nyregion/hospitals1-1310509980087/hospitals1-1310509980087-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/nyregion/hospitals1-1310509980087/hospitals1-1310509980087-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/13/nyregion/hospitals1-1310509980087/hospitals1-1310509980087-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Effort to Cut U.S. Deficit, New York Teaching Hospitals May Lose Aid', 'kicker':