ecutive of New Mountain Capital, defends the accomplishments of his industry while acknowledging the praise for venture capital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Klinsky is founder and chief executive of New Mountain Capital and chairman of the Growth Capital Committee within his industry’s Private Equity Growth Capital Council.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Defending Private Equity From a Flawed Picture', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T19:31:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Klinsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klinsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42c1fee9-5268-5d4d-ac03-b6e9fee77454', 'word_count': 765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42c1fee9-5268-5d4d-ac03-b6e9fee77454'}, {'abstract': 'The scene along West 84th Street in Manhattan has changed in 50 years, with children’s sculptures replacing the weapons of yore.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/a-half-century-later-student-artwork-sprouts-where-sociopaths-roamed/', 'snippet': 'The scene along West 84th Street in Manhattan has changed in 50 years, with children’s sculptures replacing the weapons of yore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fifty years ago, 84th Street, between Columbus and Amsterdam Avenues, beat out some pretty stiff competition to be designated the “worst block” in New York City — a “ghetto of sociopaths,” in the words of the rector of a nearby Episcopal church who despaired that city officials would ever do anything to clean up the mess.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Half-Century Later, Student Artwork Sprouts Where ‘Sociopaths’ Roamed', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T19:39:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/344b7dbf-394c-57b2-ab34-9906e1cd66a5', 'word_count': 753, 'uri': 'nyt://article/344b7dbf-394c-57b2-ab34-9906e1cd66a5'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the results from a game that allowed readers to put themselves in the seats of the Wilmington, N.C. officials.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/fiscal-puzzle-trying-to-balance-the-budget/', 'snippet': 'Some of the results from a game that allowed readers to put themselves in the seats of the Wilmington, N.C. officials.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By at least one rather unscientific measure, armchair budget-makers seem to find it easier to close shortfalls by raising taxes than do elected officials who must bear the political brunt of their actions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiscal Puzzle: Trying to Balance a City Budget', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'n