/07/12/into-pistachio-country-in-southeastern-turkey/', 'snippet': 'The highly affordable Gaziantep is a culinary hot spot in Turkey, not to mention the country’s pistachio capital.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Into Pistachio Country, in Southeastern Turkey', 'kicker': 'Frugal Traveler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T19:47:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Kugel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kugel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12617e5c-eb7b-5d80-b78d-78e2c2805e7e', 'word_count': 1972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12617e5c-eb7b-5d80-b78d-78e2c2805e7e'}, {'abstract': 'Moody’s, the credit-ratings agency, said Ireland would probably need more international financing to recover.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/business/global/ireland-bonds-downgraded.html', 'snippet': 'Moody’s, the credit-ratings agency, said Ireland would probably need more international financing to recover.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Moody’s Investors Service downgraded Ireland’s government bonds on Tuesday, saying the country was likely to need another bailout before its finances recover.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ireland Bonds Downgraded', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ireland Bonds Downgraded', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Moody's Corporation", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T19:50:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victoria Shannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victoria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2b01275-c347-5895-ba99-a0def20caaef', 'word_count': 187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2b01275-c347-5895-ba99-a0def20caaef'}, {'abstract': 'We nonexperts are in no position to argue against the consensus of scientific experts.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/on-experts-and-global-warming/', 'snippet': 'We nonexperts are in no position to argue against the consensus of scientific experts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Stone is featuring occasional posts by Gary Gutting, a professor of philosophy at the University of Notre Dame, that apply critical thinking to information and events that have appeared in the news.Experts have always posed a problem for democracies.\xa0 Plato scorned democracy, rating it the worst form of government short of tyranny, largely because it gave power to the ignorant many rather than to knowledgeable experts (philosophers, as he saw it).\xa0 But, if, as we insist, the people must ultimately decide, the question remains: How can we, nonexperts, take account of expert opinion when it is relevant to decisions about public policy?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Experts and Global Warming', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T20:01:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Gutting', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gutting', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cced6276-b277-54df-a495-9b24d1b85aba', 'word_count': 899, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cced6276-b277-54df-a495-9b24d1b85aba'}, {'abstract': '“Henry V,” which is being staged outdoors by New York Classical Theater, is part theater, part audience workout.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/theater/reviews/henry-v-new-york-classical-theater-review.html', 'snippet': '“Henry V,” which is being staged outdoors by New York Classical Theater, is part theater, part audience workout.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are actors around spitting out Shakespeare, but the real star of the boisterous “Henry V” being staged outdoors by New York Classical Theater as part of the River to River Festival might be whatever’s on the feet of the audience members. A good pair of walking shoes is essential for this rollicking exercise in participatory theater, because the spectators turn into Henry’s army as he invades France, charging up hillsides and bellowing battle cries. Oh, and there’s a boat ride.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/arts/HENRY1/HENRY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 312, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/13/arts/HENRY1/HENRY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 312}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/arts/HENRY1/HENRY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 533, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Islands Are a Stage, and All Are Actors', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Henry V'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Islands Are a Stage, And All Are Actors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blanchard, Justin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'River to River Festival (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Governors Island (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Classical Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T20:03:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bd961a5-bb74-598f-860a-a536849f7989', 'word_count': 350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bd961a5-bb74-598f-860a-a536849f7989'}, {'abstract': 'The ratio for May was more than twice the average seen during the boom years that preceded the Great Recession.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/nearly-5-workers-for-every-available-job/', 'snippet': 'The ratio for May was more than twice the average seen during the boom years that preceded the Great Recession.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More bad news on the job market front: the number of jobless workers per job opening stayed flat at 4.7 in May, according to a new report from the Labor Department. That is more than twice the average ratio seen during the boom years that preceded the Great Recession.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nearly 5 Workers for Every Available Job', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-12T20:05:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7a6764d-cbcc-510a-9f6a-7d1a0b385077', 'word_count': 183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7a6764d-cbcc-510a-9f6a-7d1a0b385077'}, {'abstract': 'The creators of “The Magdalene,” a musical based on an alternate telling of the story of Mary Magdalene, have managed to take stunningly incendiary material and douse it with blandness and bathos.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/theater/reviews/in-the-magdalene-gnostic-gospels-take-the-stage-review.html', 'snippet': 'The creators of “The Magdalene,” a musical based on an alternate telling of the story of Mary Magdalene, have managed to take stunningly incendiary material and douse it with blandness and bathos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The creators of “The Magdalene,” a musical based on an alternate telling of the story of Mary Magdalene, have achieved the seemingly impossible: they have managed to take stunningly incendiary material and douse it with blandness and bathos. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Married, Reimagined Jesus', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Magdalene'", 'content_k