bama and Republicans, prompting the Senate Republican leader to propose a “last-choice option” that piqued the administration’s interest but angered conservatives in his own party. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/us/jp-FISCAL/jp-FISCAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/us/jp-FISCAL/jp-FISCAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/13/us/jp-FISCAL/jp-FISCAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Debt Talk Mired, Leader for G.O.P. Proposes Option', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Debt Talk Mired Leader For G.O.P. Proposes Options', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConnell, Mitch', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T01:20:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7c6778f-5daf-5424-9a81-602ab53e054e', 'word_count': 1154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7c6778f-5daf-5424-9a81-602ab53e054e'}, {'abstract': 'The last thing Britain needs is more restrictions on a free press. It should just enforce the laws it already has.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/opinion/13wed2.html', 'snippet': 'The last thing Britain needs is more restrictions on a free press. It should just enforce the laws it already has.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The phone hacking scandal in Britain that has already brought down The News of the World and now hangs over at least two other newspapers has exposed a culture of illegal intrusions, systematic bribes to corrupt police officials and thuggish threats of damaging publicity to silence criminal investigators. It is not over yet.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britain’s Press Scandal', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain’s Press Scandal: The last thing Britain needs is more restrictions on a free press. Just enforce the law.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cameron, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coulson, Andy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T01:22:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5bf3ab8-49ae-5b5f-8911-6032100bf51e', 'word_count': 458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5bf3ab8-49ae-5b5f-8911-6032100bf51e'}, {'abstract': 'Lottery numbers for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/nyregion/lottery-numbers-for-new-york-new-jersey-and-connecticut.html', 'snippet': 'Lottery numbers for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'July 12, 2011', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lottery Numbers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lottery Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lotteries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T01:25:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b702ed4d-659d-53e2-a4c0-d346e7d1d30b', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b702ed4d-659d-53e2-a4c0-d346e7d1d30b'}, {'abstract': 'The Civil War on land pitted former West Point friends against one another. It was a much different matter at sea.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/12/strange-waters/', 'snippet': 'The Civil War on land pitted former West Point friends against one another. It was a much different matter at sea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Civil War famously pitted brother against brother, but it also pitted comrade against comrade: especially in the opposing officer corps, the Union and Confederate armies were staffed with men who had earlier served on the same side, and in many cases were even at West Point together, both as students and instructors. One of the themes of Michael Shaara’s classic novel “The Killer Angels” is the close relationship between Lewis Armistead, who attended West Point but did not graduate, and Winfield Scott Hancock (class of 1844), who opposed each other at Gettysburg. No fewer than 20 members of the class of 1846 became Civil War general officers: 10 of them Union, 10 Confederate. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Strange Waters', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Military Academy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Naval Academy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Navy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANNAPOLIS (MD)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T01:26:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Craig L. Symonds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Symonds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72137f5f-e398-5349-854e-4ab702dd0178', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72137f5f-e398-5349-854e-4ab702dd0178'}, {'abstract': 'The drought in the Southeast and Southwest is the most extreme since the 1950s and possibly since the Dust Bowl of the 1930s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/opinion/13wed4.html', 'snippet': 'The drought in the Southeast and Southwest is the most extreme since the 1950s and possibly since the Dust Bowl of the 1930s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Right now, the official drought map of the United States looks as if it has been set on fire and scorched at the bottom edge. Scorched is how much of the Southeast and Southwest feel, in the midst of a drought that is the most extreme since the 1950s and possibly since the Dust Bowl of the 1930s. The government has classified much of this drought as D4, which means exceptional. The outlook through late September shows possible improvement in some places, but in most of Texas, Oklahoma, southern Arkansas, and northern Louisiana and Mississippi the drought is expected to worsen.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Suffering in the Parched South', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Suffering in the Parched South', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drought', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southwestern States (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T01:31:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa992dfb-5365-58fe-a256-de0d21463dee', 'word_count': 266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa992dfb-5365-58fe-a256-de0d21463dee'}, {'abstract': 'James Murdoch should stage his own tactical retreat and resign from the News Corporation. The heir apparent to his father’s media empire has lost authority.', 'web