n_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Lovett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba9a0f1b-1a76-5410-83df-a347770e7b8c', 'word_count': 426, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba9a0f1b-1a76-5410-83df-a347770e7b8c'}, {'abstract': 'The Chinese economy recorded a slowdown from the sizzling pace of previous quarters that underlined the challenges that now face policy makers in Beijing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/business/global/chinas-economy-slowed-a-bit-in-the-2nd-quarter.html', 'snippet': 'The Chinese economy recorded a slowdown from the sizzling pace of previous quarters that underlined the challenges that now face policy makers in Beijing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG -- The Chinese economy expanded at an unexpectedly robust pace during the second quarter of this year, data released Wednesday showed, but a slowdown from the sizzling pace of previous quarters also underlined the challenges that now face policy makers in Beijing.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/business/Yuan/Yuan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China’s Economy Slowed a Bit in the 2nd Quarter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China’s Growth Slowed To 9.5% in 2nd Quarter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gross Domestic Product', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inflation (Economics)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T08:03:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bettina Wassener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bettina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wassener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e56dc87-de6a-58dc-97d5-6f7a87e6ef57', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e56dc87-de6a-58dc-97d5-6f7a87e6ef57'}, {'abstract': 'Shamans’ practices in Peru and Bolivia win over Vance Gellert, a pharmacological expert who has devoted himself to photography.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/photographing-the-mystery-of-healing/', 'snippet': 'Shamans’ practices in Peru and Bolivia win over Vance Gellert, a pharmacological expert who has devoted himself to photography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Vance Gellert studied pharmacology in the early ’70s, he found that a scientific method of systematic observation, precise measurement and disciplined testing could explain the efficacy of most treatments. For that matter, it was a satisfying way of explaining much of the world around him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photographing the Mystery of Healing', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bolivia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Peru', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T09:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9252e951-e583-5912-9d48-32a943638681', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9252e951-e583-5912-9d48-32a943638681'}, {'abstract': 'The House majority whip may have the toughest job in Washington: persuading dozens of Congressional Tea Party freshmen to play well with others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/magazine/how-kevin-mccarthy-wrangles-the-tea-party.html', 'snippet': 'The House majority whip may have the toughest job in Washington: persuading dozens of Congressional Tea Party freshmen to play well with others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most mornings when he is in Washington, the House majority whip, Kevin McCarthy, wakes up at 6 a.m. on his office sofa — which is a slight improvement over the air mattress he once slept on, and probably better than the houseboat that a fellow Republican congressman, Lynn Westmoreland, has half-jokingly tried to convince him to share. McCarthy pulls on some ratty gym clothes, descends three flights of stairs and exits the building, where, when the weather is agreeable, he meets up with about a half-dozen other G.O.P. members on their mountain bikes. Together they cruise along the Mall, past the Lincoln Memorial, across a bridge and along the Potomac River. An hour later, McCarthy will shower in the House gym, head off to breakfast with some other gaggle of Republicans, attend the morning management meeting with Speaker John Boehner and Eric Cantor, the majority leader, huddle throughout the day with his whip staff and committee chairmen and conservative advocacy groups and random needy or disgruntled members until it’s finally evening and time to round up a bunch of other Republican colleagues for a dinner that can go on for several hours because McCarthy takes it upon himself to draw the others out from their professional shells with questions like, “What’s the most embarrassing thing that happened to you at college?” or “What was the first concert you went to?” By 11 at night, he is back on his office couch with his BlackBerry and the next day’s briefings, some 2,300 miles from his wife and son and daughter in Bakersfield, Calif. — and then asleep, in a federal building, by 1 a.m.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17freshman_span/17freshman_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 412, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17freshman_span/17freshman_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 412}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17freshman_span/17freshman_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17freshman_span/17freshman_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17freshman_span/17freshman_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Kevin McCarthy Wrangles the Tea Party in Washington', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "There's No 'I' In 'Tea'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Kevin (1965- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T09:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Draper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce5cea47-28c0-5f25-9dbd-02d83f20007f', 'word_count': 5113, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce5cea47-28c0-5f25-9dbd-02d83f20007f'}, {'abstract': 'Hope Solo was at the center of the United States comeback in the team’s win over Brazil in the quarterfinals, but that was nothing compared with her own personal and professional turnaround in the past four years.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/hope-solos-greatest-save/', 'snippet': 'Hope Solo was at the center of the United States comeback in the team’s win over Brazil in the quarterfinals, but that was nothing compared with her own personal and professional turnaround in the past four years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hope Solo was at the center of the United States comeback in the team’s win over Brazil in the quarterfinals, but that was nothing compared with her own personal and professional turnaround in the past four years. Solo was the Americans’ goalkeeper at the start of the last World Cup, too, but was benched by her coach and then banished by her teammates for what was seen as the unforgivable sin of criticizing the player who replaced her for the semifinal match. Now, it seems, all is right in her world again. Read Jeré Longman’s article here, and come back to comment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hope Solo’s Big Save', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T09:11:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b23eae3a-8977-521e-a600-b3f136dc806a', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b23eae3a-8977-521e-a600-b3f136dc806a'}, {'abstract': 'From March through December, the beautiful and intimate Sainte-Chapelle plays host to a series of classical concerts, and the programs for July and August are particularly rich.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/in-paris-classical-music-at-a-holy-site/', 'snippet': 'From March through December, the beautiful and intimate Sainte-Chapelle plays host to a series of classical concerts, and the programs for July and August are particularly rich.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From March through December, the beautiful and intimate Ste. Chapelle plays host to a series of concerts. Hidden behind the walls of the Palace of Justice, in the Ile de la Cité, the historical heart of Paris, the spot (8, Boulevard du Palais, 33-104-407-1238) makes a magical site for glorious music.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Paris, Classical Music at a Holy Site', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Churches (Buildings)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2be6a426-fd39-5343-8c28-a601a7a058b1', 'word_count': 270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2be6a426-fd39-5343-8c28-a601a7a058b1'}, {'abstract': 'Increasing the minimum wage is likely to cut employment, particularly among the least educated and the unskilled, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/where-a-minimum-wage-increase-would-bite/', 'snippet': 'Increasing the minimum wage is likely to cut employment, particularly among the least educated and the unskilled, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Casey B. Mulligan is an economics professor at the University of Chicago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where a Minimum-Wage Increase Would Bite', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T10:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Casey B. Mulligan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Casey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0940855e-9cd7-5d90-a04c-1ea44268141c', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0940855e-9cd7-5d90-a04c-1ea44268141c'}, {'abstract': 'To N.F.L. insiders, Cosell is a treasure trove of information. Pro football writers and broadcasters regularly hit him up privately for analysis. General managers ask for his assessment of draft prospects.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/greg-cosell-unheralded-maestro-of-n-f-l-matchup/', 'snippet': 'To N.F.L. insiders, Cosell is a treasure trove of information. Pro football writers and broadcasters regularly hit him up privately for analysis. General managers ask for his assessment of draft prospects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a February afternoon in Indianapolis. The former Auburn quarterback and Heisman winner Cam Newton is under his first N.F.L. microscope. On live national television, in front of some 200 reporters at Podium C in the N.F.L. scouting combine media center, Newton is fielding (fending off) rapid-fire questions about expectations for the N.F.L., his character, his level of “football focus,” his baggage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greg Cosell, Unheralded Maestro of N.F.L. ‘Matchup’', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NFL Films', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T10:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c6ddbcb-b2ad-5b0b-8b8d-082763525dab', 'word_count': 2884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c6ddbcb-b2ad-5b0b-8b8d-082763525dab'}, {'abstract': 'The European Commission on Wednesday presented proposals for tracking terrorist finances in Europe aimed at ending the primary role of the U.S. in those efforts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/world/europe/14terror.html', 'snippet': 'The European Commission on Wednesday presented proposals for tracking terrorist finances in Europe aimed at ending the primary role of the U.S. in those efforts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRUSSELS — The European Commission on Wednesday presented proposals for tracking the finances of terrorists in Europe that are aimed at ending the primary role of the United States in those efforts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Europe Tries to Curb U.S. Role in Tracking Terrorists’ Funds', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Europe Tries to Curb U.S. Role in Tracking Terrorists’ Fund', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Commission', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SWIFT (Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial Telecommunication)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T10:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Kanter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kanter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcd98fe8-94f9-5a92-8da9-72c814262fb1', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcd98fe8-94f9-5a92-8da9-72c814262fb1'}, {'abstract': 'The annual Hill Cumorah Pageant near Manchester, N.Y., tells the story of the Mormon Church, Cecil B. DeMille-style, and draws thousands of devoted visitors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/nyregion/hill-cumorah-pageant-offers-mormon-spectacle-way-off-broadway.html', 'snippet': 'The annual Hill Cumorah Pageant near Manchester, N.Y., tells the story of the Mormon Church, Cecil B. DeMille-style, and draws thousands of devoted visitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANCHESTER, N.Y. — With its cast of 700, 1,300 costumes, 10-level stage and thrill-a-minute special effects of earthquakes, floods, fireballs, airborne deities and burnings at the stake, the annual Hill Cumorah Pageant, one could argue, has more in common with the spectacle of “Spider-Man” than with the merry snark of “The Book of Mormon.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/nyregion/MORMON1/MORMON1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 269, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/14/nyregion/MORMON1/MORMON1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 269}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/nyregion/MORMON1/MORMON1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mormon Spectacle, Way Off Broadway', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Mormon Spectacle, Way Off Broadway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mormons (Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_d