weighed down with a sense that they were hurtling toward a crisis, and the pressure was particularly intense on Republican leaders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Federal Reserve chairman, Ben S. Bernanke, warned on Wednesday of a “huge financial calamity” if President Obama and the Republicans cannot agree on a budget deal that allows the federal debt ceiling to be increased. Moody’s, the ratings agency, threatened a credit downgrade, citing a “rising possibility” that no deal would be reached before the government’s borrowing authority hits its limit on Aug. 2.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/us/14fiscal_337-span/14fiscal_337-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/14/us/14fiscal_337-span/14fiscal_337-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/us/14fiscal_337-span/14fiscal_337-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/us/14fiscal_337-span/14fiscal_337-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/14/us/14fiscal_337-span/14fiscal_337-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tensions Escalate as Stakes Grow in Fiscal Clash', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dire Warning Amid Discord On Debt Talks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConnell, Mitch', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T18:40:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Hulse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hulse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b780849d-c45b-5890-a674-1513136881d8', 'word_count': 1303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b780849d-c45b-5890-a674-1513136881d8'}, {'abstract': 'Artists have been invited to tag the side of Rag & Bone’s Houston Street store as part of an continuing project.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/rag-bones-urban-art-project/', 'snippet': 'Artists have been invited to tag the side of Rag & Bone’s Houston Street store as part of an continuing project.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve been in Soho this past week, you may have noticed the monster-size rooster applied to the side of Rag & Bone’s Houston Street store (73 East Houston Street). Curious? So were we. Turns out the designers Marcus Wainwright and David Neville have invited visiting artists to tag their building’s wall as part of a continuing project. (Brooklyn’s ballpoint-pen extraordinaire Nicholas Forker started the series in May with an installation called “Action Man.”) What’s up now is a piece by the graffiti street artist Gaia. Here, a video of the three-day installation reveals him applying a wheat-paste backdrop of Peter Bruegel’s “The Triumph of Death,” then painting the rooster on top. As in many of Gaia’s neighborhood paintings, in which animals recur as protectors of the streets, this rooster is just looking out. Not that anyone is calling New Yorkers chicken.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rag & Bone’s Urban Art Project', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T18:43:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae1a7c06-6361-57fd-a82f-813e4eb80387', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae1a7c06-6361-57fd-a82f-813e4eb80387'}, {'abstract': 'Egypt’s transitional military government announced the early retirement of more than 600 senior police officers Wednesday in an effort to mollify thousands of protesters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/world/middleeast/14egypt.html', 'snippet': 'Egypt’s transitional military government announced the early retirement of more than 600 senior police officers Wednesday in an effort to mollify thousands of protesters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — Egypt’s transitional military government announced the early retirement of more than 600 senior police officers on Wednesday. It was an effort to mollify thousands of protesters at a six-day-old protest in Cairo’s Tahrir Square who have been demanding swifter justice for those complicit in wrongdoing under President Hosni Mubarak, who was ousted in February. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/world/14egypt-inline1/14egypt-inline1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/world/14egypt-inline1/14egypt-inline1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/14/world/14egypt-inline1/14egypt-inline1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt Purges Mubarak-Era Police Officers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt Purges Police Force Amid Protests', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hisham', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T18:44:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95f2fa0d-da34-5da7-88a5-8e2868e2c3e5', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95f2fa0d-da34-5da7-88a5-8e2868e2c3e5'}, {'abstract': 'Workers without proper documentation must be terminated, no matter how devastating it might be for a small business.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/business/smallbusiness/how-a-small-business-can-survive-an-immigration-audit.html', 'snippet': 'Workers without proper documentation must be terminated, no matter how devastating it might be for a small business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Cox was at his desk in September 2009, when his receptionist announced an unexpected visitor, a special agent from Immigration and Customs Enforcement, also known as ICE. Mr. Cox is chief executive of L. E. Cooke Company, a fourth-generation, family-owned nursery in Visalia, Calif., that grows deciduous trees and shrubs. The agent handed Mr. Cox a letter and informed him he had three days to produce I-9 employment-eligibility forms for all current employees. Mr. Cox said the agent was “pleasant and nonthreatening,” but he noticed she carried a gun. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/business/smallbusiness/14sbiz/14sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/14/business/smallbusiness/14sbiz/14sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/business/smallbusiness/14sbiz/14sbiz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/business/smallbusiness/14sbiz/14sbiz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/14/business/smallbusiness/14sbiz/14sbiz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Immigration Audits Increase, Some Employers Pay a High Price', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Immigration Audits Increase, Some Employers Pay a High Price', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Family Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Immigration and Customs Enforcement (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T18:45:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adriana Gardella', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adriana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gardella', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5605b973-8cb3-5619-ab83-a1e5d939022c', 'word_count': 1286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5605b973-8cb3-5619-ab83-a1e5d939022c'}, {'abstract': 'The service expects to begin one-way rentals of the Smart Fortwo Electric Drive in the city late this year.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/car2go-daimler-backed-sharing-program-to-go-electric-in-san-diego/', 'snippet': 'The service expects to begin one-way rentals of the Smart Fortwo Electric Drive in the city late this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'San Diego will soon be home to an all-electric, one-way car sharing service from Car2go, the Daimler subsidiary. The first of its kind in the United States, the program would allow members to rent the Smart Fortwo Electric Drive by the minute in a city that is becoming a focal point for E.V. launches.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Car2go, Daimler-Backed Sharing Program, to Go Electric in San Diego', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Exxon Mobil Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ZipCar', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Diego (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T18:49:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josie Garthwaite', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garthwaite', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1ae8148-38b4-5ecd-93c0-21d733cf4a1e', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1ae8148-38b4-5ecd-93c0-21d733cf4a1e'}, {'abstract': 'And what if it’s an employee who’s been with you for years and who has been playing an increasingly important role at your company? What do you do?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/what-if-you-suspect-an-employee-is-undocumented/', 'snippet': 'And what if it’s an employee who’s been with you for years and who has been playing an increasingly important role at your company? What do you do?', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’ve just published an article by Adriana Gardella that describes the impact of stepped-up enforcement by Immigration and Customs Enforcement, also known as ICE, on American small businesses. It cites the example of David Cox, who runs a family-owned nursery in Visalia, Calif., that was one of 1,444 businesses audited by ICE in 2009. Last year, 2,196 businesses were audited.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What If You Suspect an Employee is Undocumented?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': N