[{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T18:55:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sebnem Arsu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebnem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2d7140b-b271-51b6-bffb-cf31b04f16df', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2d7140b-b271-51b6-bffb-cf31b04f16df'}, {'abstract': 'Will the fatal fall of a fan at the Rangers Ballpark and the near tragedy of a fan taking a tumble at the Home Run Derby on Monday prompt Major League Baseball to review safety at its ballparks? It doesn’t appear so.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/will-anything-be-done-about-falling-fans/', 'snippet': 'Will the fatal fall of a fan at the Rangers Ballpark and the near tragedy of a fan taking a tumble at the Home Run Derby on Monday prompt Major League Baseball to review safety at its ballparks? It doesn’t appear so.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Following the death of the Texas firefighter Shannon Stone at a Rangers game last week and the potentially tragic near spill of another fan at the Home Run Derby at Phoenix’s Chase Field, there is no indication that Major League Baseball will order up a full-scale review of ballpark safety.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Anything Be Done About Falling Fans?', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Selig, Bud', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stone, Shannon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T18:58:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1621487-ca35-5ab0-868a-e8f8de0dd742', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1621487-ca35-5ab0-868a-e8f8de0dd742'}, {'abstract': 'Sherwood Schwartz, the creator of “Gilligan’s Island” and “The Brady Bunch” who died this week, was ahead of his time when it comes to notions of what makes a family, writes the comedian Judy Gold.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/an-appraisal-sherwood-schwartzs-modern-families/', 'snippet': 'Sherwood Schwartz, the creator of “Gilligan’s Island” and “The Brady Bunch” who died this week, was ahead of his time when it comes to notions of what makes a family, writes the comedian Judy Gold.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sherwood Schwartz passed away Tuesday at the ripe old age of 94. Sherwood Schwartz might be one of those names that you recognize, but you can’t remember from where. He created two of the most beloved sitcoms of the 1960s and 70s – “Gilligan’s Island” and “The Brady Bunch.” You may write these off as shlock, just like the critics did, but I’m sure you can recite the theme songs – both written by Schwartz, himself. He always said, “Tell them everything they need to know in the theme song.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Appraisal:  Sherwood Schwartz’s Modern Families', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T19:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Gold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cac07553-b46c-5a3f-ba01-28d8a5fae0e5', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cac07553-b46c-5a3f-ba01-28d8a5fae0e5'}, {'abstract': 'The case of Dominique Strauss-Kahn and the hotel housekeeper has thrown a spotlight on the fictions of asylum seekers. To readers of contemporary fiction, this may sound familiar: In “How to Read the Air,” a novel by Dinaw Mengestu published last year, the narrator describes how he worked at a refugee resettlement center, sorting the applications of asylum seekers — and embellishing them.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/13/the-fictions-of-asylum-seekers-in-fiction/', 'snippet': 'The case of Dominique Strauss-Kahn and the hotel housekeeper has thrown a spotlight on the fictions of asylum seekers. To readers of contemporary fiction, this may sound familiar: In “How to Read the Air,” a novel by Dinaw Mengestu published last ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The case of Dominique Strauss-Kahn and the hotel housekeeper has thrown a spotlight on the fictions of asylum seekers, with the revelation that the West African woman who accused Strauss-Kahn of sexual assault fabricated details of her asylum application to the United States.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fictions of Asylum Seekers — in Fiction', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asylum, Right of', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T19:00:26+0000', 'document_