o the subterfuge and turned it to his advantage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/crosswords/bridge/roland-wald-of-bridge-base-online-executes-steppingstone.html', 'snippet': 'West tried to hide a five-card suit, but Roland Wald was wise to the subterfuge and turned it to his advantage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bridge Base Online broadcasts the best bridge from around the planet. But the toughest job is arranging commentators, experts who type about the bidding and play as it occurs. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/13/arts/card1/card1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 237, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sniffing Out a Diamond Ruse, Then Finding a Steppingstone', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sniffing Out a Diamond Ruse, Then Finding a Steppingstone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T22:06:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cc23383-46dc-5061-98d6-ced17392a6a2', 'word_count': 439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cc23383-46dc-5061-98d6-ced17392a6a2'}, {'abstract': 'At the River to River Festival, Brooklyn Rider, a quirky string quartet, opened with a Mozart piece, then took a sharp turn toward Brazil and Japan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/arts/music/brooklyn-rider-at-michael-schimmel-center-for-the-arts-review.html', 'snippet': 'At the River to River Festival, Brooklyn Rider, a quirky string quartet, opened with a Mozart piece, then took a sharp turn toward Brazil and Japan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brooklyn Rider has found a handful of fascinating ways to keep its repertory both fresh and broad. Its involvement with Yo-Yo Ma’s Silk Road Project has helped this quirky string quartet understand musical styles beyond the Western mainstream and has put it in contact with potential collaborators who play non-Western instruments. Because a couple of the quartet’s members compose and arrange, they can supplement commissions from other composers with works of their own. And every now and then the group fills out its playlist the old-fashioned way: by looking back at the conventional repertory and spending some quality time with, say, a Mozart quartet. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/arts/Rider/Rider-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 538, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/arts/Rider/Rider-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/14/arts/Rider/Rider-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Foursome’s Boundless Musical Map', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Foursome’s Boundless Musical Map', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'River to River Festival (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Rider', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-13T22:09:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/14fdb627-aa57-59fc-a61d-32aa93da5b7a', 'word_count': 429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14fdb627-aa57-59fc-a61d-32aa93da5b7a'}, {'abstract': 'Rather than selling individual tracks to be downloaded, Spotify, from Europe, sells subscriptions to vast catalogs of music.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/technology/spotify-music-streaming-service-comes-to-us.html', 'snippet': 'Rather than selling individual tracks to be downloaded, Spotify, from Europe, sells subscriptions to vast catalogs of music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Daniel Ek, the 28-year-old co-founder and public face of Spotify, the European digital music service, paced around the company’s loftlike Manhattan office on Tuesday afternoon, clutching two mobile phones that buzzed constantly. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/business/Spotify1/Spotify1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/business/Spotify1/Spotify1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/14/business/Spotify1/Spotify1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Service Offers Music in Quantity, Not 