rving what they call “the music of Morse” tap out a tribute by resurrecting an old radio station.', 'lead_paragraph': 'POINT REYES NATIONAL SEASHORE, Calif. — It has been a little more than a decade since the last of the nation’s commercial Morse code radio stations officially went off the air, as new technology sank a system that had been a lingua franca of maritime communication since before the Titanic.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/us/MORSE-1/MORSE-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/14/us/MORSE-1/MORSE-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/us/MORSE-1/MORSE-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/us/MORSE-1/MORSE-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/14/us/MORSE-1/MORSE-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For a Night Each Year, the Airwaves Buzz With Morse Code', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For a Night Each Year, the Airwaves Buzz With Morse Code', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Codes (Ciphers)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Point Reyes National Seashore (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T03:35:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse McKinley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec3497ce-17cc-566d-be1d-65fc4ceb3898', 'word_count': 966, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec3497ce-17cc-566d-be1d-65fc4ceb3898'}, {'abstract': 'Los Angeles city councilwoman Janice Hahn survived a costly battle to defeat Republican businessman Craig Huey for a Congressional seat in a special election.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/us/14brfs-DEMOCRATWINS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Los Angeles city councilwoman Janice Hahn survived a costly battle to defeat Republican businessman Craig Huey for a Congressional seat in a special election.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Los Angeles city councilwoman, Janice Hahn, survived a costly battle to hold onto a Congressional seat for Democrats in Los Angeles on Tuesday, defeating a Republican businessman, Craig Huey, in a special election. Ms. Hahn collected 55 percent of the vote to Mr. Huey’s 45 percent, according to a semiofficial tally. She is expected to be sworn in next week to succeed the 36th Congressional District’s longtime congresswoman, Jane Harman, who retired in February. The 36th District has long been a Democratic stronghold. Turnout was low, with only 22 percent of registered voters casting ballots. Mr. Huey was able to keep the race competitive in part because he spent nearly $900,000 of his own money on the campaign. But he could not overcome Ms. Hahn’s Democratic advantage. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California: Democrat Wins House Race', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WEST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California: Democrat Wins House Race', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywor