ne, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/aa7d3912-77c5-58a6-a9b7-0ce30963be17', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/aa7d3912-77c5-58a6-a9b7-0ce30963be17'}, {'abstract': 'The public editor asks for readers’ advice for a possible future column.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/p-r-professionals-bane-or-boon/', 'snippet': 'The public editor asks for readers’ advice for a possible future column.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week I wrote about how The New York Times is curbing technology columnist David Pogue’s paid speeches in front of P.R. groups. Many readers lauded The Times’s efforts, but I also received a good deal of feedback from readers who took exception with how I portrayed P.R. professionals and said that I did not fully understand the role they play in the everyday machinations of The Times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'P.R. professionals: bane or boon?', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T16:24:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arthur S. Brisbane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arthur', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Brisbane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/571d1162-66f3-51a3-94ec-7e1099f7cce8', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/571d1162-66f3-51a3-94ec-7e1099f7cce8'}, {'abstract': 'Emergency room overcrowding is a formidable challenge that even one surgeon dedicated to emergency care couldn’t address for his mother.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/when-hospital-overcrowding-becomes-personal/', 'snippet': 'Emergency room overcrowding is a formidable challenge that even one surgeon dedicated to emergency care couldn’t address for his mother.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early in my residency, I realized that like Pavlov’s Russian dogs of yore, the other surgeons-in-training and I had developed a conditioned response to our electronic pagers. Our blood would rush and our breath disappear at the sight of one five-digit extension on our beeper’s screen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Hospital Overcrowding Becomes Personal', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T16:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pauline W. Chen, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pauline', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe3a33d4-4a2b-55cb-989c-a0606e5945e2', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe3a33d4-4a2b-55cb-989c-a0606e5945e2'}, {'abstract': 'A musician from Southern California has composed a six-disc suite, available for free, to soothe Angelenos affected by the 405 freeway closure.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/to-ease-carmageddon-woes-a-soothing-soundtrack-from-a-california-composer/', 'snippet': 'A musician from Southern California has composed a six-disc suite, available for free, to soothe Angelenos affected by the 405 freeway closure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This weekend, Los Angeles residents face a 53-hour closing of the 405 freeway, the primary North-South thoroughfare through the city’s West Side. The planned work threatens to clog surface streets in the vicinity of the interstate. Yet for one local commuter, who also is a musician, the impending traffic jams represent a green light to practice his craft.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Ease Carmageddon Woes, a Soothing Soundtrack From a California Composer', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lady Gaga', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T16:30:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Kurczewski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurczewski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0795de5d-f17a-559d-bba2-309fb6aa5069', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0795de5d-f17a-559d-bba2-309fb6aa5069'}, {'abstract': 'The Amazon tablet, which is expected to be introduced later this year, has been in the works for more than a year as executives at the company have struggled with a number of challenges.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/the-amazon-android-tablet-conundrums/', 'snippet': 'The Amazon tablet, which is expected to be introduced later this year, has been in the works for more than a year as executives at the company have struggled with a number of challenges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Waiting for Amazon to sell a full-fledged tablet feels like waiting for the grass to grow: we know it’s going to happen, but it’s taking an\xa0awfully\xa0long time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Amazon’s Tough Decisions on Its Android Tablet', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amazon Kindle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Android (Operating System)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Books and Readers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bezos, Jeffrey P', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AT&T Inc', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T16:30:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55ff5ec0-1a19-52dd-8c9e-0a532d71dab2', 'word_count': 669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55ff5ec0-1a19-52dd-8c9e-0a532d71dab2'}, {'abstract': 'Thomas Bjorn blew a three-shot lead with four holes to play in the 2003 British Open at Royal St. George’s, but jumped to a tie for the first-round lead on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/sports/golf/thomas-bjorn-8-years-later.html', 'snippet': 'Thomas Bjorn blew a three-shot lead with four holes to play in the 2003 British Open at Royal St. George’s, but jumped to a tie for the first-round lead on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SANDWICH, England — People have been tiptoeing around Thomas Bjorn and his memories of Royal St. George’s for eight years now.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/sports/golf-1/golf-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 783, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/sports/golf-1/golf-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/sports/golf-1/golf-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Depths at St. George’s to the Top of the Leader Board', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Depths at St. George’s to the Top of the Leader Board', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'British Open (Golf)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bjorn, Thomas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T16:39:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7cf9eb9-9adb-5eb5-b3f0-3ef4d31f84c9', 'word_count': 1202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7cf9eb9-9adb-5eb5-b3f0-3ef4d31f84c9'}, {'abstract': 'Why Republicans might be courting a soak-the-rich future.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/the-tax-increases-well-get/', 'snippet': 'Why Republicans might be courting a soak-the-rich future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Continuing this week’s series on what Republicans are risking if they punt on a deficit deal, I recommend reading David Leonhardt’s column on tax increases from yesterday’s Times — and particularly the conclusion, in which he points out that however much wonks may love the idea of a comprehensive tax reform that eliminates credits, loopholes and deductions, it’s just as likely that taxes will go up the old-fashioned way:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tax Increases We’ll Get', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T16:44:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ec0a2e0-3080-59df-9eb6-89e10292fbf3', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ec0a2e0-3080-59df-9eb6-89e10292fbf3'}, {'abstract': 'A soldier returning from Afghanistan sees America with a newly grateful set of eyes.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/a-burger-and-a-happy-homecoming/', 'snippet': 'A soldier returning from Afghanistan sees America with a newly grateful set of eyes.', 'lead_paragraph': ' I am a quarter of the way through my first, yearlong tour in Afghanistan. I recently left my base in eastern Afghanistan and made the three-day journey home to Michigan via Qatar, Kuwait, Ireland and Atlanta. During my layover in Atlanta waiting for my final flight, I did what any American would do after a long hiatus in a foreign land: I ate fast food. That’s how I found myself sitting at the Wendy’s in Concourse C of Atlanta’s Hartsfield-Jackson International Airport reflecting on my return to America.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Burger and a Happy Homecoming', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T16:46:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56fa5471-9e4d-5628-9775-5eb36a6910e4', 'word_count': 651, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56fa5471-9e4d-5628-9775-5eb36a6910e4'}, {'abstract': 'For Mireille Enos, an alumna of HBO’s widely praised “Big Love,” the nomination for “The Killing” is her first shot at an Emmy Award, a distinction she said she could not completely keep from her mind when she was approached to star in Veena Sud’s adaptation of the Danish series “Forbrydelsen.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/a-contentious-killing-gets-some-emmy-love/', 'snippet': 'For Mireille Enos, an alumna of HBO’s widely praised “Big Love,” the nomination for “The Killing” is her first shot at an Emmy Award, a distinction she said she could not completely keep from her mind when she was approached to star in Veena Sud’s...', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Mireille Enos learned Thursday morning that she’d been nominated for her portrayal of Detective Sarah Linden on the AMC drama “The Killing,” she was far from that murder mystery’s gloomy setting of Seattle, at least geographically. She was in London, filming the zombie horror film “World War Z,” when her manager and her publicist interrupted the undead apocalypse to advise her of the Emmy honor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Contentious ‘Killing’ Gets Some Emmy Love', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T16:48:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01effda7-7002-5781-b7ae-ec53e2b36dd4', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01effda7-7002-5781-b7ae-ec53e2b36dd4'}, {'abstract': 'Dr. Stephen Rose of the Foundation Fighting Blindess responds to readers’ questions about nutrients and vitamins to counter macular degeneration.', 'web_url': 'https://consults.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/vitamins-to-prevent-vision-loss/', 'snippet': 'Dr. Stephen Rose of the Foundation Fighting Blindess responds to readers’ questions about nutrients and vitamins to counter macular degeneration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Stephen Rose, chief research officer for the Foundation Fighting Blindness, joined the Consults blog to answer reader questions about age-related macular degeneration and related disorders. Several readers had questions about vitamins and nutrients to combat the vision loss of macular degeneration.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vitamins to Prevent Vision Loss', 'kicker': 'Consults', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIOXIDANTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eyes and Eyesight', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Macular Degeneration', 'rank': 4, 'major': '