': 635, 'uri': 'nyt://article/914858a1-e033-50d2-b4dc-c4cb8bfa1a8f'}, {'abstract': '“The Watsons” was never finished or published.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/jane-austen-manuscript-sold-for-1-6-million/', 'snippet': '“The Watsons” was never finished or published.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A heavily corrected unfinished draft of an early Jane Austen manuscript sold at auction at Sotheby’s in London on Thursday for almost 1 million pounds ($1.6 million), three times the estimated presale price, The Associated Press reported. The draft of “The Watsons” went to an unidentified buyer; Sotheby’s said it is the only major manuscript by Austen still in private hands. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jane Austen Manuscript Sold for $1.6 Million', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Manuscripts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austen, Jane', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN RED CROSS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sotheby's", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T17:06:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7598a0c4-333b-5c79-aa33-ccbad162227b', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7598a0c4-333b-5c79-aa33-ccbad162227b'}, {'abstract': 'The latest installment in George R. R. Martin’s “Song of Ice and Fire” cycle is like a sprawling 19th-century novel turned out in fantasy motley, more Balzac and Dickens than Tolkien.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/books/a-dance-with-dragons-by-george-r-r-martin-review.html', 'snippet': 'The latest installment in George R. R. Martin’s “Song of Ice and Fire” cycle is like a sprawling 19th-century novel turned out in fantasy motley, more Balzac and Dickens than Tolkien.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the arrival of “A Dance With Dragons,” Book 5 in George R. R. Martin’s rousing “Song of Ice and Fire” cycle, it’s high time we drove a stake through the heart of J. R. R. Tolkien and “The Lord of the Rings.” Like its predecessors “Dance” has its share of flagons ’n’ dragons, and swords ’n’ sorcerers, but that doesn’t make Mr. Martin the American Tolkien, as some would have it. He’s much better than that. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15book-1310663074717/15book-1310663074717-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15book-1310663074717/15book-1310663074717-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15book-1310663074717/15book-1310663074717-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Fantasyland of Liars, Trust No One, and Keep Your Dragon Close', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Fantasyland of Liars, Trust No One, and Keep Your Dragon Close', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, George R R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T17:07:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dana Jennings', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jennings', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/27df39bd-2e10-5fcd-8aaf-af10080971d7', 'word_count': 1020, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27df39bd-2e10-5fcd-8aaf-af10080971d7'}, {'abstract': 'There is debate over a recent agreement concerning improvement for the nation’s egg-laying hens.', 'web_url': 'https://bittman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/debate-over-the-egg-industry-agreement/', 'snippet': 'There is debate over a recent agreement concerning improvement for the nation’s egg-laying hens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, the Humane Society of the United States and the United Egg Producers issued a joint press release announcing their intent to urge Congress to pass an (evidently) already-agreed-upon series of improvements for the nation’s 280 million egg-laying hens. This sent most animal rights activists, much of the food policy press and many concerned, aware consumers into a frenzied victory dance. At least it appeared that way on my Twitter feed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debate Over the Egg Industry Agreement', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eggs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Humane Society of the United States', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Egg Producers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T17:19:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cd9a79a-5ee8-5a90-98c7-5d8c6b102b25', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cd9a79a-5ee8-5a90-98c7-5d8c6b102b25'}, {'abstract': 'The Minimalist artist Carl Andre is the subject of a new book from Phaidon Press and a coming exhibition at Dia:Beacon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/arts/design/carl-andres-work-is-the-subject-of-a-new-book-and-show.html', 'snippet': 'The Minimalist artist Carl Andre is the subject of a new book from Phaidon Press and a coming exhibition at Dia:Beacon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE work of Carl Andre, one of the only Minimalists to accept the term, is often as barely there as sculpture can get. In the first book to survey his ascetic half-century career, a maximalist doorstop published this month by Phaidon, the art historian Alistair Rider relates the story of a German artist who enthusiastically sought out Mr. Andre’s work at a gallery in 1967, only to become confused when he couldn’t find it. This was because the work, 100 square plates of hot-rolled steel arranged in a rectangle almost completely covering the gallery entryway, was directly beneath his feet. He had mistaken it for the flooring, a mistake that comes with the territory in Andre country, where art — as Mr. Andre has said — gets perilously “close to zero,” meant to be experienced as much with the body as with the eyes and mind.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/ANDRE-2_span/ANDRE-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/ANDRE-2_span/ANDRE-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/ANDRE-2_span/ANDRE-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 661, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/ANDRE-2_span/ANDRE-2_span-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/ANDRE-2_span/ANDRE-2_span-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Carl Andre, Less Is Still Less', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Carl Andre, Less Is Still Less', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANDRE, CARL', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dia: Beacon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Phaidon Press', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T17:22:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14179ab5-f4b2-5aef-9fa8-03e8a6f57039', 'word_count': 1878, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14179ab5-f4b2-5aef-9fa8-03e8a6f57039'}, {'abstract': 'JPMorgan Chase’s strong earnings led bank stocks higher early on, but only JPMorgan managed to hold on to its gains.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/bank-earnings-season-starts-on-a-high-note/', 'snippet': 'JPMorgan Chase’s strong earnings led bank stocks higher early on, but only JPMorgan managed to hold on to its gains.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bank earnings season has begun, a quarterly ritual in which the nation’s biggest banks emphasize their gains and explain away their losses on early-morning conference calls with analysts and investors. The stock market then gives its verdict.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bank Earnings Season Starts on a High Note', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dimon, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T17:23:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8f2bf30-dece-522c-b67d-7c6223c8346b', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8f2bf30-dece-522c-b67d-7c6223c8346b'}, {'abstract': 'The “Mad Men” creator talks about his coalescing plans to bring the series to a close and reflects on lessons he learned from his “Sopranos” boss, David Chase.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/matthew-weiner-explains-why-mad-men-will-hopefully-end-like-abbey-road/', 'snippet': 'The “Mad Men” creator talks about his coalescing plans to bring the series to a close and reflects on lessons he learned from his “Sopranos” boss, David Chase.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We couldn’t possibly encapsulate here all the twists and turns that transpired in the fourth season of “Mad Men,” which on Thursday was nominated for 19 Emmy Awards, and any attempt to do so would just keep you further away from our interview with Matthew Weiner, the creator and executive producer of that AMC period drama. So here are all the details we could squeeze from him on Thursday afternoon in a 10-minute conversation about the show’s latest Emmy bounty and his coalescing plans to end the series.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Matthew Weiner Explains Why ‘Mad Men’ Will (Hopefully) End Like ‘Abbey Road’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emmy Awards', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Matthew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T17:30:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a9548f3-41e7-5ced-8272-e08863bdb8ff', 'word_count': 802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a9548f3-41e7-5ced-8272-e08863bdb8ff'}, {'abstract': 'The N.F.L. Hall of Famer Jim Brown joins William C. Rhoden at the barbershop for a no-holds-barred video interview.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/the-buzz-around-jim-brown/', 'snippet': 'The N.F.L. Hall of Famer Jim Brown joins William C. Rhoden at the barbershop for a no-holds-barred video interview.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Hall of Famer Jim Brown has never been accused of being afraid to speak his mind, and he held back little when The Times’s William C. Rhoden got him to sit in the barber’s chair as part of a continuing series of interviews. Brown discusses his support of Muhammad Ali during the Vietnam War, the current N.F.L. labor situation, his concerns regarding the rule changes that reduce the physical nature of pro football, and why he left the game (Raquel Welch?) at age 29.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Buzz Around Jim Brown', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Jim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T17:35:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89ef0949-8345-5a3b-9959-6c15f6679b14', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89ef0949-8345-5a3b-9959-6c15f6679b14'}, {'abstract': 'Chicago’s literary heritage and reputation for design meet in the new Poetry Foundation center.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/in-chicago-a-home-for-poetry/', 'snippet': 'Chicago’s literary heritage and reputation for design meet in the new Poetry Foundation center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chicago’s literary heritage and reputation for design meet in the new Poetry Foundation center.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Chicago, a Home for Poetry', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T17:37:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b11bf55-d737-51da-af2b-eebbf1e46c10', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b11bf55-d737-51da-af2b-eebbf1e46c10'}, {'abstract': 'As the Pentagon announced a new cyberspace strategy, it disclosed a serious digital attack in March.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/world/15cyber.html', 'snippet': 'As the Pentagon announced a new cyberspace strategy, it disclosed a serious digital attack in March.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Defense Department suffered one of its worst digital attacks in history in March, when a foreign intelligence service hacked into the computer system of a corporate contractor and obtained 24,000 Pentagon files during a single intrusion, senior officials said Thursday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hackers Gained Access to Sensitive Military Files', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Suffering Damaging Cyberattack, the Pentagon Takes Defensive Action', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Cyberwarfare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lynn, William J III', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T17:42:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thom Shanker and Elisabeth Bumiller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shanker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bumiller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank'