umbers for the Presidential Candidates', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:29:52+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/01adc226-d12f-5390-a49f-baef5d60882d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/01adc226-d12f-5390-a49f-baef5d60882d'}, {'abstract': 'Nostylgia sells stylish clothes, coffee, tea and the pleasures of a gentler era amid an atmosphere of anything-goes eclecticism.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/in-one-place-store-cafe-and-hangout/', 'snippet': 'Nostylgia sells stylish clothes, coffee, tea and the pleasures of a gentler era amid an atmosphere of anything-goes eclecticism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On summer evenings, a tree-lined strip of Dyckman Street — a main thoroughfare in Inwood, near Manhattan’s northern prow — becomes a giant block party. Crowds from several splashy bars spill onto the sidewalk, clutching beers and cigarettes as they chatter loudly and peck away on their smartphones.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In One Place: Store, Cafe and Hangout', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'INWOOD (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:31:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisa Mala', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mala', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91d128b4-a74e-5de7-8e5e-144d4c077734', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91d128b4-a74e-5de7-8e5e-144d4c077734'}, {'abstract': 'A College Board report focuses on the transition from two-year community colleges to four-year institutions, a path many students take each year.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/community-college-transfer/', 'snippet': 'A College Board report focuses on the transition from two-year community colleges to four-year institutions, a path many students take each year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As many as four out of five community college students in the United States want to transfer to a four-year institution so they can obtain a bachelor’s degree, according to a report released Thursday by the College Board.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '4 Out of 5 in Community College Want to Transfer, Report Says', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:31:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily Altavena', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Altavena', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0e2ce62-811c-5d8d-b61c-182ccb1fb936', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0e2ce62-811c-5d8d-b61c-182ccb1fb936'}, {'abstract': 'Once the province of exclusive co-ops, lengthy applications and demands for years of tax returns and bank statements are turning up at condominiums.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/realestate/condos-demand-more-paperwork-from-buyers.html', 'snippet': 'Once the province of exclusive co-ops, lengthy applications and demands for years of tax returns and bank statements are turning up at condominiums.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AT first glance, 255 Hudson Street looks like a typical SoHo condominium, with a soaring glass facade, million-dollar listings and a chic designer lobby. But despite having housed entertainment figures like Bethenny Frankel and Kirsten Dunst, this building has more in common with a conservative Park Avenue co-op than a celebrity-friendly downtown high rise. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17jpdeal1/JP-Deal-A-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 703, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Condos Steal a Page (or 20) From Co-ops', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Condos Steal A Page (Or 20) From Co-ops', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Condominiums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:32:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Satow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Satow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a5de8df-7db8-504f-81e0-7acfcc2c1a03', 'word_count': 1270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a5de8df-7db8-504f-81e0-7acfcc2c1a03'}, {'abstract': 'A woman wonders if she should correct her boyfriend’s table manners, and questions about a name-dropper and a dating mother.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/fashion/correcting-a-boyfriends-table-manners-social-qs.html', 'snippet': 'A woman wonders if she should correct her boyfriend’s table manners, and questions about a name-dropper and a dating mother.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My boyfriend of five years, with whom I try not to nitpick, has terrible table manners. He eats standing up, with his fingers, and picks food off my plate without permission. When I raise the subject, it results in hurt feelings and no improvement. Then I spent time with his family: His dad eats the same way. Since my boyfriend finds his father’s table manners gross, would it be helpful to point out their similarity?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/02/21/fashion/21social-1/21social-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/02/21/fashion/21social-1/21social-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At the Table, Like Father, Like Son?', 'kicker': 'Social Q’s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At the Table, Like Father, Like Son?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:32:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/caea2d87-6272-5ef4-a26e-5e1816dde7ab', 'word_count': 801, 'uri': 'nyt://article/caea2d87-6272-5ef4-a26e-5e1816dde7ab'}, {'abstract': 'Jeni Aron decided to leave Midtown and look for an affordable rental in her favorite neighborhood, Cobble Hill, Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/realestate/after-rent-rise-time-to-pull-up-stakes.html', 'snippet': 'Jeni Aron decided to leave Midtown and look for an affordable rental in her favorite neighborhood, Cobble Hill, Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NOT all that long ago, rental prices were low enough for Jeni Aron to afford an alcove studio in a luxury doorman building, Hudson Crossing, on Ninth Avenue and 37th Street. She enjoyed a nicely furnished lobby, a landscaped patio, a storage room for her bicycle and a 24-hour gym that she used early in the morning. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/Hunt4/Hunt4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/Hunt4/Hunt4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/Hunt4/Hunt4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Rent Rise, Time to Pull Up Stakes', 'kicker': 'The Hunt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Time to Pull Up Stakes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cobble Hill (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:35:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c05ece4f-dd9e-54aa-86b5-6e66b6f6ba4c', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c05ece4f-dd9e-54aa-86b5-6e66b6f6ba4c'}, {'abstract': 'At a combative hearing in Washington, Republicans squared off against Elizabeth Warren, who is supervising the organization of the new Consumer Financial Protection Bureau.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/business/consumer-bureau-under-new-attacks-by-republicans.html', 'snippet': 'At a combative hearing in Washington, Republicans squared off against Elizabeth Warren, who is supervising the organization of the new Consumer Financial Protection Bureau.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — House Republicans threatened Thursday to roll back the scope and authority of the new Consumer Financial Protection Bureau even before it officially opens for business next week.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/business/Warren/Warren-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Republican Attacks Persist for Consumer Bureau', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Republican Attacks Persist for Consumer Bureau', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:37:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Lichtblau', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lichtblau', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a749bead-8290-5ee1-82a5-8feada10e660', 'word_count': 751, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a749bead-8290-5ee1-82a5-8feada10e660'}, {'abstract': '“Tabloid” revisits the case of the “Manacled Mormon,” which offered good, clean, dirty fun.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/movies/tabloid-errol-morriss-take-on-manacled-mormon-review.html', 'snippet': '“Tabloid” revisits the case of the “Manacled Mormon,” which offered good, clean, dirty fun.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By virtue of its title alone, “Tabloid,” Errol Morris’s astonishing new documentary, achieves a degree of serendipitous timeliness. Mr. Morris, as usual unseen, though occasionally heard shouting questions from off screen, trains his prying, voracious eyes on England in the 1970s. It was a time ripe with newspaper sleaze that offered premonitions of the present day, even as the prevailing vices, carnal and journalistic, may seem quaint by current standards.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/movies/15tabloid-sub/15tabloid-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 850, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/movies/15tabloid-sub/15tabloid-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/movies/15tabloid-sub/15tabloid-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Was It Love? The ‘Manacled Mormon’ and His Kinky Weekend', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Tabloid'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Was It Love? The ‘Manacled Mormon’ and His Kinky Weekend', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morris, Errol', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:39:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d307e54-0e21-5441-b5c9-9e3cee1b110b', 'word_count': 1188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d307e54-0e21-5441-b5c9-9e3cee1b110b'}, {'abstract': 'Five community boards and the City Council would like to close Central Park to cars during the summer. But for this reporter, driving through the park is a delightful escape from the maddening city traffic.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/the-guilty-pleasure-of-a-drive-through-central-park/', 'snippet': 'Five community boards and the City Council would like to close Central Park to cars during the summer. But for this reporter, driving through the park is a delightful escape from the maddening city traffic.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Go through the park,” Dudley Moore slurs to his chauffeur in the 1981 film “Arthur,” his character giddy and drunk and in need of simple and profound pleasure. “You know how I love the park.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Guilty Pleasure of a Drive Through Central Park', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CENTRAL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:39:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60b81fe4-6f82-5bb5-b9b8-8d138eb011fe', 'word_count': 629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60b81fe4-6f82-5bb5-b9b8-8d138eb011fe'}, {'abstract': 'Rufus Sewell plays an Italian detective seeking justice in a corrupt country in “Zen” on “Masterpiece Mystery!”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/arts/television/zen-with-rufus-sewell-review.html', 'snippet': 'Rufus Sewell plays an Italian detective seeking justice in a corrupt country in “Zen” on “Masterpiece Mystery!”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Dibdin, the British crime novelist and professional Italophile, died four years ago, having left behind 11 novels centered on Aurelio Zen, the stylish Venetian detective whom he dispatched throughout Italy to solve murders typically executed against the backdrop of political corruption, Mafia wrongdoing and aristocratic entitlement.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/ZEN/ZEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/ZEN/ZEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/ZEN/ZEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/ZEN/ZEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/ZEN/ZEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Sunny Rome, Solving a Murder Involves Sharp Suits and Sexy Talk', 'kicker': "Television Review | 'Zen'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Sunny Rome, Solving a Murder Involves Sharp Suits and Sexy Talk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sewell, Rufus', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:41:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b133246f-9d22-5e09-b870-9e6823b9c688', 'word_count': 563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b133246f-9d22-5e09-b870-9e6823b9c688'}, {'abstract': 'In the first quarter of 2011, the market share of adjustable-rate mortgage rose to the highest point since mid-2008, to 12 percent, according to a trade publication.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/realestate/why-some-borrowers-still-reach-for-arms-mortgages.html', 'snippet': 'In the first quarter of 2011, the market share of adjustable-rate mortgage rose to the highest point since mid-2008, to 12 percent, according to a trade publication.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE 30-year fixed-rate loan has earned its reputation as the sensible, conservative move in the aftermath of the financial crisis, especially with near-low interest rates. But despite risks, some borrowers still are getting or keeping adjustable-rate loans, which have even lower rates.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/realestate/mort-graphic/mort-graphic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 312, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/14/realestate/mort-graphic/mort-graphic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 312}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/14/realestate/mort-graphic/mort-graphic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/14/realestate/mort-graphic/mort-graphic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Appeal of Adjustable Rates', 'kicker': 'Mortgages', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Appeal of Adjustable Rates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:43:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maryann Haggerty', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maryann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haggerty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8fbebc2-cb13-5626-bf4a-c0953d61a540', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8fbebc2-cb13-5626-bf4a-c0953d61a540'}, {'abstract': 'In an interview with The Wall Street Journal, a newspaper he owns, Rupert Murdoch defended his son James and said that his company had responded to the phone-hacking scandal in Britain “extremely well.”', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/rupert-murdoch-defends-his-company-and-his-son/', 'snippet': 'In an interview with The Wall Street Journal, a newspaper he owns, Rupert Murdoch defended his son James and said that his company had responded to the phone-hacking scandal in Britain “extremely well.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an interview with The Wall Street Journal, a newspaper he owns, Rupert Murdoch defended the way his son James has handled the phone-hacking scandal and said that News Corporation had responded to the misconduct by its tabloid journalists “extremely well.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rupert Murdoch Defends His Company and His Son', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, James R', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Sky Broadcasting Group Plc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wall Street Journal', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:45:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee4d36a0-6a51-516b-907d-3ceb4f6f8c1b', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee4d36a0-6a51-516b-907d-3ceb4f6f8c1b'}, {'abstract': '“Life, Above All” traces the tribulations of a 12-year-old overburdened with responsibilities in a South Africa racked with AIDS.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/movies/life-above-all-aids-and-bias-in-south-africa-review.html', 'snippet': '“Life, Above All” traces the tribulations of a 12-year-old overburdened with responsibilities in a South Africa racked with AIDS.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Life, Above All,” a grave and quietly moving story about a South African girl of extraordinary character, does something that few painful dramas accomplish: It tells a tale of resilience without platitudes about the triumph of the human spirit or without false promises about an unclouded future. The battle fought by its 12-year-old heroine, who struggles to save her family and best friend from prejudiced neighbors who often look like a mob, may be over when the movie comes to its stirring close, but there’s no end to the fight.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/LIFE/LIFE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/LIFE/LIFE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/LIFE/LIFE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/LIFE/LIFE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/LIFE/LIFE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Burdened in a Ravaged South Africa', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Life, Above All'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Burdened in a Ravaged South Africa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:45:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b84bdfa-27be-591a-8661-0249d53e518d', 'word_count': 826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b84bdfa-27be-591a-8661-0249d53e518d'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Lane will play one of the stage’s great dramatic characters, the doomed salesman  Theodore (Hickey) Hickman, in the Goodman Theater’s revival of Eugene O’Neill’s “Iceman Cometh” in spring 2012.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/nathan-lane-to-star-in-the-iceman-cometh-in-chicago/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Lane will play one of the stage’s great dramatic characters, the doomed salesman  Theodore (Hickey) Hickman, in the Goodman Theater’s revival of Eugene O’Neill’s “Iceman Cometh” in spring 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nathan Lane will play one of the stage’s great dramatic characters — the doomed salesman Theodore (Hickey) Hickman — in the Goodman Theater’s revival of Eugene O’Neill’s “Iceman Cometh” in spring 2012, the Chicago-based theater announced on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nathan Lane to Star in ‘The Iceman Cometh’ in Chicago', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:46:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9e3d34d-6297-565f-a96b-2e0a464d73cd', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9e3d34d-6297-565f-a96b-2e0a464d73cd'}, {'abstract': 'Older buyers are taking advantage of big discounts, free upgrades and low mortgage interest rates.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/realestate/signs-of-improvement-in-age-restricted-market-in-the-regionnew-jersey.html', 'snippet': 'Older buyers are taking advantage of big discounts, free upgrades and low mortgage interest rates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STARTING in 2009, the bottom fell out of the age-restricted housing market in New Jersey, as people 55 and older made the decision to “age in place” rather than try to sell their longtime homes in an uncertain real estate environment.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17NJZO_Span/17NJZO_Span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17NJZO_Span/17NJZO_Span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17NJZO_Span/NJZO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17NJZO_Span/17NJZO_Span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17NJZO_Span/17NJZO_Span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Signs of Improvement in Age-Restricted Market', 'kicker': 'In the Region | New Jersey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aging Into a New Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:47:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Antoinette Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Antoinette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab947d38-1747-51ab-bdd8-82a5f85d4aae', 'word_count': 958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab947d38-1747-51ab-bdd8-82a5f85d4aae'}, {'abstract': 'The social buying site added 11 banks to its I.P.O., bringing the total number of financial firms working on the deal to 14.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/14/more-wall-street-banks-grab-groupon-i-p-o/', 'snippet': 'The social buying site added 11 banks to its I.P.O., bringing the total number of financial firms working on the deal to 14.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wall Street is lining up for a piece of Groupon’s initial public offering.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Wall Street Banks Grab Groupon I.P.O.', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Groupon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:49:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/468fc2d7-fd27-5de1-9f2c-42cc970eacea', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/468fc2d7-fd27-5de1-9f2c-42cc970eacea'}, {'abstract': 'A porch doesn’t necessarily add to a house’s value. But it has an emotional appeal that can help make the sale, real estate agents say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/realestate/porches-reclaim-their-place-in-the-regionwestchester.html', 'snippet': 'A porch doesn’t necessarily add to a house’s value. But it has an emotional appeal that can help make the sale, real estate agents say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNTIL recently, Lynn Hock, a designer with an architectural degree, was a midcentury modernist — “definitely not an old house person,” she said. But that changed this spring when, after years of looking for a home in the Hudson River village of Hastings-on-Hudson, she and her husband, Jonathan Hock, a documentary filmmaker, walked up the steps of an 1851 Gothic Victorian with a broad, welcoming porch laced with gingerbread trim — and fell in love. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17WCZO-1_Span/17WCZO-1_Span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17WCZO-1_Span/17WCZO-1_Span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17WCZO-1_Span/WCZO-A-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17WCZO-1_Span/17WCZO-1_Span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17WCZO-1_Span/17WCZO-1_Span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Porches Reclaim Their Place', 'kicker': 'In the Region | Westchester', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Porches Reclaim Their Place', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Porches', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:51:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elsa Brenner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elsa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brenner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9e02ed1-581e-5cd9-8610-3f2045f024be', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9e02ed1-581e-5cd9-8610-3f2045f024be'}, {'abstract': 'Clarifying the story of Robert S. Duncanson, an African-American painter who condemned slavery with his art; and two jewelry exhibitions with dark back stories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/arts/design/painter-robert-s-duncanson-and-2-jewelry-exhibitions.html', 'snippet': 'Clarifying the story of Robert S. Duncanson, an African-American painter who condemned slavery with his art; and two jewelry exhibitions with dark back stories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Misinformation has spread widely about Robert S. Duncanson, an African-American painter. Before and during the Civil War he specialized in portraits of abolitionists and landscapes as exotic as Roman ruins and Kashmir glades. Art historians rediscovered him in the 1920s, but soon muddied his biography with legends and typos.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/ANTIQUES-1/ANTIQUES-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/ANTIQUES-1/ANTIQUES-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/ANTIQUES-1/ANTIQUES-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Condemning Slavery With a Paintbrush', 'kicker': 'Antiques', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Condemning Slavery With a Paintbrush', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:51:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eve M. Kahn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eaa776ea-a52a-586c-afe1-159abca17afc', 'word_count': 1053, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eaa776ea-a52a-586c-afe1-159abca17afc'}, {'abstract': '“Pulp Art: The Robert Lesser Collection” is an entertaining and fascinating exhibition at the Museum of American Illustration at the Society of Illustrators.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/arts/design/illustrating-damsels-in-distress-and-emissaries-from-our-deep-dark-ids.html', 'snippet': '“Pulp Art: The Robert Lesser Collection” is an entertaining and fascinating exhibition at the Museum of American Illustration at the Society of Illustrators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor La Guardia was appalled. Out for a walk one day in Manhattan in 1942, he happened upon a store displaying a copy of a periodical called Spicy Mystery. In lurid hues and slashing graphic style, its cover pictured a curvaceous, terrified young woman in a partly shredded dress hanging by bound hands from a hook alongside slabs of meat. She was menaced by a demented, knife-wielding brute of a man who looked back over his shoulder at whoever was holding the gun that cast its shadow on him. Shocked and dismayed, the mayor vowed to ban all such scurrilous literature from his fair city.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/PULP-span/SUB-PULP-1-1310669675491-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/PULP-span/SUB-PULP-1-1310669675491-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/PULP-span/SUB-PULP-1-1310669675491-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/PULP-span/SUB-PULP-1-1310669675491-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/PULP-span/SUB-PULP-1-1310669675491-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Illustrating Damsels in Distress and Emissaries From Our Deep, Dark Ids', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Illustrating Damsels in Distress and Emissaries From Our Deep, Dark Ids', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Society of Illustrators', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lesser, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T21:54:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b99cb6d-186d-5bf2-bf21-3ad9adcc9edb', 'word_count': 893, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b99cb6d-186d-5bf2-bf21