Friday and Saturday, part of a brief summer stadium tour in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Beatles played at Shea Stadium on Aug. 15, 1965 (and again on Aug. 23, 1966), I was a little puzzled, maybe even a little miffed, at their choice of location. As an 11-year-old I had what I figured was a clear idea of what was right and proper, and to me the spanking new Shea Stadium was the home of the Mets, an upstart team that seemed destined forever to inhabit the basement in the National League standings, and not, therefore, the kind of place that deserved the Beatles’ magic. As I saw it, the Beatles were to music as the Yankees were to baseball, and if they were going to play at a baseball stadium, Yankee Stadium was the place for them. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15beatles-span/15beatles-1310661720109-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15beatles-span/15beatles-1310661720109-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15beatles-span/15beatles-1310661720109-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15beatles-span/15beatles-1310661720109-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15beatles-span/15beatles-1310661720109-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'McCartney at the Plate', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'McCartney at the Plate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shea Stadium (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCartney, Paul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yankee Stadium (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citi Field (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beatles, The', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T22:39:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abe8f6ce-1b80-5483-b0de-e0ca11e1030a', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abe8f6ce-1b80-5483-b0de-e0ca11e1030a'}, {'abstract': 'Tuesday is circled on Jon Pareles’s calendar, when the Brazilian musician Tom Zé makes a rare United States performance at Alice Tully Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/arts/music/tom-ze-a-brazilian-force-of-one-at-alice-tully-hall.html', 'snippet': 'Tuesday is circled on Jon Pareles’s calendar, when the Brazilian musician Tom Zé makes a rare United States performance at Alice Tully Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brazilian songwriter Tom Zé doesn’t make a habit of performing in New York or in the United States. The last time he toured was in 1999, backed by the Chicago instrumental band Tortoise. And there’s no one like him in Brazilian music or, for that matter, anywhere. So it’s a coup that the Lincoln Center Festival has booked him for a concert at Alice Tully Hall on Tuesday. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/PARELES/PARELES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 921, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/PARELES/PARELES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/PARELES/PARELES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Brazilian Master of Genre Mixes', 'kicker': 'The Hot List | Music', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Brazilian Master Of Genre Mixes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Costa, Gal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byrne, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Veloso, Caetano', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gil, Gilberto', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T22:40:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca55b86f-0db1-51e5-acc8-c61bbc894031', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca55b86f-0db1-51e5-acc8-c61bbc894031'}, {'abstract': 'Stephen Holden writes that Terrence Malick’s film “The Tree of Life” shows a patriarchal heirarchy and an ambiguous look at religious faith.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/movies/the-tree-of-life-and-its-exploration-of-authority.html', 'snippet': 'Stephen Holden writes that Terrence Malick’s film “The Tree of Life” shows a patriarchal heirarchy and an ambiguous look at religious faith.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first and only time I saw “The Tree of Life,” Terrence Malick’s solemn contemplation of God, family and creation, the mostly older audience at the Lincoln Plaza Cinemas remained as silent and motionless as a church congregation rapt in prayer. I quickly adjusted to the reverential mood of a film whose monumental images of erupting volcanoes and churning seas, as many have observed, recall Stanley Kubrick’s “2001: A Space Odyssey.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15subholden/15subholden-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15subholden/15subholden-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 356}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15subholden/15subholden-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 789, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15subholden/15subholden-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/15subholden/15subholden-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Authority, From God to Dad', 'kicker': 'The Hot List | Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Authority, From God to Dad', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T22:42:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d17816d-97f3-521f-a71d-a0121785356d', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d17816d-97f3-521f-a71d-a0121785356d'}, {'abstract': 'The Hungarian conductor Ivan Fischer will direct the Mostly Mozart Festival production of “Don Giovanni,” Anthony Tommasini writes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/arts/music/ivan-fischer-to-direct-don-giovanni.html', 'snippet': 'The Hungarian conductor Ivan Fischer will direct the Mostly Mozart Festival production of “Don Giovanni,” Anthony Tommasini writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The main reason I am looking forward to the Mostly Mozart Festival’s production of “Don Giovanni” is that the Hungarian conductor Ivan Fischer and the Budapest Festival Orchestra are top-notch Mozart performers. The orchestra was formed in 1983 by Mr. Fischer and the brilliant pianist Zoltan Kocsis, who recruited talented, eager and adventurous young Hungarian musicians. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/TOMmASSINI/TOMASSINI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/TOMmASSINI/TOMASSINI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/TOMmASSINI/TOMASSINI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 834, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/TOMmASSINI/TOMASSINI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/TOMmASSINI/TOMASSINI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Conductor Will Also Direct', 'kicker': 'The Hot List | Music', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Conductor Will Also Direct', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T22:42:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9385b222-d30f-5ffb-a809-437817a2aa35', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9385b222-d30f-5ffb-a809-437817a2aa35'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Ratliff wants to delve into repertory of songbirds, not only in parks but even on Web sites devoted to their riffs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/arts/music/outdoor-concerts-that-take-flight.html', 'snippet': 'Ben Ratliff wants to delve into repertory of songbirds, not only in parks but even on Web sites devoted to their riffs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has not escaped my attention that birds are some of the greatest musicians in the world. One afternoon last year in Washington Heights, I stopped for a while to listen to a little songbird up on a street lamp, throwing down a long and miraculous series of patterns in what sounded like changing keys: olé, you poet, you beast. Best thing I had heard all week. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/RATLIFF/RATLIFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 353, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/RATLIFF/RATLIFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 353}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/RATLIFF/RATLIFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/RATLIFF/RATLIFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/RATLIFF/RATLIFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Visiting the Original Birdland', 'kicker': 'The Hot List | Music', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Visiting the Original Birdland', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T22:43:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2eb9696-83c5-5026-a59e-31aa07928173', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2eb9696-83c5-5026-a59e-31aa07928173'}, {'abstract': 'Ginia Bellafante won’t mind rain on her vacation: She could then watch the whole of the original Danish version of “The Killing.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/arts/television/watching-the-killing-in-its-original-with-subtitles.html', 'snippet': 'Ginia Bellafante won’t mind rain on her vacation: She could then watch the whole of the original Danish version of “The Killing.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the most pleasurable summer vacations I ever had was spent in an early-19th-century farmhouse in Westport, Mass., one week in August some years ago, when the rain was so blessedly relentless that I had no choice but to watch the first three seasons of “The Wire,” virtually without interruption. I’d come with a file full of recipes and big shellfish ambitions, but once I was introduced to the Barksdales, McNulty, Omar, Bunk, the Baltimore waterfront and Hamsterdam, cooking seemed like a dismal waste of time. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/BELLAFANTE/BELLAFANTE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/BELLAFANTE/BELLAFANTE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/BELLAFANTE/BELLAFANTE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 921, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/BELLAFANTE/BELLAFANTE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/BELLAFANTE/BELLAFANTE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Dark Skies, a Dark Show', 'kicker': 'The Hot List | Television ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Dark Skies, A Dark Show', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMC (TV Network)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T22:46:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6397de7-3919-5de9-af1c-420b07d3227d', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6397de7-3919-5de9-af1c-420b07d3227d'}, {'abstract': '“The Chameleon” is based on the story of a French-born serial impostor and master of disguise who assumed scores of identities, especially those of missing teenagers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/movies/the-chameleon-directed-by-jean-paul-salome-review.html', 'snippet': '“The Chameleon” is based on the story of a French-born serial impostor and master of disguise who assumed scores of identities, especially those of missing teenagers.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Chameleon” fictionalizes an especially colorful chapter in the believe-it-or-not true story of Frédéric Bourdin, a French-born serial impostor and master of disguise who assumed scores of identities, especially those of missing teenagers. The movie focuses on one of his alter egos, Nicholas Barclay, a 13-year-old Texan from a working-class background who vanished without a trace in 1994 and “reappeared” three years later.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/CHAMELEON/CHAMELEON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/CHAMELEON/CHAMELEON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/CHAMELEON/CHAMELEON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/CHAMELEON/CHAMELEON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/CHAMELEON/CHAMELEON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A ‘Who Is It?’ More Than a Whodunit', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The Chameleon'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A ‘Who Is It?’ More Than a Whodunit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barkin, Ellen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T22:46:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bfc14ca-7913-5e54-903c-811765431730', 'word_count': 848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bfc14ca-7913-5e54-903c-811765431730'}, {'abstract': 'Michiko Kakutani says Don DeLillo’s 1997 novel, “Underworld,” is well worth rereading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/books/don-delillos-underworld-still-holds-power.html', 'snippet': 'Michiko Kakutani says Don DeLillo’s 1997 novel, “Underworld,” is well worth rereading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A decade after 9/11, it’s worth rereading Don DeLillo’s 1997 masterpiece, “Underworld,” to appreciate how uncannily the author not only captured the surreal weirdness of life in the second half of the 20th century but also anticipated America’s lurch into the terror and exigencies of the new millennium.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/KAKUTANI/KAKUTANI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 658, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/KAKUTANI/KAKUTANI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/KAKUTANI/KAKUTANI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Prescient Novel Retains Its Power', 'kicker': 'The Hot List | Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Prescient Novel Retains Its Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T22:46:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11421b45-a0bd-5b88-9f72-cebbe9239867', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11421b45-a0bd-5b88-9f72-cebbe9239867'}, {'abstract': 'Can a memoirist write with total honesty if she is worried about what her son might think?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/the-me-my-child-mustnt-know.html', 'snippet': 'Can a memoirist write with total honesty if she is worried about what her son might think?', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a recent weekend morning, I set out with my son to do errands. As we drove from the post office to the health food store, he began fiddling around with the radio, looking for NPR. I reached over and turned it off. He turned it back on. I turned it off again. He shot me a look, puzzled. After all, he knew I enjoyed the fact that, at age 12, he was a fan of public radio.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/shapiro-sub/shapiro-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/shapiro-sub/shapiro-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/shapiro-sub/shapiro-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Me My Child Mustn’t Know', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Me My Child Mustn’t Know', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T22:48:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dani Shapiro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shapiro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/335739fe-187f-525e-8229-ce5080c6587f', 'word_count': 1172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/335739fe-187f-525e-8229-ce5080c6587f'}, {'abstract': 'The guitar whiz John Pizzarelli and his wife, the singer Jessica Molaskey, host “All in Good Time,” a tribute to the New York radio personality Jonathan Schwartz.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/arts/music/all-in-good-time-a-tribute-to-jonathan-schwartz-review.html', 'snippet': 'The guitar whiz John Pizzarelli and his wife, the singer Jessica Molaskey, host “All in Good Time,” a tribute to the New York radio personality Jonathan Schwartz.', 'lead_paragraph': '“He put us all on the map. Without his support, you’d be looking at an empty stage.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saluting a Radio Man Who’s One of a Kind', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saluting a Radio Man Who’s One of a Kind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-14T22:48:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0de82f5-0829-5d42-b416-289168c616aa', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0de82f5-0829-5d42-b416-289168c616aa'}, {'abstract': 'Roberta Smith is struck by “Strasse auf Helgoland II,” a 1924 nearly abstract painting by Arthur Segal that is ahead of its time and hangs at the Metropolitan Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/arts/design/arthur-segals-strasse-auf-helgoland-ii-at-the-met.html', 'snippet': 'Roberta Smith is struck by “Strasse auf Helgoland II,” a 1924 nearly abstract painting by Arthur Segal that is ahead of its time and hangs at the Metropolitan Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The one thing I want to do this summer is swing by the 20th-century galleries at the Metropolitan Museum of Art and spend some time with “Strasse auf Helgoland II,” a nearly abstract street scene painted in 1924 by the Romanian artist Arthur Segal (1875-1944), who was affiliated with everything from Post Impressionism to Cubo-Futurism, with German Expressionism and Dada in between. Glowing, dramatically shaded geometric planes in tones of brown, blue and green extend outward to cover a wide and ersatz wood frame in this astute and irreverent synthesis of modernist tendencies and tics. It never fails to jolt my eye or remind me that art history is essentially nonlinear and full of overlooked treasures awaiting rediscovery. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/SMITH/SMITH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 826, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/SMITH/SMITH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/15/arts/SMITH/SMITH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At the Met, a Canvas Ahead of Its Time', 'kicker': 'The Hot List | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At the Met, a Canvas Ahead of Its Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 