um by the Mountain Goats. Maybe because of his enthusiasm, it took me a while to get into it. At first listen I was put off by the country-tinged voice of the singer and lyricist, John Darnielle. (Despite the plural in its name, the band used to be just Darnielle. Now a drummer and bassist have joined him.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Stay Cool and Seldom Speak’', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Darnielle, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T14:25:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe de Jonge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de Jonge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a41b50d-4d0e-53ac-a731-77e22b2fb022', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a41b50d-4d0e-53ac-a731-77e22b2fb022'}, {'abstract': 'Rebekah Brooks resigned as chief executive of News Corporation’s British newspaper division just hours after the Saudi prince who is the company’s second-largest shareholder suggested that she had to go.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/saudi-investor-suggested-brooks-had-to-go/', 'snippet': 'Rebekah Brooks resigned as chief executive of News Corporation’s British newspaper division just hours after the Saudi prince who is the company’s second-largest shareholder suggested that she had to go.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As my colleagues Sarah Lyall and Alan Cowell report, Rebekah Brooks, the embattled chief executive of Rupert Murdoch’s British newspaper operations, announced her resignation on Friday, after weeks of pressure.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saudi Investor Suggested Brooks Had to Go', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T14:40:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61daa634-3d2c-50b2-8ce8-3fe20fd79fd1', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61daa634-3d2c-50b2-8ce8-3fe20fd79fd1'}, {'abstract': 'Eric Fischer, a programmer and designer, created a series of beautiful maps that plot the location of people’s Twitter messages and Flickr photos.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/bits-pics-showing-the-location-of-tweets-and-flickr-photos/', 'snippet': 'Eric Fischer, a programmer and designer, created a series of beautiful maps that plot the location of people’s Twitter messages and Flickr photos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have you ever wondered where people are located when they post on Twitter or take a digital photo?\xa0Eric Fischer, a programmer and designer, recently answered this question by creating a series of maps showing people’s location when they send a Twitter message or upload a photo to Flickr, the photo-sharing Web site.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bits Pics: Showing the Location of Tweets and Flickr Photos', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Flickr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe'