les', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Witkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de3389ba-b56a-5358-b9a9-24acee401c68', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de3389ba-b56a-5358-b9a9-24acee401c68'}, {'abstract': 'Tensions have risen as opposition leaders and protesters remain dissatisfied with the pace of political change under King Abdullah II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/world/middleeast/16jordan.html', 'snippet': 'Tensions have risen as opposition leaders and protesters remain dissatisfied with the pace of political change under King Abdullah II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AMMAN, Jordan — Riot police officers wielding wooden clubs broke up a peaceful demonstration near a square in this city’s downtown on Friday afternoon, beating protesters and journalists. The episode was a sign of rising tension over the slow pace of political reform in the kingdom.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/world/16jordan2/16jordan2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/16/world/16jordan2/16jordan2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/world/16jordan2/16jordan2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 781, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/world/16jordan2/16jordan2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/16/world/16jordan2/16jordan2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jordanian Police Break Up a Peaceful March With Beatings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jordanian Police Break Up a Peaceful March With Beatings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jordan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Abdullah I, King of Jordan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T14:56:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/083c27d1-1d0a-53f5-b026-073ae5520702', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/083c27d1-1d0a-53f5-b026-073ae5520702'}, {'abstract': 'Kent Tritle, the organist and choral director, talks about his decision to move from the Church of St. Ignatius Loyola, where he has run the music program for 22 years, to the Cathedral Church of St. John the Divine.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/a-word-with-kent-tritle/', 'snippet': 'Kent Tritle, the organist and choral director, talks about his decision to move from the Church of St. Ignatius Loyola, where he has run the music program for 22 years, to the Cathedral Church of St. John the Divine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'St. Ignatius Loyola, the Roman Catholic church, rests nobly at Park Avenue and 84th Street amid the wealth and power of the Upper East Side. Its music program is equally imposing, an important part of the city’s concert scene. Kent Tritle, the organist and choral conductor, has overseen the church’s lyrical side for 22 years. It’s an established, comfortable perch.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Word With: Kent Tritle', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cathedral Church of St John the Divine (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:08:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6f3e737-907d-508a-b655-935c876c5f47', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6f3e737-907d-508a-b655-935c876c5f47'}, {'abstract': 'The U-Sabai Thai Traditional Massage and Spa in Suva, Fiji, features blind massage therapists, renowned in Asia for their heightened sense of touch.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/in-fiji-hands-that-see/', 'snippet': 'The U-Sabai Thai Traditional Massage and Spa in Suva, Fiji, features blind massage therapists, renowned in Asia for their heightened sense of touch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blind masseurs, known throughout Asia to be excellent therapists because of their heightened sense of touch, are becoming a trend for spa and wellness retreats worldwide. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Fiji, Hands That ‘See’', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:15:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sana Butler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Butler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b24e010d-5bdd-51dd-b0ec-35183597adb6', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b24e010d-5bdd-51dd-b0ec-35183597adb6'}, {'abstract': 'Rick Santorum reported raising $582,000 for his presidential bid during the last three months.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/santorum-fund-raising-numbers-near-bottom-of-the-field/', 'snippet': 'Rick Santorum reported raising $582,000 for his presidential bid during the last three months.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rick Santorum, a former Pennsylvania senator, was among the first Republican presidential candidates to file a fund-raising report on Friday with the Federal Election Commission. He raised $582,000 during the last three months, according to his report, and has $229,000 in the bank.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Santorum Fund-Raising Numbers Near Bottom of G.O.P. Field', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Election Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:17:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Zeleny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None