jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Portland Timbers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Sounders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pacific Northwestern States (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:27:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Thomson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/791a07ef-38ad-550b-a233-7399b43f67e1', 'word_count': 602, 'uri': 'nyt://article/791a07ef-38ad-550b-a233-7399b43f67e1'}, {'abstract': 'New York’s landlords are very selective, requiring prospective tenants to provide paperwork including tax returns, proof of income and sometimes even references.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/realestate/prospective-renters-have-much-to-prove-to-landlords.html', 'snippet': 'New York’s landlords are very selective, requiring prospective tenants to provide paperwork including tax returns, proof of income and sometimes even references.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LANDLORDS in New York City can afford to be picky. With a vacancy rate in Manhattan of under 1 percent, apartments sometimes rent in hours, not days or weeks. Good tenants are not that hard to find. On top of that, evicting problem tenants can be expensive and time consuming.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17getstarted/17getstarted-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 731, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17getstarted/17getstarted-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17getstarted/17getstarted-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'You Say You’re a Dream Renter? Prove It.', 'kicker': 'Getting Started', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'You Say You’re a Dream Renter? Prove It.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:28:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Rendon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rendon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f55c2343-08f9-585c-a598-97c4fd97ddf3', 'word_count': 1062, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f55c2343-08f9-585c-a598-97c4fd97ddf3'}, {'abstract': 'Speaker John A. Boehner said Friday that the House would vote next week on a three-part plan to cut the deficit, cap federal spending as a share of the economy and amend the Constitution to require a balanced budget.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/house-republicans-plan-vote-on-deficit-plan/', 'snippet': 'Speaker John A. Boehner said Friday that the House would vote next week on a three-part plan to cut the deficit, cap federal spending as a share of the economy and amend the Constitution to require a balanced budget.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With budget negotiations with the White House stalled, Speaker John A. Boehner of Ohio said Friday that the House would vote next week on a three-part plan to cut the deficit, cap federal spending as a share of the economy and amend the Constitution to require a balanced budget.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'House Republicans Plan Vote on Deficit', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cantor, Eric', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:28:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Hulse and Robert Pear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hulse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a569a5c-a258-5020-b0c4-f47118044984', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a569a5c-a258-5020-b0c4-f47118044984'}, {'abstract': 'Esmeralda Santiago’s heroine, a feminist before her time, runs a sugar plantation in 19th-century Puerto Rico.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/book-review-conquistadora-by-esmeralda-santiago.html', 'snippet': 'Esmeralda Santiago’s heroine, a feminist before her time, runs a sugar plantation in 19th-century Puerto Rico.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the American South had Scarlett O’Hara as its Civil War antiheroine, the English-speaking Caribbean of the 1800s had Annie Palmer. The real-life mistress of a Jamaican sugar estate during the final days of slavery, Palmer was the subject of legend and many lurid novels, most enduringly 1929’s “White Witch of Rosehall.” Lore says (most likely inaccurately) that Palmer practiced obeah, or sorcery; bedded slaves, then killed them; and murdered three husbands. She set the standard for cruelty and debauchery in a woman presiding over a plantation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/Bahadur/Bahadur-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/Bahadur/Bahadur-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/Bahadur/Bahadur-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tale of a Puerto Rican Plantation Mistress', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Raising Cane', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Puerto Rico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santiago, Esmeralda', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:30:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gaiutra Bahadur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gaiutra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bahadur', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ac8f7d2-ad26-5ae8-8c83-a14b00d3f0ee', 'word_count': 968, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ac8f7d2-ad26-5ae8-8c83-a14b00d3f0ee'}, {'abstract': 'Earl Swift’s account of the creation of the U.S. expressway system is textured and nuanced, easy on the asphalt, long on personalities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/book-review-the-big-roads-by-earl-swift.html', 'snippet': 'Earl Swift’s account of the creation of the U.S. expressway system is textured and nuanced, easy on the asphalt, long on personalities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When “On the Road” was published, in 1957, it may have seemed a rousing dawn chorus for an awakening generation of postwar seekers, but it was also an encomium of sorts — for the year before, construction had begun on the National System of Interstate and Defense Highways. “You can’t do what I did anymore,” Kerouac would later say. And as noted in “Why Kerouac Matters,” by the New York Times reporter John Leland, even as Kerouac was writing, the author glimpsed that his kind of rambling “may soon be obsolete as America enters its High Civilization period and no one will get sentimental or poetic anymore about trains and dew on fences at dawn in Missouri.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/17vanderbilt/17vanderbilt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 242, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/17vanderbilt/17vanderbilt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 242}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/17vanderbilt/17vanderbilt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/17vanderbilt/17vanderbilt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inventing the Interstate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gotta Keep Rollin’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Engineering and Engineers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:30:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Vanderbilt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vanderbilt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c98a25da-901f-56a1-916f-ec7b8f1193ab', 'word_count': 1276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c98a25da-901f-56a1-916f-ec7b8f1193ab'}, {'abstract': 'The historian Richard White sees the 19th-century American railways as a Gilded Age extravagance that worked social, political and environmental havoc.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/book-review-railroaded-by-richard-white.html', 'snippet': 'The historian Richard White sees the 19th-century American railways as a Gilded Age extravagance that worked social, political and environmental havoc.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Type of the modern! emblem of motion and power! pulse of the continent!” Walt Whitman sang in praise of the railroad. When he published those lines in 1876, the vast network that connected West to East was being widely hailed as the muscular marvel of the industrial age. It sped the bounty of farms and factories across the land, spawned hundreds of towns and cities along its routes, pioneered in marketing and managerial organization, and employed a huge and growing labor force. The men who created and ran the transcontinentals — Leland Stanford, Collis P. Huntington, Jay Gould, Mark Hopkins, Charles Francis Adams Jr., among others — were as famous in their era as such high-tech moguls as Bill Gates and Steve Jobs are today. Their entrepreneurial daring did much to transform the United States into a prosperous, developed nation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/bkr-Kazin-t_CA0/bkr-Kazin-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 216, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/bkr-Kazin-t_CA0/bkr-Kazin-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 216}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/bkr-Kazin-t_CA0/bkr-Kazin-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/bkr-Kazin-t_CA0/bkr-Kazin-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Robber Barons Railroaded America', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Robber Barons at Work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Richard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:31:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kazin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kazin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ceb56362-4463-55b5-9e7d-c8455a6e2e1f', 'word_count': 1235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ceb56362-4463-55b5-9e7d-c8455a6e2e1f'}, {'abstract': 'In its effort to give the Focus excellent fuel economy, Ford programmed the dual-clutch transmission to change gears in odd and infuriating ways, effectively undermining what is otherwise a solid car.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/automobiles/autoreviews/ford-focus-is-slick-package-but-gearbox-is-a-drag.html', 'snippet': 'In its effort to give the Focus excellent fuel economy, Ford programmed the dual-clutch transmission to change gears in odd and infuriating ways, effectively undermining what is otherwise a solid car.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TO the long list of things that have gone awry despite the best of intentions, add the 6-speed automatic transmission in the 2012 Ford Focus — an otherwise admirably redesigned compact car.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/automobiles/WHEE-2/WHEE-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 285, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/automobiles/WHEE-2/WHEE-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 285}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/automobiles/WHEE-2/WHEE-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 486, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/automobiles/WH