l': 'By Tudor Van Hampton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tudor', 'middlename': 'Van', 'lastname': 'Hampton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eee18e9d-b183-5a14-8c35-769e625b033e', 'word_count': 1446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eee18e9d-b183-5a14-8c35-769e625b033e'}, {'abstract': 'Some buildings are embracing new-building amenities — like gyms — only occasionally kicking and screaming.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/realestate/the-suddenly-buff-building.html', 'snippet': 'Some buildings are embracing new-building amenities — like gyms — only occasionally kicking and screaming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE apartment house at 815 Park Avenue, built in 1916, features classical swags on its facade, large arched windows and a softly lighted lobby hung with museum-quality oils in elaborate frames. Converted to a co-op some 30 years ago, 815 Park seems like the ultimate prewar apartment building, little touched by the passage of time.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17cover1_span/17cover1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17cover1_span/17cover1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17cover1_span/Sub-Cover-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17cover1_span/17cover1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17cover1_span/17cover1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In New York, the Suddenly Buff Prewar Building', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Suddenly Buff', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Clubs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:41:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Constance Rosenblum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Constance', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenblum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22c0187a-f0c2-5fb9-9e0b-faa5093b0d8c', 'word_count': 1722, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22c0187a-f0c2-5fb9-9e0b-faa5093b0d8c'}, {'abstract': 'A spokeswoman for the Royal Shakespeare Company said she did not know whether he would be able to return for Sunday’s performance.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/injured-romeo-undergoes-knee-surgery/', 'snippet': 'A spokeswoman for the Royal Shakespeare Company said she did not know whether he would be able to return for Sunday’s performance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sam Troughton, the actor playing Romeo who was injured on Tuesday during the Royal Shakespeare Company’s performance of “Romeo and Juliet” at the Park Avenue Armory, has undergone arthroscopic surgery on his knee, according to Nada Zakula, a spokeswoman for the company. She said she did not know if he would be able to return to the role on Sunday, when the next performance is scheduled. The play is being presented as part of the Lincoln Center Festival.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Injured Romeo Undergoes Knee Surgery', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Romeo and Juliet (Play)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lincoln Center Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Park Avenue Armory (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Shakespeare Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:45:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db703b5c-6a09-5752-b609-f3c465b32c8a', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db703b5c-6a09-5752-b609-f3c465b32c8a'}, {'abstract': 'The Spanish government has chosen SolarReserve to build a plant in Alcázar de San Juan, southeast of Madrid, that relies on a field of mirrors.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/solar-power-for-darker-times/', 'snippet': 'The Spanish government has chosen SolarReserve to build a plant in Alcázar de San Juan, southeast of Madrid, that relies on a field of mirrors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid the fretting over whether the renewable energy business will be dominated by foreign companies, a California venture that creates solar plants that can run around the clock has cut a deal to build a big plant in Spain, a country that is one of the most aggressive users of solar power.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Solar Power for Darker Times', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mirrors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Solar Energy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sunlight', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Energy Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nevada', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:45:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0472295b-6fc0-5be9-a809-e31e6df2d4c4', 'word_count': 571, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0472295b-6fc0-5be9-a809-e31e6df2d4c4'}, {'abstract': 'This haunting first novel’s deeply unreliable narrator is a former surgeon with Alzheimer’s and the prime suspect in her best friend’s murder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/book-review-turn-of-mind-by-alice-laplante.html', 'snippet': 'This haunting first novel’s deeply unreliable narrator is a former surgeon with Alzheimer’s and the prime suspect in her best friend’s murder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unreliable narrators come in many shapes. There are madmen, mystics, seducers, naïfs — Nabokov’s Humbert Humbert, the ghost-prone governess in “The Turn of the Screw” and Poe’s self-defeating paranoiac in “The Tell-Tale Heart,” for starters. And then there is Dr. Jennifer White, who narrates Alice LaPlante’s first novel. By the time “Turn of Mind” begins, she is losing her wits to Alzheimer’s disease and is the prime suspect in her best friend’s murder. She is as unreliable as they come.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/Slutzky/Slutzky-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 873, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/Slutzky/Slutzky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/Slutzky/Slutzky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Alzheimer’s Mystery Novel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dismembered Memory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:47:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zoë Slutzky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zoë', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Slutzky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/df7dcc66-d312-55ee-8dc4-b6596d550587', 'word_count': 853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df7dcc66-d312-55ee-8dc4-b6596d550587'}, {'abstract': 'The Chrysler executive indicated that the discontinued wagon still had fans at high levels within the company.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/srts-boss-ralph-gilles-mulls-a-replacement-for-dodge-magnum/', 'snippet': 'The Chrysler executive indicated that the discontinued wagon still had fans at high levels within the company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROSAMOND, Calif. — Could the station wagon make a comeback at Chrysler?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'SRT’s Boss, Ralph Gilles, Mulls a Replacement for Dodge Magnum', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler LLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:54:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Garrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bc0d492-e594-53e6-a066-7388926bca75', 'word_count': 339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bc0d492-e594-53e6-a066-7388926bca75'}, {'abstract': 'Eleven works stolen from New York galleries and hotels during the month of June were said to have been found during a police raid on the Hoboken, N.J., apartment of Mark Lugo, who has been charged with stealing a Picasso drawing in San Francisco last week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/missing-artworks-said-to-be-found-in-home-of-accused-picasso-thief/', 'snippet': 'Eleven works stolen from New York galleries and hotels during the month of June were said to have been found during a police raid on the Hoboken, N.J., apartment of Mark Lugo, who has been charged with stealing a Picasso drawing in San Francisco l...', 'lead_paragraph': 'A man who was arrested in the theft of a Picasso drawing from a San Francisco gallery last week was keeping a virtual museum in his apartment, including 11 works stolen from New York galleries and hotels, according to the police. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Missing Artworks Said to Be Found in Home of Accused Picasso Thief', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T15:58:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74e16312-898e-543d-bf8b-e9cced1aecae', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74e16312-898e-543d-bf8b-e9cced1aecae'}, {'abstract': 'Hidden in the foothills of the Carpathian Mountains, centuries-old wooden churches stave off the effects of time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/travel/ukraines-wooden-churches.html', 'snippet': 'Hidden in the foothills of the Carpathian Mountains, centuries-old wooden churches stave off the effects of time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN western Ukraine, the side of the highway offers more unusual scenes than many performance-art pieces. On one spring day, an old woman cradled what looked like a baby but was actually a newborn lamb dressed in baby clothes. On another day, a young woman strutted down a dirt road in a micro-mini and precarious heels. Nearby, a pair of young dandies cruised in an ox cart.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17explorer1_span/17explorer1_span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17explorer1_span/17explorer1_span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17explorer1_span/17explorer1_span-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17explorer1_span/17explorer1_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17explorer1_span/17explorer1_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Ukraine, Churches With a Distinctive Allure', 'kicker': 'Explorer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Ukraine, Weathering the Centuries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evan Rail', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rail', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f1d247b-5860-50d8-a7d5-ceec775688e6', 'word_count': 1541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f1d247b-5860-50d8-a7d5-ceec775688e6'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/magazine/reply-all-letters.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Congratulations to Dan Savage and his mantra “Good, Giving and Game.” And thanks to Mark Oppenheimer for bringing it beyond the alternative weeklies to a wider audience of mainstream, heterosexual couples (“Married, With Infidelities”). Honest communication with your partner surely is something to strive for in a long-term relationship, although it may be difficult at first for some women to define what they want, let alone articulate it.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Infidelity Keeps Us Together', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Infidelity Keeps Us Together', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADULTERY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Divorce, Separations and Annulments', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/65a4bf60-7ac9-5f6a-a59e-c5531cb634c5', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65a4bf60-7ac9-5f6a-a59e-c5531cb634c5'}, {'abstract': 'Paz de la Huerta has an uncanny ability to attract enemies (and ghosts).', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/magazine/talk-paz-de-la-huerta.html', 'snippet': 'Paz de la Huerta has an uncanny ability to attract enemies (and ghosts).', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paz de la Huerta has an uncanny ability to attract enemies (and ghosts).', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17talk/17talk-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 421, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17talk/17talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17talk/17talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Devil in a Black Dress', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Devil in a Black Dress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de la Huerta, Paz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36ead8fb-2f95-59ae-ba5b-c2dead48e269', 'word_count': 743, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36ead8fb-2f95-59ae-ba5b-c2dead48e269'}, {'abstract': 'While Wyclef Jean plainly wants to do something for Haiti, there is also, it seems, much that he wants Haiti to do for him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/magazine/what-is-wyclef-jean-trying-to-save-in-haiti.html', 'snippet': 'While Wyclef Jean plainly wants to do something for Haiti, there is also, it seems, much that he wants Haiti to do for him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wyclef Jean was in a rare state: nervous. He was trying to stick to his new role of wonky Haitian statesman, but the road kept distracting him. We were driving through Port-au-Prince as dusk fell, and he would interrupt his own discourse on trade rules or egg imports to ask the driver why a fire was burning on the roadside or to complain that our headlights were tempting danger or to mumble that the police escort, which he suspected of working for the opposition, shouldn’t sound its sirens.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17jean_span/17jean_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 559, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17jean_span/17jean_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 559}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17jean_span/17jean_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17jean_span/17jean_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17jean_span/17jean_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Would-Be Prince of Port-au-Prince', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Would-Be Prince of Port-au-Prince', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Haitian-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yele Haiti', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haiti', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jean, Wyclef', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anand Giridharadas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anand', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Giridharadas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ed3b355-e33a-5fda-9ea3-2a337cccfcf6', 'word_count': 3089, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ed3b355-e33a-5fda-9ea3-2a337cccfcf6'}, {'abstract': 'Bonus Feature: The Very First No. 1 Song', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/magazine/bonus-feature-the-very-first-no-1-song.html', 'snippet': 'Bonus Feature: The Very First No. 1 Song', 'lead_paragraph': '“Here we go with the Top 40 hits of the nation this week!” These words, spoken by Casey Kasem, kicked off the first broadcast of “American Top 40,” which aired on July 3, 1970, and it was carried by seven radio stations. Kasem, who would become one of the most recognizable broadcasters of his era (as well as providing the voice for Shaggy on “Scooby Doo”), was the host of the show until 1988, when he was replaced by Shadoe Stevens. (Despite his name, Stevens was in no way affiliated with “Scooby Doo.”) Stevens remained until 1994, when ABC canceled the show; it was reborn, under a new owner, in 1998, with Kasem once again as host. Ryan Seacrest joined the show as host in January 2004.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bonus Feature: The Very First No. 1 Song', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bonus Feature | The Very First No. 1 Song', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BILLBOARD', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e27068da-669c-5574-a62c-59f4101dca15', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e27068da-669c-5574-a62c-59f4101dca15'}, {'abstract': 'Welcome to the Mütter Museum — home to pickled organs and the answers to life’s enduring mysteries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/magazine/you-are-here-philadelphias-freaky-museum.html', 'snippet': 'Welcome to the Mütter Museum — home to pickled organs and the answers to life’s enduring mysteries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One summer Sunday years ago in Philadelphia, I stumbled onto the Mütter Museum. Back then maybe a few hundred people a year bothered to visit. A new director would soon parade human hairballs, pickled organs and Victorian surgical equipment from the museum’s collection on “Late Night With David Letterman,” and after that the Mütter joined institutions like the Metropolitan Museum of Art and the Liberace Museum in Las Vegas as beloved American attractions. But that Sunday it was still empty and neglected, a little forbidding from the outside.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17here_map/17here_map-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 834, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17here_map/17here_map-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17here_map/17here_map-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Human Hairballs ’R’ Us', 'kicker': 'You Are Here', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Human Hairballs ’R’ Us', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philadelphia (Pa)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mutter Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kimmelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kimmelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3924cf47-b7e5-56f9-844c-8b75dacca462', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3924cf47-b7e5-56f9-844c-8b75dacca462'}, {'abstract': 'What my big brother taught me about discretion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/magazine/lives-a-rats-tale.html', 'snippet': 'What my big brother taught me about discretion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was 6 when my brother John leaned across the kitchen table and casually whispered that he had killed Santa Claus. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17lives/mag-17Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 625, 'width': 654, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17lives/mag-17Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/magazine/17lives/mag-17Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Rat’s Tale', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Rat’s Tale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tony Gervino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gervino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f82637ec-ba8c-5a90-837a-e21e36a41e21', 'word_count': 781, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f82637ec-ba8c-5a90-837a-e21e36a41e21'}, {'abstract': 'Adoption by the numbers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/magazine/parental-guidance.html', 'snippet': 'Adoption by the numbers.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Unable to have biological children, my husband and I went to an adoption agency and were matched with a pregnant woman. She says she signed up because she and her husband can’t afford another child. Adoption will cost around $25,000, of which $8,000 goes to her. My conscience keeps asking me why I don’t just give the $25,000 to the birth mother and let her take care of her own child. My rational mind says this was her choice, and we need each other equally. What do you say? NAME WITHHELD, CHARLOTTE, N.C.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Parental Guidance', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Parental Guidance', 