— 23', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:02:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e617045a-a969-5a74-9911-8bcb85c12c47', 'word_count': 1443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e617045a-a969-5a74-9911-8bcb85c12c47'}, {'abstract': 'While detailing the great harm done by humans, Tim Flannery also writes hopefully about the earth’s future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/book-review-here-on-earth-by-tim-flannery.html', 'snippet': 'While detailing the great harm done by humans, Tim Flannery also writes hopefully about the earth’s future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Australian paleontologist and biologist Tim Flannery has earned his broad-brimmed field hat. Flannery is the discoverer of dozens of mammal and dinosaur species, extant and extinct, across Australia and Melanesia. In books like “The Future Eaters” (about the devastation wrought by the settlement of Australia) and “The Weather Makers” (about climate change), he has won accolades as a vivid chronicler of ecological history with an uncommonly broad sweep and an increasingly activist bent.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/SUBB-REVKIN/SUBB-REVKIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 238, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/SUBB-REVKIN/SUBB-REVKIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 238}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/SUBB-REVKIN/SUBB-REVKIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/SUBB-REVKIN/SUBB-REVKIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/SUBB-REVKIN/SUBB-REVKIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made This Mess of Planet Earth?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made This Mess?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flannery, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:06:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/85ffe4ff-e752-5c2c-ad04-c7a152f24f61', 'word_count': 1139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85ffe4ff-e752-5c2c-ad04-c7a152f24f61'}, {'abstract': 'A recent controversy over an 8-year-old girl kickboxing, and what to do when coaches favor their own kids.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/are-parents-too-involved-in-kids-sports/', 'snippet': 'A recent controversy over an 8-year-old girl kickboxing, and what to do when coaches favor their own kids.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the best parts of summer is getting children outside and active. The head-butting march of the United States women’s soccer team this particular summer underscores the power of getting physical.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are Parents Too Involved in Children’s Sports?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's World Cup (Soccer)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:15:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52c75865-0943-5899-9fcb-9f6e469490c5', 'word_count': 1132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52c75865-0943-5899-9fcb-9f6e469490c5'}, {'abstract': 'A research team tracks down the tree-dwelling Borneo rainbow toad in an area along the Malaysia-Indonesia border that had formerly been inaccessible.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/after-8-decades-tiny-toad-resurfaces-in-asia/', 'snippet': 'A research team tracks down the tree-dwelling Borneo rainbow toad in an area along the Malaysia-Indonesia border that had formerly been inaccessible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Borneo rainbow toad, with its long spindly legs, looks a bit like an Abstract Expressionist canvas splattered in bright green, purple and red. But when this amphibian was last seen, in 1924, the painter Jackson Pollock was just 12, and the only image of the mysterious creature was a black-and-white sketch. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After 8 Decades, Tiny Toad Resurfaces in Asia', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frogs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Robin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Conservation International', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Borneo (Indonesia)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Borneo (Malaysia)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:16:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Lin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/743cafa6-d732-540f-8d83-1e15d1a4609b', 'word_count': 557, 'uri': 'nyt://article/743cafa6-d732-540f-8d83-1e15d1a4609b'}, {'abstract': 'The Yankee shortstop gets his 3,000th hit and the memorabilia machine goes into action, churning out bats, balls, jerseys and more. But not all signed batting helmets are created equal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/your-money/from-honus-to-derek-baseball-memorabilia-is-more-than-signed-bats.html', 'snippet': 'The Yankee shortstop gets his 3,000th hit and the memorabilia machine goes into action, churning out bats, balls, jerseys and more. But not all signed batting helmets are created equal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE best birthday present I ever received came from my wife. She gave me a baseball from the 2009 World Series signed by Derek Jeter, the New York Yankees shortstop. I treasure it because he’s my favorite player but also because my newborn daughter slept in my arms while I watched the Yankees win that World Series.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': '