baseball players and coaches to agonize over whether it was appropriate to pursue the national title during a national emergency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SENDAI, Japan — High school baseball players across Japan dream of playing for the national title in Koshien Stadium, yet few ever go. Teams from Tohoku High School — a sports powerhouse whose alumni include Yu Darvish, the best pitcher in Japan, and Kazuhiro Sasaki, the former closer of the Seattle Mariners — expect to go. They have made 40 trips to the tournament, but have never won.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/sports/sub-y-tohoku-1/sub-y-tohoku-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/16/sports/sub-y-tohoku-1/sub-y-tohoku-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/sports/sub-y-tohoku-1/sub-y-tohoku-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/sports/sub-y-tohoku-1/sub-y-tohoku-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/16/sports/sub-y-tohoku-1/sub-y-tohoku-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Call to Play Ball in Japan Followed Much Fretting', 'kicker': 'Rebuilt Season', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Call to Play Ball in Japan Followed Much Fretting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Series', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:26:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35ea82a7-e005-590d-8ca1-72842494add5', 'word_count': 1287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35ea82a7-e005-590d-8ca1-72842494add5'}, {'abstract': 'The complete text of a personal letter of apology from Rupert Murdoch that will be published in all of Britain’s national newspapers this weekend has been released.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/complete-text-rupert-murdochs-apology/', 'snippet': 'The complete text of a personal letter of apology from Rupert Murdoch that will be published in all of Britain’s national newspapers this weekend has been released.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated | 1:44 p.m. Since, remarkably, Rupert Murdoch’s personal letter of apology for the phone hacking scandal will run in its pages as a full-page ad on Saturday, The Guardian has obtained the complete text and posted it online.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rupert Murdoch Apologizes to Victims of Phone Hacking', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Guardian (British Newspaper)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News International Ltd', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:27:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7360ae13-b230-506e-925a-732a540760f9', 'word_count': 806, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7360ae13-b230-506e-925a-732a540760f9'}, {'abstract': 'One of the pleasures (and pains) of going to the theater for many years is watching actors get older and grow up – or not, as the case may be.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/london-theater-journal-aging-acting-and-acting-up/', 'snippet': 'One of the pleasures (and pains) of going to the theater for many years is watching actors get older and grow up – or not, as the case may be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the pleasures (and pains) of going to the theater for many years is watching actors get older and grow up -– or not, as the case may be. Take Rupert Everett. The rakish, indignantly handsome Mr. Everett made his mark in theater 30 years ago in his early 20’s in Julian Mitchell’s “Another Country,” portraying a rebellious child of privilege with an aristocratic profile and a smoldering case of the sulks. As time passed, whenever Mr. Everett (who became a minor film star) returned to the theater, he continued to portray much the same person, but in a state of arrested development, like the drug-addicted, mother-fixated son in Noël Coward’s “Vortex” (1989) or a debonaire middle-aged but boyish Charles Condomine in Coward’s “Blithe Spirit,” on Broadway two years ago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Theater Journal: Aging, Acting and Acting Up', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:30:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80736ee4-2464-5fb6-9f9a-0fd8e0a7c6c5', 'word_count': 781, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80736ee4-2464-5fb6-9f9a-0fd8e0a7c6c5'}, {'abstract': 'Margaret Drabble’s short-story collection reflects the last half of the 20th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/book-review-a-day-in-the-life-of-a-smiling-woman-by-margaret-drabble.html', 'snippet': 'Margaret Drabble’s short-story collection reflects the last half of the 20th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The discursive spaciousness of Margaret Drabble’s voice and vision lends itself to the long form, as her 17 splendid novels demonstrate. This may help to explain why her “complete short stories” make up so slender a volume. Drabble, it seems, just didn’t have enough time to write short stories (with apologies to Mark Twain). Of those collected here, 14 in all, the earliest dates from the 1950s, the most recent from the 1990s. Some are reed slim, but many glimmer with the irony, lyricism, moral vision and (despite their page counts) amplitude we associate with Drabble’s novels. They reflect back to us the last half of the 20th century, albeit in Drabble’s often 19th-century voice.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/KLINE/KLINE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/KLINE/KLINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/KLINE/KLINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Margaret Drabble’s Complete Stories', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where the Forget-Me-Nots Grow', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Drabble, Margaret', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:33:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Kline', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kline', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/65330f24-ba6d-50e9-af0a-1cb9c668867f', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65330f24-ba6d-50e9-af0a-1cb9c668867f'}, {'abstract': 'On the neighborhood’s western edge, artistic and relatively prosperous newcomers have colonized an industrial zone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/realestate/a-sprawling-neighborhood-in-transition-again-living-inbushwick-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'On the neighborhood’s western edge, artistic and relatively prosperous newcomers have colonized an industrial zone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THIS sprawling neighborhood in northern Brooklyn was one of the borough’s first European settlements, and has been known for many things over its long history: for its farms, then for its breweries and factories, then for the blight and crime that descended in the decades after those employers closed.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17living_span/17living_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17living_span/17living_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17living_span/Living-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17living_span/17living_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/realestate/17living_span/17living_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Vanguard Alights', 'kicker': 'Living In | Bushwick, Brooklyn', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Vanguard Alights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bushwick (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:34:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Mooney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mooney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f77e4919-3e13-5a75-9430-1c52394df53d', 'word_count': 1478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f77e4919-3e13-5a75-9430-1c52394df53d'}, {'abstract': 'This weekend: Herrings, food and wine festivals, World Cup specials, grilling and restaurant openings.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/weekend-fare-50/', 'snippet': 'This weekend: Herrings, food and wine festivals, World Cup specials, grilling and restaurant openings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This Sunday marks the first of the New Amsterdam “market specials,” an occasional series that will bring chefs and food purveyors to New York’s original market district,\xa0 located at the Old Fulton Fish Market on South Street and Peck Slip in Lower Manhattan.\xa0 From 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. at the market, Russ & Daughters will sell the short-seasoned Hollandse Nieuwe, or New Catch Holland Herring, with traditional garnishes: chopped raw onion and cornichons ($8). P&H Soda Company will sell house-made soft drinks ($3) to accompany the herring, and for dessert, Marja Samson is making poffertjes ($5), a traditional Dutch street food. Starting at noon, Pasanella and Son (115 South Street, across the street from the market) will pour small, complimentary shots of aquavit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Fare', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ICE CREAM', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Samuelsson, Marcus', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Limelight Marketplace', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:36:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bao Ong', 'person':