r of Minnesota watched his state’s shutdown drag on, but insisted that, on principle, he could not go along with Republican lawmakers’ plans for the state’s budget. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Compromise in Minnesota Is Quick Fix on Budget', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Compromise In Minnesota Is Quick Fix On Budget', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Minnesota', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dayton, Mark B', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:45:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Davey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71f8f9e6-4845-5d41-ab15-71a3d1d565a2', 'word_count': 683, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71f8f9e6-4845-5d41-ab15-71a3d1d565a2'}, {'abstract': 'Poetry by Michael Dickman, Ross Gay, C. Dale Young and Chris Martin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/poetry-chronicle.html', 'snippet': 'Poetry by Michael Dickman, Ross Gay, C. Dale Young and Chris Martin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FLIES  By Michael Dickman.  Copper Canyon. Paper, $16.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/Chronicle/Chronicle-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Poetry Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Chris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:51:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5db1f917-dec0-51c8-bab8-76a88b3696a1', 'word_count': 1031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5db1f917-dec0-51c8-bab8-76a88b3696a1'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NINE LIVES: In Search of the Sacred in Modern India,  by William Dalrymple (Vintage Departures, $16.) Dalrymple explores the stupefyingly diverse religious world of the subcontinent through interviews with nine remarkable individuals, including a Jain nun ritually starving herself to death; a mystic who inhabits a cremation ground, drinking from skulls; and a sculptor of Hindu idols who believes he is creating live deities.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:51:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cbfff7d-bed1-5917-9eac-933e45931e65', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cbfff7d-bed1-5917-9eac-933e45931e65'}, {'abstract': 'This picture book documents an American girl’s adventures in her father’s native Sardinia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/childrens-books-orani-by-claire-a-nivola.html', 'snippet': 'This picture book documents an American girl’s adventures in her father’s native Sardinia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Picture books that involve and engage young readers in the experiences of people packing up their lives and heading off to a new country, a new culture, a totally new way of living have always interested me. Perhaps because my own Italian grandparents did exactly that in the early 1900s. My father was born in Fall River, Mass., only a month or two after his parents settled into their new home.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/Depaola/Depaola-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 812, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/Depaola/Depaola-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/Depaola/Depaola-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Girl’s Postcards From Italy', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Postcards From Italy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nivola, Claire A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:51:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tomie dePaola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tomie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de Paola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d2e979f-c96c-5036-bd27-8996c40c36e3', 'word_count': 297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d2e979f-c96c-5036-bd27-8996c40c36e3'}, {'abstract': 'A novel of girls at an elite Swiss boarding school in the 1970s, and another of two Mexican-American fifth-graders struggling with assimilation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/childrens-books-dreams-of-significant-girls-and-dancing-home.html', 'snippet': 'A novel of girls at an elite Swiss boarding school in the 1970s, and another of two Mexican-American fifth-graders struggling with assimilation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s call them the original Gossip Girls. In Cristina García’s first young adult novel, three wealthy and adventurous ninth-grade girls from different worlds converge upon a Swiss boarding school for a summer of discovery. Vivien Wahl is the Miami-reared daughter of a Jewish Cuban exile father. Shirin Firouz is from Tehran, the treasured girl and math genius in a family of older brothers. Ingrid Baum is a bad girl queen bee from Canada, the daughter of German immigrants.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Be a Bicultural Schoolgirl', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Girls of the World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garcia, Cristina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ada, Alma Flor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:51:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Veronica Chambers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Veronica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chambers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c967300e-c7d1-56fa-a125-82476366d799', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c967300e-c7d1-56fa-a125-82476366d799'}, {'abstract': 'Children’s and young adult books about baseball, Ben Franklin, Lewis and Clark and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/childrens-books-bookshelf-picture-books-about-america.html', 'snippet': 'Children’s and young adult books about baseball, Ben Franklin, Lewis and Clark and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE CROSSING  By Donna Jo Napoli. Illustrated by Jim Madsen.  40 pp. Atheneum. $16.99. (Picture book; ages 4 to 8) ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/BOOKSHELF1/BOOKSHELF1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 270, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/BOOKSHELF1/BOOKSHELF1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 270}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/BOOKSHELF1/BOOKSHELF1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 461, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/BOOKSHELF1/BOOKSHELF1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/books/review/BOOKSHELF1/BOOKSHELF1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bookshelf: America', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bookshelf: America', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:51:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec900aba-9573-523e-9c5c-34f988c9ed2f', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec900aba-9573-523e-9c5c-34f988c9ed2f'}, {'abstract': 'In Melissa Walker’s young adult novel, a girl coming of age in an evangelical community begins to test boundaries and question absolute faith.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/young-adult-small-town-sinners-by-melissa-walker.html', 'snippet': 'In Melissa Walker’s young adult novel, a girl coming of age in an evangelical community begins to test boundaries and question absolute faith.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lacey Anne Byer, the heroine of Melissa Walker’s “Small Town Sinners,” is a born good girl: the 16-year-old daughter of the children’s pastor at the House of Enlightenment, an evangelical church in a working- and middle-\xadclass enclave in the South — something like Dillon, Tex., in “Friday Night Lights.” Lacey even has a Tyra-like spitfire, a girl named Starla Joy, and a lovable Landry-like geek, Dean, for best friends.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Story of an Evangelical Girlhood', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Preacher’s Daughter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evangelical Movement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:51:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carlene Bauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/97d99fe9-7e1e-5833-9632-71713e42c115', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97d99fe9-7e1e-5833-9632-71713e42c115'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ABSOLUTE MONARCHS: A History of the Papacy,  by John Julius Norwich (Random House, $30.) Norwich, a writer of popular histories, finds that the truly great popes were outnumbered by the corrupt, the inept, the venal, the lecherous and the mediocre.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Editors' Choice: Recent Books Of Particular Interest", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:51:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fc4ce2e-0c62-5bbb-947a-2b22f4808147', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fc4ce2e-0c62-5bbb-947a-2b22f4808147'}, {'abstract': 'Wherein we ready ourselves for the onslaught of George R. R. Martin’s fantasy blockbuster “A Dance With Dragons.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Wherein we ready ourselves for the onslaught of George R. R. Martin’s fantasy blockbuster “A Dance With Dragons.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'ADVANCE MAN: A few weeks ago, I promised to eat my complete Harry Potter library if “A Dance With Dragons,” the eagerly awaited fifth installment in George R. R. Martin’s “Song of Ice and Fire” fantasy series, didn’t enter the hardcover fiction list at No. 1. That’s probably still a safe bet, though I did wonder if I should start firing up the barbecue after it was reported that Amazon Germany had accidentally shipped some 180 copies of “Dragons” ahead of the strict July 12 publication date, thus imperilling Martin’s carefully orchestrated siege of the global book market. (This week’s list reflects sales through July 9, though let it be noted that “Dragons” went into its sixth printing based on pre-orders alone. Charge!)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/24insidelist/24insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/24insidelist/24insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:51:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d164eee5-4e1a-5012-8070-29feb8d9987d', 'word_count': 594, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d164eee5-4e1a-5012-8070-29feb8d9987d'}, {'abstract': 'Robert M. Scarano has not been allowed to file new permits after being found to have made false statements to the city to avoid zoning laws in construction projects. A court rejected his appeal.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/appeals-court-upholds-building-ban-on-architect/', 'snippet': 'Robert M. Scarano has not been allowed to file new permits after being found to have made false statements to the city to avoid zoning laws in construction projects. A court rejected his appeal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Manhattan appeals court upheld a decision on Thursday that essentially prevents the famous — to some, infamous — local architect Robert M. Scarano Jr. from doing business in New York.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Appeals Court Upholds Building Ban on Architect', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Building (Construction)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Zoning', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salzman, Joan R', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scarano, Robert M Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Buildings Department (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T16:53:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Eligon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/607dbee4-f6e7-58b6-bbda-632d9dc98780', 'word_count': 641, 'uri': 'nyt://article/607dbee4-f6e7-58b6-bbda-632d9dc98780'}, {'abstract': 'Andrew Ross Sorkin, the bestselling author and columnist at The New York Times, has been hired to co-host CNBC’s morning financial show, Squawk Box. He will continue as a reporter and columnist at The Times.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/sorkin-to-co-host-cnbcs-squawk-box/', 'snippet': 'Andrew Ross Sorkin, the bestselling author and columnist at The New York Times, has been hired to co-host CNBC’s morning financial show, Squawk Box. He will continue as a reporter and columnist at The Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andrew Ross Sorkin, the best-selling author and columnist at The New York Times, has been hired by CNBC to be the co-host of the morning financial show, “Squawk Box.” Here is the internal announcement from his editor in the business section, Larry Ingrassia:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sorkin to Co-Host CNBC’s ‘Squawk Box’', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14892fc7-351f-52f9-a5af-693de33d10ba', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14892fc7-351f-52f9-a5af-693de33d10ba'}, {'abstract': 'The commander of an infantry company says repealing the “don’t ask, don’t tell” policy might not be as easy as some claim.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/dont-ask-dont-tell-repeal-not-a-quick-fix/', 'snippet': 'The commander of an infantry company says repealing the “don’t ask, don’t tell” policy might not be as easy as some claim.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Very soon, the military will signal that it has prepared itself for the repeal of the “don’t ask, don’t tell” policy. This policy, in effect since the early days of the Clinton administration, allows gays and lesbians to serve as long as they do not identify themselves as such in public. Before signing the repeal into law, President Obama asked the military to take a reasonable amount of time to teach and train its soldiers about the policy changes. The military responded with a goal of training all currently serving soldiers by July 15. So far, that training has been delivered to nearly 95 percent of the military. Repeal could be final by the fall.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Don’t Ask, Don’t Tell’ Repeal Isn’t a Quick Fix', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:03:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Capt. Adrian Bonenberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Capt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/106238eb-6ebf-55d4-9991-813758628c9d', 'word_count': 1049, 'uri': 'nyt://article/106238eb-6ebf-55d4-9991-813758628c9d'}, {'abstract': 'The Jam is a summer playlist curated by Sean Hanratty. This week’s list comes from the LA producers Daniel Pineda and Asma Maroof, aka Nguzunguzu.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/the-jam-nguzunguzu/', 'snippet': 'The Jam is a summer playlist curated by Sean Hanratty. This week’s list comes from the LA producers Daniel Pineda and Asma Maroof, aka Nguzunguzu.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The third installment of Sean Hanratty’s summer playlist is brought to you by the Los Angeles producers (and former violinists) Daniel Pineda and Asma Maroof, a k a Nguzunguzu. The group, which takes its name from a traditional Solomon Island figurehead used to protect against supernatural forces during canoe trips, offers a unique blend of R&B, house, cumbia and tribal music. They are known for improvising with, well, really weird sounds achieved through electronic instruments and a room full of synthesizers, as well as for an energy that Hanratty says is “incredible.” Pineda and Maroof, who have worked closely with the Sri Lankan songstress M.I.A., recently kicked off a tour with Gang Gang Dance and Total Freedom, and will be at Santos Party House on Thursday, July 21. (They are rumored to be playing the\xa0Capsule after-party on Monday, July 18!?)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Jam | Nguzunguzu', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:10:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d590f38-5dde-55b3-890c-b98e62d06dc2', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d590f38-5dde-55b3-890c-b98e62d06dc2'}, {'abstract': 'Dennis Albaugh of Ankeny, Iowa, has amassed what may be the definitive collection of open-top Chevrolets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/07/17/automobiles/collectibles/17chevy-slideshow.html', 'snippet': 'Dennis Albaugh of Ankeny, Iowa, has amassed what may be the definitive collection of open-top Chevrolets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dennis Albaugh of Ankeny, Iowa, has amassed what may be the definitive collection of open-top Chevrolets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/automobiles/collectibles/17chevy-slideshow-slide-1X66/17chevy-slideshow-slide-1X66-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/automobiles/collectibles/17chevy-slideshow-slide-1X66/17chevy-slideshow-slide-1X66-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/automobiles/collectibles/17chevy-slideshow-slide-1X66/17chevy-slideshow-slide-1X66-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chevys for All Seasons, Especially Summer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'chevrolet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:11:33+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'subsection_name': 'Collectible Cars', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/b4485a80-21f6-54a2-a676-f2870795b6ef', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/b4485a80-21f6-54a2-a676-f2870795b6ef'}, {'abstract': 'Shadow Cities is another take-over-the-world game, but this time it’s not a fantasy realm: You fight for your own town or one elsewhere, using a smartphone and GPS.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/arts/video-games/shadow-cities-a-new-iphone-video-game-review.html', 'snippet': 'Shadow Cities is another take-over-the-world game, but this time it’s not a fantasy realm: You fight for your own town or one elsewhere, using a smartphone and GPS.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have played the future of mobile gaming. It is called Shadow Cities. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/16SUBSHADOW/16SUBSHADOW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Brave New World That’s as Familiar as the Machine It Fights With', 'kicker': 'Video Game Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brave New World That’s as Familiar As the Machine It Fights With', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:14:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Video Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Schiesel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schiesel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd5be905-f70b-5cef-9192-d83c45a3f149', 'word_count': 963, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd5be905-f70b-5cef-9192-d83c45a3f149'}, {'abstract': 'Some teams have tried six-man pitching rotations, usually when a starter returns from an injury, but it has strained their bullpens.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/is-the-six-man-rotation-here-to-stay/', 'snippet': 'Some teams have tried six-man pitching rotations, usually when a starter returns from an injury, but it has strained their bullpens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1972 the Los Angeles Dodgers bucked seven decades of baseball precedent and added a fifth starting pitcher to their rotation. The quintet of Don Sutton, Claude Osteen, Al Downing, Tommy John and Bill Singer portended the future, and by the end of the decade the once revolutionary five-man rotation was adopted leaguewide.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is the Six-Man Rotation Here to Stay?', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Humber, Philip', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peavy, Jake', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago White Sox', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:15:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Strauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d000fad9-4e3c-56a1-8915-413e43ac0676', 'word_count': 650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d000fad9-4e3c-56a1-8915-413e43ac0676'}, {'abstract': 'Fiasco Theater’s highly inventive production of “Cymbeline,” featuring six actors in 14 roles and an imaginative set that relied on just two crates, a sheet, and trunk serving many purposes, will begin an 18-week run on August 27 at the Barrow Street Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/downtown-theater-to-take-a-chance-on-celebrated-cymbeline/', 'snippet': 'Fiasco Theater’s highly inventive production of “Cymbeline,” featuring six actors in 14 roles and an imaginative set that relied on just two crates, a sheet, and trunk serving many purposes, will begin an 18-week run on August 27 at the Barrow Str...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Commercial theater producers seeking to make money off Shakespeare usually stick to tried-and-true audience draws like “Hamlet” or “Macbeth,” but even those can be financially risky without a celebrity in the title role. (Last season’s critically acclaimed “Merchant of Venice,” starring Al Pacino, narrowly turned a profit.) Next month, however, a group of commercial producers will try to make a go of “Cymbeline,” one of Shakespeare’s trickier tragic romances — though with a staging that received strong notices from  critics this winter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Downtown Theater to Take a Chance on Celebrated ‘Cymbeline’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiasco Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Theater for a New Audience', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:15:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cf55727-ca98-5ef5-87dd-6d5b8aa17e03', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cf55727-ca98-5ef5-87dd-6d5b8aa17e03'}, {'abstract': 'Well, we’ll have to agree to disagree: “Lolita,” which finished first in the survey of the magazine staff’s favorite fiction books, only managed to eke out 11th place on Twitter.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/reader-favorites-your-twitter-fiction5/', 'snippet': 'Well, we’ll have to agree to disagree: “Lolita,” which finished first in the survey of the magazine staff’s favorite fiction books, only managed to eke out 11th place on Twitter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Well, we’ll have to agree to disagree: “Lolita,” which finished first in the survey of the magazine staff’s favorite fiction books, only managed to eke out 11th place on Twitter. Last week, after publishing our original post of our favorite fiction, we asked you to weigh in on Twitter under the hashtag #fiction5. Jer Thorp, the data artist in residence at The New York Times, gathered 4,500 tweets that used the hashtag #fiction5 over the following few days. He compiled the list below of your Twitter picks and presented us with the top contenders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reader Favorites: Your Twitter #fiction5', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:17:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nolan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nolan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec0a5196-3034-5c4c-83b9-199ccb5c7d9d', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec0a5196-3034-5c4c-83b9-199ccb5c7d9d'}, {'abstract': 'City officials said the latest round of sampling showed that less than 2 percent of the water tested had elevated levels of lead.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/lead-in-n-y-tap-water-is-back-to-normal/', 'snippet': 'City officials said the latest round of sampling showed that less than 2 percent of the water tested had elevated levels of lead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The proportion of homes in New York City with elevated lead levels in their tap water is back to normal, city officials said this week, but all residents are still urged to run their tap water for at least half a minute before drinking or cooking with it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lead in New York Tap Water Is Back to Normal', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lead', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Environmental Protection', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:25:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28dbd280-a03c-5315-a281-6ea967a96032', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28dbd280-a03c-5315-a281-6ea967a96032'}, {'abstract': 'The proportion of minority residents is rising in nursing homes — and that may not be good news.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/white-flight-from-the-nursing-home/', 'snippet': 'The proportion of minority residents is rising in nursing homes — and that may not be good news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The number of older Americans living in nursing homes continues to fall, and the proportion of residents who are black, Hispanic or Asian has climbed sharply. But don’t expect cheers from the Brown University researchers who’ve tracked this major shift in long-term care.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'White Flight From the Nursing Home', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing Homes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:28:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a584e32-9b4e-5ae6-bdcc-d8092e91079b', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a584e32-9b4e-5ae6-bdcc-d8092e91079b'}, {'abstract': 'As they enjoy the recognition of the Emmy Awards nominators on the eve of their show’s finale, Connie Britton and Kyle Chandler look back on “Friday Night Lights.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/as-the-clock-runs-down-the-taylors-look-back-on-friday-night-lights/', 'snippet': 'As they enjoy the recognition of the Emmy Awards nominators on the eve of their show’s finale, Connie Britton and Kyle Chandler look back on “Friday Night Lights.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'For almost all of the critically lauded but ratings-challenged run of “Friday Night Lights” — which ends Friday on NBC — clear eyes and full hearts couldn’t win with Emmy voters.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As the Clock Runs Down, the Taylors Look Back On ‘Friday Night Lights’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Friday Night Lights (TV Program)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emmy Awards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Britton, Connie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chandler, Kyle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DirecTV', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Egner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1dbb439-402e-5a2d-b4ce-fdbf192b73b4', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1dbb439-402e-5a2d-b4ce-fdbf192b73b4'}, {'abstract': 'First a same-sex union in Vermont, then a split. Trying to dissolve the union led to State Supreme Court in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/nyregion/bound-in-a-gay-union-by-a-state-that-didnt-recognize-it.html', 'snippet': 'First a same-sex union in Vermont, then a split. Trying to dissolve the union led to State Supreme Court in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’VE never had perfect timing. When New York finally legalized same-sex marriage last month, I had just gotten out of my lesbian union and married a guy. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/nyregion/17HASBIAN1/17HASBIAN1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 980, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bound in a Gay Union by a State Denying It', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gay Divorce: Sundering What Vermont Joined', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Divorce, Separations and Annulments', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vermont', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sunshine, Jeffrey S', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:30:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68ce235a-8f00-54ef-9077-8cf67101722b', 'word_count': 1440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68ce235a-8f00-54ef-9077-8cf67101722b'}, {'abstract': 'For the second week running, we’ve collected the best long-form journalism in the Times. A new shortlist will be posted every Friday afternoon on The 6th Floor blog, as well as on our Longreads page. If you follow #Longreads on Twitter, you’ll be able to find our picks that way, too.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/long-story-shortlist-rockets-robots-and-aretha/', 'snippet': 'For the second week running, we’ve collected the best long-form journalism in the Times. A new shortlist will be posted every Friday afternoon on The 6th Floor blog, as well as on our Longreads page. If you follow #Longreads on Twitter, you’ll be ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the second week running, we’ve collected the best long-form journalism in The Times. A new shortlist will be posted every Friday afternoon on The 6th Floor blog, as well as on our Longreads page. If you follow #Longreads on Twitter, you’ll be able to find our picks that way too.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Story Shortlist: Rockets, Robots and Aretha', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Identification Devices', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robots and Robotics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T17:38:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8325cfa-9b95-54c9-b846-0d8fa5b3ccb1', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8325cfa-9b95-54c9-b846-0d8fa5b3ccb1'}, {'abstract': 'The Financial Regulatory Authority has suspended a California-based broker for sending a series of “misrepresentative and unbalanced” message