 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kristina Shevory', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kristina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shevory', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/60172356-905a-5fdc-b53e-32ab3033a32c', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60172356-905a-5fdc-b53e-32ab3033a32c'}, {'abstract': 'Letters to the Editor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/travel/letters-the-siren-call-of-cuba.html', 'snippet': 'Letters to the Editor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor: Re the July 10 Practical Traveler column (“New Ways to Visit Cuba, Legally”): For years, my architect husband has yearned to visit Cuba for its artistic heritage. As a playwright, I’d like to go to experience firsthand the Cuban ethos. We have no interest in tourist traps or in political machinations. As travelers who like to create our own agenda, the thought of visiting lock-step with a gaggle of folks on a tour program makes us cringe. We certainly are not equipped to overthrow any government, so we are at a loss to understand why we can’t just choose our form of transit and travel to Cuba, bringing back sketches and snapshots and memories. Someone, please explain why we are kept from doing this. Anne Russell Wilmington, N.C.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: The Siren Call of Cuba', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Siren Call of Cuba', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T18:55:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0f3f70d-d5dc-58ab-9997-82ef14ca2027', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0f3f70d-d5dc-58ab-9997-82ef14ca2027'}, {'abstract': 'The artist was sheltered and inspired by this island, but never received the kind of recognition he did elsewhere.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/travel/in-spain-miros-majorca.html', 'snippet': 'The artist was sheltered and inspired by this island, but never received the kind of recognition he did elsewhere.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A BRONZE Miró sculpture interrupts the sidewalk below the grand Cathedral of Palma de Mallorca. It stretches up, the height of two men, a hollowed concave rectangle topped by a large egg-shaped nugget tilted toward the Mediterranean. Day after day, tourists rush past it in a hustle, descending from the steep side street that winds down to the shaded Passeig de Born, a busy thoroughfare filled with clothing stores and mopeds.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17MiroCover_span/17MiroCover_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17MiroCover_span/17MiroCover_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17MiroCover_span/Miro-cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17MiroCover_span/17MiroCover_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17MiroCover_span/17MiroCover_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Spain, Miró’s Majorca', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Miró’s Majorca', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Majorca (Spain)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miro, Joan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T18:55:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Wildman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wildman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bacb8401-224c-5819-9b85-edc98983d2c8', 'word_count': 2835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bacb8401-224c-5819-9b85-edc98983d2c8'}, {'abstract': 'The Rainey Street neighborhood, near the banks of Lady Bird Lake, is attracting locals with homey new bars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/travel/in-austin-rainey-street-is-a-local-hot-spot.html', 'snippet': 'The Rainey Street neighborhood, near the banks of Lady Bird Lake, is attracting locals with homey new bars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOURISTS in Austin often head straight to Sixth Street, with its jammed-together nightclubs, restaurants and bars advertising drink specials and Jell-O shots. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17heads-up_span/17heads-up-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/travel/17heads-up_span/17heads-up-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewi