abe Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gabe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/b67ed6a4-3102-57d4-9ce1-248f846d6b6d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/b67ed6a4-3102-57d4-9ce1-248f846d6b6d'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Bittman pairs skordalia, the flavorful Greek dip, with cold cooked chicken for a high-summer meal.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/the-minimalist-chicken-salad-with-skordalia/', 'snippet': 'Mark Bittman pairs skordalia, the flavorful Greek dip, with cold cooked chicken for a high-summer meal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Greek dip skordalia is most easily described as eggless mayonnaise, but it’s so much better than that. Unlike “real” mayonnaise — an emulsion of egg (or just yolk), vinegar and lots of oil — skordalia consists mainly of nuts, with just a little oil and moistened bread, or potato, as a binder. Purée these ingredients with garlic and stock or milk (the latter is creamier, obviously), and you end up with a thick, silky and, most importantly, intensely flavorful sauce that’s terrific with crudités.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Minimalist: Chicken Salad With Skordalia', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chickens', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T20:36:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'q