at an Impasse of Sorts', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Julius by Design'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All Words, Yet at an Impasse of Sorts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Access Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T21:43:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia La Rocco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Rocco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/30e10954-2fe2-5035-8160-11b778767292', 'word_count': 446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30e10954-2fe2-5035-8160-11b778767292'}, {'abstract': 'You can make pomegranate-glazed chicken without the grill.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/no-grill-needed/', 'snippet': 'You can make pomegranate-glazed chicken without the grill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You can’t make grilled pomegranate-glazed chicken without a grill. But you can still make excellent pomegranate-glazed chicken thighs in your oven. They won’t benefit from the smokiness of charcoal, but will still have plenty of tangy, complex, fruity flavor from the glaze. It’s a dish worth making. To do so, preheat your oven to 450 degrees and spread the chicken thighs on a baking pan. Roast, turning once, until brown, crisp-skinned and cooked through, 25 to 35 minutes. Then follow the rest of the recipe from that point and dig in.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Grill Needed', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chickens', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pomegranates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T21:43:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/927da8a4-41a5-5803-8de6-ec75967a9374', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/927da8a4-41a5-5803-8de6-ec75967a9374'}, {'abstract': 'Smorgasburg celebrates National Ice Cream Month.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/15/ice-cream-convergence/', 'snippet': 'Smorgasburg celebrates National Ice Cream Month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than a millennium ago, during the Tang Dynasty in China, someone had the bright idea to take fermented milk and ground rice, stir in dragon brain fragments — a k a camphor — and submerge the mixture in a pool of ice. The mothball-scented confection apparently pleased the emperor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ice Cream Convergence', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flea Markets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ICE CREAM', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T21:44:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ligaya Mishan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ligaya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mishan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e2b888f-0294-5e67-9b08-4648ebc51c64', 'word_count'