nytimes.com/2011/07/16/arts/design/empire-state-buildings-new-exhibition-review.html', 'snippet': 'Visitors to the Empire State Building don’t really come to see the building. They come to see the city around it. A show redirects attention to what we see in the building.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am pressing against a secure wall, not daring to step away. The wind is whipping against me — or is it howling? My feet feel rubbery on the narrow walkway. I think of those balconies on the upper floors of colonial homes in old ports, where pacing wives would gaze out to sea, seeking the long-overdue ships of sailor husbands: widows’ walks. If this particular walk were open to an anxiously pacing public, I can only imagine how many new widows and widowers would be left behind. But I look out, and the vision is literally breathtaking, the Hudson shimmering in the west, and a patch of green off to the north — Central Park — lying just beyond some half-hearted high-rises.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/16EMPIRE2/16EMPIRE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 850, 'width': 283, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/16EMPIRE2/16EMPIRE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/16EMPIRE2/16EMPIRE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A View Inside King Kong’s Perch', 'kicker': 'Exhibition Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A View Inside King Kong’s Perch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Empire State Building (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T23:01:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Rothstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffff2dba-43af-5202-9cc4-4f4a31a0a786', 'word_count': 1400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffff2dba-43af-5202-9cc4-4f4a31a0a786'}, {'abstract': '“Fire in Babylon,” a documentary about a cricket team from the West Indies, deals with complex issues rather than just a simple game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/movies/fire-in-babylon-looks-at-west-indies-cricket.html', 'snippet': '“Fire in Babylon,” a documentary about a cricket team from the West Indies, deals with complex issues rather than just a simple game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AMERICANS would sooner watch a spelling bee than a cricket match. No, really: ESPN’s broadcast of the Scripps National Spelling Bee draws an average of four million viewers, while the network’s online coverage of 2011 World Cup Cricket generated some 1.3 million hits here. So what does that bode for a politics-ridden documentary about a cricket team — from the West Indies, no less, a part of the world that most Americans associate with piña coladas, not postcolonial politics?', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/BABYLON-3/BABYLON-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 686, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/BABYLON-3/BABYLON-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/BABYLON-3/BABYLON-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Cricket Was a Symbol, Not Just a Sport', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Cricket Was a Symbol, Not Just a Sport', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cricket (Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ESPN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T23:18:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Baz Dreisinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Baz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dreisinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/475b7823-d384-5bdb-8bc0-010102c144e3', 'word_count': 1143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/475b7823-d384-5bdb-8bc0-010102c144e3'}, {'abstract': 'Franz Welser-Möst led the Cleveland Orchestra in an odd coupling of Bruckner and Adams works at Avery Fisher Hall on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/arts/music/franz-welser-most-leads-cleveland-orchestra-review.html', 'snippet': 'Franz Welser-Möst led the Cleveland Orchestra in an odd coupling of Bruckner and Adams works at Avery Fisher Hall on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Franz Welser-Möst and the Cleveland Orchestra have taken up residence at Avery Fisher Hall for a series — part of the Lincoln Center Festival — called “Bruckner: (R)evolution,” in which Bruckner symphonies are juxtaposed with works by John Adams. It is an odd pairing, built on Mr. Welser-Möst’s interesting assertion that Bruckner is “the grandfather of Minimalism.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/seventh/seventh-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/seventh/seventh-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/seventh/seventh-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Concept Is Minimal. The Works Sure Aren’t.', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Concept Is Minimal. The Works Sure Aren’t.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cleveland Orchestra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lincoln Center Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Welser-Most, Franz', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bruckner, Anton', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adams, John (1947- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-15T23:19:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbf74f55-ed84-51cb-a764-aff258529a30', 'word_count': 477, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbf74f55-ed84-51cb-a764-aff258529a30'}, {'abstract': 'Pop music finds itself at a time when its signature sound often feels like a spinoff from another age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/arts/music/new-pop-music-sounds-like-its-predecessors.html', 'snippet': 'Pop music finds itself at a time when its signature sound often feels like a spinoff from another age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONCE pop music was something by which you could tell the decade, or even the year. But listening to the radio nowadays is disorienting, if you’re searching for a sound that screams, “It’s 2011!” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/TIMELESS-1/TIMELESS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/TIMELESS-1/TIMELESS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/TIMELESS-1/TIMELESS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/TIMELESS-1/TIMELESS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/arts/TIMELESS-1/TIMELESS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Songs of Now Sound a Lot Like Then', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Songs of Now Sound a Lot Like Then', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sterling, Bruce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, Etta', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, Cee Lo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 