Russia since the collapse of Communism. The changes require abortion providers to devote 10 percent of any advertising to describing the dangers of abortion to a woman’s health, and they make it illegal to describe abortion as a safe medical procedure. The changes, which will take effect in 30 days, were signed by Mr. Medvedev on Monday and announced Thursday on the Kremlin’s Web site. Parliament is expected to consider tighter restrictions on abortion in a separate bill this fall. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Abortion Restrictions Signed', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: Abortion Restrictions Signed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Medvedev, Dmitri A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T03:51:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophia Kishkovsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kishkovsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/845a568a-09a8-5c7d-86f9-31feb1188f25', 'word_count': 96, 'uri': 'nyt://article/845a568a-09a8-5c7d-86f9-31feb1188f25'}, {'abstract': 'A federal appeals court has temporarily reinstated the policy that prohibits openly gay men and women from serving in the military — but prohibited any investigation, penalties or discharges under the rule.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/us/16brfs-DONTASKDONTT_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'A federal appeals court has temporarily reinstated the policy that prohibits openly gay men and women from serving in the military — but prohibited any investigation, penalties or discharges under the rule.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal appeals court has temporarily reinstated the policy that prohibits openly gay men and women from serving in the military — but prohibited any investigation, penalties or discharges under the rule. A three-judge panel on the Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit had, on July 6, ordered the military to end the policy. The judges issued the three-page order late Friday in response to an emergency request by the Obama administration to let the Department of Defense continue its repeal process in an orderly fashion. The government provided “considerably more detailed information” about the repeal in its most recent filings, including statements that the military would be presenting its certification to repeal the policy to the president and top military officials “in a matter of weeks.” “Don’t ask, don’t tell” will officially end 60 days after certification. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California: ‘Don’t Ask, Don’t Tell’ Is Back, Temporarily', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WEST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California: ‘Don’t Ask, Don’t Tell’ Is Back, Temporarily', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bb1f9fb-aaf8-592c-806b-3f5dcc50d25f', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bb1f9fb-aaf8-592c-806b-3f5dcc50d25f'}, {'abstract': 'A concoction called Bacon Explosion rolls bacon and sausage together and can be baked or cooked in a smoker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2009/01/28/dining/0128-BACON_index.html', 'snippet': 'A concoction called Bacon Explosion rolls bacon and sausage together and can be baked or cooked in a smoker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A concoction called Bacon Explosion rolls bacon and sausage together and can be baked or cooked in a smoker.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2009/01/28/dining/0128-BACON-S.JPG', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2009/01/28/dining/0128-BACON-S.JPG', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pig, Pig and More Pig', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bacon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sausages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:02:29+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Food', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/e820b9ea-2bf0-5163-8a3f-bb52ce8fe42d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/e820b9ea-2bf0-5163-8a3f-bb52ce8fe42d'}, {'abstract': 'Julie Glassberg spent more than a year with the New York chapter of the Black Label Bike Club, capturing the postindustrial landscapes its members inhabit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/17/nyregion/20110717_visual.html', 'snippet': 'Julie Glassberg spent more than a year with the New York chapter of the Black Label Bike Club, capturing the postindustrial landscapes its members inhabit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Julie Glassberg spent more than a year with the New York chapter of the Black Label Bike Club, capturing the postindustrial landscapes its members inhabit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Black Label’s Bike Kill, ‘Freedom via Mayhem’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:05:23+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/1da924b2-c9f2-5650-8108-a2c9f6b38e7b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/1da924b2-c9f2-5650-8108-a2c9f6b38e7b'}, {'abstract': 'The photographer Julie Glassberg spent time with the New York chapter of the Black Label Bike Club, capturing the postindustrial landscapes its members inhabit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/nyregion/at-black-labels-bike-kill-freedom-via-mayhem.html', 'snippet': 'The photographer Julie Glassberg spent time with the New York chapter of the Black Label Bike Club, capturing the postindustrial landscapes its members inhabit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAKE a dash of “Mad Max,” add a pinch of “Jackass,” sprinkle both over a wet batter of art students, bicycle messengers, anarchist welders and militant anti-globalist vegans, then let the mixture bake for, say, a decade in the oven of Brooklyn, and the resulting dish should taste a little like the Black Label Bike Club.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Freedom, via Mayhem’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Freedom, Via Mayhem’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:06:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Feuer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feuer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf16ec08-e334-55d8-be49-c2eb52030df7', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf16ec08-e334-55d8-be49-c2eb52030df7'}, {'abstract': 'A Kansai Electric Power official said that no radiation had leaked from their reactor in central Japan, but a loss of pressure was detected in a tank needed to cool the core.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/world/asia/16japan.html', 'snippet': 'A Kansai Electric Power official said that no radiation had leaked from their reactor in central Japan, but a loss of pressure was detected in a tank needed to cool the core.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Japan’s second-largest nuclear operator said Saturday that it was manually shutting down a reactor in central Japan after a technical malfunction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan Operator Shutting Down Nuclear Reactor After Malfunction', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan Operator Shutting Down Nuclear Reactor After Malfunction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tokyo (Japan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:07:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7467a12f-d2d2-5538-bc36-8d365062c25d', 'word_count': 271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7467a12f-d2d2-5538-bc36-8d365062c25d'}, {'abstract': 'Vance Worley continued his impressive rookie campaign on Friday, shutting down the Mets through five innings before finding some trouble in the sixth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/sports/baseball/in-worley-phillies-have-a-valuable-spare-starter.html', 'snippet': 'Vance Worley continued his impressive rookie campaign on Friday, shutting down the Mets through five innings before finding some trouble in the sixth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the array of aces at the Philadelphia Phillies’ disposal, it would almost be unfair if they were to unearth a future front-line starter to plug in at the back end of their star-studded rotation. But in Vance Worley, the Phillies might have found just that.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Worley, Phillies Have a Valuable Spare Starter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Worley, Phillies Have a Valuable Spare Starter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia Phillies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:12:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jorge Castillo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jorge', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castillo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58f25d41-cbbc-5cfd-9d0e-e29ac4f8180d', 'word_count': 541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58f25d41-cbbc-5cfd-9d0e-e29ac4f8180d'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Ragovoy infused his songs with a wrenching emotional quality, including “Time Is on My Side,” “Piece of My Heart,” and “Get It While You Can.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/arts/music/jerry-ragovoy-songwriter-and-producer-is-dead-at-80.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Ragovoy infused his songs with a wrenching emotional quality, including “Time Is on My Side,” “Piece of My Heart,” and “Get It While You Can.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jerry Ragovoy, who wrote or collaborated on some of the most soulful ballads of the 1960s, including the Rolling Stones hit “Time Is on My Side” and the Janis Joplin signatures “Piece of My Heart,” “Cry Baby” and “Try (Just a Little Bit Harder),” died on Wednesday in Manhattan. He was 80.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/RAVOGOY1-obit/RAVOGOY1-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 777, 'width': 538, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/RAVOGOY1-obit/RAVOGOY1-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/RAVOGOY1-obit/RAVOGOY1-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jerry Ragovoy, Writer of Soulful Ballads, Dies at 80', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jerry Ragovoy, Writer of Soulful Ballads, Dies at 80', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ragovoy, Jerry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:29:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/f28b3556-6d44-552a-a812-99a6024896ab', 'word_count': 804, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f28b3556-6d44-552a-a812-99a6024896ab'}, {'abstract': 'A critic bids a great show goodbye, with a wish list for the characters’ futures.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/a-friday-night-lights-farewell/', 'snippet': 'A critic bids a great show goodbye, with a wish list for the characters’ futures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are few series I can think of whose conclusions occasion feelings of sorrow. “The Sopranos” ended amid confusion and awe. We may miss the narrative and the ingeniously black comedy of it all, if not the actual characters, none of whom you really want tucking you into bed at night. The same can be said of “Big Love,” another of the greatest family dramas ever on television. Though we may miss its intelligent social commentary, we do not necessarily pine for the people in it to re-enter our lives. HBO renders characters ambiguously; we are not supposed to fall in love with each and every one of them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A ‘Friday Night Lights’ Farewell', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:31:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e658ec11-520f-5c15-98b5-54f43e1bfc5a', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e658ec11-520f-5c15-98b5-54f43e1bfc5a'}, {'abstract': 'The legal scholar and left-of-center politician dismayed some supporters by joining a harsh military government, though he later left it for exile.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/world/americas/16villagran.html', 'snippet': 'The legal scholar and left-of-center politician dismayed some supporters by joining a harsh military government, though he later left it for exile.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Francisco Villagrán Kramer, a politician and an internationally known legal scholar who went into self-imposed exile in the United States after joining and then defying Guatemala’s right-wing military government in the late 1970s, died Tuesday. He was 84. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/world/VILLAGRAN-obit/VILLAGRAN-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 847, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/world/VILLAGRAN-obit/VILLAGRAN-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/16/world/VILLAGRAN-obit/VILLAGRAN-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Francisco Villagrán Kramer, Guatemala Official, Dies at 84', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Francisco Villagrán Kramer, Guatemala Official, Dies at 84', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Villagran Kramer, Francisco', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guatemala', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:34:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randal C. Archibold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randal', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Archibold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6d921f1-ba01-52e5-903c-054529775326', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6d921f1-ba01-52e5-903c-054529775326'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on July 16, 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/pageoneplus/corrections-july-16.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on July 16, 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRONT PAGE', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: July 16', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:38:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd446520-5b68-5a18-ad00-b40145d0c3c7', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd446520-5b68-5a18-ad00-b40145d0c3c7'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Bean, a machinist and musician, created unadorned electric guitars and basses that had an uncanny ability to sustain notes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/arts/music/travis-bean-aluminum-guitar-maker-dies-at-63.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Bean, a machinist and musician, created unadorned electric guitars and basses that had an uncanny ability to sustain notes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travis Bean, a machinist who earned a nugget of rock ’n’ roll immortality by making electric guitars with necks fashioned out of aluminum instead of wood and selling them to members of the Grateful Dead and the Rolling Stones, died on July 10 in Burbank, Calif. He was 63.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/BEAN-obit/BEAN-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 615, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/BEAN-obit/BEAN-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/16/arts/BEAN-obit/BEAN-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Travis Bean, Aluminum Guitar Maker, Dies at 63', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Travis Bean, 63, Aluminum Guitar Maker', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Guitars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bean, Travis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T04:42:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/265b5886-eeea-5d85-a385-17e9b86da77f', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/265b5886-eeea-5d85-a385-17e9b86da77f'}, {'abstract': 'At 69, Mr. McCartney showed in a two-and-a-half hour show at Yankee Stadium that he is not saying goodbye, rather an enduring hello.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/arts/music/paul-mccartney-yankee-stadium-concert-review.html', 'snippet': 'At 69, Mr. McCartney showed in a two-and-a-half hour show at Yankee Stadium that he is not saying goodbye, rather an enduring hello.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cheerful, childlike doggerel of “Hello Goodbye” — ”I don’t know why you say goodbye/I say hello” — struck an unexpected note as Paul McCartney sang it on Friday night to start his two-night stand at Yankee Stadium. “Who is this Derek Jeter guy?” Mr. McCartney asked. “Somebody said he’s got more hits than me.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/MCCARTNEY/MCCARTNEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/MCCARTNEY/MCCARTNEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/MCCARTNEY/MCCARTNEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 545, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/MCCARTNEY/MCCARTNEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/MCCARTNEY/MCCARTNEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Gentle Reminder of Paul McCartney’s Survival and Vitality', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Gentle Reminder of Paul McCartney’s Survival and Vitality', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCartney, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yankee Stadium (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beatles, The', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T09:08:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3320457-6a99-5ee3-816b-e6897e3781d7', 'word_count': 741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3320457-6a99-5ee3-816b-e6897e3781d7'}, {'abstract': 'The Argentine striker Sergio Agüero has finally stepped out of some all-consuming shadows that have long shaded his soccer career.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/sergio-aguero-the-son-in-law-of-god-escapes-long-shadows/', 'snippet': 'The Argentine striker Sergio Agüero has finally stepped out of some all-consuming shadows that have long shaded his soccer career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s the dinner conversation like these days at Sergio Agüero’s house when his in-laws visit?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sergio Agüero, the Son-in-Law of God, Escapes Long Shadows', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copa America (Soccer)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T09:42:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Marcus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marcus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea9447d5-c6bf-5ea1-a969-b71a597c534c', 'word_count': 899, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea9447d5-c6bf-5ea1-a969-b71a597c534c'}, {'abstract': 'Dinah Shore’s classic is our classic ad this week.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/classic-ad-see-the-u-s-a-in-your-chevrolet/', 'snippet': 'Dinah Shore’s classic is our classic ad this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week we present what might be the most classic of all car ads: Dinah Shore’s famous ode to Chevy, which sponsored multiple incarnations of her television show for more than a decade in the 1950s and 60s. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Classic Ad: “See the U.S.A. in Your Chevrolet”', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T10:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Peele', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Peele', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff89ed9f-eeef-50f6-a4c6-e92bd4f584a6', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff89ed9f-eeef-50f6-a4c6-e92bd4f584a6'}, {'abstract': 'The Shoreditch Festival, which will run in London’s East End from July 16 to 24, puts on a colorful — and free — program of entertainment, including theater, dance and musical performances, as well as exploration of East London’s waterways.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/east-london-festival-puts-culture-on-the-water/', 'snippet': 'The Shoreditch Festival, which will run in London’s East End from July 16 to 24, puts on a colorful — and free — program of entertainment, including theater, dance and musical performances, as well as exploration of East London’s waterways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Regents Canal was built in the early 19th century to carry coal and other goods across northern London. Although its commercial days are long over, it has found a new life as a popular site for boat trips and other leisure activities — among them the Shoreditch Festival, which will run in London’s East End from today to July 24. The festival puts on a colorful — and free — program of entertainment, including theater, dance and musical performances, as well as exploration of East London’s waterways. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'East London Festival Puts Culture on the Water', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Festivals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T10:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia Barbieri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'las