 for later in the year. Goldman Sachs (no link) was pretty optimistic a few months ago; now they’ve grown quite pessimistic:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Political Economy of the Lesser Depression', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T13:07:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f171e83-c305-5481-9c6b-ac8ae0c2b6ef', 'word_count': 403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f171e83-c305-5481-9c6b-ac8ae0c2b6ef'}, {'abstract': 'Interstate 405, 10 miles of which are closed all weekend for a road-widening project, was a virtual ghost town.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/us/17freeway.html', 'snippet': 'Interstate 405, 10 miles of which are closed all weekend for a road-widening project, was a virtual ghost town.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — The warnings had been grim: a knot of traffic stretching from the Pacific Ocean to downtown Los Angeles. Motorists struggling to make it from West Los Angeles across the Sepulveda Pass into the San Fernando Valley. A display of gridlock that would drain even the most hardened Los Angeles commuter, all caused by the weekend closing of a 10-mile stretch of Interstate 405.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/us/17freeway2/17freeway2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/us/17freeway2/17freeway