.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca8e29a0-3842-5296-b9ef-48f41195a27b', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca8e29a0-3842-5296-b9ef-48f41195a27b'}, {'abstract': 'C.C. Sabathia won his seventh consecutive start, stifling the Blue Jays with a deadly slider during eight strong innings in the Yankees’ victory Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/sports/baseball/with-sabathia-yankees-take-one-from-blue-jays.html', 'snippet': 'C.C. Sabathia won his seventh consecutive start, stifling the Blue Jays with a deadly slider during eight strong innings in the Yankees’ victory Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TORONTO — The Yankees have their flaws, as the first two games of a series here against the Blue Jays showed, but on days when C. C. Sabathia pitches, none of them seem to matter. Let others fret about an understaffed bullpen, a wobbly third baseman or an unsteady rotation. With Sabathia on the mound, the Yankees do not worry. They just watch, marvel and win. ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/sports/17yankees1/17yankees1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 730, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/sports/17yankees1/17yankees1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/sports/17yankees1/17yankees1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sabathia Reminds the Yankees What They’re Not Missing', 'kicker': 'Yankees 4, Blue Jays 1', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sabathia Reminds the Yankees What They’re Not Missing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toronto Blue Jays', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sabathia, C C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T20:32:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6c30128-b203-5fdc-840a-b18bb76be09f', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6c30128-b203-5fdc-840a-b18bb76be09f'}, {'abstract': 'Brian Wilson said keeping score helped him stay seated during games and kept his stomach settled.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/brian-wilson-part-time-closer-part-time-statistician/', 'snippet': 'Brian Wilson said keeping score helped him stay seated during games and kept his stomach settled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a starting pitcher in high school, Brian Wilson said, he kept the scorebook on the days he did not pitch. At Louisiana State, he kept the scorebook on days he manned the radar gun in the stands. Many pitchers perform these duties.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brian Wilson: Part-Time Closer, Part-Time Statistician', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Brian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Francisco Giants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T20:40:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/023c2f39-4349-5069-904a-d711870a03ec', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/023c2f39-4349-5069-904a-d711870a03ec'}, {'abstract': 'The Pacific Pinball Museum on Alameda Island turns back the clock on arcade games, housing 90 machines, some dating back to the 19th century and many available for visitors to play.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/us/17bcintel.html', 'snippet': 'The Pacific Pinball Museum on Alameda Island turns back the clock on arcade games, housing 90 machines, some dating back to the 19th century and many available for visitors to play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alameda Island, the site of a former military base, can feel like a 1950s-era small town, with its Craftsman-style homes and neighborhood bars. The addition of a pinball museum, where old-fashioned bonks are more common than electronic beeps, has only heightened that effect. Michael Schiess, a former museum exhibit builder, opened his Alameda pinball palace in 2001, attracting devoted “pinheads” and casual players alike.  ANDY WRIGHT', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/us/17BCINTEL1/17BCINTEL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/us/17BCINTEL1/17BCI