James Warren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9aed7308-d009-5d01-823a-8a7f453f3cbf', 'word_count': 753, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9aed7308-d009-5d01-823a-8a7f453f3cbf'}, {'abstract': 'In an era of foreclosures, evictions and families in temporary housing, the number of pets being brought to shelters is on the rise.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/us/17cncpets.html', 'snippet': 'In an era of foreclosures, evictions and families in temporary housing, the number of pets being brought to shelters is on the rise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People are not the only victims of the dismal economy. Pets are also being forced to cope. Several Chicago-area animal shelters report a sharp uptick in the number of abandoned dogs and cats in the last four years. “We certainly have seen an increase in dogs surrendered,” said Dr. Robyn Barbiers, executive director of the Anti-Cruelty Society. In its last fiscal year, ended June 2010, the shelter received 854 dogs. This year, the number jumped to nearly 1,000.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/us/17CNCPETS/17CNCPETS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 862, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/us/17CNCPETS/17CNCPETS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/us/17CNCPETS/17CNCPETS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Foreclosure? Many Pets Are Losing Their Homes', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Foreclosure? Many Pets Are Losing Their Homes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T21:02:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': "By Bridget O'Shea", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bridget', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Shea", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28d359e8-8113-5e65-a910-0b8d0001a260', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28d359e8-8113-5e65-a910-0b8d0001a260'}, {'abstract': 'A Fourth of July celebration turned tragic when a fight led to the death of a young man, casting a dark shadow over the community of Long Beach, Ind.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/us/17cnclongbeach.html', 'snippet': 'A Fourth of July celebration turned tragic when a fight led to the death of a young man, casting a dark shadow over the community of Long Beach, Ind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a Fourth of July like any other in Long Beach, Ind., an idyllic lakeside getaway frequented by well-heeled members of Chicago’s Irish community — a “Norman Rockwell scene” in the words of several local residents — complete with a parade and fireworks over the golf course. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/us/17CNCLONGBEACH2/17CNCLONGBEACH2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit'