n': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/119852a4-4c51-5b3a-a852-fbe59825a8c3', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/119852a4-4c51-5b3a-a852-fbe59825a8c3'}, {'abstract': 'As the raw material of history is digitized, some of the mystery is lost.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/opinion/sunday/17gleick.html', 'snippet': 'As the raw material of history is digitized, some of the mystery is lost.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I GOT a real thrill in December 1999 in the Reading Room of\xa0the Morgan Library in New York when the librarian, Sylvie Merian, brought me, after I had completed an application with a letter of reference and a photo ID, the first, oldest notebook of Isaac Newton. First I was required to study a microfilm version. There followed a certain amount of appropriate pomp. The notebook was lifted from a blue cloth drop-spine box and laid on a special padded stand. I was struck by how impossibly tiny it was — 58 leaves bound in vellum, just 2 3/4 inches wide, half the size I would have guessed from the enlarged microfilm images. There was his name, “Isacus Newton,” proudly inscribed by the 17-year-old with his quill, and the date, 1659.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/sunday-review/GLEICK/GLEICK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/sunday-review/GLEICK/GLEICK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/sunday-review/GLEICK/GLEICK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Books and Other Fetish Objects', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Books and Other Fetish Objects', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Library', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Archives and Records', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T22:07:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Gleick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gleick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f94605fc-d766-54ea-a503-3a1e43cabb6e', 'word_count': 1034, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f94605fc-d766-54ea-a503-3a1e43cabb6e'}, {'abstract': 'Cancer control depends on success in scientific, political and social challenges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/opinion/sunday/17Mukherjee.html', 'snippet': 'Cancer control depends on success in scientific, political and social challenges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THREE recent events highlight the extraordinary task that lies ahead for cancer prevention.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/sunday-review/CANCER/CANCER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/17/sunday-revie