', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b70c54b-db0c-546c-bee9-1f513dbf746d', 'word_count': 1069, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b70c54b-db0c-546c-bee9-1f513dbf746d'}, {'abstract': 'Unlike earthquakes, hurricanes and geological phenomena, drought could become a permanent condition in some regions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/opinion/sunday/17drought.html', 'snippet': 'Unlike earthquakes, hurricanes and geological phenomena, drought could become a permanent condition in some regions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FLOODS, tornadoes, earthquakes, tsunamis and other geological phenomena have left a trail of destruction during the first half of 2011. But this could be just the start to a remarkable year of bad weather. Next up: drought. In the South, 14 states are now baking in blast-furnace conditions — from Arizona, which is battling the largest wildfire in its history, to Florida, where fires have burned some 200,000 acres so far. Worse, drought, unlike earthquakes, hurricanes and other rapid-moving weather, could become a permanent condition in some regions.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/sunday-review/17Drought/17Drought-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 926, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/17/sunday-review/17Drought/17Drought-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/17/sunday-review/17Drought/17Drought-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drought: A Creeping Disaster', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drought: A Creeping Disaster', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drought', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservation of Resources', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T22:22:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': "By ALEX PRUD'HOMME", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "PRUD'HOMME", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/54b1cabd-f502-5c38-8ddd-c328a5cfdb55', 'word_count': 1219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54b1cabd-f502-5c38-8ddd-c328a5cfdb55'}, {'abstract': 'The Los Angeles punk duo No Age managed to get a small part of its large crowd moshing, while the  pleasant, trippy Woods treated its set like the summer show it actually was.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/at-pitchfork-too-hot-to-mosh-or-wear-black/', 'snippet': 'The Los Angeles punk duo No Age managed to get a small part of its large crowd moshing, while the  pleasant, trippy Woods treated its set like the summer show it actually was.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO — It’s hot. Don’t-wanna-move hot. Watch-a-band-for-10-minutes-then-find-3-minutes-of-shade hot. Popular outfit for boys: tank top, cut-off jeans, Vans with no socks. Popular outfit for girls: sundress, sandals, wide-brimmed hat.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pitchfork: Too Hot to Mosh, or Wear Black', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pitchfork Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T22:23:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/626630f2-77a9-57e5-8a6a-4049c8625b35', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/626630f2-77a9-57e5-8a6a-4049c8625b35'}, {'abstract': 'Readers comment on my column about education cutbacks and the toll on my own old high school in Yamhill, Oregon.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/16/education-education-education/', 'snippet': 'Readers comment on my column about education cutbacks and the toll on my own old high school in Yamhill, Oregon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column is a despairing bleat at the cutbacks in education budgets around the country — including my old school, Yamhill-Carlton High School, here in Yamhill, Oregon. In years of writing about poverty, I’ve come to believe firmly that there are no perfect solutions either in America or abroad — but the closest thing to a silver bullet is an excellent education system. And it is so frustrating that that’s still what’s missing in so many communities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Education, Education, Education', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-16T22:30:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fc23a1b-7b78-5e72-b5e3-eaa030d9ea60', 'word_count': 112, 'uri