dent Hosni Mubarak’s lawyer said that he had suffered a stroke and fallen into a coma, but the report was countered by at least one doctor at the hospital where Mr. Mubarak has been kept.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/world/middleeast/18egypt.html', 'snippet': 'Former President Hosni Mubarak’s lawyer said that he had suffered a stroke and fallen into a coma, but the report was countered by at least one doctor at the hospital where Mr. Mubarak has been kept.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — Conflicts about the deteriorating health of former President Hosni Mubarak reached a new peak on Sunday, when his lawyer announced he had suffered a stroke and slipped into a coma, only to be contradicted hours later by state television and hospital officials who said he had a temporary bout of low blood pressure.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/world/EGYPT/EGYPT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/world/EGYPT/EGYPT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/18/world/EGYPT/EGYPT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lawyer Says Mubarak Is in a Coma, but the Report Is Quickly Contradicted', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lawyer Says Mubarak Is in a Coma, but the Report Is Quickly Contradicted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T18:42:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4919f10-da31-563c-a157-0cd5ef09fd76', 'word_count': 824, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4919f10-da31-563c-a157-0cd5ef09fd76'}, {'abstract': 'Jan Mohammed Khan, a close adviser to President Hamid Karzai who had been a fierce foe of the Taliban, was killed after a team of gunmen stormed his home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/world/asia/18afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'Jan Mohammed Khan, a close adviser to President Hamid Karzai who had been a fierce foe of the Taliban, was killed after a team of gunmen stormed his home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — A close adviser to President Hamid Karzai was killed on Sunday night after two gunmen stormed his walled home here. It was the second killing in less than a week of one of the president’s trusted but controversial political allies.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/world/asia/18afghanistan/18afghanistan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 631, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/world/asia/18afghanistan/18afghanistan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/18/world/asia/18afghanistan/18afghanistan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Karzai Adviser Is Killed at Kabul Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2nd Killing In a Week Claims Ally Of Karzai', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karzai, Hamid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T19:12:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Healy and Abdul Waheed Wafa', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Abdul', 'middlename': 'Waheed', 'lastname': 'Wafa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ea2c00c-79d5-5e8d-aac5-445cced342c0', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ea2c00c-79d5-5e8d-aac5-445cced342c0'}, {'abstract': 'China has delayed the opening of the new Harry Potter and “Transformers: Dark Side of the Moon” until a state-sponsored Communist epic becomes a state-sponsored hit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/movies/in-china-no-harry-potter-until-revolutionary-epic-is-a-hit.html', 'snippet': 'China has delayed the opening of the new Harry Potter and “Transformers: Dark Side of the Moon” until a state-sponsored Communist epic becomes a state-sponsored hit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — It has been something of cruel summer for Chinese movie audiences. The latest installments of Hollywood blockbusters like “Transformers: Dark of the Moon” and “Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows: Part 2” have been delayed, and it is doubtful that American-made crowd pleasers like “Horrible Bosses” and “The Hangover Part II” will ever arrive in theaters. In the meantime countless moviegoers have been driven into cinemas as part of a government campaign to promote a sprawling epic about the Chinese Communist Party.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/FLICKS/FLICKS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/FLICKS/FLICKS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/FLICKS/FLICKS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/FLICKS/FLICKS-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/FLICKS/FLICKS-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Waiting for Harry, Chinese Filmgoers Get Patriotic Epic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Waiting for Harry, Chinese Filmgoers Get Patriotic Epic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T19:41:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shao Heng and Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heng', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/244cf382-bf3e-5a10-8eb1-bfe1af0493f8', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/244cf382-bf3e-5a10-8eb1-bfe1af0493f8'}, {'abstract': 'Readers recall the subway in the 1970s and 1980s, when cars were not crowded on weekends, but there were plenty of other problems to contend with.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/when-subways-werent-sardine-cans/', 'snippet': 'Readers recall the subway in the 1970s and 1980s, when cars were not crowded on weekends, but there were plenty of other problems to contend with.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a City Room post accompanying his article about the rise in weekend subway ridership, Michael M. Grynbaum asked readers to recount their memories of the city subway system of the 1970s and 1980s, when riders had plenty of leg room on weekends, but had to contend with plenty of problems, too. Below, some of the more interesting reader responses, edited for style and space.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Subways Weren’t Sardine Cans', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Eighties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Seventies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T19:45:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_materia