rsons', 'value': 'Garrido, Nancy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dugard, Jaycee Lee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T21:24:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd2067ca-524d-54a2-b86f-8c851491e6d1', 'word_count': 959, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd2067ca-524d-54a2-b86f-8c851491e6d1'}, {'abstract': 'Russ Arnold has been selected to receive the Von Zedtwitz Award.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/crosswords/bridge/russ-arnold-wins-von-zedtwitz-award-bridge.html', 'snippet': 'Russ Arnold has been selected to receive the Von Zedtwitz Award.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are two new inductees to the American Contract Bridge League’s Hall of Fame, Russ Arnold of Sunny Isles Beach, Fla., and Eric Kokish of Toronto.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inspired Play From an Award-Worthy Career', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inspired Play From an Award-Worthy Career', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T21:28:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e84cb8e7-1791-539a-b637-b669144ad9e6', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e84cb8e7-1791-539a-b637-b669144ad9e6'}, {'abstract': 'The scourge of the typo in print and digital book publishing.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/the-price-of-typos/', 'snippet': 'The scourge of the typo in print and digital book publishing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some readers like to see portraits of authors they admire, study their personal histories or hear them read aloud. I like to know whether an author can spell. Nabokov spelled beautifully. Fitzgerald was crummy at spelling, bedeviled by entry-level traps like “definate.” Bad spellers, of course, can be sublime writers and good spellers punctilious duds. But it’s still intriguing that Fitzgerald, for all his gifts, didn’t perceive the word “finite” in definite, the way good spellers automatically do. Did this oversight color his impression of infinity? Infinaty?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Price of Typos', 'kicker': 'Virginia Heffernan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spelling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T21:30:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Virginia Heffernan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Virginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heffernan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7523c82c-51b7-5c91-add0-fc91bfb55ee1', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7523c82c-51b7-5c91-add0-fc91bfb55ee1'}, {'abstract': 'In “Annie Get Your Gun” at Glimmerglass, the soprano Deborah Voigt brings clear diction, sweetness and sassiness to the title role.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/arts/music/annie-get-your-gun-at-glimmerglass-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “Annie Get Your Gun” at Glimmerglass, the soprano Deborah Voigt brings clear diction, sweetness and sassiness to the title role.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COOPERSTOWN, N.Y. — Not three months after singing her first Brünnhilde in the Metropolitan Opera’s new production of Wagner’s “Walküre,” the soprano Deborah Voigt is performing the title role in Irving Berlin’s “Annie Get Your Gun” here at the Glimmerglass Festival. Though a wide-ranging repertory is an admirable quality in an artist, it is quite a leap from Wagner’s “Ring” to a classic Broadway musical. Coming into this production, Ms. Voigt risked looking foolish.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/SUBGUN/SUBGUN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/SUBGUN/SUBGUN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/SUBGUN/SUBGUN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 843, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/SUBGUN/SUBGUN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/SUBGUN/SUBGUN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gun-Totin’ Brünnhilde', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gun-Totin’ Brünnhilde', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Glimmerglass Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berlin, Irving', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Voigt, Deborah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zambello, Francesca', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T21:38:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/82228e9c-46db-508a-bc71-e9d68e0fc371', 'word_count': 822, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82228e9c-46db-508a-bc71-e9d68e0fc371'}, {'abstract': 'The Royal Shakespeare Company’s “King Lear” is mechanically sound but lacking depth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/theater/reviews/king-lear-by-royal-shakespeare-company-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Royal Shakespeare Company’s “King Lear” is mechanically sound but lacking depth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Royal Shakespeare Company’s production of “King Lear,” directed by David Farr, can be strongly recommended for hypersensitive souls. Have you always found the play to be too devastating to endure, its serial acts of cruelty too relentless to witness? Do you share Samuel Johnson’s moral outrage at the death of Cordelia in the last act? Hie thee to the Park Avenue Armory, where this competent but immaculately unwrenching production of Shakespeare’s harrowing tragedy is providing more than three hours of placid traffic on the stage.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/KING/KING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 937, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/KING/KING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/KING/KING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Old, Inexorable Story of a King’s Disintegration', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'King Lear' ", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Old, Inexorable Story Of a King’s Disintegration', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lincoln Center Festival', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Shakespeare Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T21:43:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ab18a14-6996-5d82-a271-5361f6038ee5', 'word_count': 1353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ab18a14-6996-5d82-a271-5361f6038ee5'}, {'abstract': 'These days it can be hard for a new Web site to attract attention. So many companies have tried creating a sense of exclusivity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/technology/start-ups-try-to-beckon-users-by-invitation-only.html', 'snippet': 'These days it can be hard for a new Web site to attract attention. So many companies have tried creating a sense of exclusivity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Demand for Google’s new social networking service, Google+, has been intense since its debut last month in a limited test mode. To get in, you must be invited by someone who is already a member, so Facebook and Twitter are peppered with requests for invitations to the service, and they have even been sold on eBay.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Pique Interest, Start-Ups Try a Digital Velvet Rope', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Some Start-Ups Pique Interest by Invitation Only', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T21:53:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f550d527-1fe5-5215-aa53-9c2a1ab9e3f6', 'word_count': 923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f550d527-1fe5-5215-aa53-9c2a1ab9e3f6'}, {'abstract': 'A Merce Fair on Saturday, part of the Lincoln Center Festival, occupied seven separate spaces in the Frederick P. Rose Hall: it was called a fair because a wide range of goods was on offer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/arts/dance/merce-fair-celebrating-merce-cunningham-review.html', 'snippet': 'A Merce Fair on Saturday, part of the Lincoln Center Festival, occupied seven separate spaces in the Frederick P. Rose Hall: it was called a fair because a wide range of goods was on offer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s be pessimistic. Let’s say that when the Merce Cunningham Dance Company shuts down after Dec. 31, his choreography will very seldom be seen again. What will have left the world with it? Many different answers arose while watching the all-day Merce Fair on Saturday. This, as part of the Lincoln Center Festival, occupied seven separate spaces in the Frederick P. Rose Hall. It was called a fair because a wide range of goods was on offer. Cunningham died two years ago this month; this day was the last of his company’s many Lincoln Center appearances over the years. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/FAIR/FAIR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/FAIR/FAIR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/FAIR/FAIR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 666, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/FAIR/FAIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/18/arts/FAIR/FAIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Such Stuff as Dreams Are Made on: A Tribute to Cunningham', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Such Stuff as Dreams Are Made On: A Tribute to Cunningham', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cunningham, Merce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cunningham, Merce, Dance Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lincoln Center Festival', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T21:58:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/24de0e7a-f2a5-5bc9-86dc-2bc75a776f1a', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24de0e7a-f2a5-5bc9-86dc-2bc75a776f1a'}, {'abstract': 'Does it matter whether our belief in human dignity originates in a belief in God?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/the-sacred-and-the-humane/', 'snippet': 'Does it matter whether our belief in human dignity originates in a belief in God?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Human Rights are all the rage. They have become, currently, a very popular arena for both political activism and rampant discourse. Human rights, as we all know, are the rights humans are due simply by virtue of being human. But there is nothing simple here, since both “human” and “rights” are concepts in need of investigation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sacred and the Humane', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "B'Tselem", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rabbis for Human Rights', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T22:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anat Biletzki', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Biletzki', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/442fe832-dfe2-5c8c-81c2-a8083f2ba586', 'word_count': 2087, 'uri': 'nyt://article/442fe832-dfe2-5c8c-81c2-a8083f2ba586'}, {'abstract': 'A carefully measured crossword by Kevin Donovan.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/above/', 'snippet': 'A carefully measured crossword by Kevin Donovan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — I realize that some of you solve the puzzle when it comes out the day before, but for the sake of making my point, let’s pretend that it’s Monday morning and you are settling in to solve with your first cup of coffee. You’ve spent the weekend wrestling with the tough Friday and Saturday grids, and you’ve devoted most of your Sunday to finishing the big puzzle and the variety. A challenge is a wonderful thing, but now you need something to ease you into your week. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Attack From Above', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T22:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f74fe98-3b58-59e3-8a73-ee7f5de7466e', 'word_count': 302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f74fe98-3b58-59e3-8a73-ee7f5de7466e'}, {'abstract': 'Fans of TNT’s “Men of a Certain Age” fretted this month that the show might not make it to a third season, and it turns out they were right to worry: the channel said on Friday that it was canceling the drama after two seasons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/arts/television/tnt-ends-the-run-of-ray-romanos-men.html', 'snippet': 'Fans of TNT’s “Men of a Certain Age” fretted this month that the show might not make it to a third season, and it turns out they were right to worry: the channel said on Friday that it was canceling the drama after two seasons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fans of TNT’s “Men of a Certain Age” fretted this month that the show might not make it to a third season, and it turns out they were right to worry: the channel said on Friday that it was canceling the drama after two seasons. “This was an extremely difficult decision for us,” TNT said in a statement that read in part, “While the show has featured great storytelling and impeccable performances, the audience simply hasn’t built to the point where we can continue the series.” The series — which averaged fewer than three million viewers in its second season — starred (above from left) Ray Romano, André Braugher and Scott Bakula as men who confronting life in their 50s, making the show something of an anomaly on TNT, which is best known for its crime procedurals. Mike Royce, a creator of the show, told AOL TV that he thanked TNT for trying: “It’s different from their other shows. But even though it’s different, they took a huge chance on it, and they gave it two seasons. The bottom line is that we would have gotten three episodes on a broadcast network.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TNT Ends the Run of Ray Romano’s ‘Men’', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TNT Ends the Run Of Ray Romano’s ‘Men’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T22:05:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/60647b0d-9668-5d8b-b532-cfaf90f76f2d', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60647b0d-9668-5d8b-b532-cfaf90f76f2d'}, {'abstract': 'The 2011 New York Dance and Performance Awards, also known as the Bessies, will be presented on Oct. 24 at the Apollo Theater, organizers are to announce at a news conference on Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/arts/dance/bessie-nominations-to-be-announced.html', 'snippet': 'The 2011 New York Dance and Performance Awards, also known as the Bessies, will be presented on Oct. 24 at the Apollo Theater, organizers are to announce at a news conference on Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 2011 New York Dance and Performance Awards, also known as the Bessies, will be presented on Oct. 24 at the Apollo Theater, organizers are to announce at a news conference on Monday. For the first time nominees in the “outstanding production” categories are being announced in advance. For outstanding production of a work performed in a location with more than 400 seats, the nominees are “Thirteen Diversions” by Christopher Wheeldon, performed by American Ballet Theater at the Metropolitan Opera House; “Quartet” by Merce Cunningham, performed by the Merce Cunningham Dance Company at the Joyce Theater; and “The Bright Stream” by Alexei Ratmansky, performed by American Ballet Theater at the Met.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bessie Nominations to Be Announced', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bessie Nominations To Be Announced', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T22:06:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f51a2adf-eb04-5689-96f8-084891a02840', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f51a2adf-eb04-5689-96f8-084891a02840'}, {'abstract': 'Hertz has agreed to buy the Donlen Corporation, a vehicle leasing and management company, for $250 million in cash and will assume $680 million in debt.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/hertz-to-buy-donlen-a-fleet-leasing-company/', 'snippet': 'Hertz has agreed to buy the Donlen Corporation, a vehicle leasing and management company, for $250 million in cash and will assume $680 million in debt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hertz Global Holdings agreed on Sunday to buy the Donlen Corporation, a vehicle leasing and management company, for $250 million in cash. Hertz will also assume Donlen’s $680 million of debt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hertz to Buy Donlen, a Fleet Leasing Company', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hertz Global Holdings Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T22:07:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b01187c-e1df-5f59-9f33-7854452c9217', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b01187c-e1df-5f59-9f33-7854452c9217'}, {'abstract': 'Tom Cruise has agreed to play Jack Reacher in “One Shot,” an action thriller based on the best-selling series of books by Lee Child, according to Deadline.com.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/movies/tom-cruise-agrees-to-play-jack-reacher.html', 'snippet': 'Tom Cruise has agreed to play Jack Reacher in “One Shot,” an action thriller based on the best-selling series of books by Lee Child, according to Deadline.com.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Never one to shy away from controversy, Tom Cruise, below, has agreed to play Jack Reacher in “One Shot,” an action thriller based on the best-selling series of books by Lee Child, according to Deadline.com. Reacher, a decorated military officer turned drifter, is described in the novels as standing 6 foot 5 and weighing 250 pounds; Mr. Cruise’s reported height is 5 foot 7. The physical discrepancy has already created some discontent among fans of the series, though Mr. Child has publicly supported the casting. “Reacher’s size in the books is a metaphor for an unstoppable force, which Cruise portrays in his own way,” he said. The movie, which is scheduled to begin filming in the fall, will be directed by Christopher McQuarrie, whose writing credits include “Valkyrie” and the coming “Mission: Impossible — Ghost Protocol,” both starring Mr. Cruise.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tom Cruise Agrees to Play Jack Reacher', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tom Cruise Agrees To Play Jack Reacher', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruise, Tom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Child, Lee', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-17T22:08:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c22aee13-da4e-5327-b057-52cd7c62ad9d', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c22aee13-da4e-5327-b057-52cd7c62ad9d'}, {'abstract': 'At the Village Voice/4Knots Music Festival, the bands chose an era, or scrambled together a few of them, and personalized their favorite parts of rock history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/arts/music/village-voice4knots-music-festival-south-street-seaport.html', 'snippet': 'At the Village Voice/4Knots Music Festival, the bands chose an era, or scrambled together a few of them, and personalized their favorite parts of rock history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Guitars, guitars, guitars. They droned and scrabbled, pinged and screeched, buzzed and pealed through Saturday afternoon at the Village Voice/4Knots Music Festival, a free indie-rock concert at Pier 17 at South Street Seaport on Saturday afternoon. With just one stage and six bands, it was the pared-down successor to 10 years of the Siren Music Festival that The Voice used to present at Coney Island. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'prin