'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Republican Retreat', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Republican Retreat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T01:23:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/637a5b13-043e-5d76-8795-be1ce6791c4e', 'word_count': 814, 'uri': 'nyt://article/637a5b13-043e-5d76-8795-be1ce6791c4e'}, {'abstract': 'A study of Oregon’s Medicaid program clearly demonstrates the health and financial benefits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/opinion/18mon1.html', 'snippet': 'A study of Oregon’s Medicaid program clearly demonstrates the health and financial benefits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Medicaid is under assault these days from nearly every direction. Governors complain that they cannot afford to put up their share of the money. Congressional Republicans led by Paul Ryan want to reduce the federal contribution by $771 billion over the next decade and shift more costs to the states and low-income Americans. President Obama has expressed willingness to cut Washington’s contribution by $100 billion over that period to help reduce the deficit. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Value of Medicaid', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Value of Medicaid: A study of Oregon’s program clearly demonstrates the health and financial benefits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oregon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T01:28:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c97146c2-159e-55eb-8de2-980f95f251dc', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c97146c2-159e-55eb-8de2-980f95f251dc'}, {'abstract': 'How a tune about the executed abolitionist became the Union’s most popular battle song.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/17/john-brown-marches-on/', 'snippet': 'How a tune about the executed abolitionist became the Union’s most popular battle song.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On July 18, 1861, the 12th Massachusetts Volunteer Infantry left Fort Warren, in Boston Harbor, and marched through the city for an official review on the Boston Common. Leading the way was the regiment’s colonel, Fletcher Webster, the eldest son of Daniel Webster. The regiment’s association with such an eminent personage endeared it to Boston’s most respectable citizens, who lined the streets and cheered as the men trooped by. On the Common, Edward Everett, representing the “ladies of Boston,” presented the regiment with an ornate flag — and then with an equally ornate celebratory address. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'John Brown Marches On', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T01:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Soskis and John Stauffer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soskis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stauffer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f0f6ee4-97d2-50e4-af8c-b5ddc848c475', 'word_count': 1621, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f0f6ee4-97d2-50e4-af8c-b5ddc848c475'}, {'abstract': 'A worrying new law in Israel that bans boycotts against the state chips away at free speech and political rights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/opinion/18mon2.html', 'snippet': 'A worrying new law in Israel that bans boycotts against the state chips away at free speech and political rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Israel’s reputation as a vibrant democracy has been seriously tarnished by a new law intended to stifle outspoken critics of its occupation of the West Bank.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not Befitting a Democracy', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Befitting a Democracy: A worrying new law in Israel chips away at free speech and political rights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'na