tion': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: July 18', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T03:10:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/a928efb7-1126-563c-99d3-97e120c95166', 'word_count': 452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a928efb7-1126-563c-99d3-97e120c95166'}, {'abstract': 'Lawmakers who think they can solve the dispute over reducing debt by asking economists are out of luck. Economists can’t agree, either.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/us/18econ.html', 'snippet': 'Lawmakers who think they can solve the dispute over reducing debt by asking economists are out of luck. Economists can’t agree, either.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The politicians grappling over how to pay the nation’s debts have been contributing to the heat of summer with back-and-forth charges that their opponents are disregarding the laws of economics.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/us/econ-1/econ-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/us/econ-1/econ-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/18/us/econ-1/econ-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Politicians Can’t Agree on Debt? Well, Neither Can Economists', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Politicians Can’t Agree on Debt? Well, Neither Can Economists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T03:31:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4caf156-d661-5f6d-810f-a128abaff18d', 'word_count': 1107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4caf156-d661-5f6d-810f-a128abaff18d'}, {'abstract': 'A quick, informal selection of voices from across the country this weekend found cynicism about the state of negotiations in Washington and resignation about the partisan jousting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/us/18voices.html', 'snippet': 'A quick, informal selection of voices from across the country this weekend found cynicism about the state of negotiations in Washington and resignation about the partisan jousting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — On Friday morning, President Obama insisted that he completely understood how the American people — a phrase he mentioned more than two dozen times — felt about the slow pace of negotiations over the debt ceiling. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/18/us/voices/voices-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/18/us/voices/voices-articleLarg