ple in the investigation into phone-hacking and the relationship between the London police and newspapers owned by News International.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/18/world/europe/statements-by-top-figures-in-the-hacking-scandal.html', 'snippet': 'Statements made by some of the key people in the investigation into phone-hacking and the relationship between the London police and newspapers owned by News International.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Statements made by some of the key people in the investigation into phone-hacking and the relationship between the London police and newspapers owned by News International.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Statements by Top Figures in the Hacking Scandal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T21:06:32+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/46321bbb-70b6-5ae3-8350-b191c6bf7db3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/46321bbb-70b6-5ae3-8350-b191c6bf7db3'}, {'abstract': 'The medical branch of the National Academy of Sciences estimates that 116 million Americans suffer from chronic pain, which can alter the nervous system over time.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/giving-chronic-pain-a-medical-platform-of-its-own/', 'snippet': 'The medical branch of the National Academy of Sciences estimates that 116 million Americans suffer from chronic pain, which can alter the nervous system over time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most doctors view pain as a symptom of an underlying problem — treat the disease or the injury, and the pain goes away.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Giving Chronic Pain a Medical Platform of Its Own', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T21:10:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19985004-d219-55c5-8e04-be53499e742b', 'word_count': 830, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19985004-d219-55c5-8e04-be53499e742b'}, {'abstract': 'In a study, pediatric cardiologists reading electrocardiograms correctly identified only 68 percent of truly abnormal cases and did not always offer the correct recommendation on whether a patient should participate in sports.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/health/research/19exercise.html', 'snippet': 'In a study, pediatric cardiologists reading electrocardiograms correctly identified only 68 percent of truly abnormal cases and did not always offer the correct recommendation on whether a patient should participate in sports.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Screening young athletes with electrocardiograms to prevent sudden cardiac death may be ineffective, a new study has found. As it turns out, even pediatric cardiologists often fail to interpret the tests correctly.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exercise: Limits in Heart Screenings for Youths', 'kicker': 'Vital Signs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exercise: Limits in Heart Screenings for Youths', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Heart', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T21:12:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7df21baf-59bf-506c-ad4b-28ee7e1fafb7', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7df21baf-59bf-506c-ad4b-28ee7e1fafb7'}, {'abstract': 'It may take years to act on recommendations for shoring up safety at American nuclear plants, but the N.R.C. hopes to give strong guidance on necessary steps within three months.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/n-r-c-chief-plans-quick-response-to-post-fukushima-study/', 'snippet': 'It may take years to act on recommendations for shoring up safety at American nuclear plants, but the N.R.C. hopes to give strong guidance on necessary steps within three months.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The chairman of the Nuclear Regulatory Commission said Monday that it should decide within 90 days on how to address recommendations to be issued this week by a task force that examined the lessons of the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear accident in Japan. Almost simultaneously, House Republicans and the industry’s trade association warned him not to rush.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.R.C. Chief Plans Quick Response to Post-Fukushima Study', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jaczko, Gregory B', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Press Club', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nuclear Regulatory Commission', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T21:14:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97e75479-0e43-5b9b-bdbf-4f5e03fba1b1', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97e75479-0e43-5b9b-bdbf-4f5e03fba1b1'}, {'abstract': 'A 17-year-old from Fort Worth won the $50,000 grand prize at Google’s science fair last week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/science/19google.html', 'snippet': 'A 17-year-old from Fort Worth won the $50,000 grand prize at Google’s science fair last week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a budding inventor and scientist, Shree Bose, in second grade, tried to make blue spinach. In fourth grade she built a remote-controlled garbage can. In eighth grade she invented a railroad tie made out of recycled plastic and granite dust, an achievement that got her to the top 30 in a national science competition for middle school students.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3