night, John Yates announced his resignation in a recorded statement in which he protested his complete innocence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated | 6:47 p.m. As my colleagues Alan Cowell and Sarah Lyall report, John Yates, the assistant commissioner of London’s Metropolitan Police Service and the country’s most senior counterterrorism officer, resigned his post on Monday over his role in the botched investigation of phone hacking at The News of the World.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Yates of the Yard’ Explains His Resignation', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallis, Neil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yates, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scotland Yard (Metropolitan Police Service)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T21:19:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/310622e0-ec49-57a4-b826-79ef3744fbdb', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/310622e0-ec49-57a4-b826-79ef3744fbdb'}, {'abstract': 'I was only peripherally aware of Miranda July before Katrina Onstad wrote about her for this Sunday’s magazine. Netflix occasionally suggests I stream her 2005 film “Me and You and Everyone We Know,” probably because I have viewed other so-called indie films.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/you-say-pretentious-as-if-its-a-bad-thing/', 'snippet': 'I was only peripherally aware of Miranda July before Katrina Onstad wrote about her for this Sunday’s magazine. Netflix occasionally suggests I stream her 2005 film “Me and You and Everyone We Know,” probably because I have viewed other so-called ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was only peripherally aware of Miranda July before Katrina Onstad wrote about her for this Sunday’s magazine. Netflix occasionally suggests I stream her 2005 film “Me and You and Everyone We Know,” probably because I have viewed other so-called indie films. (It’s “quirky” — like “Clockwork Orange” and “Withnail and I”?) So I started to watch it. The movie begins with July doing a kind of spoken-word piece, and I knew I had heard her voice before. After a few maddening minutes, it hit me: She’s the one who did “The Arky Girl.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'You Say ‘Pretentious’ as if It’s a Bad Thing', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T21:22:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Ferguson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ferguson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0261eba-f735-56b6-99c8-6c8dfbc455c9', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0261eba-f735-56b6-99c8-6c8dfbc455c9'}, {'abstract': '“Awkward,” a new comedy about suburban high school life, beginning on Tuesday on MTV, is both homage and rebuke to the teen genre.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/arts/television/awkward-on-mtv-starring-ashley-rickards-review.html', 'snippet': '“Awkward,” a new comedy about suburban high school life, beginning on Tuesday on MTV, is both homage and rebuke to the teen genre.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Awkward,” a new comedy about suburban high school life, beginning on Tuesday on MTV, is both homage and rebuke: a tribute to the exquisite mix of satire and sincerity that distinguished “Freaks and Geeks,” and a takedown of the cloying sanctimony you witness after only a few minutes of something like “The Secret Life of the American Teenager.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/19/arts/AWKWARD/AWKWARD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/19/arts/AWKWARD/AWKWARD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/19/arts/AWKWARD/AWKWARD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/19/arts/AWKWARD/AWKWARD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/19/arts/AWKWARD/AWKWARD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Teenager’s High-Five Is Plastered in Place', 'kicker': "Television Review | 'Awkward'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Teenager’s High-Five Is Plastered In Place', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MTV Networks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T21:29:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/201c6a8f-7b93-572b-8e80-2491af2a6fcc', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/201c6a8f-7b93-572b-8e80-2491af2a6fcc'}, {'abstract': 'The big draw at a U Street wine bar is a bocce ball lane made of tile and crushed oyster shells.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/bocce-and-barolo-vinoteca-washington-d-c/', 'snippet': 'The big draw at a U Street wine bar is a bocce ball lane made of tile and crushed oyster shells.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At Vinoteca, a wine bar in the U Street corridor of Washington, D.C.’s, the District’s popular bocce ball scene meets open-air snacking. Vinoteca itself is a perfectly fine wine bar, if a bit hushed and dimly lit, but the real action is happening around back: a clustered courtside next to a mock-rustic bocce ball lane made of tile and crushed oyster shells. Bocce — a game that can be described as shuffleboard with balls, or curling sans ice — is enjoying something of a youthful renaissance these days in Washington, spearheaded by the DC Bocce League, an organization with teams across the D.C. area and a membership roll that includes staffers, interns, pollsters, wonks and all manner of Washingtonian young professionals. They play mostly in outdoor parks and on lawns, but these sites have nothing on the backyard at Vinoteca, which could be a miniaturized village square in Torre del Greco, were it not for the condo developments towering above or the Rite Aid across the street. Such open-air surroundings are best accompanied by offerings from the outdoor grill: fresh barbecued monkfish in romesco, warmed olives and almonds, a bluefish tuna sandwich on rustic bread and assorted Mediterranean sausages. Perhaps best of all is the playful by-the-glass wine list, including a dry assyrtiko from Santorini, Greece, and a viognier from nearby Rappahannock Cellars, in Virginia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Washington, Bocce and Barolo', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T21:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jordan Michelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jordan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Michelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcf53952-766c-5d9f-b365-c7174ea4e71c', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcf53952-766c-5d9f-b365-c7174ea4e71c'}, {'abstract': 'A plethora of moths might have to do with what you are storing and how.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/science/19qna.html', 'snippet': 'A plethora of moths might have to do with what you are storing and how.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. Why are there more moths than ever before in my Manhattan apartment this season?', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/19/science/19qa/19QA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1008, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/19/science/19qa/19QA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/19/science/19qa/19QA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbna