, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad1f8481-b5fa-5864-8b5e-5d01dcd5bcfc', 'word_count': 468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad1f8481-b5fa-5864-8b5e-5d01dcd5bcfc'}, {'abstract': 'The state withdrew a charge of criminal abuse of office on Monday against Energy Minister Elton Mangoma, a leader of the Movement for Democratic Change.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/world/africa/19briefs-Zimbabwe.html', 'snippet': 'The state withdrew a charge of criminal abuse of office on Monday against Energy Minister Elton Mangoma, a leader of the Movement for Democratic Change.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The state withdrew a charge of criminal abuse of office on Monday against Energy Minister Elton Mangoma, a leader of the Movement for Democratic Change that has opposed President Robert Mugabe. Mr. Mangoma had been scheduled to go on trial on Monday. The state also further downgraded charges against Munyaradzi Gwisai and five other activists who had been accused in February of plotting to topple Mr. Mugabe after watching videos of the uprising in Egypt. The original treason charge has been replaced by a charge of conspiring to commit public violence and participating in a gathering to promote violence. They are scheduled for trial on Aug. 22. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zimbabwe: Charges Are Dropped Against Energy Minister', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | AFRICA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Zimbabwe: Charges Are Dropped Against Energy Minister', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mangoma, Elton', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Movement for Democratic Change', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Zimbabwe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T23:21:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Celia W. Dugger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Celia', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dugger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/89940072-6a00-56aa-aac1-a9abb8c22ff0', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89940072-6a00-56aa-aac1-a9abb8c22ff0'}, {'abstract': 'The Vatican and the predominantly Muslim nation of Malaysia established diplomatic relations on Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/world/europe/19briefs-Vatican.html', 'snippet': 'The Vatican and the predominantly Muslim nation of Malaysia established diplomatic relations on Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Vatican and the predominantly Muslim nation of Malaysia established diplomatic relations on Monday, a sign that the Southeast Asian nation’s government was opening to religious minorities after interfaith tensions had shaken the country’s Catholic community. The Holy See announced the agreement after Pope Benedict XVI and high-ranking Vatican officials met with Prime Minister Najib Razak of Malaysia at Castel Gandolfo, the pope’s summer residence outside Rome. In recent years, tensions have been high between Catholics, who make up about 3 percent of Malaysia’s population, and the Malaysian government. Catholics have complained of vandalism against churches and the government’s confiscation of Malay-language Bibles because of a ban on the use of “Allah” as a Malay-language translation for God.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vatican and Malaysia Normalize Ties', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vatican and Malaysia Normalize Ties', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benedict XVI', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Malaysia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Najib Razak', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T23:32:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gaia Pianigiani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gaia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pianigiani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e25c43da-46bc-5eee-b596-dde908b13e56', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e25c43da-46bc-5eee-b596-dde908b13e56'}, {'abstract': 'If the right-hander’s struggles of his past two starts continue, the Yankees may be forced to start shopping for a replacement in the rotation.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/18/yankees-waiting-to-see-how-much-colon-has-left/', 'snippet': 'If the right-hander’s struggles of his past two starts continue, the Yankees may be forced to start shopping for a replacement in the rotation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. PETERSBURG, Fla. — In another two starts, Bartolo Colon should surpass the 100-inning mark, which he has not done since 2005. The Yankees were concerned about his durability heading into the season, and his struggles over his last two outings — 13 runs and 16 hits in six and a third innings — raised the possibility that he is nearing his expiration date. That issue has come into focus with the July 31 nonwaiver trade deadline less than two weeks away.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yankees Waiting to See How Much Colon Has Left', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colon, Bartolo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Girardi, Joe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pena, Ramiro', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T23:44:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d8942f0-5081-563b-bab2-c1ef30326ee0', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d8942f0-5081-563b-bab2-c1ef30326ee0'}, {'abstract': 'The top judicial body of the United Nations ordered Cambodia and Thailand to withdraw troops from a disputed temple and established a demilitarized zone along their border.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/world/asia/19briefs-Thailand.html', 'snippet': 'The top judicial body of the United Nations ordered Cambodia and Thailand to withdraw troops from a disputed temple and established a demilitarized zone along their border.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The top judicial body of the United Nations ordered Cambodia and Thailand to withdraw troops from a disputed temple on Monday and established a demilitarized zone along their mountainous border. The two countries have fought each other numerous times in recent years near Preah Vihear, an ancient hilltop temple that stirs nationalist sentiments in both countries and where Cambodian troops are seen in the photo above. Both sides said they would comply with the order by the International Court of Justice in The Hague. However, the Thai military has in the past criticized concessions on the temple by the country’s civilian leadership and may balk at what they perceive as giving further ground to Cambodia. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Court Orders Thailand and Cambodia to Withdraw from Disputed Temple', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | ASIA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Court Orders Thailand and CambodiaTo Withdraw From Disputed Temple', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Thailand', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cambodia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T23:46:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Fuller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cef18c47-b056-51de-8e12-d4f1a560fa00', 'word_count': 117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cef18c47-b056-51de-8e12-d4f1a560fa00'}, {'abstract': 'Russian authorities have arrested four people suspected of planning a “major” terrorist attack in the Moscow region, the head of Russia’s domestic intelligence agency said Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/world/europe/19briefs-Russia.html', 'snippet': 'Russian authorities have arrested four people suspected of planning a “major” terrorist attack in the Moscow region, the head of Russia’s domestic intelligence agency said Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Russian authorities have arrested four people suspected of planning a “major” terrorist attack in the Moscow region, Aleksandr Bortnikov, the head of Russia’s domestic intelligence agency, said Monday. The suspects, who were arrested several days ago, were from Russia’s North Caucasus, a violence-plagued, mostly Muslim region that is a hotbed of terrorist activity in Russia. Mr. Bortnikov said the suspects had planned to bomb transportation infrastructure and other crowded places, but did not provide further details. The police confiscated a homemade bomb with an estimated explosive force of 22 pounds of TNT, as well as other weapons and maps. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Four Arrested for Plotting Attack', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: 4 Arrested for Plotting Attack', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Caucasus (Russia)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T23:48:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Schwirtz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwirtz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d589dca4-2c61-5364-bb05-ad0c58a581fa', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d589dca4-2c61-5364-bb05-ad0c58a581fa'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to an article on a push for federal standards on hen cages.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/opinion/lweb19egg.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to an article on a push for federal standards on hen cages.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Rules for Hens', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Rules for Hens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eggs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cruelty to Animals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Standards and Standardization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poultry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T23:51:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8e05878-5a99-52cd-bee0-c579e51182cf', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8e05878-5a99-52cd-bee0-c579e51182cf'}, {'abstract': 'The District of Columbia public schools has sent termination notices to 413 teachers and other school employees under an evaluation system that has become a national model.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/education/19brfs-SCHOOLSDROPP_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The District of Columbia public schools has sent termination notices to 413 teachers and other school employees under an evaluation system that has become a national model.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The District of Columbia public schools has sent termination notices to 413 teachers and other school employees, including 288 for poor performance, under an evaluation system that has become a national model. District officials said that 113 of the employees had been rated ineffective, and that an additional 175 had been rated minimally effective for a second consecutive year in a system that emphasizes students’ performance on standardized tests as well as on classroom observations. In addition, 104 employees will be let go because they failed to meet state license requirements, and 21 others because they were unable to find permanent positions a year after losing their previous jobs as a result of budget cuts and program changes. Supporters of the evaluation system have praised it as setting clear standards and enforcing consequences for ineffective teaching. But it is disliked by some unionized teachers and has been criticized as doing little to help struggling teachers and not factoring in the challenges faced by those working in poor schools. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Schools Dropping 413 From Staff', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Schools Dropping 413 From Staff', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T23:51:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Winnie Hu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fecb7558-dc6c-58ea-b24f-7c083042e9e0', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fecb7558-dc6c-58ea-b24f-7c083042e9e0'}, {'abstract': 'The Fish and Wildlife Service said Monday that the whitebark pine, devastated by bark beetles and fungus, deserved to be listed as threatened or endangered under the Endangered Species Act.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/science/earth/19brfs-NOPROTECTION_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The Fish and Wildlife Service said Monday that the whitebark pine, devastated by bark beetles and fungus, deserved to be listed as threatened or endangered under the Endangered Species Act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Fish and Wildlife Service said Monday that the whitebark pine, devastated by bark beetles and fungus, deserved to be listed as threatened or endangered under the Endangered Species Act. But it said it could not spare money and staff time to do so and to give the trees the protections that come from a listing. The agency’s finding noted that scientific studies showed “a substantial and pervasive decline throughout almost the entire range of the species” in the West. The decline has been linked to global warming because the pine beetle is increasingly able to survive warmer winters and cause more damage when spring arrives. In the hardest-hit areas, Yellowstone and Glacier National Parks, about two-thirds of whitebark pines are gone. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Protection for Pines in Peril', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Protection for Pines in Peril', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fish and Wildlife Service', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-18T23:53:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2464ee7c-e8fa-55d3-a85f-28dbadf1fcad', 'word_count': 123, 'uri': '