 not just Mr. Murdoch’s News Corporation, but also Britain’s political and law-enforcement elite. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/20/world/subHACKING/subHACKING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/20/world/subHACKING/subHACKING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/20/world/subHACKING/subHACKING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/20/world/subHACKING/subHACKING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/20/world/subHACKING/subHACKING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Murdochs Deny That They Knew of Illegal Acts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Murdochs Deny That They Knew Of Illegal Acts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scotland Yard (Metropolitan Police Service)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cameron, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yates, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Rebekah', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, James R', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T08:47:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Lyall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2f8354e-0c2a-54ad-a3cc-f5729328d24f', 'word_count': 1421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2f8354e-0c2a-54ad-a3cc-f5729328d24f'}, {'abstract': 'James Hill sees the future(s) at a photo festival in Arles, France.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/artist-journalist-vestige/', 'snippet': 'James Hill sees the future(s) at a photo festival in Arles, France.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photography has entered such an extraordinary state of transformation that labels no longer serve a purpose. Photojournalist? Photographer? Artist? Today, it seems that we need to be all of the above if we’re to continue working in this sea of photography.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artist? Journalist? Vestige?', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARLES (FRANCE)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T09:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Hill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51e25394-51f6-54d8-8ad6-7dd29eeed5fb', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51e25394-51f6-54d8-8ad6-7dd29eeed5fb'}, {'abstract': 'Which is the more insidious problem in soccer: corruption among the powerful men that rule the game, or match-fixing by the players and referees at the bottom of it?', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/tilting-the-soccer-field-in-their-favor/', 'snippet': 'Which is the more insidious problem in soccer: corruption among the powerful men that rule the game, or match-fixing by the players and referees at the bottom of it?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Which is the more insidious problem in soccer: corruption and entitlement among the powerful men who rule the game from FIFA headquarters, or match-fixing by the players and referees at the bottom of it? Doreen Carvajal looked into the former in Monday’s Times, and Jeff Z. Klein tackled the latter — a growing, worldwide scourge — in Tuesday’s editions. Read both articles, then come back to comment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tilting the Soccer Field in Their Favor', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T09:09:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d1be380-4716-532b-b69a-525f4ddda959', 'word_count': 67, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d1be380-4716-532b-b69a-525f4ddda959'}, {'abstract': 'A debt default would endanger national security and justifies the presidential exercise of extraordinary powers, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/the-debt-limit-and-national-security/', 'snippet': 'A debt default would endanger national security and justifies the presidential exercise of extraordinary powers, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bruce Bartlett held senior policy roles in the Reagan and George H.W. Bush administrations and served on the staffs of Representatives Jack Kemp and Ron Paul.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Debt Limit and National Security', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Bartlett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bartlett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/089845fc-a1e5-5e0b-b6f1-a6d93d40fa26', 'word_count': 919, 'uri': 'nyt://article/089845fc-a1e5-5e0b-b6f1-a6d93d40fa26'}, {'abstract': 'Through Oct. 23, the Rijksmuseum in Amsterdam is presenting “Two Young Artists,” featuring more than 20 self-portraits of Rembrandt and Degas as they were beginning their early careers.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/rembrandt-and-degas-an-odd-pairing-in-amsterdam/', 'snippet': 'Through Oct. 23, the Rijksmuseum in Amsterdam is presenting “Two Young Artists,” featuring more than 20 self-portraits of Rembrandt and Degas as they were beginning their early careers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rembrandt van Rijn and Edgar Degas don’t make for the most obvious pairing in art: the 17th-century Dutch painter is more associated with dark, somber portraits and landscapes, while the French Impressionist is best-known for his romantic pastel-hued scenes from ballet. But the Rijksmuseum (Jan Luijkenstraat 1; www.rijksmuseum.nl) is ingeniously devoting one show each year to the influence of great Dutch painters on those of other periods and nationalities, providing insight into relatively unexplored links between famous artists. Last year, Jan Steen was paired with Joan Miró. This year, through Oct. 23, the museum presents “Two Young Artists,” featuring more than 20 self-portraits of Rembrandt and Degas as they were beginning their early careers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rembrandt and Degas: An Odd Pairing in Amsterdam', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Degas, Edgar', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rembrandt Harmenszoon van Rijn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rijksmuseum', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMSTERDAM (NETHERLANDS)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T10:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bbe273a-ef05-5cae-82bb-f96ff3ffc990', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bbe273a-ef05-5cae-82bb-f96ff3ffc990'}, {'abstract': 'Where did you go wrong? Here’s a list.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/fantasy-focu