with-mitch-winehouse/', 'snippet': 'Mitch Winehouse, the father of the British soul singer Amy Winehouse, answers some questions about his new jazz album, “Rush of Love to the Heart.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitch Winehouse, a former salesman and London cabby, is quick to say that he knows he is only getting a shot at being a professional jazz singer at the age of 60 because his daughter is Amy Winehouse, the British soul singer and songwriter whose struggles with addiction have been fodder for the tabloids. But he also makes it plain that he thinks he can sing, and says that he will dispel doubts about his talent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Word With Mitch Winehouse', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T12:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a2add95-8ba8-5c7d-8974-f7b614730ca4', 'word_count': 1169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a2add95-8ba8-5c7d-8974-f7b614730ca4'}, {'abstract': 'Earnings benefited from the reversal of about $1 billion that the nation’s biggest consumer bank had previously set aside to cover loan losses.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/wells-fargo-second-quarter-results/', 'snippet': 'Earnings benefited from the reversal of about $1 billion that the nation’s biggest consumer bank had previously set aside to cover loan losses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wells Fargo, the nation’s biggest consumer bank, said profit rose 29 percent in the second quarter from the period a year earlier as loan losses eased significantly.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wells Fargo Exceeds Expectations With $3.9 Billion Profit, Up 29%', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wells Fargo & Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T12:19:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Dash', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dash', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d14cd416-08fc-5ea3-869a-14b162b29d0d', 'word_count': 768, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d14cd416-08fc-5ea3-869a-14b162b29d0d'}, {'abstract': 'Entergy must decide whether to spend millions on refueling a nuclear plant while it battles a state effort to shut it down.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/a-gamble-for-vermont-yankee/', 'snippet': 'Entergy must decide whether to spend millions on refueling a nuclear plant while it battles a state effort to shut it down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The owners of the Vermont Yankee nuclear plant may be coming up fast on a crucial decision about the reactor’s future. The plant’s initial 40-year operating license expires in March 2012; the Nuclear Regulatory Commission is willing to let it run another 20 years, but the state of Vermont is not. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Gamble for Vermont Yankee', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Entergy Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T12:24:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name'