', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rigali, Justin F', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philadelphia (Pa)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chaput, Charles J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T12:40:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce60255b-bacb-5c49-815a-4b842001c92c', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce60255b-bacb-5c49-815a-4b842001c92c'}, {'abstract': 'Calorie-counting means your weight-loss plan may be outdated, antismoking vaccine fails in clinical trial, reviving the trusted traveler program for fliers and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/tuesday-reading-counting-calories-your-diet-may-need-updating/', 'snippet': 'Calorie-counting means your weight-loss plan may be outdated, antismoking vaccine fails in clinical trial, reviving the trusted traveler program for fliers and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday Reading: Counting Calories? Your Diet May Need Updating', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T12:45:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81081268-990e-5121-97ce-49ee749ccaef', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81081268-990e-5121-97ce-49ee749ccaef'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Surveillance cameras can be effective in helping to hunt down crime suspects, but whether they deter crime remains open to debate. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/the-cameras-watch-but-do-they-give-criminals-pause/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Surveillance cameras can be effective in helping to hunt down crime suspects, but whether they deter crime remains open to debate. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Videos from surveillance cameras were important tools in capturing the man accused of abducting and murdering 8-year-old Leiby Kletzky in Brooklyn last week. They caught the boy at several points along his fatal walk in Borough Park, including the moment when he got into a car and was driven away. In short order, they led investigators to their suspect, Levi Aron. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Cameras Watch, but Do They Give Criminals Pause?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T12:50:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46a41b48-068a-5d6e-a3d8-ba750b69cbb9', 'word_count': 1102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46a41b48-068a-5d6e-a3d8-ba750b69cbb9'}, {'abstract': 'Scribd is starting a digital news stand with articles from People, Fortune, Salon and Time magazine.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/scribd-hope-paid-news-site-will-float/', 'snippet': 'Scribd is starting a digital news stand with articles from People, Fortune, Salon and Time magazine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Relatively few people are willing to pay for an online newspaper or magazine. But what if they could pay a monthly fee for a vast library of news, like Netflix’s subscription service for movies?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scribd Creates a Paid News Site', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magazines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scribd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T13:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verne G. Kopytoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verne', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Kopytoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e6a4099-3e53-5b2e-a792-c8c3e59b1040', 'word_count': 533, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e6a4099-3e53-5b2e-a792-c8c3e59b1040'}, {'abstract': 'In a new video for “Don’t Play No Game That I Can’t Win,” that rapping trio is reimagined as a team of G. I. Joe action figures battling zombies in the snow.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/beastie-boys-fight-beastly-toys-in-new-video/', 'snippet': 'In a new video for “Don’t Play No Game That I Can’t Win,” that rapping trio is reimagined as a team of G. I. Joe action figures battling zombies in the snow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Beastie Boys Don’t Play No Game That I Can’t Win, featuring Santigold, from Beastie Boys', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beastie Boys Fight Beastly Toys in New Video', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T13:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f41a9667-a2f2-56e9-b32e-975f6d0ab105', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f41a9667-a2f2-56e9-b32e-975f6d0ab105'}, {'abstract': 'Builders broke ground at a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 629,000 units in June, up 14 percent from May, the Commerce Department reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/business/economy/housing-starts-lifted-by-apartments-in-june.html', 'snippet': 'Builders broke ground at a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 629,000 units in June, up 14 percent from May, the Commerce Department reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The pace of new construction of private homes in the United States last month was the highest since January, according to government figures released on Tuesday. Economists, however, warned that the sector had still not stabilized for a full recovery.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Housing Starts Are Hig