uer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0fcad5c-5998-581c-b5a8-758ce07fb451', 'word_count': 1218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0fcad5c-5998-581c-b5a8-758ce07fb451'}, {'abstract': 'Even as schools aim to better prepare students for a global work force, fewer than one in three American students are proficient in geography.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/education/20geography.html', 'snippet': 'Even as schools aim to better prepare students for a global work force, fewer than one in three American students are proficient in geography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even as schools aim to better prepare students for a global work force, fewer than one in three American students are proficient in geography, with most eighth graders unable to explain what causes earthquakes or accurately describe the American Southwest, according to a report released Tuesday morning. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Geography Report Card Finds Students Lagging', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Geography Report Card Finds Students Lagging', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Geography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Assessment of Educational Progress', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T16:59:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Winnie Hu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/313ed241-3295-57a4-a1e2-c567b0484eb3', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/313ed241-3295-57a4-a1e2-c567b0484eb3'}, {'abstract': 'The kitchen hummed as if we had never missed a meal and the front of the house, accustomed to openings, quickly found its comfort level. I did not.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/the-very-long-weekend/', 'snippet': 'The kitchen hummed as if we had never missed a meal and the front of the house, accustomed to openings, quickly found its comfort level. I did not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I apologize. This blog post is anything but timely. I admit to being laid low by recent events: a Memorial Day fire led to a nightmare June of inspector hunting and permit gathering, culminating in two days of actual repair — all of which brought us to our third opening in less than a calendar year. Just in time for the Fourth of July. And fireworks. Not the celebratory exploding rainbows of the Grucci Brothers, more like the gruesome tales of the Brothers Grimm where an evil queen casts evil spells while stirring a smoldering cauldron behind a castle parapet in some dark forest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Very Long Weekend', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Southampton Press', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bridgehampton (NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T17:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Buschel', 'person':