Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7599ca5-d04c-5baa-9c9e-713ad11d6738', 'word_count': 458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7599ca5-d04c-5baa-9c9e-713ad11d6738'}, {'abstract': 'The plug-in hybrids draw their charge from a solar array at the company’s facility in Plainville, Conn.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/at-g-e-plant-in-connecticut-volts-plug-in-to-the-sun/', 'snippet': 'The plug-in hybrids draw their charge from a solar array at the company’s facility in Plainville, Conn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PLAINVILLE, Conn. — On Monday morning, no fewer than five Chevrolet Volts were charging on 100 percent renewable energy from General Electric’s parking-lot Solar Carport. Opened in May, the 100-kilowatt solar installation, which also offers some shade and weather protection and has 10 connected G.E.-built chargers, is projected to last 25 years and provide 125 megawatt-hours of electricity annually, according to G.E.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At G.E. Plant in Connecticut, Volts Plug In to the Sun', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Solar Energy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chevrolet Division of General Motors Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T21:04:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Motavall