s a Change of Venue, From Cable to Satellite', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Legal Drama Has a Change of Venue, From Cable to Satellite', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Close, Glenn', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byrne, Rose', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kessler, Todd A (1972- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tomlin, Lily', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodman, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DirecTV', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kessler, Glenn (1970- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T21:58:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0b297a2-b3a0-5fec-be1a-f102c5a283ed', 'word_count': 1238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0b297a2-b3a0-5fec-be1a-f102c5a283ed'}, {'abstract': 'Cold-brewed teas and coffees tend to contain less caffeine and less acid than their hot counterparts. And, of course, they taste different.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/dining/cold-brewing-coffee-and-tea-the-curious-cook.html', 'snippet': 'Cold-brewed teas and coffees tend to contain less caffeine and less acid than their hot counterparts. And, of course, they taste different.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ICED coffees and teas should be some of summer’s simplest pleasures, especially when we just steep them in cold tap water, with no kitchen heat and next to no effort. But if you make even a desultory search for advice on cold brewing, you may find yourself mopping your brow deciding how, and even whether, to proceed. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/20/dining/20CURI3_span/20CURI3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/20/dining/20CURI3_span/20CURI3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/20/dining/20CURI3_span/20CURI3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 845, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/20/dining/20CURI3_span/20CURI3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/20/dining/20CURI3_span/20CURI3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Iced Coffee and Tea: (Not) Taking the Heat', 'kicker': 'The Curious Cook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iced Coffee and Tea: (Not) Taking the Heat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coffee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T22:03:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harold McGee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harold', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/897ea935-25f8-535d-80c4-e74b383f8165', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/897ea935-25f8-535d-80c4-e74b383f8165'}, {'abstract': 'Harold McGee, the author of several books on the science of food and cooking, will be answering readers’ questions.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/19/q-and-a-with-harold-mcgee-on-brewing-coffee-and-tea/', 'snippet': 'Harold McGee, the author of several books on the science of food and cooking, will be answering readers’ questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his Curious Cook column this week, Harold McGee, whose most recent book is “Keys to Good Cooking: A Guide to Making the Best of Foods and Recipes,” writes about making iced coffee and tea. On Diner’s Journal he’ll be answering readers’ questions about brewing coffee and tea, hot or cold. Submit your question as a comment below and he’ll respond later this week. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A. With Harold McGee on Brewing Coffee and Tea', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coffee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-19T22:04:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/838dd159-e13b-53ee-afd9-2fce5d2a0aeb', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/838dd159-e13b-53ee-afd9-2fce5d2a0aeb'}, {'abstract': 'Tart-sweet and 33 percent alcohol, Averall is an updated take on old-fashioned sloe gin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/dining/averell-a-gin-made-from-upstate-plums.html', 'snippet': 'Tart-sweet and 33 percent alcohol, Averall is an updated take on old-fashioned sloe gin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Damson plum jam is utterly English. But take those dark little plums, grown by Red Jacket Orchards in Geneva, N.Y., extract the juice and mix it with small-batch gin from upstate and you have a thoroughly New York drink. It’s old-fashioned sloe gin, updated (sloes are a small plumlike fruit). Tart-sweet and 33 percent alcohol, it’s labeled liqueur by Scott Krahn, who makes it in the Finger Lakes region to add to the line of products made by his company, the American Gin Company based in Manhattan. It does wonders for iced black tea on a late summer afternoon. Use it in sparkling wine to give a new twist to kir royale. Or mix it 50-50 with plain gin, a splash of orange juice and a few drops of orange bitters over ice, and call it whatever you want. The liqueur’s name, Averell, nails its Empire State pedigree as it refers to a former governor, a Harriman, from a line of railroad moguls.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/20/dining/20STUF-DRINKS/20STUF-DRINKS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/20/dining/20STUF-DRINKS/20STUF-DRINKS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/20/dining/20STUF-DRINKS/20STUF-DRINKS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Damson Plums Flavor Finger Lakes Gin', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pedigreed Plums Flavor Finger Lakes Liqueur', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gin (Liquor)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}]