nal': 'By Mark Viera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c67f3983-f2d1-5523-9314-fd5a85c13ce0', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c67f3983-f2d1-5523-9314-fd5a85c13ce0'}, {'abstract': 'The British phone-hacking affair that has enveloped the News Corporation has emboldened American advocacy groups who believe that media companies have become too big and powerful.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/business/media/murdoch-scandal-stirs-us-debate-on-big-media.html', 'snippet': 'The British phone-hacking affair that has enveloped the News Corporation has emboldened American advocacy groups who believe that media companies have become too big and powerful.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Progressive activists and public interest groups have long blasted Rupert Murdoch and his News Corporation for political biases. But in recent weeks they have seized on a new and more tangible reason to call for the revocation of his TV licenses and the breakup of his company: the British hacking scandal. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/20/business/Media1/Media1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scandal Stirs U.S. Debate on Big Media', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scandal Stirs U.S. Debate On Big Media', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-0