e Brink: European leaders have to make hard choices, right now, about Greece’s crushing debt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Merkel, Angela', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sarkozy, Nicolas', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T02:58:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c5807d1-7e0d-51a1-b656-309349c4afc0', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c5807d1-7e0d-51a1-b656-309349c4afc0'}, {'abstract': 'A new bill in Congress would allow taking money from schools that need it most.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/opinion/20wed3.html', 'snippet': 'A new bill in Congress would allow taking money from schools that need it most.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Extremists in Congress have long wanted to gut the spending restrictions in Title I, a federal law dating back to the 1960s that underwrites extra help for disadvantaged schoolchildren. A bill, approved by a House committee last week, would do just that, damaging one of most important civil rights programs in the country.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hurting Poor Students', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hurting Poor Students', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Aid (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brown v Board of Education (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T02:59:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/883739b0-2a7f-5bdd-9b48-d79c13991153', 'word_count': 297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/883739b0-2a7f-5bdd-9b48-d79c13991153'}, {'abstract': 'Governor Cuomo should sign a bill that would serve taxi passengers in the outer boroughs, reduce illegal pickups and generate more money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/opinion/20wed4.html', 'snippet': 'Governor Cuomo should sign a bill that would serve taxi passengers in the outer boroughs, reduce illegal pickups and generate more money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Manhattan looks like a sea of taxis, but it’s a different picture in many other parts of the city. A city study of GPS data shows that 97.5 percent of the more than 13,230 yellow cabs pick up their fares in Manhattan or at the two city airports. That leaves about 80 percent of the city’s population underserved.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taxis Beyond Manhattan', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taxis Beyond Manhattan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {