ivian Bearing in the first Broadway production of  “Wit.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Take note, “Sex and the City” fans: not one but two of its leading ladies will be performing on Broadway in the 2011-12 season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cynthia Nixon to Star in ‘Wit’ on Broadway', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cattrall, Kim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nixon, Cynthia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manhattan Theater Club', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T16:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2b5606f-af8a-555f-8fc6-8ab7b48e931d', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2b5606f-af8a-555f-8fc6-8ab7b48e931d'}, {'abstract': 'A Williamsburg restaurant whose owners were inspired by the Catskills lake they summered at as children.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/now-dining-masten-lake-in-williamsburg/', 'snippet': 'A Williamsburg restaurant whose owners were inspired by the Catskills lake they summered at as children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The owners of Masten Lake, which opens its doors Wednesday, had their hearts set on creating a special, intimate space inspired by the Catskills lake where they spent the summers at as children. The cork ceilings, stripped white benches and ceiling-to-floor glass front conjure the feel of a lakeside bungalow, where people cheerfully gather over conversation, local food and drink.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Dining | Masten Lake in Williamsburg', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T16:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Chang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba2a5d5a-d6e5-5d41-a804-e30633138864', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba2a5d5a-d6e5-5d41-a804-e30633138864'}, {'abstract': 'The sanctioning body recently tested new fuel-injected engines, which it mandated for the 2012 Sprint Cup season and beyond.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/waving-goodbye-to-carburetors-nascar-prepares-switch-to-fuel-injection/', 'snippet': 'The sanctioning body recently tested new fuel-injected engines, which it mandated for the 2012 Sprint Cup season and beyond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nascar racing engines have been fueled by carburetors since the sanctioning body’s first race on Daytona Beach in 1948, but for the 2012 season, carburetors will be abandoned in Sprint Cup, Nascar’s premier race series, in favor of electronic fuel injection. The sanctioning body announced the new specification in February and completed its first on-track tests of fuel-injection systems this month at Kentucky Speedway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Waving Goodbye to Carburetors, Nascar Prepares Switch to Fuel Injection', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Engines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nascar Sprint Cup Series', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Busch, Kyle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn of Stock Car Auto Racing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T16:03:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0753f36c-2d85-542a-83f2-fab5083c0333', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0753f36c-2d85-542a-83f2-fab5083c0333'}, {'abstract': 'Supply, demand, and Rick Perry.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/more-on-the-texas-story/', 'snippet': 'Supply, demand, and Rick Perry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On further reflection, I think it’s possible to make the points I was trying to make in my post on Texas more clearly using – wait for it – supply and demand.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More on the Texas Story', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T16:03:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40b1ff50-27bf-53bd-b2e6-2f1b4348d614', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40b1ff50-27bf-53bd-b2e6-2f1b4348d614'}, {'abstract': 'A new novel takes its inspiration from a story in the New York Times Magazine, and raises questions of where to draw the line with reproductive technology.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/a-baby-with-three-mothers/', 'snippet': 'A new novel takes its inspiration from a story in the New York Times Magazine, and raises questions of where to draw the line with reproductive technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly three years ago, the best-selling novelist Jennifer Weiner read an article in The New York Times Magazine by Alex Kuczynski, about hiring a gestational surrogate to carry her baby. I’m guessing that many of you remember the article, titled “Her Body, My Baby,” because it made so many readers furious. (You were also none too pleased with a similar piece two years later, by Melanie Thernstrom, about hiring two surrogates at one time.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Baby With Three Mothers', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infertility', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surrogate Motherhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T16:05:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf9bd12d-f17f-59b0-9907-af62d892bdb6', 'word_count': 1166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf9bd12d-f17f-59b0-9907-af62d892bdb6'}, {'abstract': 'A year after the Dodd-Frank Act was signed into law, Barney Frank has positioned himself as its chief defende