lename': None, 'lastname': 'Meadows', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff8a2e5b-a7d6-52da-b442-15d85f21659a', 'word_count': 1027, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff8a2e5b-a7d6-52da-b442-15d85f21659a'}, {'abstract': 'Whence the sudden optimism about the Gang of Six’s proposal?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/from-the-grand-bargain-to-the-gang-of-six/', 'snippet': 'Whence the sudden optimism about the Gang of Six’s proposal?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like Jon Chait and Matt Yglesias, I’m a little puzzled by the sudden wave of optimism about a potential deal based on the Gang of Six’s just-released bipartisan deficit reduction plan. So far as I can tell, the Gang of Six proposal bears a broad resemblance to the “grand bargain” that President Obama supposedly offered to John Boehner, except that the Gang’s tax reform proposals are more detailed (and raise more revenue) and its proposals for Social Security and Medicare cuts are less specific (there’s a lot of “requiring” Senate committees to come up with budget-cutting reforms). Depending on what came out of the entitlement reform process, I suppose that the Gang’s proposal could be a better deal for conservatives than what Obama offered Boehner. But more likely it would turn out to be a somewhat worse deal, with higher taxes and fewer real entitlement cuts, than the bargain House Republicans already inclined against when their own leader seemed to be considering it. (I recommend reading James Capretta for the sharpest conservative case against the Gang’s proposals, and Reihan Salam for some important qualifiers.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From the Grand Bargain to the Gang of Six', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T2