'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rajaratnam, Raj', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T23:08:06+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'Mac William Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mac', 'middlename': 'William', 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/d0ab7a6c-ee8f-5218-bcc6-c96f24ff3cd6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/d0ab7a6c-ee8f-5218-bcc6-c96f24ff3cd6'}, {'abstract': 'A woman in Berlin falls in love with an apartment once lived in by the chief architect for East Berlin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/greathomesanddestinations/in-germany-minimalism-gives-way-to-transparency-on-location.html', 'snippet': 'A woman in Berlin falls in love with an apartment once lived in by the chief architect for East Berlin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — Long before Eva-Maria Steidel bought the stately apartment that once belonged to the chief architect for East Berlin, Hermann Henselmann, she had fallen in love with the grand boulevard that he helped mastermind. The broad street, Karl-Marx-Allee, which her apartment overlooks, was a kind of East Berlin Champs-Élysées lined with monumental eight-story buildings done in a muscular style of Stalinist architecture known as Socialist Realism. The boulevard is where East Germany’s top-ranking communist officials lived in generous apartments in wedding-cake buildings. After the Berlin Wall fell in 1989, she was determined to live there.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/20/greathomesanddestinations/20gh-locationspan/20gh-locationspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType'