 cash to expand its human resource offerings in the United States.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Randstad to Buy SFN for $710 Million', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T23:55:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c272787-b3be-5fc9-bac2-d707b1b04e98', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c272787-b3be-5fc9-bac2-d707b1b04e98'}, {'abstract': 'On this week’s podcast, Claire Cain Miller on tension over Twitter apps, safeguarding voicemail and Intel’s 320 Series solid-state drive.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/tech-talk-podcast-twitters-developers/', 'snippet': 'On this week’s podcast, Claire Cain Miller on tension over Twitter apps, safeguarding voicemail and Intel’s 320 Series solid-state drive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On this week’s Bits: Tech Talk show, Bettina Edelstein talks to Claire Cain Miller, a Times technology reporter, about tension between Twitter and its third-party developers. Twitter has been buying companies that produce apps for Twitter or developing the apps on its own, which has raised questions about how the company treats competitors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tech Talk Podcast: Twitter’s Developers', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T23:56:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f270683-7f6d-5c9a-8694-f75bce3b4518', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f270683-7f6d-5c9a-8694-f75bce3b4518'}, {'abstract': 'The absence of Alex Rodriguez has Joe Girardi thinking about different ways to lift a sagging offense.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/yankees-still-toying-with-batting-order/', 'snippet': 'The absence of Alex Rodriguez has Joe Girardi thinking about different ways to lift a sagging offense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. PETERSBURG, Fla. — Brett Gardner leads the Yankees in on-base percentage, and he bats eighth or ninth. No one on the team has driven in more runs than Curtis Granderson, who is also tied for the most home runs, and he bats second. That lineup configuration seemed to work for Manager Joe Girardi when he had a full complement of players.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yankees Still Toying With Batting Order', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gardner, Brett', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Girardi, Joe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeter, Derek', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-20T23:59:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17f3e513-28b7-5b24-ab68-87c8e1cdc232', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17f3e513-28b7-5b24-ab68-87c8e1cdc232'}, {'abstract': 'An acting stint on “The Closer” puts Eric Garcetti in an office for which he is considering campaigning.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/us/21mayor.html', 'snippet': 'An acting stint on “The Closer” puts Eric Garcetti in an office for which he is considering campaigning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Like most aspiring actors here, Eric Garcetti has a day job: he is president of the City Council, and he is openly pondering a run for mayor of Los Angeles.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/21/us/MAYOR/MAYOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/21/us/MAYOR/MAYOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/21/us/MAYOR/MAYOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/21/us/MAYOR/MAYOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/21/us/MAYOR/MAYOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A TV Mayor Is Mulling a Run for the Real Job', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A TV Mayor Is Mulling A Run for the Real Job', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mayors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'City Councils', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T00:04:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Lovett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c7cf529-5726-5007-b0d4-3d08a4d2cd49', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c7cf529-5726-5007-b0d4-3d08a4d2cd49'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney chose a desolate strip mall in the San Fernando Valley, just north of Los Angeles, as the spot to criticize President Obama’s economic policies.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/in-l-a-romney-returns-to-familiar-script/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney chose a desolate strip mall in the San Fernando Valley, just north of Los Angeles, as the spot to criticize President Obama’s economic policies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NORTH HOLLYWOOD – With the paint peeling from the rooftops of the shuttered store fronts here, it was not exactly a glamorous backdrop. But it is likely for that reason that Mitt Romney chose this desolate strip mall in the San Fernando Valley, just north of Los Angeles, as the spot to criticize President Obama‘s economic policies. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In L.A., Romney Returns to Familiar Script', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T00:05:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Medina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Medina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d1e190a-13a6-534e-91a4-dceff6ffad15', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d1e190a-13a6-534e-91a4-dceff6ffad15'}, {'abstract': 'A start-up helps companies dig into potential employees’ activity online, from Facebook posts to e-commerce usage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/technology/social-media-history-becomes-a-new-job-hurdle.html', 'snippet': 'A start-up helps companies dig into potential employees’ activity online, from Facebook posts to e-commerce usage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Companies have long used criminal background checks, credit reports and even searches on Google and LinkedIn to probe the previous lives of prospective employees. Now, some companies are requiring job candidates to also pass a social media background check.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/21/business/SOCIAL/SOCIAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/21/business/SOCIAL/SOCIAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/21/business/SOCIAL/SOCIAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/21/business/SOCIAL/SOCIAL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/21/business/SOCIAL/SOCIAL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Social Media History Becomes a New Job Hurdle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Social Media History Becomes a New Job Hurdle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T00:08:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1c090c1-9ae0-5c25-8108-54f47e5062be', 'word_count': 1147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1c090c1-9ae0-5c25-8108-54f47e5062be'}, {'abstract': 'Campus Crusade for Christ, a ministry founded by the evangelist Bill Bright, is changing its name because the word “crusade” has negative associations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/us/21brfs-CAMPUSCRUSAD_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Campus Crusade for Christ, a ministry founded by the evangelist Bill Bright, is changing its name because the word “crusade” has negative associations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Campus Crusade for Christ, a ministry founded by the evangelist Bill Bright, is changing its name because the word “crusade” has negative associations with the bloody Christian conquests of the 11th to 13th centuries. The new name, “Cru,” is already used by many of the group’s members and will go into full circulation in early 2012. When the group was founded in 1951, it was common for American evangelists to refer to their ministries as crusades. But missionaries working internationally say they now find that the term often causes offense, especially to Muslims. Another reason for the name change, according to a spokesman: Much of the work of the group, which has 25,000 missionaries in 191 countries, is no longer only on campuses. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Campus Crusade for Christ Is Renamed', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Religion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Campus Crusade for Christ Is Renamed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Campus Crusade for Christ', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Organizational', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T00:09:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurie Goodstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ada5493-32ae-5fba-bc1b-a1001fd45337', 'word_count': 123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ada5493-32ae-5fba-bc1b-a1001fd45337'}, {'abstract': 'How to transfer an iTunes account from a desktop PC to a Mac laptop.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/qa-moving-the-music-to-a-new-machine/', 'snippet': 'How to transfer an iTunes account from a desktop PC to a Mac laptop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Moving the Music to a New Machine', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T00:10:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7030f7a9-a321-575d-968e-9ee27115623e', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7030f7a9-a321-575d-968e-9ee27115623e'}, {'abstract': 'The federal government was right to claim 10 rare gold coins worth millions of dollars each from a Philadelphia family in 2004, a jury decided Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/us/21brfs-FAMILYLOSESD_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The federal government was right to claim 10 rare gold coins worth millions of dollars each from a Philadelphia family in 2004, a jury decided Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The federal government was right to claim 10 rare gold coins worth millions of dollars each from a Philadelphia family in 2004, a jury decided Wednesday. The government had argued that since the mint never officially released the $20 “Double Eagles,” the coins — in the possession of the family of a Philadelphia gold dealer who died 21 years ago — must have been stolen and remained the property of the government. Only one such coin has ever been sold, bringing $7.6 million at auction in 2002. Judge Legrome D. Davis of Federal District Court will further rule on a motion that the government is the rightful owner of the coins. Assistant United States Attorney Jacqueline Romero said that the jurors “understood why it is we’re going after these things, and that there’s a larger principle involved here.” The Langbord family, heirs of the dealer, Israel Switt, are expected to appeal the decision. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pennsylvania: Family Loses Decision in Dispute Over Rare Coins', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Mid-Atlantic', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pennsylvania: Family Loses Decision In Dispute Over Rare Coins', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Currency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Switt, Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T00:12:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7f9b11f-8328-5d59-a2f5-ac08106a0bd0', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7f9b11f-8328-5d59-a2f5-ac08106a0bd0'}, {'abstract': 'The Pittsburgh Pirates, of all teams, are winning again, tied for the lead in the National League Central. It is the latest they have led their division since winning it in 1992.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/sports/baseball/over-500-pirates-return-to-the-mix.html', 'snippet': 'The Pittsburgh Pirates, of all teams, are winning again, tied for the lead in the National League Central. It is the latest they have led their division since winning it in 1992.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PITTSBURGH — For 18 years, baseball teams have known where to shop for help in the pennant race. The Pittsburgh Pirates have finished each of those seasons with a losing record, and by July, things are usually so hopeless that they trade veterans to contenders. The last two World Series winners have each included two players acquired from Pittsburgh for prospects.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New Yor