e': None, 'lastname': 'Simone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a14df96e-d2d6-5e69-b7f9-a1c85387101c', 'word_count': 1708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a14df96e-d2d6-5e69-b7f9-a1c85387101c'}, {'abstract': 'A play-by-play of Bull Run, the first major battle of the Civil War, by one of its leading historians.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/20/where-ignorant-armies-clash/', 'snippet': 'A play-by-play of Bull Run, the first major battle of the Civil War, by one of its leading historians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After months of preparation by both the Union and Confederate governments, more than 80,000 soldiers held strategic positions across northern Virginia during the first weeks of July 1861. The largest army ever fielded by the United States, 35,000 strong and commanded by Brig. Gen. Irvin McDowell, occupied the area around Arlington and Alexandria, just across the Potomac River from Washington (see Map 1). A second Union force of 18,000 men, led by Brig. Gen. Robert Patterson, guarded the lower Shenandoah Valley, near Harpers Ferry. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Ignorant Armies Clash', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Thomas J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnston, Joseph E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Winfield', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sherman, William Tecumseh', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T01:30:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion',