'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16c375f5-c70c-50ed-88ae-9c82e5940858', 'word_count': 60, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16c375f5-c70c-50ed-88ae-9c82e5940858'}, {'abstract': 'For younger New Yorkers, the advent of same-sex marriage brings a range of views.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/young-new-yorkers-on-gay-marriage/', 'snippet': 'For younger New Yorkers, the advent of same-sex marriage brings a range of views.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday, New York will become the largest state to allow same-sex couples to wed, a move that was approved by the State Senate last month to tearful and raucous applause. For many couples who have been waiting for decades for their union to be legally recognized, it was a joyous victory in a hard-fought battle for the same rights afforded to straight couples.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Young New Yorkers on Gay Marriage', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T11:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily S. Rueb, Kim Nowacki and Michael Kolomatsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Rueb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nowacki', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kolomatsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d0fd32f-5a36-5aff-951a-83aff39affb7', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d0fd32f-5a36-5aff-951a-83aff39affb7'}, {'abstract': 'For better or worse, the term “exit” is synonymous with one of two things in a business owner’s mind: selling or dying. And who cashes out their baby?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/when-words-get-in-the-way-of-selling-a-business/', 'snippet': 'For better or worse, the term “exit” is synonymous with one of two things in a business owner’s mind: selling or dying. And who cashes out their baby?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been in love, some might say obsessed, with the written word for as long as I can remember. That’s why, as an undergraduate, I declared my English major as early as I could. At the time I believed that such decisions should be based entirely on what you love to do. I loved to read and write, so it never occurred to me that I would be anything but an English major.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Words Get in the Way of Selling a Business', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T11:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barbara Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barbara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14b67cf1-0580-5ea7-b4a4-840571b66b59', 'word_count': 740, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14b67cf1-0580-5ea7-b4a4-840571b66b59'}, {'abstract': 'Just weeks ago, Tim Pawlenty was positioning himself as the top alternative to Mitt Romney; now he and his staff are busy trying to lower expectations.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/pawlenty-lowers-expectations-so-he-can-beat-them/', 'snippet': 'Just weeks ago, Tim Pawlenty was positioning himself as the top alternative to Mitt Romney; now he and his staff are busy trying to lower expectations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a time not too long ago when Tim Pawlenty was actively positioning himself as the No. 2 heavyweight in the Republican presidential race, the obvious alternative to the front-runner, Mitt Romney.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pawlenty Lowers Expectations, So He Can Beat Them', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pawlenty, Tim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T11:19:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c0b412d-6cf6-51d2-b19f-0fce6adf65b7', 'word_count': 843, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c0b412d-6cf6-51d2-b19f-0fce6adf65b7'}, {'abstract': 'The financial firm reported a loss of $558 million, but three crucial divisions posted significant and promising gains.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/morgan-stanley-posts-loss-of-558-million/', 'snippet': 'The financial firm reported a loss of $558 million, but three crucial divisions posted significant and promising gains.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:59 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morgan Stanley Posts Loss That Hints at Recovery', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gorman, James P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Stanley', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T11:52:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fb9d5df-3a03-5381-b5df-299dc49698f2', 'word_count': 759, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fb9d5df-3a03-5381-b5df-299dc49698f2'}, {'abstract': 'BC Partners is near a deal to buy Com Hem, the biggest cable company in Sweden, from the Carlyle Group and Providence Equity Partners for about $2.6 billion.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/bc-partners-nears-a-deal-to-buy-swedens-com-hem-for-2-6-billion/', 'snippet': 'BC Partners is near a deal to buy Com Hem, the biggest cable company in Sweden, from the Carlyle Group and Providence Equity Partners for about $2.6 billion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BC Partners is near a deal to buy Com Hem, the biggest cable company in Sweden, from the Carlyle Group and Providence Equity Partners for about $2.6 billion, a person briefed on the matter told DealBook on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'BC Partners Said to Be Near $2.6 Billion Deal for Swedish Cable Company', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BC Partners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carlyle Group', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Providence Equity Partners Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T11:55:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c9e93bc-eb77-59e9-b329-91f25278d6cd', 'word_count': 104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c9e93bc-eb77-59e9-b329-91f25278d6cd'}, {'abstract': 'Five vastly diverse and fascinating documentaries resist easy summary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/movies/complicated-truths-in-tabloid-and-the-interrupters.html', 'snippet': 'Five vastly diverse and fascinating documentaries resist easy summary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AT a time when journalism seems to be consumed in ever smaller bites — OMG! #NoTW! — a welcome and perhaps inevitable opposing tendency has emerged, devoted to the preservation of the long form. Basic information can arrive via tweets and updated blog posts, opinion can be dispensed and harvested across social networks and aggregations sites, but the pleasures and challenges of extended narrative require a different kind of time and space. Several worthy sites (notably longreads.com and longform.org) have popped up to steer the curious and patient readers and tablet wielders toward the kind of fact-based writing that resists the easy takeaway. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24SCOTT1/24SCOTT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24SCOTT1/24SCOTT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24SCOTT1/24SCOTT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24SCOTT1/24SCOTT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24SCOTT1/24SCOTT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Captivating Films, Complicated Truths', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Captivating Films, Complicated Truths', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marsh, James (1963- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiseman, Frederick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Herzog, Werner', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turturro, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morris, Errol', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, Steve (1954- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T12:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2f93037-e875-5cd0-824f-6f73c8cfb8fc', 'word_count': 1617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2f93037-e875-5cd0-824f-6f73c8cfb8fc'}, {'abstract': 'Could there be a dreamier dram than the mummified remnants of Sir Ernest Shackleton’s whisky stash?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/magazine/drinking-ernest-shackletons-whisky.html', 'snippet': 'Could there be a dreamier dram than the mummified remnants of Sir Ernest Shackleton’s whisky stash?', 'lead_paragraph': '“It’s daft,” a man settled in a Glasgow pub said to me not long ago, talking about the sums that rare Scotch whiskies sometimes fetch at auction — the bottle of Dalmore 64-year-old, for example, that sold last month for nearly $200,000. “If you pay that much, you canna drink it, and wha’s the use a just lookin’ at the bottle?”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24whiskey_span/mag-24Whiskey-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24whiskey_span/mag-24Whiskey-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24whiskey_span/mag-24Whiskey-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24whiskey_span/24whiskey_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24whiskey_span/24whiskey_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Spirits of the South Pole', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spirits of the South Pole', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whiskey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shackleton, Ernest', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Glasgow (Scotland)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scotch (Whiskey)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T12:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86b6a06f-9cb5-5c44-8129-8b2f28984c66', 'word_count': 3896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86b6a06f-9cb5-5c44-8129-8b2f28984c66'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from Capsule, the popular two-day men’s-wear trade show that concluded Tuesday in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/to-the-trade/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from Capsule, the popular two-day men’s-wear trade show that concluded Tuesday in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': ' NYTD.fullScreenSlideShowFactory.createSlideshow("#131", 592, 400, true, chameleonData);', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To the Trade', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T12:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Pask', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pask', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90782ce7-4d2d-5ca1-9517-fabbf4c49940', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90782ce7-4d2d-5ca1-9517-fabbf4c49940'}, {'abstract': '“Captain America,” like its unapologetically corny hero, is propelled by unpretentious and plucky ingenuity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/movies/captain-america-with-chris-evans-review.html', 'snippet': '“Captain America,” like its unapologetically corny hero, is propelled by unpretentious and plucky ingenuity.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Captain America: The First Avenger” turns out in the end — and this is really the opposite of a spoiler — to have been a two-hour teaser for another movie. That picture, foreshadowed in the second “Hulk,” the first “Thor” and both “Iron Man” episodes and scheduled to open next May, will be called “The Avengers.” Whether you regard its imminence with resignation, dread or uncontainable glee depends on your standing in the Marvel Universe. Shareholders and die-hard fans no doubt already have the opening date circled on their calendars, and many of the rest of us will probably show up as well, either out of curiosity or solemn duty.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/22CAPTAIN/22CAPTAIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 648, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/22CAPTAIN/22CAPTAIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/22CAPTAIN/22CAPTAIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hey, Brooklyn, Where’d You Get Those Muscles?', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Captain America'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hey, Brooklyn, Where’d You Get Those Muscles?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnston, Joe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Evans, Chris (1981- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T12:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffa39758-491b-5a5d-87b4-ebb1b214c13e', 'word_count': 1201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffa39758-491b-5a5d-87b4-ebb1b214c13e'}, {'abstract': 'By removing dikes or highways that function as dikes, planners can make way for rising seas that are expected to push an ecosystem farther inland in Washington State.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/as-a-river-warms-hope-beckons/', 'snippet': 'By removing dikes or highways that function as dikes, planners can make way for rising seas that are expected to push an ecosystem farther inland in Washington State.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In my article in Thursday’s Times about the future of the Nisqually watershed in Washington State, I mention efforts by conservation groups and land trusts across the country to buy up land parcels that are expected to turn into valuable ecological assets someday — marshes or swamplands, for example — as the sea rolls further inland because of global warming.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As a River Warms, Hope Beckons', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Land Use Policies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wetlands', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ducks Unlimited', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pacific Northwestern States (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Puget Sound', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (State)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T12:08:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leslie Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leslie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/005b3d37-8273-5ebc-a87b-03d83b5fedae', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/005b3d37-8273-5ebc-a87b-03d83b5fedae'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: An earlier press baron probably would have handled the phone hacking and bribery scandal quite differently. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/if-it-were-hearst-instead-of-murdoch/', 'snippet': 'The Day: An earlier press baron probably would have handled the phone hacking and bribery scandal quite differently. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rupert Murdoch, who became a force to reckon with in this city when he bought The New York Post 35 years ago, is routinely compared to a news media titan of the past, William Randolph Hearst. Not that “news media” was a term in vogue in Hearst’s heyday, the early decades of the 20th century. “Press baron” was more like it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'If It Were Hearst Instead of Murdoch …', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hearst, William Randolph', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T12:19:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fffab413-d321-5b31-a94f-df87fe673fab', 'word_count': 1117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fffab413-d321-5b31-a94f-df87fe673fab'}, {'abstract': 'The company had a net loss in the second quarter, in part because of a write-down reflecting the declining value of its regional newspapers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/business/media/times-company-posts-loss-on-write-down.html', 'snippet': 'The company had a net loss in the second quarter, in part because of a write-down reflecting the declining value of its regional newspapers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Times Company reported on Thursday that nearly a quarter of a million people had purchased subscriptions to The Times website, a sign that its digital strategy was paying off so far.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Optimism for Digital Plan, But Times Co. Posts Loss', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Optimism for Digital Plan, But Times Co. Posts Loss', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords':