40d33a5-38a6-5ba3-8646-188c99b087eb'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/morning-take-out-290/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOP STORIES', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T13:57:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a3920b6-0102-51e3-82e1-6186065a2ef7', 'word_count': 1672, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a3920b6-0102-51e3-82e1-6186065a2ef7'}, {'abstract': 'Frank Quattrone, the star tech investment banker, says the tech industry is at the beginning of a boom and compares LinkedIn’s I.P.O. to Netscape’s in 1995.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/frank-quattrone-is-having-flashbacks-to-the-bubble/', 'snippet': 'Frank Quattrone, the star tech investment banker, says the tech industry is at the beginning of a boom and compares LinkedIn’s I.P.O. to Netscape’s in 1995.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ASPEN, Colo. — Frank P. Quattrone is the banker who helped set off the dot-com boom when he took Netscape public in 1995. Today he is having flashbacks. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Frank Quattrone Is Having Flashbacks to the Bubble', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quattrone, Frank P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netscape', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T13:58:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/379a036e-7de2-5409-aeb7-6c5b208bff58', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/379a036e-7de2-5409-aeb7-6c5b208bff58'}, {'abstract': 'A new study says no, since the rate of migration for homeowners and renters fell roughly in tandem during the recession.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/are-unsellable-homes-holding-back-job-growth/', 'snippet': 'A new study says no, since the rate of migration for homeowners and renters fell roughly in tandem during the recession.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many economists have argued that the continually deteriorating housing market may be holding workers back from relocating to areas where jobs are more plentiful. If you can’t sell your home in Detroit, where the unemployment rate is 11.6 percent, you’re not able to move to Fargo, where the rate is a mere 3.5 percent. That creates a geographic mismatch in the labor market, and keeps job growth lower than it might otherwise be if workers were more mobile.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are Unsellable Homes Holding Back Job Growth?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T14:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31245657-961a-5421-9624-049aff72ad81', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31245657-961a-5421-9624-049aff72ad81'}, {'abstract': 'With a major gift, the mayor says he hopes to help the environmental group retire as many as a third of the nation’s oldest coal-fired power plants by 2020.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/bloomberg-and-sierra-club-join-forces-to-slow-coal/', 'snippet': 'With a major gift, the mayor says he hopes to help the environmental group retire as many as a third of the nation’s oldest coal-fired power plants by 2020.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg of New York announced on Thursday that his main charitable organization would donate $50 million over four years to the Sierra Club’s campaign to shut down coal plants and move the United States toward cleaner sources of energy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bloomberg and Sierra Club Join Forces to Slow Coal', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'C40 Cities Climate Leadership Group', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sierra Club', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T14:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8c5f415-a4ef-5179-9d00-e0cf1d7305b1', 'word_count': 594, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8c5f415-a4ef-5179-9d00-e0cf1d7305b1'}, {'abstract': 'On Aug. 31, groups of 40 people will go from restaurant to restaurant in the market for $180 each.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/for-eatalys-birthday-dine-around-the-store/', 'snippet': 'On Aug. 31, groups of 40 people will go from restaurant to restaurant in the market for $180 each.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eataly, the Italian megamarket, will mark its first birthday with lots of products on sale, but also a special event: a dine-around in all its restaurants on Aug. 31. Groups of 40 people will go from restaurant to restaurant in the market, starting with Manzo for a taste of mortadella-stuffed suckling pig, then Le Verdure for a fresh vegetable soup, La Pizza for focaccia with toppings, next La Pasta for spaghetti alla chitarra with sea urchin, then Le Pesce for swordfish “filet mignon,” and finally La Piazza for dessert. Each course, served at communal tables, includes wine. The cost is $180 per person including tax and tip. This extravaganza can be booked  at 45 minute intervals from 5 to 9:30 p.m.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Eataly’s Birthday, Dine Around the Store', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eataly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T14:19:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/366b509f-e62e-519f-83c9-cc0091d9d84c', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/366b509f-e62e-519f-83c9-cc0091d9d84c'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/what-were-reading-230/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T14:20:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By THE NEW YORK TIMES', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/462d5470-fa4a-512d-86bc-ad7fc8ca2a09', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/462d5470-fa4a-512d-86bc-ad7fc8ca2a09'}, {'abstract': 'MetLife announced on Thursday that it was considering a sale of its MetLife Bank depository business in a bid to focus on its insurance business.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/metlife-mulls-sale-of-bank-business/', 'snippet': 'MetLife announced on Thursday that it was considering a sale of its MetLife Bank depository business in a bid to focus on its insurance business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MetLife announced on Thursday that it was considering selling its MetLife Bank depository business in a bid to focus on its insurance business.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'MetLife Weighs Sale of Bank Unit', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MetLife Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T14:25:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/deb90316-df1a-53f3-917f-ff55569faf87', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/deb90316-df1a-53f3-917f-ff55569faf87'}, {'abstract': 'Lessons in any one of 18 languages are offered by Projects Abroad in courses that combine volunteer work with classroom instruction.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/learn-a-language-while-you-volunteer/', 'snippet': 'Lessons in any one of 18 languages are offered by Projects Abroad in courses that combine volunteer work with classroom instruction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travelers with an interest in learning Amharic, Ewe, Patois or Mandarin can sign up for the new language programs offered by Projects Abroad in Ethiopia, Togo, Jamaica and China, respectively.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Learn a Language While You Volunteer', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Volunteers and Community Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ethiopia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T14:29:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79aa929c-c04f-5052-86e4-ff4338f33a2e', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79aa929c-c04f-5052-86e4-ff4338f33a2e'}, {'abstract': 'Not.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/welcome-to-the-recovery-2/', 'snippet': 'Not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Companies step up layoffs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Welcome to the Recovery', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T14:47:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1d3d589-5a43-54c6-b3a3-97fb160e361f', 'word_count': 83, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1d3d589-5a43-54c6-b3a3-97fb160e361f'}, {'abstract': '“Rain: A Tribute to the Beatles on Broadway” will play its final performance on July 31. It was previously scheduled to run through Sept. 4.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/broadway-beatles-tribute-says-sorry-but-its-time-to-go/', 'snippet': '“Rain: A Tribute to the Beatles on Broadway” will play its final performance on July 31. It was previously scheduled to run through Sept. 4.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Beatles are breaking up even sooner than expected – on Broadway, anyway. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Broadway Beatles Tribute Says Sorry, But It’s Time to Go', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beatles, The', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T14:48:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b80d564-e53a-59ac-b2e5-7853717bd0ce', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b80d564-e53a-59ac-b2e5-7853717bd0ce'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s pairing: the poem “After a Rainstorm” by Robert Wrigley and an article by David Carr on a documentary film about the horse whisperer Buck Brannaman.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/poetry-pairing-july-2-2011/', 'snippet': 'This week’s pairing: the poem “After a Rainstorm” by Robert Wrigley and an article by David Carr on a documentary film about the horse whisperer Buck Brannaman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In our weekly “Poetry Pairing” series, we collaborate with the Poetry Foundation to feature a work from its American Life in Poetry project alongside content from The Times that somehow echoes, extends or challenges the poem’s themes. Each poem is introduced briefly by Ted Kooser, a former United States poet laureate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry Pairing | July 21, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T15:01:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46b8f605-1abb-53b6-8ba8-3afeb1b9a6a2', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46b8f605-1abb-53b6-8ba8-3afeb1b9a6a2'}, {'abstract': 'Wagging the housing dog.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/jump-start-possibilities/', 'snippet': 'Wagging the housing dog.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brad DeLong and Karl Smith have been arguing that America, after 5 years of housing bust, now has a housing shortfall:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jump Start Possibilities', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T15:10:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1f4351f-975c-5524-84cd-1ab2f7cb97b6', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1f4351f-975c-5524-84cd-1ab2f7cb97b6'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Friedman, echoing many Broadway producers, said virtually every production-related expense was more expensive in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/the-staggering-cost-of-broadway/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Friedman, echoing many Broadway producers, said virtually every production-related expense was more expensive in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How high is the cost of producing shows on Broadway these days? Consider this:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Staggering Cost of Broadway', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friedman, Sonia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinter, Harold', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott Thomas, Kristin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T15:15:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fd1fd7e-94c0-5f51-9343-7113425dd000', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fd1fd7e-94c0-5f51-9343-7113425dd000'}, {'abstract': 'This capital city, which celebrates its bicentennial this year, is elevating its cultural imprint by adding new museums and expanding older ones. A new museum, above, has opened in an abandoned prison.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/guess-where-this-is-24/', 'snippet': 'This capital city, which celebrates its bicentennial this year, is elevating its cultural imprint by adding new museums and expanding older ones. A new museum, above, has opened in an abandoned prison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s time again for our weekly contest: guess the location of the photograph above.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Guess Where This Is', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T15:18:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4887b5fd-9461-5633-88bb-fb5e611f13e4', 'word_count': 80, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4887b5fd-9461-5633-88bb-fb5e611f13e4'}, {'abstract': 'The campaign, which tried to humorously suggest that men suffer along with women from premenstrual syndrome, is being replaced with a discussion over the issue.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/under-fire-pms-related-milk-campaign-shut-down-early/', 'snippet': 'The campaign, which tried to humorously suggest that men suffer along with women from premenstrual syndrome, is being replaced with a discussion over the issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A campaign by a milk marketer to address the serious subject of premenstrual syndrome in a humorous fashion — by focusing on how men purportedly suffer from its effects along with women — is being ended early after an outpouring of criticism and comments about the tone of the ads.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Under Fire, PMS-Related Milk Campaign Shut Down Early', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND 