bject', 'value': 'Genetic Engineering', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Macular Degeneration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stem Cells', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T16:46:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55fc8960-d543-5f2a-ace3-b56c9f9cf1f4', 'word_count': 577, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55fc8960-d543-5f2a-ace3-b56c9f9cf1f4'}, {'abstract': 'A business owner wonders if he can prohibit his employees from eating at a restaurant with which he’s feuding.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/can-i-tell-my-employees-where-not-to-eat/', 'snippet': 'A business owner wonders if he can prohibit his employees from eating at a restaurant with which he’s feuding.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this week, FrankJ, a reader from California, raised a question on a post written by Paul Downs. Because the comment raises several interesting issues — including how much control business owners have over their employees on their own time — we thought it was a worth a post of its own. Please read FrankJ’s question and leave a comment below.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can I Tell My Employees Where Not to Eat?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T16:46:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': "By You're the Boss Editors", 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff10f8bb-2402-53d1-9633-892f3f932e51', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff10f8bb-2402-53d1-9633-892f3f932e51'}, {'abstract': '“The assumption was that states don’t go bankrupt, which is obviously not true,” said one banking expert in Germany. “It’s totally crazy.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/business/global/europes-new-bank-rules-still-favor-government-debt.html', 'snippet': '“The assumption was that states don’t go bankrupt, which is obviously not true,” said one banking expert in Germany. “It’s totally crazy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN European banks bought loads of Greek bonds over the years, making themselves more vulnerable to default and amplifying the current crisis, they were just doing what regulators encouraged them to do — and, indeed, still encourage them to do. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/business/BONDS/BONDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Greek Debt Risk-Free? European Banking Officials Think So', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Is Greek Debt Risk-Free? European Banking Officials Think So', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T16:48:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By