yline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94ae1a0c-e280-5148-8d04-2d5796475b39', 'word_count': 967, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94ae1a0c-e280-5148-8d04-2d5796475b39'}, {'abstract': 'Implicit in the Belarus Free Theater troupe’s production of the international hodgepodge “Eurepica. Challenge” at the Almeida Theater in London is the message that Belarus isn’t alone in making a mess of its own country.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/london-theater-journal-familiar-faces-and-themes-all-over-the-world/', 'snippet': 'Implicit in the Belarus Free Theater troupe’s production of the international hodgepodge “Eurepica. Challenge” at the Almeida Theater in London is the message that Belarus isn’t alone in making a mess of its own country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — It seldom surprises me when I run into people here that I recognize from other places. London is an international city, and the theater is a small town. I’d seen Joe Mantello, the Broadway actor and director, in the audience for “Much Ado About Nothing” in the West End last week. And a week later, there was Karen Brooks Hopkins, the president of Brooklyn Academy of Music, in front of Shakespeare’s Globe in Southwark.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Theater Journal: Familiar Faces and Themes All Over the World', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Attenborough, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hopkins, Karen Brooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Landsman, Aaron', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mantello, Joe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ALMEIDA THEATER', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Belarus Free Theater', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T19:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc779e3e-a813-5447-a8d9-bc559bd6d74a', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc779e3e-a813-5447-a8d9-bc559bd6d74a'}, {'abstract': 'Hitting it out of the South Park.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/from-the-gnomes-of-zurich/', 'snippet': 'Hitting it out of the South Park.', 'lead_paragraph': '… to the Underpants Gnomes of Frankfurt. Commenter jeevmon (seconded by several others) reacts to my 1937 in Europe post with a summary of the plan:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From the Gnomes of Zurich …', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T19:03:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bba65c08-f26f-5c95-95cd-66a2bede60d6', 'word_count': 43, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bba65c08-f26f-5c95-95cd-66a2bede60d6'}, {'abstract': 'One Police Plaza: A detective and writer retires; the many