to the Nissan NV200 in New York’s competition to designate a new taxi for the city’s fleet, the Ford has been granted a license to operate while the NV200 is being prepared for service.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Because New York’s anointed Taxi of Tomorrow won’t be here tomorrow, the city’s Taxi and Limousine Commission approved a measure on Thursday that would allow fleet owners to put Ford Transit Connect vans, the Ford Taurus and the Chevrolet Impala on the streets as yellow cabs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For a New York Minute, Ford Transit Connect Is a Taxi of Tomorrow', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chevrolet Division of General Motors Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nissan Motor Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taxi and Limousine Commission', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:07:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a56adb52-ada2-54b4-bdeb-89e7e18aadcd', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a56adb52-ada2-54b4-bdeb-89e7e18aadcd'}, {'abstract': 'This week on Popcast: Jon Caramanica on the Pitchfork Festival in Chicago; Jim McKinley on Mitch Winehouse.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/popcast-a-report-from-indie-nation/', 'snippet': 'This week on Popcast: Jon Caramanica on the Pitchfork Festival in Chicago; Jim McKinley on Mitch Winehouse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week on Popcast, Jon Caramanica, a pop music critic for The Times, talks about what he saw last weekend at the Pitchfork Music Festival in Chicago’s Union Park.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: A Report From Indie Nation', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Rourke, Jim", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winehouse, Amy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ANIMAL COLLECTIVE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NRBQ', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pitchfork Media', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:09:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Egner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d41bf37-6e55-5bf7-81bf-1636bfccdd0a', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d41bf37-6e55-5bf7-81bf-1636bfccdd0a'}, {'abstract': 'World War I has spawned a cultural cottage industry of war-themed stories in theater, film and art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/theater/world-war-i-reverberates-in-silver-tassie-and-war-horse.html', 'snippet': 'World War I has spawned a cultural cottage industry of war-themed stories in theater, film and art.', 'lead_paragraph': '“WORLD WAR I is thought of as this old-fashioned war,” the director Garry Hynes said, “but to my point of view it has the force of an apocalypse.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24WORLDWAR2/24WORLDWAR2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24WORLDWAR2/24WORLDWAR2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 378}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24WORLDWAR2/24WORLDWAR2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24WORLDWAR2/24WORLDWAR2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24WORLDWAR2/24WORLDWAR2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'World War I Returns to Center Stage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'World War I Returns To Center Stage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lincoln Center Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:10:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Grode', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grode', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8386c59-436b-55f8-8bbb-02984409d861', 'word_count': 1131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8386c59-436b-55f8-8bbb-02984409d861'}, {'abstract': 'TLC, well known for its reality shows about abnormally big and small families in the heartland, has ordered a series about five Muslim families.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/arts/television/tlc-plans-series-on-muslim-families.html', 'snippet': 'TLC, well known for its reality shows about abnormally big and small families in the heartland, has ordered a series about five Muslim families.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TLC, well known for its reality shows about abnormally big and small families in the heartland, has ordered a series about five Muslim families living in Dearborn, Mich. The series, called “All-American Muslim,” will have its debut in November, the channel said on Thursday. It is notable partly because Muslim-Americans are depicted relatively rarely on American television. “Through these families and their diverse experiences, we will explore how they blend their values and traditions with everyday life in America, providing insight into their culture with care and compassion,” Amy Winter, the general manager of TLC, said in a news release. Those in the series include a woman who is marrying an Irish Catholic man who is converting to Islam, a couple who are having their first child and a police official who works to reduce discrimination in his community.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TLC Plans Series On Muslim Families', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TLC Plans Series On Muslim Families', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:16:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4732de1b-f117-5d49-b1f8-6a4d3ae85ee9', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4732de1b-f117-5d49-b1f8-6a4d3ae85ee9'}, {'abstract': 'The reason “Man of Steel” will come out on June 14, 2013 — instead of December 2012, as fans expected — involves Hollywood’s version of air traffic control.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/a-change-in-the-flight-plan-for-next-superman-movie/', 'snippet': 'The reason “Man of Steel” will come out on June 14, 2013 — instead of December 2012, as fans expected — involves Hollywood’s version of air traffic control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN DIEGO— The news moved through Comic-Con like wildfire in a wheat field on Thursday: Warner Brothers is pushing the release of “Man of Steel” back by six months! There must be big problems! The reality, however, may be much less interesting, even if it does mean that fans will need to wait longer than they thought for Warner’s newest Superman flick.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Change in the Flight Plan for Next Superman Movie', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snyder, Zack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:18:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4924c54b-c6a3-5822-8c29-bb2e3a497755', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4924c54b-c6a3-5822-8c29-bb2e3a497755'}, {'abstract': 'Will Gluck’s “Friends With Benefits,” starring Mila Kunis and Justin Timberlake, is a romantic comedy about romantic comedy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/movies/justin-timberlake-in-friends-with-benefits-review.html', 'snippet': 'Will Gluck’s “Friends With Benefits,” starring Mila Kunis and Justin Timberlake, is a romantic comedy about romantic comedy.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Friends With Benefits,” a breezy, speedy and (no kidding) funny comedy with a nicely matched Justin Timberlake and Mila Kunis that is about love and sex in the age of social networking, gets some of its juice and tang partly by trash-talking its own genre. The setup is familiar, as are the essential elements: a single man and a single woman, two battered hearts yet a pair of resilient, eager, pretty bodies. But Ms. Kunis’s character is dark and savvy, not blond and dippy, and when she comically rails against an unseen Katherine Heigl you may sigh with relief (fingers crossed) that you’re watching the “Scream” of romantic comedies.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/FRIENDS/FRIENDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/FRIENDS/FRIENDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/FRIENDS/FRIENDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 783, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'im