n the Lower East Side.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/arts/design/ella-kruglyanskayaand-benjamin-senior.html', 'snippet': 'Senior pair up for a bit of summer fun, depicting passive and active activities, at James Fuentes LLC on the Lower East Side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James Fuentes LLC ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ELLA KRUGLYANSKAYAAND BENJAMIN SENIOR', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ella Kruglyanskaya and Benjamin Senior', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:45:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/00763ab9-8738-5fb2-8cbf-989fdc054d1a', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00763ab9-8738-5fb2-8cbf-989fdc054d1a'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of four tales of sexual confusion in Chicago, “Autoerotic” is a low-key, naturalistically styled film by Joe Swanberg and Adam Wingard.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/movies/joe-swanberg-and-adam-wingards-autoerotic-review.html', 'snippet': 'A collection of four tales of sexual confusion in Chicago, “Autoerotic” is a low-key, naturalistically styled film by Joe Swanberg and Adam Wingard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'William Blake said that what men and women require of one another is “the lineaments of Gratified Desire.” Woody Allen said of masturbation, “It’s sex with someone I love.” But nothing is ever as simple as these sages would suggest, a point made with a lot of skin and a measure of wit in “Autoerotic,” Joe Swanberg and Adam Wingard’s collection of four tales of sexual confusion in Chicago.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/AUTO/AUTO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/AUTO/AUTO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/AUTO/AUTO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/AUTO/AUTO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/AUTO/AUTO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Sex Is Loneliness Multiplied', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Autoerotic'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Sex Is Loneliness Multiplied', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swanberg, Joe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:47:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.&nbsp;O. SCOTT', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/760784e3-bd68-5f1d-9057-25ee9915cced', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/760784e3-bd68-5f1d-9057-25ee9915cced'}, {'abstract': 'Offstage, she may still wear T-shirts and sneakers, but Taylor Swift has moved her songs out of the high school realm and into the adult.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/arts/music/taylor-swift-at-the-prudential-center-review.html', 'snippet': 'Offstage, she may still wear T-shirts and sneakers, but Taylor Swift has moved her songs out of the high school realm and into the adult.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWARK — One big set piece for Taylor Swift’s new touring arena show acts out the title song of her 2010 album, “Speak Now.” On Wednesday night at the Prudential Center here, where her four-night run concludes on Sunday, it was a full-scale wedding scene, with a bride whose gown was, per the lyrics, “shaped like a pastry.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/sub-SWIFT/sub-SWIFT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/sub-SWIFT/sub-SWIFT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/sub-SWIFT/sub-SWIFT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 577, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/sub-SWIFT/sub-SWIFT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/sub-SWIFT/sub-SWIFT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Her Fans, Squealing, Won’t Hold Their Peace', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Her Fans, Squealing, Won’t Hold Their Peace', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swift, Taylor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:49:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3dfa547-d329-50b9-94e7-88f0c1a3928d', 'word_count': 550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3dfa547-d329-50b9-94e7-88f0c1a3928d'}, {'abstract': 'The pianists Cyprien Katsaris and Gesa Kuecker performed at the International Keyboard Institute and Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/arts/music/cyprien-katsaris-and-gesa-kuecker-on-piano-review.html', 'snippet': 'The pianists Cyprien Katsaris and Gesa Kuecker performed at the International Keyboard Institute and Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These days many performers in classical music speak to audiences to share insights and stories. But it is not often that an artist disavows a performance he has just given.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/PIANO-1/PIANO-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Improvisation, as Well as Intensity', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Improvisation, as Well as Intensity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Katsaris, Cyprien', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mannes College of Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:52:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/50a33ef1-d15f-5087-99e4-71371d0dfa41', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50a33ef1-d15f-5087-99e4-71371d0dfa41'}, {'abstract': 'Many Google+ users are already facing a problem they had on Facebook: How do you deal with people who want to be your Google+ buddy if you don’t really like them that much?', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/how-to-hide-from-annoying-people-on-google/', 'snippet': 'Many Google+ users are already facing a problem they had on Facebook: How do you deal with people who want to be your Google+ buddy if you don’t really like them that much?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Google’s new social network, Google+, has been open to new members for only a few weeks, yet an estimated 10 million people have already signed up. This means many Google+ users are already facing a problem they had on Facebook: How do you deal with people who want to be your Google+ buddy if you don’t really like them that much?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Hide From Annoying People on Google+', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:52:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Boutin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boutin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40c03307-330d-5e29-9274-6294d51d38d4', 'word_count': 274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40c03307-330d-5e29-9274-6294d51d38d4'}, {'abstract': 'Despite being censured by his House colleagues, Mr. Rangel is no longer being shunned by fellow Democrats, judging from the preparations for his party at the Plaza hotel.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/rangels-birthday-fund-raiser-regains-its-popularity/', 'snippet': 'Despite being censured by his House colleagues, Mr. Rangel is no longer being shunned by fellow Democrats, judging from the preparations for his party at the Plaza hotel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been barely half a year since Representative Charles B. Rangel, the long-serving Harlem Democrat and dean of New York’s Congressional delegation, was censured by an overwhelming majority of his House colleagues for failing to pay income taxes and misusing his office to solicit fund-raising donations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rangel’s Birthday Fund-Raiser Regains Its Popularity', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quinn, Christine C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rangel, Charles B', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumer, Charles E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T21:53:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Confessore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Confessore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab0aaf4a-d40a-5da2-ac1c-2d053539da29', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab0aaf4a-d40a-5da2-ac1c-2d053539da29'}, {'abstract': '“The Myth of the American Sleepover” looks at the unsteady mixture of sophistication and naïveté that is central to modern American teenage life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/movies/the-myth-of-the-american-sleepover-review.html', 'snippet': '“The Myth of the American Sleepover” looks at the unsteady mixture of sophistication and naïveté that is central to modern American teenage life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The teenagers in “The Myth of the American Sleepover,” David Robert Mitchell’s lovely debut feature, are linked in a great chain of liking, which is their euphemism for longing. “Do you smoke?” a boy asks a girl when she hands him a lighter to use on a bug-repellent candle. “No,” she says, “but I used to like a guy who smoked.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/MYTH/MYTH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/MYTH/MYTH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/MYTH/MYTH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 549, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/MYTH/MYTH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/22/arts/MYTH/MYTH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Night Belongs to Teenage Yearning', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The Myth of the American Sleepover'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Night Belongs to Teenage Yearning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-21T22:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/63f14009-84fc-5f25-b938-604136c9fcce', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63f14009-84fc-5f25-b938-604136c9fcce'}, {'abstract': 'A favorite theme of Comic-Con, and the subject of Morgan Spurlock’s new book and film, is the thing itself: Comic-Con.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/21/everyone-meets-stan-lee-at-the-con/', 'snippet': 'A favorite theme of Comic-Con, and the subject of Morgan Spurlock’s new book and film, is the thing itself: Comic-Con.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN DIEGO — A favorite theme at Comic-Con has always been – what else – Comic-Con. Those who attend count their years of faithful attendance, and love to tell stories about their first visit, their weirdest encounter, or how they met a wife, or an ex-wife, while navigating through a herd of Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles at some long-ago “Con.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Everyone Meets Stan Lee at the ‘Con’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None