WEST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California: Hunger Strike Has Ended, State Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hunger Strikes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pelican Bay State Prison', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T04:55:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Lovett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/029c1ce5-2314-56c7-8c3f-b567ce79879b', 'word_count': 56, 'uri': 'nyt://article/029c1ce5-2314-56c7-8c3f-b567ce79879b'}, {'abstract': 'The Senate set in motion a process on Thursday to extend the tenure of Robert S. Mueller as director of the Federal Bureau of Investigation for about two years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/us/22brfs-Mueller.html', 'snippet': 'The Senate set in motion a process on Thursday to extend the tenure of Robert S. Mueller as director of the Federal Bureau of Investigation for about two years.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Senate set in motion a process on Thursday to extend the tenure of Robert S. Mueller as director of the Federal Bureau of Investigation for about two years, after Senator Rand Paul, Republican of Kentucky, agreed to stop blocking legislation necessary for that effort. By a voice vote, the Senate passed a bill that would create a special shortened term for the directorship that would last through 2012, and made an exception to a law that forbids anyone from serving in the position for more than 10 years. The House is expected to pass the bill swiftly. After President Obama signs it into law, he will nominate Mr. Mueller for the shortened term, and the Senate has already agreed to vote on confirming him after just two hours of debate. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Senate Acts to Keep Mueller as Director of F.B.I.', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WASHINGTON', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Senate Acts to Keep Mueller as Director of F.B.I.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mueller, Robert S III', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T04:56:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/410ef5b6-5aa0-5a7d-9657-2ab21d2b48d3', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/410ef5b6-5aa0-5a7d-9657-2ab21d2b48d3'}, {'abstract': 'The Pentagon’s leadership is expected to tell President Obama as early as Friday that the armed forces are now trained and ready to implement a repeal of the military’s ban on openly gay troops, officials disclosed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/us/22brfs-Military.html', 'snippet': 'The Pentagon’s leadership is expected to tell President Obama as early as Friday that the armed forces are now trained and ready to implement a repeal of the military’s ban on openly gay troops, officials disclosed.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Pentagon’s leadership is expected to tell President Obama as early as Friday that the armed forces are now trained and ready to implement a repeal of the military’s ban on openly gay troops, officials disclosed. A repeal of the “don’t ask, don’t tell” legislation was signed by Mr. Obama in December, and the Defense Department and the individual military services have been training their personnel in preparation for lifting the ban. But the defense secretary, Leon E. Panetta, and the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, Adm. Mike Mullen, are required to certify to the president that the military is ready to implement a repeal without jeopardizing national security. The president then must endorse that certification, with a repeal coming into force in 60 days. News that certification was expected Friday was first reported by The Wall Street Journal online. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Military Reportedly Set on Repeal of ‘Don’t Ask’', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Military Reportedly Set on Repeal of ‘Don’t Ask’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T04:57:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thom Shanker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shanker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e42c1371-6bd1-5343-9a71-be0426250c41', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e42c1371-6bd1-