-translation/', 'snippet': 'What’s that deal?', 'lead_paragraph': 'FT Alphaville summarizes the Greek deal; if you aren’t confused, you aren’t paying attention.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greek Translation', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T14:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f9cfb4c-47e4-5cd3-85cc-b1d6895db5a0', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f9cfb4c-47e4-5cd3-85cc-b1d6895db5a0'}, {'abstract': 'A video for Ms. Styrene’s song “Ghoulish,” inspired by coverage of Jackson at the end of his life, has been released following her death in April.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/poly-styrene-pays-a-posthumous-tribute-to-michael-jackson/', 'snippet': 'A video for Ms. Styrene’s song “Ghoulish,” inspired by coverage of Jackson at the end of his life, has been released following her death in April.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As far as posthumous tributes go, this one is a double rarity. Before she died in April, Poly Styrene, the leader of the pioneering punk act X-Ray Spex, recorded a song inspired by the death of another seminal performer: Michael Jackson.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poly Styrene Pays a Posthumous Tribute to Michael Jackson', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recordings and Downloads (Video)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poly Styrene', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T14:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6905b6f8-83d8-5298-9100-bca897b6743a', 'word_count': 339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6905b6f8-83d8-5298-9100-bca897b6743a'}, {'abstract': 'T Magazine introduces the countdown to it’s 100th issue with a series of made-for-web original model T’s.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/art-for-ts-sake/', 'snippet': 'T Magazine introduces the countdown to it’s 100th issue with a series of made-for-web original model T’s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For every print edition of T, we ask an artist to reimagine our logo, and that original artwork opens the feature pages of the magazine. We’ve hosted the pyrotechnically inclined, the ephemera-minded, a chocolatier and a fashion designer or two you might have heard of. We’re always surprised and amazed by what comes in, and these stabs of pure visual pleasure have become one of our signatures. (We don’t like to play favorites with our editorial assignments … but this is one of our favorites.) To celebrate our 100th issue, which will be published on Aug. 21, we decided to commission a series of online-only versions. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Art for T’s Sake', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T14:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ethan Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ethan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname':