_paragraph': 'IN 1811 Uruguay began the long process of winning independence. In this bicentennial year, Montevideo, the capital, has been adding new museums and expanding older ones as the country re-examines art and history.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/travel/24HEADS_SPAN/24HEADS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/travel/24HEADS_SPAN/24HEADS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/travel/24HEADS_SPAN/24HEADS_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/travel/24HEADS_SPAN/24HEADS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/travel/24HEADS_SPAN/24HEADS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Uruguay, Prison Cells as Galleries', 'kicker': 'Heads Up', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Montevideo, Prison Cells as Galleries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montevideo (Uruguay)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:24:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael T. Luongo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Luongo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bc205cd-631a-5385-8db6-fdec23f79f94', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bc205cd-631a-5385-8db6-fdec23f79f94'}, {'abstract': 'Dance students invade the city for summer, taking classes in technique, repertory or choreography until dinnertime every day, in what turns out to be an extended audition for the major troupes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/arts/dance/ballet-and-modern-summer-classes-one-long-audition.html', 'snippet': 'Dance students invade the city for summer, taking classes in technique, repertory or choreography until dinnertime every day, in what turns out to be an extended audition for the major troupes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR 15-year-old Mikayla Lambert this summer hasn’t been what most girls her age would consider awesome. Instead of sleeping late, she wakes up each morning at 8:30. While her friends might spend their days outside relaxing by a pool, she stays inside for hours. She has a curfew.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24SUBINTENSIVES1/24SUBINTENSIVES1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24SUBINTENSIVES1/24SUBINTENSIVES1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24SUBINTENSIVES1/24SUBINTENSIVES1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'They’re Called Intensives for a Reason', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They&#8217;re Called Intensives For a Reason', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taylor, Paul, Dance Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Graham, Martha, Dance Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morris, Mark, Dance Group', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'School of American Ballet', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:29:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Milzoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Milzoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/405c0861-55eb-53f7-90bc-21093d5efe0a', 'word_count': 1117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/405c0861-55eb-53f7-90bc-21093d5efe0a'}, {'abstract': 'Dan Parent talks about the creation of Kevin Keller, the first openly gay resident of Riverdale.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/being-gay-in-riverdale-creator-of-archie-character-gives-the-backstory/', 'snippet': 'Dan Parent talks about the creation of Kevin Keller, the first openly gay resident of Riverdale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After the news this week that Kevin Keller, the only openly gay resident of Riverdale, the hometown of Archie Andrews, would be getting his own series we asked Dan Parent, who created Kevin, to answer a few questions by e-mail.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Being Gay in Riverdale: Creator of ‘Archie’ Character Gives the Backstory', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kubert, Joe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:30:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f305f901-e413-5b1a-8501-fcbe260faba0', 'word_count': 608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f305f901-e413-5b1a-8501-fcbe260faba0'}, {'abstract': 'From a strictly literary point of view, prison was the best thing that ever happened to the Marquis de Sade. Other writers should be so lucky.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/why-writers-belong-in-prison.html', 'snippet': 'From a strictly literary point of view, prison was the best thing that ever happened to the Marquis de Sade. Other writers should be so lucky.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While researching the Marquis de Sade several years ago, I came across an intriguing biographical tidbit: that crazed French libertine, whose string of luridly violent works gave rise to the term “sadism,” actually began his literary career as a travel writer. In 1775, Sade embarked on a yearlong grand tour of Italy, and wrote an enormous (and enormously tedious) manuscript about the journey entitled “Voyage d’Italie.” The rambling opus, filled with ruminations on Florentine museums and Neapolitan customs, was never completed. Sade’s attention wandered to more carnal pleasures, and in 1777, he was arrested for a long list of unsavory imbroglios, including one that historians call the Little Girls Episode. Sade was thrown into the prison of Vincennes, and would spend most of his remaining life incarcerated. “Voyage d’Italie” soon joined a range of half-finished manuscripts from his youth, scraps of verse and staid dramatic pieces, none of which Sade ever had the discipline to bring to fruition.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Perrotet/Perrotet-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 783, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Perrotet/Perrotet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Perrotet/Perrotet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Writers Belong Behind Bars', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Serving the Sentence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:30:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tony Perrottet', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perrottet', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d768e1a6-27b1-5e73-8fc6-e4862e6336e4', 'word_count': 1326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d768e1a6-27b1-5e73-8fc6-e4862e6336e4'}, {'abstract': 'Along with a focus on Sibelius, Bard SummerScape is including a little-produced Richard Strauss opera, “Die Liebe der Danae,” on its program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/arts/music/richard-strauss-is-surprise-guest-at-bard-summerscape.html', 'snippet': 'Along with a focus on Sibelius, Bard SummerScape is including a little-produced Richard Strauss opera, “Die Liebe der Danae,” on its program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOW, you may ask, does an opera by Richard Strauss find its way into a music festival devoted largely to the world of Jean Sibelius? This great Finnish composer is the focal point of this year’s SummerScape extravaganza now under way at Bard College in Annandale-on-Hudson, N.Y., and an important operatic novelty is invariably a highlight of the festival. But Sibelius never wrote an opera of any size or substance. To include one by Strauss — even a relative rarity like “Die Liebe der Danae,” which receives what is said to be its first fully staged performance in the New York region on Friday evening — seems a bit of a stretch.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24BARD2_span/24BARD2_span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24BARD2_span/24BARD2_span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24BARD2_span/24BARD2_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 771, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24BARD2_span/24BARD2_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24BARD2_span/24BARD2_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Strauss Joins Sibelius’s Vacation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strauss Joins Sibelius’s Vacation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bard College', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:31:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter G. Davis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Davis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fe1fbe4-6019-5b2d-b2af-8a34e3f79e97', 'word_count': 1465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fe1fbe4-6019-5b2d-b2af-8a34e3f79e97'}, {'abstract': 'Witnesses to the aftermath of an explosion in the Norwegian capital, Oslo, quickly uploaded footage to video-sharing sites.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/video-of-oslo-explosion-aftermath/', 'snippet': 'Witnesses to the aftermath of an explosion in the Norwegian capital, Oslo, quickly uploaded footage to video-sharing sites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This post was updated throughout the day, as more material became available.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video and Updates on Attacks in Norway', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives',