e Racing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALONSO, FERNANDO', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ecclestone, Bernie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosberg, Nico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumacher, Michael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vettel, Sebastian', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Webber, Mark', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CVC Capital Partners', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Formula One', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Red Bull', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WilliamsF1', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:32:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d929ffaa-cf78-5397-ad1a-76558f31ce13', 'word_count': 468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d929ffaa-cf78-5397-ad1a-76558f31ce13'}, {'abstract': 'Marc-André Hamelin finds the word “virtuoso,” which is invariably applied to his playing, a somewhat derogatory descriptive that implies mere showmanship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/arts/music/marc-andre-hamelin-at-mannes-college.html', 'snippet': 'Marc-André Hamelin finds the word “virtuoso,” which is invariably applied to his playing, a somewhat derogatory descriptive that implies mere showmanship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN Marc-André Hamelin gave a piano recital at Le Poisson Rouge in September, he displayed all the hallmarks of a first-rate artist: a stellar technique, poise and probing musicianship. He did so in a program consisting entirely of his own compositions, a rare feat in an era when the composer-pianist is an increasingly endangered species. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24MARC_span/24MARC_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24MARC_span/24MARC_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24MARC_span/24MARC_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24MARC_span/24MARC_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24MARC_span/24MARC_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'King of Virtuosos Is Weary Of His Crown', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'King of Virtuosos Is Weary Of His Crown', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mannes College of Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamelin, Marc-Andre', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:32:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2657c43c-c320-5344-a07e-a7155fd83956', 'word_count': 1468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2657c43c-c320-5344-a07e-a7155fd83956'}, {'abstract': 'Mystery novels by P. L. Gaus, Linda Castillo, Harry Dolan, Colin Cotterill and Lars Kepler.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/crime-mystery-novels-by-p-l-gaus-linda-castillo-harry-dolan-colin-cotterill-and-lars-kepler.html', 'snippet': 'Mystery novels by P. L. Gaus, Linda Castillo, Harry Dolan, Colin Cotterill and Lars Kepler.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once women got tired of being cast as helpless victims in crime novels and began running their own investigative agencies, authors had to scramble for other marginalized groups requiring the services of a detective hero. For more than a decade, P. L. Gaus has been writing quietly spellbinding mysteries about one such group, the conservative Old Order Amish of Holmes County, Ohio. Returning to the region in the seventh installment of his series, HARMLESS AS DOVES (Ohio University, $24.95), Gaus offers a sensitive account of the impact on this community when outsiders (that is, the cops) descend to deal with an Amish youth who has confessed to the murder of his fiancée’s older, richer and very persistent admirer.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Crime/Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 913, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prying Eyes', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prying Eyes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gaus, P L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMISH', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dolan, Harry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotterill, Colin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:34:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d84b8763-7bac-5590-8104-a80019cb530a', 'word_count': 893, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d84b8763-7bac-5590-8104-a80019cb530a'}, {'abstract': 'Beginning this week, Geoff Dyer will write a regular column for the Book Review, “Reading Life,” detailing the ups and down of his long relationship with the written word.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/up-front-introducing-geoff-dyers-new-column.html', 'snippet': 'Beginning this week, Geoff Dyer will write a regular column for the Book Review, “Reading Life,” detailing the ups and down of his long relationship with the written word.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Geoff Dyer, the author of uncategorizable books variously related to D. H. Lawrence, jazz, photography and India, has been strongly praised by his British contemporaries. According to Zadie Smith, “his prose is the equal of anyone in the country,” and James Wood refers to his work’s “louche and canny delight.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/upfront/upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 858, 'width': 563, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/upfront/upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/upfront/upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Introducing Geoff Dyer’s New Column', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dyer, Geoff', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:34:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03fde421-9c9d-5ac5-aaf0-ecf9235bb6ad', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03fde421-9c9d-5ac5-aaf0-ecf9235bb6ad'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Fried’s genius is to manage to tell you what he is not doing, what he has not done and what he is not going to do.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/an-academic-authors-unintentional-masterpiece.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Fried’s genius is to manage to tell you what he is not doing, what he has not done and what he is not going to do.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this column I want to look at a not uncommon way of writing and structuring books. This approach, I will argue, involves the writer announcing at the outset what he or she will be doing in the pages that follow. The default format of academic research papers and textbooks, it serves the dual purpose of enabling the reader to skip to the bits that are of particular interest and — in keeping with the prerogatives of scholarship — preventing an authorial personality from intruding on the material being presented. But what happens when this basically plodding method seeps so deeply into a writer’s makeup as to constitute a stylistic signature, even a kind of ongoing flourish or extravagance?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Dyer-1310417430031/Dyer-1310417430031-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 339, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Dyer-1310417430031/Dyer-1310417430031-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 339}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Dyer-1310417430031/Dyer-1310417430031-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 711, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Dyer-1310417430031/Dyer-1310417430031-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Dyer-1310417430031/Dyer-1310417430031-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Academic Author’s Unintentional Masterpiece', 'kicker': 'Reading Life', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not to Get Ahead Of Myself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fried, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:35:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Dyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e116fc7-189a-5521-b5bd-cd0c44ccd37c', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e116fc7-189a-5521-b5bd-cd0c44ccd37c'}, {'abstract': 'The MoMA meditates on personality in design, from the MetroCard vending machines to a billboard that derives messages from animal noises.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/now-showing-talk-to-me/', 'snippet': 'The MoMA meditates on personality in design, from the MetroCard vending machines to a billboard that derives messages from animal noises.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Talk to Me: Design and the Communication between People and Objects,” which opens on July 24 at the Museum of Modern Art, explores the ways in which people and objects communicate through design. In nearly 200 recent and new projects, the exhibition considers the “personalities” that designed objects now have through their interactive relationships with their users. Organized by Paola Antonelli, the senior curator in the museum’s department of architecture and design, and Kate Carmody, a curatorial assistant in the department, the exhibition examines everything from the now-famous MetroCard vending machines in New York to the Bat Billboard, which uses voice-recognition software to translate the calls of bats into messages that are displayed on the screen of the billboard. (The billboard itself also serves a low-tech function — that of housing the bats.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Showing | ‘Talk to Me’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:36:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6fe5f04-a5bd-5d51-a9c8-ded995534f63', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6fe5f04-a5bd-5d51-a9c8-ded995534f63'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s fiction list feature one out-of-nowhere literary sensation: S. J. Watson well-reviewed neuro-thriller, “Before I Go to Sleep.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'This week’s fiction list feature one out-of-nowhere literary sensation: S. J. Watson well-reviewed neuro-thriller, “Before I Go to Sleep.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'SLEEP, MEMORY: George R. R. Martin, as expected, claims the hardcover fiction throne with “A Dance With Dragons,” the fifth installment in his “Song of Ice and Fire” fantasy series.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31insidelist/31insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31insidelist/31insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:37:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c90decd-42dc-56be-a587-95a2ab676d7e', 'word_count': 571, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c90decd-42dc-56be-a587-95a2ab676d7e'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE ART OF CRUELTY: A Reckoning,  by Maggie Nelson (Norton, $24.95.) Nelson examines representations of violence in the media, but refreshingly she largely aims her laments high up the cultural ladder — at the fine arts, literature, theater and even poetry.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books Of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:37:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/04580a89-aba9-580c-b78b-62bb10800472', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04580a89-aba9-580c-b78b-62bb10800472'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BIG GIRLS DON’T CRY: The Election That Changed Everything for American Women,  by Rebecca Traister (Free Press, $15.) Traister, who covered the 2008 presidential campaign for Salon, analyzes it from a personal point of view, paying special attention to Hillary Clinton’s polarizing run, Michelle Obama’s potential as a first lady and Sarah Palin’s emergence, which “empowered Republican women eager to claim their share of the feminist legacy.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:37:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8e9a11a-46ec-5f80-9fa3-4462bc41d05b', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8e9a11a-46ec-5f80-9fa3-4462bc41d05b'}, {'abstract': 'The utility said it is ready to handle the load with overall system demand is on pace to break the all-time record today.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/con-ed-restores-full-power-to-queens/', 'snippet': 'The utility said it is ready to handle the load with overall system demand is on pace to break the all-time record today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 12:04 p.m. | Consolidated Edison restored full power to about 136,000 customers in Queens and Westchester County by noon Friday, several hours after reducing voltage to more than 100,000 households there overnight. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Con Ed Restores Full Power to Queens and Westchester', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consolidated Edison Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:41:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6622e998-796f-548c-9a80-a27327404f5c', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6622e998-796f-548c-9a80-a27327404f5c'}, {'abstract': 'A sense of workplace alienation permeates this first story collection, which explores the limits of social interaction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/orientation-by-daniel-orozco-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A sense of workplace alienation permeates this first story collection, which explores the limits of social interaction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The stories in Daniel Orozco’s debut collection convey a sense of workplace alienation that would make Karl Marx cringe. The opening lines of “Orientation,” the first story, place us squarely under the fluorescent lights of comically absurd employment: “Those are the offices and these are the cubicles. That’s my cubicle there, and this is your cubicle. This is your phone. Never answer your phone. Let the Voicemail System answer it. This is your Voicemail System Manual.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u