D’Andrea Tyson answer questions on the move to help Greece and save the euro — and the effects it may have, intended or otherwise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'European leaders reached an accord Thursday to reduce Greece’s debt burden and prevent a collapse of confidence that has threatened to engulf some of the region’s largest economies. Economix has asked three prominent economists to offer their views on the accord and the effects it may have, intended or otherwise, in Europe and the United States. They are:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Economist Q. & A. on Europe’s Debt Accord', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Euro (Currency)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:48:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By ECONOMIX EDITORS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Economix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'EDITORS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf5dcc95-eb33-5ff0-9090-d9dc51e64886', 'word_count': 2402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf5dcc95-eb33-5ff0-9090-d9dc51e64886'}, {'abstract': 'IN Vogue’s August “age issue,” the editors gush about the style proclivities of the teenage Fanning sisters, Dakota and Elle, who appear in a full-page photograph turned out in puff-sleeved glad rags from Louis Vuitton.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/several-sizes-too-small/', 'snippet': 'IN Vogue’s August “age issue,” the editors gush about the style proclivities of the teenage Fanning sisters, Dakota and Elle, who appear in a full-page photograph turned out in puff-sleeved glad rags from Louis Vuitton.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Vogue’s August “age issue,” the editors gush about the style proclivities of the teenage Fanning sisters, Dakota and Elle, who appear in a full-page photograph turned out in puff-sleeved glad rags from Louis Vuitton. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Several Sizes Too Small', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fanning, Dakota', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fanning, Elle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:53:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth La Ferla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Ferla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b0007d8-e861-512a-a8b1-eb839848b3a5', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b0007d8-e861-512a-a8b1-eb839848b3a5'}, {'abstract': 'Alchemy, gunplay and prospecting mix in this picaresque of the gold rush.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/the-sisters-brothers-by-patrick-dewitt-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Alchemy, gunplay and prospecting mix in this picaresque of the gold rush.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is 1851 in the Oregon Territory, and Charlie and Eli Sisters have been ordered by the Commodore to go to California and kill a man called Hermann Kermit Warm. They ride their horses, Nimble and Tub, and along the way meet “the weeping man” and a dentist, Reginald Watts. These characters and their names, not completely Dickensian, or even Pynchonian, but not exactly commonplace either, are emblematic of Patrick DeWitt’s novel “The Sisters Brothers” — not always serious, not always funny, sometimes derivative of old westerns, sometimes a parody of them.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/bkr-Vernon-t_CA0/bkr-Vernon-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/bkr-Vernon-t_CA0/bkr-Vernon-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/bkr-Vernon-t_CA0/bkr-Vernon-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Picaresque of the Gold Rush', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Troubled Shooters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:55:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Vernon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vernon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/68baed09-c2d6-5ebb-9ac9-9d428b05186e', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68baed09-c2d6-5ebb-9ac9-9d428b05186e'}, {'abstract': 'The Turkish club Besiktas, which recently signed the Nets’ Deron Williams, has made an offer to Lakers guard Kobe Bryant to play during the N.B.A. lockout, its coach said Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/23/sports/basketball/turkish-club-makes-offer-to-kobe-bryant.html', 'snippet': 'The Turkish club Besiktas, which recently signed the Nets’ Deron Williams, has made an offer to Lakers guard Kobe Bryant to play during the N.B.A. lockout, its coach said Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Turkish basketball club Besiktas has made an offer to Los Angeles Lakers guard Kobe Bryant to play for its team during the N.B.A. lockout and is awaiting an answer from Bryant’s agent, Besiktas Coach Ergin Ataman said Friday.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/sports/23bryant1/23bryant1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 771, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/sports/23bryant1/23bryant1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/23/sports/23bryant1/23bryant1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkish Club Seeks to Add Another Star: Kobe Bryant', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turkish Club Seeks to Add Kobe Bryant', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bryant, Kobe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Besiktas JK', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:56:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8346fff-c41f-542c-90bd-546a2b955e7c', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8346fff-c41f-542c-90bd-546a2b955e7c'}, {'abstract': 'The media are saying the video of Arnaud Lagardère, the French billionaire, and his girlfriend is an embarrassment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/23/business/global/heir-apparent-at-eads-appears-in-internet-video.html', 'snippet': 'The media are saying the video of Arnaud Lagardère, the French billionaire, and his girlfriend is an embarrassment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Arnaud Lagardère, the French billionaire who has been widely expected to become the chairman of the European aerospace group EADS next year, became an unwitting Internet sensation this past week after a Belgian magazine released a video of him posing and cuddling with his young, supermodel girlfriend.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/business/global/23eads-inline/23eads-inline-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 520, 'width': 645, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/business/global/23eads-inline/23eads-inline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/23/business/global/23eads-inline/23eads-inline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Heir Apparent at EADS Stirs Controversy With Web Video', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Heir Apparent at EADS Stirs Controversy With Web Video', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Aeronautic Defense and Space Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:57:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicola Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicola', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3f2855c-eeb2-5731-a113-77a03616a422', 'word_count': 634, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3f2855c-eeb2-5731-a113-77a03616a422'}, {'abstract': 'The Barbizon, the 23-story brick building on the corner of 63rd Street and Lexington Avenue, is up for consideration as a landmark.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/big-deal-landmark-status-weighed-for-barbizon/', 'snippet': 'The Barbizon, the 23-story brick building on the corner of 63rd Street and Lexington Avenue, is up for consideration as a landmark.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Barbizon, the 23-story brick building on the corner of 63rd Street and Lexington Avenue, has been reinvented several times over the last century, and the next chapter may be written by the city’s Landmarks Preservation Commission. At a city hearing scheduled for Tuesday, the building is up for consideration as a landmark.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Deal | Landmark Status Weighed for Barbizon', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barbizon Hotel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T15:58:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kershaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/322bda10-20af-54bd-90ad-32a045ef1c29', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/322bda10-20af-54bd-90ad-32a045ef1c29'}, {'abstract': '“Life in a Day” features montages of ordinary acts from YouTube videos submitted by thousands of people who filmed themselves on July 24, 2010. Watch six clips from the 95-minute documentary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/24/magazine/dayinthelife.html', 'snippet': '“Life in a Day” features montages of ordinary acts from YouTube videos submitted by thousands of people who filmed themselves on July 24, 2010. Watch six clips from the 95-minute documentary.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Life in a Day” features montages of ordinary acts from YouTube videos submitted by thousands of people who filmed themselves on July 24, 2010. Watch six clips from the 95-minute documentary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Around the World in One Day', 'kicker': 'Undertakings', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/84fbc7ca-556a-5129-afc7-2e537e3d04d0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/84fbc7ca-556a-5129-afc7-2e537e3d04d0'}, {'abstract': 'South Africans believed the World Cup would bump their country into the first world. So did it?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/magazine/you-are-here-overtime-in-soccer-city.html', 'snippet': 'South Africans believed the World Cup would bump their country into the first world. So did it?', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the second Sunday in July, exactly one year after the final game of the 2010 World Cup was played here at Johannesburg’s Soccer City stadium, the cappuccino machine inside the V.I.P. suite was firing up. A bottle of wine cooled in the fridge across the bar. In the stadium’s bathrooms, fresh liners were tucked into the wastebaskets and the soap dispensers were refilled to the brim, as if the 90,000 fans that flooded the venue in 2010 might reappear at any moment. Ephraim Nong, a stadium tour guide, showed me the pitch from the V.I.P. viewing deck. It is maintained religiously, he said: cut three times a week, the grass in shadow artificially sunned using giant lamps hung on wheeled racks. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24here_map/24here_map-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 911, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24here_map/24here_map-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24here_map/24here_map-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Overtime in Soccer City', 'kicker': 'You Are Here', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Overtime in Soccer City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Johannesburg (South Africa)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2010 (Soccer)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eve Fairbanks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fairbanks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/948dff56-9117-55b4-b016-caa8ca8c5d78', 'word_count': 801, 'uri': 'nyt://article/948dff56-9117-55b4-b016-caa8ca8c5d78'}, {'abstract': 'How more than 80,000 videos and 4,500 hours of raw footage turned into one unexpectedly emotional 95-minute movie.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/magazine/around-the-world-in-one-day-on-youtube.html', 'snippet': 'How more than 80,000 videos and 4,500 hours of raw footage turned into one unexpectedly emotional 95-minute movie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How exactly do you make a documentary about a day in the life of a planet — about how people all over the world eat, sleep, pray, work and even learn to shave? You could start by asking everyone on YouTube to film their daily activities on a specific date; in this case, on July 24, 2010. Then you could ask all those people to send you their raw footage; in this case, 4,500 hours worth.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24lifeday_span/24lifeday_span-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24lifeday_span/24lifeday_span-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24lifeday_span/24lifeday_span-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24lifeday_span/24lifeday_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24lifeday_span/24lifeday_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Around the World in One Day', 'kicker': 'Undertakings', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Around the World in One Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Sternbergh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sternbergh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25710092-9fc5-56e0-acee-69587a15ff5f', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25710092-9fc5-56e0-acee-69587a15ff5f'}, {'abstract': 'The voice on the other end wanted more than friendship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/magazine/lives-the-cold-call.html', 'snippet': 'The voice on the other end wanted more than friendship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He wanted to meet inside the men’s restroom in the undergraduate library. I said, “No, outside, by the reference books.” When he asked how he’d know me, I told him I’d be wearing a baseball cap and a Marilyn Monroe T-shirt. I didn’t mention that I was a scrawny 115 pounds or that my face was ravaged by acne. And I didn’t mention that the hair beneath the baseball cap was a newly dyed neon orange.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24lives/24lives-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24lives/24lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24lives/24lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Cold Call', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Cold Call', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rahul Mehta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rahul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mehta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fbab6af-4a8c-5e7c-b35f-db28d9084cf0', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fbab6af-4a8c-5e7c-b35f-db28d9084cf0'}, {'abstract': 'The famed Princeton professor explains his very big problems with Obama.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/magazine/talk-cornel-west.html', 'snippet': 'The famed Princeton professor explains his very big problems with Obama.', 'lead_paragraph': ' What’s with the black suit, white shirt, black tie outfit you always wear? Do you have anything else in your closet?  I’ve got four black suits that I circulate, and they are my cemetery clothes — my uniform that keeps me ready for battle. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24talk/24talk-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 447, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24talk/24talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24talk/24talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cornel West Flunks the President', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cornel West Flunks the President', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86fe4ccb-48e5-5be9-810e-543729eea9c2', 'word_count': 735, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86fe4ccb-48e5-5be9-810e-543729eea9c2'}, {'abstract': 'Sleeping on it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/magazine/the-ethicist-dont-ask-do-tell.html', 'snippet': 'Sleeping on it.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A woman I was sleeping with, in an explicitly nonexclusive, “don’t ask, don’t tell” relationship, found a pimple and panicked that she had a sexually transmitted disease. I encouraged her to let a doctor assuage her fears, which he did the next morning, but first she insisted I tell her if I’d slept with anyone else. I had. (Safe sex.) But I worried a “yes” might end the relationship; saying nothing would have been the same as saying yes. So I lied. Was I ethically obligated to respond honestly? NAME WITHHELD, NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Ask, Do Tell?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t Ask, Do Tell?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/66c79f1c-553f-5893-9443-148a695f3167', 'word_count': 526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66c79f1c-553f-5893-9443-148a695f3167'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/magazine/reply-all-a-regulator-scorned.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I first met Sheila Bair when she worked for Senator Bob Dole on his Senate finance committee staff; Joe Nocera’s article demonstrates what an outstanding public servant she has been as the former head of the F.D.I.C. She will probably hate me for saying this, but President Obama need look no further than Sheila as the successor to Treasury Secretary Tim Geithner. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Regulator Scorned', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Regulator Scorned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/8abfe75f-ff03-589b-a0de-671ea2b17b89', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8abfe75f-ff03-589b-a0de-671ea2b17b89'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/magazine/reply-all-the-dark-art-of-breaking-bad.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is a great program, unique among television series for its implacable moral ambiguities. I don’t blame Walt, then I do, then I don’t. The inscrutable morality in the program is cathartic for me, and it’s a formidable artistic achievement to keep the totally metaphysical conflict going. But I found it curious in David Segal’s article that Vince Gilligan, the show’s creator, said that he could not understand why someone would chose virtue in an absurd universe. The answer to this question is easily taken from Camus’s “Plague” and any number of existentialist texts. I think Gilligan is putting us on. In fact, when the debris from the exploded airplane falls into Walt’s pool, I thought the author’s intention was to tip his hand and justify the need for a social contract. (This scene would have made a good ending for the series.)', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dark Art of ‘Breaking Bad’', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dark Art Of ‘Breaking Bad’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/8eb8e5dd-0ab7-5c66-949b-b1e12ecd9de0', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8eb8e5dd-0ab7-5c66-949b-b1e12ecd9de0'}, {'abstract': 'Thoughts on “Trollhunter,” the faux documentary from Norwegian filmmaker Andre Ovredal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/magazine/bonus-feature-the-enthusiast-trollhunter.html', 'snippet': 'Thoughts on “Trollhunter,” the faux documentary from Norwegian filmmaker Andre Ovredal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Enthusiast: Tony Gervino, contributing editor, The New York Times Magazine', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '45', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Enthusiast', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bonus Feature | The Enthusiast', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ovredal, Andre', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norway', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b23a53e7-fc0f-52d1-946f-ef694a8daf35', 'word_count': 183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b23a53e7-fc0f-52d1-946f-ef694a8daf35'}, {'abstract': 'Since HBO’s “Entourage” made its debut seven years ago, our attitudes toward celebrities have totally changed. The characters on “Entourage” totally haven’t.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/magazine/riff-the-boys-of-entourage-will-always-be-boys.html', 'snippet': 'Since HBO’s “Entourage” made its debut seven years ago, our attitudes toward celebrities have totally changed. The characters on “Entourage” totally haven’t.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harriet Daimler, the protagonist of Iris Owens’s 1973 novel, “After Claude,” has a knack for eviscerating self-satisfied urban types. After rolling her eyes at a slender girl passing a joint (“ ‘I’m wrecked,’ she bragged, as though it took a special talent to get stoned”), Harriet utters a relatable prayer: “Lord, spare me these dimpled darlings who are always congratulating themselves for not having any thoughts or feelings.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24riff_span/24riff_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 103, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24riff_span/24riff_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 103}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/magazine/24riff_span/mag-24Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 210, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'j