ss/global/china-mobile-executive-sentenced-to-death-over-bribes.html', 'snippet': 'Zhang Chunjiang, the former vice chairman of China Mobile, was sentenced to death with a two-year reprieve, giving him the possibility of life in prison for good behavior.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SHANGHAI — A former executive at China Mobile, one of this country’s biggest state-owned telecommunications companies, was sentenced to death with a two-year reprieve Friday for accepting bribes, according to Xinhua, the state-run news agency.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/business/Mobile/Mobile-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 820, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/business/Mobile/Mobile-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/23/business/Mobile/Mobile-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Former China Mobile Official Sentenced in Bribery Case', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Former China Mobile Official Sentenced in Bribery Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'China Mobile Ltd', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:18:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Barboza', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barboza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1110954d-4b9c-5bf6-9687-f44e9eefb173', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1110954d-4b9c-5bf6-9687-f44e9eefb173'}, {'abstract': 'Filandia, Finland, Brooklyn, Manhattan, Finntown, Midtown: distinctions without apparent difference to the Department of Transportation workers responsible for misspelling a new street sign and then installing it in the wrong borough.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/you-say-filandia-i-say-freedonia/', 'snippet': 'Filandia, Finland, Brooklyn, Manhattan, Finntown, Midtown: distinctions without apparent difference to the Department of Transportation workers responsible for misspelling a new street sign and then installing it in the wrong borough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Filandia is a town set high in the mountain slopes of the Cordillera Central range in Colombia. It is not connected with Finland, a European country that is much larger and considerably farther north, and has a different flag.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'You Say Filandia, I Say Freedonia', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Correction Stories', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'SUNSET PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:23:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7d1960a-8d6c-59fc-80e3-6ae609632fbd', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7d1960a-8d6c-59fc-80e3-6ae609632fbd'}, {'abstract': 'Bonnie Jo Campbell’s solitary, sharpshooting heroine fends for herself in rural Michigan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/once-upon-a-river-by-bonnie-jo-campbell-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Bonnie Jo Campbell’s solitary, sharpshooting heroine fends for herself in rural Michigan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bonnie Jo Campbell’s first novel, “Q Road,” published in 2002, told the story of Rachel Crane, an isolated and determined young woman who lives in meth country outside Kalamazoo, Mich., with her even more eccentric mother, Margo. After Margo shoots a local womanizer who has designs on the under-age Rachel, she disappears, and Rachel, more out of practicality than love, marries the farmer who owns the property where she and her mother have always lived. “Q Road” was widely praised for its authentic rendering of rural Michigan: part dump, part wilderness and part farmland. “Once Upon a River,” Campbell’s second novel, leaves off where “Q Road” began — it is the story of Margo Crane, herself a difficult and willful young woman, who is 15 when the novel opens in the late 1970s.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/bkr-Smiley-t_CA0/bkr-Smiley-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 824, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/bkr-Smiley-t_CA0/bkr-Smiley-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/bkr-Smiley-t_CA0/bkr-Smiley-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bonnie Jo Campbell’s Rural Michigan Gothic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Survival Skills', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Michigan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campbell, Bonnie Jo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:27:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Smiley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smiley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4917fc02-6737-5685-9697-8a2cc3cace79', 'word_count': 1320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4917fc02-6737-5685-9697-8a2cc3cace79'}, {'abstract': 'On Friday, Newark set a record for temperature, at 108 degrees. New York City wasn’t far behind, at 104.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/23/nyregion/heat-wave-envelops-the-northeast.html', 'snippet': 'On Friday, Newark set a record for temperature, at 108 degrees. New York City wasn’t far behind, at 104.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The temperature climbed to 104 in New York City — 2 degrees shy of the city’s record — and 108 in Newark, the highest temperature ever recorded there, as a heat wave around the region intensified on Friday afternoon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/nyregion/23heat/23heat-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20