bitions at Comic-Con', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic-Con (Convention)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tull, Thomas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Legendary Entertainment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Legendary Pictures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Warner Brothers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:32:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cb3b1b6-e7bc-56e3-ac6d-a95ddfe33089', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cb3b1b6-e7bc-56e3-ac6d-a95ddfe33089'}, {'abstract': 'Lucian Freud always understood the uses of mystery, for aiding his reputation and saving it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/23/arts/design/lucian-freud-painter-and-provocateur-appraisal.html', 'snippet': 'Lucian Freud always understood the uses of mystery, for aiding his reputation and saving it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He was the most interesting man in London, conventional wisdom had it. I don’t think I ever met anyone more remarkable. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/arts/23freudcap/23freudcap-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/23/arts/23freudcap/23freudcap-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/arts/23freudcap/23freudcap-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/arts/23freudcap/23freudcap-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/23/arts/23freudcap/23freudcap-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Painter and Provocateur, Set in His Ways', 'kicker': 'An Appraisal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Painter and Provocateur, Set in His Ways', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freud, Lucian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Portrait Gallery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:33:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kimmelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kimmelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'An Appraisal', '_id': 'nyt://article/18c556cf-9226-569e-9284-0a19b663ea0b', 'word_count': 1222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18c556cf-9226-569e-9284-0a19b663ea0b'}, {'abstract': 'The last time it was this hot in New York City, Bucky Dent played shortstop for the Yankees and the Son of Sam stalked the streets.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/101-degrees-in-central-park-tying-record/', 'snippet': 'The last time it was this hot in New York City, Bucky Dent played shortstop for the Yankees and the Son of Sam stalked the streets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 3:15 p.m. | The mercury reached 104 degrees in Central Park at 2:10 p.m. on Friday, the hottest New York City has been since July 21, 1977, and only two degrees shy of the hottest temperature ever officially recorded in the city, the National Weather Service said. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '104 Degrees in Central Park and Power Use Set Records', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Records and Achievements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Weather Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CENTRAL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:35:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman and Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1995739-1cc0-5eb6-aa5d-35a2756627eb', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1995739-1cc0-5eb6-aa5d-35a2756627eb'}, {'abstract': 'Fake Apple stores in China ended up all over the news this week. Now the stores are dealing with angry customers and even a Taiwanese cartoon news video.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/fake-apple-stores-get-fake-news-video/', 'snippet': 'Fake Apple stores in China ended up all over the news this week. Now the stores are dealing with angry customers and even a Taiwanese cartoon news video.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this week I wrote about a series of fake Apple Stores that have been cropping up in China. They look so real that even the store employees, who wear authentic Apple T-shirts and branded name-tags, believe they work for the real Apple.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fake Apple Stores Get Fake News Video', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kunming (China)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:41:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df4577be-99aa-567a-a8eb-97d7da98854b', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df4577be-99aa-567a-a8eb-97d7da98854b'}, {'abstract': 'Some potential fantasy football breakout and sleeper candidates to keep an eye on for the coming season.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/fantasy-football-breakouts-and-sleepers/', 'snippet': 'Some potential fantasy football breakout and sleeper candidates to keep an eye on for the coming season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jason and Justin Sablich are here to help you with your fantasy football draft. The Sablich brothers will provide fantasy football advice throughout the season on this blog and on Twitter (@5thDownFantasy).  ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantasy Football: Breakouts and Sleepers', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fantasy Sports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradford, Sam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greene, Shonn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manningham, Mario', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mathews, Ryan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nelson, Jordy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tebow, Tim', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:46:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Sablich and Justin Sablich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f375d9a1-97f8-5aed-8529-e425f0649279', 'word_count': 1066, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f375d9a1-97f8-5aed-8529-e425f0649279'}, {'abstract': 'Eleven essays encompass the entire career of the historian Gordon S. Wood, whose work re-envisioned the American Revolution and, unusually, has appealed to readers all across the political spectrum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/the-idea-of-america-by-gordon-s-wood-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Eleven essays encompass the entire career of the historian Gordon S. Wood, whose work re-envisioned the American Revolution and, unusually, has appealed to readers all across the political spectrum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gordon S. Wood is more than an American historian. He is almost an American institution. Of all the many teachers and writers of history in this Republic, few are held in such high esteem. Part of his reputation rises from his productivity — a stream of books, monographs, articles, lectures and commentary. Now he has added “The Idea of America” (along with a new edition of John Adams’s Revolutionary writings in two volumes for the Library of America series).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/24fischer/24fischer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 827, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/24fischer/24fischer-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/24fischer/24fischer-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gordon S. Wood, Historian of the American Revolution', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Revolution Re-envisioned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Revolutionary War, American (1775-83)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wood, Gordon S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:51:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Hackett Fischer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'Hackett', 'lastname': 'Fischer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a9b1b3a-a8d3-550c-8565-0680cff3bc16', 'word_count': 1717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a9b1b3a-a8d3-550c-8565-0680cff3bc16'}, {'abstract': 'One of them was more conservative than the other.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/obama-nixon/', 'snippet': 'One of them was more conservative than the other.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bruce Bartlett says what you’re not supposed to say: Obama has governed as a moderate conservative, somewhat to the right of Richard Nixon. The frothing-at-the-mouth comments are an extra bonus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama / Nixon', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:55:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ab47e47-2aa9-5c08-93d5-888723e5170f', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ab47e47-2aa9-5c08-93d5-888723e5170f'}, {'abstract': 'The billionaire investor Carl C. Icahn had some advice for the cellphone unit, which was spun off earlier this year as an independent company: sell your patents.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/the-icahn-effect-motorola-edition/', 'snippet': 'The billionaire investor Carl C. Icahn had some advice for the cellphone unit, which was spun off earlier this year as an independent company: sell your patents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Will Motorola Mobility feel the Icahn effect? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Icahn Effect, Motorola Edition', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Icahn, Carl C', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Motorola Mobility Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nortel Networks Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:57:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/725e4832-238c-511d-9d35-7237ee6f5ea4', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/725e4832-238c-511d-9d35-7237ee6f5ea4'}, {'abstract': 'A letter conveys the concerns of the domestic automakers, which contend that they are at a disadvantage because Asian and European manufacturers depend less on sales of trucks, crossovers and minivans and can more easily meet the government targets.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/michigan-lawmakers-call-auto-mileage-proposals-overly-aggressive/', 'snippet': 'A letter conveys the concerns of the domestic automakers, which contend that they are at a disadvantage because Asian and European manufacturers depend less on sales of trucks, crossovers and minivans and can more easily meet the government target...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michigan’s congressional delegation has warned in a letter to the White House that new automobile fuel economy and emissions standards being drafted by the Obama administration are “not reasonably feasible” given the state of current technology and consumer buying habits.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Michigan Lawmakers Call Auto Mileage Proposals ‘Overly Aggressive’', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Becker, Daniel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Conyers, John Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Bob', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Upton, Fred', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Michigan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T16:59:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c26442aa-35b7-57c1-bb8a-ddf67e05870d', 'word_count': 1060, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c26442aa-35b7-57c1-bb8a-ddf67e05870d'}, {'abstract': 'This novel’s hero, a World War II crash survivor, sets out to find the people who risked their lives to help him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/the-girl-in-the-blue-beret-by-bobbie-ann-mason-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'This novel’s hero, a World War II crash survivor, sets out to find the people who risked their lives to help him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bobbie Ann Mason’s new novel opens with a little history. A note explains that “during World War II, thousands of Allied aviators crashed or parachuted into Occupied Europe,” and many were helped home by the “escape-and-evasion networks” of the French Resistance. This information is followed by the roster of a flight crew based at Molesworth Airfield in England, home of the 303rd Bomb Group — specifically the men who took one Flying Fortress, the Dirty Lily, on a raid over Frankfurt on Jan. 31, 1944. This squadron did indeed bomb this German city from this airfield, but not on this night. And the names of the crew are fictitious. “The Girl in the Blue Beret” is a work in which the real and the imaginary are joined. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/SWIFT/SWIFT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 357, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/SWIFT/SWIFT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 357}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/SWIFT/SWIFT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 610, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/SWIFT/SWIFT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/SWIFT/SWIFT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A World War II Veteran Revisits His Saviors', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Return Flight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mason, Bobbie Ann', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T17:02:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Swift', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swift', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/82c64c92-dde0-5583-998d-41250f116ace', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82c64c92-dde0-5583-998d-41250f116ace'}, {'abstract': 'The author of “Acceptance,” on the importance of maintaining perspective in the college search.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/slow-down-and-savor/', 'snippet': 'The author of “Acceptance,” on the importance of maintaining perspective in the college search.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dave Marcus, the author of “Acceptance: A Legendary Guidance Counselor Helps Seven Kids Find the Right Colleges — and Find Themselves,” is a reliable source of measured advice on the college admissions process and on the importance of keeping it in the proper perspective. (Regular readers of The Choice know this first-hand from his posts for us, including one last year about a son who marches to the beat of a different educational drummer, which prompted several hundred comments.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Slow Down and Savor Middle and High School’', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T17:05:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacques Steinberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacques', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c5e26dd-bb3c-5163-9c69-13a369939e30', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c5e26dd-bb3c-5163-9c69-13a369939e30'}, {'abstract': 'Guyana, Suriname and French Guiana, a tangled green knot of jungle, rock and savannah, are vividly described in this travelogue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/wild-coast-by-john-gimlette-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Guyana, Suriname and French Guiana, a tangled green knot of jungle, rock and savannah, are vividly described in this travelogue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have you ever considered visiting the Guianas, that fabled South American realm of gold and sugar, home to, as the British travel writer John Gimlette reports,“head-crushing jaguars, strangling snakes, rivers of stingrays and electric eels,” not to mention giant otters, “half-puppy, half-torpedo,” that snack on piranhas? The region’s tangled green knot of jungle, rock and savannah — now home to the countries of Guyana, Suriname and French Guiana — crouches between Venezuela and Brazil, veined by thousands of turbulent rivers, mulchily recoiling from 900 miles of Atlantic coastline. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Schillinger-1310213864381/Schillinger-1310213864381-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 729, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Schillinger-1310213864381/Schillinger-1310213864381-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Schillinger-1310213864381/Schillinger-1310213864381-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Travels North of the Amazon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Flora and Fantasy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South America', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gimlette, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'French Guiana', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Suriname', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guyana', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T17:06:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/140d51df-f5a0-5c68-af1a-440632190890', 'word_count': 1233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/140d51df-f5a0-5c68-af1a-440632190890'}, {'abstract': 'N.F.L. owners passed a proposed collective bargaining agreement, 31 to 0 with one abstention, but some sportswriters felt the move was merely gamesmanship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/23/sports/nfl-owners-vote-puts-pressure-on-players.html', 'snippet': 'N.F.L. owners passed a proposed collective bargaining agreement, 31 to 0 with one abstention, but some sportswriters felt the move was merely gamesmanship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a way, you do have to hand it to the N.F.L owners. Once they set sail on the Good Ship Lockout, they have been all in. Never mind that it was an obvious ploy to fatten their bottom lines, to grab more of the league’s $9 billion in revenue and take advantage of a players union it believed would fold like a $2 umbrella. And now that the players have shown to be more in the $20 umbrella neighborhood, the owners rolled out their latest strategy, ratifying a collective bargaining agreement the players hadn’t agreed to and telling them they’re the ones holding up the season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.F.L. Owners’ Vote Puts Pressure on Players', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'N.F.L. Owners’ Vote Puts Pressure on Players', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League Players Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodell, Roger', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Deron', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mozgov, Timofey', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bryant, Kobe', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Adam', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beltran, Carlos', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Marion', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T17:08:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cccd5b5-6c42-5006-8fff-006698bcc052', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cccd5b5-6c42-5006-8fff-006698bcc052'}, {'abstract': 'Recent budget cuts make us more vulnerable to nuclear terrorism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/23/opinion/23Luongo-Brill.html', 'snippet': 'Recent budget cuts make us more vulnerable to nuclear terrorism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Washington', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Budget Cuts to Dirty Bombs', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Budget Cuts to Dirty Bombs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radiation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Security and Warning Systems', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T17:13:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenneth N. Luongo and Kenneth C. Brill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenneth', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Luongo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Kenneth', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Brill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fdb218c-65e5-5e88-b599-989e290c150e', 'word_count': 764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fdb218c-65e5-5e88-b599-989e290c150e'}, {'abstract': 'Caitlin Horrocks’s sharp, rugged-hearted fictions share one consuming fixation: We live in a world studded with cruelty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/this-is-not-your-city-by-caitlin-horrocks-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Caitlin Horrocks’s sharp, rugged-hearted fictions share one consuming fixation: We live in a world studded with cruelty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “At the Zoo,” one of the stories in Caitlin Horrocks’s impressively sharp first book, a little boy recalls his grandfather’s interpretation of mouse thoughts: “I’m small! I’m frightened! Oh no! An owl!” The boy thinks, “I’m sorry you’re small and frightened; we are the same.” But quickly, his mind shifts. “Not the same. I am much bigger than you. I could hurt you.” Still in day care, he’s already exhibiting this most Horrocksian trait, “excitement at the capacity for harm.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/ROMM/ROMM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 634, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/ROMM/ROMM-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/ROMM/ROMM-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stories of the Unkindness of Strangers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unkindness of Strangers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T17:13:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Romm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/319f44ba-5a12-51aa-bea3-ac8249fd289e', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/319f44ba-5a12-51aa-bea3-ac8249fd289e'}, {'abstract': 'Yuval Levin’s case for a small debt-ceiling deal.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/grand-bargains-pro-and-con/', 'snippet': 'Yuval Levin’s case for a small debt-ceiling deal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While we wait (and wait, and wait …) to find out if John Boehner and Barack Obama are about to snatch a significant deal from the jaws of a parliamentary maneuver, I recommend reading Yuval Levin’s powerful case against the sort of larger bargain that I’ve been suggesting conservatives should consider in these negotiations. The problem with the “big” deals that have been floated to date, Levin points out, is that none of them seriously address the long-term growth of Medicare spending: The entitlement reforms being considered, from retirement-age increases to modest means-testing, will generate only extremely modest savings when set against the projected cost explosion in the coming decades. And so it would be a mistake, he argues, for Republicans to make a significant concession on taxes without getting genuine structural reform to Medicare (along the lines of the Ryan plan, or the more centrist Rivlin-Domenici alternative) in return — a policy mistake, because it would concede too much for too little, and a political mistake, because calling something a “grand bargain” would leave the impression that our deficit problem has somehow been dealt with, when in fact this round of negotiations is just the opening act for a much larger and necessarily more controversial process of reform.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grand Bargains, Pro and Con', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T17:13:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/281e8f19-108c-5a25-b69d-45e0ebb0648b', 'word_count': 1030, 'uri': 'nyt://article/281e8f19-108c-5a25-b69d-45e0ebb0648b'}, {'abstract': 'For the characters in Adam Ross’s clever story collection, good intentions often go awry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/books/review/adam-rosss-unsettling-stories.html', 'snippet': 'For the characters in Adam Ross’s clever story collection, good intentions often go awry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adam Ross’s first novel, “Mr. Peanut,” which came out last year, showcased blazingly original work by a writer whose influences ranged from Raymond Chandler to Italo Calvino. His second book, a collection of stories, confirms the promise of his first. “Ladies and Gentlemen” is clever in all the right ways, even while paying homage to the most traditional of forms.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Bakopoulous/Bakopoulous-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 322, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Bakopoulous/Bakopoulous-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 322}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/books/review/Bakopoulous/Bakopoulous-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 549, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/