themselves enough time to study and drill. “This is not a test you can cram for,” says Lee Weiss, the director of graduate programs for Kaplan Test Prep.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tips on Turning Back the Clock', 'kicker': 'Continuing Ed | Testing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tips on Turning Back the Clock', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Schools and Students', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law School Admission Test (LSAT)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Management Admission Test (GMAT)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Record Examination (GRE)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T19:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathleen McElroy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathleen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McElroy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a460d54-6f38-593a-a58d-c9c673b9609d', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a460d54-6f38-593a-a58d-c9c673b9609d'}, {'abstract': 'Europeans are challenging the champion of the M.B.A., the U.S.A.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/education/edlife/edl-24europe-t.html', 'snippet': 'Europeans are challenging the champion of the M.B.A., the U.S.A.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Master of Business Administration began as a distinctly American creation, developed in response to the galloping industrial growth of the late 19th century and the new American phenomena to which it gave rise: the modern corporation, scientific marketing, mass advertising, investment banking. The professional manager was born and, to train him, the world’s first graduate school of management — at Dartmouth, in 1900. Eight years later, Harvard began offering what it claims was the world’s first M.B.A. And for the next century, the American academy was the uncontested capital of practical business education.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24europe-t_CA0/edl-24europe-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Business Worldview', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A New Worldview', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Business Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Schools and Students', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T19:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Sayare and Steven Erlanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sayare', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69d0e077-3b97-5855-9432-d76bfd0b0b34', 'word_count': 1957, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69d0e077-3b97-5855-9432-d76bfd0b0b34'}, {'abstract': 'Preparing for the G.R.E., LSAT, et al., is tough when class is a distant memory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/education/edlife/edl-24continuinged-t.html', 'snippet': 'Preparing for the G.R.E., LSAT, et al., is tough when class is a distant memory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the first day of test prep, after we had tackled a mini-G.R.E., the instructor assured our class that though she was 30 years old, she was still up on the latest test-taking trends. Finally, I thought, a problem I could easily solve. Answer: the last math class I had taken was three years before she was born.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24contedBOX-t_CA0/edl-24contedBOX-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24contedBOX-t_CA0/edl-24contedBOX-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24contedBOX-t_CA0/edl-24contedBOX-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Little Rusty?', 'kicker': 'Continuing Ed | Testing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Little Rusty?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Schools and Students', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Management Admission Test (GMAT)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law School Admission Test (LSAT)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Record Examination (GRE)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T19:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathleen McElroy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathleen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McElroy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a3ea03a-ddb0-517e-914f-bbebb7277c78', 'word_count': 1060, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a3ea03a-ddb0-517e-914f-bbebb7277c78'}, {'abstract': 'From Costa Rica to Cambridge and back in 72 hours.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/education/edlife/edl-24exemba-t.html', 'snippet': 'From Costa Rica to Cambridge and back in 72 hours.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To make class, Valeri Cordón gets up at 5:30 a.m. on a Thursday to catch a flight from Costa Rica to Boston. At 8:30 a.m. Friday, he’s in his seat at Building E62 of the M.I.T. Sloan School of Management in Cambridge, where he will pretty much stay put for two intensive days of business education. Sunday morning — 72 hours after leaving home — he flies back to his wife and three children, usually through Miami, Atlanta or Dallas.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24exemba-t_CA0/edl-24exemba-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 718, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24exemba-t_CA0/edl-24exemba-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24exemba-t_CA0/edl-24exemba-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Betting on an E.M.B.A.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Betting on an E.M.B.A.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Business Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Schools and Students', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T19:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geraldine Fabrikant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geraldine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabrikant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07da0da5-a003-54cb-aa8b-1bbc4af08a2e', 'word_count': 817, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07da0da5-a003-54cb-aa8b-1bbc4af08a2e'}, {'abstract': 'A Q. and A. with the new dean of Harvard Business School on education, leadership and character.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/education/edlife/edl-24Harvard-t.html', 'snippet': 'A Q. and A. with the new dean of Harvard Business School on education, leadership and character.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The pace of change in business is quickening, and business schools are scrambling to keep up. A number of prominent B-schools, the Wharton School of the University of Pennsylvania and Harvard Business School among them, have rethought their programs and are making changes to reflect the increasingly global scope of business and to improve students’ leadership and teamwork skills. Some schools are asking students to wrestle with questions about the ethical lapses that contributed to the financial crisis. Nitin Nohria, new dean of Harvard Business School and co-author of “Paths to Power: How Insiders and Outsiders Shaped American Business Leadership,” discussed the new courses, and the goals and thinking behind them.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24Harvard-t_CA0/edl-24Harvard-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24Harvard-t_CA0/edl-24Harvard-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24Harvard-t_CA0/edl-24Harvard-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking Ahead Behind the Ivy', 'kicker': 'Q & A With Nitin Nohria', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looking Ahead Behind the Ivy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Business Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Schools and Students', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard Business School', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T19:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Bryant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bryant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3fc8ec7-ac6d-5e70-950a-f703700f6845', 'word_count': 1181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3fc8ec7-ac6d-5e70-950a-f703700f6845'}, {'abstract': 'Building an industry out of the postgraduate plight.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/education/edlife/edl-24phd-t.html', 'snippet': 'Building an industry out of the postgraduate plight.', 'lead_paragraph': "Jorge Cham's comics started out as just another way for a grad student — himself — to procrastinate. Now he’s helping millions of others do the same.", 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24phd-t_CA0/edl-24phd-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 260, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24phd-t_CA0/edl-24phd-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 260}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24phd-t_CA0/edl-24phd-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24phd-t_CA0/edl-24phd-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/24/education/edl-24phd-t_CA0/edl-24phd-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Joke Is on the Ph.D.', 'kicker': 'Blackboard | PH. D. Funnies', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding the Humor In the Postgraduate Plight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Schools and Students', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T19:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jaywon Choe', 'person': [{'firstn