s', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6f306e9-af99-5f4d-a17f-c01f281a5528', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6f306e9-af99-5f4d-a17f-c01f281a5528'}, {'abstract': 'Jason Adasiewicz and Chris Dingman, vibraphonists, are both seekers and are both invading the space between free improvisation and the consensual mainstream jazz language.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/arts/music/the-vibraphonists-jason-adasiewicz-and-chris-dingman.html', 'snippet': 'Jason Adasiewicz and Chris Dingman, vibraphonists, are both seekers and are both invading the space between free improvisation and the consensual mainstream jazz language.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OVER the last year I thought a lot about two albums by young jazz musicians: “Sun Rooms,” by Jason Adasiewicz, and “Waking Dreams,” by Chris Dingman. Both are vibraphonists, Mr. Adasiewicz from Chicago, Mr. Dingman from New York. They’re both seekers, both invading the space between free improvisation and the consensual mainstream jazz language, both suggesting changes of style and even function in their instrument. ... Hold on. What? They’re both vibraphonists? ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24VIBE1_span/24VIBE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24VIBE1_span/24VIBE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/arts/24VIBE1_span/24VIBE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit'