adway, as a voice for those who are frightened and frustrated by terrorism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Danny Aiello, a big guy with a big New Yawk accent, is best known for roles like Sal, the boisterous pizzeria owner in Spike Lee’s 1989 film, “Do the Right Thing,” which won him an Oscar nomination. In the play “The Shoemaker,” which is now in previews and opens on Sunday at the Acorn Theater, Mr. Aiello is a different kind of New Yorker. A wounded immigrant, he seethes with rage and sadness in the title role of an Italian Jewish shoemaker named Giuseppe, holed up in his shop in Hell’s Kitchen on 9/11, trying to shut out the chaos outside and the memories of loss it ignites. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/arts/23AIELLO/23AIELLO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/arts/23AIELLO/23AIELLO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/23/arts/23AIELLO/23AIELLO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Emotional Guy, Speaking for Others', 'kicker': 'A Word With: Danny Aiello', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Emotional Guy, Speaking for Others', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AIELLO, DANNY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T23:02:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/04080557-8349-506b-9cc8-c024dc8258ac', 'word_count': 964, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04080557-8349-506b-9cc8-c024dc8258ac'}, {'abstract': 'For summer entertaining, tacos are fast, easy and tasty.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/22/a-taco-truck-in-your-kitchen/', 'snippet': 'For summer entertaining, tacos are fast, easy and tasty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A good taco is a wonderful thing, whether you get it through a stainless steel truck window or at a favorite taqueria. Or at your house. For summer entertaining, tacos are fast, easy, and mighty tasty, made from the simplest ingredients. Take a steaming corn tortilla, add a little sizzled spicy pork and splash of salsa. Now, open a cold beer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Taco Truck in Your Kitchen', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tacos', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-22T23:05:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Tanis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4453bc14-5090-5669-b414-b036ce445a9e', 'word_count': 116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4453bc14-5090-5669-b414-b036ce445a9e'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Pawlenty hits the ice after a week in which he has portrayed  himself in Iowa as a scrappy underdog, like the United States hockey team that defeated the Russians in the 1980 Olympics.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nyt