me” has become a popular term for the weather phenomenon tormenting the middle and eastern parts of the country, but where did it come from, and how accurate is it?', 'lead_paragraph': '“Everybody talks about the weather, but I guess TV meteorologists have to do it more cleverly,” said Jay Trobec, a weatherman at KELO-TV in Sioux Falls, S.D., as he sought to explain why the phrase “heat dome” is suddenly being heard and seen everywhere. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/us/DOME/DOME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 917, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/23/us/DOME/DOME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/23/us/DOME/DOME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Heat ... Wave? Bubble? Dome? Seeking an Apt Name as the Hot Days Pile Up', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Heat ... Wave? Bubble? Dome? Seeking an Apt Name as the Hot Days Pile Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Temperature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-23T00:59:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aab96107-cc76-5f73-bd8b-fb45430b0d81', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aab96107-cc76-5f73-bd8b-fb45430b0d81'}, {'abstract': 'On a night off, two restaurant servers check out Sud Vino & Cucina in Bedford-Stuyvesant, Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/nyregion/a-table-for-2-at-sud-in-bedford-stuyvesant.html', 'snippet': 'On a night off, two restaurant servers check out Sud Vino & Cucina in Bedford-Stuyvesant, Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A classic neighborhood spot in a neighborhood without many, Sud Vino & Cucina has already become the kind of place the locals root for after just four months. In a developing corner of Bedford-Stuyvesant, on Bedford Avenue just south of Lexington, the space hits many familiar notes: a long exposed brick wall functions as a rotating art gallery; Edison bulbs dangle from the ceiling on red cords; the bar is fashioned out of wood reclaimed from a Brooklyn water tower. But it has won ardent fans for its homey feeling (a painting of the restaurant’s name by the owner Giuseppe Di Franco’s daughter Jaiden, 7, hangs at the front of the room), its selection of about 20 wines and its menu of rustic Italian pastas, salads, antipasti and crostini. On Wednesday, two waiters on a culinary expedition were quickly won over.\xa0', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/nyregion/24TABLE_SPAN/24TABLE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/24/nyregion/24TABLE_SPAN/24TABLE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/24/nyregion/24TABLE_SPAN/24TABLE_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': 