': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-23T21:30:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/50194c8a-bd5b-5248-9abe-815755f524b9', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50194c8a-bd5b-5248-9abe-815755f524b9'}, {'abstract': 'The ban on gays serving in the military will soon be repealed, but same-sex couples are still waiting for their equal rights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/opinion/sunday/24sun2.html', 'snippet': 'The ban on gays serving in the military will soon be repealed, but same-sex couples are still waiting for their equal rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a profoundly important and deeply gratifying sight: Some of the top officers and civilian leaders of the United States military announcing at the Pentagon on Friday that they had, at last, given their approval to tearing down one of the last walls of official bigotry and discrimination in the United States.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Step Forward, More to Take', 'kicker': 'Editorial ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Step Forward, More to Take: Anti-gay discrimination in the military will end, but the federal ban on marriage persists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mullen, Michael G', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon V', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-23T21:30:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/42b758aa-1905-550b-85f0-98690e327649', 'word_count': 546, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42b758aa-1905-550b-85f0-98690e327649'}, {'abstract': 'Herman Cain stands out in the field of Republican presidential candidates by using religious bigotry to gain political traction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/opinion/sunday/24sun3.html', 'snippet': 'Herman Cain stands out in the field of Republican presidential candidates by using religious bigotry to gain political traction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among a dreary Republican field, Herman Cain stands out for using religious bigotry to gain political traction for his presidential ambitions. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Herman Cain’s Bigotry', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Herman Cain’s Bigotry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cain, Herman', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shariah (Islamic Law)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-23T21:30:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cd6343e-4d75-55cd-af45-c794154011e8', 'word_count': 397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cd6343e-4d75-55cd-af45-c794154011e8'}, {'abstract': 'A look at how the economy went from healthy surpluses at the end of the Clinton era to the current deficit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/opinion/sunday/24sun4.html', 'snippet': 'A look at how the economy went from healthy surpluses at the end of the Clinton era to the current deficit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With President Obama and Republican leaders calling for cutting the budget by trillions over the next 10 years, it is worth asking how we got here — from healthy surpluses at the end of the Clinton era, and the promise of future surpluses, to nine straight years of deficits, including the $1.3 trillion shortfall in 2010. The answer is largely the Bush-era tax cuts, war spending in Iraq and Afghanistan, and recessions.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Deficit Got This Big', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Deconstruction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How the Deficit Got This Big', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'va