n Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9206b79-8482-52b9-9c58-1fb73430e4bf', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9206b79-8482-52b9-9c58-1fb73430e4bf'}, {'abstract': 'Jim Kelly-Evans, 62, said he wanted to marry Dan Evans, 67, on the first day to make a statement: “We are still in a war.”', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/gay-marriage-after-a-battle-a-celebration/', 'snippet': 'Jim Kelly-Evans, 62, said he wanted to marry Dan Evans, 67, on the first day to make a statement: “We are still in a war.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the last weddings performed at Brookhaven Town Hall, on Long Island, was of Dan Evans, 67, and Jim Kelly (now Kelly-Evans), 62, who divide their time between the Fairmount section of Philadelphia and Cherry Grove, on Fire Island. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gay Marriage | After a Battle, a Celebration', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:00:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Angela Macropoulos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Angela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macropoulos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e2c2dad-de83-5e65-b6c2-8eb37827ccb0', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e2c2dad-de83-5e65-b6c2-8eb37827ccb0'}, {'abstract': 'Data will include the S.&P./Case-Shiller housing price index for May, new home sales for June and consumer confidence for July.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/business/looking-ahead-to-economic-reports-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Data will include the S.&P./Case-Shiller housing price index for May, new home sales for June and consumer confidence for July.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ECONOMIC REPORTS Data will include the S.& P./Case-Shiller housing price index for May; new home sales for June; consumer confidence for July (Tuesday); durable goods for June; the Federal Reserve’s beige book (Wednesday); weekly jobless claims and pending home sales for May (Thursday); second-quarter gross domestic product; the Chicago PMI and Thomson Reuters/University of Michigan consumer sentiment index for July (Friday).', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Economic Reports', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looking Ahead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:19:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/750a0725-e73f-56f5-8d1b-b91ec3d5e004', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/750a0725-e73f-56f5-8d1b-b91ec3d5e004'}, {'abstract': 'Must-have gadgets are causing a stir, with stores packed and inventory sparse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/technology/apple-sales-in-china-zoom-ahead-of-competitors.html', 'snippet': 'Must-have gadgets are causing a stir, with stores packed and inventory sparse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SHANGHAI — A year after opening a flagship store here, Apple has a problem that any retailer would envy: the huge 16,000-square-foot space is already too small.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/business/25apple1/25apple1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/business/25apple1/25apple1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/25/business/25apple1/25apple1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In China, Apple Finds a Sweet Spot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In China, Apple Finds Market’s Sweet Spot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:25:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Barboza', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barboza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5904bdf2-7cac-56ca-bcb8-8f3f5955956b', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5904bdf2-7cac-56ca-bcb8-8f3f5955956b'}, {'abstract': 'Third Point’s founder, Daniel S. Loeb, has again picked up his pen to criticize the White House’s leadership in the hedge fund’s latest letter to investors.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/in-epistle-writing-return-third-points-loeb-takes-swipe-at-obama/', 'snippet': 'Third Point’s founder, Daniel S. Loeb, has again picked up his pen to criticize the White House’s leadership in the hedge fund’s latest letter to investors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Daniel S. Loeb, the founder of Third Point, isn’t shy about speaking his mind. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Writing Again, Third Point’s Loeb Takes Swipe at Obama', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Loeb, Daniel S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:27:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31c5d88a-55c3-5cf5-8d20-7789cd94cbb3', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31c5d88a-55c3-5cf5-8d20-7789cd94cbb3'}, {'abstract': 'It’s some guys, not the market.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/moodys-blues-poor-standards-and-the-debt/', 'snippet': 'It’s some guys, not the market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The invaluable Mike Konczal tells us the truth about the rating agencies and public-sector debt: not only do they constantly make mistakes, they do so in a consistent direction. Namely, they hold public-sector borrowers to vastly higher standards than they hold private borrowers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Moody’s Blues, Poor Standards, and the Debt', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:27:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fd89c14-5ca9-5c6a-bf19-49a1008219da', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fd89c14-5ca9-5c6a-bf19-49a1008219da'}, {'abstract': 'The new social network makes us re-think, even briefly, what  online reality means.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/the-confidence-game-at-google/', 'snippet': 'The new social network makes us re-think, even briefly, what  online reality means.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s extremely satisfying having just 20 friends on the new Google+. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Confidence Game at Google+', 'kicker': 'Virginia Heffernan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:30:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Virginia Heffernan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Virginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heffernan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c54cd8c-d789-506a-b5e2-22f58cfc7b21', 'word_count': 1227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c54cd8c-d789-506a-b5e2-22f58cfc7b21'}, {'abstract': 'The Royal Shakespeare Company’s production of “The Winter’s Tale” strikes a fresh note of immediacy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/theater/reviews/the-winters-tale-at-park-avenue-armory-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Royal Shakespeare Company’s production of “The Winter’s Tale” strikes a fresh note of immediacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A thrill of true feeling runs through virtually every scene of the Royal Shakespeare Company’s production of “The Winter’s Tale” at the Park Avenue Armory. Scalding jealousy, hunger for revenge, young love in rapturous bloom, the soul-corroding sorrow of regret: all are evoked in the saturated colors that have sometimes been missing from the company’s New York repertory season this summer.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/WINTER/WINTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 369, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/WINTER/WINTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 369}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/WINTER/WINTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 662, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/WINTER/WINTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/WINTER/WINTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Cool Ferocity of a King Inflamed by Jealousy', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Winter's Tale'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Cool Ferocity of a King Inflamed by Jealousy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Shakespeare Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:33:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d47e61d-7491-5077-b89f-3d69c3544fca', 'word_count': 1248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d47e61d-7491-5077-b89f-3d69c3544fca'}, {'abstract': 'Dance is just one of the ingredients in Stacy Grossfield’s “Sugar doesn’t live here,” which is being performed at the Studio in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/arts/dance/stacy-grossfield-in-sugar-doesnt-live-here-review.html', 'snippet': 'Dance is just one of the ingredients in Stacy Grossfield’s “Sugar doesn’t live here,” which is being performed at the Studio in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Studio, as it’s called, is a rectangular room with a gray floor and a silver-tin ceiling. It is one of those anonymous places in Brooklyn — on an industrial block in Sunset Park — where dances are built and then delivered to a theater. For “Sugar doesn’t live here,” seen on Friday night, the choreographer Stacy Grossfield stays put. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/GROSSFIELD/GROSSFIELD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/GROSSFIELD/GROSSFIELD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/GROSSFIELD/GROSSFIELD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 639, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/GROSSFIELD/GROSSFIELD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/GROSSFIELD/GROSSFIELD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Through a Portal, to a World Where Listless Blue Creatures Await', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Through a Portal, to a World Where Listless Blue Creatures Await', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:41:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/801f2ff4-a9a4-5fc0-a152-30381712eb8c', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/801f2ff4-a9a4-5fc0-a152-30381712eb8c'}, {'abstract': 'Steve Robinson’s team and Warren Spector’s make the Morehead final at the Summer North American Championships in Toronto.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/crosswords/bridge/morehead-teams-final-is-florida-vs-maryland-bridge.html', 'snippet': 'Steve Robinson’s team and Warren Spector’s make the Morehead final at the Summer North American Championships in Toronto.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the Summer North American Championships in Toronto the final of the Morehead Grand National Teams Championship Flight was scheduled to end late Sunday. It was between District 9, Florida (Warren Spector, Michael Becker, David Berkowitz, Gary Cohler, Jeff Meckstroth and Eric Rodwell), and District 6, Maryland, Virginia and Washington (Steve Robinson, Peter Boyd, Beth Palmer, Bill Pettis, Bill Cole and Steve Landen).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Passing Up Three No-Trump and Earning a Game Swing', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Passing Up Three No-Trump And Earning a Game Swing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:45:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d48c2a5-d974-5b51-8d62-534db85f6c5c', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d48c2a5-d974-5b51-8d62-534db85f6c5c'}, {'abstract': 'E M A brought her scorched sound to the Mercury Lounge on Wednesday in her first New York concert, playing songs from most of her debut album, “Past Life Martyred Saints.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/arts/music/e-m-as-first-new-york-show-at-mercury-lounge-review.html', 'snippet': 'E M A brought her scorched sound to the Mercury Lounge on Wednesday in her first New York concert, playing songs from most of her debut album, “Past Life Martyred Saints.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'E M A declaims more than she sings, though she can sing quite well. Sometimes she just mumbles. She’s got a husky voice, the sort that sounds bruised regardless of how it’s deployed. She owes something — many things — to the Velvet Underground, but without that band’s urbane grit.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/EMA/EMA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 829, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/EMA/EMA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/EMA/EMA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Coming Up for Air in a Suffocating Life', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Coming Up for Air In a Suffocating Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:49:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a918a38-b4c2-57ff-8823-10855fde25a4', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a918a38-b4c2-57ff-8823-10855fde25a4'}, {'abstract': 'Performances by Keigwin + Company and Jamel Gaines’s Creative Outlet Dance Theater added up to a thematically random program at SummerStage in Central Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/arts/dance/keigwin-company-and-works-by-jamel-gaines-review.html', 'snippet': 'Performances by Keigwin + Company and Jamel Gaines’s Creative Outlet Dance Theater added up to a thematically random program at SummerStage in Central Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In terms of watching dance in a park, the only circumstance more unappealing than SummerStage on its own — seeing dances with little or no nuance gets dull — is SummerStage during a heat wave. (Although it does help to imagine the life of a baseball catcher in August.) On a steamy Saturday night the festival offered a somewhat thematically random program, showcasing Jamel Gaines and his Creative Outlet Dance Theater of Brooklyn (the warm-up act) and Keigwin + Company (the headliner). ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/KEIGWIN/KEIGWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 223, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/KEIGWIN/KEIGWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 223}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/KEIGWIN/KEIGWIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 561, 'width': 873, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/KEIGWIN/KEIGWIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/KEIGWIN/KEIGWIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Steady Stream of Poses, Leaps and Everyday Chaos', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Steady Stream of Poses, Leaps and Everyday Chaos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Central Park Summerstage', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Creative Outlet Dance Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Keigwin + Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keigwin, Larry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:52:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5c61901-85c8-538f-8086-1efa099594fd', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5c61901-85c8-538f-8086-1efa099594fd'}, {'abstract': 'The HBO documentary “There’s Something Wrong With Aunt Diane” takes a hard look at Diane Schuler’s horrifying car crash in 2009, which killed her and seven other people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/arts/television/theres-something-wrong-with-aunt-diane-on-hbo-review.html', 'snippet': 'The HBO documentary “There’s Something Wrong With Aunt Diane” takes a hard look at Diane Schuler’s horrifying car crash in 2009, which killed her and seven other people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The story of Diane Schuler and the horrifying collision she caused is a series of mysteries that probably can’t be solved but that could, from some angles, be seen as fairly easy to explain. The layers of anger, guilt and what many would call blind faith that surround the case, however, have given it a tragic intensity, making the unanswerability of the questions seem profoundly painful.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/SUBDIANE2/SUBDIANE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 618, 'width': 692, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/SUBDIANE2/SUBDIANE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/SUBDIANE2/SUBDIANE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pursuing Answers, Two Years After a Tragic Collision', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pursuing Answers, Two Years After a Tragic Collision', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garbus, Liz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schuler, Diane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:55:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9278b13b-ed63-59b9-a919-2ae6718108f0', 'word_count': 576, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9278b13b-ed63-59b9-a919-2ae6718108f0'}, {'abstract': 'The Irish playwright Dermot Bolger’s “Parting Glass,” at the Barrow Street Theater, is about perpetual underdogs in love, sports and the economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/theater/reviews/the-parting-glass-at-barrow-street-theater-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Irish playwright Dermot Bolger’s “Parting Glass,” at the Barrow Street Theater, is about perpetual underdogs in love, sports and the economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What the Chicago Cubs are to baseball, Ireland is to the global economy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/PARTING/PARTING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 945, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Unluck of the Irish in Love, Soccer and Business', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Parting Glass'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Unluck of the Irish in Love, Soccer and Business', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T21:59:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/34d8ae95-91e5-509e-8edf-7f437adf32e5', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34d8ae95-91e5-509e-8edf-7f437adf32e5'}, {'abstract': 'Nina Rulon-Miller serves up a refreshing puzzle.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/flowers/', 'snippet': 'Nina Rulon-Miller serves up a refreshing puzzle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — Mondays — the day, not the puzzle — are tough on everyone, but today we have a unique way to start off the week. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Flowers on a Proverbial Path', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T22:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f41f724f-0d3f-58af-89ee-38f5c1cc12cd', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f41f724f-0d3f-58af-89ee-38f5c1cc12cd'}, {'abstract': 'The producer Mike Medavoy has drawn on his boyhood in Chile to tell the story of the 33 trapped miners from there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/business/media/for-mine-rescue-movie-medavoy-looks-to-his-roots.html', 'snippet': 'The producer Mike Medavoy has drawn on his boyhood in Chile to tell the story of the 33 trapped miners from there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — In a study crammed with keepsakes of his Hollywood life, Mike Medavoy, the movie producer, keeps a black-and-white snapshot of some boyhood soccer pals. He is the gangly mop-top in the middle. The legend reads: “Chile, 1957.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/business/miners1/miners1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 747, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/business/miners1/miners1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/25/business/miners1/miners1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Movie of Mine Rescue, Producer Looks to His Roots', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Movie of Mine Rescue, Producer Looks to His Roots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Copiapo (Chile)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Medavoy, Mike', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T22:03:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b81842ca-c444-5872-a133-58fda5bc90df', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b81842ca-c444-5872-a133-58fda5bc90df'}, {'abstract': 'La MaMa Experimental Theater Club has announced the lineup for its 50th season and is calling it Homecomings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/theater/la-mama-lineup-for-50th-season.html', 'snippet': 'La MaMa Experimental Theater Club has announced the lineup for its 50th season and is calling it Homecomings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'La MaMa Experimental Theater Club has announced the lineup for its 50th season and is calling it Homecomings. It’s the company’s first full season since the death of its founder, Ellen Stewart, in January. A two-day celebration, on Oct. 16 and 17, to open the season and honor Ms. Stewart, above, will feature a World Block Party with Blue Man Group and the Silver Cloud Singers, among others, and a performance by Patti Smith at a La MaMa 50 Gala. Season highlights include a revival of the director and choreographer Ping Chong’s experimental 1985 play “Angels of Swedenborg”; a Hip-Hop Theater Festival; and the world premiere of “Stopped Bridge of Dreams,” a new work by John Jesurun. A new artists-in-conversation series, Monday Nights at La MaMa, is also scheduled and is to feature Julie Taymor, Philip Glass and other La MaMa alumni discussing their work. In a statement released by the company the artistic director, Mia Yoo, said the coming season would bring “together artists who have worked here through the years with artists who are new to La MaMa, creating a dialogue, an energy, whatever you want to call it, this combustible creative force that will move the theater into the next 50 years.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'La MaMa Lineup for 50th Season', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'La MaMa Lineup For 50th Season', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'La MaMa ETC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T22:07:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a6518a4-af65-539e-a953-588bffd3c8cf', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a6518a4-af65-539e-a953-588bffd3c8cf'}, {'abstract': '“Captain America: The First Avenger” was a strong No. 1 over the weekend at the North American box office.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/movies/captain-america-outperforms-his-peers.html', 'snippet': '“Captain America: The First Avenger” was a strong No. 1 over the weekend at the North American box office.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Superhero fatigue? Not at the North American box office, where the summer’s fourth major movie based on comic book characters, “Captain America: The First Avenger” (right, Chris Evans in the title role) was a strong No. 1 over the weekend, selling an estimated $65.8 million in tickets. That result beat the opening totals for the summer’s other three superhero films: “Thor,” “X-Men: First Class” and “Green Lantern.” The Disney-owned Marvel Studios, which produced the well-reviewed “Captain America” for about $140 million, and Paramount Pictures, which distributed it, had worried about competition from “Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows: Part 2” (Warner Brothers). But the boy wizard fell to second place in the second weekend, taking in about $48 million for a new domestic total of $274 million, according to Hollywood.com, which compiles box-office data. “Friends With Benefits” (Sony Pictures Entertainment) was third with an estimated $18.5 million. Showing continued strength, “Transformers: Dark of the Moon” (Paramount) was fourth, taking in about $12 million for a four-week total of $326 million. “Horrible Bosses” (Warner) chugged along in fifth place, selling an estimated $11.7 million for a three-week total of $82.4 million.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Captain America Outperforms His Peers', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Captain America Outperforms His Peers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T22:07:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/56d61c4e-2946-5830-9cae-2fa9f57610a5', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56d61c4e-2946-5830-9cae-2fa9f57610a5'}, {'abstract': 'The actor Rhys Ifans was cited with a misdemeanor battery charge in San Diego before a panel at Comic-Con International.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/movies/spider-man-villain-cited-in-comic-con-scuffle.html', 'snippet': 'The actor Rhys Ifans was cited with a misdemeanor battery charge in San Diego before a panel at Comic-Con International.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rhys Ifans, who has been cast to play a villain in “The Amazing Spider-Man” film next summer, was cited with a misdemeanor battery charge on Friday night in San Diego before a panel at Comic-Con International, the country’s most important comic book convention, in which he was scheduled to appear to promote the movie, according to TheWrap.com. The police said that Mr. Ifans, above, shoved a female security guard after a member of his entourage was unable to enter the hall where the panel was taking place. In a statement Lt. Andra Brown, a spokeswoman for  the San Diego Police, said that Mr. Ifans had been “abusive, belligerent and aggressive,” and that he had been given a citation. The panel was organized to quell fans’ doubts about the direction of the popular Spider-Man character. The movie is a large financial risk for Sony Pictures Entertainment, which plans a series of wall-crawling adventures featuring Andrew Garfield in the title role. “Rhys Ifans was detained by Comic-Con security after a brief incident backstage prior to and immediately following the ‘Amazing Spider-Man’ panel,” a Sony spokesman said in a statement. “Police were notified, and a citation was issued to Mr. Ifans, who deeply regrets this incident.” The film is to be released next July.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Spider-Man’ Villain Cited in Comic-Con Scuffle', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Spider-Man’ Villain Cited In Comic-Con Scuffle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ifans, Rhys', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic-Con (Convention)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T22:09:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b3961e9-ebdc-556f-9920-89c926cd206f', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b3961e9-ebdc-556f-9920-89c926cd206f'}, {'abstract': 'The accuser of Dominique Strauss-Kahn has gone public with her account of the alleged attack.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/accuser-of-strauss-kahn-goes-public/', 'snippet': 'The accuser of Dominique Strauss-Kahn has gone public with her account of the alleged attack.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The woman who has accused Dominique Strauss-Kahn, the former head of the International Monetary Fund, of sexual assault in a Manhattan hotel room has granted her first interviews to Newsweek and ABC News, which will begin broadcasting segments of the interview Monday morning on its “Good Morning America” program.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Accuser of Strauss-Kahn Goes Public', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC News', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-24T22:21:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fcdc699-5f08-5344-80ab-a8140929da12', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fcdc699-5f08-5344-80ab-a8140929da12'}, {'abstract': 'In “This Beautiful Life” Helen Schulman traces an e-mail caper at a New York private school that goes out of control.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/books/helen-schulmans-novel-this-beautiful-life-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “This Beautiful Life” Helen Schulman traces an e-mail caper at a New York private school that goes out of control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The readers to whom Helen Schulman’s novel “This Beautiful Life” will most appeal are those who already know everything about it. Ms. Schulman holds a mirror up to the lives of moneyed, elite New York private-school families and invites such people to nod in recognition. In terms of a less provincial audience “This Beautiful Life” should please anyone who enjoys seeing the destruction of a happy family framed as a self-fulfilling prophecy. The cover art depicts a house of cards. What might happen to that house of cards by the time Ms. Schulman’s story of a calamitous prep-school sex scandal is over?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/2dSUBJPBOOK/2dSUBJPBOOK-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 745, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/2dSUBJPBOOK/2dSUBJPBOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/2dSUBJPBOOK/2dSUBJPBOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Vaccine for Agony From Viral E-Mail', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Vaccine For Agony From Viral E-Mail', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'su