y to those who are unwilling to derive their morality from a religion, it appears unavoidable.\xa0 Where would absolute facts about right and wrong come from, they reason, if there is no supreme being to decree them? We should reject moral absolutes, even as we keep our moral convictions, allowing that there can be right and wrong relative to this or that moral code, but no right and wrong per se. \xa0(See, for example, Stanley Fish’s 2001 op-ed, “Condemnation Without Absolutes.”)[1]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Maze of Moral Relativism', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T01:49:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Boghossian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boghossian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5ce2eaf-bb86-50ef-a879-81ba50f0dcde', 'word_count': 1858, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5ce2eaf-bb86-50ef-a879-81ba50f0dcde'}, {'abstract': 'The kidnapping and killing of a boy was called part of a pattern of killings of children with even suspected connections to security forces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/world/asia/25afghan.html', 'snippet': 'The kidnapping and killing of a boy was called part of a pattern of killings of children with even suspected connections to security forces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KANDAHAR, Afghanistan —Kidnappers believed to be Taliban abducted the 8-year-old son of a police officer in southern Afghanistan, strangled him with a man’s shawl and dumped his body into a stream during the weekend, in apparent retaliation for the father’s refusal to give them his police truck.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taliban Blamed in Death of Afghan Officer’s 8-Year-Old Son', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taliban Blamed in Death of Afghan Officer’s 8-Year-Old Son', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T01:57:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Taimoor Shah and Jack Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Taimoor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b57d805-0bae-5b1d-826d-a6c925263a1c', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b57d805-0bae-5b1d-826d-a6c925263a1c'}, {'abstract': 'Bill Pennington looks at three new golf books for your bookshelf.', 'web_url': 'https://onpar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/24/tales-and-tips-for-your-bookshelf/', 'snippet': 'Bill Pennington looks at three new golf books for your bookshelf.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As you read “The Swinger” (Simon and Schuster), the new golf novel by Michael Bamberger and Alan Shipnuck based on the tribulations that grounded Tiger Woods, it’s easy to catch yourself wondering: Is this what really happened?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tales and Tips for Your Bookshelf