ari SpA', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Formula One', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indy Racing League', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'McLaren Racing', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn of Stock Car Auto Racing', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Penske Racing', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Red Bull', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:04:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e783d10-dff6-5211-9765-0797e96f5597', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e783d10-dff6-5211-9765-0797e96f5597'}, {'abstract': 'Years after designation, many districts still lack the characteristic brown street signs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/many-historic-districts-have-no-hardware-to-show-for-it/', 'snippet': 'Years after designation, many districts still lack the characteristic brown street signs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the city officially recognized Douglaston Hill in Queens as a historic district in 2004, the community celebrated. Three years later, residents of Dumbo, Brooklyn, breathed a sigh of relief when their neighborhood, too, finally won its historic designation. With landmarking, the neighborhoods’ buildings would be protected by the city and all changes to the area would need the city’s approval.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Many Historic Districts Have No Hardware to Show for It', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Signals and Signs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diamonstein-Spielvogel, Barbaralee', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Historic Districts Council', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dumbo (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:10:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ca7a32f-86b8-5c91-991b-6e5e6326ea1d', 'word_count': 853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ca7a32f-86b8-5c91-991b-6e5e6326ea1d'}, {'abstract': 'Joshua Zucker returns with a lemonade stand paradox.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/numberplay-bart-and-lisa-paradox/', 'snippet': 'Joshua Zucker returns with a lemonade stand paradox.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joshua Zucker is back to nourish our neurons. Before we begin, a quick note — let’s keep the conversation accessible for the first three days this week. If you’re a newbie (and aren’t we all in some ways?) this is the time to ask questions and throw out ideas. There are some terrific solvers in the Numberplay community. This is a great opportunity to mix it up with them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Numberplay: The Bart and Lisa Paradox', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snack Foods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:11:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Antonick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Antonick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a70a4a2e-7d8e-52e8-bb0b-72e3ed8cdada', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a70a4a2e-7d8e-52e8-bb0b-72e3ed8cdada'}, {'abstract': 'Australia will send 800 illegal immigrants to Malaysia, where their refugee claims will be processed, and it will accept 4,000 certified refugees from Malaysia by 2015.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/world/asia/26malaysia.html', 'snippet': 'Australia will send 800 illegal immigrants to Malaysia, where their refugee claims will be processed, and it will accept 4,000 certified refugees from Malaysia by 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KUALA LUMPUR, Malaysia — Australia and Malaysia signed a refugee-swapping agreement on Monday that aims to stem the flow of asylum seekers to Australia’s shores. Although the plan promises that the migrants Australia will send to Malaysia will have more rights than the migrants already here, it has failed to satisfy refugee advocates, who accuse Australia of abandoning its international obligations. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Australia and Malaysia Sign a Refugee Swap Deal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Australia and Malaysia Sign a Refugee Swap Deal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Malaysia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asylum, Right of', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:32:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Gooch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gooch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08f5eca1-b956-5548-b082-a490b25bc916', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08f5eca1-b956-5548-b082-a490b25bc916'}, {'abstract': 'Now that the quiet period for banks involved in the public offering has lifted, the company’s top underwriters, Morgan Stanley, JPMorgan Chase and Citigroup, all initiated coverage on Pandora.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/a-mixed-reception-for-pandora/', 'snippet': 'Now that the quiet period for banks involved in the public offering has lifted, the company’s top underwriters, Morgan Stanley, JPMorgan Chase and Citigroup, all initiated coverage on Pandora.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pandora edged up modestly on Monday, even as analysts gave mixed reviews to the online music service.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mixed Reception for Pandora', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, Joe', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citigroup Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Stanley', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pandora Media Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:36:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16c74f24-828c-5ff0-a185-043384b8e971', 'word_count': 452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16c74f24-828c-5ff0-a185-043384b8e971'}, {'abstract': 'Uruguay claimed its record 15th Copa América championship on Sunday, beating Paraguay decisively in the final, 3-0, and reinforcing its claim as the best team in South America.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/copa-america-uruguay-tops-paraguay-in-final/', 'snippet': 'Uruguay claimed its record 15th Copa América championship on Sunday, beating Paraguay decisively in the final, 3-0, and reinforcing its claim as the best team in South America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Uruguay claimed its record 15th Copa América championship on Sunday, beating Paraguay decisively in the final, 3-0, at the Estadio Monumental in Buenos Aires and reinforcing its claim as the best team in South America. Luis Suárez scored Uruguay’s first goal — his fourth of the tournament — and Diego Forlán added two for the champions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Copa América: Uruguay Tops Paraguay in Final', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copa America (Soccer)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:39:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3c7524a-0aa0-59a3-8451-847fd4176b51', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3c7524a-0aa0-59a3-8451-847fd4176b51'}, {'abstract': 'The medical examiner’s office listed the cause of death as “hyperthermia due to exposure to high environmental temperature with underlying natural disease.”', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/heat-claims-life-of-brooklyn-woman-94/', 'snippet': 'The medical examiner’s office listed the cause of death as “hyperthermia due to exposure to high environmental temperature with underlying natural disease.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'An ailing 94-year-old Brooklyn woman died from the heat on Saturday afternoon; it is the first heat-related death the city medical examiner’s office has certified this summer. The medical examiner’s office listed the cause of death, which occurred at 12:35 p.m., as “hyperthermia due to exposure to high environmental temperature with underlying natural disease.” It provided no other information about the woman or the circumstances of her death. At that time, the outside temperature was 92 to 95 degrees in New York City, the National Weather Service said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Heat Claims Life of Brooklyn Woman, 94', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of the Chief Medical Examiner of the City of New York', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:43:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8c98a61-6c84-5c6f-a28e-d659a6abd846', 'word_count': 88, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8c98a61-6c84-5c6f-a28e-d659a6abd846'}, {'abstract': 'Syria’s Cabinet has passed a draft law allowing the formation of political parties, a step in a series of promised changes that anti-government protesters have dismissed as superficial.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/world/middleeast/26syria.html', 'snippet': 'Syria’s Cabinet has passed a draft law allowing the formation of political parties, a step in a series of promised changes that anti-government protesters have dismissed as superficial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — Syria’s cabinet has passed a draft law allowing the formation of political parties to work alongside the ruling Baath Party of President Bashar al-Assad, a step in a series of promised changes that antigovernment protesters have dismissed as superficial and useless.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Draft Reform Law in Syria Fails to Mollify Protesters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Syrian Protesters Reject Draft Law on New Parties', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:50:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nada Bakri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nada', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75ea66be-fd3e-575a-9463-afe962b7cae3', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75ea66be-fd3e-575a-9463-afe962b7cae3'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists in Namibia observe a dominant elephant’s delicate balancing act between aggressive and affiliative behaviors.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/carrots-and-sticks-in-elephant-land/', 'snippet': 'Scientists in Namibia observe a dominant elephant’s delicate balancing act between aggressive and affiliative behaviors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Caitlin O’Connell-Rodwell, who teaches at the Stanford University School of Medicine, writes from Etosha National Park, Namibia, where she is studying elephant societies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Carrots and Sticks in Elephant Land', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elephants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Namibia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:55:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': "By Caitlin O'Connell-Rodwell", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caitlin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connell-Rodwell", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9728d32-a641-5f73-877e-462c126cb74a', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9728d32-a641-5f73-877e-462c126cb74a'}, {'abstract': 'Placing tracking devices on netbooks, discarded cellphones, batteries, printer cartridges and so on, researchers tracked the paths of e-waste across the nation as well as to developing countries.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/a-global-pinball-game-tracking-e-waste/', 'snippet': 'Placing tracking devices on netbooks, discarded cellphones, batteries, printer cartridges and so on, researchers tracked the paths of e-waste across the nation as well as to developing countries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our electronic gadgets are such an integral part of our daily routines that they have become extensions of ourselves. Some people are so attached to their smartphones that they even talk and text while showering.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Global Pinball Game: Tracking E-Waste', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Laptop Computers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Massachusetts Institute of Technology', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kenya', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nepal', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:58:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna M. Foster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier