rd', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/video-backtothefuture/video-backtothefuture-thumbStandard-v6.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/25/arts/video-backtothefuture/video-backtothefuture-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Critics’ Picks: ‘Back to the Future’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fox, Michael J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zemeckis, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T15:58:59+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Gabe Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gabe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/097409bb-82a6-565f-9032-78d42b40a6f2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/097409bb-82a6-565f-9032-78d42b40a6f2'}, {'abstract': 'New data show men and women doing remarkably equal shares at home and at work.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/equal-workloads-for-husbands-and-wives/', 'snippet': 'New data show men and women doing remarkably equal shares at home and at work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Motherlode got its start with a magazine piece I wrote three years ago about the elusive goal of “equally shared parenting.” Back then I cited the latest figures from the University of Wisconsin National Survey of Families and Households, which showed that the ratio of housework done by women to that done by men was about two to one, that the child care ratio was three to one and that both measurements had been at those levels for decades.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Equal Workloads for Husbands and Wives', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T16:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7453d208-6504-50ad-97a0-118c9234cbf3', 'word_count': 877, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7453d208-6504-50ad-97a0-118c9234cbf3'}, {'abstract': 'The bailed-out automaker is the first of the Big Three to start negotiations on worker contracts that expire in September.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/business/uaw-opens-contract-talks-with-chrysler.html', 'snippet': 'The bailed-out automaker is the first of the Big Three to start negotiations on worker contracts that expire in September.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AUBURN HILLS, Mich. — Contract talks between Chrysler and the United Automobile Workers began Monday with promises by both sides to reach an agreement that benefits workers, the company and the American taxpayers who bailed out the nation’s third-largest automaker.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chrysler And U.A.W. Open Talks On Contract', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chrysler And U.A.W. Open Talks On Contract', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler LLC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Automobile Workers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T16:01:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Vlasic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vlasic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84562780-0a38-5c88-b04e-cc49c9bc6b17', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84562780-0a38-5c88-b04e-cc49c9bc6b17'}, {'abstract': 'Timing is everything in comedy. Well, that and location, as “One Man, Two Guvnors,” Richard Bean’s smash play at the National Theater, demonstrates.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/london-theater-journal-funny-then-and-there-here-and-now/', 'snippet': 'Timing is everything in comedy. Well, that and location, as “One Man, Two Guvnors,” Richard Bean’s smash play at the National Theater, demonstrates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — There is timely comedy, which is what most satire, stand-up and sitcoms are. And then there is timeless comedy, which, properly performed, should feel as old as the hills and as fresh as ocean air. Or in the case of “One Man, Two Guvnors,” Richard Bean’s smash play at the National Theater, ocean air scented with the fumes of warm beer, melting taffy and frying fish and chips.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Theater Journal: Funny Then and There, Here and Now', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'valu