 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T19:09:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/c451ad04-82bd-5286-8426-aee0055b04ce', 'word_count': 1009, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c451ad04-82bd-5286-8426-aee0055b04ce'}, {'abstract': 'In June, 14 American soldiers were killed from enemy attacks, the most in three years. The three Iranian-backed Shiite groups are blamed for 12 of these deaths, say American officials.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/weapons-retrieved-in-iraq-point-to-iran/', 'snippet': 'In June, 14 American soldiers were killed from enemy attacks, the most in three years. The three Iranian-backed Shiite groups are blamed for 12 of these deaths, say American officials.', 'lead_paragraph': ' VICTORY BASE COMPLEX, Iraq – Arrayed on a table in an air-conditioned room are the killing tools from attacks on American soldiers over the last several months – the detritus of munitions smuggled in from Iran, according to the soldiers here whose job it is to conduct the forensic analysis of evidence collected from the battlefield.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weapons Retrieved in Iraq Point to Iran', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shiite Muslims', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maliki, Nuri Kamal al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sadr, Moktada Al-', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA IN MESOPOTAMIA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T19:10:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Arango', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arango', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d02e81d-b6fa-56ad-a8c3-954797f82992', 'word_count': 941, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d02e81d-b6fa-56ad-a8c3-954797f82992'}, {'abstract': '“60 minutes” and “Big Brother” helped CBS win the ratings race on Sunday night. And AMC continues to find success with “Breaking Bad.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/cbs-wins-sunday-night-with-familiar-shows/', 'snippet': '“60 minutes” and “Big Brother” helped CBS win the ratings race on Sunday night. And AMC continues to find success with “Breaking Bad.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'CBS once again held the top ratings spot for Sunday in both total viewers and adults 18-49 with two mainstays, “60 minutes” (8.2 million viewers) and “Big Brother” (6.9 million). They were followed by the debut of the network’s latest reality show, “Same Name,” in which a celebrity lives the life of an ordinary person who shares the same name; 5.3 million viewers watched the episode on Sunday featuring the actor David Hasselhoff. Future guests are to include Kathy Griffin, Mike Tyson and Reggie Bush of the New Orleans Saints. On AMC, “Breaking Bad” retained a large portion of its season-premiere audience with a total of 2 million viewers for the second episode. It was the second-most-watched episode in series history. “Breaking Bad” is ahead of last season’s viewership total by 30 percent. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CBS Wins Sunday Night With Familiar Shows', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMC (TV Network)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T19:15:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96e96416-f7fa-5caf-b8dc-57018763ab9f', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96e96416-f7fa-5caf-b8dc-57018763ab9f'}, {'abstract': 'A Stanford finance professor issues a call to investors and argues that banks’ continued use of excessive leverage remains a threat to the economy.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/beware-of-banks-flawed-focus-on-return-on-equity/', 'snippet': 'A Stanford finance professor issues a call to investors and argues that banks’ continued use of excessive leverage remains a threat to the economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anat Admati is the George G.C. Parker professor of finance and economics at Stanford University‘s graduate school of business and a co-author of “Fallacies, Irrelevant Facts and Myths in the Discussion of Capital Regulation: Why Bank Equity is Not Expensive.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beware of Banks’ Flawed Focus on Return on Equity', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, '