ology Prevent the Death of Children Left Behind in Cars?', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T21:24:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Richtel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richtel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b2df956-2497-5181-87b8-700adc933797', 'word_count': 437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b2df956-2497-5181-87b8-700adc933797'}, {'abstract': 'A letter to the editor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/health/26letters-MIXEDMESSAGE_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'A letter to the editor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mixed Messages on Pain (1 Letter)', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mixed Messages On Pain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T21:24:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce44ada2-edd8-52f3-a7f1-268d58aee006', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce44ada2-edd8-52f3-a7f1-268d58aee006'}, {'abstract': 'CBS once again held the top ratings spot for Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/arts/television/60-minutes-delivers.html', 'snippet': 'CBS once again held the top ratings spot for Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CBS once again held the top ratings spot for Sunday in both total viewers and adults 18 to 49 with two mainstays, “60 minutes” (8.2 million viewers) and “Big Brother” (6.9 million). They were followed by the debut of the network’s latest reality show, “Same Name,” in which a celebrity lives the life of an ordinary person who shares the same name; 5.3 million viewers watched the episode on Sunday featuring the actor David Hasselhoff. \xa0Future guests are to include Kathy Griffin, Mike Tyson and Reggie Bush of the New Orleans Saints. On AMC, “Breaking Bad” retained a large portion of its season-premiere audience with a total of two million viewers for the second episode. It was the second-most-watched episode in series history.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘60 Minutes’ Delivers', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘60 Minutes’ Delivers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T21:26:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6646cbb9-98a4-5142-9f7b-d5e9aa2acfe7', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6646cbb9-98a4-5142-9f7b-d5e9aa2acfe7'}, {'abstract': 'A study presented recently at the Alzheimer’s Association International Conference in Paris suggested that simple lifestyle changes might lower a person’s risk for developing dementia.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/can-you-lower-your-risk-for-alzheimers/', 'snippet': 'A study presented recently at the Alzheimer’s Association International Conference in Paris suggested that simple lifestyle changes might lower a person’s risk for developing dementia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A study presented recently at the Alzheimer’s Association International Conference in Paris suggested that simple lifestyle changes might lower a person’s risk for developing dementia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can You Lower Your Risk for Alzheimer’s?', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-25T21:26:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5c3a0c7-6fff-5745-8d86-360ee27166d8', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5c3a0c7-6fff-5745-8d86-360ee27166d8'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists have calculated that if people address certain risks, a significant number of Alzheimer’s cases could be prevented, with the operative word being “could.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/health/26alzheimer.html', 'snippet': 'Scientists have calculated that if people address certain risks, a significant number of Alzheimer’s cases could be prevented, with the operative word being “could.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is there a way to prevent Alzheimer’s disease? Last week, a study presented at the Alzheimer’s Association International Conference in Paris suggested there might be, something that would give hope to millions who worry that one day they may be struggling with dementia. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grasping for Any Way to Prevent Alzheimer’s', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kick