ot the Sheep, Get Early Lessons in the Rodeo Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Little Lambs, Not the Sheep, Get Early Lessons in the Rodeo Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rodeos', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sheep', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T01:24:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2873b8a2-cbf4-5bbb-b6e9-961e8640d0d3', 'word_count': 1099, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2873b8a2-cbf4-5bbb-b6e9-961e8640d0d3'}, {'abstract': 'A survey of a remote stretch of the South Atlantic reveals a chain of volcanoes.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/where-can-you-hide-12-volcanoes-hint-its-big-and-blue/', 'snippet': 'A survey of a remote stretch of the South Atlantic reveals a chain of volcanoes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s a reminder that we know more about the topography of Mars than that of the Earth’s ocean-covered regions and that there is much remaining to be discovered here on our home planet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Can You Hide 12 Volcanoes? (Hint: It’s Big and Blue)', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Volcanoes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlantic Ocean', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T01:28:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fec4b2d9-0aa0-5be7-9521-67b1fd86e70b', 'word_count': 295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fec4b2d9-0aa0-5be7-9521-67b1fd86e70b'}, {'abstract': 'Mathew Brady, drawn at great cost to the front lines of the battle, changed our understanding of both photography and war.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/25/the-all-seeing-eye/', 'snippet': 'Mathew Brady, drawn at great cost to the front lines of the battle, changed our understanding of both photography and war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the aftermath of Bull Run, Washington was invaded — not by Confederate troops, but by a horde of stragglers, limping back in various degrees of discombobulation and post-traumatic stress. Some were soldiers, some simply hangers-on who had come out for the show and lost their way in the fog of war. One of the stranger-looking refugees was a short, bearded man with a straw hat and spectacles, and a watch fob dangling from his belt — along with a sword concealed awkwardly under his overcoat. A contemporary said he looked like “a Paris art student.” This confused apparition, more comical than belligerent, was Mathew Brady, the most famous photographer in the United States. Appropriately, he went straight to the nearest camera, and promptly recorded a not-very-heroic moment in American history. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The All-Seeing Eye', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brady, Mathew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T01:30:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ted Widmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ted', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Widmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bf4a4e2-9d0f-5559-bce5-11ca7c9a104a', 'word_count': 2297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bf4a4e2-9d0f-5559-bce5-11ca7c9a104a'}, {'abstract': 'Carlos Beltran, for at least one more day, remained a Met, taking his usual position in right and batting third in a 4-2 victory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/sports/baseball/mets-pagan-is-sent-for-tests-reds-dickey.html', 'snippet': 'Carlos Beltran, for at least one more day, remained a Met, taking his usual position in right and batting third in a 4-2 victory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CINCINNATI — That Carlos Beltran, the Mets’ star right fielder, will be traded in the coming days is almost inevitable, his departure from the team an apparent formality. Only the details remain in the air, and conjecture regarding his impending departure will hang in the Mets clubhouse until a trade is completed. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/26/sports/METSFLOATER/METSFLOATER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 764, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thum