snippet': 'Topsy is a dedicated site for searching Twitter. It’s better, it turns out, than Twitter’s own search functions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve tried to search for tweets on Twitter’s built-in search box, you know it can be frustrating. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Better Way to Search Twitter', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T09:55:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Boutin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boutin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c1cc04f-06da-5c7f-9820-43b4c7fc2882', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c1cc04f-06da-5c7f-9820-43b4c7fc2882'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/the-early-word-dueling/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Dueling', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/820c5199-c481-5787-a0e7-0c267ad494d6', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/820c5199-c481-5787-a0e7-0c267ad494d6'}, {'abstract': 'What this week’s inevitable player movement might mean for your fantasy team.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/fantasy-focus-trade-deadline-observations/', 'snippet': 'What this week’s inevitable player movement might mean for your fantasy team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you have a pitcher who is a candidate to be traded — James Shields, Jeff Niemann, Erik Bedard, Hiroki Kuroda or Wandy Rodriguez, for example — things are more likely to get worse than they are to get better. That’s because most of the prospective buyers are teams in hitters’ parks. None of the teams with the most extreme pitchers’ parks — the Mariners, the Rays, the Athletics, the Padres, the Mets, the Dodgers or the Marlins — are buying right now, and that means your pitcher is likely to wind up in Yankee Stadium, Arlington, the Great American Ballpark, Miller Field, U.S. Cellular Field or some other place where baseballs tend to fly into the seats. The presumably better run support will help, as will not having to face that team’s offense anymore, but if you’re a Shields owner, you would probably rather the deadline come and go uneventfully.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantasy Focus: Trade-Deadline Observations', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beltran, Carlos', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jimenez, Ubaldo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kuroda, Hiroki', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Brendan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shields, James', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T10:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Liss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11c456d5-193c-5850-9199-b826d2563401', 'word_count': 656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11c456d5-193c-5850-9199-b826d2563401'}, {'abstract': 'An economist asks how so many Republican leaders can ignore the facts about the Bush-era tax cuts and their effect on revenue.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/are-the-bush-tax-cuts-the-root-of-our-fiscal-problem/', 'snippet': 'An economist asks how so many Republican leaders can ignore the facts about the Bush-era tax cuts and their effect on revenue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bruce Bartlett held senior policy roles in the administrations of Ronald Reagan and George H.W. Bush and served on the staffs of Representatives Jack Kemp and Ron Paul.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are the Bush Tax Cuts the Root of Our Fiscal Problem?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T10:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Bartlett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bartlett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46b93dd5-4f60-5bd2-ac92-7dabe16932dc', 'word_count': 868, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46b93dd5-4f60-5bd2-ac92-7dabe16932dc'}, {'abstract': 'You may not be able to go snorkeling in the Seine, but this summer, Paris has again transformed the banks of its river into a Caribbean-like beach paradise.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/the-beaches-of-paris-return-to-the-banks-of-the-seine/', 'snippet': 'You may not be able to go snorkeling in the Seine, but this summer, Paris has again transformed the banks of its river into a Caribbean-like beach paradise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You may not be able to go snorkeling in the Seine, but this summer, Paris has again transformed the banks of its river into a Caribbean-like beach paradise. In 2002, Bertrand Delanoe, the mayor of Paris, inaugurated the idea of Paris Plages. Open through Aug. 21, the beaches offer an unusual perspective on the city and a great opportunity to slow down between museum and monument visits.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Beaches of Paris Return to the Banks of the Seine', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beaches', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seine River (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T10:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6dd6940c-a74c-520f-813f-007c022649ae', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6dd6940c-a74c-520f-813f-007c022649ae'}, {'abstract': 'The humble beginnings of our humblest coin.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/who-made-that-penny/', 'snippet': 'The humble beginnings of our humblest coin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The penny is a neglected coin. Frequently orphaned on the street. Mercilessly taunted for its mediocrity. In an age of trillion-dollar debt and billion-dollar bank bailouts, America’s lowest common denomination seems to bear little value as an individual monetary unit. Yet the copper-toned coin bearing Lincoln’s profile still holds a unique place in our national identity. Phrases like Benjamin Franklin’s “A penny saved is a penny earned” and the actual artifact itself (shown above is a photographic penny spectrum by the designer Bob Dinetz,) are as American is it gets. The same cannot be said of the origins of its designer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Penny?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Currency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franklin, Benjamin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T10:05:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dff12946-4cf7-53c2-bdbd-bc3d117b8c7e', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dff12946-4cf7-53c2-bdbd-bc3d117b8c7e'}, {'abstract': 'Apple has been promoting iCloud before its fall introduction, but what will happen to the iDisk part of MobileMe?', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/qa-moving-away-from-mobileme/', 'snippet': 'Apple has been promoting iCloud before its fall introduction, but what will happen to the iDisk part of MobileMe?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Moving Away From MobileMe', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'iCloud', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T10:31:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac431fa8-0ac4-5976-859d-37af29615858', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac431fa8-0ac4-5976-859d-37af29615858'}, {'abstract': 'The lawyer representing the attack suspect said he thought his client would spend life in prison.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/world/europe/27oslo.html', 'snippet': 'The lawyer representing the attack suspect said he thought his client would spend life in prison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OSLO — The Oslo police on Tuesday evening began a gradual release of the names of the dead in the Norway massacre, as the lawyer representing the man who admitted responsibility said he thought his client was insane and would spend the rest of his life incarcerated. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': '