t is as crowded as a Midtown subway station. So it didn’t seem all that odd to witness two middle-school students, Ariel Rogiers, 13 and Javonna Cato, 12, from the Robert F. Kennedy School on the Upper East Side, lining up recently on the museum’s third floor to check the balances on their MetroCards. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Art That Begs to Be Touched', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'MetroCard (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moeslinger, Sigi', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ANTENNA DESIGN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Transit Authority', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midtown Area (NYC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6613ee33-34f0-57e6-a9aa-40f0456a5a5a', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6613ee33-34f0-57e6-a9aa-40f0456a5a5a'}, {'abstract': 'Huntsman Gay’s sale of the oil services firm Grand Isle Shipyard is the first exit by the private equity firm from a portfolio company.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/huntsman-gay-sells-grand-isle-shipyard/', 'snippet': 'Huntsman Gay’s sale of the oil services firm Grand Isle Shipyard is the first exit by the private equity firm from a portfolio company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Huntsman Gay Global Capital, the buyout firm that counts the former N.F.L. player Steve Young and a former Citigroup chief financial officer among its ranks, plans to announce on Tuesday that it has sold the oilfield services company Grand Isle Shipyard to the Nana Development Corporation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Huntsman Gay Sells Grand Isle Shipyard', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T11:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbaf6147-426a-518c-ac48-d46c4990e031', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbaf6147-426a-518c-ac48-d46c4990e031'}, {'abstract': 'Two speeches on Monday night capped a remarkable series of dueling theatrical performances by the nation’s two most powerful politicians.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/stagecraft-of-the-obama-boehner-debt-debate/', 'snippet': 'Two speeches on Monday night capped a remarkable series of dueling theatrical performances by the nation’s two most powerful politicians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Set aside the great policy debate and the ideological struggles of the debt fight in Washington, and what’s left is a remarkable series of dueling theatrical performances by the nation’s two most powerful politicians, played out for all to see on television.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stagecraft of the Obama-Boehner Debt Debate', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T11:40:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dd3ce06-bcbb-5cfe-baec-2c32370a725d', 'word_count': 1120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dd3ce06-bcbb-5cfe-baec-2c32370a725d'}, {'abstract': 'A look at three maps from the emerging field of spatial humanities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/27/arts/spatial-maps.html', 'snippet': 'A look at three maps from the emerging field of spatial humanities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A look at three maps from the emerging field of spatial humanities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Examples of Spatial Humanities Projects', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History (Academic Subject)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Geography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/e6b14bc7-bb5d-5678-aaca-7d07a70c0918', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/e6b14bc7-bb5d-5678-aaca-7d07a70c0918'}, {'abstract': 'Some employers will accept job applications only from workers who are currently employed or very recently employed. Might this policy unintentionally favor workers who are less qualified?', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/discriminating-against-the-unemployed/', 'snippet': 'Some employers will accept job applications only from workers who are currently employed or very recently employed. Might this policy unintentionally favor workers who are less qualified?', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I wrote in an article today, there are many job ads on sites like CareerBuilder.com and Monster.com stating that employers will accept applications only from people who already have jobs or who very recently had jobs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Discriminating Against the Unemployed', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/853e837b-f0d1-5940-aa28-c4b50576a9a2', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/853e837b-f0d1-5940-aa28-c4b50576a9a2'}, {'abstract': 'Many-layered mapmaking is helping scholars recreate vanished landscapes and envision history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/arts/geographic-information-systems-help-scholars-see-history.html', 'snippet': 'Many-layered mapmaking is helping scholars recreate vanished landscapes and envision history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few battles in history have been more scrutinized than Gettysburg’s three blood-soaked days in July 1863, the turning point in the Civil War. Still, there were questions that all the diaries, official reports and correspondence couldn’t answer precisely. What, for example, could Gen. Robert E. Lee actually see when he issued a series of fateful orders that turned the tide against the Confederate Army nearly 150 years ago? ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/26/arts/26spatial-2-SF/26spatial-2-SF-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 975, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/26/arts/26spatial-2-SF/26spatial-2-SF-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/26/arts/26spatial-2-SF/26spatial-2-SF-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Digital Maps Are Giving Scholars the Historical Lay of the Land', 'kicker': 'Humanities 2.0', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Digital Maps Are Giving Scholars the Historical Lay of the Land', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Geography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History (Academic Subject)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/930ae367-eb3a-5261-ac66-61107ff3a76e', 'word_count': 1291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/930ae367-eb3a-5261-ac66-61107ff3a76e'}, {'abstract': 'A disappointing night.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/meh-bleh-and-eek/', 'snippet': 'A disappointing night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At this point, we just have to accept it as a fact of life: Obama doesn’t, and maybe can’t, do outrage — no matter how much the situation calls for it. The purpose of last night’s speech, if there was one, was to rally the nation against crazy Republicans. But there were no memorable lines, no forceful statements of the very stark reality. “Now, now, that’s not reasonable” isn’t going to move multitudes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Meh, Bleh, and Eek', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:12:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1af4b7c7-f9cb-5f80-bcb8-eed440c5cec4', 'word_count': 123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1af4b7c7-f9cb-5f80-bcb8-eed440c5cec4'}, {'abstract': 'The carmaker’s earnings of $2.4 billion in the second quarter — its ninth straight quarterly profit — fell 7 percent from the period a year earlier.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/business/ford-continues-profit-streak.html', 'snippet': 'The carmaker’s earnings of $2.4 billion in the second quarter — its ninth straight quarterly profit — fell 7 percent from the period a year earlier.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DEARBORN, Mich. — The Ford Motor Company on Tuesday said second-quarter earnings declined 7 percent, to $2.4 billion, from the period a year earlier, largely because of higher costs for raw materials and new product development.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ford Continues Profit Streak', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ford Continues Profit Streak', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:14:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bunkley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bunkley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3799b75-af08-56ca-9b9a-ccc40ea28492', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3799b75-af08-56ca-9b9a-ccc40ea28492'}, {'abstract': 'Acer, best known for its computers and computer monitors, has branched out with monitor made to handle 3D from multiple sources.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/an-acer-monitor-with-an-extra-dimension/', 'snippet': 'Acer, best known for its computers and computer monitors, has branched out with monitor made to handle 3D from multiple sources.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Acer, best known for its computers and computer monitors, has branched out with a screen made to handle 3-D from television, Blu-ray discs, computers, cameras, game consoles and other video sources.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Acer Monitor With an Extra Dimension', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Monitors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Three-Dimensional Devices and Effects', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ACER INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NVIDIA CORP', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:20:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ee82cc2-a2c0-5862-95aa-77987f226c6f', 'word_count': 229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ee82cc2-a2c0-5862-95aa-77987f226c6f'}, {'abstract': 'The good news is that Times writers don’t feel the need to use the words panegyric, immiscible or Manichaean very often. That’s fortunate because the bad news is, when we do use them, a lot of readers don’t know what we’re talking about.', 'web_url': 'https://topics.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/more-fancy-words/', 'snippet': 'The good news is that Times writers don’t feel the need to use the words panegyric, immiscible or Manichaean very often. That’s fortunate because the bad news is, when we do use them, a lot of readers don’t know what we’re talking about.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Notes from the newsroom on grammar, usage and style. (Some frequently asked questions are here.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Fancy Words', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a47627c-5bc0-5601-9fc2-92e5b7a0c49d', 'word_count': 1252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a47627c-5bc0-5601-9fc2-92e5b7a0c49d'}, {'abstract': 'The good news is that Times writers don’t feel the need to use the words panegyric, immiscible or Manichaean very often. That’s fortunate because the bad news is, when we do use them, a lot of readers don’t know what we’re talking about.', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/more-fancy-words/', 'snippet': 'The good news is that Times writers don’t feel the need to use the words panegyric, immiscible or Manichaean very often. That’s fortunate because the bad news is, when we do use them, a lot of readers don’t know what we’re talking about.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Notes from the newsroom on grammar, usage and style. (Some frequently asked questions are here.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Fancy Words', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/973fbe39-3b83-50d5-98c5-1a83da31269f', 'word_count': 1252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/973fbe39-3b83-50d5-98c5-1a83da31269f'}, {'abstract': 'Employment, the non-difference.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/the-end-of-eurosclerosis/', 'snippet': 'Employment, the non-difference.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve written a number of times about the weird way American perceptions of Europe seem stuck in the past. It’s common — especially on the right, but more broadly too — to see Europe as a land of stagnant economies and lack of jobs. This vision had some truth in the 1990s, but was becoming less and less true even before the crisis, which hit US employment much harder than European employment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The End of Eurosclerosis', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:43:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1494e676-bde1-59d0-ab31-017401204ce7', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1494e676-bde1-59d0-ab31-017401204ce7'}, {'abstract': 'Spending cuts, not the financial markets.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/the-disaster-we-know/', 'snippet': 'Spending cuts, not the financial markets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just to make a point that could be overlooked in the confusing discussion about the effects of default on financial markets: It’s true that nobody really knows what effect failure to make full payment on the debt will have. It could produce calamity, or it could be contained, with borrowing rates for the private sector barely affected. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Disaster We Know', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:51:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6359272-73ce-53e3-9ee5-dcdb83b01696', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6359272-73ce-53e3-9ee5-dcdb83b01696'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: By stepping forward and identifying herself as the accuser in the Dominique Strauss-Kahn case, Nafissatou Diallo has raised questions about the practice of withholding names. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/lifting-the-veil-of-anonymity-in-sex-assault-cases/', 'snippet': 'The Day: By stepping forward and identifying herself as the accuser in the Dominique Strauss-Kahn case, Nafissatou Diallo has raised questions about the practice of withholding names. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'High-profile criminal cases are routinely tried in the court of public opinion as much as they are settled in a court of law. If you harbor any doubts about this, I have only two words for you: Casey Anthony.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lifting the Veil of Anonymity in Sex Assault Cases', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diallo, Nafissatou', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC News', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Newsweek', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:51:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddab12bc-11b5-56c7-9cd2-31b0bfc7dd28', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddab12bc-11b5-56c7-9cd2-31b0bfc7dd28'}, {'abstract': 'Chrysler would have posted net income of $181 million, but for a charge related to paying off government loans from the United States and Canada.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/business/chrysler-posts-a-loss-but-operating-income-improves.html', 'snippet': 'Chrysler would have posted net income of $181 million, but for a charge related to paying off government loans from the United States and Canada.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — Chrysler reported a second-quarter net loss because of the cost of paying back its government loans, but said improved vehicle sales were a sign that its overall revival was gaining traction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/business/27chrysler-span/27chrysler-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/business/27chrysler-span/27chrysler-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/27/business/27chrysler-span/27chrysler-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chrysler Posts a Loss, but Sales Improve', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chrysler Posts a Loss, but Sales Improve', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler LLC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiat SpA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T12:55:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Vlasic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vlasic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7efca994-23a6-5564-a84b-d7d678e951d6', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7efca994-23a6-5564-a84b-d7d678e951d6'}, {'abstract': 'Gretchen Morgenson, the Fair Game columnist for Sunday Business, discussed her new book on the mortgage crisis, “Reckless Endangerment,” on Quora.com.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/answering-questions-on-the-mortgage-crisis/', 'snippet': 'Gretchen Morgenson, the Fair Game columnist for Sunday Business, discussed her new book on the mortgage crisis, “Reckless Endangerment,” on Quora.com.', 'lead_paragraph': '4:04 p.m. | Updated Gretchen Morgenson, the Fair Game columnist for Sunday Business, answered questions posed online today about her new book on the mortgage crisis, “Reckless Endangerment,” written with Joshua Rosner. The session was held on Quora, a site devoted to curating knowledge collaboratively by compiling questions and answers. It is the second of three weekly question-and-answer sessions on Quora featuring Times reporters. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Answering Questions on the Mortgage Crisis', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morgenson, Gretchen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Quora.com', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T13:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By ECONOMIX EDITORS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Economix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'EDITORS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/366b2fff-9ed2-54e3-93ec-b18deac6e713', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/366b2fff-9ed2-54e3-93ec-b18deac6e713'}, {'abstract': 'T Magazine asks the curator of the Peggy Rockefeller Rose Garden to weigh in on some of the fragrance world’s newest rose-tinged offerings.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/vain-glorious-rosy-outlook/', 'snippet': 'T Magazine asks the curator of the Peggy Rockefeller Rose Garden to weigh in on some of the fragrance world’s newest rose-tinged offerings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lucas Zarebinski', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vain Glorious | Rosy Outlook', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T13:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Siegel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Siegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b21c463-89cb-5afb-839e-8d6541461b7b', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b21c463-89cb-5afb-839e-8d6541461b7b'}, {'abstract': 'A military plane crashed in a mountainous region of southern Morocco, the official MAP news agency reported, in one of the country’s worst air disasters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/world/africa/27morocco.html', 'snippet': 'A military plane crashed in a mountainous region of southern Morocco, the official MAP news agency reported, in one of the country’s worst air disasters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At least 78 people died when a military plane crashed in a mountainous region of southern Morocco on Tuesday, the official MAP news agency reported, in one of the country’s worst air disasters. Three people were reported to have survived with severe injuries. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/world/27morocco_span/27morocco_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/27/world/27morocco_span/27morocco_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/world/27morocco_span/27morocco_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '78 Killed in Military Plane Crash in Morocco', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Africa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Morocco: Scores Killed in Crash Of Military Plane in Mountains', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Morocco', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airplane Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T13:07:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b63795a-15c3-599b-979b-0a01f149c0fc', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b63795a-15c3-599b-979b-0a01f149c0fc'}, {'abstract': 'The ceremony for Ms. Winehouse was held at Edgwarebury Cemetery in north London and was expected to be followed by a cremation and a family gathering at a local synagogue.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/funeral-is-held-for-amy-winehouse/', 'snippet': 'The ceremony for Ms. Winehouse was held at Edgwarebury Cemetery in north London and was expected to be followed by a cremation and a family gathering at a local synagogue.', 'lead_paragraph': '11:14 a.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Funeral Is Held for Amy Winehouse', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ronson, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winehouse, Amy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T13:10:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/060cfc10-6ada-5da6-b6a6-9ecdafd245b4', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/060cfc10-6ada-5da6-b6a6-9ecdafd245b4'}, {'abstract': 'Three scenarios for the debt ceiling endgame.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/getting-to-a-deal/', 'snippet': 'Three scenarios for the debt ceiling endgame.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last night’s prime-time back-and-forth between President Obama and John Boehner was interesting political theater, and a useful digest of the arguments (“shared sacrifice” versus “no new taxes”) that we’re going to hear a lot over the next year or so — both in the presidential election and, if we only get a short-term increase in the debt ceiling, in the run-up to the next round of deficit brinksmanship. But it felt irrelevant to the endgame that’s currently taking shape in Congress. The president will almost certainly sign any bill that can pass both houses of Congress, and we’re down to three scenarios for how that happens. Assuming that Boehner can muster enough Republican votes to pass the plan he announced yesterday\xa0— which would cut a trillion dollars in discretionary spending, raise the debt ceiling for six months, and then empower yet another bipartisan commission try to negotiate a longer-term deal — and assuming that Harry Reid can find the votes to push his plan, with similar spending cuts but a longer debt-ceiling extension, through the Democratic-controlled Senate, then some compromise between the Boehner plan and the Reid plan seems like a natural solution to the impasse. (Ezra Klein has one plausible example of what such a compromise might look like.) However, enough Republicans regard Boehner’s plan as a sellout to begin with that the speaker of the House might not be able to make that compromise, in which case it becomes a question of who cracks first: Enough moderate Senate Democrats to push the Boehner plan through that body, or enough moderate House Republicans to push a version of the Reid plan through the House. (Note that Nancy Pelosi has endorsed Reid’s plan, suggesting that most House Democrats would vote for it if necessary.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting To a Deal', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T13:26:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e73376e8-e4a9-51e2-a106-1f6adf52cd19', 'word_count': 547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e73376e8-e4a9-51e2-a106-1f6adf52cd19'}, {'abstract': 'A private survey said home prices rose a thin 1 percent in May, largely on seasonal factors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/business/economy/home-prices-ticked-up-in-may.html', 'snippet': 'A private survey said home prices rose a thin 1 percent in May, largely on seasonal factors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Home prices in the United States rose slightly for the second consecutive month in May, but the real estate market still has a long way to go before a recovery, analysts said on Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': Non