rial': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ca37477-cede-5929-9f3f-a91f2c393fcd', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ca37477-cede-5929-9f3f-a91f2c393fcd'}, {'abstract': 'John Lithgow will return to Broadway next spring to star in “The Columnist,” playing the famous midcentury newspaperman Joseph Alsop in a new drama by David Auburn, who won both the Pulitzer Prize for drama and the Tony Award for best play in 2001 for “Proof.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/a-return-to-broadway-and-a-columnists-role-for-lithgow/', 'snippet': 'John Lithgow will return to Broadway next spring to star in “The Columnist,” playing the famous midcentury newspaperman Joseph Alsop in a new drama by David Auburn, who won both the Pulitzer Prize for drama and the Tony Award for best play in 2001...', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Lithgow will return to Broadway next spring to star in “The Columnist,” playing the famous midcentury newspaperman Joseph Alsop in a new drama by David Auburn, who won both the Pulitzer Prize for drama and the Tony Award for best play in 2001 for “Proof.” Mr. Lithgow, a two-time Tony winner for “Sweet Smell of Success” (in which he also played a columnist) and “The Changing Room” (his Broadway debut in 1973), was last on Broadway in the 2008-9 season in the revival of “All My Sons.” He has also won five Emmy Awards for various television roles, most notably for his starring role on “Third Rock From the Sun.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Return to Broadway, and a Columnist’s Role, for Lithgow', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALSOP, JOSEPH', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Auburn, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lithgow, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T17:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6e7a74d-f450-5958-b8f6-21ba4e409545', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6e7a74d-f450-5958-b8f6-21ba4e409545'}, {'abstract': 'Tyne Daly’s performance in “Master Class” was based on Maria Callas’s Juilliard sessions in 1971-72, but as recordings indicate, Callas was not much like the imperious, self-absorbed prima donna of the play.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/arts/music/tyne-dalys-master-class-callas-vs-the-real-callas.html', 'snippet': 'Tyne Daly’s performance in “Master Class” was based on Maria Callas’s Juilliard sessions in 1971-72, but as recordings indicate, Callas was not much like the imperious, self-absorbed prima donna of the play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Terrence McNally’s 1995 play, “Master Class,” was inspired by the now legendary master classes that Maria Callas gave during the 1971-72 academic year at the Juilliard School. There were 23 two-hour sessions in all, and Callas worked with 25 students whom she had selected after listening to some 300 young singers in auditions. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/theater/27callas-web/27callas-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/27/theater/27callas-web/27callas-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/theater/27callas-web/27callas-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 600, 'su