 the House speaker, John Boehner, gave dueling speeches on how to fix the deficit. One line from Mr. Boehner’s speech confused me:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Inconvenient Precedent for the Debt Crisis', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T17:56:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a576b555-0bf4-55ec-b8a5-896ad0f2c40a', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a576b555-0bf4-55ec-b8a5-896ad0f2c40a'}, {'abstract': '“London Road,” at the National Theater, and “Loyalty,” at the Hampstead, are two productions based on actual events, but only one finds the truth.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/london-theater-journal-nothing-rings-true-like-authenticity/', 'snippet': '“London Road,” at the National Theater, and “Loyalty,” at the Hampstead, are two productions based on actual events, but only one finds the truth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – I heard the city singing as I walked along the South Bank on Monday night. O.K., what I really heard was just people talking – and occasionally yelling, barking or squealing. As usual. But because I had just left a show in which the line between song and speech had been thoroughly dissolved, my ears were suddenly tuned into previously unheard melodies lurking in human conversation. I felt kind of like Gene Kelly being an American in Paris, but in a grimmer, more reflective mood.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Theater Journal: Nothing Rings True Like Authenticity', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arditti, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cork, Adam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hall, Edward', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hare, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Helm, Sarah', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Norris, Rufus', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Theater Co', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T18:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad6be824-f0c4-5105-a29f-fac0b46c036a', 'word_count': 1209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad6be824-f0c4-5105-a29f-fac0b46c036a'}, {'abstract': 'The group gave Santos Party House and host Tim Barber a set filled with macho music paired with nerdy charm.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/notes-from-the-underground-the-pablo-picasso/', 'snippet': 'The group gave Santos Party House and host Tim Barber a set filled with macho music paired with nerdy charm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I pushed my way to the front of the thick crowd in the basement of Santos Party House, I encountered no less than a dozen shirtless dudes throwing themselves around, whirling sweat and crowd surfing. The Pablo Picasso were playing what could be described as the soundtrack to the March of the Alpha Male, and I expected them to look the part. On the contrary, the band of five boys is refreshingly nerdy in appearance. The endearingly scrawny lead singer could easily be mistaken an I.T. tech who broke free of his keyboard and grabbed a microphone, were it not for his surprisingly deep baritone wailing over heavy, rolling drums. The curator and photographer Tim Barber, who hosted the party, looked pleased with the chaotic state of affairs in the mosh pit, as he ambled about, pressing extra rolls of film into my hand to be sure the moment was documented.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes From the Underground | The Pablo Picasso', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T18:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By IO Tillett Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Io', 'middlename': 'Tillett', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df6bf770-2c54-5b63-853b-a1887e3b7450', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df6bf770-2c54-5b63-853b-a1887e3b7450'}, {'abstract': 'Can conservatives declare victory?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/getting-to-a-deal-ii/', 'snippet': 'Can conservatives declare victory?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yuval Levin, on the debt ceiling state of play:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting to a Deal (II)', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T18:01:26+0000', 'document_type': 