 railroads.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/world/asia/27china.html', 'snippet': 'The inconsistent accounts of how last week’s accident happened add to a public skepticism about the government’s administration of railroads.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SHANGHAI — The train collision on a high-speed rail line in eastern China last Saturday that killed 39 people and injured 210 others has raised fresh doubts about the safety of one of the largest, most expensive public works projects ever undertaken.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/world/CHINA/CHINA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/world/CHINA/CHINA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/27/world/CHINA/CHINA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Crash Raises Questions on China’s Push to Build High-Speed Passenger Rail Lines', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crash Raises Questions on China’s Push to Build High-Speed Passenger Rail Lines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High-Speed Rail Projects', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroad Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-26T19:04:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Barboza and Sharon LaFraniere', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barboza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LaFraniere', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6612b7ca-53e1-5a1d-9be0-58c0569161ff', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6612b7ca-53e1-5a1d-9be0-58c0569161ff'}, {'abstract': 'Just how many antiaircraft missiles did the Qaddafi government have before they were carted off by rebels and spread around the battlefield?', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/26/reading-the-refuse-counting-col-qaddafis-heat-seeking-missiles-and-tracking-them-back-to-their-sources/', 'snippet': 'Just how many antiaircraft missiles did the Qaddafi government have before they were carted off by rebels and spread around the battlefield?', 'lead_paragraph': 'One unwelcome consequence of the war in Libya has been the escape from Libyan state custody of untold numbers of portable antiaircraft missiles, which have been carried off from storage bunkers as Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s military has ceded territory and military bases to opposition fighters. The missiles seen in the largest quantities have been the type visible in the photograph above, the SA-7, a Soviet-era weapon of the same class as the more widely known American-made Stinger.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reading the Refuse: Counting Qaddafi’s Heat-Seeking Missiles, and Tracking Them Back to their Sources', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subje