me': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb7c1dfc-8499-5d04-bae3-56c7d0aba54a', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb7c1dfc-8499-5d04-bae3-56c7d0aba54a'}, {'abstract': 'James O’Keefe is out to get the liberal establishment, one embarrassing video at a time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/stinger-james-okeefes-greatest-hits.html', 'snippet': 'James O’Keefe is out to get the liberal establishment, one embarrassing video at a time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The temperature was hovering near 90 degrees on the afternoon of Memorial Day when James O’Keefe III emerged from the woods and ambled over to my car. He was tall and thin, with pale skin and matted reddish hair. When his mug shot ran in the papers, some people told him he looked like Matthew Modine. Others said Lee Harvey Oswald. On the day I met him, he wore muddy work boots, filthy jeans and, despite the heat, a long-sleeved shirt. “Keeps the mosquitoes off,” he said. All day he was in the outback of a regional park just west of the Hudson, breaking rocks with a pickax to construct a trail. As a boy he was an Eagle Scout, but this wasn’t a nature project. O’Keefe, the man whose video stings helped take down high-ranking people at National Public Radio and led to the demise of Acorn, the nation’s biggest grass-roots community organizing group, was doing federal time.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31okeefe_span/mag-31OKeefe-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31okeefe_span/mag-31OKeefe-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31okeefe_span/mag-31OKeefe-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31okeefe_span/mag-31OKeefe-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31okeefe_span/mag-31OKeefe-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stinger: James O’Keefe’s Greatest Hits', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stinger', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Keefe, James E III", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recordings and Downloads (Video)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Public Radio', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Assn of Community Organizations for Reform Now (ACORN)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Journalism Schools', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T12:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zev Chafets', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zev', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chafets', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ee29e08-f3ad-518a-862a-cdbe0e41b07b', 'word_count': 3710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ee29e08-f3ad-518a-862a-cdbe0e41b07b'}, {'abstract': 'Pierre Boulle’s idea of peculiarly advanced simians has evolved from his 1963 novel “Planet of the Apes” to Rupert Wyatt’s new film, “Rise of the Planet of the Apes.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/movies/a-new-film-in-the-planet-of-the-apes-line.html', 'snippet': 'Pierre Boulle’s idea of peculiarly advanced simians has evolved from his 1963 novel “Planet of the Apes” to Rupert Wyatt’s new film, “Rise of the Planet of the Apes.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE evolution of species takes place over millenniums. Pop-culture franchises just don’t have that kind of time. Rupert Wyatt’s “Rise of the Planet of the Apes,” opening Aug. 5, is the seventh film about the peculiarly advanced simians invented by Pierre Boulle in his 1963 novel “Planet of the Apes” and the first in 10 years. The last “Apes” picture, directed by Tim Burton, was a remake of Franklin J. Schaffner’s original 1968 adaptation of the Boulle novel; the first film generated four sequels, a couple of TV series (one live action, one animated), a line of comic books and a jungleful of merchandise before the brand began to peter out, ceding its dominance to other, stronger market beasts like “Star Wars” and “Batman.” The apes had a nice run, but nothing lasts forever. Species die out. Empires fall. Profits decline. New heads of studios rise.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31APES1/31APES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31APES1/31APES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31APES1/31APES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31APES1/31APES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31APES1/31APES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Apes From the Future, Holding a Mirror to Today', 'kicker': 'Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Apes From the Future, Holding a Mirror to Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science Fiction', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wyatt, Rupert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T12:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terrence Rafferty', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rafferty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15e36ec6-0d40-5e9d-a42b-bcf87228fc9d', 'word_count': 1368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15e36ec6-0d40-5e9d-a42b-bcf87228fc9d'}, {'abstract': 'The male archetypes populating contemporary Hollywood offer clues about what the men of our dreams look like, or at least what moviemakers are trying to sell us.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/movies/male-archetypes-in-the-movies-big-baby-to-brave-boy.html', 'snippet': 'The male archetypes populating contemporary Hollywood offer clues about what the men of our dreams look like, or at least what moviemakers are trying to sell us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE are times, particularly during the summer, when the big screen seems overrun with the alpha and omega of contemporary masculinity: the big babies of comedies and the hard-bodied manly men of superhero fantasies. There are a handful of other types in play, yet even these represent a fairly limited spectrum, from the idealized to the abject. The movies may be male dominated, but images of men are surprisingly narrow: often missing in action is the regular guy who wakes up every morning, kisses his wife (or husband) and manages not to do anything especially silly or heroic in the course of his working day. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/movies/critics-front/critics-front-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/27/movies/critics-front/critics-front-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Babies to Heroes: A Field Guide to Big-Screen Men', 'kicker': 'Arts & Leisure', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Babies to Heroes: A Field Guide To Big-Screen Men', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T12:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott and Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a94b4a4-423d-5640-aaf8-2865f659ec5b', 'word_count': 1845, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a94b4a4-423d-5640-aaf8-2865f659ec5b'}, {'abstract': 'Standard & Poor’s cut the rating of Greece further, while Moody’s Investors Service cut Cyprus’s rating.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/business/global/moodys-downgrades-cyprus-over-economic-woes.html', 'snippet': 'Standard & Poor’s cut the rating of Greece further, while Moody’s Investors Service cut Cyprus’s rating.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the latest downgrades to hit the euro zone, Greece’s credit rating was cut by Standard & Poor’s on Wednesday, while Moody’s Investors Service cut the rating for Cyprus. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/business/Euro/Euro-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Greek and Cyprus Credit Ratings Cut', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'S.&P. Cuts Greece’s Credit Rating, While Moody’s Cuts Cyprus’s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cyprus', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Moody's Corporation", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Bonds', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ratings and Rating Systems', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T12:21:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e44a2d5f-e128-5180-b6a1-a2d625b5b454', 'word_count': 669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e44a2d5f-e128-5180-b6a1-a2d625b5b454'}, {'abstract': 'Easy ways to make sure that, no matter what happens to your hardware, your data is retrievable when you’re on the go.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/3-ways-to-keep-your-data-with-you-at-all-times/', 'snippet': 'Easy ways to make sure that, no matter what happens to your hardware, your data is retrievable when you’re on the go.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It always happens: When traveling for business, you always forget to send yourself an important file or drag a presentation onto your flash drive at the last minute. The result? Frantic phone calls to a distant office or rummaging through old files to find a replacement.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '3 Ways to Keep Your Data With You at All Times', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Storage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dropbox Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T12:22:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Biggs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Biggs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb1a8002-d02c-5e1e-b5de-b93043b11254', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb1a8002-d02c-5e1e-b5de-b93043b11254'}, {'abstract': 'Careerism at America’s expense.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/the-guilty-parties/', 'snippet': 'Careerism at America’s expense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s actually a simple way to resolve the debt ceiling crisis: non-crazy Republican leaders could support something like the Reid plan — which is, let’s be clear, a huge victory for the right and defeat for progressives — and pass it with limited GOP support and overwhelming Democratic support. Situation resolved.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Guilty Parties', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T12:31:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56d4f2cd-a329-500d-8dab-418f05685b90', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56d4f2cd-a329-500d-8dab-418f05685b90'}, {'abstract': 'The Dow was down nearly 200 points and the S.&P. 500 more than 2 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/business/daily-stock-market-activity.html', 'snippet': 'The Dow was down nearly 200 points and the S.&P. 500 more than 2 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Skittish investors pulled back from stocks on Wednesday as the impasse over lifting the nation’s debt limit wore on in Washington. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debt Impasse Fuels Sharp Fall in Stocks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Debt Impasse Fuels Sharp Fall in Stocks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T12:44:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93c0fe59-3892-52e9-b9cd-4f132091ad63', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93c0fe59-3892-52e9-b9cd-4f132091ad63'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Rudolph W. Giuliani is going to host to a series of gangster movies on AMC; imagine other possibilities for such pairings. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/giuliani-and-mob-films-good-idea-but-why-stop-there/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Rudolph W. Giuliani is going to host to a series of gangster movies on AMC; imagine other possibilities for such pairings. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A “Day” column last week on the state of the subways included references to a few superb New York films. That prompted reactions from readers who offered their own favorites, including a few who rapped me on the knuckles for having ignored Jules Dassin’s “Naked City.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Giuliani and Mob Films, Good Idea. But Why Stop There?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giuliani, Rudolph W', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T12:58:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0381f43-ac92-5fd1-aa64-d005b857360e', 'word_count': 1064, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0381f43-ac92-5fd1-aa64-d005b857360e'}, {'abstract': 'Bad all over.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/eurowoe/', 'snippet': 'Bad all over.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some people commenting here seem puzzled by what I write about Europe. How can I write favorable things about the performance of European welfare states, then turn around and write negative things about European monetary policy and developments?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eurowoe', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T13:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e20c7754-c938-5ad6-b175-c1a6c50ce8ce', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e20c7754-c938-5ad6-b175-c1a6c50ce8ce'}, {'abstract': 'Kickstand’s iron-calved deliverymen are hoping their new cold-brew coffee will help Brooklyn’s caffeine junkies survive the summer.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/ristretto-kickstand-concentrate/', 'snippet': 'Kickstand’s iron-calved deliverymen are hoping their new cold-brew coffee will help Brooklyn’s caffeine junkies survive the summer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is turning into the summer when New York’s flirtation with cold-brew coffee became a hopeless romance, or maybe a desperate obsession. Coffee made by letting grounds and cool water steep overnight was nearly impossible to find in the city as recently as 2008; now it’s sold in growlers, tapped in Queens, plied at a pushcart on the High Line and swigged from stubbies on the sidewalk outside the Ace Hotel.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ristretto | Kickstand Concentrate', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T13:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Oliver Strand', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Oliver', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strand', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d105bf08-3beb-580d-827c-46a27ff3f44a', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d105bf08-3beb-580d-827c-46a27ff3f44a'}, {'abstract': 'Video broadcast on Libyan state television on Tuesday appeared to show Abdel Basset Ali al-Megrahi, the only person convicted in the 1988 bombing of Pan Am Flight 103 over Lockerbie, Scotland, at a rally in support of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s government.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/lockerbie-convict-appears-at-rally-in-libya/', 'snippet': 'Video broadcast on Libyan state television on Tuesday appeared to show Abdel Basset Ali al-Megrahi, the only person convicted in the 1988 bombing of Pan Am Flight 103 over Lockerbie, Scotland, at a rally in support of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s gov...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Video broadcast on Libyan state television on Tuesday of a rally in support of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s government appeared to show Abdel Basset Ali al-Megrahi, the only person convicted in the 1988 bombing of Pan Am Flight 103 over Lockerbie, Scotland, that killed 270 people. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lockerbie Convict Appears at Rally in Libya', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airplane Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacAskill, Kenny', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Megrahi, Abdelbaset Ali Mohmed al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T13:07:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman and Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea8ff84a-0ba8-5980-aaee-5725bb072be7', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea8ff84a-0ba8-5980-aaee-5725bb072be7'}, {'abstract': 'Heritage, 1989.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/conservative-origins-of-obamacare/', 'snippet': 'Heritage, 1989.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s a useful resource for tracking the history of the ideas embodied in the Affordable Care Act.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Conservative Origins of Obamacare', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T13:14:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87f7ced0-332b-50fe-a4f2-cf5d14f77533', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87f7ced0-332b-50fe-a4f2-cf5d14f77533'}, {'abstract': 'A meeting of the countries’ foreign ministers signaled that broad-based talks were back on track.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/world/asia/28india.html', 'snippet': 'A meeting of the countries’ foreign ministers signaled that broad-based talks were back on track.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI — The foreign ministers of India and Pakistan met here on Wednesday, agreeing to a set of small but significant concessions to ease tensions in the disputed border region of Kashmir and pledging to work toward closer ties between their mutually wary, nuclear-armed countries.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/world/28indiaspan/28indiaspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/world/28indiaspan/28indiaspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/world/28indiaspan/28indiaspan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/world/28indiaspan/28indiaspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/world/28indiaspan/28indiaspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'India and Pakistan Report Progress in Easing Strains', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'India and Pakistan Report Progress in Easing Strains', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kashmir and Jammu (India)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T13:21:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Polgreen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Polgreen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7a1ea6a-6b1c-54aa-a362-80afbf6da480', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7a1ea6a-6b1c-54aa-a362-80afbf6da480'}, {'abstract': 'A business consulting firm forecasts continued sluggishness in car sales in the United States, despite hope that the sector could help fuel an economic recovery.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/the-auto-industry-stuck-in-the-slow-lane/', 'snippet': 'A business consulting firm forecasts continued sluggishness in car sales in the United States, despite hope that the sector could help fuel an economic recovery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many of those grasping for a sign of economic optimism these days have pointed to the auto industry. The argument is that as supply chains come back on line following the Japanese earthquake and tsunami, more automobiles will be available for sale to consumers who have been waiting for cars to arrive.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Auto Industry, Stuck in the Slow Lane', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T13:29:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Motoko Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Motoko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4850936-b997-55d8-8eb2-14c386c1faa4', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4850936-b997-55d8-8eb2-14c386c1faa4'}, {'abstract': 'A crash course in all things debt ceiling.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/us/politics/28default.html', 'snippet': 'A crash course in all things debt ceiling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a time it seemed safe for many people going about their summers to try to ignore the debt ceiling drama playing out in Washington. If Wall Street had not seemed overly concerned that the United States was headed toward default, why should anyone else worry? And there is the long history of crying wolf in Washington: in April everyone finally got up to speed on the threatened shutdown of the federal government just in time to see it averted by an 11th-hour deal.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/us/DEFAULT-1/DEFAULT-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 313, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/us/DEFAULT-1/DEFAULT-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 313}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/us/DEFAULT-1/DEFAULT-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 534, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/us/DEFAULT-1/DEFAULT-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/us/DEFAULT-1/DEFAULT-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A. on the Debt Ceiling', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As 11th Hour Nears, a Look at the Debt Ceiling', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T13:32:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper and Louise Story', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Louise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Story', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca5bb91f-ed22-5e79-be56-050a4a26b5ff', 'word_count': 2334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca5bb91f-ed22-5e79-be56-050a4a26b5ff'}, {'abstract': 'The nonpartisan Congressional Budget Office scores the Democrats’ budget plan, which edges out the Republican plan in its 10-year savings.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/budget-office-gives-reid-relatively-good-news/', 'snippet': 'The nonpartisan Congressional Budget Office scores the Democrats’ budget plan, which edges out the Republican plan in its 10-year savings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Congressional Budget Office said Wednesday that a deficit reduction plan offered by the Senate majority leader, Harry Reid, would reduce budget deficits by $2.2 trillion over the next 10 years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Budget Office Gives Reid Relatively Good News', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T13:43:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Pear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8b7c194-4a9b-5206-9b69-c8f6cbdbe612', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8b7c194-4a9b-5206-9b69-c8f6cbdbe612'}, {'abstract': 'A Harvard analysis shows that patients with the government’s Part D drug plan appear to have been less likely to need costly inpatient care.', 'web_url': 'https://prescriptions.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/study-finds-medicare-drug-plan-reduces-health-spending/', 'snippet': 'A Harvard analysis shows that patients with the government’s Part D drug plan appear to have been less likely to need costly inpatient care.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The federal government’s prescription drug plan for the elderly, known as Medicare Part D, has been heralded as a success when it comes to reducing out-of-pocket expenses for many patients and encouraging them to use and continue taking essential medicines.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Study Finds Medicare Drug Plan Reduces Health Spending', 'kicker': 'Prescriptions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard Medical School', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T13:55:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3f882a5-4e33-55d1-9f98-a1d55175ddef', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3f882a5-4e33-55d1-9f98-a1d55175ddef'}, {'abstract': 'Andrew Ainsworth, a British artist who helped design the storm trooper uniforms in “Star Wars” has won the right to sell replicas of those costumes without permission from George Lucas or his studio.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/the-empire-strikes-out-artist-prevails-in-british-suit-over-star-wars-costumes/', 'snippet': 'Andrew Ainsworth, a British artist who helped design the storm trooper uniforms in “Star Wars” has won the right to sell replicas of those costumes without permission from George Lucas or his studio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Take a lesson from “Star Wars”: you can have all the resources in the galaxy at your disposal, and sometimes the little guy still prevails. In this case, that little guy is Andrew Ainsworth, a British artist who helped design the stark, almost featureless costumes worn by the villainous storm troopers in George Lucas‘s original 1977 space-adventure movie, and who has won the right to sell replicas of those costumes without permission from Mr. Lucas or his studio, Lucasfilm.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Empire Strikes Out: Artist Prevails in British Suit Over ‘Star Wars’ Costumes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lucas, George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lucasfilm Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T14:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b10326f9-a0c1-5e30-bda9-99102a8fdb2f', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b10326f9-a0c1-5e30-bda9-99102a8fdb2f'}, {'abstract': 'The firm’s Automotive Performance, Execution and Layout study found that consumers’ enjoyment of their new vehicles was at a record high.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/j-d-power-study-for-consumers-new-vehicles-hold-historic-levels-of-appeal/', 'snippet': 'The firm’s Automotive Performance, Execution and Layout study found that consumers’ enjoyment of their new vehicles was at a record high.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The overall appeal of new vehicles has reached its highest point since 1996, when J.D. Power & Associates first began surveying customers about how much they enjoyed their purchases, according to a study by the consumer research and consulting firm. The 2011 Automotive Performance, Execution and Layout (Apeal) study, released Wednesday, showed that the industry average for the survey continued its steady rise, increasing from 778 in 2010 to 781 this year, based on a 1,000-point scale.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'J.D. Power Study: For Consumers, New Vehicles Hold Record Levels of Appeal', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ratings and Rating Systems', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T14:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cheryl Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cheryl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b67bf0f-c2ae-54cb-8487-4b6e1ca5a636', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b67bf0f-c2ae-54cb-8487-4b6e1ca5a636'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/what-we-reading/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multi