_paragraph': 'Rail travel can be one of the most convenient modes of transport when jaunting across Europe, especially when you have flexible travel plans. When the Rail Europe app for the iPhone and iPod Touch was announced, it seemed a welcome way to book tickets on the fly and a useful addition to the growing library of travel apps.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Europe by E-Rail, Sort of', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stations and Terminals (Passenger)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eurostar', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'rail europe', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T15:23:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danielle Belopotosky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belopotosky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e3e7d98-8501-5720-9d14-94fa1c546d13', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e3e7d98-8501-5720-9d14-94fa1c546d13'}, {'abstract': 'A water main break flooded 176th Street on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/morris-heights-the-bronx-910-a-m/', 'snippet': 'A water main break flooded 176th Street on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Talkie Mangal stands in the doorway of 17 East 176th Street in the Bronx, following a water main break earlier Wednesday morning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morris Heights, the Bronx, 9:10 A.M.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservation of Resources', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T15:23:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d3831de-a982-5c2f-a427-f7fd9fe1ff4a', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d3831de-a982-5c2f-a427-f7fd9fe1ff4a'}, {'abstract': 'Why Harry Reid’s debt ceiling plan might be better than John Boehner’s.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/see-you-in-six-months/', 'snippet': 'Why Harry Reid’s debt ceiling plan might be better than John Boehner’s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Boehner’s debt ceiling proposal, which the House will presumably vote on (and pass, I suspect) tomorrow, would raise the debt ceiling sufficiently to allow the government to function through February of 2012. In the mean time, it would empower a bipartisan commission to come up with $1.6-1.8 trillion in further savings by November of 2011, which would then be voted on in both houses of Congress under up-or-down, no filibusters allowed rules. If the bipartisan commission’s recommendations are passed, the president would be authorized to request a further debt ceiling increase that would carry the country through the rest of his first term.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'See You in Six Months?', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T15:27:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e80bfe88-b475-51f8-a0ea-cb3cc31b4e57', 'word_count': 564, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e80bfe88-b475-51f8-a0ea-cb3cc31b4e57'}, {'abstract': 'Four new picture books about pirate brutes and the pleasures of not bathing, brushing your teeth or speaking proper English.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/books/review/pirate-picture-books-ahoy.html', 'snippet': 'Four new picture books about pirate brutes and the pleasures of not bathing, brushing your teeth or speaking proper English.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just how child-friendly are pirates? Given the horrific headlines about actual pirates off the coast of Somalia, isn’t it odd that parents happily read bedtime stories about the descendants of Long John Silver and eagerly look forward to the day when their preschoolers will be old enough to watch “Pirates of the Caribbean” on DVD?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/books/review/27childens-2/27childens-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 277, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/27/books/review/27childens-2/27childens-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 277}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/27/books/review/27childens-2/27childens-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 473, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'i