lves. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Theater Talkback: In Protest of Pre-Show Announcements', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Motta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Motta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fe24242-d656-5676-9933-fc4471c5636f', 'word_count': 861, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fe24242-d656-5676-9933-fc4471c5636f'}, {'abstract': 'The fund will seek companies that have already proven themselves, generating revenue of at least $20 million a year and profit of $3 million to $5 million.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/s-b-a-unveils-new-venture-capital-fund/', 'snippet': 'The fund will seek companies that have already proven themselves, generating revenue of at least $20 million a year and profit of $3 million to $5 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Small Business Administration announced on Tuesday that it had formed a $130 million venture capital fund to invest in high-growth companies in Michigan. The fund is the first of what Karen Mills, the S.B.A. administrator, said is a $1 billion commitment over five years through what the agency calls Impact Investment funds, part of the Obama administration’s Startup America initiative announced in January.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'S.B.A. Unveils New Venture Capital Fund', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mills, Karen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dow Chemical Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Small Business Administration', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Michigan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robb Mandelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mandelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e00e76ba-dfa4-504a-91cb-cac8fa4fea2b', 'word_count': 748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e00e76ba-dfa4-504a-91cb-cac8fa4fea2b'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the design exhibitions on view at Ralph Pucci International in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/now-showing-knotty-and-nice/', 'snippet': 'A look at the design exhibitions on view at Ralph Pucci International in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York design aficionados who are looking to beat the heat will find nothing but cool this summer at Ralph Pucci International, where a series of exhibitions, all on view through the end of the year, illustrates the very different approaches of their subjects. “Revival,” by the French designer Jerome Abel Seguin, contains nine sculptural pieces made of slices of enormous mahogany trees that were felled in central Java early in the 20th century. The pieces, some of which even contain large stones that became embedded in the trees, are at once graphic and textural, primal, and extremely powerful.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Showing | Knotty and Nice', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92e56def-145e-554a-8edc-d67acfeb9eaf', 'word_count': 229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92e56def-145e-554a-8edc-d67acfeb9eaf'}, {'abstract': '“America’s Got Talent” on NBC was once again the most watched show on Tuesday and is the most watched show overall of the summer on any night.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/americas-got-talent-makes-another-splash-in-the-ratings/', 'snippet': '“America’s Got Talent” on NBC was once again the most watched show on Tuesday and is the most watched show overall of the summer on any night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Buoyed by the record-setting performance of Professor Splash, a professional diver who belly-flopped into a 12-inch kiddie pool from 3 stories up, “America’s Got Talent” on NBC was once again the most watched show on Tuesday with 11.2 million viewers overall, and it was No. 1 in the 18-to-49-year-old demographic, according to Nielsen data.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘America’s Got Talent’ Makes Another Splash in the Ratings', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:01:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70d29d18-0d8f-54ed-9ee8-729e9179db2d', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70d29d18-0d8f-54ed-9ee8-729e9179db2d'}, {'abstract': 'Political leaders fear such tragedies could drive changes in public opinion as far-right parties react.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/world/europe/28europe.html', 'snippet': 'Political leaders fear such tragedies could drive changes in public opinion as far-right parties react.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — Less than a week after the mass killings in Norway, evidence of a shift in the debate over Islam and the radical right in Europe already appeared to be taking hold on a traumatized Continent.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/world/28europe/28europe-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/world/28europe/28europe-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/world/28europe/28europe-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shift in Europe Seen in Debate on Immigrants', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shift Seen in European Immigration Debate as Inflammatory Comments Are Condemned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norway', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breivik, Anders Behring', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:02:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kulish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kulish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf2f4eb0-8214-5faf-a258-2f124cf16223', 'word_count': 1164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf2f4eb0-8214-5faf-a258-2f124cf16223'}, {'abstract': 'In Turning Point, Leah Millis, 23, talks about shooting in Haiti and her admiration for the Pulitzer Prize-winning photographer Preston Gannaway.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/finding-humanity-by-breaking-rules/', 'snippet': 'In Turning Point, Leah Millis, 23, talks about shooting in Haiti and her admiration for the Pulitzer Prize-winning photographer Preston Gannaway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Leah Millis grew up in Denver. A trip to Haiti as a teenager inspired her pursuit of photojournalism as a career. Now 23, she is studying at the Metropolitan State College of Denver and spending the summer as an intern at The Star Tribune in Minneapolis. Her conversation with Kerri MacDonald, part of the Turning Point series, has been edited.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding Humanity by Breaking Rules', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pulitzer Prizes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haiti', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:06:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kerri MacDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kerri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1d459a0-5326-5016-914c-1756dc1fbc3c', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1d459a0-5326-5016-914c-1756dc1fbc3c'}, {'abstract': 'The negotiations and discussions about the proposals on cutting the deficit and raising the debt ceiling   looks to be mostly a matter of face-saving.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/its-all-over-but-the-face-saving/', 'snippet': 'The negotiations and discussions about the proposals on cutting the deficit and raising the debt ceiling   looks to be mostly a matter of face-saving.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It remains unclear whether the House will pass a bill by the speaker, John A. Boehner, to raise the debt ceiling, though prospects appear to be significantly better today than they did yesterday. Still,\xa0the math\xa0looks to be pretty close.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s All Over but the Face-Saving?', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:10:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bd3a703-04be-542e-80f1-18f97819bc9c', 'word_count': 1001, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bd3a703-04be-542e-80f1-18f97819bc9c'}, {'abstract': 'Guest blogger Josh Ruxin writes about two Rwandan doctors who are building a medical school in Rwanda–only the second in the country–to deal with the dearth of physicians in Rwanda.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/reversing-the-brain-drain-expanding-medical-opportunities-in-rwanda/', 'snippet': 'Guest blogger Josh Ruxin writes about two Rwandan doctors who are building a medical school in Rwanda–only the second in the country–to deal with the dearth of physicians in Rwanda.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For decades, young doctors and nurses have been fleeing poor countries for the bright lights and fat paychecks of wealthier nations, abandoning the parts of the world with the highest burden of disease and most desperate need for medical help. Sixty percent of medical doctors who trained in Ghana in the 1980s have since left the country, and there are more Ethiopian doctors in Washington DC than in all of Ethiopia. Sub-Saharan Africa today houses more than a tenth of the world’s population, and shoulders a quarter of the global burden of disease, but it has just three percent of the world’s health care workers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reversing the Brain Drain:  Expanding Medical Opportunities in Rwanda', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:12:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Ruxin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ruxin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e027e141-d434-514a-bd8b-d3347bb13c02', 'word_count': 808, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e027e141-d434-514a-bd8b-d3347bb13c02'}, {'abstract': 'OK Go is premiering their latest video “All Is Not Lost,” which includes an interactive HTML5 version.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/31/magazine/31Mag-OKGO.html', 'snippet': 'OK Go is premiering their latest video “All Is Not Lost,” which includes an interactive HTML5 version.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OK Go is premiering their latest video “All Is Not Lost,” which includes an interactive HTML5 version.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'O.K. to Go Dance Crazy', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'OK Go', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pilobolus Dance Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:14:20+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/a50ea3bf-eb07-573b-89e4-2bc291b3cdf1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/a50ea3bf-eb07-573b-89e4-2bc291b3cdf1'}, {'abstract': 'The nonprofit Sundance Institute on Wednesday unveiled a long-anticipated expansion of its Artist Services program that allows filmmakers to sell and rent their movies independently via special channels on iTunes, Amazon.com, Hulu, SundanceNow.com and YouTube.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/sundances-digital-move-seeks-to-link-filmmakers-and-audiences/', 'snippet': 'The nonprofit Sundance Institute on Wednesday unveiled a long-anticipated expansion of its Artist Services program that allows filmmakers to sell and rent their movies independently via special channels on iTunes, Amazon.com, Hulu, SundanceNow.com...', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— Every year more than 50 percent of the movies shown at the Sundance Film Festival fail to achieve the big prize: acquisition by a theatrical distributor. Can Sundance’s new year-round digital delivery plan help those cinematic leftovers find an audience?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sundance’s Digital Move Seeks to Link Filmmakers and Audiences', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Redford, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:30:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bf4e215-9394-5245-87ab-e62aeeed225b', 'word_count': 333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bf4e215-9394-5245-87ab-e62aeeed225b'}, {'abstract': 'We removed 20 words, phrases and names from the Times Topics overview about the federal debt ceiling. Can you fill them all in?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/fill-in-the-federal-debt-ceiling/', 'snippet': 'We removed 20 words, phrases and names from the Times Topics overview about the federal debt ceiling. Can you fill them all in?', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fill-In | The Federal Debt Ceiling', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:32:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/068464be-4948-5d17-9084-7800896f596c', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/068464be-4948-5d17-9084-7800896f596c'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Powell explores how the combination of support by Mayors Koch, Dinkins, Giuliani and Bloomberg and the work of community groups helped restore areas feared to be beyond saving.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/how-the-south-bronxs-ruins-became-fertile-ground/', 'snippet': 'Michael Powell explores how the combination of support by Mayors Koch, Dinkins, Giuliani and Bloomberg and the work of community groups helped restore areas feared to be beyond saving.', 'lead_paragraph': ' This week in my Gotham column I wrote of the resurrection of the South Bronx, a brilliant coming back to life that owes much to the multidecade efforts of government.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How the South Bronx’s Ruins Became Fertile Ground', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mott Haven (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:36:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Powell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Powell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/452b119e-3a9c-5aa6-94b8-fcd54c4041cb', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/452b119e-3a9c-5aa6-94b8-fcd54c4041cb'}, {'abstract': 'The Deal Professor examines the S.E.C.’s options and the prospects for proxy access after a federal appeals court panel strikes it down.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/proxy-access-in-limbo-after-court-rules-against-it/', 'snippet': 'The Deal Professor examines the S.E.C.’s options and the prospects for proxy access after a federal appeals court panel strikes it down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit has taken a chainsaw to the Securities and Exchange Commission’s proxy access rule, striking it down in a 21-page opinion. A panel of three judges from the appeals court based its ruling last week on what it perceived to be the S.E.C.’s failure to fully consider the costs and the benefits of this rule. With the S.E.C. wounded and proxy access seemingly on life support, if not dead, the question is what comes next? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Proxy Access in Limbo After Court Rules Against It', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boards of Directors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:36:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71209432-6247-5d90-9e90-273acdb4d7d1', 'word_count': 1337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71209432-6247-5d90-9e90-273acdb4d7d1'}, {'abstract': 'The I.R.S. says that passengers are entitled to a refund if they paid an airfare tax for a flight when the tax was not in effect. But getting your money back may not be that simple.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/getting-a-refund-for-taxes-paid-on-airfares/', 'snippet': 'The I.R.S. says that passengers are entitled to a refund if they paid an airfare tax for a flight when the tax was not in effect. But getting your money back may not be that simple.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this week our colleague Joe Sharkey reported on a lapse in federal airfare taxes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting a Refund for Taxes Paid on Airfares', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'REFUNDS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:41:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nadia Taha', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nadia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taha', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/156af3ed-3e80-5e6b-8387-304c473a190b', 'word_count': 266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/156af3ed-3e80-5e6b-8387-304c473a190b'}, {'abstract': 'The Times reviewed thousands of pages of documents related to shale gas, including hundreds of industry e-mails, internal agency documents and analyst reports. A selection is here.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/27/us/natural-gas-drilling-down-documents-6.html', 'snippet': 'The Times reviewed thousands of pages of documents related to shale gas, including hundreds of industry e-mails, internal agency documents and analyst reports. A selection is here.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Times reviewed thousands of pages of documents related to shale gas, including hundreds of industry e-mails, internal agency documents and analyst reports. A selection is here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unredacted Documents: Federal Officials Quietly Question Shale Gas', 'kicker': 'Drilling Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drilling and Boring', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:41:41+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/ab3cbdcb-6e24-51a1-82ed-b7ac8d4ed216', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/ab3cbdcb-6e24-51a1-82ed-b7ac8d4ed216'}, {'abstract': 'This month, via Spotify, the music world took a great step forward toward the holy grail of a free, legal, song-specific and convenient service.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/technology/personaltech/spotify-unshackles-online-music-david-pogue.html', 'snippet': 'This month, via Spotify, the music world took a great step forward toward the holy grail of a free, legal, song-specific and convenient service.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everybody seems to think that record company executives are big, greedy dunderheads. “How dare you charge for music?” the college set shouts. “Music wants to be free!”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/21/technology/21pogue-web/21pogue-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/21/technology/21pogue-web/21pogue-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/21/technology/21pogue-web/21pogue-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Online Music, Unshackled', 'kicker': 'State of the Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Online Music, Unshackled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Spotify', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:45:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/227b5c4e-afb7-55f1-ab4c-4cfd60960745', 'word_count': 1331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/227b5c4e-afb7-55f1-ab4c-4cfd60960745'}, {'abstract': 'Bank stocks have been persistently weak, and the logical solution is that they shed business lines in a bid to improve their prospects in the eyes of Wall Street.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/once-unthinkable-breakup-of-big-banks-now-seems-feasible/', 'snippet': 'Bank stocks have been persistently weak, and the logical solution is that they shed business lines in a bid to improve their prospects in the eyes of Wall Street.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What was made can be unmade.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Once Unthinkable, Breakup of Big Banks Now Seems Feasible', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citigroup Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wells Fargo & Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T19:48:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Eisinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eisinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6169bac-7511-5e9d-a388-2f45afc71255', 'word_count': 810, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6169bac-7511-5e9d-a388-2f45afc71255'}, {'abstract': 'A decision by a watchdog group in the Britain on Wednesday forced L’Oréal to withdraw two makeup advertisements that were deemed misleading.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/british-authority-bans-two-ads-by-loreal/', 'snippet': 'A decision by a watchdog group in the Britain on Wednesday forced L’Oréal to withdraw two makeup advertisements that were deemed misleading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For L’Oréal, beauty is not always in the eye of the beholder.', 'source': 'The New Y