 'nyt://article/2e5c8518-1584-5f2e-9af5-1e554fe03e72'}, {'abstract': 'Oklahoma City’s calamitous domestic aftermath offers a cautionary tale for Norway.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/homegrown-hurt/', 'snippet': 'Oklahoma City’s calamitous domestic aftermath offers a cautionary tale for Norway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We are so accustomed to seeing terrorism through the lens of 9/11 that it may be hard to recapture how shocking it was to realize, 16 years ago in Oklahoma City, that devastating terrorism can be completely homegrown. The mass murder committed in Norway last Friday by a Norwegian anti-immigrant extremist brings Oklahoma City forcefully to mind for the first time in a long while. The bombing of the Alfred P. Murrah Federal Building on April 19, 1995, by two disaffected Americans killed 168 people. At the time, it was the worst single incident of terrorism ever committed on American soil. Today’s young adults, not yet then even in their teens, may barely remember it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Homegrown Hurt', 'kicker': 'Linda Greenhouse', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Habeas Corpus', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mcveigh, Timothy James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norway', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City (Okla)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T20:11:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e961bf0-7bd2-566f-97b5-fe333e17c214', 'word_count': 