icists or big names, has bubbled from the underground, into the mainstream, as a way of moving toward a sustainable future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An eco-ideology with no publicists or big names, has bubbled from the underground, into the mainstream, as a way of moving toward a sustainable future.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/garden/20110728-PERMACULTURE-slide-QQGN/20110728-PERMACULTURE-slide-QQGN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/garden/20110728-PERMACULTURE-slide-QQGN/20110728-PERMACULTURE-slide-QQGN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/garden/20110728-PERMACULTURE-slide-QQGN/20110728-PERMACULTURE-slide-QQGN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Permies at Work', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T21:25:38+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Home & Garden', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/3a00cb94-8dec-5147-9de5-0fe58332b270', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/3a00cb94-8dec-5147-9de5-0fe58332b270'}, {'abstract': 'A No. 1 song in Japan in 1961 provides some comfort after the earthquake.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/japans-comfort-music/', 'snippet': 'A No. 1 song in Japan in 1961 provides some comfort after the earthquake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About a week ago, Rob Walker had a Riff column in the magazine on the declining interest in hit charts and No. 1 songs. That was on my mind when I saw a report last weekend on TV Japan, a cable service in the United States. The report said that sales of oldies in Japan had taken off since the earthquake/tsunami/nuclear disaster. People, it seems, are seeking comfort in those old, familiar tunes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan’s Comfort Music', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T21:26:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Dickson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dickson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4903986-7192-55b7-9c79-2cd8f5545476', 'word_count': 316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4903986-7192-55b7-9c79-2cd8f5545476'}, {'abstract': 'A report that could propose a tougher approval process prompted an extraordinary campaign by the industry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/health/28institute.html', 'snippet': 'A report that could propose a tougher approval process prompted an extraordinary campaign by the industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Allies of the medical device industry are waging an extraordinary campaign in Washington to discredit a coming report by one of the country’s pre-eminent scientific groups that examines possible new regulations on the industry.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/business/Institute/Institute-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 569, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/business/Institute/Institute-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/business/Institute/Institute-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Study of Medical Device Rules Is Attacked, Unseen', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fight Over Medical Device Rules, Sight Unseen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medical Devices', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Institute of Medicine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T21:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barry Meier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6da10193-be08-5dcf-a30d-62777cdd9cae', 'word_count': 1159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6da10193-be08-5dcf-a30d-62777cdd9cae'}, {'abstract': 'Sean O’Casey’s rarely performed “Silver Tassie,” part of the Lincoln Center Festival, mixes humor and the horror of war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/theater/reviews/the-silver-tassie-at-the-lincoln-center-festival-review.html', 'snippet': 'Sean O’Casey’s rarely performed “Silver Tassie,” part of the Lincoln Center Festival, mixes humor and the horror of war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The journey from invincible optimism to embittered defeat is a short one for Harry Heegan, the young soldier at the center of Sean O’Casey’s 1928 play “The Silver Tassie.” The echoes of Harry’s joyous cries at a soccer victory in the play’s opening scene still seem to be resounding in the air when he raises his voice at its mournful end, strumming a banjo and hymning the death of his young spirit as his friends and family look on in awkward sympathy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/arts/silver/silver-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 326, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/arts/silver/silver-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 326}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/arts/silver/silver-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 462, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/arts/silver/silver-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/arts/silver/silver-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'War Changes a Man, and a Play', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Silver Tassie'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'War Changes a Man, and a Play', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lincoln Center Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Druid Theater Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hynes, Garry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Casey, Sean", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T21:33:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c7ad63d-f8ac-587c-bdcb-64f76e7b61e9', 'word_count': 1214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c7ad63d-f8ac-587c-bdcb-64f76e7b61e9'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the children were infants ages 4 months or younger, the Ministry of Public Security said, adding that 2,600 police officers participated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/world/asia/28china.html', 'snippet': 'Some of the children were infants ages 4 months or younger, the Ministry of Public Security said, adding that 2,600 police officers participated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — In a significant illustration of China’s illicit trade in babies, the Ministry of Public Security said Wednesday that the police had rescued 81 children from a major child trafficking ring that had operated throughout eastern China.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '81 Children Rescued in Raids on Trafficking Ring, Chinese Officials Say', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Officials Say They Saved 81 Babies From Child Traffickers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Trafficking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T21:35:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon LaFraniere', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LaFraniere', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5af4869f-25f0-55df-a5e2-d2de3d6ed29a', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5af4869f-25f0-55df-a5e2-d2de3d6ed29a'}, {'abstract': 'In the world of western water politics, most people are known for the way they fight. Tom Clark, who led California’s Kern County Water Agency, had infighting and negotiating skills but was a peacemaker, too.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/in-western-water-wars-fighters-and-peacemakers/', 'snippet': 'In the world of western water politics, most people are known for the way they fight. Tom Clark, who led California’s Kern County Water Agency, had infighting and negotiating skills but was a peacemaker, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Writing about water in a region where water is scarce can be a daunting business. In a graduate program, the prerequisites would be geology, hydrology, chemistry, law and engineering — especially law and engineering. The people involved in the issue (who call themselves “water buffaloes,” not without pride) tend to be defined by their fights as much as their accomplishments. In fact, when they are won, the fights are synonymous with their accomplishments.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Western Water Wars, Fighters and, Yes, Peacemakers', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Tom', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mulholland, William', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Department of Water and Power', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado River', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Owens Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-27T21:37:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f2763ce-9bbf-5ad1-a04b-d444fc314227', 'word_count': 754, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f2763ce-9bbf-5ad1-a04b-d444fc314227'}, {'abstract': 'A plan to revive Creem magazine, the irreverent and sardonic rock publication from the 1970s, is bogged down in financial and legal problems.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/arts/music/the-future-of-creem-magazine-is-complicated.html', 'snippet': 'A plan to revive Creem magazine, the irreverent and sardonic rock publication from the 1970s, is bogged down in financial and legal problems.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Baltin had been toiling as a freelance music writer for a decade when an entrepreneur named Jason Turner approached him a year ago about resurrecting Creem magazine, the irreverent and sardonic publication that defined rock in the 1970s. The offer to edit a once-famous magazine was tempting, to say the least.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'ra