icleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/business/INVEST1/INVEST1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/business/INVEST1/INVEST1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/business/INVEST1/INVEST1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Investors, Worried About Debt Talks, Look for Havens', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Investors, Worried About Debt Talks, Look for Havens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Derivatives (Financial Instruments)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T01:57:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Dash and Nelson D. Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dash', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nelson', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d144b84-f177-599e-acd0-89d7ff01deb3', 'word_count': 1082, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d144b84-f177-599e-acd0-89d7ff01deb3'}, {'abstract': 'The jig is up, in a puzzle by Alan Arbesfeld.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/27/get/', 'snippet': 'The jig is up, in a puzzle by Alan Arbesfeld.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THURSDAY’S PUZZLE — Want to feel a lot better about yourself? We all have our Achilles’ heels when it comes to solving crossword puzzles. For some, it might be categorical, like pop culture or music. For others, it might be directional: I received a lot of mail from confused readers when Caleb Rasmussen’s “U-Turn” puzzle was published (Across Lite, Solution, blog).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday: Now I Get It', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T02:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99663c65-eaf1-5fb6-809e-ac97da9a68c9', 'word_count': 544, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99663c65-eaf1-5fb6-809e-ac97da9a68c9'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the politics that surrounded the passage of the Emancipation Proclamation seems more relevant today than ever.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/opinion/28coates.html', 'snippet': 'A look at the politics that surrounded the passage of the Emancipation Proclamation seems more relevant today than ever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The administration of President Obama has never held much regard for its left flank. Admonished by the vice president to “stop whining,” inveighed against by the president himself for “griping and groaning,” the liberal critics have been generally viewed by the White House as petulant children. “The Professional Left,” former press secretary Robert Gibbs dubbed them, a gang of nettlesome romantics who “ought to be drug-tested,” and would not be happy until “we have Canadian health care and we’ve eliminated the Pentagon.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/opinion/TaNehisi_img/TaNehisi_img-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 653, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/opinion/TaNehisi_img/TaNehisi_img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 653, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/opinion/TaNehisi_img/TaNehisi_img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/08/opinion/TaNehisi_img/TaNehisi_img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/opinion/TaNehisi_img/TaNehisi_img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama and His Discontents', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama and His Discontents', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emancipation Proclamation (1863)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T02:01:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ta-Nehisi Coates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ta-nehisi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Coates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d105bb7e-0fee-54d2-ab43-3d9c6bdd7ff3', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d105bb7e-0fee-54d2-ab43-3d9c6bdd7ff3'}, {'abstract': 'A major survey of teachers, students and principals in South Africa’s public schools found that a third of principals believed the highest corruption risk was embezzlement at the provincial level, in textbook procurement and school construction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/world/africa/28briefs-SURVEYONSCHO_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'A major survey of teachers, students and principals in South Africa’s public schools found that a third of principals believed the highest corruption risk was embezzlement at the provincial level, in textbook procurement and school construction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A major survey of teachers, students and principals in South Africa’s public schools by Transparency International found that a third of principals believed the highest corruption risk was embezzlement at the provincial level, in textbook procurement and school construction. The report also found that a quarter of students said schools were unsafe, with rape and violence major problems, while half the students said they did not always have a desk. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Africa: Survey on Schools Details Corruption and Worries', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | AFRICA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Africa: Survey on Schools Details Corruption and Worries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embezzlement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transparency International', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T02:01:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Celia W. Dugger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Celia', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dugger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2cacbf7-f1a4-5b20-9aa8-04683413aac9', 'word_count': 71, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2cacbf7-f1a4-5b20-9aa8-04683413aac9'}, {'abstract': 'A consortium of professors and a team of students are researching the history of Seneca Village, home to about 260 people from the 1820s to the 1850s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/nyregion/unearthing-an-african-american-village-displaced-by-central-park.html', 'snippet': 'A consortium of professors and a team of students are researching the history of Seneca Village, home to about 260 people from the 1820s to the 1850s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than a decade, anthropologists and historians pieced together the history of a short-lived African-American community that was snuffed out in the 1850s by the creation of Central Park. They combed vital records and tax documents, scanned parkland using radar and studied soil borings.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/nyregion/SENECA1/SENECA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 678, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/nyregion/SENECA1/SENECA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/nyregion/SENECA1/SENECA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unearthing Traces of African-American Village Displaced by Central Park', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unearthing Traces of African-American Village Displaced by Central Park', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHAEOLOGY AND ANTHROPOLOGY', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CENTRAL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T02:04:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa W. Foderaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Foderaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c81b996-a6d4-5482-816c-c4620935b113', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c81b996-a6d4-5482-816c-c4620935b113'}, {'abstract': 'Investors showed first-day exuberance for shares of Dunkin’ Brands, but the mature company might have a hard time living up to the hype.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/business/dunkin-offers-a-bubble-lesson.html', 'snippet': 'Investors showed first-day exuberance for shares of Dunkin’ Brands, but the mature company might have a hard time living up to the hype.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Silicon Valley has no monopoly on bubble thinking. This week’s initial public offering of Dunkin’ Brands, owner of the coffee and doughnuts chain and Baskin-Robbins ice cream shops, shows that in a low-growth world, even old-school businesses are capable of exciting investors to new heights of giddiness.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/business/Views/Views-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dunkin’ Donuts Offers a Bubble Lesson', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dunkin’ Offers A Bubble Lesson', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dunkin Donuts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Banco Santander', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T02:06:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By ROB COX, LISA LEE, FIONA MAHARG-BRAVO and GEORGE HAY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'COX', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LEE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Fiona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MAHARG-BRAVO', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'HAY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f5be585-8107-5552-a624-0116e0d2fe9b', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f5be585-8107-5552-a624-0116e0d2fe9b'}, {'abstract': 'The cost of fecklessness in Congress should be clear to everyone. The markets and the rest of the world are worried. We all should be.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/opinion/28thu1.html', 'snippet': 'The cost of fecklessness in Congress should be clear to everyone. The markets and the rest of the world are worried. We all should be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until this week, Wall Street has shrugged off each new low in the debt-limit debate, confident — in a whistling-past-the-graveyard kind of way — that Washington would raise the debt limit on time. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'America’s Credibility Is at Risk', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Costs of Stalemate: America’s credibility and its credit-worthiness are at risk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T02:15:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/298c142d-0ea4-518e-9a03-43a2e742cffd', 'word_count': 541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/298c142d-0ea4-51