ippet': 'While South Carolina has long produced more of the fruit, Georgians say victory comes down to a matter of taste.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CLEMSON, S.C. — The South has plenty of rivalries. Auburn and Alabama fight over football dominance. North Carolina and Tennessee battle over barbecue.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/us/jp-PEACHES-1/jp-PEACHES-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/us/jp-PEACHES-1/jp-PEACHES-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/us/jp-PEACHES-1/jp-PEACHES-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/us/jp-PEACHES-1/jp-PEACHES-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/us/jp-PEACHES-1/jp-PEACHES-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Peach Rivalry Becomes War Between the Tastes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Peach Rivalry Becomes War Between the Tastes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Peaches', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T03:13:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kim Severson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e00ed28-aed5-5625-bf54-0e990a6d8371', 'word_count': 905, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e00ed28-aed5-5625-bf54-0e990a6d8371'}, {'abstract': 'A federal judge in Orlando on Wednesday declared the state’s controlled-substances laws unconstitutional.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/us/28brfs-DRUGLAWSRULE_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'A federal judge in Orlando on Wednesday declared the state’s controlled-substances laws unconstitutional.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal judge in Orlando on Wednesday declared the state’s controlled-substances laws unconstitutional. A 2002 Florida law eliminated the requirement of a “guilty mind,” or “mens rea,” as part of a drug offense. Briefs attacking the Florida law, in which a defendant need not know that a substance is illegal to be convicted of possessing or selling it, had been filed by groups including the National Association of Criminal Defense Lawyers, the American Civil Liberties Union and dozens of law professors. Judge Mary S. Scriven of Federal District Court threw out the drug delivery charges against Mackle Vincent Shelton, and ordered a new sentencing hearing on other charges. Florida’s unique law expressly eliminating mens rea for drug offenses, the judge wrote, is “atavistic and repugnant to the common law.” The state is expected to appeal the decision, which could leave hundreds, if not thousands, of convictions in question and affect pending cases. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Florida: Drug Laws Ruled Unconstitutional', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | SOUTH', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Florida: Drug Laws Ruled Unconstitutional', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T03:35:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/593f2bc5-f6a5-5fd6-95c6-75f0ac5e8f20', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/593f2bc5-f6a5-5fd6-95c6-75f0ac5e8f20'}, {'abstract': 'The Senate on Wednesday voted unanimously to extend the tenure of Robert S. Mueller III as director of the Federal Bureau of Investigation for two more years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/us/28brfs-SENATEEXTEND_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The Senate on Wednesday voted unanimously to extend the tenure of Robert S. Mueller III as director of the Federal Bureau of Investigation for two more years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Senate on Wednesday voted unanimously to extend the tenure of Robert S. Mueller III as director of the Federal Bureau of Investigation for two more years. Mr. Mueller, first appointed by President George W. Bush, was to step down on Aug. 2 because of a 10-year term limit imposed during the post-Watergate era. But President Obama asked Congress to allow Mr. Mueller to stay on, arguing that his national security team needed continuity because the Pentagon and the Central Intelligence Agency were getting new leaders. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Senate Extends Term of F.B.I. Director', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WASHINGTON', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Senate Extends Term of F.B.I. Director', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mueller, Robert S III', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T03:37:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa732e8b-07cd-5802-9ce3-bc31c9af9aa5', 'word_count': 87, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa732e8b-07cd-5802-9ce3-bc31c9af9aa5'}, {'abstract': 'For the second consecutive year, Manchester United proved to be the rude visitors, defeating the M.L.S. All-Star team, 4-0, before a sellout crowd of 26,760 at Red Bull Arena on Wednesday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/sports/soccer/manchester-united-dismantles-mlss-best-in-repeat-of-last-year.html', 'snippet': 'For the second consecutive year, Manchester United proved to be the rude visitors, defeating the M.L.S. All-Star team, 4-0, before a sellout crowd of 26,760 at Red Bull Arena on Wednesday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HARRISON, N.J. — Once again, one of the richest soccer clubs in the world, which boasts some of the best players on the planet, showed a team of the best Major League Soccer has to offer that you get what you pay for, or don’t.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/sports/SOCCER/SOCCER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'article