he film shows how former criminals are trying to change a culture, one non-violent act at a time.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/and-speaking-of-sentences/', 'snippet': 'The film shows how former criminals are trying to change a culture, one non-violent act at a time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As long as we are making lists of great sentences, let me include my favorite line from a film trailer: “We’ve got 500 years of prison time at this table. And that’s a lot of wisdom.” It comes from “The Interrupters,” a new documentary by Steve James (“Hoop Dreams”) and Alex Kotlowitz, the author of several books, including “There Are No Children Here,” and many great stories for this magazine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'And Speaking of Sentences …', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kotlowitz, Alex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Independent Film Channel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T10:07:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vera Titunik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vera', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Titunik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54fb4c0f-2b92-5b66-a53d-66c3072919f9', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54fb4c0f-2b92-5b66-a53d-66c3072919f9'}, {'abstract': 'How to make sure older applications will run on Apple’s new operating system.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/qa-making-sure-older-mac-programs-can-tame-lion/', 'snippet': 'How to make sure older applications will run on Apple’s new operating system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Making Sure Older Mac Programs Can Tame Lion', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mac OS (Operating System)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intuit Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T10:11:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3888892-3b24-5e9d-956d-9ad9d2663c1b', 'word_count': 337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3888892-3b24-5e9d-956d-9ad9d2663c1b'}, {'abstract': 'The JF3 Freedom Bluetooth stereo headphones from Jaybird are made for this heat wave —they come with a lifetime guarantee against damage caused by sweat.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/earbuds-that-dont-sweat-sweat/', 'snippet': 'The JF3 Freedom Bluetooth stereo headphones from Jaybird are made for this heat wave —they come with a lifetime guarantee against damage caused by sweat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The JF3 Freedom Bluetooth stereo headphones from Jaybird are made for this heat wave — the headphones are designed to be sweat-proof. They even come with a lifetime guarantee against damage caused by sweat.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Earbuds That Don’t Sweat Sweat', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Headphones and Headsets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T10:51:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50c698cb-4a05-5f92-9bc7-dd5b2152574b', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50c698cb-4a05-5f92-9bc7-dd5b2152574b'}, {'abstract': 'It is the unspoken scourge of dining in the Hamptons — a game where people make three or four reservations and cancel all but one. At the last possible moment.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/losing-at-restaurant-roulette/', 'snippet': 'It is the unspoken scourge of dining in the Hamptons — a game where people make three or four reservations and cancel all but one. At the last possible moment.', 'lead_paragraph': '“You should feel honored we are here tonight,” said the young woman at the bar.“I do feel honored. Any particular reason?” I asked.“We had reservations at three restaurants and chose this one.”“She’s not kidding,” said her boyfriend.“I’m delighted,” I said. “But I feel bad for the other guys.”“Don’t worry,” she said. “We called and canceled.”“We’re very polite people,” added her beau. “Picky, but polite.”“Just curious,” I said. “When did you call them?”“When was it, honey? Six, six-thirty?”“Yes. Just before we came over to Southfork.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Losing at Restaurant Roulette', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reservations (Airlines, Hotels, Etc.)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hamptons (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T11:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Buschel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buschel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c25f718-a6d2-52a0-ab81-acce863ee20e', 'word_count': 1148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c25f718-a6d2-52a0-ab81-acce863ee20e'}, {'abstract': 'Let’s put 60 seconds on the proverbial clock and see how many we can come up with, shall we?', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/alex-trebek-chases-suspected-burglar-inspiring-awful-jeopardy-jokes/', 'snippet': 'Let’s put 60 seconds on the proverbial clock and see how many we can come up with, shall we?', 'lead_paragraph': 'KGO Television in San Francisco reports that the “Jeopardy!” host Alex Trebek appeared Wednesday at the National Geographic World Championship on crutches, having torn his Achilles tendon earlier that morning when he chased after a burglar who broke into his room and tried to steal cash and other items from him. (The suspect, a 56-year-old woman, was arrested and charged with felony burglary and possession of stolen property.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Alex Trebek Chases Suspected Burglar, Inspiring Awful ‘Jeopardy!’ Jokes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T11:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97877489-b633-53bf-add6-79b50b657a94', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97877489-b633-53bf-add6-79b50b657a94'}, {'abstract': 'Two explosions struck outside a bank in Saddam Hussein’s hometown, as soldiers were lining up to cash paychecks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/world/middleeast/29iraq.html', 'snippet': 'Two explosions struck outside a bank in Saddam Hussein’s hometown, as soldiers were lining up to cash paychecks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — Two explosions struck outside a bank in Tikrit, Saddam Hussein’s hometown, on Thursday, just as soldiers were lining up to cash their paychecks. The first report on casualties indicated that 12 people died and 28 were wounded, a local official said.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Twin Blasts Leave 12 Dead in Iraqi City', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Civilians Killed in Attack on Iraqi Soldiers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T11:19:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Arango', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arango', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ec85e31-7fbf-59cb-9715-d53f549f843e', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ec85e31-7fbf-59cb-9715-d53f549f843e'}, {'abstract': 'Things falling apart, again.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/eurofail/', 'snippet': 'Things falling apart, again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For some reason events in European bonds markets aren’t making big headlines. But they should be: even as the GOP does its best to destroy America’s credit, things are falling apart, with a vengeance, on the other side of the Atlantic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eurofail', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T11:31:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d34a8e5-efb2-5684-9240-fc1e9c3d3f11', 'word_count': 204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d34a8e5-efb2-5684-9240-fc1e9c3d3f11'}, {'abstract': 'Emma Marris, an environmental journalist, suggests that it is no longer feasible to focus so much attention on dwindling pockets of wilderness while ignoring the many other spaces that could still be reclaimed as nature.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/q-and-a-the-rambunctious-garden/', 'snippet': 'Emma Marris, an environmental journalist, suggests that it is no longer feasible to focus so much attention on dwindling pockets of wilderness while ignoring the many other spaces that could still be reclaimed as nature.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Madrona Woods cascade down a hillside and through a ravine, connecting the Seattle neighborhood where I live with Lake Washington below. Thanks to volunteer efforts and timely financing, native plants thrive along the pathways, and a restored creek empties into the lake at a recreated cove, offering habitat for salmon fingerlings. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A.: ‘The Rambunctious Garden’', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Invasive Species', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wilderness Areas', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seattle (Wash)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T11:45:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bryn Nelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13094f46-328f-52f5-ae0a-b503857e02de', 'word_count': 1042, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13094f46-328f-52f5-ae0a-b503857e02de'}, {'abstract': 'Harmony Santana is among a small group of openly transgender actors who are increasingly recognizable and finding a place on screen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/movies/new-roles-for-transgender-performers.html', 'snippet': 'Harmony Santana is among a small group of openly transgender actors who are increasingly recognizable and finding a place on screen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR someone whose only acting experience was playing a Boy George lookalike in a high school production of the musical “The Wedding Singer,” Harmony Santana is having an incredible year. Ms. Santana is making her big-screen debut in Rashaad Ernesto Green’s coming-out drama “Gun Hill Road,” which had its premiere in January at the Sundance Film Festival. Last month the movie made a splash on the gay film-festival circuit, opening Outfest in Los Angeles and closing Newfest in New York. It opens commercially in New York on Aug. 5.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31TRANSGENDER2-SPAN/31TRANSGENDER2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31TRANSGENDER2-SPAN/31TRANSGENDER2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31TRANSGENDER2-SPAN/31TRANSGENDER2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1014, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31TRANSGENDER2-SPAN/31TRANSGENDER2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31TRANSGENDER2-SPAN/31TRANSGENDER2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When They Play Women, It’s Not Just an Act', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When They Play Women, It’s Not Just an Act', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transgender and Transsexual', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T12:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8acafde-1e2d-5d37-828f-ede34c1cdae4', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8acafde-1e2d-5d37-828f-ede34c1cdae4'}, {'abstract': 'Italy paid almost a full percentage point more than a month ago at an auction of 10-year government bonds, in the latest sign of investor jitters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/business/global/italys-borrowing-costs-jump.html', 'snippet': 'Italy paid almost a full percentage point more than a month ago at an auction of 10-year government bonds, in the latest sign of investor jitters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Italy’s borrowing costs rose almost a full percentage point from a month ago at an auction of 10-year bonds Thursday, to the highest rate in more than a decade, in another troubling sign that the latest rescue package for the euro zone has not eased investors’ concerns.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/business/global/29euro-span/29euro-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 738, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': "Italy's Borrowing Costs Jump", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bond Yields in Euro Zone Countries Continue an Upward March', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T12:01:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/afab9627-2e40-5341-a122-2022a016a2bd', 'word_count': 535, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afab9627-2e40-5341-a122-2022a016a2bd'}, {'abstract': 'As users flock to apps, redefining the gaming world, the company’s 3DS has not sold well and will be heavily discounted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/technology/Nintendo-earnings.html', 'snippet': 'As users flock to apps, redefining the gaming world, the company’s 3DS has not sold well and will be heavily discounted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — In a striking reversal of fortune for the world’s largest video game maker, Nintendo drastically cut its annual profit outlook on Thursday and said it would deeply discount its new 3DS hand-held device as it struggles to stem a flow of users to casual online games.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/business/Nintendo/Nintendo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 858, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/business/Nintendo/Nintendo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/29/business/Nintendo/Nintendo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidt