rds': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T12:21:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7be15a7-9e5b-5e06-9c7c-b035f3e5fc8c', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7be15a7-9e5b-5e06-9c7c-b035f3e5fc8c'}, {'abstract': 'Increasing deal activity and the growth of assets under management helped the investment bank’s results.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/lazards-profit-rises-23-but-misses-estimates/', 'snippet': 'Increasing deal activity and the growth of assets under management helped the investment bank’s results.', 'lead_paragraph': '8:45 a.m. | Updated Lazard‘s second-quarter profit rose 23 percent from the period a year earlier, as increasing deal activity and growth in assets under management bolstered results, the investment bank said on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lazard Profit Rises 23%, but Misses Estimates', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lazard Ltd', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T12:27:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6db0220c-80fe-5297-96d8-0c061013821c', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6db0220c-80fe-5297-96d8-0c061013821c'}, {'abstract': 'Fifteen percent of New York City restaurants got  a B and 4 percent a C.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/after-a-year-of-inspections-69-percent-of-nyc-restaurants-have-as/', 'snippet': 'Fifteen percent of New York City restaurants got  a B and 4 percent a C.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A full year after the city’s health commissioner, Thomas A Farley, placed the first blue-and-white Grade A food-safety placard in the front window of Spark’s Deli in Long Island City, Queens, officials say nearly every restaurant in New York has been inspected and most are making the grade.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0Of the city’s 24,000 restaurants, 69 percent had been issued an A, 15 percent a B and 4 percent a C as of July 1, said Susan Craig, a health department spokeswoman. Twelve percent had “grade pending” designations.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0During the course of the year, 40 percent of the restaurants received an A grade on initial inspection, Ms. Craig said. Of the restaurants initially granted a C grade, 55 percent had advanced to an A or B grade on their next inspection. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After a Year of Inspections, 69 Percent of City Restaurants Have A’s', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Farley, Thomas A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health and Mental Hygiene Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T12:30:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43db2e78-35b0-53aa-85c7-9a967777d42b', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43db2e78-35b0-53aa-85c7-9a967777d42b'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: George Washington’s warning notwithstanding, there have been a bunch of them lately involving prominent New Yorkers. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/foreign-entanglements-new-york-style/', 'snippet': 'The Day: George Washington’s warning notwithstanding, there have been a bunch of them lately involving prominent New Yorkers. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'lead_paragraph': ' George Washington took the presidential oath of office in Lower Manhattan in 1789, but he didn’t spend much time here; the country’s nascent government soon moved to Philadelphia. Maybe if he had stuck around longer, people in this city would have taken closer to heart his later warning against foreign entanglements.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Foreign Entanglements, New York Style', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch, Edward I', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Markowitz, Marty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walder, Jay H', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T12:36:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f530b0b-3bfc-5e2e-98ea-896d01c2d364', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f530b0b-3bfc-5e2e-98ea-896d01c2d364'}, {'abstract': 'L-3 Communications plans to spin off some of its government services operations into a new company, freeing it from potential conflicts of interest with its parent.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/l-3-to-spin-off-a-government-services-unit/', 'snippet': 'L-3 Communications plans to spin off some of its government services operations into a new company, freeing it from potential conflicts of interest with its parent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'L-3 Communications Holdings said on Thursday that it planned to spin off some of its government services operations into a new company, freeing it from potential conflicts of interest with its parent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'L-3 to Spin Off a Government Services Unit', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'L-3 Communications Holdings Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Relational Investors LLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T12:56:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45c7b3b1-49b4-5769-97ae-4833e978b1d5', 'word_count': 241, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45c7b3b1-49b4-5769-97ae-4833e978b1d5'}, {'abstract': 'As a vicious drug war rages right on the other side of the Mexican border, El Paso quietly prospers. This isn’t illogical. This is just how it works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/28/magazine/elpaso-slideshow.html', 'snippet': 'As a vicious drug war rages right on the other side of the Mexican border, El Paso quietly prospers. This isn’t illogical. This is just how it works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a vicious drug war rages right on the other side of the Mexican border, El Paso quietly prospers. This isn’t illogical. This is just how it works.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Border Crossers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'El Paso (Tex)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T12:58:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/5e229820-391d-515b-9ae5-7ac777176ef4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/5e229820-391d-515b-9ae5-7ac777176ef4'}, {'abstract': 'As a vicious drug war rages on the other side of the Mexican border, El Paso is prospering.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/life-on-the-line-between-el-paso-and-juarez.html', 'snippet': 'As a vicious drug war rages on the other side of the Mexican border, El Paso is prospering.', 'lead_paragraph': 'El Paso and Ciudad Juárez lie together uncomfortably like an estranged couple, surrounded on all sides by mountains and desert. The cities are separated by the thin trickle of the Rio Grande, which flows through concrete channels, built to put an end to the river’s natural habit of changing course and muddying boundaries. One side is Texas; the other, Mexico. The border’s way of life — its business, legitimate and otherwise — has always relied upon the circumvention of this dividing line.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31elpaso_span/mag-31El-Paso-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31elpaso_span/mag-31El-Paso-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31elpaso_span/mag-31El-Paso-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31elpaso_span/31elpaso_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31elpaso_span/31elpaso_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life on the Line', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life on Line', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'El Paso (Tex)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Juarez (Mexico)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Cartels', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T12:58:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rice', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rice', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ea475d6-c2cf-5028-80cc-fb221f294628', 'word_count': 6626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ea475d6-c2cf-5028-80cc-fb221f294628'}, {'abstract': 'A team of suicide bombers orchestrated a surprise attack in the capital of southern Afghanistan’s Uruzgan Province, killing at least 19 people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/world/asia/29afghan.html', 'snippet': 'A team of suicide bombers orchestrated a surprise attack in the capital of southern Afghanistan’s Uruzgan Province, killing at least 19 people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — The Taliban launched one of their most audacious surprise attacks on Thursday, sending a squad of at least seven suicide bombers into the capital of a relatively stable southern province with the aim of assassinating the provincial governor and an influential powerbroker. Neither was killed, but security and health officials said at least 21 civilians had died in the collateral mayhem, half of them children.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Suicide Bombers Attack Afghan Provincial Capital', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Suicide Bombers Attack Afghan Provincial Capital', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khan, Jan Mohammed', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khan, Matiullah (d 2015)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T13:15:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alissa J. Rubin and Taimoor Shah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alissa', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Rubin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Taimoor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5d3e9b3-0d9f-5a9d-a5d2-48618f4683b8', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5d3e9b3-0d9f-5a9d-a5d2-48618f4683b8'}, {'abstract': 'Wade Slitkin, one of the media gurus behind the Panelfly, seeks to revolutionize storytelling in modern times.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/the-lowdown-wade-slitkin/', 'snippet': 'Wade Slitkin, one of the media gurus behind the Panelfly, seeks to revolutionize storytelling in modern times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Wade Slitkin leaves a room, the most common utterance is: “That kid’s gonna do big things.” Slitkin just has this air about him — of intelligence and keen intuition. He’s the kind of guy you would trust with your puppy. A street-smart product of inner-city public schools, Slitkin worked in “special markets” for several major book publishers, until it became clear that he was building rocket ships in the belly of a dinosaur. Two years ago, he scraped up his savings and, together with two of his buddies, Stephen Lynch and Brett Dovman, created Panelfly, an app designed to revolutionize storytelling in modern times. Cryptically described as “SuperMedia,” Panelfly, according to the official description, unlocks the full potential around a media franchise, pulling related films, books, games, interviews and art together into a singular experience, transforming how we experience entertainment altogether. At a time when everyone in the publishing and media industries is scratching their heads and bordering on desperation, Slitkin is being heralded as a boy genius.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lowdown | Wade Slitkin', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T13:15:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By IO Tillett Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Io', 'middlename': 'Tillett', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2b75540-9679-5b9b-a90a-e62ba0245c70', 'word_count': 452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2b75540-9679-5b9b-a90a-e62ba0245c70'}, {'abstract': 'Other films that will be show in competition include Roman Polanski’s “Carnage”; “Dark Horse,” a new film by Todd Solondz; William Friedkin’s film version of “Killer Joe”; and David Cronenberg’s “Dangerous Method.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/george-clooneys-ides-of-march-will-open-venice-film-festival/', 'snippet': 'Other films that will be show in competition include Roman Polanski’s “Carnage”; “Dark Horse,” a new film by Todd Solondz; William Friedkin’s film version of “Killer Joe”; and David Cronenberg’s “Dangerous Method.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' Though it was bad news for Julius Caesar “The Ides of March” has already proved a good-luck charm for George Clooney: his new political thriller of that title will make its debut as the opening night offering of the Venice Film Festival, its organizers said on Thursday. Mr. Clooney directed the film, which is adapted from the Beau Willimon play “Farragut North,” and stars as an aspiring presidential candidate who turns to a novice press secretary (played by Ryan Gosling) to help manage his campaign. Other films that will be shown in competition at the festival, which will run from Aug. 31 through Sept. 10, include Roman Polanski’s “Carnage,” his film adaptation of “God of Carnage,” starring Jodie Foster, Kate Winslet, Christoph Waltz and John C. Reilly; “Dark Horse,” a new film by Todd Solondz, starring Mia Farrow, Christopher Walken, Justin Bartha and Selma Blair; William Friedkin‘s film version of “Killer Joe,” starring Matthew McConaughey and Emile Hirsch; and David Cronenberg‘s “Dangerous Method,” his drama about Carl Jung and Sigmund Freud. The festival’s out-of-competition lineup will include “W.E.”, a new film directed by Madonna; “Contagion,” directed by Steven Soderbergh; and “Wilde Salome,” directed by and starring Al Pacino. The festival’s closing-night film will be “Damsels in Distress,” the first new feature in more than a decade from Whit Stillman. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'George Clooney’s ‘Ides of March’ Will Open Venice Film Festival', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venice International Film Festival', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blair, Selma', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Caesar, Julius', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clooney, George', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cronenberg, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foster, Jodie', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freud, Sigmund', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 