middle of peak business season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The frontiersmen of the Hudson River, as some like to call themselves, arrived over two decades ago determined to transform the filthy industrial waterway slicked with oily rainbows into an aquatic playground fit for a 21st-century city.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/nyregion/29river2/29river2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/nyregion/29river2/29river2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/nyregion/29river2/29river2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/nyregion/29river2/29river2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/nyregion/29river2/29river2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Sewage in Hudson, a Big Blow to Business', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Sewage In Hudson, A Big Blow To Business', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boats and Boating', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T13:42:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer and Adriane Quinlan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Adriane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quinlan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7e55fe1-471f-561f-8764-b01c286a7c18', 'word_count': 960, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7e55fe1-471f-561f-8764-b01c286a7c18'}, {'abstract': 'Ayman al-Zawahri cast the Syrians’ movement to topple their country’s authoritarian leader as part of a wider regional conflict with the United States and Israel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/world/asia/29zawahri.html', 'snippet': 'Ayman al-Zawahri cast the Syrians’ movement to topple their country’s authoritarian leader as part of a wider regional conflict with the United States and Israel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In what appeared to be his first video message since succeeding Osama bin Laden as the leader of Al Qaeda, Ayman al-Zawahri expressed strong support for antigovernment protesters in Syria, claiming that their movement to topple the country’s authoritarian leader was rooted in a wider regional conflict with the United States and Israel.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/world/29zawahri/29zawahri-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/29/world/29zawahri/29zawahri-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '