ntly gave two interviews and plans to make her first public appearance on Thursday, at a rally in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/nyregion/strauss-kahns-accuser-was-misquoted-lawyer-says.html', 'snippet': 'Nafissatou Diallo, who had declined to reveal her identity until this week, recently gave two interviews and plans to make her first public appearance on Thursday, at a rally in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lawyer for the hotel housekeeper who accused Dominique Strauss-Kahn of sexually assaulting her in May said Wednesday that taped conversations, two of them made a day after the encounter, prove that his client had no intention of exploiting the charges against Mr. Strauss-Kahn to make money.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/nyregion/29dsk3/29dsk3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/nyregion/29dsk3/29dsk3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/29/nyregion/29dsk3/29dsk3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Housekeeper’s Lawyer Says She Was Misquoted', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Housekeeper’s Lawyer Says She Was Misquoted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diallo, Nafissatou', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T14:10:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': "By Jim Dwyer, John Eligon and Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dwyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cbc54da-d013-5f01-b272-6f0658c01a5b', 'word_count': 1126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cbc54da-d013-5f01-b272-6f0658c01a5b'}, {'abstract': 'There aren’t any winners here, but you can argue that Republicans have sustained more damage.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/surveying-the-electoral-damage-of-the-debt-debate/', 'snippet': 'There aren’t any winners here, but you can argue that Republicans have sustained more damage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Republicans may not have scored a knockout blow on the policy elements of the debt ceiling debate. But since the remaining proposals consist entirely of spending cuts while still reducing the debt significantly, the judges ought to award them a unanimous decision unless something changes in the last round.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Surveying the Electoral Damage of the Debt Debate', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T14:10:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fc9bc69-a2b5-5d49-ae41-a65035d3a3bd', 'word_count': 1101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fc9bc69-a2b5-5d49-ae41-a65035d3a3bd'}, {'abstract': 'The singer discusses her latest song, from a new album of music written by Alan and Marilyn Bergman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/arts/music/100000000961172/barbra-streisand-so-many-stars.html', 'snippet': 'The singer discusses her latest song, from a new album of music written by Alan and Marilyn Bergman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The singer discusses her latest song, from a new album of music written by Alan and Marilyn Bergman.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/multimedia/video-streisand/video-streisand-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/22/multimedia/video-streisand/video-streisand-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/22/multimedia/video-streisand/video-streisand-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/22/multimedia/video-streisand/video-streisand-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': "Barbra Streisand: 'So Many Stars'", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T14:21:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/7636c4ae-651b-50c6-a330-b5b5c2aa5a44', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/7636c4ae-651b-50c6-a330-b5b5c2aa5a44'}, {'abstract': 'G.O.P. leaders in the House abruptly put off a vote on the debt ceiling bill Thursday night, an indication that House Speaker John A. Boehner remained short of the votes necessary to pass his bill.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/us/politics/29fiscal.html', 'snippet': 'G.O.P. leaders in the House abruptly put off a vote on the debt ceiling bill Thursday night, an indication that House Speaker John A. Boehner remained short of the votes necessary to pass his bill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Short of support from their conservative members, House Republican leaders on Thursday abruptly put off a vote on their proposal to raise the debt ceiling and cut government spending, throwing last-ditch efforts to avert a government default next week into disarray.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/us/JP-FISCAL/JP-FISCAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/29/us/JP-FISCAL/JP-FISCAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/us/JP-FISCAL/JP-FISCAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'House Puts Off Debt Vote as Press by Boehner Fails', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'House Puts Off Debt Vote As Press by Boehner Fails', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reid, Harry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T14:21:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Hulse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hulse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e22e44f-d65c-52dd-92bd-34703b92ba73', 'word_count': 1064, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e22e44f-d65c-52dd-92bd-34703b92ba73'}, {'abstract': 'The share of California Republicans who believe climate change is already under way jumped noticeably, from 30 percent to 40 percent, in the past year. But the proportion who consider it a serious threat remained unchanged at 18 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/cataclysms-climate-change-and-california-opinion/', 'snippet': 'The share of California Republicans who believe climate change is already under way jumped noticeably, from 30 percent to 40 percent, in the past year. But the proportion who consider it a serious threat remained unchanged at 18 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the pleasures of following the annual surveys of the state’s environmental attitudes by the Public Policy Institute of California is witnessing how one-time cataclysms (financial crises, tsunamis, oil spills and the like) throw the neat progression of opinion change out of whack for a year or so. And then, like a rubber band springing back into shape, the attitudes largely revert to their old patterns; the cataclysm seems to have little lasting influence.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cataclysms, Climate Change and California Opinion', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Offshore Drilling and Exploration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party (Japan)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hewlett, William and Flora, Foundation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T14:23:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57693d34-34ad-5bc5-8de1-747554df140b', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57693d34-34ad-5bc5-8de1-747554df140b'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/what-were-reading-234/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T14:29:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a88188ba-72ab-5783-8907-c4f99f72505f', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a88188ba-72ab-5783-8907-c4f99f72505f'}, {'abstract': 'The song, originally made popular by by Sérgio Mendes & Brasil ’66, is from Ms. Streisand’s coming album, “What Matters Most: Barbra Streisand Sings the Lyrics of Alan and Marilyn Bergman.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/hear-so-many-stars-a-song-from-the-new-barbra-streisand-album/', 'snippet': 'The song, originally made popular by by Sérgio Mendes & Brasil ’66, is from Ms. Streisand’s coming album, “What Matters Most: Barbra Streisand Sings the Lyrics of Alan and Marilyn Bergman.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'You know Alan and Marilyn Bergman as the husband-and-wife songwriters of award-winning songs like “The Way We Were,” “The Windmills of Your Mind” and “It Might Be You.” You know Barbra Streisand as Barbra Streisand. The three have been friends since the Bergmans first saw the 18-year-old Ms. Streisand perform at the Bon Soir, a Greenwich Village club where she shared a dressing room with Phyllis Diller, and though Ms. Streisand has since graduated to bigger performance spaces, her artistic collaboration with the Bergmans has endured. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hear ‘So Many Stars,’ a Song From the New Barbra Streisand Album', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bergman, Marilyn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Streisand, Barbra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia Records', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T14:30:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5635d824-fd3d-5688-834e-4d22b5ea695c', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5635d824-fd3d-5688-834e-4d22b5ea695c'}, {'abstract': 'The cost of treating veterans will continue to rise, a challenge to the health care law may reach the Supreme Court, two Southern states vie for the best peaches and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/thursday-reading-veteran-care-costs-will-continue-to-rise/', 'snippet': 'The cost of treating veterans will continue to rise, a challenge to the health care law may reach the Supreme Court, two Southern states vie for the best peaches and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet