York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Underground | House of Hackney', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T15:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elias Redstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elias', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Redstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/768994e6-e8c4-50c9-899a-6ec1cdc81423', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/768994e6-e8c4-50c9-899a-6ec1cdc81423'}, {'abstract': 'The most conscientious and intelligent effort to turn 5-to-10-minute Webisodes into a half-hour television comedy — “Web Therapy,” on Showtime — is not doomed to failure, exactly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/arts/television/web-therapy-transitions-to-tv-on-showtime-review.html', 'snippet': 'The most conscientious and intelligent effort to turn 5-to-10-minute Webisodes into a half-hour television comedy — “Web Therapy,” on Showtime — is not doomed to failure, exactly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GIVEN the (purportedly) rising status of, and increased investment in, online video, you would expect Showtime’s television version of “Web Therapy” — one of the most accomplished and high-profile of Web series — to be getting a lot more attention than it is. It’s hard to generate buzz, though, when a show is given little or no promotion and buried at 11 p.m. on Tuesdays.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31WATCHLIST1_SPAN/31WATCHLIST1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 331, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'ima