, 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T18:30:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/7f168c43-56f7-5f34-8528-361500a6327d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/7f168c43-56f7-5f34-8528-361500a6327d'}, {'abstract': 'Under a hazy sky, with the temperature in the 80s, jubilant Packer backers filled about a third of the stands at Lambeau Field for the annual shareholders meeting.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/celebrating-packers-revival-at-annual-meeting/', 'snippet': 'Under a hazy sky, with the temperature in the 80s, jubilant Packer backers filled about a third of the stands at Lambeau Field for the annual shareholders meeting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GREEN BAY, Wis. — Football is back. That was quite evident Thursday morning, when fans tailgated in the Lambeau Field parking lot, jammed the Packers Pro Shop and the Packers Hall of Fame, and posed for pictures in front of the bronze statues of Vince Lombardi and Curly Lambeau.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Packers’ Faithful Celebrate a Revival at Annual Meeting', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T18:30:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patty La Duca', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patty', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Duca', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81588c80-475e-5714-8115-e03ee89373f1', 'word_count': 469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81588c80-475e-5714-8115-e03ee89373f1'}, {'abstract': 'With a service called Heathrow by Invitation, travelers (willing to pay) never need set foot in the terminal.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/royal-treatment-at-heathrow/', 'snippet': 'With a service called Heathrow by Invitation, travelers (willing to pay) never need set foot in the terminal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Any commoner with deep pockets can now get the sort of arrival and departure service at Heathrow Airport near London usually reserved for royalty and popes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Royal Treatment at Heathrow', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Heathrow Airport (London)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T18:35:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30ee2326-7ed7-5961-964b-3809364963dd', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30ee2326-7ed7-5961-964b-3809364963dd'}, {'abstract': 'David Pogue seeks answers to a condundrum: Why does Apple have Lion users drag the scrollbar up to move the page up?', 'web_url': 'https://pogue.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/zen-and-the-art-of-scrolling/', 'snippet': 'David Pogue seeks answers to a condundrum: Why does Apple have Lion users drag the scrollbar up to move the page up?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Master, may I enter?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zen and the Art of Scrolling', 'kicker': 'Pogue&#039;s Posts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T18:41:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa3cb7c2-9933-5285-a142-4134aea2750e', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa3cb7c2-9933-5285-a142-4134aea2750e'}, {'abstract': 'Evelyn Waugh’s 1938 novel “Scoop” features journalists and the police in cahoots, and a press lord with a cult of personality. Sound familiar?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/07/books/review/the-great-fleet-street-novel-evelyn-waughs-scoop.html', 'snippet': 'Evelyn Waugh’s 1938 novel “Scoop” features journalists and the police in cahoots, and a press lord with a cult of personality. Sound familiar?', 'lead_paragraph': '“If you’d gone to a publisher in 1981 with a proposal for a science-fiction novel that consisted of a really clear and simple description of the world today,” William Gibson told The Paris Review recently, “they’d have read your proposal and said: ‘Well, it’s impossible. This is ridiculous.’ ” You’d have gotten a similar reaction if you’d gone to a publisher earlier this year with a proposal for a novel that went something like this: journalists on a British tabloid hack into and delete the voice mail messages of a missing (murdered, it turns out) teenage girl. Their activities are exposed by a rival outfit, to widespread public outrage. The paper in question is shut down by its owner, despite having a circulation of millions. Senior policemen, who hired former journalists from the paper as press officers, resign. Evidence emerges of illegal payments from the paper to corrupt police in exchange for information about celebrities, politicians and crime victims. Several journalists are arrested, including two former editors of the paper, one of whom had worked for the prime minister. A whistle-blower is found dead (though police say it isn’t suspicious). The paper’s owner, mogul of an international media empire, is summoned to testify before Parliament. And the allegations of wrongdoing just keep coming.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/07/books/review/Jones/Jones-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 614, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/07/books/review/Jones/Jones-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/08/07/books/review/Jones/Jones-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Great Fleet Street Novel', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Murdochgate Foretold', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waugh, Evelyn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News International Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 6, '