domestic food specialty identified as a “hungarikum,” or tracking down every up-and-coming designer, a weekend here provides a glimpse of the city’s justifiable pride, as well as an authentic sense of place.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31HOURS_SPAN/31HOURS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31HOURS_SPAN/31HOURS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 225}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31HOURS_SPAN/31HOURS_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31HOURS_SPAN/31HOURS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31HOURS_SPAN/31HOURS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Budapest', 'kicker': '36 Hours', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Budapest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Budapest (Hungary)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T19:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evan Rail', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rail', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51b43125-de4c-56b3-9cc4-1f2350519ba9', 'word_count': 1385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51b43125-de4c-56b3-9cc4-1f2350519ba9'}, {'abstract': 'This week: summer percussion special. Larry Rohter talks music and philosophy with the percussionist/composer Adam Rudolph; Ben Ratliff and Nate Chinen sort out the new vibraphone scene in jazz. And new CDs by Kelly Rowland, Joss Stone and Cerebral Ballzy. Ben Ratliff is the host.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2011/07/28/arts/music/28popcast.html', 'snippet': 'This week: summer percussion special. Larry Rohter talks music and philosophy with the percussionist/composer Adam Rudolph; Ben Ratliff and Nate Chinen sort out the new vibraphone scene in jazz. And new CDs by Kelly Rowland, Joss Stone and Cerebra...', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week: summer percussion special. Larry Rohter talks music and philosophy with the percussionist/composer Adam Rudolph; Ben Ratliff and Nate Chinen sort out the new vibraphone scene in jazz. And new CDs by Kelly Rowland, Joss Stone and Cerebral Ballzy. Ben Ratliff is the host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Summer Percussion Special', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Podcasts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T19:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/3f040bb5-7622-5176-a8ba-18ae861ed9d4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/3f040bb5-7622-5176-a8ba-18ae861ed9d4'}, {'abstract': 'A foundation tries an innovative approach to finding places to use its money to foster progress — through innovation.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/innovation-from-the-bottom-up/', 'snippet': 'A foundation tries an innovative approach to finding places to use its money to foster progress — through innovation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Rockefeller Foundation, in deciding how to dispense tens of millions of dollars in grants each year, typically sends scouting teams around the world to search out problems impeding human well-being and organizations or individuals working to address them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Innovation from the Bottom Up', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edison, Thomas A', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rockefeller, John D', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodin, Judith S', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'InnoCentive', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Rice Research Institute', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rockefeller Foundation', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Pennsylvania', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T19:04:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43e3db79-b348-5bdf-9562-012837018d30', 'word_count': 1874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43e3db79-b348-5bdf-9562-012837018d30'}, {'abstract': 'Moral agency in “Breaking Bad” and “The Sopranos”', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/good-and-evil-on-cable/', 'snippet': 'Moral agency in “Breaking Bad” and “The Sopranos”', 'lead_paragraph': "In the interest of talking about something besides the debt ceiling for a moment, here’s an engaging Chuck Klosterman piece comparing moral agency on “Mad Men,” “The Wire” and “The Sopranos” to the moral landscape of AMC’s returning hit “Breaking Bad” (currently the best show on television, for my money). Klosterman notes that “all of these series grapple with morality — more than anything else, it’s the reason they’re better than the shows around them.” But he argues that “the first three examples all create realities where individual agency is detached” and questions of personal moral choice aren’t foregrounded: “Mad Men” because it’s set in the 1960s, making its characters’ actions more a “commentary on the era” than a window into their souls, “The Wire” because it’s “more political than philosophical,” bent on constantly emphasizing the way “everyone is simultaneously good and bad” in order to offer a critique of American society as a whole, and “The Sopranos” because the fact all its major characters were involved with organized crime and its protagonist murdered people for money meant that there were never any unresolved questions over Tony Soprano’s ‘goodness.'” (We knew he was “fundamentally bad,” Klosterman argues, because “otherwise he couldn’t exist as the person he was.”)", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Good and Evil on Cable', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T19:04:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfdbde3a-43fc-558c-a0ee-d9c22dfdee66', 'word_count': 1019, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfdbde3a-43fc-558c-a0ee-d9c22dfdee66'}, {'abstract': 'After a five-year run as coach of the United States men’s national team, Bob Bradley is fired.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/bradley-fired-as-national-team-coach/', 'snippet': 'After a five-year run as coach of the United States men’s national team, Bob Bradley is fired.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sunil Gulati, president of United States Soccer, announced on Thursday that Bob Bradley had been relieved of his duties as head coach of the men’s national team. More details here. Is this the solution for the program’s recent struggles?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bradley Fired As National Team Coach', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coaches and Managers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradley, Bob', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "United States Men's National Soccer Team", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T19:12:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa406393-319e-5db2-a3ee-823121390b4d', 'word_count': 41, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa406393-319e-5db2-a3ee-823121390b4d'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Washington, Libya, Iraq and Thailand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/07/28/blogs/20110728POD.html', 'snippet': 'Photographs from Washington, Libya, Iraq and Thailand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from Washington, Libya, Iraq and Thailand.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/blogs/20110728POD-slide-PD7T/20110728POD-slide-PD7T-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: Washington and Elsewhere', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T19:13:16+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Blogs', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/20ff0009-e603-5f33-b4a4-78b47bfd8231', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/20ff0009-e603-5f33-b4a4-78b47bfd8231'}, {'abstract': 'Less than half of Netflix users watch streaming video on their computer, preferring to connect through video-game consoles, while almost 90 percent of Hulu users watch on their computers.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/the-netflix-hulu-divide/', 'snippet': 'Less than half of Netflix users watch streaming video on their computer, preferring to connect through video-game consoles, while almost 90 percent of Hulu users watch on their computers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While it has been known that Netflix and Hulu streaming users are consuming internet bandwidth at a huge and ever increasing rate, what hasn’t been widely reported is just what they’ve been watching with these services. According to a recent survey conducted by Nielsen, less than half of Netflix users watch on their home computer, instead opting to connect via video-game consoles hooked up to their television. Conversely, almost 90 percent of Hulu users watch directly on their computer. Thirty-six percent of all Netflix users watch both movies and television shows while, unsurprisingly, 73 percent of Hulu users watch television programs. Only 9 percent who use Hulu use the service to watch movies. Streaming on mobile devices such as cellphones or tablets like the iPad hasn’t gone beyond early adopters just yet – only 6 percent of Netflix users and 3 percent of all Hulu users watch on the go.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Netflix-Hulu Divide', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lady Gaga', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marshall, Rob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hulu.com', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen Media Research', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T19:34:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac7d9931-3e74-59f1-a315-c9bdefab1c2a', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 