t Lost” on our Web site, which is a collaboration with famed dance troupe Pilobolus. In addition, the band cooked up an interactive feature, built for Google Chrome, where vie...', 'lead_paragraph': '“We get a lot of calls from people who say, ‘I’ve invented a nunchuck!’,” says OK Go’s lead singer, Damian Kulash. “So it’s rare to get a call from someone you worship creatively that heaps praise on you before you can on them. It’s like, ‘No, this call’s going the wrong way!’”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'OK Go Finds a Dance Partner', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T20:17:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tony Gervino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gervino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77fb2cdb-e7c5-55a5-934b-4785878aae37', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77fb2cdb-e7c5-55a5-934b-4785878aae37'}, {'abstract': 'What to eat on a debut trip to New York, and where to feed a world-weary New Yorker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/dining/dining-advice-for-a-first-trip-to-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'What to eat on a debut trip to New York, and where to feed a world-weary New Yorker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dead of summer, but still the postman trudges forth to the mountaintop with questions for Mr. Critic, meditating on his zafu pillow. This week brought queries about a first-time trip to New York City, about where to feed a world-weary New Yorker and about where to hold a rehearsal dinner for American expatriates in from London and seeking British food. You may add your own voices to the choir at the Diner’s Journal blog. And feel free to send your questions about restaurants in New York City to dinejournal@nytimes.com.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29CRITIC2_SPAN/29CRITIC2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29CRITIC2_SPAN/29CRITIC2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29CRITIC2_SPAN/29CRITIC2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 717, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29CRITIC2_SPAN/29CRITIC2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29CRITIC2_SPAN/29CRITIC2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New York at First Bite: Oh, the Places You’ll Go', 'kicker': 'Hey, Mr. Critic', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York at First Bite: Oh, the Places You’ll Go', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T20:23:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dbe850f-2929-5971-b035-da315c349b28', 'word_count': 1360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dbe850f-2929-5971-b035-da315c349b28'}, {'abstract': 'The storied co-op on the Upper West Side has been accused of discriminating against an owner who wanted to buy another apartment. A judge rejected motions to dismiss the case.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/discrimination-suit-against-the-dakota-allowed-to-proceed/', 'snippet': 'The storied co-op on the Upper West Side has been accused of discriminating against an owner who wanted to buy another apartment. A judge rejected motions to dismiss the case.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A racial discrimination suit against the storied Dakota co-op apartment building can go forward, a State Supreme Court judge ruled. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Discrimination Suit Against the Dakota Can Proceed', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooperatives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fletcher, Alphonse Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T20:29:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Cardwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cardwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e29fcad2-e6b2-5d8f-b78b-6e3c99ba2c27', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e29fcad2-e6b2-5d8f-b78b-6e3c99ba2c27'}, {'abstract': 'The men who people our movies.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/a-taxonomy-of-movie-men/', 'snippet': 'The men who people our movies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In their joint column this weekend, the Times film critics A.O. Scott and Manohla Dargis provide an exhaustive (and quite funny) field guide to modern male cinematic archetypes. The round-up includes the Big Baby (e.g. “smiley, sexless Kevin James” or the Zach Galafianakis type, “edgier, crueler, with beards”); the Brave Boy (“brooding and brash,” like Harry Potter or [10 points here if you can name Shia LaBoeuf’s character in the Transformer films]*); the Husband (“henpecked and undersexed”); and the Wimp (“subsets include the Nerd, the Geek, the Nebbish and, often offensively, the Gay Guy”).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Taxonomy of Movie Men', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T20:33:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Sternbergh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sternbergh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7738cc58-986e-5bc8-99c7-571b88d0b411', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7738cc58-986e-5bc8-99c7-571b88d0b411'}, {'abstract': 'Police were examining a possible suicide in the death of the former pitcher Hideki Irabu.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/ex-yankee-irabu-found-dead/', 'snippet': 'Police were examining a possible suicide in the death of the former pitcher Hideki Irabu.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hideki Irabu, the former Yankees pitcher who struggled to live up to grand expectations after coming over from Japan, was found dead in Los Angeles on Thursday. He was 42. Police are investigating Irabu’s death as a possible suicide, according to various reports.\xa0Irabu came to the United States in 1997 billed as one of Japan’s premier right-handed pitchers. The Padres owned his rights, but Irabu refused to play for any team but the Yankees. San Diego traded him to the Yankees, who signed him to a four-year, $12.8 million contract.\xa0His career began with such promise, when he struck out nine and allowed two runs as the Yankees defeated Detroit in his major league debut, on July 10, 1997. That start proved the highlight of the season. He was banished to the bullpen and the minor leagues, and he finished the year 5-4 with a 7.09 earned run average.\xa0In 1999, Steinbrenner erupted after Irabu failed to cover first base during an exhibition game, calling him a fat toad. \xa0\xa0In three seasons with the Yankees, Irabu was 29-20 and pitched in only one postseason game, when he came out of the bullpen to allow eight runs to the Red Sox in Game 3 of the 1999 American League Championship Series. Two months later, Irabu was traded to Montreal, where he started 14 games over 2000 and 2001, before finishing his career with Texas in 2002. He retired in 2004 after pitching two seasons for the Hanshin Tigers in Japan. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ex-Yankee Irabu Found Dead', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suicides and Suicide Attempts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Irabu, Hideki', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T20:37:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf93a2c2-de33-51f9-ac6f-a7db5825e1a4', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf93a2c2-de33-51f9-ac6f-a7db5825e1a4'}, {'abstract': 'The House speaker was not the only one who got a little “verklempt” at a gathering of freshmen discussing the debt ceiling negotiations.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/boehners-teary-moment/', 'snippet': 'The House speaker was not the only one who got a little “verklempt” at a gathering of freshmen discussing the debt ceiling negotiations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By all accounts, House Speaker John A. Boehner has been a relentless, cold-eyed negotiator in the debt ceiling face-off with President Obama.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Teary Moment for Boehner', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roby, Martha', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T20:38:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23f62c9c-8434-5f0b-a996-ccb7763c07be', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23f62c9c-8434-5f0b-a996-ccb7763c07be'}, {'abstract': 'Europe and America: who’s worse?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/the-halt-and-the-lame/', 'snippet': 'Europe and America: who’s worse?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Johnson writes about who’s worse — America or Europe? Basically I agree with his assessment: Europe has more fundamental problems in sheer economic terms, because it adopted a single currency without the necessary institutions to make it workable. America has a long-run budget problem, but our current mess is entirely political. Unfortunately, that doesn’t make it any easier to solve.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Halt and the Lame', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T20:41:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26d851cb-6542-5485-a468-a24cd5077274', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26d851cb-6542-5485-a468-a24cd5077274'}, {'abstract': 'Juergen Teller, Yelena Yemchuk and Federico Claravino are all preoccupied with the ex-Soviet state.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/the-curatorialist-ukrainian-passport/', 'snippet': 'Juergen Teller, Yelena Yemchuk and Federico Claravino are all preoccupied with the ex-Soviet state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in 2007, Peter Doroshenko, who was then the artistic director of the Victor Pinchuk Foundation, asked a handful of artists to respond to the question “Where is Ukraine going?” for an installation at the Ukrainian pavilion during the Venice Biennale. The photographer Juergen Teller visited Kiev three times in five months for the project, and the photos he took on these trips speak to the extraordinary wealth he saw there. Teller’s view of Kiev is steeped in the language of fashion and excess — models, hair, makeup, styling — and interspersed with more candid snaps of everyday scenes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Culturialist | Ukrainian Passport', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T20:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emma Reeves', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reeves', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a49479b-9601-58bf-b09c-089990d3ca9b', 'word_count': 890, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a49479b-9601-58bf-b09c-089990d3ca9b'}, {'abstract': 'Rebekah Brooks, a former editor of The News of the World, has said she helped publicize a campaign for better child protection by the mother of an 8-year-old girl who was murdered in 2000. The police now say that woman’s phone might have been hacked.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/brooks-boasted-of-papers-campaign-in-murdered-girls-memory/', 'snippet': 'Rebekah Brooks, a former editor of The News of the World, has said she helped publicize a campaign for better child protection by the mother of an 8-year-old girl who was murdered in 2000. The police now say that woman’s phone might have been hack...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated | Friday | 11:31 a.m. As my colleagues Sarah Lyall and Ravi Somaiya report, British police officials investigating the hacking of phones by News of the World journalists “have added to the list of probable victims a woman whose 8-year-old daughter was murdered by a repeat sex offender in 2000.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brooks Boasted of Paper’s Campaign in Murdered Girl’s Memory', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Rebekah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dowler, Milly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mulcaire, Glenn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News International Ltd', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scotland Yard (Metropolitan Police Service)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T20:46:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c735763-3fce-5c1e-b8d6-1677d35fc73a', 'word_count': 1252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c735763-3fce-5c1e-b8d6-1677d35fc73a'}, {'abstract': 'A three-par golf course in the Hamptons known as the Pox is popular for its lack of snobbery, and for its pancakes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/07/31/nyregion/20110731JOINTss.html', 'snippet': 'A three-par golf course in the Hamptons known as the Pox is popular for its lack of snobbery, and for its pancakes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A three-par golf course in the Hamptons known as the Pox is popular for its lack of snobbery, and for its pancakes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/