ng the debt limit to accommodate spending. A look at some of the issues in the debate over the nation’s debt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'America has a long history of raising the debt limit to accommodate spending. A look at some of the issues in the debate over the nation’s debt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Charting the American Debt Crisis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T21:59:04+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/fc9324bd-22f8-5ee3-8390-034fadff81e5', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/fc9324bd-22f8-5ee3-8390-034fadff81e5'}, {'abstract': 'This week, answers to readers’ questions about whether guests with smelly feet should be forced to wash them, whether women should reapply makeup at the table when dining out, and other issues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/fashion/mother-in-laws-use-of-facebook-concerns-mother-to-be.html', 'snippet': 'This week, answers to readers’ questions about whether guests with smelly feet should be forced to wash them, whether women should reapply makeup at the table when dining out, and other issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My husband and I are expecting our first child, and we plan to tell his parents after the first trimester. My mother-in-law posts photos and personal information on Facebook indiscriminately. But for privacy and safety reasons, I prefer no mention of my pregnancy on Facebook or anything about our child, even after he is born. Subtle messages don’t always register with her. How can I inform her that her involvement in my baby’s life will wane if she does not comply with my wishes? Anonymous ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Bringing Up Baby on Facebook', 'kicker': 'Social Q’s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Bringing Up Baby on Facebook', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T21:59:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cea3ad6-6eb7-5777-a7e6-9fc35566671d', 'word_count': 565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cea3ad6-6eb7-5777-a7e6-9fc35566671d'}, {'abstract': '“Point Blank” is a French thriller involving cops, gangsters, a kidnapping and a breakneck chase.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/movies/point-blank-by-fred-cavaye-review.html', 'snippet': '“Point Blank” is a French thriller involving cops, gangsters, a kidnapping and a breakneck chase.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Point Blank” is an exhilarating adrenaline rush with breakneck momentum so shrewdly calculated that you could imagine its French director, Fred Cavayé, standing by with a heart-rate monitor. Just when the beats per minute have accelerated beyond 160, and you are gasping for air, a momentary respite gives you a few seconds to wipe your brow, catch your breath and take a sip from your water bottle. Then you’re shoved back into the race. I can’t recall another thriller that has maintained this kind of velocity without going kablooey and losing its train of thought. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29POINT_SPAN/POINT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29POINT_SPAN/POINT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29POINT_SPAN/POINT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29POINT_SPAN/29POINT_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29POINT_SPAN/29POINT_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Paris, and Everyone Is Rotten but the Man Chased by the Police', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Point Blank'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s Paris, and Everyone Is Rotten but the Man Chased by the Police', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T21:59:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c99aedc8-048e-5a49-8837-85b12b382a44', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c99aedc8-048e-5a49-8837-85b12b382a44'}, {'abstract': '“Her mouth filled the screen. Purple lip gloss, clear braces. ‘Still think I’m too young?’ ”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/excerpt-this-beautiful-life-by-helen-schulman.html', 'snippet': '“Her mouth filled the screen. Purple lip gloss, clear braces. ‘Still think I’m too young?’ ”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Her mouth filled the screen. Purple lip gloss, clear braces.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘This Beautiful Life’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T22:02:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helen Schulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/4667fc08-d3f6-5f5e-b5ec-6c0c8f99fefa', 'word_count': 2835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4667fc08-d3f6-5f5e-b5ec-6c0c8f99fefa'}, {'abstract': 'In her professional life, as an editor at Salon.com, The New York Observer and The Los Angeles Times, Maria Russo has seen and felt the disruptive effects of the Internet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/up-front-maria-russo.html', 'snippet': 'In her professional life, as an editor at Salon.com, The New York Observer and The Los Angeles Times, Maria Russo has seen and felt the disruptive effects of the Internet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maria Russo is at work on a memoir of her Roman Catholic girlhood in New York, where, she recalled in a recent e-mail, as a student from Queens enrolled at Hunter College High School, she was part of an earlier wave of upwardly mobile teenagers like Jake, the troubled son in Helen Schulman’s latest novel, “This Beautiful Life.” Although, Russo explained, “we didn’t have cellphones and had to rely strictly on word of mouth about where the parties were.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 882, 'width': 554, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Maria Russo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Russo, Maria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T22:02:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f927582c-9687-53e0-a7bc-a40bef802b0c', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f927582c-9687-53e0-a7bc-a40bef802b0c'}, {'abstract': 'In this timely novel, a family’s Manhattan life comes crashing down when their 15-year-old forwards a sexually explicit video made for him, unsolicited, by a girl two years younger.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/this-beautiful-life-by-helen-schulman-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this timely novel, a family’s Manhattan life comes crashing down when their 15-year-old forwards a sexually explicit video made for him, unsolicited, by a girl two years younger.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Helen Schulman’s latest novel tells the story of the Bergamots, a family of four whose expensive new Manhattan life comes crashing down when 15-year-old Jake forwards to a friend a sexually explicit video made for him, unsolicited, by a 13-year-old girl named Daisy Cavanaugh. As the video, forwarded again and again, goes viral, the tabloid media go bananas, linking Jake and Daisy in an ominous and humiliating celebrity. What can the future hold for unformed, vulnerable kids who bumble their way into the lowliest realm of the permanent record that is the Internet? (Or, in \xadDaisy’s case, reach it by simulating sex with a toy baseball bat.) Should their parents be held responsible, or are they equally victimized by the seductions and traps of digital life?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Russo/Russo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Russo/Russo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Russo/Russo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Russo/Russo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Russo/Russo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Total Family Breakdown, 21st-Century Manhattan Style', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Modern Family', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schulman, Helen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T22:02:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a7e7818-1970-5987-a7f3-e46fcfdbb7ec', 'word_count': 1316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a7e7818-1970-5987-a7f3-e46fcfdbb7ec'}, {'abstract': 'The pianist Dmitri Alexeev performed at the International Keyboard Institute and Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/arts/music/dmitri-alexeev-at-keyboard-festival-review.html', 'snippet': 'The pianist Dmitri Alexeev performed at the International Keyboard Institute and Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seems odd that a pianist as accomplished as Dmitri Alexeev does not perform in New York more often than he does. Now 63, Mr. Alexeev studied at the Moscow Conservatory and won a string of competition prizes in the early 1970s. But he has sidestepped the stereotypes of both Russian pianism (big, brawny and loud) and the international competition style (dazzling but risk averse). His recital on Wednesday as part of the International Keyboard Institute and Festival at the Mannes College the New School for Music showed him to be a thoughtful, poetic player willing to go out on a limb, interpretively, usually to superb effect. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29KEYBOARD/KEYBOARD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 441, 'width': 342, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29KEYBOARD/29KEYBOARD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29KEYBOARD/29KEYBOARD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 