None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-28T23:11:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e79193d-5117-53c4-b1d1-e1720036e59b', 'word_count': 339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e79193d-5117-53c4-b1d1-e1720036e59b'}, {'abstract': 'The NYC Social Sports Club offers indoor broomball, a distant cousin of hockey that began in Canada.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/arts/indoor-broomball-at-nyc-social-sports-club.html', 'snippet': 'The NYC Social Sports Club offers indoor broomball, a distant cousin of hockey that began in Canada.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BROOMBALL, a remote cousin of ice hockey, has come a long way since street-car workers in Canada began swatting a small soccer ball around the ice with corn brooms 100 years or so ago. Brooms, for one thing, are just so passé. For another, broomball players need no ice.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29URBAN_SPAN/29URBAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29URBAN_SPAN/29URBAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29URBAN_SPAN/29URBAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type