s', 'value': 'Azaria, Hank', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:06:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1a28774-a97a-52f1-b517-51a17d884c7c', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1a28774-a97a-52f1-b517-51a17d884c7c'}, {'abstract': 'Big banks and companies have withdrawn $37.5 billion from money market funds that invest in Treasury debt, underscoring how institutions are rethinking their view of Treasury notes.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/debt-ceiling-debate-rattles-short-term-credit-markets/', 'snippet': 'Big banks and companies have withdrawn $37.5 billion from money market funds that invest in Treasury debt, underscoring how institutions are rethinking their view of Treasury notes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The reverberations of Washington’s impasse over a debt deal are already being felt in the short-term credit markets, a key artery of the economy that daily supplies trillions of dollars of credit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debt Ceiling Impasse Rattles Short-Term Credit Markets', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fidelity Investments', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:07:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nelson D. Schwartz and Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nelson', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed466206-5210-52db-9dd8-f8968b5a215c', 'word_count': 1042, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed466206-5210-52db-9dd8-f8968b5a215c'}, {'abstract': 'Norway’s consensus culture or Jante Law, may have taken a blow after the killings last Friday in Oslo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/world/europe/29norway.html', 'snippet': 'Norway’s consensus culture or Jante Law, may have taken a blow after the killings last Friday in Oslo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OSLO — Nearly all Norwegians of a certain age know where they were when Oddvar Bra suddenly broke his ski pole in the final sprint of a championship ski race in 1982, and Norway had to settle for a tie with the Soviet Union. But the common expression, “Where were you when Bra broke his pole?” has suddenly given way to a darker question — where were you when Anders Behring Breivik was killing Norway’s children?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/world/29norway/29norway-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/29/world/29norway/29norway-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/world/29norway/29norway-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank