'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tokyo Electric Power Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shortages', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:29:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Norimitsu Onishi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Norimitsu', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Onishi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2da5157-6191-5bd4-9249-5da1b836651c', 'word_count': 1281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2da5157-6191-5bd4-9249-5da1b836651c'}, {'abstract': 'How Bull Run turned Horace Greeley’s belligerence into a plea for peace.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/on-to-richmond-or-not/', 'snippet': 'How Bull Run turned Horace Greeley’s belligerence into a plea for peace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It didn’t take long for Horace Greeley, the nation’s most loved, hated and widely read newspaper editor, to become impatient with the progress of the war. From the moment news of Major Robert Anderson’s surrender at Fort Sumter reached New York City, drumbeats and bugle calls for immediate military action could be heard in the columns of Greeley’s paper, the New-York Tribune. Never mind the logistical challenge of assembling, arming and training a suitable military force — the people, or at least the editors, were ready, and there was hardly a moment to lose. What appeared to be lacking, from Greeley’s perspective, was a necessary vigor from President Lincoln and his administration. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On to Richmond! Or Not', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greeley, Horace', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:30:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By James M. Lundberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9832aaba-1acc-5418-be23-fd3d962b66a6', 'word_count': 1557, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9832aaba-1acc-5418-be23-fd3d962b66a6'}, {'abstract': 'A former Japanese star who wanted to play for the Yankees, Irabu apparently hanged himself in a home near Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/sports/baseball/ex-yankee-hideki-irabu-is-dead-at-42.html', 'snippet': 'A former Japanese star who wanted to play for the Yankees, Irabu apparently hanged himself in a home near Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hideki Irabu, a former Japanese baseball star who was hailed as a pitching sensation when he joined the Yankees in 1997 but who fell short of expectations, prompting an angry George Steinbrenner to label him indelibly as “a fat, pus-sy toad,” was found dead on Wednesday in the Los Angeles suburb where he lived. He was 42. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/sports/IRABU-obit/IRABU-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 956, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/sports/IRABU-obit/IRABU-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/29/sports/IRABU-obit/IRABU-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hideki Irabu, Briefly a Yankee Sensation, Dies at 42', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hideki Irabu, Briefly a Yankee Sensation, Dies at 42', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Irabu, Hideki', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steinbrenner, George M 3d', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/60951c49-5e58-52c2-ac2d-a75ec630e14a', 'word_count': 909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60951c49-5e58-52c2-ac2d-a75ec630e14a'}, {'abstract': 'Detroit’s automakers would not have rebounded without concessions by the union. It’s time for them to start giving back to their workers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/opinion/detroit-the-union-and-improving-times.html', 'snippet': 'Detroit’s automakers would not have rebounded without concessions by the union. It’s time for them to start giving back to their workers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Detroit’s automakers have rebounded since the dismal days of 2009, when Washington steered General Motors and Chrysler through bankruptcy proceedings on the taxpayers’ dime. Sales of cars and light trucks have slowed slightly recently, but they are still expected to hit 13 million this year, up from 10 million in 2009. The carmakers posted a combined profit of $6 billion in the first quarter of the year. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Detroit, the Union and Improving Times', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Detroit, the Union and Improving Times: Workers gave up a lot, and automakers should start giving back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Automobile Workers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler LLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:31:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ea7e5ca-088f-5f1f-84d3-aa2638ac17aa', 'word_count': 474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ea7e5ca-088f-5f1f-84d3-aa2638ac17aa'}, {'abstract': 'The poorly executed sting operation that let weapons slip across the border into Mexico shows the need for more gun control.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/opinion/no-excusing-the-atf-or-congress.html', 'snippet': 'The poorly executed sting operation that let weapons slip across the border into Mexico shows the need for more gun control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives — perennially hogtied by the gun lobby’s power over Congress — made a colossally dumb decision when it concocted a sting operation that let 2,020 high-powered weapons slip across the border into Mexico. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Excusing the A.T.F., or Congress', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Excusing the A.T.F., or Congress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco and Firearms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:32:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/0899a048-f00c-52fb-8b23-a61e5963e8e1', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0899a048-f00c-52fb-8b23-a61e5963e8e1'}, {'abstract': 'The redistricting panel in New York State has shamelessly violated the prison gerrymandering law.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/opinion/oh-is-that-the-redistricting-law.html', 'snippet': 'The redistricting panel in New York State has shamelessly violated the prison gerrymandering law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are so many things wrong with New York State’s self-dealing redistricting process. Time to add one more to the list: One of the first decisions made by the bipartisan mapmaking committee — made up of two state senators, two members of the Assembly and two politically connected experts — was to violate a new state law requiring that prisoners be counted in their home districts, not where they are serving time. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oh, Is That the Law?', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oh, Is That the Law?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Redistricting and Reapportionment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:34:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4c4d16f-20ee-5098-93b4-f2f3cbeedcca', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4c4d16f-20ee-5098-93b4-f2f3cbeedcca'}, {'abstract': 'In “House of Boys” the director Jean-Claude Schlim looks at coming of age at the onset of AIDS.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/movies/house-of-boys-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “House of Boys” the director Jean-Claude Schlim looks at coming of age at the onset of AIDS.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A gay tragedy in three acts and more than a dozen excellent songs, “House of Boys” conveys an emotional honesty that overrides its dated style.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29ROUNDUP_HOUSE/2929ROUNDUP_HOUSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29ROUNDUP_HOUSE/2929ROUNDUP_HOUSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29ROUNDUP_HOUSE/2929ROUNDUP_HOUSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29ROUNDUP_HOUSE/2929ROUNDUP_HOUSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29ROUNDUP_HOUSE/2929ROUNDUP_HOUSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gay in Amsterdam in the 1980s', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Movie Review | 'House of Boys'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:34:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b46374c-9634-5192-895d-13c5e4fd8f47', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b46374c-9634-5192-895d-13c5e4fd8f47'}, {'abstract': 'The ruling could pare billions of dollars from the sum that may ultimately be recovered for victims of Bernard L. Madoff’s Ponzi scheme.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/business/banks-win-vital-ruling-in-madoff-trustee-case.html', 'snippet': 'The ruling could pare billions of dollars from the sum that may ultimately be recovered for victims of Bernard L. Madoff’s Ponzi scheme.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal judge ruled on Thursday that the bankruptcy trustee trying to recover billions for those who lost money in Bernard L. Madoff’s enormous Ponzi scheme did not have the right to sue banks and other third parties on behalf of the victims.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Judge’s Ruling in Madoff Case Could Reduce Money for Victims', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Banks Win Big Ruling In Madoff Trustee Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madoff, Bernard L', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rakoff, Jed S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Picard, Irving H', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HSBC Holdings PLC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ponzi Schemes', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:35:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diana B. Henriques', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49e73e4b-bca0-5403-b64a-74309db59c6f', 'word_count': 868, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49e73e4b-bca0-5403-b64a-74309db59c6f'}, {'abstract': 'A group is training young volunteers to treat the social factors, like poor nutrition and housing, that make patients sick.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/28/treating-the-cause-not-the-illness/', 'snippet': 'A group is training young volunteers to treat the social factors, like poor nutrition and housing, that make patients sick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1965, in an impoverished rural county in the Mississippi Delta, the pioneering physician Jack Geiger helped found one of the nation’s first community health centers. Many of the children Geiger treated were seriously malnourished, so he began writing “prescriptions” for food — stipulating quantities of milk, vegetables, meat, and fruit that could be “filled” at grocery stores, which were instructed to send the bills to the health center, where they were paid out of the pharmacy budget. When word of this reached the Office of Economic Opportunity in Washington, which financed the center, an official was dispatched to Mississippi to reprimand Geiger and make sure he understood that the center’s money could be used only for medical purposes. Geiger replied: “The last time I looked in my textbooks, the specific therapy for malnutrition was food.” The official had nothing to say and returned to Washington.In some ways, the United States has come a long way since Lyndon Johnson declared the “war on poverty.” But in others, we’re still at square one. We now have a variety of federally-supported nutrition programs, but the health care system remains senselessly disconnected from the “social determinants of health.” In this regard, the United States has fallen behind the rest of the world. If a politician in India announced a public health plan that neglected malnutrition, he would be ridiculed. Here, leaders make this kind of omission all the time. Almost all of the debate about the 2010 Affordable Care Act was consumed with questions about health care access and quality. But if we really want to improve the health of millions of people, we have to address the conditions that make them sick.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Treating the Cause, Not the Illness', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:40:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c74b73b1-e8d2-5228-bee8-637f09852c2d', 'word_count': 1573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c74b73b1-e8d2-5228-bee8-637f09852c2d'}, {'abstract': 'There were moments when both House Republicans and Senate Democrats were sure they had the necessary votes on debt ceiling legislation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/us/politics/29congress.html', 'snippet': 'There were moments when both House Republicans and Senate Democrats were sure they had the necessary votes on debt ceiling legislation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — After two hours of impassioned debate, the Capitol was braced for the grand finale of Speaker John A. Boehner’s days of strong-arming members on the most important vote of his Congressional career. Suddenly, with no warning and just minutes from a vote Thursday evening, the conversation on the House floor turned to naming post offices.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/us/CONG/CONG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/29/us/CONG/CONG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/us/CONG/CONG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 559, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/us/CONG/CONG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/29/us/CONG/CONG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Surprise Ending to Day of Strong-Arming, Head Counts and Meetings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Surprise Ending to Day of Strong-Arming, Head Counts and Meetings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:42:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer and Robert Pear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4533af0e-69e8-5090-99dd-5b8554eddfad', 'word_count': 1000, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4533af0e-69e8-5090-99dd-5b8554eddfad'}, {'abstract': 'A City Council bill would allow the city to forgo building shelters in Queens and the Bronx in exchange for greater efforts to reduce cat populations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/nyregion/new-york-city-increases-financing-to-sterilize-feral-cats.html', 'snippet': 'A City Council bill would allow the city to forgo building shelters in Queens and the Bronx in exchange for greater efforts to reduce cat populations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City has reached an agreement with animal-advocacy groups under which it will avoid having to build full-time shelters in Queens and the Bronx, but will step up its efforts to sterilize feral cats and to require owners to spay or neuter free-roaming cats.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/nyregion/ALT-KITTIES/ALT-KITTIES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'By Sterilizing Stray Cats, City Hopes to Cut Shelter Numbers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'By Sterilizing Stray Cats, City Hopes to Cut Shelter Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cats', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sterilization (Biological)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health and Mental Hygiene Department (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:42:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28eba8aa-a213-50f8-b05b-1d00073faed9', 'word_count': 751, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28eba8aa-a213-50f8-b05b-1d00073faed9'}, {'abstract': 'Marketing for Huggies Little Movers Slip-On Diapers is focusing on mothers of babies who have a tendency to squirm during a diaper change.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/business/media/making-the-diaper-change-easier.html', 'snippet': 'Marketing for Huggies Little Movers Slip-On Diapers is focusing on mothers of babies who have a tendency to squirm during a diaper change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN a new commercial for Huggies Little Movers Slip-On Diapers, which have leg holes and slide on like underwear, a baby rolls over twice in the same direction as a voiceover begins, “Huggies presents a whole new way to change a rolling pin.” ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/business/Adco/Adco-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 780, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/business/Adco/Adco-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/29/business/Adco/Adco-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Making the Diaper Change Easier for the Changer', 'kicker': 'Advertising', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Making the Diaper Change Easier for the Changer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diapers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T01:44:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Adam Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Adam', 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c9d9fb4-6d6a-505a-a755-50558723bfd6', 'word_count': 968, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c9d9fb4-6d6a-505a-a755-50558723bfd6'}, {'abstract': 'Once out of prison, an ex-con faces far tougher decisions in “Forged.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/movies/forged-review.html', 'snippet': 'Once out of prison, an ex-con faces far tougher decisions in “Forged.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Forged” Chuco (Manny Perez), just released from prison after serving eight years for the killing of his wife, has a very rough return to the world. Cesar (Jaime Tirelli), leader of the violent gang from the old days, gives him a job to do, whether he wants it or not. And Chuco’s son, Machito (David Castro), who saw his parents’ final fight as a child, has become a troubled teenager, abused and homeless. When he finds his father, he must introduce himself before warning: “You killed my mother. And I’m going to kill you.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29ROUNDUP_FORGED/29ROUNDUP4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29ROUNDUP_FORGED/29ROUNDUP4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 356}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/arts/29ROUNDUP_FORGED/29ROUNDUP4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 435, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'r