, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T08:28:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3dc0447-400c-56ad-aa9b-25d4ca222ab5', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3dc0447-400c-56ad-aa9b-25d4ca222ab5'}, {'abstract': 'Who was the first United Nations secretary general?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/test-yourself-history-july-29-2011/', 'snippet': 'Who was the first United Nations secretary general?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your knowledge of history with today’s question about United Nations leadership.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | History, July 29, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T08:52:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cdcf752-c93a-54ed-9fdd-7f3af295d91e', 'word_count': 39, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cdcf752-c93a-54ed-9fdd-7f3af295d91e'}, {'abstract': 'Shows including “Shrek the Musical” and “Legally Blonde” have found success in Europe after bombing on Broadway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/theater/flop-broadway-musicals-find-life-overseas.html', 'snippet': 'Shows including “Shrek the Musical” and “Legally Blonde” have found success in Europe after bombing on Broadway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON -- IT was the most expensive musical ever mounted on Broadway, staked on a character that was wildly popular from movies and books. Yet the lavishly designed show opened to a crush of negative reviews, leaving producers wondering how they would earn their money back once tourists stopped buying tickets.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31fix_span/31fix_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31fix_span/31fix_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31fix_span/31fix_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31fix_span/31fix_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31fix_span/31fix_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Flops on Broadway? Fix Them Overseas', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Flops on Broadway? Fix Them Overseas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T09:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1c8a606-3d3a-5537-99d5-078fe62f5093', 'word_count': 1900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1c8a606-3d3a-5537-99d5-078fe62f5093'}, {'abstract': 'Reading in today’s paper about recent bear attacks in Colorado, I was reminded of Darcy Frey’s prescient story from a few years ago about particularly audacious bears in the Canadian town of Whistler and the biologists who study them.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/the-bears-are-coming-again/', 'snippet': 'Reading in today’s paper about recent bear attacks in Colorado, I was reminded of Darcy Frey’s prescient story from a few years ago about particularly audacious bears in the Canadian town of Whistler and the biologists who study them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reading in today’s paper about recent bear attacks in Colorado, I was reminded of Darcy Frey’s prescient story from a few years ago about particularly audacious bears in the Canadian town of Whistler and the biologists who study them. Frey wrote about “bears that lift the door handles of trucks to take possession of the cabs; bears that manage to snag the bait from a trap with one foot while holding the steel gate open with the other; bears that stroll munificently through the crowds at the Canada Day parade; bears in the pubs, the hotels, the day care centers, the landfills, meat lockers, grease vents, underground parking garages. In Whistler, if a bear doesn’t get into something humans are guarding, it’s usually because too many other bears got there first.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Bears Are Coming (Again)', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ilena Silverman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ilena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silverman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/695116a9-d5c4-5e41-b0a6-c3266d193746', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/695116a9-d5c4-5e41-b0a6-c3266d193746'}, {'abstract': 'Great books for kids guides from Lemony Snicket and George Saunders.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/great-books-kids-edition/', 'snippet': 'Great books for kids guides from Lemony Snicket and George Saunders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last July, our staff writer Susan Dominus, who was then working as the paper’s Big City columnist, wrote a story about Jenny Rosenstrach, the keeper of a wonderful cooking blog called Dinner: A Love Story. The blog contains plenty of great (sometimes Bittman-esque) recipes — for grilled pizza, say, or minty peas (and also Manhattans) — and if you have any interest in the cooking or eating of food, it won’t disappoint. But it also occasionally contains book recommendations for kids. Usually, the suggestions come from Jenny’s daughters or from her husband, Andy. But in the past few weeks, something amazing has been going on over at DALS. The recommendations are coming from authors, and not just any authors, but some of the greatest children’s lit (or any lit) writers alive. Here is Pseudonymous Bosch’s summer reading list, for instance. And here are the recommendations made by Lemony Snicket (a k a Daniel Handler, who was the subject of this profile in our magazine back in 2001). And here is the summer reading list provided by George Saunders, whose genius is impossible to overstate and whose name popped up on our own great-fiction list just a couple weeks ago. Maybe once we finish contemplating our favorite sentences (and by the way, speaking of George Saunders, there are many amazing sentences in his most recent short story in the New Yorker), we’ll move on to our own subjective rankings of children’s books. Until then, these are pretty solid guides.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Great Books: Kids’ Edition', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joel Lovell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a94fdefc-a597-5fd6-a739-a5fd1b2044ee', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a94fdefc-a597-5fd6-a739-a5fd1b2044ee'}, {'abstract': 'BMW on Friday unveiled working prototypes of its i8, a plug-in hybrid sport coupe that will carry a six-figure price tag.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/automobiles/will-plug-in-bmws-turn-enthusiasts-on.html', 'snippet': 'BMW on Friday unveiled working prototypes of its i8, a plug-in hybrid sport coupe that will carry a six-figure price tag.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANKFURT -- BMW’S designers and engineers have long vowed that the company’s eventual battery-powered cars would not be geekmobiles but true BMWs, with all the zip, handling and style that the German automaker is known for. Norbert Reithofer, the chief executive, is fond of saying that an environmentally friendly auto should not be “a vow of poverty on wheels.”', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/automobiles/31BMW1/31BMW1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 254, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/automobiles/31BMW1/31BMW1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 254}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/automobiles/31BMW1/31BMW1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/automobiles/31BMW1/31BMW1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/automobiles/31BMW1/31BMW1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Plug-In BMWs Turn Enthusiasts On?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Will Plug-In BMWs Turn Enthusiasts On?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Vehicles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bayerische Motorenwerke AG', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Frankfurt (Germany)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6911d5d9-5e8f-56df-ad43-456031e23b0a', 'word_count': 1369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6911d5d9-5e8f-56df-ad43-456031e23b0a'}, {'abstract': 'In between media appearances, the “Top Gear” co-host and drifting champion is preparing to compete in the rallycross event at this weekend’s ESPN X Games.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/catching-up-with-tanner-foust-a-very-busy-driver/', 'snippet': 'In between media appearances, the “Top Gear” co-host and drifting champion is preparing to compete in the rallycross event at this weekend’s ESPN X Games.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two years ago, Tanner Foust was known in the world of drifting competitions and virtually nowhere else. Now, he is among the most recognized, and recognizable, drivers in the country, despite not competing in high-visibility race series like Nascar or IndyCar.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Catching Up With Tanner Foust, a Very Busy Driver', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indianapolis 500 (Auto Race)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'X Games', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foust, Tanner', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Pearley Huffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Pearley', 'lastname': 'Huffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0e21c9b-30ca-56e0-89b6-54f757ba4dd4', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0e21c9b-30ca-56e0-89b6-54f757ba4dd4'}, {'abstract': 'The immediate crisis facing the United States economy is the jobs deficit, not the federal debt, and budget cuts may make matters worse. an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/jobs-deficit-investment-deficit-fiscal-deficit/', 'snippet': 'The immediate crisis facing the United States economy is the jobs deficit, not the federal debt, and budget cuts may make matters worse. an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jobs Deficit, Investment Deficit, Fiscal Deficit', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': "By Laura D'Andrea Tyson", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': "D'andrea", 'lastname': 'Tyson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d65f9488-64de-55eb-8070-10dcbcfce0f7', 'word_count': 1194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d65f9488-64de-55eb-8070-10dcbcfce0f7'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of 100 breathtaking gowns and jackets by the designer Manuel Fernández are now on view through Sept. 18 at the handsome Tomas Y Valiente Center of Art, in Fuenlabrada, about 20 minutes by train from Madrid.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/outside-madrid-celebrating-a-fashion-designer-who-embraces-art/', 'snippet': 'A selection of 100 breathtaking gowns and jackets by the designer Manuel Fernández are now on view through Sept. 18 at the handsome Tomas Y Valiente Center of Art, in Fuenlabrada, about 20 minutes by train from Madrid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fans of the late Alexander McQueen who fear that the world of fashion has lost forever a designer who can combine boundless theatricality, scrupulous styling and high art might be heartened by the “Fashion Art” exhibition, which collects works by Manuel Fernández, a designer with a similar flair. A selection of 100 of his breathtaking gowns and jackets are now on view through Sept. 18 at the handsome Tomas Y Valiente Center of Art (Calle Leganés, 51, Fuenlabrada; 34-91-492-1120; www.fundacionfai.org), about 20 minutes by train from Madrid. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Outside Madrid, Celebrating a Fashion Designer Who Embraces Art', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madrid (Spain)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb955ec3-4869-553c-9204-47f7be60cbb9', 'word_count': 284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb955ec3-4869-553c-9204-47f7be60cbb9'}, {'abstract': 'Venezuela was the surprise team of the recently completed Copa América.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/venezuelas-vinotinto-the-new-vintage/', 'snippet': 'Venezuela was the surprise team of the recently completed Copa América.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Copa América in Argentina is over, Uruguay has taken home the trophy for the 15th time, and the rest of South America assesses whether the balance of soccer power has been altered. If so, the net winner is Venezuela, which was eliminated in the semifinals, on penalty kicks against Paraguay, without having lost a match.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Venezuela’s Vinotinto: The New Vintage?', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copa America (Soccer)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup (Soccer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chavez, Hugo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keyvan Antonio Heydari', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keyvan', 'middlename': 'Antonio', 'lastname': 'Heydari', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/148d496c-f4e4-560f-96f8-fcba5cab2782', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/148d496c-f4e4-560f-96f8-fcba5cab2782'}, {'abstract': '6 Q’s About the News | How has history “marched through the halls of Walter Reed Army Medical Center”?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/walter-reed-closes-as-a-wounded-times-photographer-covers-the-ceremony/', 'snippet': '6 Q’s About the News | How has history “marched through the halls of Walter Reed Army Medical Center”?', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOW has history “marched through the halls of Walter Reed Army Medical Center”?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Walter Reed Closes as a Wounded Times Photographer Covers the Ceremony', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:06:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9113c2b3-9103-5d7b-8638-721c20907e37', 'word_count': 73, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9113c2b3-9103-5d7b-8638-721c20907e37'}, {'abstract': 'North Korea must suspend its nuclear activities and allow U.N. inspectors to verify the freeze before six-nation talks can restart, the chief South Korean negotiator said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/30/world/asia/30korea.html', 'snippet': 'North Korea must suspend its nuclear activities and allow U.N. inspectors to verify the freeze before six-nation talks can restart, the chief South Korean negotiator said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — North Korea must suspend all activities at its nuclear complexes and allow United Nations inspectors to verify a freeze before the six-nation negotiations can resume on economic and other rewards for the country in exchange for ending its nuclear weapons programs, the chief South Korean nuclear negotiator said Friday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seoul Sets Terms for Resuming Talks With North Korea', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seoul Sets Terms for New Aid and Nuclear Talks With North Korea', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:10:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun and Steven Lee Myers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Myers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3053497a-5b8c-50a3-84db-eab3444b106a', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3053497a-5b8c-50a3-84db-eab3444b106a'}, {'abstract': 'The credit rating agency cited concerns about rising borrowing costs and the risk that private investors might have to bear some of the pain in any future bailouts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/30/business/global/european-sovereign-debt-crisis.html', 'snippet': 'The credit rating agency cited concerns about rising borrowing costs and the risk that private investors might have to bear some of the pain in any future bailouts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Casting a greater shadow over Spain’s economy, Moody’s Investors Service said Friday that it might cut the country’s credit rating in the coming months because of concerns about rising borrowing costs and the risk that private investors might have to bear some of the pain in any future bailouts. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Moody’s Puts Spain’s Debt on Review', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing | International News', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Moody’s Warns It May Cut Spain’s Credit Rating', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:12:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e14d527-2225-5dd2-bcb1-feb117a30fa4', 'word_count': 567, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e14d527-2225-5dd2-bcb1-feb117a30fa4'}, {'abstract': 'Longreads picks for the New York Times this week include a persistent ball player and evolution tales from New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/long-story-shortlist-rebellion-evolution-and-baseball/', 'snippet': 'Longreads picks for the New York Times this week include a persistent ball player and evolution tales from New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Kei Igawa: The Lost Yankee,”by Bill Pennington (July 23, 2011)TRENTON — In the middle of a bright Manhattan summer afternoon, the Yankees’ $46 million pitcher steps from his fashionable East Side apartment building and slips into a waiting Lexus for a chauffeured ride to the ballpark. Read more »', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Story Shortlist: Rebellion, Evolution and Baseball', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:15:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1fd78e0-0cd6-579c-8b7f-af584c310192', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1fd78e0-0cd6-579c-8b7f-af584c310192'}, {'abstract': 'An analysis of the crash of an Air France flight two years ago indicates fundamental errors by pilots.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/30/world/europe/air-france-flight-447-crash-report-july-2011.html', 'snippet': 'An analysis of the crash of an Air France flight two years ago indicates fundamental errors by pilots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — The co-pilot at the controls of the Air France jet that went down two years ago after leaving Brazil, killing 228, had not been trained to fly the aircraft in manual mode or to promptly recognize and respond to a speed-sensor malfunction at high altitude, according to a detailed analysis by French investigators released Friday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/world/France/France-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Report on Air France Crash Points to Pilot Training', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Report on ’09 Air France Crash Points to Lack of Manual-Mode Training for Pilots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airplane Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AIR FRANCE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:28:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicola Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicola', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05d9cbb6-5212-5846-a03f-f1b8038b71f6', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05d9cbb6-5212-5846-a03f-f1b8038b71f6'}, {'abstract': 'Spend the afternoon (or a lifetime) getting into the weeds of multi-trillion dollar budgets.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/cut-the-deficit-on-your-phone/', 'snippet': 'Spend the afternoon (or a lifetime) getting into the weeds of multi-trillion dollar budgets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Doubtless all this talk of debt ceilings has many Americans saying the same thing: I cannot get enough! Please, more complex budget analysis!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cut the Deficit – On Your Phone', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Android (Operating System)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congressional Budget Office', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:32:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Richtel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richtel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/528945f9-c184-5148-96f8-c4f41b81924a', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/528945f9-c184-5148-96f8-c4f41b81924a'}, {'abstract': 'Polish authorities on Friday blamed the Polish flight crew and Russian air traffic controllers for the 2010 crash in Russia that killed President Lech Kaczynski and 95 other people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/30/world/europe/30poland.html', 'snippet': 'Polish authorities on Friday blamed the Polish flight crew and Russian air traffic controllers for the 2010 crash in Russia that killed President Lech Kaczynski and 95 other people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — A Polish government report on Friday blamed the poor training of a Polish flight crew, as well as Russian air traffic controllers, for the 2010 crash in Russia that killed President Lech Kaczynski, his wife and 94 others. The report had the immediate effect of forcing out Poland’s defense minister, whose office was responsible for training the pilots.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/30/world/30poland/30poland-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 738, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/30/world/30poland/30poland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/30/world/30poland/30poland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poland Blames Crew and Controllers for Fatal Crash', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Poland Blames Crew and Controllers for Fatal 2010 Crash', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Poland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaczynski, Lech', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airplane Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T10:44:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Dempsey and David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dempsey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b183dd09-5c76-5e8b-9bb8-53722aa74c81', 'word_count': 800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b183dd09-5c76-5e8b-9bb8-53722aa74c81'}, {'abstract': 'Trying to get organized? These tools may help.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/is-better-task-management-on-your-to-do-list/', 'snippet': 'Trying to get organized? These tools may help.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like many small-business owners, Keith Goldstein often has so much on his plate that it’s hard to keep track of it all. So Mr. Goldstein, who owns 60-employee Everest Waterproofing and Restoration in San Francisco, does what most people do to remember what needs to get done: He writes it down on a “to-do” list. Sometimes he scribbles a list in a notebook, but he prefers to type it into his iPad, whence it’s zipped by app to his e-mail. “I use my inbox as my real to-do list,” he said. “If a note isn’t an action item I move it out of the inbox, so whatever is in there is about something I need to get done.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Better Task Management on Your To-Do List?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T11:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By David H. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e82f1ce6-4816-5ffe-ae4e-58749c8ee587', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e82f1ce6-4816-5ffe-ae4e-58749c8ee587'}, {'abstract': '“The Daily Show With Jon Stewart” features an interview with (and musical performances by) a figure close to our hearts: HR-4173, also known as the Dodd-Frank Act.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/how-a-bill-becomes-a-battered-law/', 'snippet': '“The Daily Show With Jon Stewart” features an interview with (and musical performances by) a figure close to our hearts: HR-4173, also known as the Dodd-Frank Act.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Daily Show With Jon Stewart” featured a musical performance on Thursday night by a figure near and dear to DealBook’s heart: HR-4173, also known as the Dodd-Frank Act.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How a Bill Becomes a (Battered) Law', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, Jon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T11:09:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed70538b-e930-54ec-8883-6a237a7efa2c', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed70538b-e930-54ec-8883-6a237a7efa2c'}, {'abstract': 'British Sky Broadcasting planned a share buyback worth £750 million, or $1.2 billion, and would return another £253 million to shareholders through a final dividend of 14.54 pence a share.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/bskyb-plans-share-buyback-and-higher-dividend/', 'snippet': 'British Sky Broadcasting planned a share buyback worth £750 million, or $1.2 billion, and would return another £253 million to shareholders through a final dividend of 14.54 pence a share.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:51 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'BSkyB Plans Share Buyback and Higher Dividend', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, James R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Sky Broadcasting Group (BSkyB)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T11:29:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe438e21-894c-553f-b804-8d446954e627', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe438e21-894c-553f-b804-8d446954e627'}, {'abstract': 'Compared with illiterate young women, educated ones desire smaller families and generally manage to achieve that goal, an article in the journal Science states.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/literacy-and-the-population-problem/', 'snippet': 'Compared with illiterate young women, educated ones desire smaller families and generally manage to achieve that goal, an article in the journal Science states.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world’s population is expected to pass seven billion in late October. A few weeks ago, I joined my colleague Celia Dugger in writing about worrisome new population projections from the United Nations. Instead of peaking in this century, as previously expected, the new projections suggested that the human population would continue growing all the way into the next century, probably topping out above 10 billion. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Literacy and the Population Problem', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illiteracy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T11:53:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Gillis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8bff0cf-df38-5a39-aa28-4dabbc9988ab', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8bff0cf-df38-5a39-aa28-4dabbc9988ab'}, {'abstract': 'A doctor is on a quest to find a drug to change the way his daughter lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/a-fathers-search-for-a-drug-for-down-syndrome.html', 'snippet': 'A doctor is on a quest to find a drug to change the way his daughter lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early in the evening of June 25, 1995, hours after the birth of his first and only child, the course of Dr. Alberto Costa’s life and work took an abrupt turn. Still recovering from a traumatic delivery that required an emergency Caesarean section, Costa’s wife, Daisy, lay in bed, groggy from sedation. Into their dimly lighted room at Methodist Hospital in Houston walked the clinical geneticist. He took Costa aside to deliver some unfortunate news. The baby girl, he said, appeared to have Down syndrome, the most common genetic cause of cognitive disabilities, or what used to be called “mental retardation.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31down_span/31down_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 439, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31down_span/31down_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 439}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31down_span/31down_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 804, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31down_span/31down_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31down_span/31down_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Drug for Down Syndrome', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "'All I Could Think Is, She's My Baby, She's A Lovely Girl And What Can I Do To Help Her?'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Down Syndrome', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Costa, Albert', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T12:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Hurley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hurley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecbc70c1-f31b-598d-bee5-a6f2b92dae7c', 'word_count': 3949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecbc70c1-f31b-598d-bee5-a6f2b92dae7c'}, {'abstract': 'Child of Eden uses the new Kinect system for the Xbox 360 to allow for a full-body experience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/30/arts/video-games/child-of-eden-video-game-is-a-sensory-trip-to-paradise.html', 'snippet': 'Child of Eden uses the new Kinect system for the Xbox 360 to allow for a full-body experience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Spectral whales undulate through ethereal mist, their hides speckled with winking gems that pulsate to a throbbing bass line. Gleaming motes of color spin and coalesce into flowers, layer upon layer of shimmering petals. A girl named Lumi, born in outer space and transported to cyberspace, beckons you to rescue her digital consciousness.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'