n Washington.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/30/business/Econ1/Econ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/30/business/Econ1/Econ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/30/business/Econ1/Econ1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Data Shows Sharp Slowdown in Growth Rate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Data Shows Sharp Slowdown In Growth Rate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gross Domestic Product', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T13:04:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47a6ec86-1400-5411-97cf-ff9257af92aa', 'word_count': 1133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47a6ec86-1400-5411-97cf-ff9257af92aa'}, {'abstract': 'Austerity already happening, hurting.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/stagnation-nation-2/', 'snippet': 'Austerity already happening, hurting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The GDP estimates for second quarter are out, and they’re ugly. Basically, very weak growth for the first half of 2011 — indeed, growth well below the economy’s potential, so we’re actually losing ground in the effort to reduce the gap between what we should be producing and what we’re actually producing. This is a recipe for rising, not falling, unemployment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stagnation Nation', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T13:05:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f67c41f-fd2c-5015-b225-4e4fa5068f9a', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f67c41f-fd2c-5015-b225-4e4fa5068f9a'}, {'abstract': 'The Whiskey Shop specializes in American craft distillers.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/whiskey-whiskey-everywhere-at-brooklyn-store/', 'snippet': 'The Whiskey Shop specializes in American craft distillers.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Whiskey nerds” is how George Ruotolo and his brother, Justin, describe themselves. With another partner, Rob Magill, they own bars, including The Whiskey Brooklyn, next to which they have carved out a wee dram of a shop with more than 100 different whiskeys. (They like to say 99 at any given time.) Most are bourbons, ryes and blended whiskeys made in America, often by craft distillers from Brooklyn to Berkeley, Calif. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Whiskey, Whiskey Everywhere at Brooklyn Store', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 