: None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7602c889-c6b4-5060-abbe-c53727fee3fd', 'word_count': 589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7602c889-c6b4-5060-abbe-c53727fee3fd'}, {'abstract': 'DealBook talks with six students from some of the nation’s top law, business and undergraduate schools about why they are interns at the Securities and Exchange Commission.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/for-s-e-c-interns-wall-street-is-a-world-away/', 'snippet': 'DealBook talks with six students from some of the nation’s top law, business and undergraduate schools about why they are interns at the Securities and Exchange Commission.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DealBook recently examined the lives of Wall Street interns, who spend their summers wrangling pitch books and trimming Excel spreadsheets. (And sometimes, blogging surreptitiously.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For S.E.C. Interns, Wall Street Is a World Away', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Internships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4597fdc9-37d9-5ff4-a6b2-32ad47621f36', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4597fdc9-37d9-5ff4-a6b2-32ad47621f36'}, {'abstract': 'Women without knickers kept showing up on the stage the other day, in two different theaters. And, honestly, I wasn’t hanging out in those hole-in-the-wall clubs in Soho, land of gentlemen’s entertainments bathed in sad neon light. No, I was in respectable fringe theaters, watching earnest and worthy productions of plays with big themes.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/london-theater-journal-a-wink-and-a-smirk-but-rarely-a-blush/', 'snippet': 'Women without knickers kept showing up on the stage the other day, in two different theaters. And, honestly, I wasn’t hanging out in those hole-in-the-wall clubs in Soho, land of gentlemen’s entertainments bathed in sad neon light. No, I was in re...', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Women without knickers kept showing up on the stage the other day, in two different theaters. And, honestly, I wasn’t hanging out in those hole-in-the-wall clubs in Soho, land of gentlemen’s entertainments bathed in sad neon light. No, I was in respectable fringe theaters, watching earnest and worthy productions of plays with big themes – which happened to involve women in search of sexual satisfaction removing their underpants and then waiting for something to happen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Theater Journal: A Wink and a Smirk, but Rarely a Blush', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALBEE, EDWARD', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burns, Nicholas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hill, Joe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skinner, Catherine', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wasserberg, Kate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Broadcasting Corp', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Court Theater', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8dec151-dd74-532c-ac91-2108a0f6c033', 'word_count': 923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8dec151-dd74-532c-ac91-2108a0f6c033'}, {'abstract': 'The critic A. O. Scott narrates a scene from Raoul Ruiz’s “Mysteries of Lisbon,” in which the central character is escorted by his mentor to find out who his mother is.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/31/magazine/29anatomy-mysteries-of-lisbon.html', 'snippet': 'The critic A. O. Scott narrates a scene from Raoul Ruiz’s “Mysteries of Lisbon,” in which the central character is escorted by his mentor to find out who his mother is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The critic A. O. Scott narrates a scene from Raoul Ruiz’s “Mysteries of Lisbon,” in which the central character is escorted by his mentor to find out who his mother is.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Anatomy of a Scene: ‘Mysteries of Lisbon’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Mysteries of Lisbon (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:01:48+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/c129328c-6612-5610-a31a-7cd40ef6bb81', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/c129328c-6612-5610-a31a-7cd40ef6bb81'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/reply-all-letters-the-all-american.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James Traub challenges Senator John Kerry’s impression that Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel “is prepared to make real sacrifices for peace” by claiming that “there’s scant evidence that this is so.” On the contrary, Netanyahu has demonstrated his commitment to peace in both word and deed. In 2009, he articulated his policy of a “Palestinian state side by side with the Jewish state” and in a recent speech to Congress, he stated that, in a peace agreement, “some settlements will end up beyond Israel’s borders” and that “with creativity and goodwill a solution can be found” to the Jerusalem issue. He removed hundreds of checkpoints to strengthen the Palestinian economy in the West Bank and implemented an unprecedented 10-month moratorium on new settlement construction. Throughout, he has repeatedly expressed his readiness to negotiate with the Palestinians, without preconditions, to achieve peace.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The All-American', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The All-American', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerry, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:01:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/865a440e-94a7-5ede-96b4-0c5b759e0883', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/865a440e-94a7-5ede-96b4-0c5b759e0883'}, {'abstract': 'A review of Sam Peckinpah’s nonexistent 1976 “Batman” movie.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/bonus-feature-block-quote-review-of-batman.html', 'snippet': 'A review of Sam Peckinpah’s nonexistent 1976 “Batman” movie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gone from this mid-’70s film are the euphoric bams and pows and the psychedelia of the television version; gone are Alfred, Robin, the bat signal and virtually everything that made the kids’ version so much fun. Indeed, Clint Eastwood makes his leather mask (mixed with his rough, leathery visage) into something akin to a fetish show. The film starts with a scene in a pool hall. Three roughnecks harass a group of women when Batman shows up. They have knives, but this Batman (with no real tricks up his sleeve) is a born brawler. When Batman finally beats the tar out of the three guys, he looks at the women with a vacuous stare. The credits then play over Batman listening to a cop-radio mix with Quincy Jones’s score. After the opening three against one, you’ve got the clever standoff fight of the heavyweight-boxer type against Batman; the 40-against-one standoff in the alleyway; the marksmen fight with its constant zoom-ins; and the raid on Joker’s palace. Had Peckinpah directed these last two sequences (and not, as has been credited, Eastwood, with an assist from Hal Needham), it might be one of the greatest films Peckinpah ever directed. Still, when people ask me what’s my favorite superhero film, I usually point to this one. Or Ang Lee’s “Hulk.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Block Quote', 'kicker': 'Bonus Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peckinpah, Sam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:01:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damon Houx', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Houx', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15e58090-f46e-5691-a8ae-7b1be08ee37a', 'word_count': 239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15e58090-f46e-5691-a8ae-7b1be08ee37a'}, {'abstract': 'The actor-director on “Cowboys & Aliens” and his obsession with making a blockbuster that has integrity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/jon-favreau-from-swingers-to-aliens.html', 'snippet': 'The actor-director on “Cowboys & Aliens” and his obsession with making a blockbuster that has integrity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new film you’ve directed, “Cowboys and Aliens,” has a pretty absurd name. The Times even reported last year that when people saw the trailer, they’d laugh, thinking it was a parody. Did you ever consider renaming it?  ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31talk/mag-31Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 433, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31talk/31talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31talk/31talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jon Favreau, From Swingers to Aliens', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Swingers And Aliens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Favreau, Jon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:01:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0cdd332-1bdc-54b5-960a-125df91e9e5c', 'word_count': 742, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0cdd332-1bdc-54b5-960a-125df91e9e5c'}, {'abstract': 'Was junior high really all that bad?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/lives-geekdom-revisited.html', 'snippet': 'Was junior high really all that bad?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In junior high school, I was bullied. A boy named Dominick made fun of my appearance until I could barely look at myself in the mirror. He and his friends snapped my training bra in the hallway. They heard I made out with a boy after a school dance and started calling me a slut. I had stomachaches in the morning and cried myself to sleep at night.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31lives/31lives-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 920, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31lives/31lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31lives/31lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Geekdom Revisited', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Geekdom Revisited', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:01:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ada Calhoun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ada', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calhoun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d05d262e-30c4-5f95-838c-b53a8ae1a832', 'word_count': 747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d05d262e-30c4-5f95-838c-b53a8ae1a832'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/reply-all-kevin-mccarthy.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I wasn’t even a page into this article before it dawned on me that you revealed the crux of the problem in Washington (where I now live). Note that Kevin McCarthy rides bikes with Republicans, meets all morning with Republicans, dines with Republicans: it’s like existing in an endless loop of self-congratulatory, self-assured (some would say arrogant) self-talk. How are these people ever to know what anyone else is thinking if all they do is meet with each other? ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'There’s No ‘I’ in ‘Tea Party’', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'There’s No ‘I’ In ‘Tea’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Kevin (1965- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:01:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/6de87377-21a8-5f02-8f6a-5661c517e10a', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6de87377-21a8-5f02-8f6a-5661c517e10a'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/reply-all-letters-miranda-july.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Miranda July is dark, dark, dark. I hate earnestness, and I am often allergic to sincerity, but if that’s all you see in her work, you’re missing the point. The cutesiness and humor are there to counterbalance the terror and anxiety of everyday life; the former gives you a little relief while you gird yourself for the latter.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Miranda July Is Totally Not Kidding', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Miranda July Is Totally Not Kidding', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'July, Miranda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:01:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/0248d8ea-301a-5bf1-8c31-a4ac5c4e4f15', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0248d8ea-301a-5bf1-8c31-a4ac5c4e4f15'}, {'abstract': 'If you give money to Dr. Leroy Thompson, will your own bank account grow?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/you-are-here-money-cometh-to-me-now.html', 'snippet': 'If you give money to Dr. Leroy Thompson, will your own bank account grow?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travis Cross, a 43-year-old unemployed hairdresser, was among the 200 or so people, most of them black and sharply dressed, filling the pews of Mount Pleasant Baptist Church in Norfolk, Va., late last month. They had come to see visiting preacher Dr. Leroy Thompson — “the man of God, himself,” Cross called him — hold his “Money Cometh to You” conference, which promises that the Lord can solve even the worst financial problems. It was a message the congregation desperately wanted to hear; some of the bordering neighborhoods have unemployment rates over 15 percent.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31here_span/mag-31YouRHere-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31here_span/mag-31YouRHere-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31here_span/mag-31YouRHere-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31here_span/mag-31YouRHere-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31here_span/mag-31YouRHere-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Money Cometh to Me Now!', 'kicker': 'You Are Here', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Man Of The (Fine) Cloth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norfolk (Va)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baptist Churches', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:01:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Brendan Dougherty', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'Brendan', 'lastname': 'Dougherty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d16b4f6-440b-5171-8626-6230e8c25ca9', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d16b4f6-440b-5171-8626-6230e8c25ca9'}, {'abstract': 'Have a strong opinion? Discuss this letter on The 6th Floor blog on Tuesday, Aug. 2, between 1 and 5 p.m., E.S.T.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/armchair-ethicist.html', 'snippet': 'Have a strong opinion? Discuss this letter on The 6th Floor blog on Tuesday, Aug. 2, between 1 and 5 p.m., E.S.T.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have a strong opinion? Discuss this letter with the Ethicist at nytimes.com/6thfloor on Tuesday, Aug. 2, between 1 and 5 p.m., E.S.T.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Armchair Ethicist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:01:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a5a03a6-ff19-5c56-bf45-97f314f06b95', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a5a03a6-ff19-5c56-bf45-97f314f06b95'}, {'abstract': 'If comic books are the grand myths of modern culture, why are superhero movies so boring? Here’s how to save them: unleash the auteurs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/the-prescription-to-save-ailing-superheroes.html', 'snippet': 'If comic books are the grand myths of modern culture, why are superhero movies so boring? Here’s how to save them: unleash the auteurs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s probably not a good sign for the superhero genre as a whole that the most talked-about comic-book movies of summer 2011 are the ones due out in 2012. But it’s not even August, and the conversation in superhero-nerdspace is all about leaked trailers for “The Dark Knight Rises” and “The Avengers,” both scheduled to be released next year, or about how Andrew Garfield, that kid from “The Social Network,” looks in his Spider-Man suit.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31comics-capt/31comics-capt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 755, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31comics-capt/31comics-capt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31comics-capt/31comics-capt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Prescription to Save Ailing Superheroes', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘One Ridiculously Vast Cosmic Soap Opera About a Huge Cast of Characters Brawling’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DC Comics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:01:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Pappademas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pappademas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e567a4f-fb40-586e-839e-69aed4cc0879', 'word_count': 1782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e567a4f-fb40-586e-839e-69aed4cc0879'}, {'abstract': 'Serious movies have as many rules and lazy conventions as summer blockbusters. But Raoul Ruiz has eluded them all.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/raoul-ruiz-a-mild-mannered-maniac.html', 'snippet': 'Serious movies have as many rules and lazy conventions as summer blockbusters. But Raoul Ruiz has eluded them all.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the small bookstore of the Cinémathèque Française in Paris, a wall of shelves is devoted to works by and about the great auteurs — monographs, coffee-table tomes, DVDs. The pantheon of world cinema is too large for the available space, so the masters are arrayed in double rows, one behind the other. In order to browse, you must dig and rearrange, and on a recent visit, I felt a bit like a cinephile-archaeologist or a monomaniacal archivist as I burrowed past Ozu and Pasolini and displaced a row of Scorsese, making my determined way to Raoul Ruiz. I unearthed a small cache of movies directed by this Chilean-born filmmaker, who has made his home in Paris since the mid-1970s. I also found a book of interviews from the 1980s and another, “Poétique du Cinéma,” based on lectures he gave, mostly at American universities, over the years. The title of one lecture is “Cinema as Clandestine Voyage,” which might describe a curious viewer’s sometimes baffling but frequently enchanting journey through Ruiz’s films.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31ruiz_span/31ruiz_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31ruiz_span/31ruiz_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 438}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31ruiz_span/mag-31ruiz-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31ruiz_span/31ruiz_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/magazine/31ruiz_span/31ruiz_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mild-Mannered Maniac', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Mild-Mannered Maniac', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruiz, Raoul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Mysteries of Lisbon (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:02:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. SCOTT', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'SCOTT', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a7475c6-b529-542d-9dd7-aa22d5d256c4', 'word_count': 2841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a7475c6-b529-542d-9dd7-aa22d5d256c4'}, {'abstract': 'Out of the box; off the leash.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/magazine/the-ethicist-duty-calls.html', 'snippet': 'Out of the box; off the leash.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been called for jury duty, and I’m shocked by the lies that other prospective jurors tell to get themselves disqualified. I know that’s not fair; everyone deserves his day in court. But it’s not fair to me that because of their lies I’m more likely to suffer the inconvenience of a long trial. May I tell the lawyers things about myself that are perfectly true but which I’m mentioning only to get out of the jury box and back to my job? DAVID S., NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Duty Calls', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Duty Calls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:02:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/807142c4-badc-56ed-8c14-cc415e7ac328', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/807142c4-badc-56ed-8c14-cc415e7ac328'}, {'abstract': 'The pharmaceutical company is cutting up to 13 percent of its work force, or as many as 13,000 jobs, by 2015.', 'web_url': 'https://prescriptions.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/merck-plans-more-job-cuts/', 'snippet': 'The pharmaceutical company is cutting up to 13 percent of its work force, or as many as 13,000 jobs, by 2015.', 'lead_pa