 Ray’s widow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nicholas Ray, the director of the 1955 film classic “Rebel Without a Cause,” was pretty much banished from Hollywood in the early 1960s as a result of his drug and alcohol addictions, and volatile temper. But his early work influenced a generation of filmmakers. Now his voluminous archives have been bought from his widow, Susan Ray, by the Harry Ransom Center, a humanities research library and museum at the University of Texas at Austin.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nicholas Ray Archives Find a Home in Texas', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venice International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dean, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mineo, Sal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ray, Nicholas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wood, Natalie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Texas at Austin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Venice (Italy)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:30:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8704af72-1d21-5361-9022-4b6e9808906e', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8704af72-1d21-5361-9022-4b6e9808906e'}, {'abstract': 'Superfund designation halted large-scale construction around the Gowanus Canal in Brooklyn. But as small businesses and artists move in, there has been a different kind of development.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/07/31/realestate/20110731_Cover_Gowanus.html', 'snippet': 'Superfund designation halted large-scale construction around the Gowanus Canal in Brooklyn. But as small businesses and artists move in, there has been a different kind of development.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Superfund designation halted large-scale construction around the Gowanus Canal in Brooklyn. But as small businesses and artists move in, there has been a different kind of development.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gowanus: Big Development Can Wait', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gowanus Canal (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T16:30:41+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Real Estate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/ea84ad95-71f1-568d-9e5f-d56d05f6d276', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/ea84ad95-71f1-568d-9e5f-d56d05f6d276'}, {'abstract': 'Jeff Madrick traces the regulatory and cultural changes that led to America’s current financial trouble.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/age-of-greed-by-jeff-madrick-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Jeff Madrick traces the regulatory and cultural changes that led to America’s current financial trouble.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seven years ago, James Mann published “Rise of the Vulcans,” a history of the neoconservative foreign-policy advisers who rose to prominence with the presidency of George W. Bush. Mann traced the group’s origins to the 1970s, when figures like Dick Cheney and Donald Rumsfeld got their start in government, and told how events from East Asia to Kuwait solidified their faith in 