'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salmon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (State)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Park Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Levees and Dams', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:02:20+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/cd557eb8-da99-5bd7-8818-6430be61964e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/cd557eb8-da99-5bd7-8818-6430be61964e'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE LONGEST WAR: The Enduring Conflict Between America and Al-Qaeda,  by Peter L. Bergen (Free Press, $16.) Bergen, a national security analyst for CNN and the author of two previous books about Osama bin Laden, covers the global sweep of the last 10 years — the Sept. 11 attacks, the invasions of Afghanistan and Iraq, America’s relationship with Pakistan and the death of Bin Laden — and provides an intimate look at Al Qaeda’s history and motives.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd20670a-682b-5cc1-868e-3713691fea76', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd20670a-682b-5cc1-868e-3713691fea76'}, {'abstract': 'Amazon.com moves one step closer to acquiring the Book Depository, a rival in Britain.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/amazon-com-moves-closer-to-acquiring-a-major-british-rival/', 'snippet': 'Amazon.com moves one step closer to acquiring the Book Depository, a rival in Britain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amazon.com, which has an estimated 70 percent of the online book market in Britain, got closer this week to controlling the rest of it when the Irish Competition Authority approved its pending purchase of a major rival, the Book Depository. But the blessing of the main regulatory body, the Office of Fair Trading, is no sure thing in a country that appears to be more concerned about media monopoly in the wake of the News Corporation hacking scandal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Amazon.com Moves Closer to Acquiring a Major British Rival', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Streitfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Streitfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c1be4ee-d1b7-5641-b580-66250990e034', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c1be4ee-d1b7-5641-b580-66250990e034'}, {'abstract': 'In this sequel to “Push,” Precious has died, and her son finds himself in a world of brutality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/the-kid-by-sapphire-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this sequel to “Push,” Precious has died, and her son finds himself in a world of brutality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fifteen years after the heralded publication of her first novel, “Push” — the basis for the 2009 film “Precious” — Sapphire has written a new novel, “The Kid,” which traces the life of Precious’ second child, Abdul. At its best, “The Kid” captures the grueling heartbreak of trying to love anything when the world doesn’t love you enough, of trying to summon desire or affection in the absence of any healthy context for either one.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31EVANS/31EVANS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1014, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31EVANS/31EVANS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31EVANS/31EVANS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Sapphire’s New Novel, Life Without Precious', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bleak Inheritance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sapphire', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danielle Evans', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Evans', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/435358eb-3f6c-5eb3-b1d4-008a91021d23', 'word_count': 843, 'uri': 'nyt://article/435358eb-3f6c-5eb3-b1d4-008a91021d23'}, {'abstract': 'In her latest best seller, “Of Thee I Zing,” the conservative pundit Laura Ingraham takes aim at the nation’s “lactating daddies,” FarmVille gamers and “creative” baby names.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/07/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'In her latest best seller, “Of Thee I Zing,” the conservative pundit Laura Ingraham takes aim at the nation’s “lactating daddies,” FarmVille gamers and “creative” baby names.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NAMING NAMES: The cover of Laura Ingraham’s latest best seller, “Of Thee I Zing: America’s Cultural Decline From Muffin Tops to Body Shots,” shows the conservative firebrand dressed up like George Washington crossing the Delaware.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/07/books/review/07insidelist/07insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/08/07/books/review/07insidelist/07insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cead652c-f600-59e8-9b96-02203bb1a8ca', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cead652c-f600-59e8-9b96-02203bb1a8ca'}, {'abstract': 'A life of Thomas Reed, the Gilded Age Congressional leader.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/mr-speaker-biography-of-thomas-reed-by-james-grant-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A life of Thomas Reed, the Gilded Age Congressional leader.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every serious student of Congress is familiar with Thomas B. Reed, a dominant speaker of the House near the end of the 19th century who was known as a “czar” for his use of the House rules to achieve the goals of his Republican majority. He was the precursor of the much more authoritarian “Uncle Joe” Cannon, whose ham-handed exercise of power led to the storied 1910 revolt that created the seniority system in Congress. It was Reed who transformed the speakership for the 20th century, but to most Americans, he is at best a dim figure from history, perhaps a candidate for a particularly tough question on “Jeopardy!” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31ORNSTEIN/31ORNSTEIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 743, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31ORNSTEIN/31ORNSTEIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31ORNSTEIN/31ORNSTEIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Master of the Gilded Age Congress', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'House Manager', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Filibusters and Debate Curbs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By NORMAN ORNSTEIN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Norman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ORNSTEIN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/236378ee-7b51-5f2f-9a65-5212888b4991', 'word_count': 1249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/236378ee-7b51-5f2f-9a65-5212888b4991'}, {'abstract': 'A history of the occupation of Germany after 1945, where there was little resistance to the Allies even as Hitler’s influence lingered.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/exorcising-hitler-by-frederick-taylor-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A history of the occupation of Germany after 1945, where there was little resistance to the Allies even as Hitler’s influence lingered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The story of the denazification of Germany after 1945 comes in two versions. The one we know best allows us to congratulate ourselves on a job well done. We turned the most powerful and frightening dictatorship in Europe into a stable, peaceable and functioning democracy for the first time in its history. This is the story we grew up with during the cold war, one that justified the partition and occupation of the country over many decades. And the invasion of Iraq in 2003 made it seem relevant in an entirely new way. It stood as a salutary contrast with the sheer superficiality and short-sightedness of the Bush administration’s approach to military occupation. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31MAZOWER/31MAZOWER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 810, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31MAZOWER/31MAZOWER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31MAZOWER/31MAZOWER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Being German in 1945', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Bitter End', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Frederick', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Mazower', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mazower', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd1006fa-5a35-5592-8834-11a36cde0633', 'word_count': 1153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd1006fa-5a35-5592-8834-11a36cde0633'}, {'abstract': 'A sexual obsession pulls this novel’s heroine into her darkest self.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/true-things-about-me-by-deborah-kay-davies-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A sexual obsession pulls this novel’s heroine into her darkest self.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The allure of destructive relationships is complicated. We don’t like them when they happen to us (or maybe sometimes we do), but we love reading or talking about them. It’s the drama, the guaranteed narrative arc. We know they almost always end badly, but the details are so irresistible: She did what? Again? What was she thinking? Why would anyone go back after the deadbeat took her car, ate all her bacon and gave his 12-year-old the keys to her apartment without telling her he had a 12-year-old? That’s crazy. Tell me more!', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Barry-t_CA0/bkr-Barry-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Barry-t_CA0/bkr-Barry-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Barry-t_CA0/bkr-Barry-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Novel of Dark Sexual Obsession', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dangerous Liaison', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domestic Violence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e39444e0-2865-5bc3-80d2-5752a76336f4', 'word_count': 776, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e39444e0-2865-5bc3-80d2-5752a76336f4'}, {'abstract': 'Jennifer Steinhauer reports on the back and forth in Washington. Also, voices of voters in Massachusetts and Arizona.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/caucus-video-debt-fight-down-to-the-wire/', 'snippet': 'Jennifer Steinhauer reports on the back and forth in Washington. Also, voices of voters in Massachusetts and Arizona.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jennifer Steinhauer reports on the back and forth in Washington on the debt ceiling. Also, voices of voters in Massachusetts and Arizona.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Caucus Video: Debt Fight Down to the Wire', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Werschkul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werschkul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86480a02-3057-5f62-bb90-c67151156dc9', 'word_count': 24, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86480a02-3057-5f62-bb90-c67151156dc9'}, {'abstract': 'In Carol Birch’s historical novel, a young boy is lured on a sea voyage to hunt for a Komodo dragon in the East Indies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/jamrachs-menagerie-by-carol-birch-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Carol Birch’s historical novel, a young boy is lured on a sea voyage to hunt for a Komodo dragon in the East Indies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his “Lectures on Literature,” Vladimir Nabokov suggests that for “The Strange Case of Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde,” Robert Louis Stevenson borrowed his “fog . . . from a Dickensian studio to envelop a conventional London.” The British writer Carol Birch similarly borrows elements of Dickens’s London for “Jamrach’s Menagerie,” her 11th novel and her first to be published in the United States. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Hale/Hale-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Hale/Hale-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Hale/Hale-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 653, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Hale/Hale-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Hale/Hale-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Scrappy Slum Urchin Goes to Sea', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outward Bound', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c11e543-b58e-5743-8ec0-199f15ad09ad', 'word_count': 1153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c11e543-b58e-5743-8ec0-199f15ad09ad'}, {'abstract': 'Lee Siegel explains how seriousness has evolved in Western society, from the age of Socrates to that of contemporary TV hosts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/are-you-serious-by-lee-siegel-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Lee Siegel explains how seriousness has evolved in Western society, from the age of Socrates to that of contemporary TV hosts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like most critics, Lee Siegel is sometimes right. Unlike most critics, he is always abrasive, even when the reader happens to agree with him. A writer’s inexhaustible readiness to vex his audience requires some fortitude, and Siegel is nothing if not energetic. In essays on many subjects involving art, literature, the Internet, politics, film and television, he presents himself as a gadfly whose assiduous pursuit of the truth must inevitably cost him some friends. “I am not what you would call a nice critic,” Siegel wrote in The Guardian of London in 2008. Fair enough. So exactly what kind of critic is he?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31RIFKIND-2/31RIFKIND-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31RIFKIND-2/31RIFKIND-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/31RIFKIND-2/31RIFKIND-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Age of Anti-Serious Seriousness', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All Kidding Aside', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, Jon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Siegel, Lee', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colbert, Stephen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donna Rifkind', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rifkind', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e651228e-d12c-5d87-8f8f-2bbad81e530d', 'word_count': 1011, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e651228e-d12c-5d87-8f8f-2bbad81e530d'}, {'abstract': 'The hero of this novel, a Brooklyn poet, is thrown out by his wife for adultery he didn’t commit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/the-astral-by-kate-christensen-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The hero of this novel, a Brooklyn poet, is thrown out by his wife for adultery he didn’t commit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lately the realist novel’s been getting realistic. Originally developed in response to a highfalutin form gone stale, realism has a long and glorious tradition of extraordinary yarns about ordinary people. But the name “realism” was never quite right. Authors as disparate as Mark Twain, Émile Zola and Edith Wharton are called “realists,” which would be fitting if you spent your adolescence drifting down the Mississippi, worked out your love problems by drowning your rival and attempted suicide by sled.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Handler/Handler-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 361, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Handler/Handler-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 361}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Handler/Handler-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 616, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Handler/Handler-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/Handler/Handler-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pitfalls and Pleasures of the Current Realist Novel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eviction Notice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christensen, Kate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADULTERY', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By DANIEL HANDLER', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'HANDLER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7f19666-2064-50cd-8ceb-71ee0f842ff1', 'word_count': 1193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7f19666-2064-50cd-8ceb-71ee0f842ff1'}, {'abstract': 'A philosopher argues that we have a poor understanding of our conscious experience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/perplexities-of-consciousness-by-eric-schwitzgebel-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A philosopher argues that we have a poor understanding of our conscious experience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few days before a review of my latest book appeared in these pages, I wrote to my editor, saying I had seen an advance copy and how much I liked the color illustration of the yellow moon. He replied that I must be mistaken, since the Book Review doesn’t use color. The next weekend he wrote to say he couldn’t think what had come over him — he reads the Book Review every week, and had somehow not noticed the color. Odd. And yet these lapses can happen to the best of us. Ask yourself what the Roman number four on the face of the church clock looks like. Most people will answer it looks like IV, but almost certainly the truth is it looks like IIII.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Humphrey-t_CA0/bkr-Humphrey-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Humphrey-t_CA0/bkr-Humphrey-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Humphrey-t_CA0/bkr-Humphrey-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 616, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Humphrey-t_CA0/bkr-Humphrey-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Humphrey-t_CA0/bkr-Humphrey-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Know Thyself: Easier Said Than Done', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The I of the Beholder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dreams', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Humphrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Humphrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d41f520-d61c-5665-902f-9057c88c8321', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d41f520-d61c-5665-902f-9057c88c8321'}, {'abstract': 'We like to think modern fiction is free from pretensions of the past. But computer analysis reveals that linguistic tics endure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/the-mechanic-muse-the-jargon-of-the-novel-computed.html', 'snippet': 'We like to think modern fiction is free from pretensions of the past. But computer analysis reveals that linguistic tics endure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We like to think that modern fiction, particularly American fiction, is free from the artificial stylistic pretensions of the past. Richard Bridgman expressed a common view in his 1966 book “The Colloquial Style in America.” “Whereas in the 19th century a very real distinction could be made between the vernacular and standard diction as they were used in prose,” Bridgman wrote, “in the 20th century the vernacular had virtually become standard.” Thanks to such pioneers as Mark Twain, Stephen Crane, Gertrude Stein and Ernest Hemingway, the story goes, ornate classicism was replaced by a straight-talking vox populi.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Zimmer-t_CA0/bkr-Zimmer-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 299, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Zimmer-t_CA0/bkr-Zimmer-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 299}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/books/review/bkr-Zimmer-t_CA0/bkr-Zimmer-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 473, 'width': 949, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 