e ways cocaine affected the careers of two clinical pathbreakers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T17:03:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0f3c452-dd42-577f-b86b-b1a406a8556d', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0f3c452-dd42-577f-b86b-b1a406a8556d'}, {'abstract': 'Two computational neuroscientists analyze Web searches to identify sexual desires and preferences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/books/review/a-billion-wicked-thoughts-by-ogi-ogas-and-sai-gaddam-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Two computational neuroscientists analyze Web searches to identify sexual desires and preferences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The concentrated essence of this curious book is contained in its 11th chapter, which attempts to explain what the “Mona Lisa” has in common with Chicken McNuggets, vampire novels and the concluding scene of most pornographic videos. Each of these works of human creativity, Ogi Ogas and Sai Gaddam write in “A Billion Wicked Thoughts,” exploits perceptual trickery to arouse and gratify our desires. The enigma of the Gioconda smile; the technologically engineered “crave\xadability” of fast food; the alluring, “alpha among alphas” quality of the paranormal hero; that climactic moment beamed to watchers on a hundred million laptop screens: all rely on the artful manipulation of our brains. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedi