lina Tugend', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tugend', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52b207f9-14c2-5f5c-8fe3-40c237d23f7d', 'word_count': 1256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52b207f9-14c2-5f5c-8fe3-40c237d23f7d'}, {'abstract': 'The first-time director of the film “Bellflower,” Evan Glodell, is a tinkerer who invented gadgets for his camera and for what his camera shot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/movies/bellflower-has-homemade-inventions-in-front-of-and-on-camera.html', 'snippet': 'The first-time director of the film “Bellflower,” Evan Glodell, is a tinkerer who invented gadgets for his camera and for what his camera shot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE indie drama “Bellflower,” opening Friday, charts a familiar tale, the blossoming and deterioration of a romance. But the way the story is told is far from conventional. This might be the first relationship tale that begins with cricket eating and involves a bleach-squirting muscle car and a homemade flamethrower.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31BELLFLOWER2/31BELLFLOWER2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31BELLFLOWER2/31BELLFLOWER2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31BELLFLOWER2/31BELLFLOWER2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 577, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31BELLFLOWER2/31BELLFLOWER2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/arts/31BELLFLOWER2/31BELLFLOWER2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Build the Apocalypse Inside Your Garage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Build the Apocalypse Inside Your Garage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T18:30:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c32c3c4-e189-5f4b-b756-1d376d7c640f', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c32c3c4-e189-5f4b-b756-1d376d7c640f'}, {'abstract': 'Sean Hanratty’s pick for this week’s summer playlist is the installation artist Erick Swenson.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/the-jam-erick-swenson/', 'snippet': 'Sean Hanratty’s pick for this week’s summer playlist is the installation artist Erick Swenson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Texas installation artist Erick Swenson is known for turning heads with his portrayals of hybrid creatures that look hauntingly real. His 2004 Whitney Biennial contribution showcased an animal with a fawnlike body, ears of a rabbit and a nose of a dog, frozen in a dramatic stance over what appears to be a woven rug (but is actually hand-painted fiberglass). He once created a half-eaten carcass of a killer whale and laid it across a monstrous crumpled-up ice floe — a piece so well done, critics said, it could be a natural history museum display. So how does Swenson fit in here with our downtown playlist, brought to you by Sean Hanratty? Hanratty chose Swenson for The Jam’s fifth installment not only because of their shared Texan roots but also because, he says, “I’ve always been so intrigued with the sort of fantastical nature of his work and materials. I knew his playlist would contain a lot of classic songs with a very strong backbone.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Jam | Erick Swenson', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T18:40:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/826ec6d0-7759-5bff-bc65-283a30245b21', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/826ec6d0-7759-5bff-bc65-283a30245b21'}, {'abstract': 'The city medical examiner’s office said two more people died of the heat on July 23, bringing total heat-related deaths for that day to four.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/2-more-deaths-attributed-to-heat/', 'snippet': 'The city medical examiner’s office said two more people died of the heat on July 23, bringing total heat-related deaths for that day to four.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two additional deaths were attributed to the heat on July 23, the city medical examiner’s office said on Friday, bringing the heat-related death toll for the day, when the temperature reached 100 degrees, to four. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2 More Deaths Attributed to Heat', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)'