, 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T18:59:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4dcbee52-39fb-5ccb-8064-f9bc3d6273d3', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4dcbee52-39fb-5ccb-8064-f9bc3d6273d3'}, {'abstract': 'Brands issued statements shortly after President Obama announced the next round of fuel economy standards on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/automakers-react-to-54-5-m-p-g/', 'snippet': 'Brands issued statements shortly after President Obama announced the next round of fuel economy standards on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After months of hand-wringing by industry lobbies and environmental groups, President Obama announced the new round of Corporate Average Fuel Economy standards, known as CAFE, on Friday in Washington. The standards call for a 5 percent annual increase in fuel economy for passenger cars from 2017 to 2025, culminating in a mandated 54.5 miles per gallon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Automakers React to 54.5 M.P.G.', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fuel Efficiency', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ALLIANCE OF AUTOMOBILE MANUFACTURERS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hyundai Motor Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corp', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Union of Concerned Scientists', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:01:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de7f829d-92bc-5c27-9f51-b1741d2c00ac', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de7f829d-92bc-5c27-9f51-b1741d2c00ac'}, {'abstract': 'What was meant to be a day of unified protest in Tahrir Square ended up in a lopsided turnout dominated by members of religious movements.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/tension-rises-as-islamists-dominate-tahrir-square/', 'snippet': 'What was meant to be a day of unified protest in Tahrir Square ended up in a lopsided turnout dominated by members of religious movements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As my colleague Anthony Shadid reports, tens of thousands of Egyptians poured into Tahrir Square on Friday for a day that had been billed as one of unified protest against the interim military government. But the turnout was lopsided, dominated by members of religious movements, ranging from the most conservative, the Salafists, to the relatively moderate Muslim Brotherhood.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tension Rises as Islamists Dominate Tahrir Square', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:02:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3ec9733-7af6-58f0-a257-92c7a47f555e', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3ec9733-7af6-58f0-a257-92c7a47f555e'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column is about the wealthy who are able to indulge one of their fantasies: owning a ranch out West.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/indulging-a-fantasy/', 'snippet': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column is about the wealthy who are able to indulge one of their fantasies: owning a ranch out West.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his Wealth Matters column this week, Paul Sullivan writes about what for some is the ultimate fantasy: owning a ranch out West. He says that owning a ranch, and selling the products raised on it, has lately emerged as an alternative investment class. But he quickly adds that the investment is only for the wealthy who are not looking for a quick return.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Indulging a Fantasy', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Net Worth Individuals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ranches', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:04:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bucks Editors', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bucks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Editors', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c4c54f5-3a1c-5e62-bbf9-90a7d69d65ea', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c4c54f5-3a1c-5e62-bbf9-90a7d69d65ea'}, {'abstract': 'The film, starring Cuba Gooding Jr. and Terrence Howard about the all-black pilot group that fought in World War II, will be released on Jan. 20.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/red-tails-lucass-tuskegee-airmen-movie-lands-a-release-date/', 'snippet': 'The film, starring Cuba Gooding Jr. and Terrence Howard about the all-black pilot group that fought in World War II, will be released on Jan. 20.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a long time ago – 1994, to be precise – when George Lucas produced his last film that was not connected to his “Indiana Jones” or “Star Wars” fantasy franchises. Now his long-in-the-works film about the fighting men of a very different war finally has a release date: on Friday, Lucasfilm said that “Red Tails,” a film about the Tuskegee Airmen, the all-black pilot group that fought in World War II, would be released on Jan. 20. The film is directed by Anthony Hemingway, who has directed for television series including “Treme,” “True Blood” and “CSI: NY,” and its ensemble cast features Cuba Gooding Jr., Terrence Howard and Bryan Cranston, as well as Tristan Wilds (of “The Wire” and “90210”), Cliff Smith (better known as the rapper Method Man), Ne-Yo and Elijah Kelley.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Red Tails,’ Lucas’s Tuskegee Airmen Movie, Lands a Release Date', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pilots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cranston, Bryan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gooding Jr, Cuba', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Terrence', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lucas, George', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Method Man', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ne-Yo', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oyelowo, David', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lucasfilm Ltd', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tuskegee Airmen', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:08:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2cd8651-62d5-5041-b691-b094f042194e', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2cd8651-62d5-5041-b691-b094f042194e'}, {'abstract': 'Long disgruntled European soccer clubs are mounting a muscular new attack against FIFA by pressing a dispute over finances and decision-making into the political arena.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/30/sports/soccer/30iht-fifa30.html', 'snippet': 'Long disgruntled European soccer clubs are mounting a muscular new attack against FIFA by pressing a dispute over finances and decision-making into the political arena.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Long disgruntled European soccer clubs are mounting a muscular new challenge to FIFA, the international soccer federation, by pressing a dispute over finances and decision-making into the political arena, where the powerful body will face scrutiny of its management. ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'European Soccer Clubs Challenging FIFA', 'kicker': 'Roundup', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'FIFA’s Missteps Embolden Some European Clubs to Challenge Its Authority', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blatter, Sepp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:09:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Doreen Carvajal and Stephen Castle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Doreen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carvajal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e8c3b2d-a28b-57a0-a878-0b8c2c814c65', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e8c3b2d-a28b-57a0-a878-0b8c2c814c65'}, {'abstract': 'The plant, Marcgravia evenia, has dish-shaped leaves that reflect sounds especially well, making it easy for bats to identify it though echolocation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/02/science/02obbat.html', 'snippet': 'The plant, Marcgravia evenia, has dish-shaped leaves that reflect sounds especially well, making it easy for bats to identify it though echolocation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Flowers attract bees and birds with their colors and scents. Now, a new study reports that a rain forest vine in Cuba attracts nectar-feeding bats with sound.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/02/science/02obbat/02obbat-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 804, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/02/science/02obbat/02obbat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/08/02/science/02obbat/02obbat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Vine’s Acoustics Send a Bat Signal', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Vine’s Acoustics Send a Bat Signal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bats', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:15:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/533f98f8-c1ad-52df-8c34-22535452a560', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/533f98f8-c1ad-52df-8c34-22535452a560'}, {'abstract': 'A potential new reality show says it is making progress toward becoming, well, a reality.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/wall-street-wives-finds-a-cast/', 'snippet': 'A potential new reality show says it is making progress toward becoming, well, a reality.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Wall Street Wives” is one step closer to the small screen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Wall Street Wives’ Finds a Cast', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wall Street (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:17:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab73084c-9070-5d85-a03a-c1bf13cab2e6', 'word_count': 644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab73084c-9070-5d85-a03a-c1bf13cab2e6'}, {'abstract': 'Irving H. Picard, the Madoff trustee, is facing potentially major obstacles in his lawsuits against banks because of a judge’s ruling against claims against HSBC.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/madoff-trustees-job-just-became-much-tougher/', 'snippet': 'Irving H. Picard, the Madoff trustee, is facing potentially major obstacles in his lawsuits against banks because of a judge’s ruling against claims against HSBC.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shannon Stapleton/ReutersIrving H. Picard, the court-appointed trustee in the Madoff fraud case.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Madoff Trustee’s Job Just Became Much Tougher', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ponzi Schemes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madoff, Bernard L', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Picard, Irving H', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rakoff, Jed S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HSBC Holdings PLC.', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:22:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46d6d019-96a4-5684-9b8a-dabe931bfc42', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46d6d019-96a4-5684-9b8a-dabe931bfc42'}, {'abstract': 'Analyzing past climate conditions using tree rings is complicated when the trees have been food for animals, researchers have found.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/02/science/02obtree.html', 'snippet': 'Analyzing past climate conditions using tree rings is complicated when the trees have been food for animals, researchers have found.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than 100 years, tree rings have served as a reliable way to estimate past climate conditions because in warmer years, which usually mean better growing conditions, tree rings tend to be wider. But herbivores that nibble away at trees also have an effect on tree ring size — a phenomenon that was known but never measured. Now, researchers from Norway and Scotland report the significant effect of grazing sheep on tree ring size over a nine-year period. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/02/science/02obtree/02obtree-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/08/02/science/02obtree/02obtree-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/02/science/02obtree/02obtree-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/02/science/02obtree/02obtree-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/08/02/science/02obtree/02obtree-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nibblers Affect Climate Tales That Tree Rings Tell', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nibblers Affect Climate Tales That Tree Rings Tell', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sheep', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:26:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29d3b9b7-6cd9-57e5-9718-3dfffb9321ab', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29d3b9b7-6cd9-57e5-9718-3dfffb9321ab'}, {'abstract': 'The Season 9 premiere of “Project Runway” on Lifetime garnered 2.7 million viewers on Thursday night.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/heidi-klum-and-tim-gunn-make-it-work-on-runway/', 'snippet': 'The Season 9 premiere of “Project Runway” on Lifetime garnered 2.7 million viewers on Thursday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fashion guru Tim Gunn and the model Heidi Klum made a not quite triumphant but still satisfactory return to New York City as “Project Runway” had its Season 9 premiere on Lifetime on Thursday night. \xa0The fashion designer competition averaged 2.7 million total viewers according to Nielsen, making small gains over the premiere from last season. \xa0It was certainly an eventful episode, as four of the twenty contestants were eliminated by the 20-minute mark.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Heidi Klum and Tim Gunn Make It Work on ‘Runway’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Models (Professional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gunn, Timothy M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klum, Heidi', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lifetime Television', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen Media Research', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:29:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f398a57-aeec-5ac7-a205-2a855f4b7021', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f398a57-aeec-5ac7-a205-2a855f4b7021'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s Book Review, I write about scholars in the digital humanities who use computer analysis to zero in on the nitty-gritty of literary style. Many of their tools are now publicly available online.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/exposing-literary-style-one-word-at-a-time/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s Book Review, I write about scholars in the digital humanities who use computer analysis to zero in on the nitty-gritty of literary style. Many of their tools are now publicly available online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s Mechanic Muse column for the Book Review, I look at how vast new databases and techniques can help scholars of the digital humanities zero in on the nitty-gritty of literary style, right down to the level of individual words. An exciting development over the past few years is that many of the tools that scholars use to plumb the depths of literary usage are now publicly available online. These resources aren’t locked up in an ivory tower, accessible only to an elite band of researchers. Call it the democratization of digital scholarship.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exposing Literary Style, One Word at a Time', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:30:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0da9f742-c377-5c1c-a824-c94fecdd77a4', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0da9f742-c377-5c1c-a824-c94fecdd77a4'}, {'abstract': 'The fashion star of the recent heat wave was the shirt, in its many styles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/fashion/100000000973237/on-the-street--sizzling.html', 'snippet': 'The fashion star of the recent heat wave was the shirt, in its many styles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fashion star of the recent heat wave was the shirt, in its many styles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/fashion/video-onthestreet/video-onthestreet-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/29/fashion/video-onthestreet/video-onthestreet-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/29/fashion/video-onthestreet/video-onthestreet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/29/fashion/video-onthestreet/video-onthestreet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Street | Sizzling', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sidewalks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cunningham, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:31:55+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'Simone S. Oliver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simone', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Oliver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/34b33052-573b-5183-819c-539ce9ff2b73', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/34b33052-573b-5183-819c-539ce9ff2b73'}, {'abstract': 'Outrageous behavior demands extraordinary responses.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/lawyers-coins-and-money/', 'snippet': 'Outrageous behavior demands extraordinary responses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So if something does pass the House, it will demand a constitutional balanced-budget amendment as the price of a second vote next year. I think we can safely say that the political process has failed. Now what?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lawyers, Coins, and Money', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:33:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4a74751-7143-52f3-856c-a2893f21f5bd', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4a74751-7143-52f3-856c-a2893f21f5bd'}, {'abstract': 'A trip around the world in one magazine.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/this-sunday-where-would-you-like-to-go/', 'snippet': 'A trip around the world in one magazine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A pleasure of making this magazine (and, I hope, reading it) is the wide range of places we get to cover. It’s one factor that we take into account as we put together an issue — how many different corners of the world can we pack into our pages? Here’s where our writers take us this week:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Sunday: Where Would You Like to Go?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:33:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/291af57f-55f3-5b01-9d56-c400434ebc78', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/291af57f-55f3-5b01-9d56-c400434ebc78'}, {'abstract': 'Children in New York can make the best of tough environments. And, as the work of Times photographers shows through the decades, they always have.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/a-citys-hard-edges-softened-by-joy/', 'snippet': 'Children in New York can make the best of tough environments. And, as the work of Times photographers shows through the decades, they always have.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is there a creature as imaginative or as indefatigable as the city child in search of a place to play? New York would seem to have been designed for everything but youthful frolic. And, to be sure, it can hold great perils. Yet from generation to generation — ever since the kids sang “Ring Around the Rosie” and “London Bridge Is Falling Down” — the sidewalks of New York have yielded to youngsters’ inexhaustible need to frisk, exult, screech, plot and plunge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A City’s Hard Edges, Softened by Joy', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:35:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap and Darcy Eveleigh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Darcy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eveleigh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c6c8349-5c61-57d6-841d-a6ef4e785abf', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c6c8349-5c61-57d6-841d-a6ef4e785abf'}, {'abstract': 'Glacier National Park in Montana was named for huge rivers of ice that defined its terrain. Today’s visitors are witnessing their retreat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/travel/glacier-national-park-montana-fading-glaciers.html', 'snippet': 'Glacier National Park in Montana was named for huge rivers of ice that defined its terrain. Today’s visitors are witnessing their retreat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AN hour or so up ahead, at the higher elevations along the trail that leads over Siyeh Pass, huckleberries were ripening. Even a dawdling day hiker like me knows that huckleberries can quickly mean grizzlies in Glacier National Park. I indulged a nervous tic and patted around for the loud red aerosol can on my belt, whose label reads Counter Assault. It’s effective as a bear repellent, but even more reliable at making an urbanite feel faintly ridiculous.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31COVER_SPAN/31COVER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31COVER_SPAN/31COVER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31COVER_SPAN/31COVER_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31COVER_SPAN/31COVER_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31COVER_SPAN/31COVER_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Twilight of the Glaciers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Twilight of the Glaciers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Glacier National Park (Mont)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hikes and Hiking', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Glaciers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T19:36:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By STEPHEN P. NASH', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'NASH', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3f7c692-5007-5e01-8b66-b7941a37a12b', 'word_count': 2678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3f7c692-5007-5e01-8b66-b7941a37a12b'}, {'abstract': 'From Paris to Seville, libraries can be quiet oases from the bustle of the tourist trail.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/travel/libraries-that-offer-respite-from-the-crowds.html', 'snippet': 'From Paris to Seville, libraries can be quiet oases from the bustle of the tourist trail.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EUROPEAN cities are feet-on-the-pavement destinations, where many of us find ourselves trudging along familiar routes with hordes of tourists just like us. To take a break, we are likely to stop at the cafes and museum gardens already overcrowded with fellow travelers.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/travel/31CULTURED_SPAN/31CULTURED_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367,