-e9e6-5ecf-935b-9b6ae2e0b0a6'}, {'abstract': 'The bears that have been wandering into backyards or homes, looking for food and sometimes attacking people, have in some ways been shaped by humans. But in a broader, wilder way, they are simply being bears.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/29/projecting-our-nature-onto-ravenous-bears/', 'snippet': 'The bears that have been wandering into backyards or homes, looking for food and sometimes attacking people, have in some ways been shaped by humans. But in a broader, wilder way, they are simply being bears.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Park managers in the United States and Canada have responded to a recent wave of encounters between bears and people with new rules -– for the human side of the equation, anyway. Bears will still be allowed to be bears, but in Grand Teton National Park in Wyoming, managers clarified rules this week about how close visitors can “willfully approach” the animals for, say, a photograph: 100 yards.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Projecting Our Nature Onto Ravenous Bears', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bears', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Walking', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Grand Teton National Park (Wyo)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wyoming', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-29T21:10:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': '