paragraph': 'Robert C. W. Ettinger, a science fiction writer and physics instructor whose idea of freezing the dead for future reanimation repelled most scientists, inspired Woody Allen and Mike Myers to some of their best work and persuaded at least 105 game humans to pay $28,000 each to have their bodies preserved in liquid nitrogen at his Cryonics Institute in suburban Detroit, died on July 23 at his home in Clinton Township, Mich. He was 92.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/30/world/30ettinger/30ettinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/30/world/30ettinger/30ettinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/30/world/30ettinger/30ettinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert C. W. Ettinger, a Proponent of Life After (Deep-Frozen) Death, Is Dead at 92', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Robert C. W. Ettinger Is Dead at 92; Proponent of Life After (Frozen) Death', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science Fiction', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ettinger, Robert C W', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-30T01:53:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Vitello', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vitello