'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-30T22:02:31+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/6263dd65-6917-53bb-9cad-e566c5f2f88c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/6263dd65-6917-53bb-9cad-e566c5f2f88c'}, {'abstract': 'A swirl of fame swept down a dirt road to find Bobby Kirk and his country wisdom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/us/31fish.html', 'snippet': 'A swirl of fame swept down a dirt road to find Bobby Kirk and his country wisdom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOGART, Ga. — It was hard not to notice that the stream of tobacco juice spit expertly by Bobby Kirk barely missed the sassy summer sandals on the feet of the New York television producer.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/us/31fish/FISH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/31/us/31fish/31fish-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/31/us/31fish/31fish-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'He Said It Was Too Hot to Fish, and That Was Just for Starters', 'kicker': 'Bogart Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'He Said It Was Too Hot to Fish, And That Was Just for Starters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-30T22:06:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kim Severson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/334fd6fb-410f-5d37-8e6a-65c41f96dcff', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/334fd6fb-410f-5d37-8e6a-65c41f96dcff'}, {'abstract': 'Even under the most generous assumptions, the Senate majority leader will need to coax three Republicans to support his bill. A list of the most likely candidates.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/30/which-republicans-might-support-reids-debt-bill/', 'snippet': 'Even under the most generous assumptions, the Senate majority leader will need to coax three Republicans to support his bill. A list of the most likely candidates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sooner or later, if the debt ceiling is to be increased, some kind of bill will need to be passed out of the Democratic-led Senate. Unless Senator Harry Reid, the majority leader, is willing to take a procedural gamble, it will need 60 votes to overcome a Republican filibuster.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Which Republicans Might Support Reid’s Debt Bill?', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reid, Harry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-30T22:14:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b7e7437-2e2d-5053-9e9e-101ef695fbbe', 'word_count': 775, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b7e7437-2e2d-5053-9e9e-101ef695fbbe'}, {'abstract': 'Readers comment on my column about John Stott and evangelical Christians — and whether liberals display a reverse intolerance toward them.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/30/john-stott-and-evangelical-christians/', 'snippet': 'Readers comment on my column about John Stott and evangelical Christians — and whether liberals display a reverse intolerance toward them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m actually off on vacation in Ashland, Oregon, enjoying the Oregon Shakespeare Festival with my family, and I hadn’t intended to write a column for Sunday. But then I saw that John Stott had died, and I didn’t want to miss the chance to write about him and evangelicals more broadly — hence my Sunday column on the subject. We in the op-ed world don’t write about religion perhaps as much as we should: it’s a huge force shaping society and policy, and yet rarely makes the op-ed pages. And evangelicals in particular, especially serious ones, tend to be neglected since there are so few evangelicals in major news organizations. About one-third of America is evangelical, yet you’d be hard pressed to find many (with the exception of black evangelicals, whose politics are very different from white evangelicals) in leading news organizations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'John Stott and Evangelical Christians', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-30T22:15:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'sectio