 do?', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/as-states-struggle-how-much-for-the-arts/', 'snippet': 'Across the country this is a tough time for small arts groups because state grants have largely shriveled up. Thirty-one states, still staggered by the recession, cut their arts budgets for the  2012 fiscal year. What are artists and arts groups i...', 'lead_paragraph': '8:15 p.m. |Update: Readers Respond\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As States Struggle, How Much for the Arts?', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brownback, Sam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Arts Commission', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Inge, William, Center for the Arts', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T15:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b7b63aa-6126-5dc3-af03-3c6389c4022b', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b7b63aa-6126-5dc3-af03-3c6389c4022b'}, {'abstract': 'A player who always folds is great for the other side.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/my-get-rich-scheme/', 'snippet': 'A player who always folds is great for the other side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Please, just let me play poker with President Obama. I’ll make millions in no time at all.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Get-Rich Scheme', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T15:32:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e60a99cb-1b1c-5227-baa3-835d7135edf6', 'word_count': 46, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e60a99cb-1b1c-5227-baa3-835d7135edf6'}, {'abstract': 'Susana Baca has been named minister of culture in Peru, where President Ollanta Humala took office last week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/perus-new-culture-minister-is-susana-baca/', 'snippet': 'Susana Baca has been named minister of culture in Peru, where President Ollanta Humala took office last week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Afro-Peruvian folk singer Susana Baca has been named minister of culture in Peru for the new populist government of President Ollanta Humala, who took office last week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Peru’s New Culture Minister Is Susana Baca', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baca, Susana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Humala, Ollanta', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Peru', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T15:58:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d22ad52-e515-59ee-bd92-2dc8bba6ded0', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d22ad52-e515-59ee-bd92-2dc8bba6ded0'}, {'abstract': '“The Smurfs,” a new film from Sony Pictures Entertainment, has performed well at North American movie theaters over the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/the-smurfs-are-blue-but-theyre-rolling-in-green/', 'snippet': '“The Smurfs,” a new film from Sony Pictures Entertainment, has performed well at North American movie theaters over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Smurfs humiliated a cluster of Hollywood’s heaviest weights at the weekend box office. Analysts had predicted that “Cowboys & Aliens” — starring Daniel Craig and Harrison Ford, directed by Jon Favreau (“Iron Man”) and produced by Brian Grazer and Steven Spielberg — would easily dominate ticket sales. Instead, it was a photo finish with “The Smurfs,” a cartoon-live action hybrid. Both films took in an estimated $36.2 million at North American theaters, as “The Smurfs” outperformed expectations, and “Cowboys & Aliens” failed to capture young men in significant numbers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Smurfs’ Are Blue, but They’re Rolling in Green', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DreamWorks Studios', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Pictures Entertainment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T16:06:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38a16125-e15e-5bbb-a736-478c63b57c9d', 'word_count': 261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38a16125-e15e-5bbb-a736-478c63b57c9d'}, {'abstract': 'Congressional leaders of both parties and President Obama had a framework for a deal to present to their caucuses, moving closer to a measure that could pass both chambers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/01/us/politics/01FISCAL.html', 'snippet': 'Congressional leaders of both parties and President Obama had a framework for a deal to present to their caucuses, moving closer to a measure that could pass both chambers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama and Congressional leaders of both parties said late Sunday that they had agreed to a framework for a budget deal that would cut trillions of dollars in federal spending over the next decade and clear the way for an increase in the government’s borrowing limit.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/01/us/01fiscal5_span/01fiscal5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/08/01/us/01fiscal5_span/01fiscal5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/01/us/01fiscal5_span/01fiscal5-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/01/us/01fiscal5_span/01fiscal5-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/08/01/us/01fiscal5_span/01fiscal5-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama and Leaders Reach Debt Deal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leaders Agree On Outlines Of Deal To End Debt Crisis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConnell, Mitch', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reid, Harry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Joint Select Committee on Deficit Reduction', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T16:18:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Hulse and Helene Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hulse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a411547-ec4b-5686-be0d-759ba736ce6b', 'word_count': 1442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a411547-ec4b-5686-be0d-759ba736ce6b'}, {'abstract': 'Eighteen people were killed in the ancient city of Kashgar, in the far western Xinjiang region.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/01/world/asia/01china.html', 'snippet': 'Eighteen people were killed in the ancient city of Kashgar, in the far western Xinjiang region.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — A weekend of violence in China’s far western Xinjiang region has left at least 18 people dead in the ancient city of Kashgar, state-controlled media reported Sunday. It was the second major episode of violence in the last several weeks in an area racked by ethnic tensions.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deadly Violence Strikes Chinese City Racked by Ethnic Tensions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deadly Violence Strikes Chinese City Racked by Ethnic Tensions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Uighurs (Chinese Ethnic Group)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Xinhua', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kashgar (China)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T16:19:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f296532f-0473-543d-b353-55e4a9002c12', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f296532f-0473-543d-b353-55e4a9002c12'}, {'abstract': 'A small group of economists and financiers argue that it would be better to focus on long-term financial health, even if it means not paying the bills right away.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/01/business/deal-may-avert-default-but-some-ask-is-that-good.html', 'snippet': 'A small group of economists and financiers argue that it would be better to focus on long-term financial health, even if it means not paying the bills right away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The resounding view on Wall Street and among many financial regulators and veteran lawmakers is that there will be a catastrophe if the United States does not raise its debt limit in the next few days.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deal May Avert Default, but Some Ask, ‘Is That Good?’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deal May Avert Default, but Some Ask if That’s Good', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T16:31:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louise Story', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Story', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dab8c1d8-c682-5acb-90c2-9db45a30daa6', 'word_count': 1587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dab8c1d8-c682-5acb-90c2-9db45a30daa6'}, {'abstract': 'Chinese travelers can call home for what it costs to make a local call in those markets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/01/technology/companies/roaming-fees-as-low-as-chinas-wont-be-matched-soon.html', 'snippet': 'Chinese travelers can call home for what it costs to make a local call in those markets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — When Su Xiaoqin, a Chinese translator living in Düsseldorf, calls family and friends back in Shanghai, she does not use the mobile network of her German operator, O2. She pops in the SIM card for China Mobile.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China’s Low Roaming Fees Won’t Be Matched Soon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chinese Mobile Operators Cut Foreign Roaming Fees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'China Mobile Ltd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'China Unicom (Hong Kong) Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'China Telecom Corporation Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Telecom Italia S.p.A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T17:13:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Companies', 'byline': {'original': "By Kevin J. O'Brien", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': "O'Brien", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05b55ca1-9a23-5f33-b566-b82795fa04f5', 'word_count': 1102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05b55ca1-9a23-5f33-b566-b82795fa04f5'}, {'abstract': 'The Democrat-controlled Senate voted 50 to 49 in favor of ending debate on the measure, but failed to get the necessary 60 votes required to move to a final vote.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/senate-blocks-reids-debt-ceiling-plan/', 'snippet': 'The Democrat-controlled Senate voted 50 to 49 in favor of ending debate on the measure, but failed to get the necessary 60 votes required to move to a final vote.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Senate voted Sunday not to move forward with a Democratic debt plan as Congress prepared to take up a bipartisan deal to raise the debt ceiling as soon as it is reached this weekend.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Senate Blocks Reid’s Debt Ceiling Plan', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T17:34:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f716e9f-b793-50ac-bdda-1b7617c05a84', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f716e9f-b793-50ac-bdda-1b7617c05a84'}, {'abstract': 'The attraction, in honor of Arnold Schwarzenegger, opened on Saturday in the Austrian village where he was born.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/schwarzenegger-now-has-a-hometown-museum/', 'snippet': 'The attraction, in honor of Arnold Schwarzenegger, opened on Saturday in the Austrian village where he was born.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new museum honoring Arnold Schwarzenegger, the bodybuilder, movie star and former California governor, opened on Saturday in the Austrian village where he was born, Thal, near Graz, to coincide with his 64th birthday. The museum, located in the house where Mr. Schwarzenegger lived from birth until he departed in 1966 for England and then the United States, clearly has his blessing: exhibits include not only his childhood bed and first weight-lifting equipment but also a sword from “Conan the Barbarian,” memorabilia from the “Terminator” movies and a copy of the desk he used as governor. Despite the recent blows to Mr. Schwarzenegger’s image in the United States with his disclosure that he fathered a child out of wedlock, “his career was so exciting and so special and I think the Austrian people are really proud” of him, Helga Forstner, the museum coordinator, told the BBC.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Schwarzenegger Now Has a Hometown Museum', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwarzenegger, Arnold', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Austria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T17:48:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2de0b7d-7534-5608-8bf4-d75b039b3c38', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2de0b7d-7534-5608-8bf4-d75b039b3c38'}, {'abstract': 'In New York, big-money prices and maxims like “location, location, location” figure in death as well.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/07/31/buying-a-final-resting-place-very-gently-used/', 'snippet': 'In New York, big-money prices and maxims like “location, location, location” figure in death as well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For sale: 101-year-old Greek Revival building in landmark area. Top-quality granite exterior, marble interior, high ceiling, custom-made window by famous artist. Immediate occupancy: vacant since the 1950s. Sleeps eight. Asking $750,000.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Buying a Final Resting Place, Very Gently Used', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cemeteries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name'