: 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T22:32:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b34a40ae-4302-5d23-bf5c-5abc8f7b7ed3', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b34a40ae-4302-5d23-bf5c-5abc8f7b7ed3'}, {'abstract': 'Using a ticket system it created, Eventbrite has entered the multibillion-dollar ticketing industry and set its sights on events below Ticketmaster’s radar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/01/business/media/eventbrite-a-ticket-sales-start-up-aims-at-smaller-events.html', 'snippet': 'Using a ticket system it created, Eventbrite has entered the multibillion-dollar ticketing industry and set its sights on events below Ticketmaster’s radar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On July 23, 3,000 carnivores salivated their way through Meatopia, a festival on a Brooklyn pier where 45 chefs from around the country offered fleshy dishes like veal belly gyros and Southern pulled-pork shooters.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ticket Sales Start-Up Aims at Smaller Box Offices', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ticket Sales Start-Up Aims at Smaller Box Offices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eventbrite', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T22:32:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81e48cbc-9c6b-5fba-9dcb-e5a257e15778', 'word_count': 794, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81e48cbc-9c6b-5fba-9dcb-e5a257e15778'}, {'abstract': 'The State Department is teaming up with the hip-hop artist will.i.am, a founder of the Black Eyed Peas, to organize a concert in Beijing this year to encourage cultural and educational exchanges between the United States and China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/01/arts/music/state-department-taps-william-for-china-effort.html', 'snippet': 'The State Department is teaming up with the hip-hop artist will.i.am, a founder of the Black Eyed Peas, to organize a concert in Beijing this year to encourage cultural and educational exchanges between the United States and China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The State Department is teaming up with the hip-hop artist will.i.am, a founder of the Black Eyed Peas, to organize a concert in Beijing this year to encourage cultural and educational exchanges between the United States and China. The announcement came after Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton met on Friday in Washington with will.i.am, above, a creator of the 2008 viral video “Yes, We Can” that boosted support for Barack Obama among the young, to discuss the Obama administration’s “100,000 Strong Initiative,” aimed at increasing the number and diversity of Americans studying in China. For the event will.i.am and other American and Chinese artists will perform; no date or location for the show has been specified.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'State Department Taps will.i.am for China Effort', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'State Department Taps will.i.am for China Effort', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T22:35:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e49015e-76b5-56c4-97a5-21803a23a0c0', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e49015e-76b5-56c4-97a5-21803a23a0c0'}, {'abstract': 'The Kings of Leon cut short a concert on Friday night in Dallas and postponed a show on Saturday in Houston.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/01/arts/music/the-kings-of-leon-are-feeling-the-heat.html', 'snippet': 'The Kings of Leon cut short a concert on Friday night in Dallas and postponed a show on Saturday in Houston.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite their image as road-hardened Southern boys, the Kings of Leon seem to have wilted over the weekend in the heat of Texas, where the band cut short a concert on Friday night in Dallas and postponed a show on Saturday in Houston. Before going offstage, promising but failing to return, the lead singer Caleb Followill told the Dallas audience, “I’m about to fall down here because I’m so goddamn hot.” But a pair of tweets by the bass player Jared Followill, his younger brother, suggested that tensions within the group, which has had a string of chart-topping CDs, may also have figured in the actions. “There are internal sicknesses & problems that have needed to be addressed,” he wrote in one message. In the other one he added: “I can’t lie. There are problems in our band bigger than not drinking enough Gatorade.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Kings of Leon Are Feeling the Heat', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Kings of Leon Are Feeling the Heat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kings of Leon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T22:36:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b2d16f8-b439-5b3b-843a-ba25f1ee5ab6', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b2d16f8-b439-5b3b-843a-ba25f1ee5ab6'}, {'abstract': 'Two major movie theater chains, AMC and Regal, have instructed their properties not to show “Electric Daisy Carnival Experience.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/01/movies/two-theater-chains-block-documentary.html', 'snippet': 'Two major movie theater chains, AMC and Regal, have instructed their properties not to show “Electric Daisy Carnival Experience.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two major movie theater chains, AMC and Regal, have instructed their properties not to show “Electric Daisy Carnival Experience,” about an increasingly popular annual electronic dance music festival, after a confrontation between fans and the police during an event last week in Los Angeles to promote the film, which is scheduled to open nationally on Thursday. “It is extremely disheartening that some theaters wrongly associated an unaffiliated block party, and its disorderly crowd, with the private screening of the film,” Insomniac, the movie’s promoter and the festival’s organizer, posted on its Web site, adding that the movie, which features interviews with or performances by rave music stars like David Guetta, Steve Aoki and DJ Kaskade, whose impromptu street performance set off the Los Angeles disturbance, “will continue to be shown in hundreds of theaters nationwide.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Theater Chains Block Documentary', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Theater Chains Block Documentary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-07-31T22:37:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3360f8ae-0573-54dd-bdca-38f7abfd65e2', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3360f8ae-0573-54dd-bdca-38f7abfd65e2'}, {'abstract': 'The trial of former President Hosni Mubarak will be held in a large hall and will be broadcast on state television.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/08/01/world/middleeast/01egypt.html', 'snippet': 'The trial of former President Hosni Mubarak will be held in a large hall and will be broadcast on state television.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — The trial of former President Hosni Mubarak will be held in a large hall that can accommodate 600 spectators, and will be broadcast on Egyptian state televis